ATSWA.put("TAX", {"intro":"Taxation is the largest, most regulation-dense subject in ATSWA Part III — a 25-chapter survey of the Nigerian tax system as it exists under the newly-consolidated Nigeria Tax Act 2025 (NTA) and Nigeria Tax Administration Act 2025 (NTAA), which replace the older patchwork of CITA, PITA, VAT Act and related legislation from the September 2026 diet onward. The chapters move from first principles and administration through the procedural machinery (returns, objections, tax clearance), the major tax types (VAT, stamp duties, withholding tax, customs and excise, the new fossil fuel surcharge), personal income tax (employment, investment, sole traders, partnerships, trusts and estates, loss relief, capital allowances), companies income tax (assessable profit, computation, specialised businesses), and closes with chargeable gains and the role of information technology in modern tax practice. Because this reflects genuinely new law, every section is transcribed directly from the study text's own statutory references (NTA/NTAA section numbers cited throughout) rather than from older, pre-reform tax knowledge.","chapters":[{"n":1,"t":"Introduction to Taxation","brief":"The foundations the entire subject builds on: what tax actually is (and how it differs from a levy), the three aspects of a tax system (policy, laws, administration), Nigeria's tax history, the objectives and principles of taxation, how taxes are classified, the sources of Nigerian tax law, tax avoidance vs. tax evasion, the multiple-taxation problem and its solutions, the VAIDS/VOARS tax amnesty schemes, and the National Tax Policy 2017 — all under the newly-consolidated Nigeria Tax Act 2025 (NTA) and Nigeria Tax Administration Act 2025 (NTAA).","outcomes":["Explain the meaning and historical background of taxation","Explain the classification, objectives and principles of taxation","Explain the sources of tax laws","Explain the policy objectives of the National Tax Policy, 2017, and the policy guidelines that future taxes are expected to align with"],"secs":[{"n":"1.2","t":"Introduction","b":[{"def":{"t":"Tax","d":"a compulsory levy or financial charge imposed on a taxpayer, or upon their property, by the government to provide security, social amenities and other amenities for the well-being of society."}},{"key":"The main purpose of taxation is to raise funds to defray expenses incurred for the **common interest** of the country, **without reference to any special benefit** conferred on the individual taxpayer. The Nigerian Government, like others worldwide, has legislative power to impose any form of tax, at whatever rate it deems appropriate — notably, no Nigerian tax law actually attempts to define the word \"tax\" itself."},{"p":"Several external definitions fill this gap:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Source","Definition"],"rows":[["Oxford Advanced Learner's Dictionary","\"Money that has to be paid to the government so that it can pay for public services.\""],["Black's Law Dictionary","\"Monetary charge imposed by the Government on persons, entities or property, levied to yield public revenue.\""],["Thomas Cooley","\"Enforced proportional contributions from persons and property, levied by the State, by virtue of its sovereignty, for the support of government and for all public needs.\""],["*Mathew v Chikory Marketing Board of Victoria Australia* (Latham C.J.)","\"A compulsory exaction of money by a public authority for public purposes.\""],["*United States v Butler* (Justice Roberts)","\"A tax…signifies an exaction for the support of the government.\""]]}},{"key":"Distilling these, tax has **three basic features**: a compulsory levy; imposed by government or a local authority; for a public purpose or to encourage social justice. Tax is **not** a voluntary payment, but a compulsory pecuniary burden placed on taxpayers for the good of society — and although it is discriminatory in the sense that it is assessed on persons/property based on profit, income or gain, the benefit conferred on citizens is given **without reference** to any individual taxpayer's own contribution."},{"p":"In conclusion: tax is a levy imposed on all residents, and non-residents doing business, within a tax jurisdiction — a civic and patriotic responsibility that also funds socio-economic and infrastructural amenities and enhances industrial efficiency. **Taxation** is the process of levying and collecting tax from taxpayers."}]},{"n":"1.3","t":"Essentials of a good tax system","b":[{"key":"A good tax system should create jobs, and a tax rise should help arrest wasteful expenditure — though \"no one pays tax with a smile,\" taxation is a means of making the private sector pay for services rendered by the public sector, shaping/directing/stimulating economic activity, redistributing financial resources, and financing social welfare."},{"p":"A tax system involves **three aspects**:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Aspect","What it covers"],"rows":[["**Tax policy**","Guidelines/principles for imposing and collecting taxes — e.g. shifting emphasis from income tax to (less evasion-prone) consumption tax; reducing individual tax burden, widening the tax net, and encouraging savings/investment; introducing self-assessment to encourage taxpayer participation; and moving from coercive taxation toward voluntary compliance, as Nigeria has recently done."],["**Tax laws**","The notable Nigerian tax legislation: the Nigeria Tax Act (NTA) 2025; the Nigeria Tax Administration Act (NTAA) 2025; the Nigeria Revenue Service (Establishment) Act (NRSA) 2015; the Joint Revenue Board of Nigeria (Establishment) Act (JRBA) 2025; the Petroleum Industry Act (PIA) 2021 (as amended); and the Nigerian Insurance Industry Act (NIIRA) 2025."],["**Tax administration**","The practical interpretation and application of tax laws, carried out by the Federal, State and Local governments — the Nigeria Revenue Service (NRS, s.3(1) NRSA 2025), the States Internal Revenue Service (SIRS, s.87(1) NTAA 2025), and the Local Government Revenue Committee (s.93(1) NTAA 2025)."]]}},{"key":"The Constitution vests legislation of income tax (personal or corporate) **exclusively** with the Federal Government, to promote uniformity — though the three tiers of government **share administration** of the various taxes. Tax laws are periodically reviewed in line with changing social/political/economic conditions; non-compliance with the machinery set up to enforce them attracts penalties and fines."}]},{"n":"1.4","t":"Historical background of taxation in Nigeria","b":[{"p":"Nigerian taxation dates to **1904**, when Lord Lugard introduced income tax — starting as a community tax that culminated in the **Native Revenue Ordinance of 1917**. An amended 1918 ordinance extended the 1917 provisions to Southern Nigeria (first applied to Abeokuta and Benin City), extended further to Eastern Nigeria in 1928."},{"p":"Nigerian income taxation proper did not begin until **1940**, when the 1917/1918/1928 Native Revenue Ordinances were incorporated into direct taxation under **Ordinance No. 4 of 1940** — discriminatory in that it applied only to natives outside the township of Lagos. The more comprehensive **Income Tax Ordinance No. 29 of 1943** (effective 1 April 1943) governed the assessment of both non-Africans resident outside Lagos and everyone (African and non-African) resident in Lagos — no significant change followed until 1956."},{"key":"Tax assessment originally rested with the **Resident** (the Governor-appointed officer in charge of a province), assisted by authorised administrative officers, chief elders and other persons of influence, and any native authority recognised by native law and custom as tax-collector."},{"p":"The **Northern Region** did not pass its own personal income tax law until **1962**; all regional tax laws later formed the basis of personal income taxation in the states later created from those regions. In Lagos, the 1943 Ordinance remained in force until the **Personal Income Tax (Lagos) Act 1961**, which gave birth to separate laws for individuals and companies — the Income Tax Management Act (ITMA) and the Companies Income Tax Act (CITA). The Petroleum Profit Ordinance was passed in 1959, but took effect from 1 January 1958."}]},{"n":"1.5","t":"Objectives of taxation","b":[{"ol":["revenue generation, to meet the needs of government;","providing a fiscal tool for stimulating economic growth and development;","social functions — redressing rural-urban population drift, curbing environmental pollution, promoting a decent society; and","redistributing income/wealth to reduce inequality — achieved mostly through progressive tax."]}]},{"n":"1.6","t":"Principles of taxation","b":[{"p":"Seven guidelines underlie a good tax system:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Principle","Meaning"],"rows":[["**Certainty**","The taxpayer should know the exact amount expected, the basis of taxation, the applicable rate, and the relevant tax authority to pay."],["**Economy in collection cost**","The cost of collecting tax should be relatively lower than the amount collected."],["**Convenience**","The method and timing of payment must be convenient to taxpayers, who must be easy to locate."],["**Efficiency**","Both compliance and administrative cost must be kept lower than the revenue raised, to avoid resentment — especially among taxpayers with smaller income."],["**Equity**","Taxes must be, and be seen to be, fair — distinguishing **vertical equity** (the rich pay more than the poor) from **horizontal equity** (equal treatment for people in similar situations)."],["**Flexibility**","The tax system must serve as a veritable fiscal tool — rates should be alterable without much difficulty."],["**Neutrality**","A good tax system should not distort economic choices or impinge adversely on economic activity."]]}}]},{"n":"1.7","t":"Types and classification of taxes","b":[{"p":"Nigerian taxes are classified by **method** (proportional, progressive, regressive) and by **incidence** (direct, indirect)."},{"h4":"1.7.1  Methods"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Type","How it works"],"rows":[["**Proportional tax**","A flat rate on total assessable income — tax payable is proportional to income (e.g. at a flat 20%, N100,000 income pays N20,000 tax, N1,000,000 income pays N200,000). Aims to redistribute income from the well-to-do to the less privileged."],["**Progressive tax**","Graduated — higher rates apply as income increases."],["**Regressive tax**","Tax payable **decreases** as income increases — not commonly applied."]]}},{"p":"The current personal income tax table illustrates the progressive concept:"},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Taxable income band (Naira)","Tax rate (%)"],"rows":[["First N800,000","0"],["Next N2,200,000","15"],["Next N9,000,000","18"],["Next N13,000,000","21"],["Over N25,000,000","23"],["Above N50,000,000","25"]]}},{"h4":"1.7.2  Incidence"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Type","Detail"],"rows":[["**Direct tax**","Assessable directly on the taxpayer, who pays on their own property/income/profit. Examples: personal income tax (PIT), companies income tax (CIT), hydrocarbon tax."],["**Indirect tax**","Imposed on commodities before they reach the consumer, paid as part of the selling price rather than as tax directly — affecting the cost of living as a form of expenditure taxation. Examples: value added tax (VAT), stamp duties, excise duties, customs duties."]]}},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Merits of direct tax","Demerits of direct tax"],"rows":[["Lower cost of collection; more equitable (higher-income taxpayers bear a greater burden); easier to ascertain tax incidence.","May discourage hard work; can enable tax evasion especially at high rates; may cause social unrest at very high rates, since taxpayers bear the whole burden."]]}},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Merits of indirect tax","Demerits of indirect tax"],"rows":[["A good revenue source; easily adjustable; a fiscal tool to check consumption of undesirable goods; can protect infant industries; more difficult to evade.","Collection cost may be higher; collusion between officials and taxpayers can cause revenue loss; may discourage local investment where export/excise duties are high."]]}},{"note":"Indirect tax can be **specific** (a fixed amount per unit of a commodity) or **ad-valorem** (a percentage of the commodity's cost)."},{"h4":"1.7.3  Classification of taxes (by tax base)"},{"p":"Taxes are also classified by what is being taxed: **capital** (e.g. Capital Gains Tax), **income** (e.g. Personal Income Tax, Companies Income Tax), and **consumption** (e.g. VAT, Excise Duties)."},{"h4":"1.7.4  Perspective of distribution of tax burden"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Type","Effect"],"rows":[["**Proportional**","The taxpayer pays the same percentage of income as tax (e.g. companies income tax)."],["**Progressive**","The more the taxpayer earns, the more they pay (e.g. PAYE)."],["**Regressive**","A smaller-income taxpayer pays a greater percentage of income as tax than a higher-income taxpayer — all flat rates are regressive in this sense (e.g. VAT)."]]}}]},{"n":"1.8","t":"Sources of Nigerian tax laws","b":[{"key":"Nigeria's tax system is administered through **statutes rather than common law**."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Source","Detail"],"rows":[["**Customary laws**","Native laws/customs governing taxation within an ethnic group — including Islamic law (applicable mainly in Northern Nigeria). Examples: **isakole** (payable in Yorubaland to titular heads/obas on farmland produce); **osusu-nkwu** (Eastern Nigeria); and **zakkat** (payable by Muslims on wealth held a full year, per Islamic law)."],["**Statute laws**","Acts of the National/State assemblies, local government bye-laws, or decrees/edicts under military government — conferring taxing power on the relevant authorities. Examples: NTA 2025 and NTAA 2025."],["**Case laws**","Judicial precedents (*stare decisis*) — judgments of the High Courts, Appeal Courts and Supreme Court on tax law principles bind lower courts. Since Nigerian tax law originated from English tax law, English common law principles also form a source — confirmed in ***Aderawos Timbers Trading Co. Ltd v Federal Board of Inland Revenue Service*** (1966), which held that English court decisions may be invoked to interpret Nigerian tax statutes using similar/substantially the same expressions as English statutes."]]}},{"p":"Other sources: circulars issued by, and practices of, the Inland Revenue; opinions of tax experts/authors, insofar as courts take judicial notice of them; budgets and pronouncements of relevant ministries; and the Constitution of the Federal Republic of Nigeria."}]},{"n":"1.9","t":"Differentiating taxes and levies","b":[{"p":"Nightingale (1997) defined tax as \"a compulsory contribution imposed by government; even though taxpayers may receive nothing identifiable in return, they nevertheless have the benefit of living in a relatively educated, healthy and safe society.\""},{"key":"From this: tax is **not** levied in return for any specific service. **Levies**, by contrast, are paid in return for specific government services — e.g. a court fee defrays the cost of a service undertaken primarily in the public interest but conferring a special advantage on the payer. Fines/penalties are another form of levy — not imposed to raise revenue, but as **punishment** for breaking the law."},{"h4":"1.9.1  Understanding tax bases and their importance"},{"p":"All taxes can be described as levies (since both are impositions), but **not all levies are taxes** — e.g. the tax under s.4(1) NTA on income/profits/gains accruing in or derived from Nigeria is rightly a tax, while the penalty/fine under s.127 NTAA is a **levy**, not a tax. Both are involuntary and compulsory, but a levy is not intended to be punitive the way a fine is; and if the underlying law is invalid, no charge can suffice as a tax regardless of what it is called."},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Aspect","Tax","Levy"],"rows":[["Definition","A mandatory financial charge on income, goods or activities to fund public services.","A broader term including taxes but also specific fees/charges/compulsory contributions for particular purposes."],["Scope","General — applies broadly to all eligible entities/individuals.","General or targeted (e.g. road tolls, sanitation fees)."],["Purpose","Funds general government operations (infrastructure, healthcare, defence).","May fund specific projects/services (roads, tolls, sanitation)."],["Legal basis","Imposed under tax laws (e.g. income tax, VAT).","Can be a tax or a special fee/charge (e.g. import levies, regulatory fees)."],["Flexibility","Usually non-negotiable and compulsory.","Some levies are conditional (paid only if using a service)."],["Examples","Income tax, corporate tax, VAT.","Road tolls, customs duties, environmental levies, municipal fees."]]}}]},{"n":"1.10","t":"Basic concepts in taxation","b":[{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Concept","Meaning"],"rows":[["**Tax base**","The measure on which tax liability is assessed — the portion of a taxpayer's income/property expected to suffer tax; the tax revenue derived from administering tax on taxpayers."],["**Tax rate**","The burden ratio (usually a percentage) at which a business/person is taxed — e.g. VAT is 5% on vatable goods/services."],["**Tax incidence**","The economic division of a tax burden between buyers and sellers — revealing who actually bears the cost. E.g. if a cigarette tax rise is fully passed into the price and consumers still buy the same amount, the incidence falls entirely on buyers."],["**Tax burden**","The amount of income/property/consumption tax levied on an individual or business — varying by income level, jurisdiction and current rates; income tax burdens are typically satisfied via payslip deductions."],["**Tax impact**","The effect of a tax on the production/consumption of the taxed product — e.g. raising tobacco levy might reduce tobacco sales."],["**Tax shift** (tax swap)","A change in taxation that eliminates/reduces some taxes while establishing/increasing others, keeping overall revenue the same."],["**Tax effect**","The general consequences of a specific tax scenario for a particular tax-paying entity."]]}},{"note":"Tax tribute paid by conquered territories in olden wars can be likened to tax — now institutionalised as government responsibilities to citizens have grown. A \"**Tax Cut**\" means government takes away **less** of people's own money, letting them spend more per their own choices rather than the government making those choices for them."},{"h4":"1.10.1  Enabling Acts"},{"def":{"t":"Enabling Act","d":"a statute empowering a person or body to take certain action — especially to make regulations, rules or orders — granting an entity that depends on it the power (or legitimacy) to act. In Nigeria, the National Assembly is the body responsible for making laws."}}]},{"n":"1.10.2","t":"Tax avoidance and tax evasion","b":[{"def":{"t":"Tax avoidance","d":"a situation where the taxpayer arranges their financial affairs to pay the least possible tax — e.g. avoiding VAT by not buying goods/services on which it is levied. Beyond simply boycotting, avoidance schemes are carried out after critically reviewing the tax laws, implementing devices to exploit loopholes."}},{"key":"Tax avoidance is, to a **large extent, legal**, once done within limits the tax laws permit. As Lord President Clyde stated in ***Ayrshire Pullman Motor Services and David M. Ritchie v C.I.R.***: \"No man in this country is under the smallest obligation, moral or otherwise, so to arrange his legal relations…as to enable the Inland Revenue to put the largest possible shovel into his stores…and the taxpayer is in like manner entitled to be astute to prevent…the depletion of his means by the Revenue.\" Belgore C.J. added, in ***Federal Board of Inland Revenue v American International Insurance Company (Nig.) Plc***: \"Tax is an obligation, not a duty. One is not a bad citizen if one can organize his business or trade in a legal manner to minimize his tax liability.\""},{"warn":"When avoidance schemes are stretched to the limit — transactions existing in mere form, lacking substance, entered into purely for avoidance with no genuine commercial effect — the Revenue Service will step in to **disallow** such transactions. Where loopholes are exploited (but no law contravened), avoidance remains legal; the Revenue's typical response is **specific anti-avoidance legislation** to block particular loopholes — since the legislature cannot foresee every scheme, **general anti-avoidance legislation** is also considered."},{"def":{"t":"Tax evasion","d":"a contravention of tax laws whereby a taxable person neglects to pay tax due, or reduces liability through fraudulent/untrue claims on tax forms/returns — minimising tax through illegal means, involving outright fraud and deceit (e.g. deliberately omitting an income source, or understating income)."}},{"p":"In Nigeria, large-scale evasion mainly occurs through failure to render tax returns. Evasion methods include: understating income; overstating expenditure; making false claims for allowances/reliefs; and omitting chargeable income from returns."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Tax avoidance","Tax evasion"],"rows":[["Legal.","Illegal."],["Achieved by exploiting loopholes in tax laws.","Achieved through deliberate fraud and deceit."],["Results in paying the minimum tax possible without breaking the law.","Results in not paying correct tax, or minimising tax by breaking tax laws."],["Supported by the courts in decided cases.","No support by the courts."],["No criminal liability.","Evader could be charged for criminal offences, with fines, penalties and, at times, imprisonment."],["When stretched to the extreme, the scheme could be disregarded.","At any level, the Revenue Service will frown at evasion."],["No Revenue Service investigation — prior years' assessments are not reopened.","Revenue Service investigation is instituted — prior assessments may be reopened beyond the six-year statute of limitation, subject to court approval."]]}}]},{"n":"1.11","t":"Multiple taxes","b":[{"note":"A numbering defect in the study text, flagged rather than silently corrected: within this section the book prints its sub-sections as 1.11.1, 1.11.2, 1.11.3, then **1.10.4**, then 1.11.5, then **1.10.6** and **1.10.7** — three of the seven carry the previous section's number. They are numbered 1.11.4, 1.11.6 and 1.11.7 here, which is plainly what was meant, since §1.10 is Basic concepts in taxation and has only two sub-sections of its own."},{"h4":"1.11.1  Nature of multiple taxes"},{"def":{"t":"Multiple taxes","d":"a tax regime where various, similar types of taxes are imposed on taxpayers by different tiers of government — e.g. the Federal Government's VAT on consumption alongside Lagos State's own sales tax on the same principle."}},{"h4":"1.11.2  Types of multiple taxes"},{"key":"Local governments are **particularly guilty** of this problem — pursuing tax/levy collection so recklessly it became a national problem attracting Federal attention. Some local governments have been recorded with **over 100** taxes/levies. Examples: entertainment tax, road tax, street-closure permits, refuse collection tax, market taxes/levies, loading/offloading levies."},{"h4":"1.11.3  Causes of multiple taxation in Nigeria"},{"p":"Causes of multiple taxation: lack of funding (states/local governments levying taxes without checking for overlap with federal/local levies); states deliberately denying local governments their due revenue, driving desperate local revenue-seeking; lack of tax education/awareness; all tiers under-funding their departments/agencies, prompting illegal levies; multiplicity of revenue agencies; overlapping taxing rights (Constitution vs. the Taxes and Levies (Approved List for Collection) Act Cap. T2 LFN 2004); and manual/unorthodox tax administration."},{"h4":"1.11.4  Collection of multiple taxes"},{"warn":"Collection methods include **mounting roadblocks** — collection agents force motorists to stop with spikes, demand evidence of payment, and impound tyres/jacks or rough-handle non-payers, seriously affecting inter-city commerce — and the **use of revenue agents/consultants** (external tax consultants under state Accelerated Revenue Generation Programmes, ARGP; local governments collecting via the Joint Revenue Association, JORA), historically enforced with law enforcement agents coercing companies, or harassing road users (especially branded vehicles)."},{"h4":"1.11.5  Federal Government's solution to the problem of multiplicity of taxes"},{"p":"The **Federal Government's solution** centres on the model Tax Harmonisation Law developed by the Presidential Fiscal Policy and Tax Reforms Committee (with the Joint Revenue Board), for adoption by States and Local Governments. Note the Taxes and Levies (Approved List for Collection) Act Cap. T2 LFN 2004 remains un-repealed/un-amended by ss.195-196 NTA 2025."},{"p":"Once the States Harmonised Taxes and Levies (Approved List for Collection) Bill 2025 is passed, collectible taxes/levies will be:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Tier","Taxes/levies collectible"],"rows":[["**States**","Income tax; stamp duties; property tax; road tax; haulage levy; economic development levy."],["**Local governments**","A harmonised levy (shops, slaughter slab fees, motor parks, domestic animal fees, bicycles, trucks, canoes, wheelbarrows/carts); liquor licences; TV/radio fees and other local levies; user charges (marriage/birth/death registration, street naming, signage/mobile advertisements, sewage/refuse disposal, burial ground permits, religious-place establishment fees, wharf landing charges); and daily ticketing (all market levies, including transporters' tickets)."]]}},{"h4":"1.11.6  The 1999 Constitution and multiplicity of taxes"},{"p":"The 1999 Constitution (Part II, paragraphs 7-10) seeks to eliminate multiple taxation: the National Assembly may allow a state government to collect capital gains/income/profit tax or stamp duties on its behalf, but must ensure such tax is **not levied on the same person by more than one State**; similarly, a House of Assembly may allow a local government council to collect tax/fee/rate, ensuring it is **not levied on the same person, for the same liability, by more than one local government council**."},{"h4":"1.11.7  Other possible solutions to multiplicity of taxes in Nigeria"},{"p":"Other possible solutions: reviewing and harmonising existing taxes; reviewing the Constitution to address overlapping taxing rights; abolishing unorthodox collection methods and implementing technology; and limiting the number of revenue agencies."}]},{"n":"1.12","t":"Tax amnesty: VAIDS and VOARS","b":[{"h4":"1.12.1  Tax amnesty, including the Voluntary Assets and Income Declaration Scheme (VAIDS)"},{"key":"Approved by the Federal Government in **2017**, VAIDS gave tax defaulters an opportunity to defray unsettled tax liabilities — requiring voluntary disclosure of previously undisclosed assets/income for the preceding **6 years of assessment**, within a **9-month window from 1 July 2017**. The scheme, due to expire 31 March 2018, was extended by 3 months and eventually closed **30 June 2018**."},{"p":"Objective: give taxpayers a time-limited opportunity to regularise their tax status and pay taxes due, in exchange for forgiveness of overdue interest/penalties and assurance against criminal prosecution or investigation."},{"p":"Eligibility covered anyone in default, including those who: earned income/owned assets but had not registered; had additional disclosures or amendments to make, or were registered but not filing; were not fully declaring taxable income/assets; were underpaying/under-remitting; were under audit/investigation; or were in a tax dispute they were prepared to settle out of court."},{"p":"A valid declaration required: voluntary disclosure; full, frank, complete and verifiable disclosure; use of the prescribed VAIDS forms/manner; and tax assessment by the relevant tax authority."},{"p":"Benefits for compliant taxpayers: immunity from prosecution and from tax audit; waiver of interest and penalties; and the option to spread outstanding liabilities over up to **3 years**. (This did **not** invalidate any court order/judgment already obtained where interest/penalty had already accrued.) Reliefs applied to all taxes administered by the Federal Inland Revenue Service and relevant State authorities."},{"warn":"Failure to comply meant liability for: the full principal sum; all interest and penalties; prosecution under extant laws; withdrawal of any relief granted; a comprehensive tax audit; and any sum already paid counted as part-payment of further outstanding tax on undisclosed information."},{"p":"All information provided was treated with **utmost confidentiality**; any official breaching this without authorisation was liable to prosecution. The executive order took effect **1 July 2017**."},{"h4":"1.12.2  Tax amnesty on offshore assets and incomes: Voluntary Offshore Assets Regularisation Scheme (VOARS)"},{"key":"Introduced via Presidential Executive Order 008 on **8 October 2018**, VOARS incentivised taxpayers who defaulted on foreign assets/foreign-sourced income tax for the preceding **30 years of assessment** to voluntarily regularise."},{"p":"Benefits: a **one-time levy of 35%** on all offshore assets, in lieu of outstanding taxes; permanent waiver of criminal prosecution for related offences; immunity from audit on the regularised assets; waiver of interest/penalties; an offshore assets regularisation compliance certificate; and freedom to use/invest the regularised assets anywhere."},{"p":"The VOARS Order mandated the Attorney-General/Minister of Justice to set up the VOARS facility in **Switzerland**, accessed via a **2% facility access fee** plus necessary disclosures."},{"warn":"Failure to comply meant liability for: the tax due; interest; investigation charges; and enforcement procedures on the offshore assets. The scheme ran for **12 months from 8 October 2018**."}]},{"n":"1.13","t":"National Tax Policy, 2017 (NTP)","b":[{"key":"The NTP provides the fundamental guidelines for the Nigerian tax system's orderly development. Objectives: guide the operation/review of the tax system; provide a basis for future tax legislation/administration; serve as a reference point for stakeholders; provide an accountability benchmark; and clarify stakeholder roles/responsibilities."},{"h4":"Guiding principles of the Nigerian tax system"},{"ol":["**Equity and fairness** — fair, equitable, devoid of discrimination; taxpayers pay according to ability;","**Simplicity, certainty and clarity** — laws and processes simple, clear, easy to understand;","**Convenience** — timing/manner of obligations account for taxpayer convenience;","**Low compliance cost** — kept to the barest minimum;","**Low cost of administration** — efficient, cost-effective, per international best practice;","**Flexibility** — dynamic, without retarding economic activity; and","**Sustainability** — promoting sustainable revenue/growth, synergised with other economic policy."]},{"h4":"Taxation as a tool for economic management and development"},{"p":"Goals the tax system should support: **wealth creation and employment** (promoting employment/export/local production; non-retroactive laws; equal investment opportunity for local/foreign business; long-term, tenured investment focus; broad, sector-based, transparent, monitored incentives; annually reported/quantified revenue forgone; no promotion of monopoly); **diversification** (attracting investment across sectors, boosting the revenue base); **focus on indirect taxation** (easier to collect/administer, harder to evade); **convergence of tax rates** (progressive rates gradually converging PIT's top marginal rate, CGT and CIT, reducing avoidance opportunity); **special arrangements/incentives** (sector-based, not entity-specific, not arbitrarily terminated, transparently granted/renewed); **creating a competitive edge** (few, broad-based, simplified taxes; avoiding multiple taxation via inter-governmental collaboration); and **international/regional treaties** (an expanding treaty network preventing double taxation without enabling non-taxation, regularly reviewed, benefiting Nigeria long term)."},{"note":"Taxation is a key national development tool — wealth creation via meaningful employment lets citizens both meet their needs and contribute taxes, making them stakeholders directly contributing to national development, sustaining a growth-and-development cycle as those currently outside the tax net eventually earn taxable income."},{"h4":"Responsibilities of stakeholders"},{"p":"Federal and state Ministries of Finance hold primary responsibility for tax policy matters. Key stakeholder categories:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Stakeholder","Responsibilities"],"rows":[["**Government**","Implement/regularly review tax policies and laws; provide quarterly revenue information; ensure adequate funding and administrative/operational autonomy of tax authorities; and ensure a 3-6 month transition period before implementing a new tax."],["**The taxpayer**","The most critical stakeholder — considers tax responsibility a civic obligation. Entitled to: relevant information for discharging obligations; prompt, courteous, professional assistance; the right to object to decisions/assessments and receive timely response; fair, impartial appeal; and self-representation or representation by an accredited agent (if for financial reward)."],["**Revenue agencies**","Treat the taxpayer as a customer; ensure efficient implementation of policies/laws/treaties; facilitate inter-agency cooperation/information exchange; undertake timely audits/investigations; undertake taxpayer education; and establish a robust process to prevent/detect/punish corrupt officials."],["**Professional bodies, practitioners, consultants, agents**","Act per professional codes of conduct/ethics; never aid/abet evasion or corrupt practices; and actively promote effective compliance."],["**Media and advocacy groups**","Promote tax education/awareness; protect/advance taxpayer rights; advance accountability/transparency in tax revenue use; ensure accurate, balanced reporting; and ensure political aspirants clearly articulate their tax agenda."]]}},{"h4":"Administration mandates across the three tiers"},{"p":"Registration: all taxable persons registered with a nationwide Taxpayer Identification (Tax ID), leveraging CBN/BVN, NIMC, NCC, CAC, FRSC and NIS databases, harmonising currently uncoordinated registration. Compliance: self-assessment kept simple; tax amnesty frameworks to expand the net; taxpayer-service focus; constant education; periodic performance measurement/reporting; and recognition of compliant taxpayers. Efficiency: modern payment/collection technology; automated, globally-benchmarked record keeping; efficient inter-agency/international information exchange; enforcement of civil/criminal sanctions; adequate authority funding (including refund funding); convenient, cost-effective payment processes (improving the global ease-of-paying-taxes ranking); and reasonable revenue autonomy for tax authorities. Technology: deployment across all administration aspects, database integrity, and secure intelligence-gathering. Dispute resolution: amicable means (including arbitration) exhausted before resorting to judicial determination."},{"h4":"Implementation measures"},{"p":"The Federal Ministry of Finance leads implementation, seeking cooperation from: the **President and Governors** (consistent budget-speech fiscal policy statements; one revenue agency per government tier; sponsoring a tax court bill); the **Legislature** (establishing dedicated taxation committees; a Joint Revenue Board establishment Act with a broader mandate; reviewing/reducing the small-business tax rate to encourage MSME compliance); the **Ministry of Finance** itself (setting tax reform in motion; establishing a tax policy implementation committee; automating MDA collection/remittance; timely executive tax bills; a tax simplification office; a dedicated tax policy website; periodic NEC reporting; a top-50 global ease-of-paying-taxes target; and a minimum VAT registration threshold to protect micro-businesses); **MDAs** (periodic implementation reports to the Ministry of Finance); **tax authorities** (a uniform national tax day via the Joint Tax Board; taxation taught at all education levels; amnesty/whistleblowing frameworks; prompt response to the changing business environment); and **INEC** (mandating political parties to publicise their tax agenda before/during campaigns)."}]},{"n":"1.14","t":"Chapter summary","b":[{"p":"This chapter defined and explained the nature and objectives of taxation, and dealt extensively with the history of the Nigerian tax system and laws. It explained the direct/indirect classification of taxes and the tax-versus-levy distinction, and adequately discussed the issue of tax multiplicity. It also covered the objectives of the National Tax Policy, the policy guidelines future taxes must align with, and the responsibilities of key stakeholders for an orderly, sustainable Nigerian tax system."}]},{"n":"1.15","t":"Worked examples — study text questions and suggested solutions","b":[{"p":"Every chapter of the Taxation study text closes with a **Worked examples** section — its own questions at *x*.1 and its own suggested solutions at *x*.2. Chapter 1's set is reproduced in full below, with the book's solutions and additional explanation."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 1.15.1, Question 1","t":"The three aspects of the tax system in Nigeria","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Explain briefly the **THREE** aspects of the tax system in Nigeria."}],"a":[{"p":"The Nigerian tax system has three aspects — **tax policy, tax laws and tax administration** — and they must work together: a sound policy expressed in bad law or administered badly will not deliver its objectives."},{"h4":"(a)  Tax policy"},{"p":"Tax policy is the **general statement of intention which guides the actions of all parties** towards the realisation of tax objectives. In Nigeria a **low tax regime** has been introduced, aimed at reducing the individual tax burden and thereby encouraging savings and investment. There is also the introduction of the **self-assessment scheme**, to encourage taxpayers' participation in the assessment process. The policy supports the movement from the traditional **coercive** method of taxation to **voluntary compliance**."},{"h4":"(b)  Tax laws"},{"p":"Tax laws include the following notable tax legislation in Nigeria:"},{"ol":["**Nigeria Tax Act, 2025**;","**Nigeria Tax Administration Act, 2025**;","**Petroleum Industry Act, 2021** (as amended);","**Nigeria Revenue Service (Establishment) Act, 2025**; and","**Joint Revenue Board of Nigeria (Establishment) Act, 2025**."]},{"p":"The **Constitution vests the legislation of income tax** — whether personal or corporate — in the Federal Government, in order to promote uniformity. The three tiers of government nevertheless **share the administration** of the various taxes. Tax laws are reviewed periodically in line with changes in social, political and economic conditions. The power to impose tax is within the **exclusive legislative authority** of the Federal Government, and various machineries are set up to ensure strict compliance; non-compliance attracts penalties and fines."},{"h4":"(c)  Tax administration"},{"p":"Tax administration involves the **practical interpretation and application of the tax laws**. The authorities charged with administering tax in Nigeria are the Federal, State and Local governments, and their tax authorities derive their formation from:"},{"ol":["the **Nigeria Revenue Service (NRS)** — section 3(1) of the NTA;","the **State Internal Revenue Service (SIRS)** — section 87(1) of the NTAA; and","the **Local Government Revenue Committee** — section 93(1) of the NTAA."]},{"key":"A useful way to hold the three together: **policy decides what the country wants tax to achieve; law gives that intention legal force; administration makes it happen in practice.** Most practical difficulties in Nigerian taxation arise not from the policy or the law but from the third — which is why the 2025 reform legislation is so heavily concerned with administration."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 1.15.1, Question 2","t":"Seven sources of Nigerian tax laws and practices","open":true,"q":[{"p":"List any **SEVEN** sources of Nigerian tax laws and practices."}],"a":[{"ol":["**Practices of the Revenue Department** — the settled administrative practice of the tax authorities.","**The Constitution of the Federal Republic of Nigeria** — which allocates taxing powers between the tiers of government.","**Departmental and official circulars** issued by the revenue authorities.","**Opinions of income tax experts and authors**, in so far as the courts take judicial notice of them.","**Accepted recommendations of commissions of inquiry.**","**Court judgments** which decide income tax cases and establish income tax principles. These are binding and must be followed in similar cases until overruled.","**The various income tax laws** — the State and Federal laws, including Federal Government Acts and State Government laws."]},{"note":"Note the hierarchy implied by the list. The **Constitution** is supreme; **statute** comes next; **case law** interprets the statute and binds until overruled; **circulars and departmental practice** bind the Revenue but not the courts; and the **opinions of authors** carry weight only so far as a court chooses to rely on them. When two sources conflict, that order decides."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 1.15.1, Question 3","t":"Five principles of taxation","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Identify and explain any **FIVE** principles of taxation."}],"a":[{"p":"The principles of an **ideal tax system** include the following."},{"h4":"(a)  Simple, certain and convenient"},{"p":"The tax should be relatively **simple** for payers to understand their liability, and it should be administered in such a way that they are aware of the amount they should pay and the due date of payment. The method of payment should not be **inconvenient**."},{"h4":"(b)  Flexible"},{"p":"The structure and rates of tax should be capable of being **altered without too much difficulty**, to cope with changes in circumstances — particularly if the system of taxation is to be used as a means of regulating the economy, which is one of the stated aims of taxation."},{"h4":"(c)  Neutral"},{"p":"A tax is **neutral** if it does not distort economic choices. Taxes should not be made to impinge adversely on economic activity."},{"h4":"(d)  Administratively efficient"},{"p":"The **cost of administering the tax should not be too high in proportion to the revenue raised**. Efficiency includes not only the costs incurred by the revenue authority in collecting the tax but also the compliance costs borne by the taxpayer."},{"h4":"(e)  Equitable (fair)"},{"p":"Taxes must not only **be** fair, they must also be **seen** to be fair. Two types of equity are to be considered:"},{"ol":["**Horizontal equity** — this requires that people in **similar** situations are treated in a **similar** way; and","**Vertical equity** — this requires that people in **unequal** situations are treated with the necessary degree of **inequality**. This would require the rich to pay more than the poor, which advocates a **progressive** system of taxation."]},{"key":"The five are not always compatible, and recognising the tensions is what distinguishes a good answer. A **progressive** rate structure serves vertical equity but complicates the system and may blunt incentives, offending simplicity and neutrality. A **flat** tax is simple and neutral but weak on vertical equity. A tax that is highly **flexible** is by that fact less **certain**. Designing a tax system is largely the business of choosing which of these principles to sacrifice, and by how much."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 1.15.1, Question 4","t":"Purposes of taxation other than raising revenue","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Taxation is often used for purposes other than the raising of revenue. Enumerate some of these other purposes."}],"a":[{"p":"Apart from raising revenue, the other core objectives of a modern tax system include:"},{"ol":["**Redistribution of income and wealth.** The tax system is a means of ensuring the redistribution of income and wealth, in order to **reduce poverty and promote social welfare**.","**Economic regulation.** The tax system is a means of promoting economic welfare and creating a **sound infrastructure for businesses** — stimulating or restraining particular sectors, attracting investment, and managing demand.","**Harmonisation / social function.** Taxation can be used to redress the **rural-urban population drift**, to curb **environmental pollution**, and to provide for the free movement of goods and services, capital and people within a geographical area — for example, **ECOWAS**."]},{"note":"The mnemonic often used for these is the \"**four R**s\": **Revenue**, **Redistribution**, **Repricing** (taxing things society wants less of — tobacco, pollution, fossil fuels; see Chapter 11 on the Surcharge on Fossil Fuel Products) and **Representation** (the historical link between paying tax and holding government accountable)."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 1.15.1, Question 5","t":"Merits and demerits of direct and indirect taxes","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Explain the merits and demerits of direct and indirect taxes."}],"a":[{"h4":"Direct taxes"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Merits of direct taxes","Demerits of direct taxes"],"rows":[["It has a **lower cost of collection**.","It may **discourage hard work**, because additional income attracts additional tax."],["It is **more equitable**, as the taxpayer with the higher income bears the greater burden.","It can give room for **tax evasion**, especially if the tax rates are high."],["It is **easier to ascertain tax incidence** — the person who pays is the person who bears it.","It may cause **social unrest**, especially if rates are very high, since the taxpayers bear the whole burden visibly."]]}},{"h4":"Indirect taxes"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Merits of indirect taxes","Demerits of indirect taxes"],"rows":[["It is a **good source of revenue** to government.","The **cost of collection may be higher**."],["It can be **adjusted easily**.","Where there is **collusion between tax officials and taxpayers** to evade tax, there could be loss of revenue."],["It is a veritable fiscal tool to **check the pattern of consumption of undesirable goods**.","It may **discourage investment in local industries**, especially in the case of high export duties or excise duties."],["It can be used to **protect infant industries**.",""],["It is **more difficult to evade**, since it is collected at the point of transaction.",""]]}},{"key":"The defining difference is **incidence**. With a **direct** tax the person assessed is the person who bears the burden, and it cannot be passed on — which is why it can be made progressive and equitable, and why it is politically visible and resented. With an **indirect** tax the person who pays it over to the Revenue (the trader) is not the person who ultimately bears it (the consumer) — which is why it is hard to evade and easy to collect, but also why it tends to be **regressive**, falling more heavily on the poor as a share of income."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 1.15.1, Question 6","t":"Responsibilities of the key stakeholders under NTP 2017","open":true,"q":[{"p":"The responsibilities of the following key stakeholders are clearly identified in **NTP 2017**. **Required:** explain the responsibilities of (a) the government; (b) the taxpayers; (c) the revenue agencies."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  The government"},{"ol":["**Implement and regularly review** tax policies and laws;","**Provide information on all revenue collected** on a quarterly basis;","Ensure **adequate funding, and administrative and operational autonomy**, of tax authorities; and","Ensure a reasonable **transition period of between three and six months** before the implementation of a new tax."]},{"h4":"(b)  The taxpayer"},{"p":"The taxpayer is expected to, and is entitled to:"},{"ol":["**Provide relevant information** for the discharge of tax obligations;","**Receive prompt, courteous and professional assistance** in dealing with tax authorities;","**Raise objections** to decisions and assessments, and receive a response within a reasonable time;","A **fair and impartial appeal**; and","**Self-representation, or representation by any agent of choice** — provided that an agent acting for financial reward is an accredited tax practitioner."]},{"h4":"(c)  Revenue agencies"},{"ol":["**Treat the taxpayer as a customer**;","Ensure **efficient implementation** of tax policies, laws and international treaties;","**Facilitate inter-agency co-operation** and exchange of information;","Undertake **timely audits and investigations**;","Undertake **tax awareness and taxpayer education**; and","Establish a **robust process to prevent, detect and punish corrupt tax officials**."]},{"note":"Notice that the taxpayer's list is a mixture of **obligations** (provide information) and **rights** (prompt assistance, objection, fair appeal, representation). That is deliberate: NTP 2017 frames the relationship as reciprocal, which is the policy foundation of the shift from coercion to **voluntary compliance** described in Question 1."}]}}]}],"formulas":[],"focus":"This chapter is pure definitional/conceptual foundation — no computation, but heavy on precise distinctions the exam tests directly: tax vs. levy (levies fund/reward a specific service; tax does not); tax avoidance vs. tax evasion (legal exploitation of loopholes vs. illegal fraud/deceit — know the case law quotes, since ICAN exams have historically quoted them directly); and the seven principles of taxation as a fixed, nameable set. Given this is the FIFTH edition reflecting the brand-new NTA/NTAA 2025 reform, do not rely on older CITA/PITA-era knowledge — the specific Act names, section numbers, and even the personal income tax band table in this chapter reflect the new law effective the September 2026 diet, and should be treated as authoritative over any older material.","errors":["Treating \"tax\" and \"levy\" as synonyms — a levy (in the strict, narrower sense used alongside \"tax\") is paid in return for a specific service or as a penalty; tax is not tied to any specific benefit to the individual payer.","Assuming any tax-minimising behaviour is automatically illegal — tax avoidance (exploiting legal loopholes) is explicitly legal and court-supported, up to the point where transactions become pure form with no commercial substance; only tax evasion (fraud, deceit, false claims) is illegal.","Confusing proportional tax (same flat RATE regardless of income) with regressive tax (smaller-income payers bear a proportionally LARGER burden) — a flat-rate tax like VAT is proportional in rate but regressive in effect on lower incomes, and the chapter tests both framings separately (by method vs. by burden distribution).","Mixing up VAIDS (2017, 9-month window, 6 years of assessment, domestic assets/income) with VOARS (2018, 12-month window, 30 years of assessment, OFFSHORE assets specifically, with its own distinct 35% levy and 2% facility access fee) — these are two separate schemes with different scope, duration and mechanics.","Believing the Federal Government has exclusive administration (not just legislation) of income tax — legislation is exclusively federal, but ADMINISTRATION is explicitly shared across all three tiers of government."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Which of the following best distinguishes a levy from a tax?","o":["A levy is always larger in amount than a tax","A levy is paid in return for a specific service or as a penalty, while tax is not tied to any specific benefit to the payer","A levy is voluntary while tax is compulsory","A levy can only be imposed by local government","There is no genuine difference between a levy and a tax"],"a":1,"w":"A levy (e.g. a court fee, or a fine/penalty) is paid in return for a specific service or as punishment for an offence — tax, by contrast, is imposed without reference to any special benefit conferred on the individual taxpayer.","src":"Chapter 1.9","sec":"1.9"},{"q":"Which of the following is a feature of tax evasion but NOT tax avoidance?","o":["It is achieved by exploiting loopholes in tax law","It is supported by the courts in decided cases","It involves deliberate fraud and deceit","It results in the taxpayer paying the minimum tax possible without breaking the law","There is no Revenue Service investigation"],"a":2,"w":"Tax evasion is specifically defined as involving deliberate fraud and deceit (e.g. false claims, deliberate omission of income) — tax avoidance, by contrast, exploits legal loopholes without breaking any law.","src":"Chapter 1.10.2","sec":"1.10.2"},{"q":"Under the Voluntary Assets and Income Declaration Scheme (VAIDS), taxpayers were required to voluntarily disclose undisclosed assets and income relating to how many preceding years of assessment?","o":["3 years","5 years","6 years","10 years","30 years"],"a":2,"w":"VAIDS covered the preceding 6 years of assessment — the 30-year figure belongs to the separate VOARS scheme for offshore assets, a common point of confusion between the two schemes.","src":"Chapter 1.12","sec":"1.12"},{"q":"Which of the following is an example of a progressive tax?","o":["Value Added Tax","Companies Income Tax","Personal Income Tax (PAYE)","Stamp duties at a flat rate","Excise duty at a fixed amount per unit"],"a":2,"w":"Personal Income Tax under the PAYE system is the chapter's own example of a progressive tax — the more a taxpayer earns, the higher the rate applied, per the graduated tax table.","src":"Chapter 1.7","sec":"1.7"},{"q":"Which principle of taxation requires that taxpayers in similar situations be treated in a similar way?","o":["Certainty","Convenience","Horizontal equity","Vertical equity","Neutrality"],"a":2,"w":"Horizontal equity specifically requires equal treatment for people in similar situations — distinct from vertical equity, which requires the rich to pay more than the poor (unequal treatment for people in unequal situations).","src":"Chapter 1.6","sec":"1.6"}],"theory":[{"q":"Distinguish between tax avoidance and tax evasion, and explain why tax avoidance is generally legal.","marks":8,"a":[{"p":"**Tax avoidance** is a situation where the taxpayer arranges their financial affairs to pay the least possible tax, by exploiting loopholes in the tax laws after a critical review of those laws. It is generally legal, because it involves no breach of any tax law — as Lord President Clyde held in *Ayrshire Pullman Motor Services v C.I.R.*, no taxpayer is under any obligation to arrange their affairs so as to maximise the tax the Revenue can extract, and is entitled to be \"astute to prevent\" unnecessary depletion of their means by the Revenue."},{"p":"**Tax evasion** is a contravention of tax laws, whereby a taxable person neglects to pay tax due or reduces liability through fraudulent or untrue claims — e.g. understating income, overstating expenditure, making false claims for allowances, or omitting chargeable income from returns. It is illegal, unsupported by the courts, and can lead to criminal prosecution, fines, penalties and imprisonment."},{"p":"Where an avoidance scheme is stretched so far that transactions exist only in form and lack commercial substance, the Revenue Service may disallow the transaction — but exploiting a genuine loophole, without breaking any law, remains legal."}],"src":"Chapter 1.10.2","sec":"1.10.2"},{"q":"Explain the three aspects of the tax system in Nigeria.","marks":6,"a":[{"p":"**Tax policy** refers to the guidelines and principles government establishes for imposing and collecting taxes — e.g. shifting emphasis from income tax to consumption tax, reducing individual tax burden to encourage savings/investment, and introducing self-assessment to encourage taxpayer participation."},{"p":"**Tax laws** are the actual legislation — notably the Nigeria Tax Act 2025 and the Nigeria Tax Administration Act 2025, alongside the NRSA 2025, JRBA 2025, PIA 2021 (as amended) and NIIRA 2025. The Constitution vests income tax legislation exclusively with the Federal Government to promote uniformity."},{"p":"**Tax administration** is the practical interpretation and application of tax laws, carried out across the Federal (NRS), State (SIRS) and Local Government (Revenue Committee) tiers, which share administrative responsibility even though legislation itself remains federal."}],"src":"Chapter 1.3","sec":"1.3"}]}},{"n":2,"t":"Administration of Taxation","brief":"The institutional machinery behind Nigerian tax law: the three tax authorities and their enabling Acts, the full organ-by-organ governance structure (Joint Revenue Board, Nigeria Revenue Service and its Board, State Service and its Board, State Joint Revenue Committee, Local Government Revenue Committee — composition, powers, functions, tenure and cessation of membership for each), tax authority jurisdiction and the taxes each tier of government collects, the Tax Appeal Tribunal, and a set of specific NTAA administrative powers — tax planning disclosure, tax agent accreditation, prohibited tax avoidance arrangements, information exchange, the national single window portal, and more.","outcomes":["Explain the nature of tax administration","Explain the organs of tax administration: federal, states and local governments, their duties and functions","Explain tax jurisdictions","Explain the taxes imposed and collectible by the tiers of government","Explain the establishment, composition, powers, and procedures of the Tax Appeal Tribunal","Explain the other powers of the relevant tax authority, including that of the President, and further administrative matters"],"secs":[{"n":"2.2","t":"Nature of tax administration","b":[{"key":"Tax administration in Nigeria is vested in **three** broad tax authorities: the Nigeria Revenue Service (NRS); the State Internal Revenue Service (SIRS); and the Local Government Revenue Committee. The enabling law for each type of tax normally specifies which body administers it."},{"p":"The relevant enabling tax laws:"},{"ol":["the **Nigeria Tax Act (NTA) 2025** — imposes tax on the income of companies (other than corporation soles), individuals, and companies in petroleum operations (upstream);","the **Petroleum Industry Act (PIA) 2021** — imposes tax on the profits of companies in petroleum operations;","the **Nigeria Tax Administration Act (NTAA) 2025**;","the **Joint Revenue Board of Nigeria (Establishment) Act (JRBA) 2025**;","the **Nigeria Revenue Service (Establishment) Act (NRSA) 2025**; and","the **Nigerian Insurance Industry Reform Act (NIIRA) 2025**."]}]},{"n":"2.3","t":"Objective and application of the NTAA (s.1)","b":[{"p":"The Act ensures uniform procedures for consistent, efficient tax law administration, to: (a) facilitate taxpayer compliance; and (b) optimise tax revenue."},{"key":"The Act applies to **any person** required to comply with any tax law provision, whether personally or **on behalf of another person**."}]},{"n":"2.4","t":"Organs of tax administration","b":[{"h4":"2.4.1  Joint Revenue Board (JRB) — s.3 JRBA 2025"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Aspect","Detail"],"rows":[["**Composition**","The Executive Chairman of the NRS (as Chairman); the Chairman of each State/FCT Internal Revenue Service; and ex-officio members representing: the Minister of Finance; the Director-General of NIMC; the Chairman of the Revenue Mobilisation, Allocation and Fiscal Commission; the Comptroller-General of the Nigerian Immigration Service; the Corps Marshal of the FRSC; the Comptroller-General of the Nigeria Customs Service; and the NRS (each not below a specified minimum rank). Members serve part-time. The Board may co-opt up to 2 additional persons/bodies/agencies as needed."],["**Quorum**","One-third of members, including the Chairman."],["**Functions** (s.5 JRBA)","Integrate/maintain a nationwide Taxpayer ID database; resolve residency-determination disputes between tax authorities; advise government on (domestic and international) double taxation matters; resolve other inter-authority disputes; maintain a data collection/exchange platform among tax authorities; promote harmonisation and uniformity of taxes/levies/rates/charges; advise on new/changed taxes; provide periodic impact analysis and capacity-building recommendations; collaborate on surveys and publish tax indices/statistics; recommend/facilitate fiscal and tax policy reform; receive/collate/publish periodic tax revenue collected; receive/collate/publish tax expenditure from waivers/exemptions/incentives; support research on tax fraud/evasion and recommend interventions; guide tax agent accreditation; and carry out any other Act-assigned function."],["**Powers** (s.6 JRBA)","Provide general policy guidelines; manage/superintend its own policies; review/approve strategic plans; employ staff and set terms/conditions (including discipline); stipulate staff remuneration/allowances/pensions; and do other things necessary for efficient performance."],["**Executive Secretary** (s.10 JRBA)","The chief executive/accounting officer, responsible for executing Board policy. Requires relevant taxation/accountancy/economics/law qualification and **≥10 years** relevant experience. Holds office for a **4-year term, renewable once (max 8 years)**. Duties: issuing meeting notices, keeping proceedings records, conducting correspondence, and other Board-determined duties."],["**Cessation of membership** (s.8 JRBA)","No longer occupying the qualifying office; resignation by notice; death or incapacity (mind/body infirmity); bankruptcy or creditor compromise; or conviction for a felony or dishonesty/fraud offence."]]}},{"h4":"2.4.2  Nigeria Revenue Service Board (NRSB) — s.6-15 NRSA"},{"key":"The NRSB has **overall supervision** of the NRS — administration of tax on incorporated companies' profits is vested in the NRS and this Board together."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Aspect","Detail"],"rows":[["**Composition** (s.7 NRSA)","The Executive Chairman (appointed by the President, Senate-confirmed); plus ex-officio members representing: the Minister of Finance; the Minister of National Planning; the Attorney-General of the Federation; the Minister of Petroleum and Gas Resources; the CBN Governor; the Chairman of the Revenue Mobilisation, Allocation and Fiscal Commission; the Comptroller-General of Customs; the Registrar-General of the CAC; and Executive Directors appointed by the President. All but the Executive Chairman and Executive Directors serve part-time."],["**Powers/functions** (s.8 NRSA)","Provide general policy guidelines; evaluate policy implementation on assessment/collection/accounting; review/approve strategic plans; employ staff and set terms/discipline; stipulate remuneration/pensions; (with Ministerial approval) make rules/regulations with compliance requirements and non-compliance consequences; assign Executive Directors their directorates; and do other necessary things."],["**Secretary to the Board** (s.13 NRSA)","Appointed by the Board from within the Service — must be a lawyer, chartered accountant or chartered secretary, at least Assistant Director rank. Duties: issuing meeting notices, keeping proceedings records, and other Board/Chairman-directed duties."],["**Tenure** (s.9 NRSA)","The Executive Chairman and non-ex-officio members each hold office for a **4-year term, renewable once (max 8 years)**; other terms per the letter of appointment."],["**Cessation of membership** (s.11 NRSA)","Resignation (non-ex-officio, by notice to the President); death/incapacity; bankruptcy/creditor compromise; conviction for felony/dishonesty/fraud; presidential removal in the public/Service interest (non-ex-officio); Code of Conduct Bureau breach or gross misconduct; professional disqualification; presidential satisfaction that continuation is not in the public/Service interest; tenure expiry; or ceasing to hold the qualifying office."],["**Delegation** (s.12 NRSA)","The Board may exercise its powers itself, or via an authorised officer — and may **review** any authorised officer's decision, with the reviewed decision taking effect as the original. An approved committee's order/ruling/directive is not treated as the Board's own until **ratified** by the Board."],["**Technical Committee** (ss.14-15 NRSA)","Comprises: the Executive Chairman (Chairman); 2 Board members; the Executive Directors; the Director Legal; and the Director for Tax Policy — with the Board Secretary as its Secretary, and power to co-opt Service directors/officers as needed. Functions: considering technical/professional tax matters and recommending to the Board; advising the Board on its functions/powers; and attending to Board-referred matters."]]}},{"h4":"2.4.3  Nigeria Revenue Service (NRS) itself — ss.3-5 NRSA"},{"p":"Established by s.3(1) NRSA as a **body corporate** with perpetual succession and a common seal — may sue/be sued in its own name, and acquire/hold/dispose of property to carry out its functions."},{"key":"The Service's functions (s.4 NRSA) are extensive — among them: assess corporate/individual taxpayers; collect/recover/enforce/remit assessed tax; account for all government revenue; review tax regimes (with relevant Ministries, National Assembly approval) to grow economic activity; carry out compliance examinations/investigations; determine financial losses from tax fraud/evasion and revenue foregone from waivers; identify/trace/freeze/confiscate/seize proceeds of tax fraud/evasion; adopt compliance/regulatory/investigative measures; exchange information with national/international agencies; exchange personnel/experts for capacity building; monitor international tax dynamics for suspicious transactions; maintain up-to-date taxpayer data/access; maintain databases/statistics/records on tax administration matters; support research on tax fraud/evasion; review government tax policy and implement it; liaise with the AGF and law enforcement on tax offences; issue Taxpayer IDs (with State/Local/JRB collaboration); run public tax-compliance awareness campaigns; carry out administrative/oversight functions over federal taxes/levies (including query/subpoena/sanction/reward power); and provide/receive cross-border revenue-collection assistance under international agreements."},{"p":"With Ministerial approval, the Service makes rules/regulations giving full effect to the Act, including compliance requirements and non-compliance consequences — and carries out other necessary activities, including specifying return/claim/statement/notice forms, and deploying technology/digital platforms to automate tax administration processes."},{"h4":"2.4.4  Assistance of Nigeria Revenue Service in tax collection — section 5 of NRSA"},{"p":"On request from a State/Local Government, the NRS may assist collect/administer a tax that government has the statutory right to collect — covering proper assessment/collection/accounting/enforcement, PIT administration for a class of taxpayers, or collection/enforcement of a particular revenue claim. Revenue collected on another authority's behalf is remitted directly to them, as agreed. Under international double-taxation/administrative-assistance agreements, the Service carries out the obligations prescribed and may agree the performance manner with the foreign competent authority; the Accountant-General opens a designated account for revenue collected on another country's behalf pending remittance. The Service applies its usual enforcement/collection powers as if the revenue were its own (except regarding time limits), may take interim (including judicial) measures to preserve collection, and may charge an agreed fee to defray the assistance cost. A **\"revenue claim\"** covers tax, levy, fee, charge or statutory contribution due (including interest, penalties and collection/conservancy cost) to a Nigerian local/state government or agency, or a foreign government under a subsisting double-taxation/administrative-assistance agreement."},{"h4":"2.4.5  Miscellaneous provisions (including confidentiality)"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Provision","Detail"],"rows":[["**Institutional confidentiality** (s.32 NRSA)","All internal Service information/communications/documents/memoranda are confidential — unauthorised disclosure is an offence, liable on conviction to a fine of **up to N5,000,000**, or imprisonment for **up to 3 years**, or both."],["**Official secrecy** (s.33 NRSA)","Officials must treat taxpayer documents/information/returns/notices/assessments as secret/confidential — unauthorised communication is an offence carrying the same penalty (up to N5,000,000 fine, and/or up to 3 years' imprisonment) — except where authorised by the Service, ordered by a court, or required for the Act's purposes. Officials need not produce documents/information except as necessary for legal proceedings relating to Nigerian tax. The secrecy obligation does NOT prevent disclosure to an authorised foreign officer under a tax treaty/agreement/arrangement with Nigeria."],["**Endorsement of transactions** (s.34 NRSA)","The Executive Chairman or an authorised officer may endorse anything done, or required to be done, under the Service's powers or the Second Schedule laws (NTA, NTAA, etc.)."],["**Service of documents** (s.36 NRSA)","Notices/summonses/documents may be served on the Service by delivery to the Executive Chairman, registered post/courier/substituted service, a designated Service email address, or other electronic means the Service provides."],["**Legislations administered** (Second Schedule)","NTA 2025; NTAA 2025; laws imposing other government-entity-collected taxes/fees/levies (signature bonus, pipeline fees, gas-flaring penalties, depot levies/licences, OEL/OML/OPL fees, royalties, rents, drilling-rig/pipeline licence fees, haulage fees, and similar oil-industry fees); related regulations/proclamations/orders/notices; and any other National Assembly law for federal revenue assessment/collection/accounting."],["**Forms** (s.85 NTAA)","The relevant tax authority may specify/simplify forms, statements and notices for effective tax administration, from time to time."],["**Assessment lists** (s.44 NTAA)","The relevant tax authority maintains assessment lists of taxed persons, recording: names/addresses of those assessed; the name/address of anyone else chargeable in their name; total profits; tax payable; and other tax-authority-determined particulars. Complete filed assessment notices/amendments themselves constitute the assessment lists."]]}},{"h4":"2.4.6  Establishment and composition of the management board of the State Service Management Board (State Board) — s.89-92 NTAA"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Aspect","Detail"],"rows":[["**Composition** (s.89(2))","The Executive Chairman of the State Service (Chairman); directors from within the State Service; a director from the State Ministry of Finance; the Legal Adviser to the State Service (Secretary); and 3 other persons appointed by the State Governor on personal merit, one per Senatorial District, with relevant taxation experience/knowledge."],["**Quorum** (s.89(4))","5 members, including the Chairman or a director."],["**Functions** (s.91)","Provide general policy guidelines; manage/superintend revenue-assessment/collection/accounting policy; review/approve strategic plans; employ staff and set terms/discipline; stipulate staff remuneration (in consultation with the Governor); retain an approved portion of collected revenue to defray collection/administration cost; make recommendations to the Unified Revenue Commission on tax policy/reform/legislation/treaties/exemptions; and do other necessary things."],["**Delegation** (s.91(2), s.98)","The State Board may authorise any person (by gazette notice or in writing) to perform its functions/duties/powers, or receive notices/documents on its behalf — and may reverse/modify an officer's erroneous or unlawful decision, or one already reviewed under dispute resolution."],["**Technical Committee** (s.92)","Comprises the State Board's Executive Chairman (Chairman), State Service directors, and the Legal Adviser — may co-opt additional staff. Functions: considering technical/professional matters and recommending to the State Board; advising on the State Board's s.91 powers/duties; and attending to referred matters."]]}},{"h4":"2.4.7  State Internal Revenue Service (SIRS) — s.87-90 NTA/NTAA"},{"key":"Established for each state (s.87 NTA), **without prejudice** to the State Government's own power over individual income tax, PAYE and stamp duties — the State Service is **autonomous** in its day-to-day financial, technical, professional and administrative affairs."},{"p":"Functions (s.88 NTAA): assess individuals, estates, trusts, settlements, communities and families; assess/collect/account/enforce state-due taxes; collect/recover/remit tax under this or any other law; carry out compliance examinations/investigations (with law enforcement); and carry out other State House of Assembly-prescribed functions."},{"p":"The **Executive Chairman** (s.90 NTAA) is appointed by the State Governor, subject to State House of Assembly confirmation — the Chief Executive/Accounting Officer, responsible for policy execution and day-to-day administration, and must have taxation experience and membership of a recognised professional body."},{"h4":"2.4.8  State Joint Revenue Committee (SJRC) — s.95-96 NTAA"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Aspect","Detail"],"rows":[["**Composition**","The State Service's Executive Chairman (Chairman); the Chairmen of each Local Government Revenue Committee; a representative of the local-government-affairs agency; a Revenue Mobilisation Allocation and Fiscal Commission representative (observer); the State Sector Commander of the FRSC (observer); the State Service's Legal Adviser; and a Secretary (a State Service staff member)."],["**Functions**","Implement JRB decisions; advise the JRB and State/Local Governments on revenue matters; harmonise state tax administration; enlighten the public on State/Local Government revenue matters; and carry out other JRB-assigned functions."]]}},{"h4":"2.4.9  Local Government Revenue Committee (LGRC) — s.93-94 NTAA"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Aspect","Detail"],"rows":[["**Composition**","The Local Government Supervisor for Finance (Chairman); 3 local government councillors; and 2 other persons experienced in revenue matters, nominated by the Local Government Chairman on personal merit."],["**Functions**","Assess and collect all taxes/fines/rates under its jurisdiction, accounting for all sums collected as the local government prescribes. The Committee is **autonomous of the local government treasury**, responsible for its own day-to-day administration as its operational arm."]]}}]},{"n":"2.5","t":"Jurisdictions of the tax authorities (s.3 NTAA)","b":[{"p":"The **NRS** has:"},{"ol":["**exclusive** responsibility to administer taxes on: companies; persons employed in the Nigerian Army/Navy/Air Force/Police (other than civilian-capacity staff); Nigerian Foreign Service officers; non-resident persons deriving profit/income from Nigeria (or income from Nigerian employment by a non-resident); and specific NTA-listed items — development levy, non-resident taxpayers, specialised-trade companies, petroleum operations income tax, VAT, economic development tax incentive, and VAT exemptions;","**power** to administer: income tax; taxation of petroleum income; stamp duties; VAT; and tax incentives; and","any other NRSA/NTAA/National Assembly-conferred powers/functions."]},{"key":"Per s.3(2) NTAA, the **relevant State/FCT tax authority** administers taxes for resident individuals — imposition of tax on income/profits/gains, ascertainment of profits/income/assessable profits/total income/chargeable gains, and rates of tax — plus any other National-Assembly-conferred powers, **subject to** the NRS's own exclusive areas above."},{"p":"With relevant-government approval, a tax authority may authorise another tax authority to administer taxes within its jurisdiction, on agreed terms. For administering taxes generally, a tax authority may do what it deems necessary/expedient for assessment/collection, accounting for all sums collected per the NTAA, NTA and any other applicable law."}]},{"n":"2.6","t":"Taxes and levies collectible by each tier of government","b":[{"p":"Taxes collectible by the **Federal Government** (s.3(1) NTAA):"},{"ol":["companies income tax;","withholding tax on companies and non-resident individuals;","hydrocarbon tax;","value added tax;","stamp duties;","federal development levy;","economic development tax incentives; and","personal income tax on members of the armed forces, the Nigerian Police Force, and staff of the Ministry of External Affairs and non-resident individuals."]},{"key":"Once the **States Harmonised Taxes and Levies (Approved List for Collection) Bill 2025** is passed (the Taxes and Levies (Approved List for Collection) Act Cap. T2 LFN 2004 remains un-repealed/un-amended by ss.195-196 NTA 2025 in the meantime), projected state/local government collectible taxes/levies are:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Tier","Taxes/levies"],"rows":[["**States**","Income tax; stamp duties; property tax; road tax; haulage levy; economic development levy."],["**Local governments**","A harmonised levy (shops, slaughter slab fees, motor parks, domestic animal fees, bicycles, trucks, canoes, wheelbarrows/carts); liquor licences; TV/radio fees and other local levies; user charges (marriage/birth/death registration, street naming, signage/mobile advertisements, sewage/refuse disposal, burial ground permits, religious-place establishment fees, wharf landing charges); and daily ticketing (market levies, including transporters' tickets)."]]}}]},{"n":"2.7","t":"Tax Appeal Tribunal","b":[{"h4":"2.7.1  Establishment of the Tax Appeal Tribunal — section 23 of JRBA, 2025"},{"key":"Established under **s.23 JRBA 2025** to exercise jurisdiction/power over any tax dispute/controversy arising from the Act's administration, or any other National Assembly law. The Minister may, by Federal Gazette notice, specify the zones/matters/places over which the Tribunal has jurisdiction."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Aspect","Detail"],"rows":[["**2.7.2 Composition** — s.24 JRBA","5 members (\"Tax Appeal Commissioners\"), appointed by the Minister. Each zone has a Chairman — a legal practitioner qualified **≥10 years**, with cognate tax-legislation experience — who presides at every sitting (in their absence, members appoint a Chairman from among themselves). **Quorum: 3 members.**"],["**2.7.3 Qualification of a Tax Appeal Commissioner** — s.25 JRBA","A recognised-institution qualification with **≥10 years** cognate experience in law/accounting/business administration/finance/economics/taxation; OR a retired public servant with **≥10 years** tax administration experience; OR a member of the organised private sector (a registered Nigerian business association)."],["**2.7.4 Term of office** — s.26 JRBA","**3 years, renewable once (max 6 years)**."],["**2.7.5 Cessation of office** — s.27 JRBA","Reaching age 70; resignation on 3 months' notice to the Minister (or until a successor assumes office, whichever is earlier); incapacity (mind/body infirmity); conviction for felony/dishonesty/fraud; ministerial removal in the public/Tribunal interest; gross misconduct; or professional disqualification."],["**2.7.6 Remuneration and conditions of service** — s.31 JRBA","Determined by the Revenue Mobilisation Allocation and Fiscal Commission, prescribed in the letter of appointment — **cannot be varied to the Commissioner's disadvantage** after appointment."],["**2.7.7 Jurisdiction of the Tribunal** — s.29 JRBA","Adjudicates tax disputes/controversies arising from the NTA, NTAA, or any National/State Assembly tax law — applying the applicable provisions of those laws."],["**2.7.8 Powers and procedures of the Tribunal** — Second Schedule, para.13","May make its own procedural rules. Powers: summon/enforce witness attendance and examine on oath; require discovery/production of documents; receive affidavit evidence; call for witness/document examination; review its own decisions; dismiss an application for default or decide ex parte; set aside its own default/ex-parte orders; and do anything incidental/ancillary to its functions."]]}},{"note":"A typesetting defect in the study text, flagged rather than silently corrected: between sub-sections 2.7.7 and 2.7.8 the book prints a stray heading numbered **\"2.8\"** whose text is the orphaned fragment *\"7(a) above, as may be applicable in the determination or resolution of any…\"*. It is the tail of the jurisdiction provision at 2.7.7, broken across a page and mis-tagged as a new section — not a section of its own, and not the real §2.8 (Disclosure of tax planning), which follows after 2.7.8."}]},{"n":"2.8","t":"Disclosure of tax planning — section 30 of NTAA","b":[{"key":"A taxpayer entering (or intending to enter) a **\"disclosable transaction or agreement\"** — whose principal purpose is obtaining a tax advantage — must, **without being asked**, provide the relevant tax authority with information about it. The tax authority may make regulations on: the required information; its form/manner of delivery; the delivery timeframe; and administrative penalties for non-disclosure, false disclosure, incomplete or late disclosure."},{"p":"**\"Transaction\"** includes any course of action, agreement, scheme or arrangement. **\"Tax advantage\"** means: relief or increased relief from tax; repayment or increased repayment of tax; avoidance or reduction of a tax charge/assessment; avoidance of a possible assessment; deferral of payment (or advancement of repayment); or avoidance of an obligation to deduct/account for tax."}]},{"n":"2.9","t":"Accreditation of tax agents — section 33 of NTAA","b":[{"p":"A taxpayer may represent themselves, or be represented by a **tax authority-accredited** tax agent — accreditation requirements are set by the relevant tax authority. No return is validly filed unless filed by the taxpayer or an accredited agent, and returns must carry a declaration/attestation: from a taxpayer, that the information is true and complete; from an agent, that they applied appropriate technical competence and the highest ethical/professional standards."}]},{"n":"2.10","t":"Prohibited tax avoidance arrangement — section 47 of NTAA","b":[{"key":"The relevant tax authority may **counteract** a prohibited tax avoidance arrangement — via adjustment, disregarding, or re-characterisation (assessment, assessment modification, or claim amendment/disallowance) — unless the taxpayer proves the benefit would genuinely accord with the object/purpose of the relevant tax law."},{"p":"A **\"prohibited tax avoidance arrangement\"** is one where, on the facts, it is reasonable to conclude: its main purpose was obtaining a tax benefit; that benefit is contrary to the relevant law's object/purpose; it results (directly or indirectly) in misuse/abuse of the law's provisions; or it lacks commercial substance/coherence, in whole or part. \"Tax benefit/advantage\" includes relief/increased relief and avoidance of a possible assessment; \"arrangement\" includes any agreement, understanding, scheme, transaction or series of transactions — whether or not legally enforceable."}]},{"n":"2.11","t":"Exchange of information and joint audits — section 48 of NTAA","b":[{"p":"Tax authorities may exchange relevant information with each other to ensure compliance. Where one authority discovers non-compliance affecting another authority's tax during an audit, it refers the matter to (or invites) that other authority for a **joint audit**."}]},{"n":"2.12","t":"Establishment of national single window portal — section 83 of NTAA","b":[{"p":"The NRS may establish/operate a **National Single Window Portal** for revenue assurance, streamlined import/export processes, and international transit facilitation. It serves as a single entry point for anyone in import/export/trade/transit to: digitally lodge documents (licensing, processing, approval); pay fees/levies on imported/exported goods and other transactions; or provide relevant data/information. The Service may make regulations for its administration, including administrative charges."}]},{"n":"2.13","t":"Powers of a relevant tax authority to deploy technology to automate tax administration — section 71 of NTAA","b":[{"key":"A relevant tax authority may deploy technology to **automate** tax assessment, collection, accounting and information gathering — including third-party payment platforms to collect/remit tax on digital services supplied to any person in Nigeria, regardless of the supply's origin. This does **not**, however, empower a **State** tax authority to collect from a non-resident or on cross-border transactions."}]},{"n":"2.14","t":"Endorsement of transaction by the Chairman of a relevant tax authority — section 70 of NTAA","b":[{"p":"Anything done, or required to be done, by the relevant tax authority must be endorsed by its CEO or another authorised officer."}]},{"n":"2.15","t":"Power of the President to exempt companies from income tax — section 78 of NTAA","b":[{"p":"Subject to National Assembly approval, the President may exempt any company (or class of companies) — or any of its profits from any source — from income tax, on sufficient grounds, provided the exemption order is published in the Official Gazette."}]},{"n":"2.16","t":"Obligations during the pendency of legal proceedings — section 86 of NTAA","b":[{"p":"Except as ordered by a competent court/tribunal, a pending legal proceeding cannot affect a taxable person's tax duties/obligations."}]},{"n":"2.17","t":"Power of the relevant tax authority to prosecute any of the offences under the Act — section 139 of NTAA","b":[{"p":"The relevant tax authority may employ its own legal officers to prosecute offences under the Act, subject to the powers of the Attorney-General (Federal or State) in any Nigerian court — this power exists **notwithstanding** any administrative penalty/interest already imposed."}]},{"n":"2.18","t":"Place of an offence — section 140 of NTAA","b":[{"p":"An offence is deemed to occur where the taxable person is registered/resident, or at such other place as the relevant tax authority decides."}]},{"n":"2.19","t":"Chapter review","b":[{"p":"This chapter discusses the **guiding principles of the Nigerian tax system**, the **administration of tax by the three tiers of government**, and the **role of the Federal Ministry of Finance** in the development and implementation of the NTP."}]},{"n":"2.20","t":"Worked examples — study text questions and suggested solutions","b":[{"p":"The study text's own questions (§2.20.1) and suggested solutions (§2.20.2), reproduced in full. All three turn on the **composition and functions** of the bodies created by the 2025 reform legislation, which is exactly how this chapter is examined."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 2.20.1, Question 1","t":"The Nigeria Revenue Service Board and the Management Board of a State Service","open":true,"q":[{"p":"The administration of taxation in Nigeria is vested in various tax authorities depending on the type of tax under consideration. **Required:** explain the **(a)** composition and powers of the **Nigeria Revenue Service Board (NRSB)**; **(b)** composition and functions of the **Management Board of the State Service**."}],"a":[{"h4":"1(a)  Composition of the NRSB — section 7 of NRSA"},{"p":"The Nigeria Revenue Service Board comprises:"},{"ol":["the **Chairman**, who shall be the Executive Chairman of the Service, appointed by the President subject to confirmation by the Senate; and","the following **ex-officio members**:"]},{"ul":["a representative of the Minister responsible for **Finance**, not below the rank of a Director;","a representative of the Minister responsible for **National Planning**, not below the rank of a Director;","a representative of the **Attorney-General of the Federation**, not below the rank of a Director;","a representative of the Minister responsible for **Petroleum and Gas Resources**, not below the rank of a Director;","the **Governor of the Central Bank of Nigeria**, or a representative not below the rank of a Deputy Governor;","the **Chairman of the Revenue Mobilisation, Allocation and Fiscal Commission**, or a representative who shall be a Commissioner representing one of the States of the Federation;","the **Comptroller-General of the Nigeria Customs Service**, or a representative not below the rank of Deputy Comptroller-General;","the **Registrar-General of the Corporate Affairs Commission**, or a representative not below the rank of a Director; and","**Executive Directors** appointed by the President under this Act."]},{"note":"All members of the Board **except the Executive Chairman and the Executive Directors serve on a part-time basis.**"},{"h4":"1(a)  Powers of the NRSB — section 8 of NRSA"},{"p":"The Board shall:"},{"ol":["provide **general policy guidelines** relating to the functions of the Service;","**evaluate the implementation** of the policies of the Service on matters relating to the assessment, collection and accounting system under this Part or any other law;","**review and approve the strategic plans** of the Service;","**employ staff**, and determine the terms and conditions of service, including disciplinary measures, of the employees of the Service;","**stipulate remuneration**, allowances, benefits and pensions of the staff and employees;","with the approval of the Minister, **make rules and issue regulations** as, in its opinion, are necessary or expedient for giving effect to the provisions of this Part and for the administration of its provisions — such rules and regulations providing compliance requirements and possibly including consequences for non-compliance;","**assign each Executive Director a directorate** as appropriate; and","do such other things which, in its opinion, are necessary to ensure the **efficient performance of the functions** of the Service under this section."]},{"h4":"1(b)  Composition of the Management Board of a State Service — section 89(2) of NTAA"},{"ol":["the **Executive Chairman of the State Service**, who shall be the Chairman of the State Board;","**directors from within the State Service**;","a **director from the State Ministry of Finance**;","**three other persons** appointed by the State Governor on their personal merit, each representing a Senatorial District in the State, who shall possess relevant experience and knowledge in taxation and other related fields; and","the **Legal Adviser to the State Service**, who shall serve as Secretary to the Board."]},{"h4":"1(b)  Functions of the Management Board of a State Service — section 91 of NTAA"},{"ol":["provide **general policy guidelines** relating to the functions of the State Service;","**manage and superintend** policies of the State Service on matters relating to the administration of the revenue assessment, collection and accounting system under this Act or any other law;","**review and approve the strategic plans** of the State Service;","**employ and determine the terms and conditions of service**, including disciplinary measures, of the employees of the State Service;","**stipulate remuneration**, allowances, benefits and pensions of staff and employees, in consultation with the Governor;","provide an amount of revenue collected, as may be approved by the State House of Assembly, which shall be **retained by the State Service to defray the cost of collection and administration**;","make **recommendations**, where appropriate, to the Unified Revenue Commission on tax policy, reform, legislation, treaties and tax exemption as may be required from time to time; and","do such other things which in its opinion are necessary to ensure the efficient performance of the functions of the State Service under this Act."]},{"note":"The State Board may, **by notice in the Gazette or in writing, authorise a person to** (i) perform or exercise on its behalf any function, duty or power conferred on it; and (ii) receive any notice or other document to be given or delivered to it, or in consequence of this Act and any subsidiary legislation made under it."},{"key":"Compare the two lists side by side and the design of the system becomes clear: the **federal and state boards have almost identical powers** — policy guidelines, oversight of assessment and collection, strategic plans, staffing and remuneration. The two real differences are that the **State Board retains a share of collections to fund itself** (subject to the State House of Assembly), and that it **makes recommendations upward** to the Unified Revenue Commission rather than issuing regulations of its own. Federal uniformity of **law**, state autonomy of **administration** — which is exactly the constitutional division described in Chapter 1."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 2.20.1, Question 2","t":"The Joint Revenue Board — composition, functions and quorum","open":true,"q":[{"p":"The Joint Revenue Board was established by **section 3 of the Joint Revenue Board of Nigeria (Establishment) Act, 2025**. **Required:** **(a)** explain the composition and functions of the Joint Revenue Board; **(b)** state what constitutes a quorum at the Board's meeting."}],"a":[{"h4":"2(a)  Composition of the Joint Revenue Board"},{"p":"The Joint Revenue Board shall comprise:"},{"ol":["the **Chairman**, who shall be the Executive Chairman of the Nigeria Revenue Service; and","the **Chairman of each State Internal Revenue Service** and of the **Federal Capital Territory Internal Revenue Service**."]},{"p":"The following persons shall be **ex-officio members** of the Board:"},{"ul":["a representative of the **Minister of Finance**, not below the rank of a Director;","the **Director-General of the National Identity Management Commission**, or a representative not below the rank of a Director;","the **Chairman of the Revenue Mobilisation, Allocation and Fiscal Commission**, or its representative not below the rank of a Director;","the **Comptroller-General of the Nigerian Immigration Service**, or a representative not below the rank of Assistant Comptroller-General;","the **Corps Marshal of the Federal Road Safety Corps**, or its representative not below the rank of an Assistant Corps Marshal;","the **Comptroller-General of the Nigeria Customs Service**, or its representative not below the rank of an Assistant Comptroller-General; and","a representative of the **Nigeria Revenue Service**, not below the rank of a Director."]},{"note":"The Board can **co-opt any person, body or agency on a need basis, not exceeding two persons.**"},{"h4":"2(a)  Functions of the Joint Revenue Board — section 5 of JRBA"},{"p":"The Board shall:"},{"ol":["**integrate and maintain a database of Taxpayer Identification Numbers** for every taxable person in Nigeria, in collaboration with the Nigeria Revenue Service, State Internal Revenue Services, Local Government Revenue Committees and other relevant government agencies;","**resolve disputes between various tax authorities** on the determination of **residency**;","**advise government on double taxation** matters within Nigeria, and in respect of double taxation arrangements concluded or under consideration with any other country, including other taxation matters having effect throughout Nigeria;","**resolve disputes between different tax authorities**;","maintain a **platform for revenue data collection, integration and exchange of information** among the various tax authorities in Nigeria;","**promote harmonisation** of taxes, levies, rates, charges and other payments, and ensure **uniformity in revenue administration** across Nigeria;","**advise the Federal and State Governments** on the introduction of new taxes and changes to existing ones;","provide **periodic impact analysis** with recommendations on the tax framework and capacity building for all tiers of government;","**collaborate with relevant agencies** to carry out surveys, and ascertain and publish relevant tax indices and statistics;","**recommend, participate in, facilitate or initiate fiscal and tax policy reform** in collaboration with relevant bodies;","**receive, collate, analyse and publish periodic tax revenue** collected by all tax authorities;","**receive, collate, analyse and publish tax expenditure** on account of tax waivers, exemptions or incentives granted by each government;","**collaborate with tax authorities to undertake or support research** with a view to stimulating economic development, and determine the manifestation, extent, magnitude and effects of **tax fraud and evasion** or other matters affecting effective tax administration, and make recommendations to government on appropriate intervention and preventive measures;","provide **guidance for the accreditation of tax agents** by the relevant tax authorities in accordance with the relevant laws; and","carry out any other function as may be assigned to it by an **Act of the National Assembly**."]},{"h4":"2(b)  Quorum — paragraph 4 of the First Schedule to the JRBA 2025"},{"p":"The quorum of any meeting of the Board shall be **one-third of members, including the Chairman of the Board**."},{"key":"The Joint Revenue Board exists because Nigeria has **one tax law-making authority but many tax administrations**, and that creates problems no single authority can fix: which state may tax a person who lives in one state and works in another (residency disputes), how to stop the same income being taxed twice, and how to keep one national TIN database. Read its fifteen functions and they fall into three groups — **integration** (the TIN database, the data-exchange platform), **arbitration** (residency and inter-authority disputes, double taxation) and **harmonisation and advice** (uniformity, new taxes, research, publishing revenue and tax-expenditure figures). It co-ordinates; it does not itself collect."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 2.20.1, Question 3","t":"The Technical Committee of the Nigeria Revenue Service Board","open":true,"q":[{"p":"In order to assist the NRS in the performance of its duties, provision is made in **section 14 of the NRSA** for the setting up of a Committee of the Board to be known as the **\"Technical Committee\"**. **Required:** **(a)** explain the composition of the Technical Committee of the Nigeria Revenue Service Board; **(b)** state the functions of the Technical Committee."}],"a":[{"h4":"3(a)  Composition of the Technical Committee — section 14 of NRSA"},{"p":"The Committee comprises:"},{"ol":["the **Executive Chairman**, as the Chairman;","**two members of the Board** of the Service;","the **Executive Directors** in the Service;","the **Director Legal** of the Service, as appointed by the Board; and","the **Director in charge of Tax Policy** matters."]},{"note":"The **Secretary to the Board shall be the Secretary to the Technical Committee.** The Technical Committee may **co-opt, from the Service**, such director or officer as it may deem necessary for the effective performance of its functions under the Act."},{"h4":"3(b)  Functions of the Technical Committee — section 15 of NRSA"},{"p":"The functions of the Committee are to:"},{"ol":["**consider all tax matters that require professional and technical expertise**, and make recommendations to the Board;","**advise the Board** on any aspect of the functions and powers of the Service under this Act; and","**attend to such other matters** as may from time to time be referred to it by the Board."]},{"key":"Note what the Technical Committee is **not**: it has no decision-making power of its own. Every one of its three functions is advisory — it *considers* and *recommends*, *advises*, and *attends to matters referred to it*. Its purpose is to put **professional and technical expertise** at the disposal of a Board whose members are largely ex-officio representatives of other ministries and agencies, so that technical tax questions are not decided by non-specialists. Its membership reflects that: every member is a serving officer of the Service except the two Board members who provide the link back to the Board."}]}}]}],"formulas":[],"focus":"This chapter is almost entirely institutional/statutory structure — expect exam questions to ask for the exact composition, quorum, functions or tenure of a NAMED body (the JRB, NRSB, State Board, Tax Appeal Tribunal, etc., exactly as the worked examples in this chapter do). Do not try to memorise every single ex-officio member by rote; instead learn the STRUCTURE each body shares (a Chairman + ex-officio representatives + a defined quorum + a functions list + a powers list + tenure + cessation grounds) so you can reconstruct the right shape even if a specific name slips. Pay particular attention to what distinguishes similar-sounding bodies: the JRB coordinates ACROSS all tax authorities nationally, while the SJRC coordinates only within ONE state between the State Service and its Local Government Revenue Committees.","errors":["Confusing the Joint Revenue Board (JRB — national coordination across federal/state/FCT tax authorities) with the State Joint Revenue Committee (SJRC — coordination within a single state between the State Service and its own local governments) — they operate at different levels and have different compositions.","Assuming the NRS directly collects personal income tax for ordinary resident individuals in the states — PIT for most resident individuals is a STATE tax authority (SIRS) responsibility; the NRS has EXCLUSIVE PIT jurisdiction only for specific categories (armed forces, police, Foreign Service staff, non-residents).","Treating tax avoidance disclosure (s.30, informing the authority about a tax-advantage transaction) as the same thing as the prohibited-avoidance-arrangement rule (s.47, the authority's power to counteract an arrangement lacking commercial substance) — these are two distinct NTAA provisions with different triggers and different consequences.","Believing a State tax authority can use s.71's technology-deployment power to collect tax from a non-resident or on a cross-border transaction — the section explicitly carves this out as beyond a STATE authority's power, reserved to the NRS.","Mixing up the Tax Appeal Tribunal's 5-member composition/3-member quorum with the JRB's one-third-of-members quorum rule — each body's quorum rule is specific to it and not interchangeable."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Which body has overall supervision of the Nigeria Revenue Service, per s.6 of the NRSA 2025?","o":["The Joint Revenue Board","The Nigeria Revenue Service Board","The Tax Appeal Tribunal","The State Joint Revenue Committee","The Federal Ministry of Finance"],"a":1,"w":"The Nigeria Revenue Service Board (NRSB), established under s.6 NRSA, is specifically responsible for the overall supervision of the Service — distinct from the Joint Revenue Board, which coordinates across ALL tax authorities nationally rather than supervising the NRS alone.","src":"Chapter 2.4","sec":"2.4"},{"q":"What is the quorum for a meeting of the Tax Appeal Tribunal?","o":["One member","Two members","Three members","One-third of members","All five members"],"a":2,"w":"The Tribunal specifically requires 3 members for quorum — a fixed number, distinct from the JRB's \"one-third of members\" quorum rule, which is proportional rather than a fixed count.","src":"Chapter 2.7","sec":"2.7"},{"q":"Under s.3 of the NTAA, which of the following does the Nigeria Revenue Service have EXCLUSIVE responsibility to administer tax on?","o":["All resident individuals in every state","Non-resident persons who derive profit or income from Nigeria","Only companies engaged in agriculture","State government employees generally","Local government councillors"],"a":1,"w":"Non-resident persons deriving profit or income from Nigeria are explicitly within the NRS's exclusive jurisdiction — resident individuals generally, by contrast, fall under the relevant State tax authority.","src":"Chapter 2.5","sec":"2.5"},{"q":"A taxpayer enters into a transaction whose principal purpose is to obtain a tax advantage. Under s.30 of the NTAA, what is the taxpayer's obligation?","o":["No obligation exists unless the tax authority specifically requests information","The taxpayer must, without being asked, provide the relevant tax authority with information about the disclosable transaction","The taxpayer must cancel the transaction immediately","The taxpayer must report the transaction only if it exceeds N10,000,000","The taxpayer must report the transaction only to the Joint Revenue Board"],"a":1,"w":"Section 30 specifically requires proactive, unsolicited disclosure — the taxpayer must provide the information \"without notice or request,\" not merely respond if asked.","src":"Chapter 2.7","sec":"2.7"},{"q":"What is the maximum term (including renewal) a Tax Appeal Commissioner may serve?","o":["3 years, non-renewable","4 years, renewable once (8 years total)","3 years, renewable once (6 years total)","6 years, renewable once (12 years total)","No fixed term limit"],"a":2,"w":"A Tax Appeal Commissioner holds office for a 3-year term, renewable for one further 3-year term and no more — a maximum of 6 years, distinct from the Executive Secretary's and NRSB members' 4-year (max 8-year) terms elsewhere in this chapter.","src":"Chapter 2.7","sec":"2.7"}],"theory":[{"q":"State the composition and functions of the Joint Revenue Board (JRB).","marks":10,"a":[{"p":"**Composition**: the Executive Chairman of the NRS (Chairman); the Chairman of each State/FCT Internal Revenue Service; and ex-officio members representing the Minister of Finance, the NIMC Director-General, the Revenue Mobilisation Allocation and Fiscal Commission Chairman, the Comptroller-General of Immigration, the FRSC Corps Marshal, the Comptroller-General of Customs, and the NRS."},{"ol":["Integrate/maintain a nationwide Taxpayer ID database.","Resolve residency-determination and other disputes between tax authorities.","Advise government on double taxation matters, domestic and international.","Maintain a data collection/exchange platform among tax authorities.","Promote harmonisation and uniformity of taxes, levies, rates and charges.","Advise on new/changed taxes.","Provide periodic impact analysis and capacity-building recommendations.","Publish tax revenue collected and tax expenditure from waivers/incentives.","Support research on tax fraud/evasion and recommend interventions.","Guide tax agent accreditation."]}],"src":"Chapter 2.4","sec":"2.4"},{"q":"Distinguish between the exclusive tax administration responsibility of the Nigeria Revenue Service and that of a State tax authority, under s.3 of the NTAA.","marks":6,"a":[{"p":"The **NRS** has exclusive responsibility for taxes on: companies; members of the armed forces/police (other than civilian capacity); Foreign Service officers; non-resident persons deriving profit/income from Nigeria; and specific NTA items (development levy, specialised-trade companies, petroleum income tax, VAT, economic development tax incentives, VAT exemptions). It also has power to administer income tax, petroleum income tax, stamp duties, VAT and tax incentives generally."},{"p":"The **relevant State/FCT tax authority** administers taxes for resident individuals — imposing tax on income/profits/gains, ascertaining profits/income/assessable profits/total income/chargeable gains, and setting rates — subject to the NRS's own exclusive areas."}],"src":"Chapter 2.5","sec":"2.5"}]}},{"n":3,"t":"Basic Ethical Issues in Taxation","brief":"The IESBA five fundamental principles (integrity, objectivity, professional competence and due care, confidentiality, professional behaviour) as they apply specifically to tax practice, the conditions under which a tax practitioner may disclose client information, what to do when facing a conflict of interest, and a brief look at the 2025 IESBA Handbook updates.","outcomes":["Explain the fundamental principles of ethics — integrity, objectivity, professional competence and due care, confidentiality, and professional behaviour — as specified by the International Ethics Standards Board for Accountants (IESBA)","Explain the conditions when information on taxpayers may be disclosed","Explain what a tax practitioner should do when there is a conflict of interest"],"secs":[{"n":"3.1","t":"The five fundamental principles of ethics (IESBA)","b":[{"h4":"3.1.1  Integrity (IESBA §111)"},{"def":{"t":"Integrity","d":"requires an accountant to be straightforward and honest in all professional and business relationships — implying fair dealing and truthfulness."}},{"key":"A professional accountant must **never knowingly** be associated with reports, returns, communications or other information that: (a) contains a materially false or misleading statement; (b) contains recklessly provided statements/information; or (c) omits or obscures required information where doing so would mislead."},{"note":"Providing a **modified** report on such information does not itself breach the integrity principle. If an accountant later becomes aware of having been associated with such information, they must take steps to become **disassociated** from it."},{"h4":"3.1.2  Objectivity (IESBA §112)"},{"def":{"t":"Objectivity","d":"requires an accountant not to compromise professional or business judgment because of bias, conflict of interest, or the undue influence of others."}},{"p":"An accountant must not undertake a professional activity if a circumstance or relationship **unduly influences** their professional judgment regarding that activity."},{"h4":"3.1.3  Professional competence and due care (IESBA §113)"},{"p":"Requires an accountant to: (a) attain and maintain professional knowledge/skill at the level needed for a client/employer to receive competent service, based on current technical/professional standards and relevant legislation; and (b) act **diligently** and in accordance with applicable technical/professional standards."},{"key":"Serving clients competently requires **sound judgment** in applying professional knowledge/skill. Maintaining competence requires continuing awareness/understanding of technical, professional and business developments — **continuing professional development** is what enables this. **Diligence** encompasses acting per an assignment's requirements, carefully, thoroughly and on a timely basis."},{"p":"In complying with this principle, an accountant must take reasonable steps to ensure those working under their authority have **appropriate training and supervision** — and, where appropriate, make clients/employers/other users aware of the **limitations** inherent in the services/activities provided."},{"h4":"3.1.4  Confidentiality (IESBA §114)"},{"def":{"t":"Confidentiality","d":"requires an accountant to respect the confidentiality of information acquired through professional and business relationships."}},{"p":"An accountant shall:"},{"ol":["be alert to the possibility of inadvertent disclosure — including in a social environment, and particularly to a close business associate or immediate/close family member;","maintain confidentiality of information within the firm/employing organisation;","maintain confidentiality of information disclosed by a prospective client/employer;","not disclose confidential information outside the firm/employer without proper, specific authority, unless there is a legal or professional duty/right to disclose;","not use confidential information for personal advantage, or a third party's advantage;","not use or disclose confidential information **after the relationship has ended**; and","take reasonable steps to ensure personnel under their control, and any advisers consulted, respect the confidentiality duty."]},{"key":"Confidentiality serves the **public interest** — it facilitates the free flow of information from a client/employer to the accountant, in the knowledge it will not be passed to a third party."},{"p":"Circumstances where disclosure is, or might be, required/appropriate:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Category","Examples"],"rows":[["**Required by law**","Producing documents or evidence in legal proceedings; disclosing infringements of the law that come to light to appropriate public authorities."],["**Permitted by law and authorised**","By the client or employing organisation."],["**Professional duty/right (where not prohibited by law)**","To comply with a professional body's quality review; to respond to an inquiry/investigation by a professional/regulatory body; to protect the accountant's own professional interests in legal proceedings; or to comply with technical/professional/ethics standards."]]}},{"p":"Factors to weigh before disclosing: whether any party's interests (including third parties') could be harmed if the client/employer consents to disclosure; whether all relevant information is known and substantiated (unsubstantiated facts, incomplete information, unsubstantiated conclusions all matter here); the proposed type of communication and its addressee; and whether the addressees are genuinely appropriate recipients."},{"warn":"Confidentiality obligations **continue even after** the accountant-client/employer relationship ends. When changing employment or gaining a new client, an accountant may use **prior experience**, but must **never** use or disclose confidential information acquired from a prior professional/business relationship."},{"h4":"3.1.5  Professional behaviour (IESBA §115)"},{"p":"Requires an accountant to comply with relevant laws/regulations, and avoid any conduct they know (or should know) might **discredit the profession**. An accountant must never knowingly engage in a business, occupation or activity that impairs (or might impair) the profession's integrity, objectivity or good reputation, incompatibly with the fundamental principles."},{"key":"\"Conduct that might discredit the profession\" is conduct a **reasonable and informed third party** would likely conclude adversely affects the profession's good reputation."},{"p":"In marketing/promotional activities, an accountant must not bring the profession into disrepute — must be honest and truthful, and must not make: exaggerated claims about services/qualifications/experience; or disparaging references/unsubstantiated comparisons about others' work. If in doubt about a form of advertising/marketing, the accountant is encouraged to consult the relevant professional body."}]},{"n":"3.2","t":"Conditions when information on taxpayers may be disclosed","b":[{"key":"Confidentiality continues even after a tax practitioner's engagement with a client ends. On ending an engagement, or taking on a new client, a tax practitioner may use prior experience — but must **never** use or disclose confidential information from the previous client, since doing so could amount to professional misconduct with potentially costly consequences."},{"p":"A tax consultant may nonetheless disclose information on a taxpayer (past or present) where:"},{"ol":["disclosure is **permitted by law**;","disclosure is **authorised by the client** (the taxpayer);","disclosure is **required by law** — e.g. producing documents/evidence in legal proceedings, or disclosing infringements of the law that come to light to appropriate public authorities; and","there is a **professional duty or right** to disclose, when not prohibited by law — e.g. responding to an inquiry/investigation by a member/regulatory body (ICAN, CITN, etc.); protecting the professional interests of a professional body in legal proceedings; or complying with technical standards/ethics requirements."]}]},{"n":"3.3","t":"Conflict of interest","b":[{"key":"A conflict of interest creates a threat to **objectivity**, and may threaten the other fundamental principles too. It may arise where: (a) the tax practitioner undertakes a professional activity on a matter for two or more clients whose interests on that matter conflict; or (b) the practitioner's own interests on a matter conflict with those of a client they act for on that same matter."},{"p":"A tax practitioner may need to resolve such a conflict while still complying with the fundamental principles. It may be in the practitioner's best interest to **document** the substance of the issue, discussions held, and decisions made."},{"p":"If a significant conflict cannot be resolved, the practitioner may seek advice from the relevant professional body (ICAN, CITN, etc.) — generally obtainable without breaching confidentiality, if discussed **anonymously** with the body, or with a legal adviser under legal privilege."},{"warn":"If, after exhausting all relevant possibilities, the ethical conflict **remains unresolved**, the tax practitioner should, where possible, **refuse to remain associated** with the matter creating the conflict — determining whether it is appropriate to withdraw from the specific assignment, or from the client relationship altogether."}]},{"n":"3.4","t":"Key updates based on the 2025 IESBA Handbook","b":[{"note":"This chapter covers only the five fundamental principles — the newer pronouncements below are covered more fully in the Advanced Taxation, Strategic Business Reporting, and Advanced Audit, Assurance and Forensics study texts."},{"p":"The 2025 IESBA Handbook incorporates:"},{"ol":["ethical behaviour requirements for **tax planning services/activities**, beginning after 30 June 2025;","the **International Ethics Standard for Sustainability Assurance (IESSA)**, effective December 2026;","revisions on **using the work of an external expert**, effective December 2026; and","fee-related provisions safeguarding auditor independence — e.g. the influence of non-audit services on audit fees, and mandatory disclosure where audit fees from public-interest entities exceed **15%** of a firm's total fees."]}]},{"n":"3.5","t":"Chapter review","b":[{"p":"This chapter discussed the five fundamental principles of ethics — integrity, objectivity, professional competence and due care, confidentiality, and professional behaviour — as specified by IESBA. It also covered the conditions under which information on taxpayers may be disclosed, and what a tax practitioner should do when facing a conflict of interest."}]},{"n":"3.6","t":"Worked examples — study text questions and suggested solutions","b":[{"p":"Chapter 3 has the fullest question set in the Taxation study text: **multiple-choice (§3.6.1), short-answer (§3.6.2) and examination-type questions (§3.6.3)**, each with its own suggested solutions at §3.6.4 to §3.6.6. All are reproduced below, with the book's answers and additional explanation. The paragraph references are to the **IESBA Code of Ethics**, which is the authority throughout."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 3.6.1 and 3.6.2","t":"Multiple-choice and short-answer questions, with the official answers","open":true,"q":[{"h4":"3.6.1  Multiple-choice questions"},{"ol":["Which of the following is **NOT** a fundamental principle of ethics as specified by the International Ethics Standards Board for Accountants (IESBA)? (A) Integrity; (B) Objectivity; (C) Literacy; (D) Professional competence and due care; (E) Confidentiality.","The following are circumstances where a tax consultant may disclose information on a taxpayer **EXCEPT** (A) Disclosure is permitted by law; (B) Disclosure is authorised by the client, that is, the taxpayer; (C) Disclosure to the appropriate public authorities of infringements of the law that come to light; (D) There is a professional duty or right to disclose, when not prohibited by law; (E) Disclosure is required by a pressure group.","When undertaking promotional activities, a professional accountant shall **not** (A) Hold too many meetings with clients; (B) Charge professional fees; (C) Make disparaging references or unsubstantiated comparisons to the work of others; (D) Send audit queries to a client; (E) Consult with the relevant professional body on any issue.","Which one of the following reports, returns, communications or other information **should** a professional accountant be associated with? (A) Reports containing a materially false statement; (B) Reports containing a misleading statement; (C) Reports containing statements or information provided recklessly; (D) Reports omitting required information where such omission would be misleading; (E) Reports containing vital information for the users of the accounts.","Given the principle of confidentiality, all professional accountants are expected to adhere strictly to the following **EXCEPT** (A) Be alert to the possibility of inadvertent disclosure, including in a social environment, and particularly to a close business associate or an immediate or close family member; (B) Maintain confidentiality of information within the firm or employing organisation; (C) Maintain confidentiality of information disclosed by a prospective client or employing organisation; (D) Disclose confidential information acquired as a result of professional and business relationships outside the firm or employing organisation without proper and specific authority; (E) Not use confidential information acquired as a result of professional and business relationships for the personal advantage of the accountant or for the advantage of a third party."]},{"h4":"3.6.2  Short answer questions"},{"ol":["The principle of ……… requires an accountant not to compromise professional or business judgement because of bias, conflict of interest or undue influence of others.","Which principle requires a professional accountant to act diligently and in accordance with applicable technical and professional standards?","Where appropriate, a professional accountant shall make clients, the employing organisation, or other users of the accountant's professional services or activities, aware of the ……… inherent in the services or activities.","A professional accountant should not use ……… information acquired as a result of professional and business relationships for the personal advantage of the accountant or for the advantage of a third party.","A tax consultant may disclose information on a ……… when disclosure is permitted by law."]}],"a":[{"h4":"3.6.4  Suggested solutions to multiple-choice questions"},{"ol":["**C — Literacy.** The five fundamental principles are **integrity, objectivity, professional competence and due care, confidentiality, and professional behaviour**. Literacy is not among them.","**E — disclosure is required by a pressure group.** A pressure group has no legal or professional standing whatever, and disclosure to one would be a plain breach of confidentiality. The other four are the recognised grounds (see examination-type question 3 below).","**C — make disparaging references or unsubstantiated comparisons to the work of others** (IESBA paragraph 115.2). The accountant must be honest and truthful in marketing and must not bring the profession into disrepute.","**E — reports containing vital information for the users of the accounts.** The question asks which the accountant **should** be associated with, and (A) to (D) are precisely the four categories paragraph R111.2 forbids.","**D — disclose confidential information … without proper and specific authority.** This is the one thing the principle **prohibits**; the other four are things it requires."]},{"h4":"3.6.5  Suggested solutions to short answer questions"},{"ol":["**Objectivity.**","**Professional competence and due care.**","**Limitations** (IESBA paragraph 113.3).","**Confidential.**","**Client** (the taxpayer)."]}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 3.6.3, Question 1","t":"The fundamental principles of integrity and objectivity","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Explain the following fundamental principles of ethics as specified by IESBA: **(a)** Integrity; **(b)** Objectivity."}],"a":[{"h4":"1(a)  Integrity"},{"p":"A professional accountant shall comply with the principle of **integrity**, which requires an accountant to be **straightforward and honest** in all professional and business relationships *(Paragraph 111.1)*. Integrity implies **fair dealing and truthfulness** *(Paragraph 111.1 A1)*."},{"p":"A professional accountant shall **not knowingly be associated** with reports, returns, communications or other information where the accountant believes that the information *(Paragraph R111.2)*:"},{"ol":["contains a **materially false or misleading statement**;","contains statements or information provided **recklessly**; or","**omits or obscures required information** where such omission or obscurity would be misleading."]},{"p":"If a professional accountant provides a **modified report** in respect of such a report, return, communication or other information, the accountant is **not in breach** of paragraph R111.2 *(Paragraph 111.2 A1)*. When a professional accountant becomes aware of having been associated with information described in paragraph R111.2, the accountant shall **take steps to be disassociated** from that information *(Paragraph R111.3)*."},{"h4":"1(b)  Objectivity"},{"p":"A professional accountant shall comply with the principle of **objectivity**, which requires an accountant **not to compromise professional or business judgement because of bias, conflict of interest or the undue influence of others** *(Paragraph 112.1)*."},{"p":"A professional accountant shall **not undertake a professional activity** if a circumstance or relationship unduly influences the accountant's professional judgement regarding that activity *(Paragraph R112.2)*."},{"key":"The two principles are related but distinct, and the examiner tests whether you can separate them. **Integrity** is about **honesty** — not lying, and not lending your name to someone else's lie. **Objectivity** is about **independence of judgement** — not allowing bias, conflict or pressure to bend your conclusion, even when you are being perfectly honest about it. An accountant can be entirely honest and still lack objectivity."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 3.6.3, Question 2","t":"The obligations imposed by IESBA paragraph 114.1 on confidentiality","open":true,"q":[{"p":"According to **paragraph 114.1 of IESBA**, the principle of confidentiality imposes an obligation on all professional accountants and expects them to refrain from taking certain action with respect to the handling of confidential information or documents of their clients. **Required:** briefly describe the obligations imposed on professional accountants by paragraph 114.1 of IESBA."}],"a":[{"p":"Paragraph 114.1 imposes the following obligations on all professional accountants. They should:"},{"ol":["be **alert to the possibility of inadvertent disclosure**, including in a social environment, and particularly to a close business associate or an immediate or a close family member;","maintain confidentiality of information **within the firm** or employing organisation;","maintain confidentiality of information disclosed by a **prospective** client or employing organisation;","**not disclose** confidential information acquired as a result of professional and business relationships outside the firm or employing organisation **without proper and specific authority**, unless there is a legal or professional duty or right to disclose;","**not use** confidential information acquired as a result of professional and business relationships for the **personal advantage of the accountant or for the advantage of a third party**;","**not use or disclose** any confidential information, either acquired or received as a result of a professional or business relationship, **after that relationship has ended**; and","take reasonable steps to ensure that **personnel under the accountant's control**, and individuals from whom advice and assistance are obtained, **respect the accountant's duty of confidentiality**."]},{"note":"Three of the seven catch people out. Confidentiality extends to a **prospective** client who in the end never engages you; it survives **after the engagement ends**, indefinitely; and it binds you for the conduct of your **staff and your own advisers**, not only for your own."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 3.6.3, Question 3","t":"When a tax consultant may disclose information on a taxpayer","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Briefly describe the circumstances when a tax consultant may disclose information on a taxpayer."}],"a":[{"p":"The following are the instances when a tax practitioner may disclose information on a taxpayer:"},{"ol":["disclosure is **permitted by law**;","disclosure is **authorised by the client**, that is, the taxpayer;","disclosure is **required by law** — for example: (i) production of documents or other provision of evidence in the course of **legal proceedings**; or (ii) disclosure to the appropriate **public authorities of infringements of the law** that come to light; and","there is a **professional duty or right to disclose**, when not prohibited by law — for example: (i) to respond to an **inquiry or investigation by a member body or regulatory body** such as ICAN or CITN; (ii) to **protect the professional interests** of a professional body such as ICAN or CITN in legal proceedings; or (iii) to **comply with technical standards and ethics requirements**."]},{"key":"Group them into three and they are easy to remember: disclosure is proper where the **client consents**, where the **law compels or permits** it, or where a **professional duty or right** arises. Everything outside those three — including pressure from an interested outsider, however well intentioned — is a breach."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 3.6.3, Question 4","t":"What a tax practitioner should do when faced with a conflict of interest","open":true,"q":[{"p":"What should a tax practitioner do when faced with a conflict of interest?"}],"a":[{"p":"A tax practitioner may be faced with a conflict of interest when undertaking a professional activity. A conflict of interest **creates a threat to objectivity** and may create threats to the other fundamental principles. Such threats may be created when:"},{"ol":["the tax practitioner undertakes a professional activity relating to a **particular matter for two or more clients whose interests with respect to that matter are in conflict**; or","the **interests of the tax practitioner** with respect to a particular matter and the interests of a client for whom the practitioner undertakes a professional activity relating to that matter **are in conflict**."]},{"h4":"What the practitioner should do"},{"steps":["**Resolve the conflict in compliance with the fundamental principles** — identify the threat, evaluate its significance, and apply safeguards.","**Document** the substance of the issue, the details of any discussions held, and the decisions made concerning that issue. It may be in the practitioner's own best interests to do so.","If a **significant conflict cannot be resolved**, consider **obtaining professional advice from the relevant professional body** — ICAN and/or CITN. Guidance on ethical issues can generally be obtained **without breaching confidentiality**, provided the matter is discussed with the professional body on an **anonymous basis**, or with a **legal adviser under the protection of legal privilege**.","If, **after exhausting all relevant possibilities**, the ethical conflict remains unresolved, the practitioner shall where possible **refuse to remain associated with the matter** creating the conflict, and shall determine whether in the circumstances it is appropriate to **withdraw from the specific assignment or from the client altogether**."]},{"note":"Note the sequence the Code lays down — **resolve, document, seek advice, and only then withdraw**. Withdrawal is the last resort, not the first response. Note also the device that makes seeking advice possible without breaching confidentiality: **anonymity**, or **legal privilege**."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 3.6.3, Question 5","t":"Professional behaviour, and professional competence and due care","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Write short notes on the following fundamental principles of ethics as specified by IESBA: **(a)** Professional behaviour; **(b)** Professional competence and due care."}],"a":[{"h4":"5(a)  Professional behaviour"},{"p":"A professional accountant shall comply with the principle of **professional behaviour**, which requires an accountant to **comply with relevant laws and regulations** and **avoid any conduct that the accountant knows or should know might discredit the profession**. A professional accountant shall not knowingly engage in any business, occupation or activity that impairs or might impair the integrity, objectivity or good reputation of the profession, and as a result would be incompatible with the fundamental principles *(Paragraph R115.1)*."},{"p":"Conduct that might discredit the profession includes conduct that **a reasonable and informed third party would likely conclude adversely affects the good reputation of the profession** *(Paragraph 115.1 A1)*."},{"p":"When undertaking **marketing or promotional activities**, a professional accountant shall not bring the profession into disrepute. The accountant shall be honest and truthful and shall not make *(Paragraph R115.2)*:"},{"ol":["**exaggerated claims** for the services offered by, or the qualifications or experience of, the accountant; or","**disparaging references or unsubstantiated comparisons** to the work of others."]},{"note":"If a professional accountant is **in doubt** about whether a form of advertising or marketing is appropriate, the accountant is **encouraged to consult with the relevant professional accountancy body** *(Paragraph 115.2 A1)*."},{"h4":"5(b)  Professional competence and due care"},{"p":"A professional accountant shall comply with the principle of **professional competence and due care**, which requires an accountant to *(Paragraph R113.1)*:"},{"ol":["**attain and maintain professional knowledge and skill** at the level required to ensure that a client or employing organisation receives **competent professional service**, based on current technical and professional standards and relevant legislation; and","**act diligently** and in accordance with applicable technical and professional standards."]},{"p":"Serving clients and employing organisations with professional competence requires the **exercise of sound judgement** in applying professional knowledge and skill when undertaking professional activities *(Paragraph 113.1 A1)*. Maintaining professional competence requires a **continuing awareness and understanding of relevant technical, professional and business developments**; **continuing professional development** enables a professional accountant to develop and maintain the capabilities to perform competently within the professional environment *(Paragraph 113.1 A2)*."},{"p":"**Diligence** encompasses the responsibility to act in accordance with the requirements of an assignment — **carefully, thoroughly and on a timely basis** *(Paragraph 113.1 A3)*."},{"p":"In complying with this principle, a professional accountant shall take reasonable steps to ensure that those working in a professional capacity **under the accountant's authority have appropriate training and supervision** *(Paragraph R113.2)*. Where appropriate, the accountant shall make clients, the employing organisation, or other users of the accountant's professional services or activities, **aware of the limitations** inherent in the services or activities *(Paragraph R113.3)*."},{"key":"The principle has **two limbs that are often confused**. **Competence** is about *capability* — having and keeping the knowledge and skill, which is why CPD is mandatory. **Due care** is about *application* — using that capability carefully, thoroughly and on time on the particular engagement. A highly competent accountant who rushes a job breaches the second limb while satisfying the first."}]}}]}],"formulas":[],"focus":"This chapter mirrors the general IESBA ethics framework already covered in the Auditing subject (Chapter 3), applied specifically to tax practice — the disclosure conditions and conflict-of-interest guidance are the genuinely tax-specific additions worth focusing on. Know the five principles by name and be able to give a one-line definition of each — MCQs here often test recognising which principle a described scenario violates, or picking the genuine exception in a list of disclosure circumstances (a \"pressure group\" demanding disclosure is never itself a valid ground).","errors":["Treating confidentiality as ending once the client relationship ends — the principle is explicit that the obligation continues even after the relationship has ended, though the practitioner may still use general prior experience gained from it.","Confusing integrity (honesty/straightforwardness, never being associated with false/misleading information) with objectivity (freedom from bias/undue influence in professional judgment) — they are adjacent but distinct principles, each with its own specific prohibited behaviours.","Assuming any request for taxpayer information is a valid disclosure ground — only the four recognised categories (legally required, legally permitted and client-authorised, professional duty/right, or otherwise per law) justify disclosure; a mere request from an interested third party (e.g. a pressure group) does not.","Believing a tax practitioner facing an unresolved ethical conflict should simply continue the engagement — the chapter is explicit that where all reasonable resolution avenues are exhausted, the practitioner should refuse to remain associated with the matter, up to and including withdrawing from the assignment or client.","Overlooking that seeking guidance from a professional body on an ethical conflict does NOT itself breach confidentiality, provided it is done anonymously or under legal privilege — practitioners sometimes wrongly assume any external consultation is itself a confidentiality violation."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a fundamental principle of ethics as specified by the International Ethics Standards Board for Accountants (IESBA)?","o":["Integrity","Objectivity","Literacy","Professional competence and due care","Confidentiality"],"a":2,"w":"\"Literacy\" is not among the five IESBA fundamental principles — the actual set is integrity, objectivity, professional competence and due care, confidentiality, and professional behaviour.","src":"Chapter 3.1","sec":"3.1"},{"q":"The following are circumstances where a tax consultant may disclose information on a taxpayer EXCEPT","o":["Disclosure is permitted by law","Disclosure is authorised by the client, that is, the taxpayer","Disclosure to the appropriate public authorities of infringements of the law that come to light","There is a professional duty or right to disclose, when not prohibited by law","Disclosure is required by a pressure group"],"a":4,"w":"A demand from a pressure group is not one of the recognised disclosure grounds — the genuine categories are legal permission/requirement, client authorisation, and professional duty/right, none of which a pressure group's demand falls under.","src":"Chapter 3.2","sec":"3.2"},{"q":"When undertaking promotional activities, a professional accountant shall not","o":["Hold too many meetings with clients","Charge professional fees","Make disparaging references or unsubstantiated comparisons to the work of others","Send audit queries to clients","Consult with the relevant professional body on any issue"],"a":2,"w":"The professional behaviour principle specifically prohibits disparaging references or unsubstantiated comparisons to others' work in marketing/promotional activities — the other options describe normal, unproblematic professional conduct.","src":"Chapter 3.1","sec":"3.1"},{"q":"Which one of the following reports, returns, communications or other information SHOULD a professional accountant be associated with?","o":["Reports containing a materially false statement","Reports containing a misleading statement","Reports containing statements or information provided recklessly","Reports omitting required information where such omission would be misleading","Reports containing vital information for the users of the accounts"],"a":4,"w":"A report containing vital, genuine information for users is precisely the kind of report the integrity principle permits association with — the other four options each describe the specific categories of false, misleading, reckless or misleadingly-incomplete information an accountant must NEVER knowingly be associated with.","src":"Chapter 3.1","sec":"3.1"},{"q":"Given the principle of confidentiality, all professional accountants are expected to adhere strictly to the following EXCEPT","o":["Be alert to the possibility of inadvertent disclosure, including in a social environment, and particularly to a close business associate or an immediate or a close family member","Maintain confidentiality of information within the firm or employing organisation","Maintain confidentiality of information disclosed by a prospective client or employing organisation","Disclose confidential information acquired as a result of professional and business relationships outside the firm or employing organisation without proper and specific authority","Not use confidential information acquired as a result of professional and business relationships for the personal advantage of the accountant or for the advantage of a third party"],"a":3,"w":"This option describes exactly what the confidentiality principle PROHIBITS — disclosing confidential information without proper, specific authority — making it the one item accountants must NOT do, unlike the other four, which are genuine obligations accountants must adhere to.","src":"Chapter 3.1","sec":"3.1"}],"theory":[{"q":"Briefly describe the obligations paragraph 114.1 of the IESBA Code imposes on professional accountants with regard to confidentiality.","marks":7,"a":[{"ol":["Be alert to the possibility of inadvertent disclosure, including in a social environment, particularly to a close business associate or immediate/close family member.","Maintain confidentiality of information within the firm or employing organisation.","Maintain confidentiality of information disclosed by a prospective client or employing organisation.","Not disclose confidential information outside the firm/employer without proper, specific authority, unless there is a legal or professional duty/right to disclose.","Not use confidential information for personal advantage, or a third party's advantage.","Not use or disclose confidential information after the relationship has ended.","Take reasonable steps to ensure personnel under their control, and advisers consulted, respect the duty of confidentiality."]}],"src":"Chapter 3.1","sec":"3.1"},{"q":"What should a tax practitioner do when faced with a conflict of interest?","a":[{"p":"A conflict of interest creates a threat to objectivity and may threaten the other fundamental principles too. It arises where the practitioner undertakes an activity for two or more clients with conflicting interests on that matter, or where the practitioner's own interests conflict with a client's on a matter they act for."},{"p":"It may be in the practitioner's best interest to document the substance of the issue, discussions held, and decisions made. If a significant conflict cannot be resolved, the practitioner may seek professional advice from the relevant body (ICAN, CITN), generally obtainable without breaching confidentiality if discussed anonymously or under legal privilege."},{"p":"If, after exhausting all possibilities, the conflict remains unresolved, the practitioner should, where possible, refuse to remain associated with the matter — determining whether it is appropriate to withdraw from the specific assignment or from the client altogether."}],"marks":6,"src":"Chapter 3.3","sec":"3.3"}]}},{"n":4,"t":"Returns, Assessments and Collection Procedures","brief":"The full procedural machinery of Nigerian tax compliance: registration and the Taxpayer Identification Number (Tax ID), what income tax returns must contain and when they are due, the duty to keep books, the timing and currency of tax payment, penalties for late registration/filing/poor record-keeping, the full assessment framework (self-assessment, additional assessment, best-of-judgement assessment, deemed profit assessment, and more), collection procedure (notice, refunds), enforcement procedure (from calling for records through to distraint and prosecution), and the extensive catalogue of tax offences and their penalties under the NTAA 2025.","outcomes":["Know the registration and filing procedures for tax returns","Understand assessment procedures for companies","Explain the assessment and collection procedures","Explain the enforcement procedures","Know the timing and currency of payment of tax","Explain applicable offences and penalties"],"secs":[{"n":"4.2","t":"Registration for tax purposes and the Taxpayer Identification Number (Tax ID)","b":[{"h4":"4.2.1  Registration with the relevant tax authority"},{"p":"**(a) Registration of resident taxable persons — sections 4 and 5 of NTAA.** For the purposes of compliance with tax obligations, **every taxable person shall register with the relevant tax authority and obtain a taxpayer identification (\"Tax ID\")**. In addition to the above, **every ministry, department or agency of the Federal or a State government, and every local government**, shall register for tax and obtain a Tax ID."},{"p":"**(b) Registration of non-resident taxable persons — section 6 of NTAA.** A non-resident person shall register for tax purposes and obtain a Tax ID when it **supplies taxable goods or services to any person in Nigeria**, or **derives income from Nigeria**."},{"p":"A non-resident person who derives **only passive income from investment in Nigeria** may not be required to register for tax, but shall provide relevant information as may be required by the Service."},{"p":"The relevant tax authority is empowered to **issue guidelines** for the purpose of giving effect to the provisions relating to registration of taxable persons."},{"note":"Read (a) and (b) together and the rule is: residence decides *whether you must register at all*, not *whether you are taxable*. A resident registers because it is a taxable person, full stop — government ministries, departments and agencies included, which is the part candidates most often miss. A non-resident registers only once it has a **Nigerian nexus** (a supply into Nigeria, or Nigerian-source income), and even then the one carve-out is **pure passive investment income**, where information-reporting replaces registration."},{"h4":"4.2.2  Registration requirements and processes"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Entity type","Requirements"],"rows":[["**Limited liability companies**","Two registration letters (income tax and VAT). The **income tax letter** discloses: company name, registered office, business address, precise business nature, commencement date, accounting date, bankers, major shareholders, directors, auditors, and tax representative — accompanied by: a copy of the certificate of incorporation (original for sighting); CTC of the memorandum/articles of association; a status report or Forms CAC 2/CAC 7/CAC 1.1; a recent utility bill; the company stamp/seal; and the completed taxpayer registration input form. The **VAT letter** is accompanied by a completed VAT Form 001 (signed by an authorised representative) and the certificate of incorporation."],["**Business names**","Submit a VAT registration application on the firm's letterhead to the nearest NRS Tax Controller; complete the VAT/input forms; provide the certificate of registration and Form CAC/BN/A1 (originals for sighting); a recent utility bill; and the business stamp/seal."]]}},{"p":"Set out in the book's own enumerated form, **(a) limited liability companies** must submit **two letters of registration** — one for income tax and one for VAT. The **income tax registration letter** shall disclose the following information on the company:"},{"ol":["name of the company;","registered office;","business address;","precise nature of business;","date of commencement of business;","accounting date;","name and address of bankers;","particulars of major shareholders;","particulars of directors;","name and address of auditors; and","name and address of tax representative."]},{"p":"Also, the **income tax** registration letter shall be accompanied by the following documents:"},{"ol":["copy of certificate of incorporation (original for sighting);","CTC (certified true copy) of memorandum and articles of association;","copy of status report, or Form CAC 2 and CAC 7, or Form CAC 1.1;","recent utility bill of business address;","company stamp or seal; and","the completed taxpayer registration input form for companies."]},{"p":"The **VAT** registration letter shall be accompanied by: **(i)** a completed copy of VAT form 001 (the VAT registration form), duly signed by an authorised company representative; and **(ii)** the certificate of incorporation."},{"p":"**(b) Business names.** A business name is required to register for VAT purposes as follows:"},{"ol":["submit an application for VAT registration on the firm's letterhead, addressed to the Tax Controller of the nearest office of the NRS;","complete all registration forms given (VAT and input forms);","provide a copy of the certificate of registration and Form CAC/BN/A1, and submit the originals for sighting;","recent utility bill; and","business stamp or seal, to be applied on the NRS' forms given."]},{"note":"Note the asymmetry the book draws without stating it outright: a **company** registers for two taxes (income tax and VAT) and so files two letters, while a **business name** is described here only as registering for **VAT** — its proprietor is an individual, assessed to personal income tax by the State authority rather than assessed as a company. This is also why only the company list calls for memorandum and articles, CAC 2/CAC 7 and directors' particulars: a business name has none of those."},{"h4":"4.2.3  Issuance of Tax ID — section 7 of NTAA"},{"p":"On request, the relevant tax authority registers and issues a Tax ID — refusal must be notified, with reasons, within **5 working days**. The authority may also register and issue a Tax ID (based on information it already holds) to a person who should have applied but did not, promptly informing them. A person with a valid Tax ID needs no other; multiple Tax IDs discovered must be promptly reported for unification; and a Tax ID is **not transferable** between persons."},{"h4":"4.2.4  Notification of change in particulars — section 9 of NTAA"},{"key":"A taxable person must notify the relevant tax authority of any change in particulars **within 30 days** (s.9 NTAA) — name/trading name/location/contact details/registered address; for an incorporated person, changes in ≥5% shareholders or beneficial owners; for a trust, trustee/beneficiary details; for a partnership, partner details; or details of a sale/liquidation/acquisition/take-over/merger."},{"h4":"4.2.5  Objectives and uses of Tax ID — section 8 of NTAA"},{"def":{"t":"Taxpayer identification (Tax ID)","d":"a unique number allocated to each taxpayer with a view to identifying an individual, business or any other entity on tax returns and other documents filed with the tax authorities."}},{"p":"Objectives of the Tax ID (s.8 NTAA): identifying/registering taxable persons; widening the tax base; minimising evasion/fraud; enhancing administrative efficiency; reducing compliance cost; and serving as a precondition for bidding on government contracts."},{"p":"Uses: disclosed on returns/notices/correspondence/documents for tax compliance; disclosed on transaction documents; and a precondition for contracting with any government ministry/department/agency. Banking, insurance, stock-broking and other financial service providers must make Tax ID submission a precondition for opening or operating any account."},{"h4":"4.2.6  Suspension, deregistration and cancellation of Tax ID — section 10 of NTAA"},{"p":"A **temporary** cessation of trade requires notifying the tax authority within 30 days, whereupon the Tax ID is classified dormant/suspended (re-issued with the same ID on reapplication). A **permanent** cessation similarly requires 30-day notification, after which the authority deregisters the Tax ID. The authority also deregisters/cancels a Tax ID where the person is deceased (or a body corporate is wound up/dissolved), or where a person holds a duplicate ID."}]},{"n":"4.3","t":"Contents of income tax returns","b":[{"h4":"4.3.1  Resident companies — section 11(1) of NTAA"},{"key":"Every company — with or without notice from the Service — must file a **self-assessment return** at least once a year (s.11(1) NTAA), containing: a completed self-assessment form; audited financial statements plus tax/capital allowance computations for each profit source (a **small company** may substitute a taxpayer-attested statement of accounts for audited statements); evidence of tax payment; the effective tax rate/additional tax computation, where applicable; and an attestation signed by a principal officer."},{"h4":"4.3.2  Non-resident companies — section 11(2) of NTAA"},{"p":"A **non-resident** company taxable in Nigeria (s.11(2) NTAA) must submit a return containing: full audited financial statements plus the Nigerian operations' financial statements (attested by an independent qualified/certified accountant in Nigeria); tax computation schedules based on Nigerian-attributable profits; a true, correct written statement of profits from each Nigerian source; completed CIT self-assessment forms; evidence of tax payment; and effective tax rate/additional tax computation, where applicable."},{"note":"A non-Nigerian company earning **only** income on which withholding tax is the final tax is exempt from this filing obligation for that year."},{"h4":"4.3.3  Companies on cessation — section 11(4) of NTAA"},{"p":"A company **permanently ceasing** Nigerian operations (s.11(4)) must file returns for the cessation year and any outstanding returns."},{"note":"The three sub-sections answer one question — *what goes in the envelope* — for three different filers. The resident list and the non-resident list look alike, but the non-resident list adds two things a resident never needs: financial statements **of the Nigerian operations specifically**, attested by an independent qualified or certified accountant **in Nigeria**, and a written statement of profits **from each and every Nigerian source**. That is the apportionment problem a non-resident return exists to solve."}]},{"n":"4.4","t":"Due dates for filing of tax returns","b":[{"h4":"4.4.1  Limited liability companies — section 11(5) of NTAA"},{"p":"Statutory timelines for audited accounts and income tax returns (s.11(5) NTAA):"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Company type","Due date"],"rows":[["**Existing company** (in business >18 months)","6 months after the end of the accounting year."],["**Newly incorporated company**","Within 18 months of incorporation, or not later than 6 months after the end of its first accounting period — **whichever is earlier**."],["**Company ceasing trade/business**","Not later than 6 months from the date of cessation."]]}},{"h4":"4.4.2  Appointment of agents to file returns — section 11(6) of NTAA"},{"key":"A company has the **prerogative to appoint representative(s)** to deal with its tax matters, and where the person so appointed is an **agent**, such a person shall be an **accredited agent** (s.11(6) NTAA)."},{"h4":"4.4.3  Other returns"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Filer","Requirement"],"rows":[["**Banks/insurance/stock-broking/financial institutions** (s.29(1))","Prepare (with or without demand) annual returns on new/existing customer names/locations/transactions, where cumulative monthly transactions reach **≥N50,000,000** (individual) or **≥N250,000,000** (body corporate) — plus, as prescribed, returns on specified-sum transactions, customer addresses/details. Institutions may also be required, by tax authority notice, to provide named-person information — but need not disclose ADDITIONAL customer/bank information unless required by a notice signed by the tax authority's CEO."],["**Virtual Asset Service Providers (VASPs)** (s.25)","In addition to standard income tax returns, submit returns describing: the virtual asset service provided; transaction date; asset type/value; sales value; customer name/address/phone/email/Tax ID (and national ID, if an individual); counterparty details; and other prescribed particulars. The authority may request further information at any time."],["**Priority companies** (s.26)","File income tax returns annually, in the manner specified, covering both priority and non-priority products/services."],["**Entities enjoying incentives** (s.27)","Submit Annual Tax Incentives returns (covering income tax and any non-general incentive) alongside annual tax returns. State tax authorities transmit individual incentive returns to the Service within 60 days of the annual return due date; the Service reports annually to the Minister."],["**Persons deducting tax at source** (s.28)","Render **monthly** returns to the appropriate tax authority, per the prescribed regulation."]]}}]},{"n":"4.5","t":"Maintenance of books — section 31 of NTAA","b":[{"ol":["Every taxable entity must maintain books/records of account — **regardless** of whether it is actually liable to pay tax;","the records must contain sufficient information to ascertain tax liability;","records must be in **English**, in a format the relevant tax authority may prescribe;","records kept in another language must, on demand, be translated into English (at the taxpayer's expense) by a **sworn translator**; and","records must be kept for **at least 6 years** after the year of assessment to which the income relates."]}]},{"n":"4.6","t":"Time within which to pay tax assessed — section 49 of NTAA","b":[{"ol":["Tax due is paid on or before the filing due date — in one lump sum or instalments, with the final instalment due by the filing due date;","tax charged by an assessment not subject to objection/appeal is payable (after any set-off/deposit) within **30 days** of the notice of assessment being served — the tax authority may extend this at its discretion;","where an objection/appeal has been lodged, collection of the **disputed** tax remains in abeyance until determined — but the **undisputed** portion remains payable;","once an objection/appeal is determined, a new payable-tax notice is served, payable within 30 days of service;","unpaid balances attract interest and penalties;","if a payment extension is granted but the taxpayer still fails to pay within it, penalty/interest accrues from the ORIGINAL due date, as if no extension was ever granted; and","absent an objection/appeal, the tax authority may proceed to enforce recovery."]}]},{"n":"4.7","t":"Penalties for late registration, late filing of returns, and failure to keep proper books or records","b":[{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Failure","First month","Each subsequent month"],"rows":[["**Failure to register** (s.100)","N50,000","N25,000"],["**Awarding a contract to an unregistered person** (s.100)","N5,000,000 (flat)","—"],["**Late/incomplete/inaccurate return filing** (s.101)","N100,000","N50,000"],["**Failure to keep proper books** — person other than a company","N10,000 (flat)","—"],["**Failure to keep proper books** — a company","N50,000 (flat)","—"]]}},{"h4":"4.7.1  Failure to register — section 100 of NTAA"},{"ol":["A taxable person who **fails or refuses to register** with the relevant tax authority for tax shall be liable to pay an administrative penalty of **N50,000 in the first month** in which the failure occurs, and **N25,000 for each subsequent month** in which the failure continues.","A **statutory body or company who awards a contract to an unregistered person** shall be liable to pay an administrative penalty of **N5,000,000**."]},{"note":"Limb (b) is the one worth pausing on: the penalty falls on the **contract-awarding body**, not on the unregistered contractor, and it is a flat N5,000,000 rather than a monthly accrual. It is a deliberate enforcement design — it makes every large buyer police the Tax ID of every supplier, which is the same lever as the rule at §4.2.5 making a Tax ID a condition for government contracts."},{"h4":"4.7.2  Late filing of returns — section 101 of NTAA"},{"p":"A taxable entity who **fails or refuses to file returns**, or **knowingly files incomplete or inaccurate returns**, to the relevant tax authority in accordance with the provisions of this Act shall be liable to pay an administrative penalty of:"},{"ol":["**N100,000.00** in the first month in which the failure occurs; and","**N50,000.00** for each subsequent month in which the failure continues."]},{"note":"Filing an **incomplete or inaccurate** return knowingly is penalised on exactly the same scale as filing nothing at all. A partial return buys no relief."},{"h4":"4.7.3  Failure to keep proper books"},{"p":"A taxable entity who:"},{"ol":["fails to keep accounts, books and records of business transactions and income, to allow for the correct ascertainment of tax and filing of returns to the relevant tax authority; or","upon request by the relevant tax authority, fails to provide any record or book prescribed in this Act,"]},{"p":"shall be liable to pay an administrative penalty of, in the case of **(i)** a person other than a company, **N10,000.00**, and **(ii)** in the case of a company, **N50,000.00**."}]},{"n":"4.8","t":"Assessment procedures","b":[{"key":"Where a company files a satisfactory self-assessment return, the NRS may accept it as made. Where a company **fails** to self-assess by the deadline, the NRS proceeds to assess it directly."},{"h4":"4.8.1  Self-assessment — section 34 of NTAA"},{"def":{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"a system where the taxpayer is legally granted the right to compute their own tax liability, submit a return, pay the tax due, and file the return with payment evidence, by the due date."}},{"p":"A taxpayer submitting a self-assessment return is **deemed** to have made an assessment of the amount payable (including a negative/nil amount) for that period. Completing/submitting an approved electronic return is still self-assessment, even if the form contains pre-entered tax-authority information, or computes an estimate electronically as data is entered."},{"p":"Once a return is delivered, the tax authority may: accept it without further assessment; accept it and make an additional assessment; or reject it and, to the best of its judgement, determine and assess the tax due. Undeclared income/tax makes the taxpayer liable for the shortfall from the return's due date, subject to penalty and interest."},{"h4":"4.8.2  Additional assessment — section 36 of NTAA"},{"key":"Where a taxpayer was not assessed, or under-assessed, the tax authority may assess (or re-assess) within **6 years** of the original assessment — this limit does not prevent continuing a tax audit/raising an additional assessment if the audit began before the 6 years expired. Where there is **deliberate misstatement**, the 6-year limit does **not** apply at all — the authority may assess at any time, as often as necessary, to make good the resulting tax loss."},{"h4":"4.8.3  Best of judgement (BOJ) / administrative assessment — section 35 of NTAA"},{"p":"Where a taxpayer has not delivered a return, and the tax authority believes tax is payable, it may assess **to the best of its judgement** — without prejudice to any other liability arising from the failure to file. An assessment can be made even before the normal filing deadline expires, if the authority considers the assessment to be **in jeopardy**."},{"h4":"4.8.4  Deemed profit assessment or income statement — section 38 of NTAA"},{"p":"Where a business produces no assessable profit, or less than expected, or the true profit cannot be readily ascertained: a **resident** is assessed on a fair/reasonable percentage of gross income the authority determines; a **non-resident**'s profit is deemed to be their profit margin (EBIT ÷ revenue, per their own or comparable companies' published accounts) applied to their Nigerian turnover."},{"h4":"4.8.5  Substitution of dividend for total profit — section 9 of NTA"},{"key":"Where a Nigerian company declares a dividend out of profits on which no tax is payable (because there are no total profits, or total profits are less than the dividend), the company is charged tax **as if the dividend were its total profits** for the relevant year. This does **not** apply to: dividends from already-taxed retained earnings; dividends from tax-exempt profits; franked investment income; or distributions by a real estate investment company from rental/dividend income received on shareholders' behalf."},{"h4":"4.8.6  Assessments based on undistributed profits treated as distributed — section 10 of NTA"},{"p":"For a Nigerian company controlled by **≤5 individuals**, which has not distributed available profits, the Service may **direct** that the distributable proportion be construed as distributed — becoming taxable income for shareholders proportionate to their shareholding. Any such direction must be in writing, specify the deemed distribution day, the gross deemed-distributed amount, the deduction-at-source rate and the net amount, and be made **within 3 years** of receiving the audited accounts. The company has a right of appeal. For a limited liability partnership, all profits are similarly deemed distributed among partners."},{"h4":"4.8.7  Assessments to be final and conclusive — section 43 of NTAA"},{"key":"An assessment becomes **final and conclusive** where: no valid objection/appeal is lodged within 30 days; the total profits amount has been agreed; the amount has been determined/revised on objection; or the assessment has been agreed/revised/determined on appeal. Unpaid tax on a final assessment (beyond 30 days) is subject to normal recovery/penalty provisions, subject only to set-off of any agreed/appeal-determined refund claim. This does not prevent a **fresh** assessment on different facts for the same year."},{"h4":"4.8.8  Errors and defects in assessment and notice — section 42 of NTAA"},{"p":"An assessment/notice/warrant is **not invalidated** for a formal defect, mistake or omission, provided its substance/effect conforms to the Act and the assessed party is identified per common intent/understanding — including mistakes as to a company/person's name, profit description, or tax amount, or assessment-notice variances, provided the notice was duly served and substantively contains the assessment particulars."},{"h4":"4.8.9  Artificial transactions — section 46 of NTAA"},{"key":"Where the tax authority considers a disposition not genuinely given effect to, or a tax-reducing transaction **artificial or fictitious**, it may disregard it or direct adjustments to counteract the reduction. Transactions between **connected persons** are deemed artificial/fictitious if not made on terms comparable persons dealing at **arm's length** would fairly use. A right of appeal applies as if the direction were an assessment. \"**Disposition**\" includes any trust, grant, covenant, agreement or arrangement."},{"h4":"4.8.10  Liability of manager or principal officer — section 45 of NTAA"},{"p":"A **manager or principal officer of a company** shall be liable for doing all such acts, matters and things as are required to be done by virtue of this Act for the **assessment of the company and payment of the tax**."},{"p":"Based on the provision of **section 52 of NTAA**, a manager or agent answerable for payment of tax on behalf of a taxable person: **(a)** may **retain out of any money received** on behalf of the taxable person so much as shall be sufficient to pay the tax; and **(b)** is **indemnified** against any person for all payments made."},{"note":"Section 45 creates the liability and section 52 makes it bearable. Without the retention-and-indemnity provision, an agent would be personally exposed to a tax bill while holding the taxpayer's money in trust; s.52 lets the agent pay the tax out of that money and be protected for having done so."},{"h4":"4.8.11  Company wound up — section 53 of NTAA"},{"ol":["In the process of liquidating a company, the **liquidator shall not distribute any of the assets** of the company to the shareholders **unless provision has been made for the payment in full of any tax** which may be found payable by the company, including any tax deductions made by the company under any law in effect in any part of Nigeria.","Where tax is not paid in accordance with the provision of this section or any other law, the **liquidator shall be personally liable**."]}]},{"n":"4.9","t":"Collection procedure","b":[{"h4":"4.9.1  Service of notice of assessment — section 40 of NTAA"},{"p":"**Service of assessment notice** (s.40 NTAA): served on, or sent by registered post/courier/electronic means to, each taxable person (or the person in whose name they are chargeable) — stating the payment place and the right to seek revision/amendment."},{"h4":"4.9.2  Currency of assessment and payment — section 39 of NTAA"},{"key":"**Currency of payment** (s.39 NTAA): income tax is paid in the **currency of the underlying transaction** — if a company's transactions are in a foreign currency, tax on that income is payable in that same currency."},{"h4":"4.9.3  Tax refund — section 55 of NTAA"},{"ol":["taxpayers are entitled to a refund of overpayment/excess tax, after tax-authority audit;","the authority may make rules/conditions for refunds;","a due refund is paid within **90 days** of the authority's decision, with the option of setting it off against other tax liability;","the Accountant-General (Federal or State) opens a dedicated account per tax type for refund settlement, funded per the authority's own estimate; and","no refund claim is allowed unless made in writing within **6 years** after the end of the relevant year of assessment."]},{"h4":"4.9.4  Transfer of tax revenue for refund — section 82 of NTAA"},{"p":"**Transfer of tax revenue for refund** (s.82 NTAA): each tax authority furnishes the Accountant-General with monthly tax-collection/refund-claim schedules within **7 days** of month-end; the Accountant-General deducts total refund claims from gross tax revenue before distribution and remits to the refund accounts, reconciling monthly — any credit balance may reduce the following month's refund funding need."}]},{"n":"4.10","t":"Enforcement procedures","b":[{"key":"Where a company fails to pay assessed tax, the NRS may enforce payment/recovery through any NTAA-stipulated method."},{"h4":"4.10.1  Call for returns, books, documents, and information — section 57 of NTAA"},{"p":"The tax authority may notify a taxable entity to: complete/deliver a specified return; appear personally for examination; produce books/documents/records for examination; provide information orally/in writing; or grant access to electronically-stored records (computers, cloud, magnetic media, etc.). The notice period is **at least 7 days**. An entity may apply in writing for a time extension, with good cause shown before the original deadline expires. Delivered returns/information must carry a true-and-correct attestation."},{"h4":"4.10.2  Power to assess lands, buildings, books and documents — section 58 of NTAA"},{"p":"An authorised officer has **free access, at all reasonable times**, to land/buildings/places/books/documents (including electronically-stored ones) for collecting tax. Where hard copies are unavailable (data is only digital), the authority may take **immediate possession** of the removable media/equipment to prevent destruction/alteration — or, if able to make exact duplicate digital copies, use those as evidence instead of seizing the originals. The occupier must provide reasonable facilities/assistance and answer questions. Entry into a **private dwelling** requires either occupier consent or judicial authorisation — a warrant valid for **3 months** (renewable), directed to a named officer, per the prescribed Second Schedule form, produced on first entry and whenever subsequently reasonably required."},{"h4":"4.10.3  Power to remove books and documents — section 59 of NTAA"},{"p":"An officer may remove/copy books/documents/items (under CEO authority), returning originals as soon as practicable. A CEO-certified copy (including digital evidence) is admissible in court as if original. The owner may inspect/copy the removed items at their own expense; the remover bears the removal cost itself, and must issue a written receipt describing what was removed and when/where."},{"h4":"4.10.4  Power to distrain — section 61 of NTAA"},{"key":"Once an assessment is **final and conclusive** and a demand notice unpaid within its stated time, the tax authority may **distrain** — seizing goods/chattels/bonds/securities, or land/premises/assets the person owns — to recover the tax by sale."},{"p":"Distrained assets may be kept **14 days**; if the tax/costs/charges remain unpaid, they may be sold **only with a High Court order**. Sale proceeds first cover keeping/sale costs, then the tax due; any balance is refunded within 90 days of sale (with or without demand). Immovable property sale always requires a High Court order. Distraint reaches all the debtor's assets wherever found, and also applies to recovering tax evasion proceeds where the offender cannot be located."},{"h4":"4.10.5  Enforcement of powers — section 62 of NTAA"},{"p":"The tax authority may request law enforcement assistance. A warranted officer (accompanied by law enforcement) may enter premises to search for/seize/copy books/documents/items (including requiring passwords/access codes), search persons on the premises, open containers, force entry, and remove obstructions with reasonable force. A **bodily search** must be conducted by an officer of the **same gender** as the person searched."},{"h4":"4.10.6  Power of substitution — section 60 of NTAA"},{"p":"The tax authority may appoint, by written notice, any person as **agent** of a taxable person — where tax is due/payable and unpaid, or the agent holds/is expected to hold the taxpayer's money/assets. The agent must pay tax due from any held funds; a defaulting agent becomes liable to all the same enforcement/recovery actions (including distraint) as if originally liable themselves. Objection/appeal rights apply to such a notice as if it were an assessment/demand notice."},{"h4":"4.10.7  Remission of penalty — section 66 of NTAA; 4.10.8  Recovery of tax — section 67; 4.10.9  Assignment of tax debts — section 68"},{"p":"The tax authority **must**, for good cause shown, remit whole/part of a penalty/interest, reporting monthly to the Minister on waivers granted. Tax due is a **debt** to the tax authority — unpaid beyond 30 days triggers a demand notice; continued non-payment allows recovery via any NTAA power, including legal action. Under-assessed or erroneously-repaid amounts are similarly recoverable on demand, subject to a **6-year** limit (waived for untrue documents/statements). Outstanding tax debts may be **assigned** to an accredited third party (banks, debt-recovery practitioners, etc.) once all internal recovery steps are exhausted and the debt is significant/long-outstanding — the taxpayer is notified in writing, and the authority may reverse an assignment at any time."},{"h4":"4.10.10  Power to pay reward — section 69 of NTAA"},{"p":"**Power to pay reward** (s.69 NTAA): the tax authority may reward (on its own determined terms) any non-employee/non-related informant whose information assists its duties — the informant's identity is kept confidential, with unauthorised disclosure itself an offence."}]},{"n":"4.11","t":"Service of notice of assessment — section 40 of NTAA","b":[{"p":"The relevant tax authority can **serve on, or send by registered post, courier service or electronic means** to each taxable person, or person in whose name a taxable person is chargeable, a **notice of assessment** in respect of any tax charged, including the **place at which payment should be made**, and setting out the **rights of that person to apply to the relevant tax authority for the revision and amendment** of the assessment made upon it."},{"note":"The study text prints section 40 **twice** — once as §4.9.1 inside collection procedure, and again here as a section of its own, word for word. That is the book's own duplication, not an omission on this site, and both appearances are kept so that a reader working from the printed text finds the content at either number. Substantively the rule belongs in both places: service of the notice is the act that *starts the clock* — the 30 days to pay under §4.6, and the 30 days to object under §4.8.7 both run from service, which is why a defective service argument is the first thing a taxpayer's adviser looks for."}]},{"n":"4.12","t":"Offences and penalties","b":[{"h4":"4.12.1  Failure to pay tax on due date — section 65 of NTA"},{"key":"Late payment (s.65 NTA): unpaid tax beyond 30 days attracts a **10% surcharge**, plus interest — at the CBN Monetary Policy Rate plus spread (Naira remittances), or SOFR plus spread (foreign currency remittances) — both recoverable as tax. A demand notice is served; non-payment within a further 30 days triggers enforcement. This penalty/interest is **never** treated as part of the tax paid for relief-claim purposes."},{"p":"The NTAA catalogues an extensive set of further offences and administrative penalties:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Offence","Penalty"],"rows":[["**4.12.2** Failure to grant access for the deployment of technology (s.103)","N1,000,000 (first day) + N10,000/subsequent day."],["**4.12.3** Failure to use the focalization system (s.104)","N200,000 + 100% of tax due + CBN MPR interest."],["**4.12.4** Failure to deduct tax (s.105)","40% of the amount not deducted."],["**4.12.5** Failure to make attribution (s.106)","N1,000,000."],["**4.12.6(a)** Failure to attend to demands, requests or notices (s.108(a))","N100,000 (first day) + N10,000/subsequent day."],["**4.12.6(b)** Failure/refusal to supply demanded information (s.108(b))","N200,000 (first day) + N10,000/subsequent day."],["**4.12.6(c)** Failure to submit legal-arrangement information (s.108(c))","N1,000,000 (first day) + N10,000/subsequent day."],["**4.12.7** Failure to notify change of address (s.112)","N100,000 (first month) + N50,000/subsequent month."],["**4.12.8** Offence by authorised and unauthorised persons — bribery, embezzlement, false returns, document theft, compromising assessments, etc. (s.114)","A fine of 200% of the sum in question, or imprisonment up to 3 years, or both."],["**4.12.9** Inducement of an authorised officer (s.115)","N500,000 (individual) or N2,000,000 (body corporate), or up to 3 years' imprisonment, or both — plus the tax due."],["**4.12.10** Use of weapon (s.116)","Up to 5 years' imprisonment — up to 10 years if it causes injury to an officer."],["**4.12.11** Impersonation of an authorised officer (s.117)","Up to N1,000,000, or up to 3 years' imprisonment, or both."],["**4.12.12** Aiding and abetting commission of an offence (s.118)","N1,000,000, or up to 3 years' imprisonment, or both."],["**4.12.13** Obstruction — hindering, impeding a search/seizure, rescuing seized goods, etc. (s.119)","An administrative penalty of N1,000,000, AND, on conviction, up to N1,000,000 fine or up to 3 years' imprisonment, or both."],["**4.12.14** Unauthorised disclosure (s.120)","Up to N1,000,000, or up to 3 years' imprisonment, or both."],["**4.12.15** False claim of tax refund (s.121)","Recovery of the amount, plus a 50% penalty, plus CBN MPR interest (without prejudice to prosecution)."],["**4.12.16** False declaration (s.124)","N1,000,000 administrative penalty (plus the undercharged/unpaid tax), OR, on conviction, N1,000,000 fine or up to 3 years' imprisonment, or both (plus the tax due)."],["**4.12.17** Counterfeiting documents (s.125)","N1,000,000 administrative penalty, AND, on conviction, up to 3 years' imprisonment or N1,000,000 fine, or both."],["**4.12.18** Offence by a body corporate (s.126)","Directors/managers/partners/trustees/persons in management are personally liable as if they committed the offence, unless proven to have acted without knowledge/consent/connivance."],["**4.12.19** General penalty (s.127) — any unspecified contravention","N1,000,000 administrative penalty, or, on conviction, up to 3 years' imprisonment or a fine, or both."]]}}]},{"n":"4.13","t":"Chapter review","b":[{"p":"This chapter discussed the need for incorporated companies to file annual tax returns, the nature/composition of such returns, and the statutory filing time limits. It also covered assessment and collection procedures in detail, including payment time limits (by basis of assessment), currency of payment, late-payment penalties, and the Revenue authority's enforcement procedures."}]},{"n":"4.14","t":"Worked examples — study text questions and suggested solutions","b":[{"p":"The study text sets **four examination-type questions** at §4.14.1, with its own suggested solutions following. All four are reproduced here with the book's solutions in full, plus added explanation. Each answer is cross-referenced to the section of this chapter the material comes from, so a part-answer can be traced straight back to the statute."},{"note":"A numbering defect in the book, flagged rather than silently corrected: the solutions are headed **\"4.12.2 Suggested solutions to questions\"**, which is wrong twice over — it should read **4.14.2**, and 4.12.2 is already in use for \"Failure to grant access for the deployment of technology\". The fourth solution is also printed as a bare **\"4.\"** heading outside the numbered list of the other three. The content is unaffected."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 4.14.1, Question 1","t":"Registration documents, filing time lags and the late-filing penalty","open":true,"q":[{"p":"The powers to assess the incomes of corporate organisations to taxes are vested in Nigeria Revenue Service as entrenched in Nigeria Tax Administration Act, 2025. To comply with the statutory provisions, corporate organisations are required to file tax returns within a specified period of time to the relevant tax authority."},{"p":"**Required:** Highlight the:"},{"ol":["documents/information required to be forwarded to the relevant tax authority when registering with the nearest integrated tax office;","time lag for filing the first set of returns and subsequent ones; and","penalty for late filing of returns at the due dates."]}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  Documents and information required on registration"},{"p":"Corporate organisations are required by law to file tax returns to the Nigeria Revenue Service. The returns must be submitted to the **integrated tax office nearest the business's registered address**."},{"p":"**(i) Limited liability companies.** Every limited liability company registering with the NRS for tax purposes is required to submit **two letters of registration** — one for income tax and the other for VAT. The registration letter for **income tax** shall disclose the following information on the company:"},{"ul":["name of the company;","registered office;","business address;","precise nature of business;","date of commencement of business;","accounting date;","name and address of bankers;","particulars of major shareholders;","particulars of directors;","name and address of auditors; and","name and address of tax representative."]},{"p":"Also, the registration letter shall be accompanied by the following documents:"},{"ul":["copy of certificate of incorporation (original for sighting);","CTC of memorandum and articles of association;","copy of status report or Form CAC 2 and CAC 7 or Form CAC 1.1;","recent utility bill of business address;","company stamp or seal; and","the completed taxpayer registration input form for companies."]},{"p":"The registration letter for **VAT** shall be accompanied by the following documents:"},{"ul":["completed copy of VAT form 001, that is, the VAT registration form, duly signed by an authorised company representative; and","certificate of incorporation."]},{"p":"**(ii) Business names.** A business name is required to register for VAT purposes as follows:"},{"ul":["submit an application for VAT registration on the firm's letterhead, addressed to the Tax Controller of the nearest office of the NRS;","complete all registration forms given (VAT and input forms);","provide a copy of the certificate of registration and Form CAC/BN/A1, and submit the originals for sighting;","recent utility bill; and","business stamp or seal, to be applied on the NRS' forms given."]},{"h4":"(b)  Time lag for filing the first set of returns and subsequent ones"},{"ol":["**First set of returns** — within **18 months from the date of incorporation**, or **6 months after the end of its first accounting period**, **whichever is earlier**.","**Subsequent returns** — within **6 months from the accounting year end** of the business entity."]},{"note":"The words **\"whichever is earlier\"** carry the marks here. A company incorporated on 1 January 2026 that closes its first accounts on 31 December 2026 has two candidate dates: 30 June 2027 (6 months after the period end) and 30 June 2027 (18 months after incorporation) — identical. Shift the first accounting date to 31 March 2026 and the two diverge: 30 September 2026 versus 30 June 2027, and the **earlier**, 30 September 2026, governs. Candidates who default to \"18 months for a new company\" lose the mark whenever the first accounting period is short."},{"h4":"(c)  Penalty for late filing of returns on the due dates"},{"p":"**Section 101 of NTAA** makes provision for payment of a penalty by any company that fails to comply with the requirements for filing of tax returns. A defaulting company shall pay:"},{"ol":["**N100,000** in the first month in which it fails to file its annual returns; and","**N50,000** for each subsequent month in which the failure continues."]},{"key":"Source sections for this answer: **§4.2.2** (registration requirements), **§4.4.1** (due dates, s.11(5) NTAA) and **§4.7.2** (late-filing penalty, s.101 NTAA)."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 4.14.1, Question 2","t":"When an assessment becomes final and conclusive","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Not all assessments raised on a company by the NRS are final and conclusive. **Required:** Explain when an assessment becomes final and conclusive."}],"a":[{"p":"**Assessments to be final and conclusive — section 43 of NTAA.**"},{"p":"**(a)** Where:"},{"ol":["a company **does not lodge a valid objection or appeal** within the time stipulated under this Act (**30 days** of receiving an assessment) against an assessment as regards the amount of the total income or profits assessed;","the amount of the total income or profits **has been agreed to**;","the amount of such total profits has been **determined on objection or revised**; or","the assessment as made has been **agreed to, revised or determined on appeal**,"]},{"p":"such assessment shall be **final and conclusive** for all purposes of this Act as regards the amount of such total profits."},{"p":"**(b)** Where the full amount of tax in respect of any final and conclusive assessment is **not paid within 30 days**, the provisions relating to the recovery of tax, and to any penalty, shall apply to the collection and recovery."},{"p":"**(c)** The provision in (b) above shall be subject only to the **set-off** of the amount of any tax repayable under any claim, made under the relevant provisions of this Act, which has been agreed to by the relevant tax authority or determined on any appeal against a refusal to admit any such claim."},{"p":"**(d)** No provisions of this Act shall prevent the tax authority from making any assessment or additional assessment for any year **which does not involve re-opening any issue on the same facts** which has been determined for that year of assessment by agreement or on appeal."},{"key":"Limb (d) is what stops \"final and conclusive\" from meaning \"the year is closed for ever\". The year is closed only on **the issues actually determined, on the facts then before the authority**. New facts — or a different issue entirely — can still be assessed, which is how §4.8.2 (additional assessment within six years, or at any time on deliberate misstatement) continues to operate after an assessment has become final. *(Source: §4.8.7.)*"}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 4.14.1, Question 3","t":"The operation of the self-assessment system and its benefits","open":true,"q":[{"p":"The self-assessment system was introduced with effect from **January 1, 1996**, to run parallel and in conjunction with the existing government assessment for both individual and corporate taxpayers. **Required:** Explain the operation of the self-assessment system and its benefits."}],"a":[{"p":"**Self-assessment system in Nigeria (section 34 of NTAA) and its benefits.**"},{"p":"**(a)** Self-assessment is a system of tax administration in which the **taxpayer is granted the right, by law, to compute his own tax liability**, submit his return with the relevant tax authority, pay the tax due, and file his tax returns with evidence of payment of the tax paid **on or before the due date**."},{"p":"**(b)** A taxpayer who has submitted a self-assessment return in the prescribed form for a reporting period is **deemed to have assessed** the amount of tax payable — **including a negative or nil amount** — for the reporting period to which the return relates."},{"p":"**(c)** When a taxpayer completes and submits a tax return in the approved form **electronically**, it is a self-assessment notwithstanding that: **(i)** the form contains **pre-entered information supplied by the tax authority**; and **(ii)** an **estimate of the tax payable is computed electronically** as information is being entered into the form."},{"p":"**(d)** Where a taxable person has delivered a tax return, the relevant tax authority may:"},{"ol":["accept the tax return **without making an additional assessment**;","accept the tax return **and make an additional assessment**; or","**reject** the tax return and, to the best of its judgement, determine the amount of the tax due from the taxable person and make an assessment accordingly."]},{"p":"**(e)** Where the taxable person **fails to declare the true and correct amount** of income or tax payable in its self-assessed tax returns, the taxable person is liable to pay any outstanding tax **from the due date of the returns**."},{"p":"**(f)** The outstanding tax shall be subject to **penalty and interest**, in accordance with the provisions of this Act or other relevant tax law, from the date the return becomes due."},{"h4":"Benefits of a self-assessment system"},{"ol":["**Improved tax compliance** — self-assessment encourages taxpayers to take responsibility for their tax obligations, leading to improved compliance.","**Reduced administrative burden** — the revenue authority reduces its administrative burden, as taxpayers compute and report their own tax liabilities.","**Increased transparency** — self-assessment promotes transparency, as taxpayers are required to disclose their financial information.","**Efficient tax collection** — self-assessment enables the revenue authority to collect taxes efficiently, as taxpayers pay their tax liabilities directly.","**Reduced tax evasion** — self-assessment helps reduce tax evasion, as taxpayers are more likely to report their true income and pay the correct amount of tax.","**Improved taxpayer education** — self-assessment educates taxpayers about their tax obligations and encourages them to seek professional advice when needed."]},{"note":"Two wrinkles are worth holding on to. First, limb (b): a **nil or negative** self-assessment is still an assessment, so a loss-making company that files has self-assessed and the clock on §4.8.2's six years starts running — it has not simply \"filed nothing\". Second, limb (e): where the declaration is untrue, the outstanding tax runs **from the original due date**, not from the date the understatement was discovered, so penalty and interest under §4.12.1 accrue over the whole intervening period. *(Source: §4.8.1.)*"},{"warn":"The book's own benefit list names \"**the FIRS**\" as the body relieved of administrative burden. Under the Nigeria Tax Administration Act, 2025 the Federal Inland Revenue Service has been succeeded by the **Nigeria Revenue Service (NRS)**, which is the name used everywhere else in this study text. Write **NRS** in an answer; the substance of the point is unchanged."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 4.14.1, Question 4","t":"The penalty for late payment of tax","open":true,"q":[{"p":"When a company has filed a self-assessment return, comprising its audited accounts and tax computations, after the due date, the NRS may raise a penalty. **Required:** Explain the penalty for late payment of tax."}],"a":[{"p":"**Penalty for late payment of tax — section 65 of NTAA.**"},{"p":"**(a)** Subject to section 67 of this Act (recovery of tax), where any tax is not paid within the period (**within 30 days**) prescribed under this Act:"},{"ol":["a sum equal to **ten percent of the amount of the tax payable** shall be added thereto, and the provisions of this Act relating to the collection and recovery of tax shall apply to the collection and recovery of the sum;","in the case of **naira remittances**, the tax due shall carry **interest at the prevailing monetary policy rate of the Central Bank of Nigeria plus a spread** to be determined by the Minister, from the date when the tax becomes payable until it is paid, and the provisions of this Act relating to collection and recovery of tax shall apply to the collection and recovery of the interest; and","in the case of **foreign currency remittance**, the tax due shall incur **interest at the prevailing Secured Overnight Financing Rate (\"SOFR\")** or any successor rate, **plus a spread** to be determined by the Minister, from the date when the tax becomes payable until it is paid, and the provisions of this Act relating to collection and recovery of tax shall apply to the collection and recovery of the interest."]},{"p":"**(b)** The relevant tax authority shall **serve a demand notice** upon the company or person in whose name a tax is chargeable, and where payment is not made **within 30 days from the date of the service** of such demand notice, the relevant tax authority may **proceed to enforce payment** under this Act."},{"p":"**(c)** The penalty and interest imposed under this subsection **shall not be deemed to be part of the tax paid** for the purpose of claiming relief under any of the provisions of this Act or any other tax law."},{"key":"Three separate charges, and the exam wants all three named: a **one-off 10% surcharge** on the tax, **running interest** from the due date to payment, and — if the demand notice expires unpaid — the **enforcement machinery of §4.10** (distraint, substitution, assignment of the debt). The interest benchmark depends on the **currency of remittance**, which ties back to §4.9.2: tax on foreign-currency income is payable in that currency, so it carries a foreign-currency rate (SOFR), not the CBN MPR."},{"note":"Limb (c) is a quiet trap. The 10% and the interest are **not tax**, so they can never be counted as tax paid when computing any relief, credit or set-off. A company that remits N11m against a N10m liability has paid N10m of tax and N1m of penalty, and only the N10m enters any relief computation. *(Source: §4.12.1.)*"},{"warn":"The study text heads this provision \"**section 65 of NTA**\" in §4.12.1 but \"**section 65 of NTAA**\" in the solution to this question. The latter is correct: late-payment penalties are administration, and sit in the Nigeria Tax **Administration** Act, 2025."}]}}]}],"formulas":[],"focus":"This chapter is the most penalty-figure-dense in the subject — the exam draws directly on specific Naira amounts and day/month multipliers, so treat the offences/penalties table as a genuine memorisation target, not just background reading. Beyond the figures, the structural distinctions matter most: self-assessment vs. additional assessment vs. best-of-judgement assessment vs. deemed profit assessment are four DIFFERENT assessment mechanisms triggered by different circumstances (voluntary compliance, discovered under-assessment, no return filed, no ascertainable profit, respectively) — know which applies when. Also know the specific day/month counts that recur throughout (30 days for most payment/notice deadlines, 6 years for most limitation periods, 90 days for refunds) as a set, since the exam likes testing whether a specific timeframe has been correctly recalled.","errors":["Confusing additional assessment (correcting a discovered under-assessment, 6-year limit unless deliberate misstatement) with best-of-judgement assessment (assessing where NO return was ever filed) — they are triggered by different taxpayer behaviour and have different statutory bases (s.36 vs. s.35 NTAA).","Assuming the 6-year assessment/refund/recovery limitation period is absolute — it is explicitly waived where the under-assessment or erroneous repayment resulted from an untrue document or statement, and also does not prevent continuing an audit that began before the 6 years expired.","Treating distraint as an immediate sale power — distrained assets must first be kept 14 days, and any actual sale (except of already-perishable-type goods addressed elsewhere) requires a High Court order, especially for immovable property.","Believing a scope limitation on document access always requires a warrant — free access to land/buildings/books applies \"at all reasonable times\" without a warrant; a warrant is specifically required only for entering a PRIVATE DWELLING without the occupier's consent.","Missing that the late-payment penalty/interest (10% surcharge plus CBN MPR or SOFR-based interest) is explicitly NEVER treated as part of the tax paid for relief-claim purposes — it cannot be used to reduce a taxpayer's claimed relief elsewhere."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"A taxable entity fails to register with the relevant tax authority. What administrative penalty applies in the first month of default?","o":["N25,000","N50,000","N100,000","N5,000,000","No penalty applies in the first month"],"a":1,"w":"Section 100 NTAA sets the first-month penalty for failure to register at N50,000, stepping down to N25,000 for each subsequent month of continued failure.","src":"Chapter 4.7","sec":"4.7"},{"q":"Within how many days must a taxpayer notify the relevant tax authority of a change in particulars, such as a change of business address?","o":["7 days","14 days","21 days","30 days","90 days"],"a":3,"w":"Section 9 NTAA requires notification of any change in particulars within 30 days of its occurrence — the same 30-day window recurs for the related failure-to-notify penalty under s.112.","src":"Chapter 4.2","sec":"4.2"},{"q":"Which assessment mechanism applies where a company produces no assessable profit, or a profit less than expected, and the true amount cannot be readily ascertained?","o":["Additional assessment","Best of judgement (BOJ) assessment","Deemed profit assessment","Self-assessment","Undistributed profits assessment"],"a":2,"w":"Deemed profit assessment under s.38 NTAA is specifically designed for this scenario — a resident is assessed on a fair/reasonable percentage of gross income, while a non-resident's profit is deemed via applying their profit margin to Nigerian turnover.","src":"Chapter 4.8","sec":"4.8"},{"q":"Within how many days of the decision must the relevant tax authority pay a due tax refund, under s.55 of the NTAA?","o":["30 days","60 days","90 days","6 months","1 year"],"a":2,"w":"Section 55 NTAA specifically sets a 90-day refund payment window from the date of the authority's decision, with the option of setting it off against other tax liability.","src":"Chapter 4.9","sec":"4.9"},{"q":"A person who demands or accepts gratification from a taxable person while performing official duties under the NTAA is liable, on conviction, to","o":["A fine equivalent to 50% of the sum in question only","A fine equivalent to 200% of the sum in question, or imprisonment for up to 3 years, or both","A fixed fine of N100,000 only","Dismissal from service only, with no fine or imprisonment","A fine equivalent to 100% of the sum in question only"],"a":1,"w":"Section 114 NTAA (offences by authorised/unauthorised persons) sets this specific penalty — a fine of 200% of the sum in question, or imprisonment for up to 3 years, or both — for demanding/accepting gratification and related misconduct by tax officials.","src":"Chapter 4.12","sec":"4.12"}],"theory":[{"q":"Explain the self-assessment system of tax administration and state its benefits.","marks":8,"a":[{"p":"Self-assessment is a system where the taxpayer is legally granted the right to compute their own tax liability, submit their return to the relevant tax authority, pay the tax due, and file the return with evidence of payment, by the due date. A taxpayer who submits a self-assessment return is deemed to have assessed the amount of tax payable (including a nil or negative amount) for that period."},{"ol":["Improved tax compliance — taxpayers take responsibility for their own obligations.","Reduced administrative burden on the tax authority, since taxpayers compute and report their own liabilities.","Increased transparency, as taxpayers must disclose their financial information.","Efficient tax collection, since taxpayers pay directly.","Reduced tax evasion, since taxpayers are more likely to report true income.","Improved taxpayer education, encouraging taxpayers to seek professional advice when needed."]}],"src":"Chapter 4.8","sec":"4.8"},{"q":"State the circumstances under which an assessment becomes final and conclusive, and explain the consequence of not paying tax under such an assessment within 30 days.","marks":6,"a":[{"p":"Under s.43 NTAA, an assessment becomes final and conclusive where: no valid objection/appeal is lodged within the stipulated 30 days; the total income/profits amount has been agreed; the amount has been determined or revised on objection; or the assessment has been agreed, revised or determined on appeal."},{"p":"Where the full amount of tax under a final and conclusive assessment is not paid within 30 days, the provisions relating to recovery of tax and to penalty apply to its collection and recovery — subject only to set-off of any agreed or appeal-determined refund claim."}],"src":"Chapter 4.8","sec":"4.8"}]}},{"n":5,"t":"Objections and Appeal Procedures","brief":"The full dispute-resolution ladder available once a tax assessment is in question: advance rulings (avoiding disputes before they arise), amicable settlement, the newly-created Office of the Tax Ombud, the formal objection procedure against an assessment, and the three-tier appeal path — Tax Appeal Tribunal, Federal High Court, Court of Appeal — each with its own strict timelines and procedural rules under the NTAA 2025 and the Joint Revenue Board of Nigeria (Establishment) Act (JRBEA) 2025.","outcomes":["Understand the need for a relevant tax authority to make an advance ruling on a provision of a tax law","Understand the procedure for settlement of a dispute between the relevant tax authority and the taxable person","Explain the relevance of the establishment of the Office of the Tax Ombud","Explain the procedures available to a taxpayer for raising objections to tax assessments","Know how to file an appeal before the Tax Appeal Tribunal","Explain how the hearing of an appeal is conducted","Know the other appeal options available"],"secs":[{"n":"5.2","t":"Advance rulings (ss.73-76 NTAA)","b":[{"def":{"t":"Advance ruling (s.147 NTAA)","d":"any ruling issued by the tax authority on a disputed or controversial tax matter, in accordance with this Act, the Nigeria Tax Act or any other tax law enacted by the National Assembly; or any written opinion/decision issued to a taxpayer on a transaction, proposed transaction or tax matter, to provide direction or clarification."}},{"h4":"5.2.1  Issuance of advance rulings — section 73 of NTAA"},{"p":"A relevant tax authority may issue an advance ruling for clarity, consistency and certainty regarding the interpretation/application of a tax law — provided this does not amount to amending or replacing the law. It may make a ruling on any provision of a tax law, administration, precedent or policy, and must **issue the ruling within 21 days** of receiving an application, or give written reasons for its inability to do so."},{"p":"An advance ruling must be signed by an authorised officer and state:"},{"ol":["whether the ruling applies generally or is limited to the applicant taxpayer;","the name, Tax ID and postal address of the taxpayer;","the relevant statutory provisions or legal issues addressed;","any assumptions made or conditions imposed on the ruling's validity; and","the period for which the ruling is valid."]},{"p":"An application (by a single party or jointly by two or more parties to the issue/transaction) must contain: the taxpayer's name/Tax ID/postal address/email/phone; a complete description of the tax issue/transaction (including financial implications); a complete description of the impact on the taxpayer's (or a connected person's) tax liability; details of any ongoing audit and prior correspondence/decisions on the issue; the relevant statutory/legal basis relied on; reasons the ruling should be granted; and a written statement on whether the matter is before any tribunal/court. The authority may request more information, must give the applicant a reasonable opportunity to make representations, and the applicant may withdraw the application at any time before a ruling issues."},{"h4":"5.2.2  Rejection of application for advance rulings — section 74 of NTAA"},{"p":"The authority may reject an application requiring an opinion/conclusion/determination on: the application or interpretation of foreign law; an issue already before a competent court/tribunal; interpretation of the Constitution; an issue that is academic, hypothetical, frivolous or vexatious; or a ruling that would substantially interfere with an ongoing audit/investigation/proceeding."},{"h4":"5.2.3  Effect of advance rulings — section 75 of NTAA"},{"key":"A ruling binds only the applicant and the specific transaction ruled on — it cannot be of general application. It is **void** where: the actual issue/transaction is materially different from the one described in the ruling; there was fraud, misrepresentation or non-disclosure of a material fact; or a stated assumption/condition is not satisfied. It **ceases to be effective** (without being void from the start) where the ruled-on provision is later repealed/amended in a materially-affecting way (effective from the amendment date), or a court overturns/modifies the interpretation it rested on (effective from the judgement date, unless that decision is itself overturned)."},{"h4":"5.2.4  Withdrawal or modification of advance rulings — section 76 of NTAA"},{"p":"The authority must withdraw or modify a ruling — retrospectively — where it was **fraudulently obtained**, and may specify the date its withdrawal/modification decision takes effect."}]},{"n":"5.3","t":"Settlement of disputes between the relevant tax authority and a taxable person (s.141 NTAA)","b":[{"p":"Without prejudice to any other provision, the tax authority and a taxable person may, at any stage of a dispute, resolve tax matters amicably, subject to prescribed terms and conditions."},{"h4":"When settlement is available"},{"p":"The authority may settle a dispute, wholly or partly, where:"},{"ol":["settlement serves the interest of public revenue or public policy;","due consideration has been given to litigation cost against possible benefit;","a participant (or group) in a tax arrangement has accepted the authority's position, so the settlement adjusts the arrangement/disposition accordingly; or","under a whistleblowing arrangement, settlement will facilitate full disclosure of undisclosed tax planning/evasion schemes leading to significant tax recovery."]},{"warn":"Settlement is **not** available where the dispute arises from intentional tax evasion/fraud inimical to government revenue, or where judicial clarification is in the public interest and would significantly promote taxpayer compliance."},{"h4":"Settlement procedure and finality"},{"ol":["A participant must disclose all relevant facts during the settlement discussion phase — facts disclosed solely for settlement purposes are **confidential**;","a settlement (whole or partial) must be evidenced by a written agreement, in the prescribed format, covering: how each issue is settled, relevant undertakings, treatment of the issue in future years, withdrawal of objections/appeals, and payment arrangements; and","the agreement must be signed by authorised officers of both parties."]},{"key":"A settlement agreement is the parties' **final** agreed position on all or the specified aspects of the dispute — the authority enforces collection of the settled amount as a debt, under the Act's ordinary collection provisions."}]},{"n":"5.4","t":"The Tax Ombud","b":[{"h4":"5.4.1  Establishment of the Office of the Tax Ombud — section 36 of the Joint Revenue Board of Nigeria (Establishment) Act (JRBEA), 2025"},{"key":"Established by **s.36 of the Joint Revenue Board of Nigeria (Establishment) Act (JRBEA) 2025** as a body corporate — with perpetual succession, a common seal, power to sue/be sued in its corporate name, and power to own/dispose of movable or immovable property. Its head office is in the FCT, with **at least one branch office in each of the six geo-political zones**."},{"h4":"5.4.2  Appointment of the Tax Ombud — section 37 of JRBEA"},{"p":"The Tax Ombud is appointed by the **President**, on the Minister's recommendation — must be a Nigerian citizen with relevant qualifications and **at least 10 years' cognate experience** in taxation, law, accounting, auditing, administration or dispute resolution, and holds office for a **4-year term, renewable once** (max 2 terms). The Tax Ombud is the Office's Chief Executive and Accounting Officer."},{"h4":"5.4.3  Cessation of tenure of the Tax Ombud — section 38 of JRBEA"},{"p":"Tenure ceases where the Tax Ombud: resigns by written notice to the President; becomes incapable through infirmity of mind or body; becomes bankrupt or compromises with creditors; is convicted of a felony or an offence involving dishonesty/fraud; is removed by the President in the public interest; is found guilty of contravening the Code of Conduct Bureau and Tribunal Act or of gross misconduct; or is disqualified by a professional body from the qualification that made them eligible for the appointment."},{"h4":"5.4.4  Oath administration and document authentication — section 40 of JRBEA"},{"p":"**Oath administration** (s.40 JRBEA): an Office officer may administer oaths and authenticate affidavits/affirmations/declarations — such an authenticated document is accepted as legal evidence with no further proof of signature, seal or official capacity required."},{"h4":"5.4.5  Powers and functions of the Office of the Tax Ombud — section 41 of JRBEA"},{"p":"The Office of the Tax Ombud may:"},{"ol":["serve as an independent, impartial arbiter reviewing/resolving complaints on tax, levy, regulatory fee/charge, customs duty or excise matters;","review complaints against tax officials/authorities and resolve them by mediation or conciliation, using informal, fair, cost-effective procedures;","receive and investigate taxpayer complaints about the actions/decisions of tax authorities, agencies or their officials;","enter and inspect any premises where a tax authority/agency/official performs a function, for investigation purposes;","invite and examine any person with relevant information or evidence;","make recommendations to revenue authorities/government agencies for implementation;","institute legal proceedings on the taxpayer's behalf;","provide information and raise awareness of taxpayer rights/obligations, tax-authority functions, and the Office's own role;","identify and review systemic/emerging fiscal-policy issues in collaboration with relevant agencies;","serve as a watchdog against arbitrary government fiscal policy and report it to the National Assembly;","issue guidelines, directives or orders for resolving complaints/implementing recommendations; and","delegate any power or function to any of its own officers."]},{"note":"The Office **may not charge a fee** for exercising its functions."},{"h4":"5.4.6  Conflict of interest — section 42 of JRBEA"},{"p":"An officer of the Office must: act in utmost good faith with care/skill/diligence; maintain independence and impartiality at all times; never act where personal interest conflicts with the Office's functions; disclose any conflict of interest and refrain from the affected matter; never make secret profit; never accept a gift/advantage for anything done or to be done in their official capacity; and may publish studies/research/findings/recommendations/insights/proposals on matters under the Office's consideration."},{"h4":"5.4.7  Limitations on the authority of the Office of the Tax Ombud — section 43 of JRBEA"},{"warn":"The Office of the Tax Ombud may **not**: interpret tax legislation beyond operational/procedural/administrative issues arising from its application; review or determine an issue already sub judice before a court/tribunal at the date the complaint is received; determine any tax liability or duty, or issue a tax assessment; or review a tax official's complaint concerning a personal grievance relating to their own office."},{"h4":"5.4.8  Procedure of the Office of the Tax Ombud — Third Schedule to JRBEA"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Stage","Key rules"],"rows":[["**Complaints** (¶1)","Lodged in writing or via any Office-provided platform — only once the issue is **unresolved by the relevant agencies**. The Office reviews for jurisdiction/mandate, does **not entertain anonymous complaints**, may strike out a false/misleading complaint, and refers any complaint alleging criminal malicious misrepresentation to law enforcement."],["**Investigation** (¶2)","Initiated by engaging the complainant/officer/agency for more information, or via site visits/interviews/inspections. Must be conducted within **14 days** of complaint receipt, extendable by a further **7 days**. Does not affect any action the relevant agency has taken or will take. If the Office decides not to investigate, it must communicate reasons to the complainant within 7 days."],["**Resolution and recommendations** (¶3)","Resolved by recommendations to the agency/complainant. Where the complaint amounts to abuse of office/breach of procedure, recommendations go to the tax authority within **14 days** of determination. Where criminal proceedings appear warranted, the matter is referred to the agency for action — if the agency fails to act within **30 days**, the Office refers it directly to law enforcement."],["**Non-compliance with recommendations** (¶4)","The agency must report to the Office within **21 days** of receiving recommendations, stating reasons for non-compliance. Persistent unexplained non-compliance is referred to the National Assembly / State House of Assembly for oversight."],["**Dispute resolution process** (¶5)","The Office may encourage alternative dispute resolution. Parties/representatives must appear when requested and must not impede the Office or prejudice a pending matter — failure is treated as the complaint being **resolved against that party**."]]}}]},{"n":"5.5","t":"Revision of assessment in case of objection / time limit for objection and appeal — section 41 of NTAA","b":[{"key":"A company disputing a tax assessment may give a **written** notice of objection — delivered in person, by courier, or electronically — to the NRS, seeking revision/amendment. The time limit for objection is **30 days** from the date the disputed assessment notice was served."}]},{"n":"5.6","t":"Contents of a notice of objection — section 41(2) of NTAA","b":[{"p":"A valid notice of objection must:"},{"ol":["be in writing, delivered in person, by courier or electronic mail, addressed to the Chairman, NRS; and","state the grounds of objection — specifically: the specific issues disputed or errors observed, with their monetary values; the amendment required to resolve the dispute/correct the error; justification for the amendments; the amount of assessable/total profits, income or transaction value the taxpayer admits for the reporting period; and the amount of tax admitted as payable, or that no amount is admitted."]},{"note":"The authority may, for satisfactory and good cause shown, extend the time for making the objection to a reasonable period in the circumstances."}]},{"n":"5.7","t":"Revision of assessment in case of objection — section 41 of NTAA","b":[{"p":"On receiving the notice of objection, the tax authority may: require the taxable person to furnish further particulars and produce all relevant books/documents; or summon any person to give evidence — appearing for examination before an authorised officer, or making a declaration on oath."},{"p":"Where the taxpayer and authority **agree** on the tax amount, the disputed assessment is amended and a revised notice of tax payable is served."},{"key":"The tax authority must respond to the objection within **90 days** — otherwise the taxpayer's objection is **automatically upheld**. Where the authority considers the notice invalid, or the parties cannot agree on the tax amount, the taxpayer may exercise the **right of appeal** to the Tax Appeal Tribunal."}]},{"n":"5.8","t":"Hearing before the Tax Appeal Tribunal","b":[{"h4":"5.8.1  Appeals from the decisions of the relevant tax authority — section 35 of JRBEA and the second paragraph of the Second Schedule"},{"p":"A taxable entity aggrieved by an assessment, demand notice, action, decision, or a residency dispute, may appeal to the Tribunal within the stipulated period, in the prescribed form and with the prescribed fee."},{"ol":["The appeal must be filed within **30 days** of the date the decision being appealed was made (or deemed made);","the Tribunal may still entertain a late appeal if satisfied there was **sufficient cause for the delay**;","where no notice of appeal is given within the period, the assessment/demand notice becomes **final and conclusive**, and the authority may charge interest/penalties in addition to recovering the outstanding liability; and","a relevant tax authority itself aggrieved by a taxable entity's non-compliance may appeal to the Tribunal in the Zone where the taxpayer is resident (2nd Schedule ¶3)."]},{"h4":"5.8.3 – 5.8.5  Special appeal procedures — Second Schedule, paragraphs 4 to 6"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Procedure","Rule"],"rows":[["**5.8.3 Liquidated money claim affidavit** (¶4)","Where the appeal is to recover a debt/liquidated money demand, supported by an affidavit stating there is no defence, the Tribunal — if satisfied there are good grounds — enters it for hearing as a \"Liquidated Money Claim Appeal\"."],["**5.8.4 Appeal by summons** (¶5)","Where the issue is purely one of interpretation of tax law/rules/regulations, without dispute of fact, the Tribunal hears and determines the appeal by **affidavit evidence**."],["**5.8.5 Settlement out of Tribunal** (¶6)","On an appeal's first appearance, the Chairman may grant the parties **up to 30 days** to explore settlement. The case proceeds to trial if they fail to settle within that (or an extended) period."]]}},{"h4":"5.8.6  Procedure before the Tax Appeal Tribunal — Second Schedule, paragraph 7"},{"ol":["Tax Appeal Commissioners meet as often as necessary to hear appeals in their assigned zone;","the Secretary gives **7 clear days'** notice of the hearing date/place (not needed for an adjourned hearing already fixed at a prior sitting);","notices/documents (other than Tribunal decisions) may be signified under the Secretary's hand;","hearings are held **in public**;","the **burden of proof** that an assessment is excessive (or due for payment) lies on the appellant;","the Tribunal may **adjourn** and order the appellant to deposit, before the adjourned hearing, the **lesser of** the tax charged for the preceding assessment year or **one half** of the tax charged by the assessment under appeal — triggered where the authority proves the appellant failed to file returns, the appeal is frivolous/vexatious/abusive, or a security deposit is otherwise expedient;","failure to comply with that deposit order means the assessment is **confirmed**, with **no further right of appeal** to the Tribunal on it;","after giving the parties a hearing, the Tribunal may **confirm, reduce, increase or annul** the assessment, or make any order it deems fit;","the Chairman records every decision in writing — a certified copy is supplied on request within **7 days** of the decision; and","where no accounts/books were produced, or those produced were rejected as incomplete/unsatisfactory, or the appellant/representative neglected a Secretary's notice, or refused (without reasonable cause) to answer a Tribunal question — other than through a legal practitioner or accountant acting for them — the Chairman records these particulars in the written decision."]},{"h4":"5.8.7 – 5.8.11  Further Tribunal rules — Second Schedule, paragraphs 8 to 14"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Rule","Detail"],"rows":[["**5.8.7 Evidence Act** (¶8)","Tribunal proceedings/decisions must comply with the Evidence Act."],["**5.8.8 Procedure following decision of the Tribunal** (¶9)","Notice of the Tribunal-determined tax chargeable is served by the tax authority on the taxpayer (or the chargeable person). An award/judgement is enforced as a Federal High Court judgement once registered with the Chief Registrar by the enforcing party."],["**5.8.9 Right to representation** (¶11)","A party may appear in person or through legal practitioners, a suitably qualified tax professional, or an officer. If a representative cannot attend for good cause, the Tribunal may adjourn, or admit the appeal by another person or by written address."],["**5.8.10 Application of statutes of limitation** (¶12)","The Act's statute of limitation/pre-action-notice rules and the Public Officer Protection Act do **not** apply to appeals before the Tribunal."],["**5.8.11 Costs** (¶14) — printed in the book under the heading *\"Oaths\"*","Each party bears its **own cost** of the appeal."]]}}]},{"n":"5.9","t":"Appeal to the Federal High Court (2nd Schedule, ¶10 JRBEA)","b":[{"ol":["A person dissatisfied with a Tribunal decision may appeal on a **point of law** to the Federal High Court, giving written notice to the Tribunal Secretary within **30 days** of the decision;","the notice of appeal must set out the **grounds of law** the appeal is based on;","the relevant tax authority, similarly dissatisfied, may likewise appeal on points of law within the same 30-day window;","on receiving the notice, the Secretary forwards it — with all exhibits tendered at the Tribunal hearing — to the Chief Registrar of the High Court; and","the Chief Judge may make rules for the procedure of such appeals; until then, ordinary High Court appeal-hearing rules apply."]}]},{"n":"5.10","t":"Further appeal to the Court of Appeal (2nd Schedule, ¶15 JRBEA)","b":[{"key":"An appeal against a Federal High Court decision, at the instance of **either party**, lies to the **Court of Appeal** — the final rung of this appeal ladder covered in the syllabus."}]},{"n":"5.11","t":"Chapter review","b":[{"p":"This chapter dealt with the procedure for raising an objection to a tax assessment served on a company/taxpayer by the NRS. The options available to an aggrieved appellant are: appeal to the Tax Appeal Tribunal, and — if no relief is obtained — appeal to the Federal High Court. Beyond the Federal High Court, an appellant may proceed to the Court of Appeal."}]},{"n":"5.12","t":"Worked examples — study text questions and suggested solutions","b":[{"p":"The study text sets **three examination-type questions** at §5.12.1, with its own suggested solutions at §5.12.2. All three are reproduced here in full, with the book's answers and added explanation."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 5.12.1, Question 1","t":"Particulars that must be included in a notice of appeal","open":true,"q":[{"p":"There are specific particulars that must be included in a notice of appeal against an assessment pending before the Tax Appeal Tribunal. **Required:** Explain the specific particulars that must be included in a notice of appeal against an assessment pending before the Tax Appeal Tribunal."}],"a":[{"p":"A notice of appeal shall specify the following specific particulars:"},{"ol":["the **official number of the assessment** and the **year of assessment** for which it was made;","the **amount of the tax charged** by such assessment;","the **amount of the total profit** upon which such tax was charged, as appearing in the notice of assessment;","the **date upon which the appellant was served with notice of refusal** by the revenue authority to amend the assessment as desired;","the **precise grounds of law** on which the appellant's case is based;","an **address for service** of notices, receipts or other documents to be given by the Secretary to the Tax Appeal Tribunal — provided that at any time the appellant may give notice to such Secretary and to the NRS, by delivering the same by registered post, of a change of such address, but any such notice shall not be valid until delivered or received; and","**evidence of payment of the lesser of** the tax charged upon the appellant for the **preceding assessment year** or **50% of the tax charged** by the assessment under appeal."]},{"key":"Item (g) is the one candidates forget, and it is the one with teeth. The appellant must pay something **before** the appeal is heard — the **lesser of** last year's tax or **half** of the disputed tax. It is the same deposit the Tribunal may order under §5.8.6, and the sanction for not paying is severe: the assessment is **confirmed with no further right of appeal to the Tribunal**. An appeal is therefore never a way of deferring payment entirely."},{"warn":"The book's solution writes \"**notice of refusal by the FIRS**\". Under the 2025 Acts the Federal Inland Revenue Service has been succeeded by the **Nigeria Revenue Service (NRS)** — which the same solution then uses two lines later in item (f). Write **NRS**."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 5.12.1, Question 2","t":"Procedure for an appeal against a decision of the Tax Appeal Tribunal","open":true,"q":[{"p":"There are laid-down procedures for an appeal against a decision of a Tax Appeal Tribunal. **Required:** Explain the procedure for an appeal against the decision of a Tax Appeal Tribunal."}],"a":[{"p":"**Appeal against the decision of the Tax Appeal Tribunal.**"},{"ol":["Where a person is **dissatisfied with a decision of the Tribunal** constituted under this Act, he may appeal against such decision **on a point of law** to the **Federal High Court**, upon giving notice in writing to the **Secretary to the Tribunal within 30 days** after the date on which such decision was given.","A notice of appeal filed pursuant to (a) above shall **set out the grounds of law** on which the appellant's case is based.","Where the **relevant tax authority** is dissatisfied with the decision of the Tribunal, it may appeal against such decision to the High Court **on points of law** by giving notice in writing as specified in (a) above, to the Secretary **within 30 days** after the date on which such decision was given.","On receipt of a notice of appeal under (a) and (c) above, the **Secretary to the Tribunal** shall cause the notice to be given to the **Chief Registrar of the High Court**, along with **all the exhibits** tendered at the hearing before the Tribunal.","The **Chief Judge of the High Court may make rules** providing for the procedure in respect of appeals made under this Act, and until such rules are made, the **High Court rules relating to hearing of appeals** shall apply."]},{"p":"Where the appellant or the relevant tax authority is **not satisfied with the decision of the Federal High Court**, an appeal shall lie to the **Court of Appeal**."},{"key":"Two features define the whole appellate route. First, it is **symmetrical** — the tax authority has exactly the same 30-day right of appeal as the taxpayer. Second, beyond the Tribunal the appeal lies **only on a point of law**: findings of *fact* are settled at the Tribunal, which is precisely why §5.8.6 places the **burden of proof** on the appellant there, and why the Tribunal — not the court — is the stage at which accounts and books must be produced. *(Sources: §5.9 and §5.10.)*"}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 5.12.1, Question 3","t":"Advising a client whose assessment is higher than the documents support","open":true,"q":[{"p":"As a tax consultant, the Managing Director of your client brought an assessment notice served on the company by the Nigeria Revenue Service, complaining that the assessment was outrageous. You observed that the amount assessed was higher than what is supposed to be, based on the documents submitted. **Required:** Explain what could be done in this circumstance."}],"a":[{"p":"The actions to be taken to remedy the disputed tax assessment:"},{"steps":["The tax consultant will **review the accounts and documents** which were earlier sent to the tax office, to determine whether there was **any basis for the variation** in the assessments. If there is any **genuine reason** for the increase in the assessment, the taxpayer will be advised **not to pursue** the disputed assessment, but to **make payment without any further delay**.","However, if there is **no ground or basis** for the variation in the assessment, then a **valid notice of objection** will be written to the NRS, **within 30 days** of the receipt of the notice of assessment. The notice of objection will contain the **grounds of objection**.","The tax consultant will expect the NRS to **see reasons and amend the assessment**, which if satisfactory would be remitted. On the other hand, where the NRS **refuses to revise and amend** the assessment, the taxpayer may **exercise the right to appeal**."]},{"key":"Where the relevant tax office **fails to respond to the objection within 90 days, the objection of the taxpayer shall be upheld.** This is the deadline the examiner is really testing, and it cuts in the taxpayer's favour: silence is not neutral. *(Source: §5.5.)*"},{"note":"Notice the order of the answer, which is where the marks sit. The first duty is **not** to object — it is to **check whether the Revenue is right**. Advising a client to object to an assessment that the documents actually support would waste the client's money, delay payment, and expose the client to the late-payment penalty and interest of §4.12.1. Only once the review shows no basis for the variation does the 30-day objection clock become the thing to act on."}]}}]}],"formulas":[],"focus":"This chapter is a sequence of strict, numbered deadlines that the exam tests directly — keep the full set straight as a single mental timeline: 21 days for an authority to issue (or refuse) an advance ruling; 30 days to file a notice of objection against an assessment; 90 days for the authority to respond to that objection (silence = the objection is upheld); 30 days to appeal a Tribunal decision to the Federal High Court; and, within Tax Ombud procedure specifically, 14 days to investigate a complaint (extendable by 7), 14 days to report abuse-of-office findings, 21 days for an agency to explain non-compliance with a recommendation, and 30 days before an unactioned criminal matter is escalated to law enforcement. Also keep the three-tier appeal path itself firmly in order: Tax Appeal Tribunal → Federal High Court (points of law only) → Court of Appeal.","errors":["Confusing the 30-day objection deadline (against an assessment, to the NRS) with the separate 30-day appeal deadline (against a Tribunal decision, to the Federal High Court) — both are 30 days, but they run from different triggering events and go to different bodies.","Treating the Tax Ombud as able to determine tax liability or issue assessments — s.43 JRBEA explicitly denies the Office this power; it can only investigate, mediate and recommend, never adjudicate the underlying tax debt itself.","Assuming an appeal to the Federal High Court can raise any ground — it is expressly restricted to **points of law**, not fresh factual disputes already dealt with by the Tribunal.","Missing that failure to comply with a Tribunal security-deposit order does not just delay the appeal — it causes the assessment to be **confirmed outright**, with no further right of appeal to the Tribunal on that assessment.","Forgetting that if the tax authority simply does not respond to a valid notice of objection within 90 days, the objection is automatically upheld — inaction favours the taxpayer, it does not leave the assessment standing by default."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Within how many days of receiving an application must a relevant tax authority issue an advance ruling, or give written reasons for its inability to do so?","o":["7 days","14 days","21 days","30 days","90 days"],"a":2,"w":"Section 73 NTAA requires the tax authority to issue an advance ruling within 21 days of receiving the application, or provide written reasons for its inability to do so.","src":"Chapter 5.2","sec":"5.2"},{"q":"What is the time limit for a company to give a written notice of objection against a disputed tax assessment, from the date of service of the assessment notice?","o":["7 days","14 days","21 days","30 days","90 days"],"a":3,"w":"Section 41 NTAA sets the objection deadline at 30 days from the date the disputed notice of assessment was served on the taxpayer.","src":"Chapter 5.4","sec":"5.4"},{"q":"Where the relevant tax authority fails to respond to a valid notice of objection within the statutory period, what is the consequence?","o":["The assessment automatically becomes final and conclusive","The taxpayer's objection is automatically upheld","The matter is automatically referred to the Tax Ombud","The taxpayer forfeits the right to appeal","Nothing — the taxpayer must resubmit the objection"],"a":1,"w":"Section 41 NTAA gives the authority 90 days to respond to a notice of objection — if it fails to do so within that period, the taxpayer's objection is automatically upheld.","src":"Chapter 5.4","sec":"5.4"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT within the powers of the Office of the Tax Ombud, under s.43 of the JRBEA?","o":["Receiving and investigating taxpayer complaints against tax officials","Making recommendations to revenue authorities for implementation","Determining a taxpayer's tax liability or issuing a tax assessment","Entering and inspecting premises where a tax authority performs its functions","Serving as a watchdog against arbitrary fiscal policy"],"a":2,"w":"Section 43 JRBEA explicitly excludes the Office of the Tax Ombud from determining any tax liability or duty, or issuing a tax assessment — it is a review/mediation/recommendation body, not an adjudicating tax authority.","src":"Chapter 5.4","sec":"5.4"},{"q":"On what basis, and within what timeframe, may a party appeal against a decision of the Tax Appeal Tribunal to the Federal High Court?","o":["On any ground, within 14 days","On a point of law only, within 30 days","On a point of fact only, within 60 days","On any ground, within 90 days","On a point of law only, within 21 days"],"a":1,"w":"Paragraph 10 of the Second Schedule to JRBEA allows an appeal to the Federal High Court on a point of law only, by written notice to the Tribunal Secretary within 30 days of the decision.","src":"Chapter 5.9","sec":"5.9"}],"theory":[{"q":"Explain the specific particulars that must be included in a notice of appeal against an assessment pending before the Tax Appeal Tribunal.","marks":7,"a":[{"p":"The notice of appeal must specify:"},{"ol":["the official number of the assessment and the year of assessment it was made for;","the amount of tax charged by the assessment;","the amount of total profit upon which the tax was charged, as it appears in the notice of assessment;","the date the appellant was served with notice of the tax authority's refusal to amend the assessment as desired;","the precise grounds of law on which the appellant's case is based;","an address for service of notices/receipts/documents by the Tribunal Secretary — the appellant may later change this address by registered-post notice to the Secretary and the tax authority, effective only once delivered/received; and","evidence of payment of the lesser of the tax charged for the preceding assessment year, or 50% of the tax charged by the assessment under appeal."]}],"src":"Chapter 5.12","sec":"5.8"},{"q":"As a tax consultant, your client's Managing Director brings you an assessment notice from the NRS, complaining that the amount assessed is far higher than the figures supported by the documents submitted. Explain what could be done in this circumstance.","marks":6,"a":[{"ol":["First, review the accounts/documents earlier submitted to the tax office to determine whether there is any genuine basis for the variation. If there is a genuine reason for the higher assessment, advise the taxpayer not to pursue a dispute, but to pay without further delay.","If there is no genuine ground or basis for the variation, write a valid notice of objection to the NRS within 30 days of receiving the assessment notice, stating the grounds of objection.","Expect the NRS to review and, if satisfied, amend the assessment accordingly. If the NRS instead refuses to revise/amend the assessment, the taxpayer may exercise the right of appeal to the Tax Appeal Tribunal."]},{"note":"Where the tax authority fails to respond to the objection within 90 days, the taxpayer's objection is automatically upheld."}],"src":"Chapter 5.12","sec":"5.4"}]}},{"n":6,"t":"Tax Clearance Certificate (TCC)","brief":"A compact but practically critical chapter: what a Tax Clearance Certificate is, the distinct application procedures for a new versus an existing company, the statutory conditions and contents governing its issuance under s.72 NTAA, the real-world problems taxpayers face obtaining one, the specific government/bank transactions that require producing one, and the penalties for procuring one through false information or forged documents.","outcomes":["Define a tax clearance certificate","Explain the procedure for applying for a TCC","Explain the conditions for granting a TCC","Explain the problems of obtaining a TCC","Identify the transactions for which a TCC is required","Explain the penalties for providing false information to obtain a TCC"],"secs":[{"n":"6.2","t":"Definition of TCC","b":[{"def":{"t":"Tax Clearance Certificate (TCC)","d":"a document issued by the Nigeria Revenue Service (in respect of companies) or the States Board (in respect of individuals), showing that the applicant's tax affairs are in order."}},{"key":"A TCC is issued only **upon application** by the taxpayer. In issuing it, the relevant tax authority satisfies itself that the tax assessed on the applicant's income for the **3 years immediately preceding** the current assessment year has been fully paid, or that no tax is due on that income, or that the applicant is not liable to tax for any of the 3 years."},{"note":"The certificate must be issued **within 2 weeks** of demand — otherwise the tax authority must give reasons for the delay or denial. Payment of the **current** year's tax is **not** a condition for issuance, unless the applicant is finally **leaving the country** (s.72 NTAA)."}]},{"n":"6.3","t":"Procedure for applying for a TCC","b":[{"p":"The registration route for TCC issuance depends on whether the applicant is a **new** or an **existing** company."},{"h4":"6.3.1  A new company"},{"steps":["Register with the NRS for a taxpayer identification number (Tax ID) — either via a consultant or an employee, by a formal application letter accompanied by: certificate of incorporation; memorandum and articles of association; Forms CAC 2 and CAC 7; plus registration-letter information covering company name, registered office, business address, precise nature of business, commencement date, bankers' name/address, and particulars of major shareholders.","Register with the NRS for VAT — done **concurrently** with Tax ID registration, via a formal application letter accompanied by a completed VAT Form 001 and the certificate of incorporation. Both steps can now be done online via the FIRS e-service solution.","Validate the issued Tax ID at the local NRS office — a prerequisite for transacting electronically with the NRS; only after validation can the company apply for TCC electronically via the NRS web portal.","Apply for TCC — once the company has uploaded/submitted copies of its statement of affairs, it applies for TCC electronically via the NRS online portal.","Processing and issuance — the NRS approves TCC **within 2 weeks** of application; on approval, an electronic email confirmation is sent and the company downloads its TCC copy."]},{"h4":"6.3.2  An existing company"},{"steps":["Pay income tax and file income tax returns — both electronically and as hard-copy submissions at the NRS office — within the statutory timeline of **6 months after the end of the financial year**.","Apply for TCC — once returns and evidence of tax payment have been uploaded/submitted, apply for TCC electronically via the NRS online portal.","Processing and issuance — the NRS approves TCC **within 2 weeks** of application; on approval, an electronic email confirmation is sent and the company downloads its TCC copy."]}]},{"n":"6.4","t":"Conditions for granting a TCC (s.72(5) NTAA)","b":[{"p":"A TCC is granted only where:"},{"ol":["taxes have been paid **up to date**;","penalties and interest raised have been **paid**;","returns have been filed **up to date**; and","there are **no outstanding** tax queries, audits or investigations."]}]},{"n":"6.5","t":"Contents of TCC (s.72(3) NTAA)","b":[{"p":"A TCC must disclose, for the **last 3 years of assessment**:"},{"ol":["total profits or chargeable income;","tax payable;","tax paid; and","tax outstanding, or a statement that no tax is due."]},{"p":"In addition, a TCC discloses:"},{"ol":["TCC number, tax office, date of issue, company name, RC number, date of incorporation, Tax ID, and effective business address;","revenue/turnover;","source of income;","other comments; and","expiration date."]}]},{"n":"6.6","t":"Problems of obtaining a TCC","b":[{"p":"Although a TCC is issued based on tax compliance for the **preceding** year, tax authorities sometimes insist on compliance up to the date of application itself — a challenge, since the Revenue may use the opportunity to raise additional assessments and demand payment before issuing the certificate."},{"warn":"The certificate often takes far longer to issue than the statutory **2-week** limit, despite that limit being set in legislation. A taxpayer may consequently lose a major contract opportunity, or be unable to clear imported goods, for want of a timely TCC."},{"p":"A further problem is the **authenticity** of TCCs as evidence of compliance — certificates are sometimes issued under questionable circumstances, or forged in extreme cases. The suggested remedies are enhancing the security features of TCCs to prevent forgery, and computerising the process as much as possible to speed up issuance and reduce human intervention."}]},{"n":"6.7","t":"Transactions for which a TCC is required (s.72(4) NTAA)","b":[{"key":"Anyone dealing with a government Ministry/Department/Agency or a commercial bank, in respect of the following transactions, must — on demand — submit a TCC covering the 3 years immediately preceding the current assessment year:"},{"ol":["application for a certificate of occupancy;","approval of building plans;","application for award of a government (or agency) contract;","application for a firearms licence;","application for an import/export licence; and","application for a trade licence."]}]},{"n":"6.8","t":"Penalties","b":[{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Offence","Penalty"],"rows":[["**False information to obtain a TCC** — giving incorrect information affecting one's tax liability, for the purpose of obtaining a TCC (s.124(1) NTAA)","Administrative penalty of **N1,000,000**, plus payment of the tax undercharged/not charged; **on conviction**: a further fine of N1,000,000 or up to **3 years'** imprisonment, or both, plus payment of any unpaid tax or over-claimed repayment."],["**Counterfeiting/falsifying documents** — counterfeiting or falsifying a document required for business transacted under the Act/any tax law, knowingly accepting a counterfeited document, altering a document after official issuance, or forging a seal/signature (s.125 NTAA)","Administrative penalty of **N1,000,000**; **on conviction**: up to **3 years'** imprisonment or a fine of N1,000,000, or both."]]}}]},{"n":"6.9","t":"Chapter review","b":[{"p":"This chapter discussed the procedure for applying for a TCC and the associated problems, the contents of a TCC, the transactions requiring submission of a TCC, and the relevant offences and penalties."}]},{"n":"6.10","t":"Worked examples — study text questions and suggested solutions","b":[{"p":"The study text sets **three questions** at §6.10.1 with suggested solutions at §6.10.2. All three are reproduced here in full with the book's answers and added explanation."},{"note":"A numbering defect in the book, flagged rather than silently corrected: the three questions at §6.10.1 are printed as **\"(1)\", \"(1)\", \"(2)\"** — the first number is used twice and the third question is labelled (2). The suggested solutions at §6.10.2, by contrast, are correctly numbered **(1), (2), (3)** and answer the questions in the order printed. They are numbered 1, 2 and 3 here, matching the solutions."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 6.10.1, Question 1","t":"Transactions for which a tax clearance certificate must be produced","open":true,"q":[{"p":"It is the habit of some taxable individuals or companies to avoid paying tax. One of the measures introduced by the government to bring taxpayers into the tax net was the introduction of the Tax Clearance Certificate. **Required:** List any **three** transactions in respect of which a tax clearance certificate must be produced."}],"a":[{"p":"The transactions in respect of which a tax clearance certificate must be produced are as follows:"},{"ol":["application for a **certificate of occupancy**;","approval of **building plans**;","application for **award of contract** by government or its agencies;","application for a **firearms licence**;","application for an **import and export licence**; and","application for a **trade licence**."]},{"key":"The question asks for **three**, and the book's solution gives six. Give three and stop — marks are awarded per valid item up to the number asked for, and nothing is gained by listing more. The fuller statutory list is at §6.7 (s.72(4) NTAA), which is where to look if a question asks for the complete set rather than a sample."},{"note":"The common thread is worth seeing: every transaction on the list is one where the taxpayer wants something **from the state** — land title, planning permission, a public contract, a licence. The TCC turns each of those into a compliance checkpoint, which is exactly the enforcement design the question's preamble describes."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 6.10.1, Question 2","t":"Procedure for applying for a tax clearance certificate","open":true,"q":[{"p":"A tax clearance certificate is a document issued by the Nigeria Revenue Service to a company, stating that the company's tax affairs are in order at the date of issue. **Required:** Explain the procedure for applying for a tax clearance certificate."}],"a":[{"p":"**Procedure for applying for a tax clearance certificate in Nigeria.** In Nigeria, a Tax Clearance Certificate (TCC) is a document issued by the **Nigeria Revenue Service (NRS)** or **State Internal Revenue Service (SIRS)** to confirm that a taxpayer has fulfilled their tax obligations."},{"p":"The procedure for applying for a TCC by a taxpayer is as follows:"},{"steps":["ensure that **all tax liabilities have been settled**;","verify that **all tax returns have been filed** and that there is **no outstanding tax issue**;","ensure that the **Tax ID is up to date and accurate**;","ensure that **all tax returns for the relevant years** have been completed and submitted;","gather **receipts for all tax payments** made;","submit the application **online through the NRS or SIRS portal**;","alternatively, submit the application **manually at the nearest NRS or SIRS office**;","pay the **application fee online** through the NRS portal;","alternatively, pay the application fee **at a designated bank**;","wait for the application to be **processed** by the NRS or SIRS, which verifies the application and supporting documents — this may take **several days or weeks**; and","once the application is **approved, collect the TCC** from the NRS or SIRS office."]},{"p":"It is pertinent to state that the procedure and requirements for obtaining a TCC **may vary depending on the state or local government**. Taxpayers are advised to consult the relevant tax authority or a tax professional to ensure compliance with all requirements."},{"key":"Note which authority issues the certificate, because the examiner varies it: the **NRS** issues the TCC of a **company**, while a **SIRS** issues the TCC of an **individual** resident in that State. The question's own preamble names the NRS because it speaks of a company."},{"note":"Steps 1 to 5 are **pre-conditions**, not parts of the application — nothing is gained by applying before the returns are filed and the liabilities paid, since §6.4 makes settlement of liabilities a condition of the grant. The distinct procedures for a **new** company and an **existing** company are at §6.3.1 and §6.3.2; a question naming one of those specifically wants that answer rather than this general one."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 6.10.1, Question 3","t":"Conditions for granting a tax clearance certificate","open":true,"q":[{"p":"A TCC is a very important document necessary for various corporate transactions in Nigeria. **Required:** Explain the conditions for granting a tax clearance certificate in Nigeria."}],"a":[{"p":"**Conditions for granting a tax clearance certificate in Nigeria.** In Nigeria, the Nigeria Revenue Service and the State Internal Revenue Service (SIRS) grant tax clearance certificates (TCCs) to taxpayers who meet the following specific conditions:"},{"ol":["the taxpayer must be **registered** with the NRS or SIRS and have a **valid taxpayer identification number (Tax ID)**;","the taxpayer must have **filed all required tax returns**, including income tax, value added tax (VAT) and withholding tax (WHT);","the taxpayer must have **paid all taxes due**, including any **interest and penalties**;","the taxpayer must be **in compliance with all tax laws and regulations**; and","the taxpayer must have **answered all tax queries**, including those relating to **audit and investigation**."]},{"p":"It is apposite to state that the NRS or SIRS **may request additional information or documentation** to verify the taxpayer's compliance with these conditions before issuing a TCC."},{"warn":"The book's solution to this question writes \"registered with the **FIRS** or SIRS and have a valid taxpayer identification number (**TIN**)\". Both terms are superseded: the Federal Inland Revenue Service is now the **Nigeria Revenue Service (NRS)**, and the TIN is now the **Tax ID** (s.7 NTAA — see §4.2.3). The same solution uses \"NRS\" in its own opening line, so the inconsistency is the book's. Write **NRS** and **Tax ID**."},{"key":"Condition (c) is the one to hold on to: it is **all taxes due, including interest and penalties**. A taxpayer who has paid the tax but not the §4.12.1 late-payment surcharge and interest has **not** met the condition and cannot be granted a TCC — which, given the transactions at §6.7, is a far more expensive consequence than the penalty itself. The statutory conditions are at §6.4 (s.72(5) NTAA); this solution is the practical restatement of them."}]}}]}],"formulas":[],"focus":"The two numbers that anchor this entire chapter are 3 and 2 — a TCC certifies compliance for the **3** years preceding the current assessment year (both in its own contents and in the transactions that require producing it), while the authority must issue it within **2 weeks** of demand/application (a deadline flagged in the chapter's own text as commonly missed in practice, even though the statute sets it firmly). Also keep straight that current-year tax payment is explicitly NOT a condition for TCC issuance — except in the one specific case of a taxpayer finally leaving the country — since this is an easy point to get backwards.","errors":["Assuming a TCC requires current-year tax to be paid — s.72 NTAA is explicit that this is NOT a condition for issuance, except where the applicant is finally leaving the country.","Confusing the 2-week statutory issuance deadline with how long the process actually takes in practice — the chapter itself flags that TCCs frequently take much longer than 2 weeks despite the legislated limit, which is exactly why \"problems of obtaining a TCC\" is tested as its own topic.","Mixing up the new-company procedure (starts with Tax ID/VAT registration, since none exists yet) with the existing-company procedure (starts with paying and filing income tax returns, since registration is already done) — they share a final \"apply → process → issue\" tail but begin from genuinely different starting points."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"A tax clearance certificate, once demanded, must be issued within how many weeks?","o":["1 week","2 weeks","4 weeks","6 weeks","8 weeks"],"a":1,"w":"Section 72 NTAA requires the TCC to be issued within 2 weeks of demand — otherwise the tax authority must give reasons for the delay or denial.","src":"Chapter 6.2","sec":"6.2"},{"q":"A TCC discloses total profits, tax payable, tax paid, and tax outstanding for how many years of assessment?","o":["The current year only","The last 2 years","The last 3 years","The last 5 years","The last 6 years"],"a":2,"w":"Section 72(3) NTAA requires a TCC to disclose these particulars for the last 3 years of assessment.","src":"Chapter 6.5","sec":"6.5"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the statutory conditions for granting a TCC under s.72(5) NTAA?","o":["Taxes have been paid up to date","Penalties and interest raised have been paid","Returns have been filed up to date","There are no outstanding tax queries, audit or investigation","The applicant has been in business for at least 5 years"],"a":4,"w":"A minimum period in business is not one of the four conditions in s.72(5) NTAA — the conditions are about up-to-date tax payment, penalties/interest, filed returns, and the absence of outstanding queries/audits/investigations, not business longevity.","src":"Chapter 6.4","sec":"6.4"},{"q":"Is payment of the current year's tax a condition for the issuance of a TCC?","o":["Yes, always","No, never, under any circumstance","No, unless the applicant is finally leaving the country","Yes, but only for companies, not individuals","Only if the taxpayer has a history of default"],"a":2,"w":"Section 72 NTAA explicitly states that payment of the current year's tax is not a condition for TCC issuance, unless the applicant is finally leaving the country.","src":"Chapter 6.2","sec":"6.2"},{"q":"A person who counterfeits a document required for a transaction under the NTAA, or knowingly accepts such a counterfeited document, is liable to an administrative penalty of what amount, in addition to any conviction penalty?","o":["N100,000","N500,000","N1,000,000","N5,000,000","N10,000,000"],"a":2,"w":"Section 125 NTAA sets the administrative penalty for counterfeiting/falsifying documents at N1,000,000, in addition to a possible conviction penalty of up to 3 years' imprisonment or a further N1,000,000 fine, or both.","src":"Chapter 6.8","sec":"6.8"}],"theory":[{"q":"List any three transactions in respect of which a tax clearance certificate must be produced.","marks":3,"a":[{"p":"Any three of the following (s.72(4) NTAA):"},{"ol":["application for a certificate of occupancy;","approval of building plans;","application for award of a government (or agency) contract;","application for a firearms licence;","application for an import/export licence; and","application for a trade licence."]}],"src":"Chapter 6.10","sec":"6.7"},{"q":"Explain the conditions for granting a tax clearance certificate in Nigeria.","marks":5,"a":[{"p":"The relevant tax authority (NRS or SIRS) grants a TCC where the taxpayer meets these conditions:"},{"ol":["taxes have been paid up to date;","penalties and interest raised have been paid;","returns have been filed up to date; and","there are no outstanding tax queries, audit or investigation."]},{"note":"The tax authority may request additional information or documentation to verify the taxpayer's compliance with these conditions before issuing a TCC."}],"src":"Chapter 6.10","sec":"6.4"}]}},{"n":7,"t":"Value Added Tax","brief":"The largest indirect-tax chapter in the subject: VAT's nature and administration, registration/deregistration, filing and remittance, the full definitional framework (goods, services, taxable persons/supplies), VAT invoicing and the new e-invoicing fiscalisation system, place and time of supply, input/output VAT mechanics and credit/refund, the complete exempt/zero-rated/suspended supply lists, VAT computation (with a full worked illustration), record-keeping, withholding by third parties, import/export/restructuring treatment, financial institutions' VAT position, the VAT compliance threshold and inter-government sharing formula, the three VAT model variants, enforcement, merits/demerits, and offences/penalties — all under the Nigeria Tax Act 2025 and NTAA 2025.","outcomes":["Understand the nature, objectives, and administration of VAT in Nigeria","Understand the basic terms and terminologies of VAT","Understand the regulatory framework of VAT in Nigeria","Appreciate the structure of the VAT model in Nigeria","Compute VAT payable and prepare a VAT account","Identify the taxable services rendered by financial institutions and their VAT-exempt income","Understand the provisions of the NTA and NTAA relating to VAT","Explain the offences and penalties associated with VAT"],"secs":[{"n":"7.2","t":"Nature, objectives, and administration of VAT","b":[{"h4":"7.2.1  The nature of VAT"},{"p":"VAT achieved global acceptance in the early twenty-first century, largely for its ease of administration and high collection yield."},{"def":{"t":"Value Added Tax (VAT)","d":"a consumption tax — a multi-stage levy collected at every stage of the production and sale of goods and the rendition of services."}},{"h4":"7.2.2  Objectives of VAT"},{"p":"Objectives of VAT: increase government revenue; make the tax system more transparent; avoid a cascading effect; reduce tax evasion; increase exports; simplify the tax collection procedure; and check income tax evasion by concealment of actual income."},{"h4":"7.2.3  Administration of VAT — section 3 of NTAA"},{"key":"Administration (s.3 NTAA): the **Nigeria Revenue Service (NRS)** is empowered to administer VAT — the NRS is the central body responsible for administering all federal taxes, VAT included."}]},{"n":"7.3","t":"Registration and deregistration of value added tax","b":[{"h4":"7.3.1  Registration"},{"p":"**Residents** (ss.4-5 NTAA): every taxable entity must register with the relevant tax authority and obtain a Tax ID; every Federal/State ministry, department, agency, and every local government must likewise register."},{"p":"**Non-residents** (s.6 NTAA): a non-resident supplying taxable goods/services in Nigeria, or deriving Nigerian income, must register and obtain a Tax ID — except a non-resident deriving only **passive investment income**, who need not register but must still provide prescribed information."},{"h4":"7.3.2  Deregistration"},{"ol":["A taxable person **temporarily** ceasing trade must notify the tax authority of its intention to suspend registration within **30 days** of the cessation;","the tax authority then classifies the Tax ID as **dormant** and places it on suspension;","a taxable person **permanently** ceasing trade must similarly notify its intention to deregister within **30 days**;","the tax authority then **deregisters** the Tax ID;","the authority also deregisters/cancels a Tax ID where satisfied the person is deceased (or, for a body corporate, wound up/dissolved), or holds a duplicate Tax ID; and","a person whose Tax ID was suspended is, on reapplication, **reissued the same Tax ID**."]}]},{"n":"7.4","t":"Requirements for filing of VAT returns and remittance of VAT liability — section 22 of NTAA","b":[{"key":"A taxable person must, with or without notice and whether or not an economic activity has taken place, file a VAT return — in the prescribed form — **on or before the 21st day of the following month**, reflecting the input tax paid, output tax collected, and VAT payable for all taxable supplies in the preceding month."},{"note":"An extension of the filing period does **not** imply an extension of the time to pay the tax."},{"h4":"Small business treatment"},{"ol":["The standard filing obligation does **not** apply to a small business;","a small business may, by written notice to the Service, **opt out** of the small-business exemption — including registration, charging tax on taxable supplies, and filing returns;","once a business **ceases to be small**, it must file monthly VAT returns;","in determining whether the small-business threshold is met, the value of a supply of a capital asset, and a supply made solely from selling all/part of the business or permanently ceasing trade, are **excluded**;","a small business is exempt from the requirement to pay VAT to a supplier (s.151 NTA) and from the penalties for failure to register (s.100) and failure to keep books (s.102);","the small-business opt-out does **not** apply to companies engaged in petroleum operations;","where the Service deploys technology, a taxable person must render returns in real time or as the Service prescribes; and","for attribution purposes, a return must show details of consumption of taxable supplies, regardless of where the return is filed."]}]},{"n":"7.5","t":"Goods — section 201 of NTA","b":[{"def":{"t":"Goods","d":"all forms of tangible properties, movable or immovable."}}]},{"n":"7.6","t":"Services","b":[{"h4":"7.6.1  Definition"},{"def":{"t":"Services","d":"anything other than goods, or services provided under a contract of employment; and includes any intangible or incorporeal product/asset/property over which a person has ownership or rights, or from which they derive benefits, and which can be transferred between persons — excluding interest in land/building, money, or security."}},{"h4":"7.6.2  Exported service — section 201 of NTA"},{"note":"An **exported service** is one rendered to a non-resident person outside Nigeria by a taxable person, regardless of where it is rendered — but a service rendered to the Nigerian permanent establishment of a non-resident does **not** qualify as exported."}]},{"n":"7.7","t":"Taxable persons — section 201 of NTA","b":[{"def":{"t":"Taxable person","d":"a company, individual or body of individuals, family, community, corporation sole, trustee, executor or any other legal arrangement; or a person carrying out an economic activity, exploiting tangible/intangible property for income by way of trade or business; or a person/government agency acting in that capacity."}}]},{"n":"7.8","t":"VAT invoice — section 152 of NTA","b":[{"p":"A taxpayer making a taxable supply must maintain sequential invoice numbering and furnish the purchaser with a VAT invoice showing: the supplier's Tax ID; the supplier's name/address and sequential invoice number; the supplier's incorporation/business registration number, as applicable; the date of supply; the purchaser's/client's name; the gross transaction amount; and the VAT charged and rate."},{"ol":["A VAT invoice must be issued on supply, whether or not payment is made at that time;","it must be issued by the taxable person making the supply, or a person appointed by the Service; and","the Service may direct a taxpayer to adopt electronic invoicing, giving **at least 30 days'** notice."]}]},{"n":"7.9","t":"Taxable supplies of goods and services — section 201 of NTA","b":[{"def":{"t":"Taxable supply","d":"any transaction for the sale of goods or the performance of a service, for a consideration."}},{"h4":"7.9.1  Value of imported taxable supplies — section 149 of NTA"},{"p":"The value equals the price of the imported taxable supply, plus: taxes/duties/other charges levied outside or by reason of importation (other than VAT itself), and costs of commission, packing, transport and insurance up to the port/point of entry."},{"h4":"7.9.2  Value of taxable supplies — section 148 of NTA"},{"ol":["For a **money consideration**, the value is the amount which, plus the VAT chargeable, equals the consideration;","for a **non-money consideration**, the value is the supply's **market value**;","where a taxable supply is not the only transaction the consideration relates to, the value is the properly attributable **part** of the consideration; and","the market value (for a non-money-consideration supply, or a supply between **connected persons**) is the money consideration payable in an **arm's-length** transaction."]}]},{"n":"7.10","t":"Value added tax fiscalisation system","b":[{"h4":"7.10.1  Value added tax fiscalisation system — section 23 of NTAA"},{"p":"Where the NRS deploys an Electronic Fiscal System (EFS), any person making a taxable supply must use it for recording/reporting all supplies, and remains responsible for maintaining accurate transaction records. The Service specifies the fiscalisation system and its transition arrangement, and issues implementing regulations."},{"h4":"7.10.2  Fiscalisation of supplies of VAT — section 157 of NTA"},{"key":"All registered businesses must use the NRS-sanctioned, real-time e-invoicing system known as the **Merchant Buyer Solution (MBS)** for all transactions — invoices must indicate the specific location of consumption, to support digital tracking and proper VAT allocation across states. The NRS adopts a **clearance model**, validating invoices before they reach buyers, via its e-invoicing portal, on which businesses must register."}]},{"n":"7.11","t":"When goods and services shall be deemed to be supplied in Nigeria — section 145 of NTA","b":[{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Supply type","Deemed in Nigeria where"],"rows":[["**Goods**","the goods are physically present, imported into, assembled or installed in Nigeria at the time of supply; OR the beneficial owner of the rights in/over the goods is a Nigerian taxable person and the goods/right are situated/registered/exercisable in Nigeria."],["**Services**","the service is provided to and consumed by a person in Nigeria — regardless of where it is rendered or whose legal obligation it is to render it; OR the service is connected with immovable property (agents/experts/engineers/architects/valuers, etc.) located in Nigeria."],["**Incorporeals**","the right is exploited by a person in Nigeria or usually resident in Nigeria; the right is registered in, assigned to, or acquired by, a person in Nigeria (regardless of where payment is made); OR the incorporeal is connected with a tangible/immovable asset located in Nigeria."]]}}]},{"n":"7.12","t":"Input VAT — section 151 of NTA","b":[{"def":{"t":"Input VAT (s.151 NTA)","d":"tax paid by a taxable person to the supplier of taxable goods/services purchased or supplied to them."}}]},{"n":"7.13","t":"Credit for input tax and remission of VAT — section 155 of NTA","b":[{"ol":["By the return due date, a taxable person whose **output VAT exceeds input VAT** must remit the excess to the Service; one whose **input VAT exceeds output VAT** may utilise the excess as a credit against subsequent months;","a taxable person is entitled to a **refund** of excess VAT not utilised as credit, on request and supporting documentation;","an importer must pay VAT on taxable goods **before clearing** them;","input tax on a taxable supply (including services and fixed assets) may be deducted from tax payable at the end of the tax period in which the supply occurred — but only to the extent incurred for consumption/use/supply in making taxable supplies; where input tax relates to **both** taxable and non-taxable supplies, only the taxable-supply proportion is deductible; and input tax is allowable for deduction within **5 years** after the end of the tax period it was incurred in; and","deductible input tax is limited to taxable supplies made from the Act's commencement."]}]},{"n":"7.14","t":"Output VAT — section 153 of NTA","b":[{"def":{"t":"Output VAT (s.153 NTA)","d":"tax collected by a taxable person on the supply of taxable goods/services to an accredited distributor, agent, client or customer."}}]},{"n":"7.15","t":"Exempt supplies — section 185 of NTA","b":[{"p":"VAT-exempt supplies:"},{"ol":["oil and gas exports;","crude petroleum oil and feed gas for all processed gas;","goods purchased for humanitarian donor-funded projects (the donor first pays VAT and requests a refund);","baby products;","locally manufactured sanitary towels, pads or tampons;","military hardware, arms, ammunition and locally manufactured uniforms supplied to the armed forces/paramilitary/security agencies;","shared passenger road-transport service;","purchase, hire, rental or lease of tractors, ploughs and other agricultural equipment;","supplies consumed by an approved entity in export processing/free trade zones, on its approved activity;","goods/services supplied to a diplomatic mission/diplomat/recognised person whose activity is in the public interest and not for profit;","plays/performances by educational institutions as part of learning;","land or building, including any interest in it;","money, stakes or securities, including any interest in them;","government licences; and","assistive devices and disability-related products (hearing aids, wheelchairs, braille materials)."]}]},{"n":"7.16","t":"Exemption by Order of the President — section 187 of NTA","b":[{"note":"The **President** may, by Official Gazette order, extend a VAT exemption to supplies made under a government development-financing project agreed with another country/donor agency (s.187 NTA)."}]},{"n":"7.17","t":"Zero-rated supplies and services — section 186 of NTA","b":[{"p":"Taxable supplies charged VAT at **0%**:"},{"ol":["basic food items;","all medical/pharmaceutical products, including medicinal herbal products;","educational books and materials;","fertilisers;","locally produced agricultural chemicals;","locally produced veterinary medicine;","locally produced animal feeds;","live cattle, goats, sheep and poultry;","agricultural seeds and seedlings;","electricity generated by GENCOs and supplied to the National Grid or NBET;","electricity transmitted by TCN to DISCOs;","medical services;","nursery/primary/secondary/tertiary tuition;","exported goods (excluding oil and gas);","exported services;","exported incorporeal property; and","medical equipment."]}]},{"n":"7.18","t":"Taxable supply by non-residents — section 150 of NTA","b":[{"ol":["A non-resident must register for tax and include VAT on its invoice for all taxable supplies made into Nigeria;","where a non-resident supplies from outside Nigeria to a Nigerian recipient, the **recipient withholds** the VAT due and remits it to the Service;","the Service may appoint any person (including a non-resident supplier) to collect and remit VAT;","once an appointed person has supplied a taxable person in Nigeria, that recipient has no withholding obligation — unless the appointed person has failed to collect the VAT;","an appointed person who does not process payments but earns commission uses the mechanism by which it collects its commission to collect the VAT;","a non-resident supplier may appoint a representative for VAT compliance; and","goods imported via an online/digital platform operated by a non-resident supplier, on which VAT has already been collected by the Service or an appointed person, are not further subjected to VAT before clearing, on proof of payment."]}]},{"n":"7.19","t":"Items on which tax is suspended — section 185(2) of NTA and paragraph 1 of the Eleventh Schedule to the NTA","b":[{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Power","Detail"],"rows":[["**Suspension** (s.185(2) NTA; ¶1 of 11th Sch.)","The Minister may, by Gazette order, suspend or commence charging/collecting VAT on: petroleum products; renewable-energy equipment; compressed natural gas (CNG); liquefied petroleum gas (LPG); and other gaseous hydrocarbons."]]}},{"note":"The suspension power is a policy lever, not a permanent exemption: the same section that lets the Minister **suspend** VAT on these items lets the Minister **commence** charging it again, by a further Gazette order. Petroleum products, renewable-energy equipment, CNG, LPG and other gaseous hydrocarbons are therefore best described as *suspended*, not *exempt* — and the distinction matters, because a supplier of suspended items is in a different input-VAT position from a supplier of the exempt items at §7.15."}]},{"n":"7.20","t":"Items on which the Minister may vary the classification — paragraph 2 of the Eleventh Schedule to the NTA","b":[{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Power","Detail"],"rows":[["**Reclassification** (¶2 of 11th Sch.)","The Minister may, by Gazette order, classify as exempt or zero-rated: all equipment/components/infrastructure for converting/installing/expanding CNG and LPG use (including conversion kits); and all services relating to their conversion/installation."]]}},{"key":"Read §7.19 and §7.20 together and the policy is plain: both powers point at the same target — **gas as a transport and domestic fuel**. §7.19 lets the Minister suspend VAT on the **fuel itself**; §7.20 lets the Minister exempt or zero-rate the **equipment, infrastructure and conversion services** needed to use it. One without the other would be half a measure."}]},{"n":"7.21","t":"Rate of VAT — section 147 of NTA","b":[{"key":"The **rate of VAT** is **7.5%** on the value of all taxable supplies (s.147 NTA)."}]},{"n":"7.22","t":"Time of supply — section 146 of NTA","b":[{"p":"A taxable supply occurs at the **earliest** of: an invoice/receipt being issued; the goods being delivered/made available for use; or payment being due/received — whichever occurs first."},{"p":"Where the supplier and recipient are **connected persons** and no invoice is issued, supply is deemed to occur: at removal (goods to be removed); when goods not to be removed are made available; on commencement of a service; or when an incorporeal becomes available for use."},{"p":"Special timing rules apply to periodic/instalment arrangements: rental or periodic-payment agreements are deemed successively supplied, each instalment occurring when payment is due or received (whichever is earlier); progressive/periodic supplies (including construction/assembly/manufacturing/alteration/improvement/repair paid in instalments) are similarly deemed successive, occurring at the earliest of payment due, payment received, or invoice issuance; and an instalment-credit-agreement supply is deemed to occur at delivery or at receipt of any payment, whichever occurs first."}]},{"n":"7.23","t":"Taxable supply of non-residents / VAT reverse charge — section 150 of NTA","b":[{"def":{"t":"VAT reverse charge","d":"a mechanism where the **recipient** of a supply — not the supplier — accounts for VAT; the seller does not charge VAT on the invoice. It applies to non-resident suppliers unregistered for Nigerian VAT, and where local vendors fail to charge VAT. The recipient must self-assess the VAT payable and remit it to the NRS."}},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Benefits","Challenges"],"rows":[["Simplifies VAT compliance for non-resident suppliers.","Complex to implement and administer."],["Reduces the administrative burden on non-resident suppliers.","Places a significant compliance burden on recipients."],["Ensures VAT revenue is collected on non-resident-supplier transactions.","Can lead to errors in VAT accounting and remittance."]]}}]},{"n":"7.24","t":"Computation of VAT liability, including the treatment of opening and closing inventories","b":[{"p":"Effective 1 February 2020, VAT liability is computed by first applying the 7.5% rate to **total sales net of allowable sales adjustments** (VAT output — see VAT Form 002), then applying 7.5% to **purchases net of allowable purchases adjustments** (VAT input). VAT liability for the period is Output VAT less Input VAT."},{"p":"This liability may be further adjusted for: VAT reverse charges already deducted by third parties (oil and gas companies, government MDAs, etc.) in the same period; and any VAT credit (excess input over output) carried from a previous period. The net result may be a VAT credit or a VAT payable."},{"key":"Where the computation involves **inventories**, the input VAT must be further adjusted: period purchases are first increased by the cost of **opening** inventory, then reduced by the cost of **closing** inventory — the net figure is adjusted as above, then input VAT is computed by applying the 7.5% rate. Inventories are measured at the **lower of cost and net realisable value** (IAS 2) — cost comprises all costs of purchase, conversion, and other costs of bringing inventory to its present location/condition."},{"eg":{"t":"Illustration 7.1 — VAT remittable (ABC Nigeria Limited)","q":[{"p":"ABC Nigeria Limited, incorporated years ago and importing/selling chemical products, had these February 2026 transactions (all goods received and sold during the month):"},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Item","N"],"rows":[["Total value of taxable supplies (incl. exempt/zero-rated supplies and VAT)","18,600,000"],["Value of exempt/zero-rated supplies included above","4,800,000"],["VAT on domestic supplies/purchases with invoicing requirements met","110,300"],["VAT on import","285,600"],["VAT on purchases not wholly used in making vatable supplies","62,000"],["VAT paid on equipment","97,500"],["VAT paid on professional fees","48,000"],["Bank charges (VAT inclusive)","93,000"]]}},{"p":"**Required:** Compute the VAT remittable to the tax authority in March 2026."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Solution — VAT remittable to NRS, March 2026","N"],"rows":[["Value of taxable supplies, incl. exempt/zero-rated supplies and VAT","18,600,000"],["Less: value of exempt/zero-rated supplies included above","(4,800,000)"],["**Total supplies subject to VAT**","**13,800,000**"],["VAT received on supplies (7.5/107.5 × N13,800,000)","962,791"],["VAT on domestic supplies/purchases (invoicing requirements met)","110,300"],["VAT on import","285,600"],["Subtotal","395,900"],["Less: VAT on purchases not wholly used in vatable supplies","(62,000)"],["Subtotal","333,900"],["VAT paid on equipment","97,500"],["VAT paid on professional fees","48,000"],["VAT included in bank charges (7.5/107.5 × N93,000)","6,488"],["**Total deductible input VAT**","**(485,888)**"],["**VAT remittable**","**476,903**"]]}},{"note":"Tutorial notes: (i) deductible input VAT is limited to VAT paid on goods purchased/imported directly for resale, or forming inventory used for direct production of taxed output; (ii) VAT paid on overhead, service and general administration is allowed as input VAT only if directly attributable to taxable supplies; (iii) the same applies to VAT paid on capital items."}]}}]},{"n":"7.25","t":"Obligation to keep books of accounts — section 31 of NTAA","b":[{"ol":["Every person registered with the NRS for VAT must keep records/books of all transactions, operations, imports and other activities relating to taxable goods/services, sufficient to determine the correct tax due;","every person — including a company granted exemption from incorporation — must maintain books/records **regardless** of whether they are actually liable to pay tax;","records must contain sufficient information to ascertain the person's tax liability;","records must be in **English**, in a format the tax authority may prescribe;","records in another language must, on demand, be translated into English (at the taxpayer's expense) by a **sworn translator**; and","records must be kept for **at least 6 years** after the assessment year the income relates to."]}]},{"n":"7.26","t":"Collection of VAT by persons other than the supplier — section 155 of NTA","b":[{"p":"Per s.155 NTA:"},{"ol":["Federal/State/Local Government (and their MDAs), and any other person appointed by the Service, must **collect or withhold** VAT on taxable supplies made to them and remit it within the prescribed time;","the Service may direct a taxable recipient of a supply, invoiced without VAT included, to **self-account** for the VAT payable and remit it;","remission under either of the above must be accompanied by a schedule showing the contractor/supplier's name, Tax ID and address, invoice number, gross invoice amount, VAT amount, and the relevant month;","VAT collected/withheld/self-accounted under this section is remitted **on or before the 14th day** of the month following the transaction month, or as the Service prescribes; and","a person with a VAT-collection obligation must keep proper records and make returns/remittances, with the ordinary compliance provisions of the NTA/NTAA applying to them as if they were the taxable person."]}]},{"n":"7.27","t":"Treatment of VAT on imported and exported goods and services","b":[{"h4":"7.27.1  VAT on imported goods and services"},{"p":"VAT on **imported goods** is charged on non-exempt goods, assessed via the Nigeria Customs Service through one of four points: the Nigeria Customs Service (NCS), the Nigerian Port Authority (NPA), shipping companies/airlines, or clearing and forwarding agents."},{"key":"The NCS assessment is calculated by summing the CIF (cost, insurance, freight) value of the goods, the import duty value, surcharge, and other NCS-collectable charges — the total is then multiplied by the **7.5%** VAT rate."},{"p":"VAT on **imported services** is payable where services received from outside Nigeria are supplied to a Nigerian customer."},{"h4":"7.27.2  VAT on export"},{"key":"All exported goods and services are **exempt** from VAT — consistent with the **destination principle**, under which VAT is retained by the country where the taxed product is sold/consumed."}]},{"n":"7.28","t":"VAT exempt items and zero-rated items — input VAT treatment compared","b":[{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Supply category","Input VAT treatment"],"rows":[["**Exempt supplies**","Not subject to VAT — the input VAT incurred on them is **not recoverable** and is instead treated as a **cost**."],["**Zero-rated supplies**","Subject to VAT, but at 0% — the input VAT incurred **is recoverable** even though the output VAT is nil (e.g. a company exporting zero-rated goods can recover the input VAT it incurred). Treated as a recoverable input tax credit."]]}}]},{"n":"7.29","t":"Treatment of VAT on business restructuring — section 189 of NTA","b":[{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Restructuring type","Treatment"],"rows":[["**Merger of two or more trades/businesses**","No new trade is deemed to have commenced — cessation-of-trade provisions do not apply to the merged-away business; chargeable gains do not apply to transferred assets; assets are deemed transferred at the residue of qualifying capital expenditure the day after the merger; the remaining capital-allowance basis periods equal the total years less years already claimed; unutilised capital allowance transfers to the new/surviving business; unabsorbed losses of the merging entities transfer to the surviving business; and taxes deducted at source in respect of the merged businesses remain available to the merged entity."],["**Sale/transfer causing cessation of trade**","Ordinary cessation-of-trade provisions apply; the asset is recognised at its sale/transfer value; unutilised capital allowance does **not** transfer to the new/surviving business; unabsorbed losses do **not** transfer; and taxes deducted at source do **not** transfer."],["**Sale/transfer of a business asset without cessation**, at a price not exceeding the residue of qualifying capital expenditure plus unutilised capital allowance","Capital allowance applies only to the residue of the asset; unutilised capital allowance transfers to the buying business; the selling business cannot claim any part of the unutilised allowance on the transferred asset; and chargeable gains do not apply to the asset."]]}},{"ol":["The relevant tax authority must be **notified** of any restructuring before it commences;","where a business (or a separately-operable part) is transferred as a **going concern**, and the purchaser continues the same kind of business, the transfer is **not** treated as a supply of goods/services for VAT purposes — provided the purchaser is registered for tax compliance; and","VAT does not apply to business restructuring carried out in accordance with the Act's own provisions."]}]},{"n":"7.30","t":"Vatable services rendered by financial institutions and their VAT exempt income","b":[{"h4":"7.30.1  Introduction"},{"p":"Services rendered by **microfinance banks, people's banks and mortgage institutions** are specifically VAT-exempt — services by other financial institutions are liable to VAT."},{"h4":"7.30.2  Definition of financial institutions"},{"def":{"t":"Financial institution","d":"any bank, individual, body, association or group of persons (corporate or unincorporated), licensed under BOFIA or a related Act, carrying on the business of a discount house, finance company, money brokerage, or whose principal objects include factoring, project financing, equipment leasing, debt administration, fund management, private ledger services, investment management, local purchases/order/export finance, project/financial consultancy, pension fund management, and such other business as the CBN, NDIC, PenCom or other regulator may designate."}},{"h4":"7.30.3 – 7.30.5  Services liable to VAT, vatable services, and non-vatable income of financial institutions"},{"p":"Commissions, fees or other charges for services financial institutions render to customers are liable to VAT — but **loans/advances and interest charged on them are not**, while additional services like documentation/perfection of loan or overdraft agreements **are** vatable. For insurance intermediaries (brokers, agents, loss adjusters, surveyors), fees/commissions for services rendered are liable to VAT, but **premium received on policies is not**."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Vatable charges","Non-vatable income"],"rows":[["Commissions on forex trading/remittance","Interest on loans and advances (including overdrafts)"],["Commissions on sale of bank drafts/certified cheques","Interest on savings accounts"],["Commissions to brokers/reinsurers/underwriters/insurance agents","Interest on bank deposits"],["Commission on asset trading","Interest on interbank placements"],["Account maintenance/ledger fees","Premium on insurance policies"],["Legal and other fees on lease arrangements","Dividends"],["Advisory fees (M&A, financial strategy counselling)","Profit/gain on disposal of securities"],["Fees on public/private issues",""],["Debt conversion fees",""],["Fees earned on fund management",""],["Fees on letters of credit / documentary collection (import/export finance)",""],["Fees on stock-brokerage and trust services",""],["Fees on electronic banking, POS and ATM charges",""],["Fees on electronic bill payments",""],["Fees on mobile money transactions and similar",""]]}},{"h4":"7.30.6  Registration for VAT and rendition of returns"},{"p":"Financial institutions must register for VAT and obtain a Tax ID, remitting VAT **within 21 days** of the month following the transaction month. VAT paid on capital items, overheads, services and general administration is allowable as input VAT provided it directly relates to VAT-liable supplies; input VAT on goods supplied to customers is allowed against the output tax on the same goods."},{"h4":"7.30.7 – 7.30.8  Treatment of input tax, and the duty to account for VAT"},{"note":"Duty to account: ordinarily the service provider charges/remits VAT — but where an agent/broker acts as intermediary, the **agent/broker** bears that duty; if the agent/broker fails to charge VAT, or cannot (being an individual or below the VAT threshold), the financial institution must **self-account and remit**."}]},{"n":"7.31","t":"VAT refund — section 56 of NTAA","b":[{"ol":["A refund request is made to the Service in the prescribed form by a qualifying taxable person;","the request must be made **not later than 12 months** after the refund-triggering transaction, or it **lapses**; and","on a valid request, the Service must, **within 30 days** of receipt, refund the tax or make it eligible for set-off against the taxpayer's other liability."]}]},{"n":"7.32","t":"VAT compliance threshold","b":[{"key":"Only taxable persons with taxable supplies of **N100 million or more** must charge, collect, remit VAT and file monthly returns — a business with gross turnover of N100 million or less **and** total fixed assets not exceeding **N250 million** is not required to charge VAT. A small business may, however, opt out of this exemption by written notice to the Service (s.22(5) NTAA)."},{"eg":{"t":"Illustration 7.2","q":[{"p":"A taxable person achieved taxable supplies of N100 million in January 2026. When should they collect tax and file monthly VAT returns?"}],"a":[{"p":"Having achieved N100 million in taxable supplies in January 2026, the taxable person should charge, collect tax and file monthly VAT returns from **February 2026**."}]}}]},{"n":"7.33","t":"VAT sharing formula amongst the tiers of government — section 81 of NTAA","b":[{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Recipient","Share of net VAT revenue"],"rows":[["Federal Government","10%"],["State Governments and the FCT","55%"],["Local Governments","35%"]]}},{"p":"The share standing to the credit of states and local governments is further distributed among them: **50%** equally, **20%** by population, and **30%** by consumption — consumption being determined by the **place of consumption**, irrespective of where the return is filed."}]},{"n":"7.34","t":"Types of VAT","b":[{"table":{"align":"llll","head":["Variant","Basis","Merits","Demerits"],"rows":[["**Gross product type**","The gross value of goods/services produced or sold, with **no deduction** of input VAT; a single rate applies to the gross value.","Simplifies VAT administration (no input-VAT tracking); lowers compliance costs; incentivises production/sales.","Double taxation (no input deduction); inequitable burden on high-volume producers/sellers; difficult to implement in complex tax systems. **Used in**: Brazil, Argentina."],["**Income type**","The income/profits earned by businesses (not sales value); a single rate applies to income/profits, after deducting certain expenses (input VAT, wages, raw materials).","Reduces the burden on low-margin businesses; encourages investment; simplifies administration (report income/profits only).","Complex to implement; hard to define \"income/profits\" for VAT purposes; high evasion risk via underreporting. **Used in**: no country currently."],["**Consumption type**","The value of goods/services consumed by households/businesses; a single rate applies, with input VAT fully deductible against output VAT.","Neutral taxation (levied on consumption, not production); encourages economic growth; simplifies administration (report output VAT, claim input VAT).","Hard to ascertain value consumed for exempt/zero-rated supplies; high evasion risk (underreporting output/overclaiming input); cross-border complexity. **Used in**: Canada, Australia, Nigeria."]]}}]},{"n":"7.35","t":"VAT enforcement and practice","b":[{"h4":"7.35.1 – 7.35.2  VAT visits and VAT drives"},{"p":"VAT inspectors from the local VAT office visit vatable persons' premises to: ensure compliance with VAT law; ensure amounts deducted as VAT are promptly accounted for; examine transaction-record-keeping and suggest improvements; and educate VAT agents on new developments. **VAT drives** — periodic exercises at the instance of zonal coordinators — stimulate collection of VAT proceeds from defaulters."}]},{"n":"7.36","t":"VAT audit and investigation","b":[{"p":"Instituted regularly by NRS headquarters — checking both VAT officers' and VAT payers' records to ensure strict compliance with the Act and accountability for proceeds collected."}]},{"n":"7.37","t":"Merits and demerits of VAT","b":[{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Merits","Demerits"],"rows":[["Reliable source of government revenue.","High administrative cost for government."],["Eliminates the narrow scope and cascading effect of the repealed sales tax.","High record-keeping cost for companies/agents."],["A consumption tax, hence easy to collect.","Injustice in distribution of VAT proceeds."],["Fair — consumers pay in line with their own consumption.","Corruption among tax officials can render VAT ineffective."],["A fiscal-policy tool — items can be exempted, and rates varied, for specific economic objectives.","Inadequate information can undermine administration."]]}}]},{"n":"7.38","t":"Offences and penalties","b":[{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Offence","First month","Each subsequent month"],"rows":[["**Failure to register for VAT** (s.100 NTAA)","N50,000","N25,000"],["**Awarding a contract to an unregistered person** (s.100 NTAA)","N5,000,000 (flat)","—"],["**Failure to file (or knowingly filing incomplete/inaccurate) VAT returns** (s.101 NTAA)","N100,000","N50,000"]]}},{"note":"Other general tax offences and penalties (bribery, false declarations, obstruction, counterfeiting, etc.) apply equally to VAT — see Chapter 4.10."}]},{"n":"7.39","t":"Interpretations — section 188 of NTA","b":[{"p":"Selected VAT-specific definitions:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Term","Meaning"],"rows":[["**Baby products**","Products for babies from birth to age 3 — activity/entertainment products, bathing/grooming items, carriages, garments, feeding products, sanitary wares, and others (e.g. monitors)."],["**Basic food items**","Agriculture- or aquatic-based staples — honey, bread, cereals, cooking oils (for culinary use only), culinary herbs, fish, flour/starch, fruits, live/raw meat and eggs, milk, nuts, pulses, tubers, culinary salt, vegetables, and water (natural/table, excluding sparkling/flavoured water or water sold in hospitality settings or by contractors/caterers)."],["**Educational books/materials**","Physical or electronic books/materials used for instruction across all levels of education — booklets, newspapers, journals, children's books, printed music, maps/charts, and related instructional/assessment materials."],["**Equipment / Machinery / Plant**","Equipment: tools/devices/machines/specialised vehicles assisting beyond normal human capability. Machinery: a mechanical device or its co-operating parts performing a single task. Plant: the assemblage of fixtures/tools/machinery/apparatus (including land, buildings, specialised structures) necessary to carry on a trade or industrial business."],["**Exported goods**","Goods produced/procured for commercial purposes in Nigeria and supplied to a person outside Nigeria."],["**Farming machinery/implements/transportation equipment**","Equipment used exclusively for farming/agricultural production — tractors, ploughs, harrows, ridgers, harvesters and the like."],["**Humanitarian donor-funded projects**","Projects by NGOs and recognised religious/social clubs/societies whose activity is not-for-profit and in the public interest."],["**Locally produced agricultural/veterinary medicines**","Nigerian-produced drugs/vaccines for animals, fish and plants — veterinary pharmaceuticals/nutraceuticals/biologicals, fish dietary supplements/vaccines, and agro-chemical crop-protection products (insecticides, fungicides, herbicides, etc.)."],["**Medical equipment / products / services / pharmaceutical products**","Medical equipment: devices needing calibration/maintenance/training for diagnosis/treatment (excludes cosmetology/fitness devices). Medical products: articles/instruments/software for prevention/diagnosis/treatment (excludes cosmetology/fitness). Medical services: healthcare for humans/animals by a qualified practitioner (excludes cosmetology/spa/gym). Pharmaceutical products: modern/traditional medicine components for human use, regulator-approved."],["**Shared passenger road-transport service**","Public-use passenger road transport, excluding leased/hired/rented vehicles, business/private transport, and car/ride-hailing."],["**Purchase**","To obtain/acquire/take possession of an asset/property/item/right by paying money or money's worth."],["**Stake**","An amount waged on a game."],["**Utilisation of gas in downstream petroleum operations**","Marketing/distribution of natural gas for commercial purposes — power generation, LNG/gas-to-liquid production, fertiliser plants, and gas distribution pipelines."]]}}]},{"n":"7.40","t":"Chapter review","b":[{"p":"This chapter covered the nature, objectives and administration of VAT, taxable persons and taxable supplies of goods and services, registration and records/accounts-keeping obligations, valid VAT invoices and VAT account preparation, filing and remittance requirements, treatment of VAT on imported/exported goods and services, the types of VAT, and offences/penalties associated with VAT."},{"p":"It also addressed returns, remittances and refunds, VAT administration, VAT enforcement and practice, the merits/demerits of VAT, and a glossary of key terms under the VAT provisions of the NTA."}]},{"n":"7.41","t":"Worked examples — study text questions and suggested solutions","b":[{"p":"The study text sets **three questions** at §7.41.1 — two computations and one theory question — with its own suggested solutions at §7.41.2. All three are reproduced here in full, with the book's answers, the arithmetic shown step by step, and added explanation."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 7.41.1, Question 1","t":"JOHNSON Idowu Trading Company — VAT payable from VAT-inclusive figures","open":true,"q":[{"p":"JOHNSON Idowu Trading Company registered for value added tax (VAT) purposes. The accounting records of the company revealed the following projected information for the year ending **December 31, 2026**. The projected financial transactions relate to **taxable supplies**:"},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["","N"],"rows":[["Revenue (VAT inclusive)","30,400,500"],["Purchase of raw materials (VAT inclusive)","20,140,650"],["Transport and travelling (VAT inclusive)","1,215,800"],["Vehicle running expenses (VAT inclusive)","850,420"],["Office equipment (VAT inclusive)","2,100,500"],["Furniture and fittings (VAT inclusive)","1,500,000"],["Motor vehicle (VAT inclusive)","2,000,600"],["Audit and accountancy fees (VAT inclusive)","150,000"],["Bank charges (VAT inclusive)","185,050"],["Miscellaneous expenses (VAT inclusive)","550,500"]]}},{"p":"**Required:** Compute the VAT payable by Johnson Idowu Trading Company for the year ending December 31, 2026."}],"a":[{"key":"Every figure in the question is **VAT inclusive**, so none of them is a tax base. At 7.5%, a VAT-inclusive amount is **107.5%** of the net value, so the VAT embedded in it is extracted with the fraction $\\dfrac{7.5}{107.5}$ — *not* by taking 7.5% of the inclusive figure, which would understate the tax. $\\dfrac{7.5}{107.5} = 0.0697674…$, or about **6.9767%** of the gross."},{"stmt":{"t":"Johnson Idowu Trading Company — computation of VAT payable for the year ending December 31, 2026","rows":[["Output tax (Revenue) — W1","2,120,965","","@hd"],["Less: Input VAT (purchase of raw materials) — W2","(1,405,162)",""],["","715,803","","@t"],"@gap","Less: Input VAT",["Transport and travelling (7.5/107.5 × N1,215,800)","(84,823)","","@ind"],["Vehicle running expenses (7.5/107.5 × N850,420)","(59,332)","","@ind"],["Office equipment (7.5/107.5 × N2,100,500)","(146,547)","","@ind"],["Furniture and fittings (7.5/107.5 × N1,500,000)","(104,651)","","@ind"],["Motor vehicle (7.5/107.5 × N2,000,600)","(139,577)","","@ind"],["Audit and accountancy fees (7.5/107.5 × N150,000)","(10,465)","","@ind"],["Bank charges (7.5/107.5 × N185,050)","(12,910)","","@ind"],["Miscellaneous expenses (7.5/107.5 × N550,500)","(38,407)","","@ind"],["VAT payable","119,091","","@tt"]]}},{"h4":"Working notes"},{"tex":"W1:\\ \\text{Output tax} = \\dfrac{7.5}{107.5} \\times N30{,}400{,}500 = N2{,}120{,}965"},{"tex":"W2:\\ \\text{Input tax} = \\dfrac{7.5}{107.5} \\times N20{,}140{,}650 = N1{,}405{,}162"},{"note":"Proof of the final figure, since the book prints the total without showing the addition: the eight further input-VAT amounts come to N84,823 + N59,332 + N146,547 + N104,651 + N139,577 + N10,465 + N12,910 + N38,407 = **N596,712**, and N715,803 − N596,712 = **N119,091**. Each individual amount is the gross figure × 7.5/107.5, rounded to the nearest naira."},{"key":"Note what is being claimed here, because it is a **change in the law**. Input VAT is deducted on *everything* — including **office equipment, furniture and fittings and a motor vehicle**. Under the repealed VAT Act, input VAT on **fixed assets** and on **general administrative/overhead expenses** was expressly **not** allowable: it had to be capitalised with the asset or expensed. Section 151 and section 155 of the NTA now allow input tax on a taxable supply — **services and fixed assets included** — provided it was incurred for use in making **taxable supplies** (see §7.13). An answer written from the old rule would deduct only the raw materials and arrive at N715,803, and would now be wrong."},{"warn":"The deduction is allowed only **to the extent the input is used in making taxable supplies**. Every item in this question is stated to relate to taxable supplies, which is why all of it is claimable. Had any of it related to **exempt** supplies, that proportion would have been irrecoverable and treated as a cost instead (see §7.28), and where an input serves **both**, only the taxable-supply proportion is deductible."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 7.41.1, Question 2","t":"Oropo Limited — total VAT payable across a manufacturer–wholesaler–retailer chain","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Oropo Limited is a manufacturing outfit based in Ado, Ekiti State. The company sold its vatable products to a wholesaler, Lapo Enterprises, for **N2,000,000** in 2025. The wholesaler in turn sold the products to a retailer, Mrs. Adele, for **N3,500,000**, who finally sold them to consumers for **N4,200,000**."},{"p":"Assume there was **no closing inventory** at each stage of the transactions."},{"p":"**Required:** Compute the total VAT payable to the Nigeria Revenue Service for the year ended December 31, 2025."}],"a":[{"note":"Each amount here is a **selling price exclusive of VAT** — contrast Question 1, where every figure was VAT inclusive. So VAT is **7.5% of** each figure, and the fraction 7.5/107.5 has no place in this answer. Reading which of the two a question gives you is worth more marks than any other single step."},{"table":{"align":"llrr","head":["Stage","Computation","N","VAT payable (N)"],"rows":[["**A**","Manufacturer — sales value","2,000,000",""],["","Gross VAT at 7.5%","150,000",""],["","Net VAT","150,000","**150,000**"],["**B**","Wholesaler — sales value","3,500,000",""],["","Gross VAT at 7.5%","262,500",""],["","Net VAT (N262,500 − N150,000)","","**112,500**"],["**C**","Retailer — sales value","4,200,000",""],["","Gross VAT at 7.5%","315,000",""],["","Net VAT (N315,000 − N262,500)","","**52,500**"],["","**Total VAT payable to the NRS**","","**315,000**"]]}},{"key":"The check that confirms the whole answer: **N315,000 is exactly 7.5% of N4,200,000**, the final consumer price. That is the defining property of a value added tax — however many stages the goods pass through, the total collected by the Revenue equals the tax on the **final** selling price, because each trader remits only the tax on the value *it* added. N150,000 + N112,500 + N52,500 = N315,000."},{"note":"Why the assumption of **no closing inventory** matters: it makes each trader's purchases for the period equal to its cost of sales, so input VAT equals the output VAT of the stage before. With closing inventory, period purchases would have to be adjusted up by opening inventory and down by closing inventory before computing input VAT — the rule at §7.24 — and the chain would no longer net so cleanly."},{"warn":"The book's final row is captioned \"Total VAT payable to **FIRS**\". The Federal Inland Revenue Service has been succeeded by the **Nigeria Revenue Service (NRS)**, which is the name used in the question's own requirement. Write **NRS**."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 7.41.1, Question 3","t":"The three types (variants) of VAT","open":true,"q":[{"p":"VAT is a consumption tax which is a multi-stage levy collected at every stage of production, sale of goods, and rendition of services. **Required:** Explain: **(a)** the gross product type variant; **(b)** the income type variant; **(c)** the consumption type variant."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  Gross product type variant of VAT"},{"p":"This is a method of computing value added tax based on the **gross value of goods and services produced or sold, without deducting input VAT**. Businesses are **not allowed to deduct input VAT** from their VAT liability, and a single VAT rate is applied to the gross value of goods and services."},{"p":"**(i) Merits** of the gross product type variant include:"},{"ul":["**simplification of VAT administration**, as businesses do not need to track input VAT;","**reduction in compliance costs**, as businesses incur lower compliance costs — they do not need to maintain detailed records of input VAT; and","**enhancement of economic growth**, as businesses are incentivised to produce and sell more goods and services."]},{"p":"**(ii) Demerits** of the gross product type variant include:"},{"ul":["**double taxation**, as VAT is charged on the gross value of goods and services without deducting input VAT;","**inequitable distribution of the VAT burden**, as businesses that produce and sell more goods and services will bear a disproportionate share of the VAT burden; and","**difficulty in implementation**, especially in countries with complex tax systems."]},{"p":"Countries that use the gross product type variant of VAT include **Brazil and Argentina**."},{"h4":"(b)  Income type variant of VAT"},{"p":"This is a method of calculating value added tax based on the **income or profits earned by businesses**. The VAT base is the income or profits earned, rather than the value of goods and services sold, and a single VAT rate is applied to that income or profit. Businesses are allowed to deduct certain expenses — such as input VAT, wages and raw materials — from their income or profits before calculating VAT."},{"p":"**(i) Merits** of the income type variant include:"},{"ul":["**reduction of the tax burden** on businesses, especially those with low profit margins;","**encouragement of investment**, as businesses are incentivised to invest in new projects and expand their operations; and","**simplification of VAT administration**, as businesses only need to report their income or profits, rather than tracking input VAT and output VAT."]},{"p":"**(ii) Demerits** of the income type variant include:"},{"ul":["**complexity of implementation**, especially in countries with complex tax systems;","**difficulty in defining income or profits** for VAT purposes, especially for businesses with complex financial structures; and","**high risk of tax evasion**, as businesses may under-report their income or profits to avoid paying VAT."]},{"p":"Currently, **no country uses** the income type variant of VAT."},{"h4":"(c)  The consumption type variant of VAT"},{"p":"This is a method of computing value added tax based on the **value of goods and services consumed by households and businesses**. The VAT base is the value of goods and services consumed; a single VAT rate is applied to that value; and businesses are **allowed to deduct input VAT from their output VAT liability**. Countries that use the consumption type variant include **Canada, Australia and Nigeria**."},{"p":"**(i) Merits** of the consumption type variant include:"},{"ul":["ensuring **neutral taxation**, as VAT is levied on the value of goods and services consumed rather than on the production process;","**encouragement of economic growth**, as businesses are incentivised to produce and sell goods and services that meet the demands of households and businesses; and","**simplification of VAT administration**, as taxpayers only need to report their output VAT liability and claim input VAT deductions."]},{"p":"**(ii) Demerits** of the consumption type variant include:"},{"ul":["**difficulty in ascertaining the value** of goods and services consumed, especially in the case of exempt or zero-rated supplies;","**high risk of tax evasion**, as taxpayers may under-report their output VAT liability or over-claim input VAT deductions; and","**complexity in cross-border transactions**, as businesses may need to account for VAT in multiple jurisdictions."]},{"key":"The single fact that organises all three: **what happens to input VAT.** The gross product variant **denies** the deduction entirely — which is why it double-taxes. The income variant allows it **alongside wages and other expenses**, because its base is profit, not consumption. The consumption variant allows it **in full** against output VAT, which is what makes the tax fall on the final consumer and on nobody else — as Question 2 demonstrates arithmetically. **Nigeria uses the consumption type**, and that is why §7.13 governs the credit for input tax."},{"note":"Worth remembering for a one-mark gift: the gross product variant is used in **Brazil and Argentina**, the income variant is used **nowhere**, and the consumption variant is used in **Canada, Australia and Nigeria**. The book names these countries explicitly, so they are examinable."}]}}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"VAT extraction, from a VAT-inclusive amount","tex":"\\text{VAT} = \\dfrac{7.5}{107.5} \\times \\text{VAT-inclusive amount}"},{"lb":"VAT liability for the period","tex":"\\text{VAT liability} = \\text{Output VAT} - \\text{Input VAT}"},{"lb":"Value of an imported taxable supply","tex":"\\text{Value} = \\text{Price of supply} + \\text{import taxes/duties/charges} + \\text{commission, packing, transport \\& insurance to port}","nt":"Import taxes/duties/charges and the commission/packing/transport/insurance costs exclude VAT itself."}],"focus":"This is the densest computational chapter so far — master the VAT-inclusive extraction formula (7.5/107.5 × amount) cold, since it recurs in every VAT-payable illustration, and practise distinguishing which input VAT items are genuinely deductible (goods for resale/production, plus overheads/capital items ONLY if directly attributable to taxable supplies) from those that are not (input VAT on exempt supplies, which is a cost, not a credit). Keep the three supply categories straight as a set: **exempt** (no VAT charged, input VAT NOT recoverable), **zero-rated** (VAT charged at 0%, input VAT IS recoverable), and **standard-rated** (VAT charged at 7.5%, input VAT recoverable) — confusing exempt with zero-rated is the single most common VAT error, since both show 0% output VAT on the face of an invoice but have opposite input-VAT consequences. Also memorise the VAT rate (7.5%), the N100m/N250m compliance threshold, the 10/55/35% sharing split, and the 21st-day monthly filing deadline as a fixed reference set.","errors":["Treating \"exempt\" and \"zero-rated\" as interchangeable — an exempt supply carries NO input VAT recovery (input VAT becomes a cost), while a zero-rated supply carries FULL input VAT recovery despite charging 0% output VAT; the two categories have opposite economic effects despite both showing \"no VAT\" on an invoice.","Forgetting that VAT reverse charge shifts the accounting obligation to the RECIPIENT of a supply, not the supplier — the seller's invoice shows no VAT charged, but the buyer must still self-assess and remit it, so \"no VAT on the invoice\" does not mean \"no VAT is due.\"","Missing that a small business's exemption from VAT registration/collection is NOT automatic and irrevocable in either direction — a small business may voluntarily opt in by written notice, and a business that grows past the threshold must switch to monthly filing.","Confusing the VAT compliance threshold's two limbs — a business must have turnover of N100 million or LESS **and** total fixed assets not exceeding N250 million to qualify for the small-business exemption; failing EITHER limb removes the exemption.","Applying the wrong denominator when extracting VAT from a VAT-inclusive figure — the correct fraction is 7.5/107.5 (VAT as a fraction of the gross, VAT-inclusive amount), not 7.5/100 (which would only apply to a VAT-exclusive, net amount)."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"At what rate is VAT charged on the value of all taxable supplies, under s.147 of the NTA?","o":["5%","7.5%","10%","12.5%","15%"],"a":1,"w":"Section 147 NTA sets the VAT rate at 7.5% on the value of all taxable supplies.","src":"Chapter 7.9","sec":"7.9"},{"q":"A business has annual gross turnover of N95 million and total fixed assets of N200 million. Under the VAT compliance threshold, is it required to charge, collect and remit VAT?","o":["Yes, because turnover exceeds N50 million","No, because both turnover (≤N100m) and fixed assets (≤N250m) are within the small-business limits","Yes, because fixed assets exceed N150 million","No, but only if it is a sole proprietorship","Yes, all businesses must charge VAT regardless of size"],"a":1,"w":"The small-business VAT exemption applies where turnover is N100 million or less AND total fixed assets do not exceed N250 million — this business meets both limbs, so it falls within the exemption (unless it opts in voluntarily).","src":"Chapter 7.16","sec":"7.16"},{"q":"Which of the following best distinguishes a zero-rated supply from an exempt supply?","o":["There is no difference — both simply mean no VAT is charged","A zero-rated supply allows recovery of input VAT, while an exempt supply does not","An exempt supply allows recovery of input VAT, while a zero-rated supply does not","Zero-rated supplies are taxed at 7.5%, exempt supplies are taxed at 0%","Only individuals can make exempt supplies; only companies can make zero-rated supplies"],"a":1,"w":"A zero-rated supply is charged VAT at 0% but still permits full recovery of input VAT; an exempt supply is outside the VAT system entirely, so its input VAT is not recoverable and is instead treated as a cost.","src":"Chapter 7.13","sec":"7.13"},{"q":"Under the VAT sharing formula in s.81 of the NTAA, what percentage of net VAT revenue is allocated to State Governments and the Federal Capital Territory?","o":["10%","35%","50%","55%","65%"],"a":3,"w":"Section 81 NTAA allocates net VAT revenue as 10% Federal Government, 55% State Governments and the FCT, and 35% Local Governments.","src":"Chapter 7.16","sec":"7.16"},{"q":"By what day of the month following a transaction month must a taxable person file a VAT return?","o":["7th day","14th day","21st day","28th day","Last day of the month"],"a":2,"w":"Section 22 NTAA requires a VAT return to be filed on or before the 21st day of the month following the transaction month.","src":"Chapter 7.3","sec":"7.3"}],"theory":[{"q":"JOHNSON Idowu Trading Company, registered for VAT, projects the following transactions (all VAT-inclusive) for the year ending 31 December 2026: Revenue N30,400,500; Purchase of raw materials N20,140,650; Transport and travelling N1,215,800; Vehicle running expenses N850,420; Office equipment N2,100,500; Furniture and fittings N1,500,000; Motor vehicle N2,000,600; Audit and accountancy fees N150,000; Bank charges N185,050; Miscellaneous expenses N550,500. Compute the VAT payable by the company for the year.","marks":10,"a":[{"p":"Output tax = 7.5/107.5 × N30,400,500 = **N2,120,965**"},{"p":"Less: Input VAT (purchases of raw materials) = 7.5/107.5 × N20,140,650 = **N1,405,162**"},{"p":"Subtotal = N2,120,965 − N1,405,162 = **N715,803**"},{"p":"Less further input VAT (each at 7.5/107.5 of the VAT-inclusive amount): transport/travelling N84,823; vehicle running expenses N59,332; office equipment N146,547; furniture and fittings N104,651; motor vehicle N139,577; audit/accountancy fees N10,465; bank charges N12,910; miscellaneous expenses N38,407 — total N596,712."},{"p":"**VAT payable = N715,803 − N596,712 = N119,091.**"}],"src":"Chapter 7.41","sec":"7.10"},{"q":"Oropo Limited, a manufacturer in Ado, Ekiti State, sold vatable products to a wholesaler, Lapo Enterprises, for N2,000,000 in 2025. Lapo sold the products to a retailer, Mrs. Adele, for N3,500,000, who sold them to consumers for N4,200,000. Assuming no closing inventory at any stage, compute the total VAT payable to the Nigeria Revenue Service for the year ended 31 December 2025.","marks":8,"a":[{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Multi-stage VAT payable","N"],"rows":[["Manufacturer — sales value N2,000,000; gross VAT at 7.5% = N150,000; net VAT (no prior credit)","150,000"],["Wholesaler — sales value N3,500,000; gross VAT at 7.5% = N262,500; net VAT (N262,500 − N150,000)","112,500"],["Retailer — sales value N4,200,000; gross VAT at 7.5% = N315,000; net VAT (N315,000 − N262,500)","52,500"],["**Total VAT payable to NRS**","**315,000**"]]}},{"note":"The total VAT payable (N315,000) equals 7.5% of the final consumer price (N4,200,000) — since each stage only remits VAT on the value it added, the sum collected across all stages always equals the VAT on the final sale price."}],"src":"Chapter 7.41","sec":"7.10"}]}},{"n":8,"t":"Stamp Duties","brief":"The tax on legal instruments: what makes a document \"dutiable,\" the full Ninth Schedule rate table (fixed vs. ad valorem duties, instrument by instrument), exempt instruments, the NRS-vs-States administration tension, how electronic documents and money transfers are treated, the obligation to stamp and the legal consequences of not doing so, the adjudication and appeals process, a cluster of specific-instrument duty provisions, and the offences/penalties regime — all under the Nigeria Tax Act 2025 and NTAA 2025.","outcomes":["Understand the nature of stamp duties","Appreciate the administration of stamp duties","Identify instruments and receipts liable to, and exempt from, stamp duties","Explain the manner of denoting stamp duties and the obligation to stamp","Compute stamp duty payable on fixed- and ad-valorem-duty instruments","Explain the legal implications of unstamped dutiable instruments","Explain the adjudication, appeal and recovery procedures for stamp duties","Explain the offences and penalties relating to stamp duties"],"secs":[{"n":"8.2","t":"Nature and objectives","b":[{"def":{"t":"Stamp duty (s.147 NTAA)","d":"a duty on dutiable instruments under the NTAA, the NTA, or any other law."}},{"key":"The primary objective of stamping documents (s.126 NTA) is to ensure the **validity of instruments**, support **legal enforcement**, and enhance **efficient tax collection**, among other things."}]},{"n":"8.3","t":"Imposition of duties on instruments, including relevant terminologies","b":[{"h4":"8.3.1  Imposition of duties — section 123 of NTA"},{"p":"Duties are imposed on dutiable instruments, at the Ninth Schedule rates, on any instrument that is: **first executed in Nigeria**; or **executed outside Nigeria** but relates to property situated, or a matter/thing done, in Nigeria."},{"h4":"8.3.2  Definitions — section 201 of NTA"},{"def":{"t":"Ad valorem","d":"the value of a transaction or property."}},{"def":{"t":"Receipt","d":"a note, memorandum, writing or electronic inscription whereby money, a bill of exchange or a promissory note for which money is acknowledged/expressed as received, deposited or paid, or whereby a debt or demand (or part of it) is acknowledged as settled/satisfied/discharged, or which signifies/imports such an acknowledgement — whether or not signed with a name."}},{"def":{"t":"Instrument","d":"any document relating to transactions consummated through conventional, electronic or other means."}},{"h4":"8.3.3  Other definitions (ss.127–131 NTA)"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Term","Definition"],"rows":[["**Bill of exchange** (s.127)","Includes a draft, order, cheque and letter of credit, and any document (except a bank note) entitling/purporting to entitle a person to payment by, or to draw on, another person for any sum of money. A \"bill of exchange payable on demand\" includes an order for payment of a sum by bill/promissory note (or for its delivery) in satisfaction or upon a condition/contingency; or a periodic (weekly/monthly/etc.) payment order sent by the payer to the payee (not the reverse)."],["**Promissory note** (s.128)","Any document/writing (except a bank note) containing a promise to pay a sum of money — including a note promising payment out of a particular fund (whether or not available) or upon a condition/contingency (whether or not it occurs)."],["**Sale/purchase of options** (s.129)","Contract notes apply to a contract giving/taking an option to buy/sell stock or marketable security at a future time/price, as they apply to an outright sale/purchase. A **double option** contract is deemed a separate contract for each option."],["**Conveyance on sale** (s.130)","Every transfer of interest/rights in real property is dutiable as a conveyance on sale."],["**Conveyance in consideration of a debt** (s.131)","Where property is conveyed wholly/partly in consideration of a debt due to the recipient, the debt itself is deemed the consideration chargeable with ad valorem duty."]]}}]},{"n":"8.4","t":"Instruments and receipts liable to stamp duties — Ninth Schedule to NTA","b":[{"key":"The Ninth Schedule to the NTA (ss.123, 132, 135, 136(1)-(2)(c), 141(2)) sets a specific duty rate for each named instrument. It groups instruments into two rate structures: **ad valorem** duty (a percentage of value) and **fixed** duty (a flat Naira amount)."},{"h4":"Ad valorem instruments"},{"table":{"align":"llll","head":["Instrument","Rate","Person liable","Exemption"],"rows":[["Agreement/contract accompanied with a deposit (see Mortgage)","0.375%","Mortgagee","Property valued below N10,000,000"],["Agreement for sale of real property (see Conveyance on sale)","1.5%","Transferee",""],["Annuity (see Conveyance on sale)","1.5%","Transferee",""],["Assignment (by way of security, or of any security) (see Mortgage)","0.375%","Mortgagee","Property valued below N10,000,000"],["Irrevocable assignment (upon a sale or otherwise) (see Conveyance)","1.5%","Transferee",""],["Bill of exchange — of any other kind (local and foreign)","0.10%","Payee",""],["Bonds (repayment of money and transfer of stocks)","0.375%","Party taking security",""],["Capital duty on nominal shares","0.75%","Company",""],["Capital duty on loan capital","0.125%","Borrower","Bank overdraft; loan raised for ≤12 months; loan for onward disbursement in an on-lending arrangement"],["Capital duty on loan capital issued wholly/partly to convert or consolidate existing capital","0.1%","Lender",""],["Contract notes for marketable security","0.8%","Transferee","A contract note sent by a broker/agent to their principal, where the principal is itself acting as a broker/agent for a further principal"],["Contract notes (continuation note)","0.08%","Transferee",""],["Contract note (option note)","0.04%","Transferee",""],["Contract note (following a duly stamped option note)","0.04%","Transferee",""],["Conveyance or transfer on sale","1.5%","Transferee","Property valued at N10,000,000 or less; a transfer between associated companies holding ≥90% shareholding in each other (directly or via a third party), where the property was already stamped on initial purchase"],["Conveyance or transfer by way of security of any property/security (see Mortgage)","0.375%","Mortgagee","Property valued below N10,000,000"],["Conveyance or transfers (dispositions inter vivos, except to an entity created by Act for the benefit of Nigeria) (see Conveyance on sale)","1.5%","Transferee",""],["Covenant (payment of money, or transfer/re-transfer of stock) (see Mortgage)","0.375%","Mortgagee","Property valued below N10,000,000"],["Covenant on annuity (original creation and sale) (see Conveyance)","1.5%","Transferee",""],["Covenant on annuity (not original creation and sale) (see Bond, Covenant)","0.375%","Party taking security",""],["Defeasance (of a conveyance apparently absolute but intended only as security for money/stock) (see Mortgage)","0.375%","Mortgagee","Property valued below N10,000,000"],["Demise (see Lease)","Up to 7 years = 0.78%; above 7 years = 3%","Lessee","Property with annual value below N1,000,000"],["Further charge of further security","0.375%","Mortgagee",""],["Marketable security (all types)","0.225%","—",""],["Mortgage, bond, debenture, covenant (see Bonds)","0.375%","Party taking security",""],["Mutual disposition (see Exchange)","1.5%","Transferee",""],["Partition or division (see Conveyance on sale)","1.5%","Transferee",""],["Policy of insurance (life insurance, on premium)","0.075%","Policy holder",""],["Policy of insurance (of any other kind)","0.075%","Policy holder",""],["Promissory note (see Bill of Exchange)","0.1%","—",""],["Reconveyance (of any security) (see Mortgage)","0.375%","Mortgagee","Property valued below N10,000,000"],["Superannuation annuity (see Bond, Covenant)","0.375%","Party taking security","Property valued below N10,000,000"],["Transfer (see Conveyance)","1.5%","Transferee",""],["Transfer of mineral assets","2%","Transferee",""]]}},{"h4":"Fixed-duty instruments"},{"table":{"align":"llll","head":["Instrument","Rate","Person liable","Exemption"],"rows":[["Agreement or contracts (all types)","N1,000","Beneficiary of service","Subject value below N1,000,000; hire of a labourer/employee/artificer/manufacturer/menial servant; sale of goods/wares/merchandise (including a hire-purchase agreement)"],["Agreement or memorandum of agreement under hand","N500","Beneficiary of service","(See Agreement)"],["Bill of exchange — payable on demand","N500","Payee",""],["Bill of lading","N500","—","Master's copy"],["Cheque leaf (bill of exchange)","N50","Account owner",""],["Counterpart or duplicate","N500","Same as original",""],["Draft for money (see Bill of Exchange)","N50","Payee",""],["Guarantee","N500","Guarantor",""],["Letter of credit (see Bill of Exchange)","N500","—",""],["Order (for payment of money) (see Bill of Exchange)","N50","Payee",""],["Policy of marine insurance","N500","Policy holder","Cover notes, slips or other instruments made in anticipation of a formal marine insurance policy"],["Policy of insurance against personal injury","N500","Policy holder","Insurance policies on personal or household effects"],["Receipt (for value from N10,000)","N50","Same as liable for the underlying transaction",""],["Electronic receipt or transfer of N10,000 upwards","N50","Transferor","Money paid into one's own account, or transferred electronically between accounts of the same owner within the same bank"]]}}]},{"n":"8.5","t":"Instruments exempt from stamp duties — section 184 of NTA","b":[{"ol":["transfer of shares in Government or legislative stocks or funds of Nigeria;","any instrument for the sale, transfer or other disposition (absolute, by way of mortgage, or otherwise) of any ship/vessel, or a part/interest/share/property of or in one;","any instrument on which duty would be payable by the Nigerian Government or any of its ministries/departments/agencies;","any instrument on which duty would be payable by a consular officer arising from their official functions — provided the foreign government they represent grants similar exemption to Nigerian consular officers;","any instrument executed by or on behalf of a co-operative society registered under any Act or law;","shares/stocks/securities transferred by a lender to its approved agent, or a borrower, in furtherance of a Regulated Securities Lending Transaction;","shares/stocks/securities returned to a lender or its approved agent by a borrower under such a transaction;","all documents relating to the transfer of stocks and shares; and","electronic transfers/receipts of money below N10,000 (or its equivalent), transfers for salary payment, and intra-bank self-transfers."]}]},{"n":"8.6","t":"Relevant tax authority for collection — section 3(1)(b) of NTAA","b":[{"p":"**Nigeria Revenue Service** (s.3(1)(b) NTAA): empowers the NRS to administer stamp duties without distinguishing between corporate and individual parties. Before the NTAA, the NRS could only collect stamp duty on transactions involving a company, a body of individuals, or banking transactions — even where the parties themselves were individuals."},{"warn":"**State Internal Revenue Service** (s.87 NTAA): this section separately empowers **States** to collect stamp duties — appearing to **contradict** s.3(1)(b), which empowers only the NRS to administer stamp duties. Before the NTAA, states were allowed to collect stamp duty only on transactions **among individuals**. This tension between the two sections is noted in the source text itself, not resolved — candidates should be aware both provisions exist side by side under the new Act."}]},{"n":"8.7","t":"Types of stamp duties","b":[{"p":"There are two types of stamp duties:"},{"h4":"8.7.1  Fixed duties"},{"def":{"t":"Fixed duty","d":"a duty that does not vary with the consideration for the document being stamped — applied at a flat rate (e.g. N1,000, N500)."}},{"p":"Examples: agreements/contracts (all types); bill of exchange payable on demand; bill of lading; cheque leaf; counterpart or duplicate; guarantee; letter of credit; and electronic receipt/transfer of N10,000 upwards."},{"h4":"8.7.2  Ad valorem duties"},{"def":{"t":"Ad valorem duty","d":"a duty that varies with the amount of consideration, per a scale the Act prescribes — the tax authority must know the transaction amount to determine the appropriate duty."}},{"p":"Examples: contract note; bonds (repayment of money and transfer stocks); annuity; conveyance or transfer on sale; promissory note; marketable security; mortgage/bond/debenture/covenant; capital duty on loan capital; agreement for sale of real property, legal mortgage and debenture loans; capital duty on nominal shares; and irrevocable assignment."}]},{"n":"8.8","t":"Administration of stamp duties","b":[{"h4":"8.8.1  Nigeria Revenue Service — section 3(1)(b) of NTAA"},{"p":"The **Nigeria Revenue Service** is the relevant tax authority for assessing, collecting and accounting for stamp duties on instruments where **at least one of the parties is a company** — see §8.6, where the statutory provision is set out in full."},{"h4":"8.8.2  State Internal Revenue Service — section 87 of NTAA"},{"p":"A **State Internal Revenue Service** is the relevant tax authority for stamp duties on instruments executed **between individuals** in that State — again set out in full at §8.6."},{"note":"The study text gives §8.6 and §8.8 as two separate sections although they state the same division of authority — §8.6 under the heading \"relevant tax authority for collection\" and §8.8 under \"administration of stamp duties\". Both numbers are kept so that a reader working from the printed text finds the material at either one. The operative rule is a single one: **a company party means NRS; individuals only means the State Service.**"}]},{"n":"8.9","t":"Electronic documents received in Nigeria — NRS circular No. 2020/05 dated April 29, 2020","b":[{"p":"For an electronic document, receipt or instrument executed outside Nigeria to be regarded as **received in Nigeria**, it must be:"},{"ol":["retrieved or accessed in or from Nigeria;","stored (or an electronic copy stored) on a device — including a computer or magnetic storage — that is brought into Nigeria; or","stored (or an electronic copy stored) on a device or computer already in Nigeria."]},{"eg":{"t":"Illustration 8.1 — electronic records accessed from Nigeria","q":[{"p":"XYZ Limited and ABC Limited carried out a transaction in Togo, using electronic records stored on a server in Togo (or a cloud facility). ABC Limited, using an electronic device in Nigeria, was able to view a copy of the electronic records stored in Togo. Were the electronic records received in Nigeria?"}],"a":[{"p":"ABC Limited accessed the electronic instrument comprising the electronic records while in Nigeria — this constitutes **receipt in Nigeria**, since retrieving or accessing an electronic document from within Nigeria satisfies the Circular's test, regardless of where the underlying records are physically stored."}]}}]},{"n":"8.10","t":"Manner of denoting stamp duties — section 124 of NTA","b":[{"p":"Duties payable on an instrument are paid and denoted by any of:"},{"ol":["tax stamps;","a die;","electronic or digital tagging;","electronic receipt;","issuance of a certificate; or","any other means the relevant tax authority determines."]},{"note":"The Minister may, by Gazette regulation, determine further modes of denoting duties payable, and specify the processes/requirements for taxing dutiable instruments."},{"eg":{"t":"Illustration 8.2 — a WhatsApp acknowledgement as a receipt","q":[{"p":"In response to an amount sent by Mr James, an employee of Circular Limited, the Chief Accountant of Apex Limited sent him a WhatsApp message reading: \"this is to acknowledge the receipt of the sum of N200,000.\" Is stamp duty payable based on the message?"}],"a":[{"p":"Yes. The WhatsApp message evidencing receipt of N200,000 constitutes a **receipt** — hence stamp duty is payable. Mr James must inform the relevant tax authority (NRS) via the e-stamp duty platform; the NRS then raises an assessment and issues a stamp duty certificate/acknowledgement once the appropriate duty is paid."}]}}]},{"n":"8.11","t":"Obligation to stamp — section 125 of NTA","b":[{"key":"A dutiable instrument executed in Nigeria must be stamped **not later than 30 days** after its execution, by the person required to pay the appropriate duty."},{"p":"Liability to pay falls on: the transferee of an interest in real property (other than a voluntary lifetime disposition by the transferor); the beneficiary of a service for which consideration was paid; or any other person taking security in a transaction for which an instrument is executed."}]},{"n":"8.12","t":"Admissible evidence — section 126 of NTA","b":[{"warn":"An **unstamped dutiable instrument is not admissible in evidence** in any court, judicial or arbitration proceeding, and cannot satisfy any evidentiary requirement, unless the Act otherwise states."},{"note":"Exception: an unstamped instrument **may** be given in evidence in a **criminal proceeding**."}]},{"n":"8.13","t":"Recoverability of outstanding duties — section 67 of NTAA","b":[{"ol":["Since stamp duty is a tax, any duty due is a **debt** owed to the relevant tax authority;","where tax due is unpaid within **30 days**, the authority may issue a demand notice for the tax plus penalty and interest;","where the tax/penalty/interest remains unpaid by the notice date, the authority may exercise any recovery power, including legal action;","where tax has been **under-assessed**, the person who should have paid the under-assessed amount must, on demand, pay it;","where tax has been **erroneously repaid**, the recipient of the erroneous repayment must, on demand, repay it;","such amounts may be recovered as if they were tax the person was originally liable for; and","the authority may **not** demand payment more than **6 years** after the date of the under-assessment or erroneous repayment — unless it resulted from an **untrue** document/statement, in which case the 6-year limit does not apply."]}]},{"n":"8.14","t":"Process of adjudication, the time limits, and appeals procedure","b":[{"h4":"8.14.1  Adjudication"},{"def":{"t":"Adjudication","d":"the process by which the relevant tax authority determines the correct amount of duty payable — a document is duly stamped and adjudicated only once the applicable duty has been paid."}},{"p":"Benefits of adjudication: it is the first step in disputing the stamp duties office's view on the correct duty amount; it demonstrates the document is genuine; it satisfies the statutory requirement; and it makes an adjudicated instrument admissible for all purposes, notwithstanding any objection as to the duty payable."},{"h4":"8.14.2  The time limits — section 125 of NTA"},{"key":"Time limit (s.125 NTA): dutiable instruments must be stamped **not later than 30 days** after execution, by the person required to pay the duty."},{"h4":"8.14.3  Appeals procedure — a judicial review of the adjudication process"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Stage","Rule"],"rows":[["**Tax Ombud** (s.36 JRBEA; ¶2 of 3rd Sch.)","The Office of the Tax Ombud serves as an independent, impartial arbiter reviewing/resolving tax/levy complaints — a taxpayer may lodge a complaint, and an investigation must be conducted within **14 days** of receipt."],["**Tax Appeal Tribunal (TAT)** (s.23(1) JRBEA)","TAT settles any tax dispute arising from adjudication of stamp duties. An appeal must be filed within **30 days** of receiving the notice of assessment."],["**Federal High Court** (¶10, 2nd Sch. to JRBEA)","A person dissatisfied with the Tribunal's decision may appeal on a point of law to the Federal High Court, within **30 days** of the decision."],["**Court of Appeal** (¶15, 2nd Sch. to JRBEA)","An appeal against the Federal High Court's decision lies to the Court of Appeal."]]}}]},{"n":"8.15","t":"Legal implications of unstamped dutiable instruments — section 126 of NTA","b":[{"p":"The legal consequences are the subject of §8.12 above, where section 126 is set out in full: an **unstamped dutiable instrument is not admissible in evidence** in civil proceedings, with the single exception there noted (criminal proceedings, and proceedings by the tax authority to recover the duty itself)."},{"key":"The practical implication is the one worth stating in an answer: an unstamped instrument is **not void** — the underlying contract remains valid between the parties — but it becomes **unenforceable in court**, because the party seeking to rely on it cannot put it before the judge. The sanction is therefore evidential, not contractual, and it is cured by paying the duty and the §8.16 penalty and having the instrument stamped, after which it may be admitted."},{"note":"The study text prints section 126 twice — as §8.12 (\"Admissible evidence\") and again here as §8.15 (\"Legal implications of unstamped dutiable instruments\"). Both numbers are kept so the material can be found at either."}]},{"n":"8.16","t":"Mode of calculating ad-valorem duty — Ninth Schedule to the NTA","b":[{"h4":"Mode of calculating ad valorem duty (Ninth Schedule)"},{"p":"Stamp duties are calculated based on instrument type, with rates ranging from flat fees to ad valorem (percentage of value): ad valorem duty is calculated at a specified percentage of the transaction value; fixed duty is applied at a flat rate."}]},{"n":"8.17","t":"Stamp duty on electronic money transfer — Ninth Schedule to the NTA","b":[{"key":"A flat **N50** stamp duty is payable on electronic money transfers of **N10,000 and above** — paid by the **sender**, not the receiver. Banks deduct the amount for onward transmission to the NRS."},{"note":"Exempt: money paid into one's own account, or transferred electronically between accounts of the same owner within the same bank; and salary payments."}]},{"n":"8.18","t":"Provisions as to exchange of real property — section 133 of NTA","b":[{"p":"Any consideration exceeding **N1,000,000**, or a sum equal to the annual national minimum wage (whichever is **higher**), on an exchange of real property for another, is charged the same ad valorem duty as a conveyance on sale."},{"key":"Note the **\"whichever is higher\"** threshold, which recurs through §8.18 and §8.19: the trigger is the greater of a **fixed naira figure** and a **multiple of the annual national minimum wage**. Indexing to the minimum wage keeps the threshold from being eroded by inflation without an amendment to the Act, so the figure to apply is whichever is larger **at the date of the instrument**."}]},{"n":"8.19","t":"Duty on leases with respect to the letting of land and building — section 134 of NTA","b":[{"p":"A lease agreement — grant, sublease, or assignment of a lease — is charged the same duty as an actual lease for the same term/consideration; **exempt** where the annual value is below **N10,000,000**, or below **10× the annual minimum wage** (whichever is higher)."},{"note":"The exemption is generous and is easy to misread: the test is the **annual value** of the lease, not the total consideration over its term. A 10-year lease at N9,000,000 a year has a total consideration of N90,000,000 but an annual value below the threshold."}]},{"n":"8.20","t":"Duty on share capital — section 135 of NTA","b":[{"p":"A company's share capital (or increase of capital) is charged ad valorem duty per the Ninth Schedule rate."}]},{"n":"8.21","t":"Duty on loan capital — section 136 of NTA","b":[{"p":"A company's loan capital is charged ad valorem duty per the Ninth Schedule. \"Loan capital\" means any debenture stock, other stock or funded debt (however named), or any debt raised by a Nigerian corporation/company/body of persons — **excluding** an overdraft, a loan for ≤12 months, and a loan for onward disbursement in an on-lending arrangement (in the last case, the **disbursement beneficiary** pays the duty)."},{"key":"The three exclusions are what the examiner tests: an **overdraft**, a loan for **12 months or less**, and a loan for the purpose stated in the section are **not** loan capital. The common thread is that loan capital means *long-term funded debt* — short-term and revolving bank facilities are outside it."}]},{"n":"8.22","t":"Duty on instrument made for the purpose of issuing marketable security — section 137 of NTA","b":[{"p":"An instrument for issuing marketable securities by/on behalf of a Nigerian company or body of persons is dutiable — whether or not the securities are issued in Nigeria."},{"note":"The words **\"whether or not the securities are issued in Nigeria\"** are the point of the section. It attaches duty to the *instrument of a Nigerian company*, not to the place of issue, so an offshore issue by a Nigerian company is still dutiable."}]},{"n":"8.23","t":"Duty on appraisements — section 138 of NTA","b":[{"p":"Every appraisement/valuation to ascertain the value of real property is dutiable, accounted for by the **appraiser** — a person who values/appraises an estate/real property or an interest in it, for a fee."}]},{"n":"8.24","t":"Duplicates and counterparts of a dutiable instrument — section 139 of NTA","b":[{"p":"A duplicate/counterpart is not deemed duly stamped unless stamped as an original, or certified by the tax authority that full duty on the original has been paid."}]},{"n":"8.25","t":"Duty relating to one instrument covering multiple transactions — section 140 of NTA","b":[{"p":"Where an instrument covers more than one transaction or several distinct matters, **each** is charged duty **separately**."}]},{"n":"8.26","t":"Duty relating to multiple instruments covering the same transaction — section 141 of NTA","b":[{"p":"Where several ad-valorem-dutiable instruments effect the **same** transaction, only **one** (as the authority determines) bears the ad valorem duty — the others are stamped as counterparts at the flat rates in the Ninth Schedule."},{"key":"Sections 140 and 141 are mirror images, and candidates routinely confuse them. **§8.25 — one instrument, several transactions:** duty is charged on **each** transaction separately, so the duty multiplies. **§8.26 — several instruments, one transaction:** only **one** instrument bears the ad valorem duty and the rest are stamped as counterparts at the flat rate, so the duty does *not* multiply. The principle behind both is the same — duty follows the **number of transactions**, never the number of pieces of paper."}]},{"n":"8.27","t":"Provisions on non-monetary consideration — section 142 of NTA","b":[{"p":"Where an ad-valorem-dutiable instrument has non-monetary consideration, its value is deemed the **market value** of that consideration."}]},{"n":"8.28","t":"Computation of stamp duty payable","b":[{"steps":["Identify the type of instrument, to determine whether it is subject to fixed duty or ad valorem duty.","For an ad-valorem instrument, multiply the transaction value by the specified rate — where there is no monetary value, use the market value instead.","For a fixed-duty instrument, apply the flat fee specified in the Ninth Schedule."]}]},{"n":"8.29","t":"Offences and penalties","b":[{"h4":"8.29.1 – 8.29.3  Failure to stamp (s.110 NTAA), failure to disclose facts in a dutiable instrument (s.111 NTAA), and fraud in relation to stamps (s.113 NTAA)"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Offence","Penalty"],"rows":[["**Failure to stamp** (s.110 NTAA)","10% of the unpaid duty, plus interest at the prevailing CBN Monetary Policy Rate."],["**Failure to disclose facts in a dutiable instrument, or underpayment of duty** (s.111 NTAA)","Administrative penalty of N100,000 (in addition to the duty due), OR on conviction: a fine of N50,000, or up to 3 years' imprisonment, or both."],["**Fraud in relation to stamps** (s.113 NTAA) — removing a revenue stamp for reuse, affixing a removed stamp to evade duty, selling/offering a removed stamp for sale, or forging a stamp/denoting implement","On conviction: up to 3 years' imprisonment, or a fine of not less than N2,000,000, or both."]]}},{"h4":"8.29.4  Other offences and penalties of general application"},{"note":"Other general-application offences and penalties (non-registration, late filing of returns, etc.) are covered in Chapter 4, or ss.100-137 NTAA."}]},{"n":"8.30","t":"Chapter review","b":[{"p":"This chapter explained stamp duties and their importance to the legalisation of instruments under the NTA and NTAA, provided definitions for key terms, and set out the relevant tax authority's powers, the manner of denoting stamp duties, and the split of stamping authority between federal and state governments."},{"p":"It identified transactions/instruments requiring stamping for legalisation, as well as those specifically exempt, and discussed the mode of calculating ad valorem duty — duty that varies with the value of the instrument's subject matter."}]},{"n":"8.31","t":"Worked examples — study text questions and suggested solutions","b":[{"p":"The study text sets **three examination-type questions** at §8.31.1, with its own suggested solutions at §8.31.2. All three are reproduced here in full, with the book's answers and added explanation."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 8.31.1, Question 1","t":"Fixed duties, ad valorem duties, and the manner of denoting duty","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Stamp duties are duties basically on **instruments** (defined to include every written document). The scope of instruments liable to stamp duties was expanded to include **electronic documents**. **Required:** Explain the following: **(a)** fixed duties; **(b)** ad valorem duties; **(c)** manner of denoting duty."}],"a":[{"h4":"1(a)  Fixed duties"},{"p":"These are duties that **do not vary with the consideration** for the document being stamped."},{"p":"Examples of instruments being assessed by fixed duties are:"},{"ol":["agreement of contracts (all types);","bill of exchange payable on demand;","bill of lading;","cheque leaf (bill of exchange);","counterpart or duplicate;","guarantee;","letter of credit; and","electronic receipt or transfer of **N10,000 upwards**."]},{"h4":"1(b)  Ad valorem duties"},{"p":"These are duties that **vary with the amount of consideration** and are in accordance with a **scale prescribed by the Act**. The relevant tax authority will need to know the amount involved in the transaction; this will enable it to determine the appropriate duty to be paid."},{"p":"Examples of instruments that are assessed on an ad valorem basis are:"},{"ol":["contract note;","bonds (repayment of money and transfer of stocks);","annuity;","conveyance or transfer on sale;","promissory note;","marketable security;","mortgage, bond, debenture, covenant;","capital duty on loan capital;","agreement for sale of real property, legal mortgage and debenture loans;","capital duty on nominal shares; and","irrevocable assignment."]},{"h4":"1(c)  Manner of denoting duty"},{"p":"**(i)** Duties payable on any instrument shall be paid and denoted by any of the following means:"},{"ul":["tax stamps;","a die;","electronic or digital tagging;","electronic receipt;","issuance of certificate; or","any other means as may be determined by the relevant tax authority."]},{"p":"**(ii)** The **Minister** is empowered, by regulation published in the Gazette, to determine any other modes of denoting duties payable under (i) above, and may specify the processes and requirements for the application of the taxation of dutiable instruments."},{"key":"The distinction in (a) and (b) is the examinable heart of this chapter, and the test is simple: **does the duty move when the consideration moves?** If yes it is **ad valorem** (Latin, *according to value*) — and the authority must be told the amount of the transaction before it can assess. If no, it is a **fixed** duty, assessed on the document itself regardless of the sums in it. Note the pattern in the two lists: fixed duties attach to **documents of process** (agreements, bills of lading, guarantees, letters of credit), while ad valorem duties attach to **transfers of value** (conveyances, mortgages, share and loan capital, marketable securities)."},{"note":"Note the single hybrid on the fixed list: an **electronic receipt or transfer of N10,000 upwards**. The threshold is a size test, but once it is crossed the duty is the flat **N50** of §8.17 — it does not scale with the transfer. That is why it belongs under fixed duties and not ad valorem."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 8.31.1, Question 2","t":"Administration of stamp duties, and four chargeable instruments","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Both section 3(1)(b) of the NTAA and section 87 of the NTAA grant the NRS and the State Internal Revenue Service, respectively, power to administer stamp duties. **Required:** **(a)** Explain the administration of stamp duties in Nigeria. **(b)** Identify **four** instruments that are chargeable to stamp duties."}],"a":[{"h4":"2(a)  Administration of stamp duties in Nigeria"},{"p":"**(i) Nigeria Revenue Service — section 3(1)(b) of NTAA.** This section empowers the Nigeria Revenue Service (the Service) to administer stamp duties **without distinguishing between corporate bodies and individuals**. Before the enactment of the NTAA, the NRS was empowered to collect stamp duties on transactions involving a company, a body of individuals or banking transactions — even if the parties were individuals."},{"p":"**(ii) State Internal Revenue Service — section 87 of NTAA.** This section also empowers states to collect stamp duties. This section **appears to contradict** section 3(1)(b) of the NTAA, which empowers only the NRS to administer stamp duties. Before the enactment of the NTAA, states were allowed to collect only stamp duties on transactions **amongst individuals**."},{"warn":"This is the one place in the chapter where the study text says plainly that the two statutory provisions **conflict**, and an answer that notices the conflict earns the marks. The pre-2025 position was clean — NRS where a company or a bank was involved, the State Service where only individuals were. Section 3(1)(b) now reads as giving the NRS everything, while section 87 still gives the States a collection power, and the Act does not reconcile them. Until it is resolved by amendment, guideline or litigation, state the conflict rather than picking a side."},{"h4":"2(b)  Instruments that are chargeable to stamp duties"},{"p":"**(i) Fixed duties.** Examples of instruments being assessed by fixed duties are:"},{"ul":["agreement of contracts (all types);","bill of exchange payable on demand;","bill of lading;","cheque leaf (bill of exchange);","counterpart or duplicate;","guarantee;","letter of credit; and","electronic receipt or transfer of **N10,000 upwards**."]},{"p":"**(ii) Ad valorem.** Examples of instruments that are assessed on an ad valorem basis are:"},{"ul":["contract note;","bonds (repayment of money and transfer of stocks);","annuity;","conveyance or transfer on sale;","promissory note;","marketable security;","mortgage, bond, debenture, covenant;","capital duty on loan capital;","agreement for sale of real property, legal mortgage and debenture loans;","capital duty on nominal shares; and","irrevocable assignment."]},{"key":"The requirement asks for **four** instruments. Name four — two fixed and two ad valorem is the safest answer, because it shows the distinction as well as the instruments, and costs nothing extra. The book's solution reproduces both full lists, which is far more than the question asks for."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 8.31.1, Question 3","t":"Offences and penalties relating specifically to stamp duties","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Section 100 of NTAA provides for offences and penalties of general application. **Required:** Explain the offences and penalties relating **specifically to stamp duties** under the NTA, 2025."}],"a":[{"p":"**Offences and penalties in relation to stamp duties.**"},{"h4":"(a)  Failure to stamp — section 110 of NTAA"},{"p":"Any person that fails to stamp dutiable instruments in accordance with the relevant provisions of the Nigeria Tax Act is liable to pay **10% of the unpaid duty** and **interest at the prevailing Central Bank of Nigeria Monetary Policy Rate**."},{"h4":"(b)  Failure to disclose facts in a dutiable instrument — section 111 of NTAA"},{"p":"Any person that fails, neglects or omits to fully disclose all the facts and circumstances relating to a dutiable instrument, **or underpays any duty**, is liable — **in addition to the payment of the duty due**:"},{"ol":["to an **administrative penalty of N100,000.00**; or","on conviction, to a **fine of N50,000.00**, or to a term of **imprisonment not exceeding three years**, or to both fine and imprisonment."]},{"h4":"(c)  Fraud in relation to stamps — section 113 of NTAA"},{"p":"Any person who:"},{"ol":["**removes** or causes to be removed from a document any revenue stamp, with intent that the stamp may be **reused**;","**affixes to any other document** a revenue stamp which has been removed, for the purpose of **evading the payment of duty**;","**sells or offers for sale** any revenue stamp so removed; or","**forges a stamp**, or any implement for denoting stamp duties,"]},{"p":"is liable **on conviction** to imprisonment for a term not exceeding **three years**, or a fine of **not less than N2,000,000.00**, or both fine and imprisonment."},{"key":"Notice how the severity escalates with the taxpayer's state of mind, which is the structure an answer should bring out. **Failure to stamp** is treated as a payment default — 10% plus interest, the same shape as the late-payment charge at §4.12.1, with no criminal element at all. **Failure to disclose or underpayment** adds a penalty and makes prosecution possible. **Fraud in relation to stamps** is purely criminal, carries the only mandatory *minimum* fine in the chapter (N2,000,000) and has no administrative-penalty alternative."},{"note":"Two drafting slips in the book worth knowing about. First, the question cites **section 100 of NTAA** for \"offences and penalties of general application\" — section 100 is in fact the **failure to register** penalty (see §4.7.1); the general-application provisions are the rest of the NTAA's Part on offences, covered at §8.29.4. Second, the solution heads (c) \"**section 113 of NTA**\" while its own sub-heading in §8.29 says NTAA — the penalty provisions sit in the Nigeria Tax **Administration** Act, so **NTAA** is correct."},{"warn":"The N50,000 fine on conviction under section 111 is **lower** than the N100,000 administrative penalty for the same conduct. That is what the Act says, and it is not a transcription error — but it is the kind of oddity an examiner may expect you to reproduce accurately rather than \"correct\"."}]}}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Ad valorem duty payable","tex":"\\text{Duty} = \\text{Transaction value (or market value)} \\times \\text{specified rate}"}],"focus":"The Ninth Schedule rate table is the exam's primary target in this chapter — rather than memorising every single rate, anchor on the recurring benchmark figures: 1.5% for most conveyances/transfers on sale, 0.375% for mortgage-related security instruments, N10 million as the recurring exemption threshold for property-related ad valorem items, and N50/N500/N1,000 as the three fixed-duty tiers. Also keep the two administration sections (NRS under s.3(1)(b) vs. States under s.87) straight as a genuine, source-acknowledged tension rather than assuming one simply overrides the other. Finally, the 30-day stamping deadline and its consequence (inadmissibility as evidence, except in criminal proceedings) is a frequently tested pairing — know both halves together.","errors":["Assuming an unstamped instrument is completely void — it is instead merely INADMISSIBLE as evidence in civil/judicial/arbitration proceedings (with a specific carve-out for criminal proceedings), not void or illegal in itself; stamping can generally still be done late, subject to the s.110 penalty.","Treating the NRS as the sole stamp-duty authority in every case — s.87 NTAA gives States a separate collection power that the source itself flags as apparently contradicting s.3(1)(b); do not silently resolve this tension in an exam answer as if only one body has authority.","Confusing \"exempt from stamp duty\" (s.184 — the instrument is entirely outside the duty net) with the many instrument-specific VALUE-BASED exemptions inside the Ninth Schedule itself (e.g. property below N10,000,000) — the latter only exempts small-value transactions of an otherwise-dutiable instrument type, not the instrument type as a whole.","Missing that electronic receipt/transfer duty on money transfers has its OWN specific flat N50 rate and N10,000 threshold, separate from the general \"receipt (for value from N10,000)\" line — both exist side by side in the Ninth Schedule with the same rate but different triggering events (an electronic transfer specifically, versus a receipt generally)."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Within how many days after execution must a dutiable instrument be stamped, under s.125 of the NTA?","o":["7 days","14 days","21 days","30 days","60 days"],"a":3,"w":"Section 125 NTA requires a dutiable instrument executed in Nigeria to be stamped not later than 30 days after its execution.","src":"Chapter 8.9","sec":"8.9"},{"q":"What is the legal consequence of an unstamped dutiable instrument, under s.126 of the NTA?","o":["It is automatically void and unenforceable","It is inadmissible in evidence in any civil/judicial/arbitration proceeding, but may still be given in evidence in a criminal proceeding","It cannot be stamped at all after execution","It becomes admissible only after a 6-year waiting period","It is admissible in every proceeding without restriction"],"a":1,"w":"Section 126 NTA makes an unstamped dutiable instrument inadmissible in evidence in civil/judicial/arbitration proceedings, but expressly allows it to be given in evidence in a criminal proceeding.","src":"Chapter 8.10","sec":"8.10"},{"q":"What flat stamp duty is payable on an electronic money transfer of N10,000 and above, and who bears it?","o":["N50, paid by the sender","N500, paid by the receiver","N100, paid equally by both parties","N50, paid by the receiver","1.5% of the transfer value, paid by the sender"],"a":0,"w":"Per the Ninth Schedule, a flat N50 stamp duty applies to electronic money transfers of N10,000 and above, payable by the sender — banks deduct it for onward remittance to the NRS.","src":"Chapter 8.14","sec":"8.14"},{"q":"A conveyance or transfer on sale of real property valued at N8,000,000 attracts which stamp duty treatment?","o":["1.5% ad valorem duty, since all conveyances are dutiable","Exempt, since the property value is N10,000,000 or less","A flat N1,000 fixed duty","Exempt only if the transferee is a government agency","2% ad valorem duty as a mineral asset transfer"],"a":1,"w":"The Ninth Schedule exempts a conveyance/transfer on sale where the property has a value of N10,000,000 or less — N8,000,000 falls within this exemption.","src":"Chapter 8.3","sec":"8.3"},{"q":"Which of the following correctly describes the tension the study text itself identifies between s.3(1)(b) and s.87 of the NTAA regarding stamp duties?","o":["There is no tension — s.87 was repealed by s.3(1)(b)","s.3(1)(b) empowers only the NRS to administer stamp duties, while s.87 separately empowers States to collect stamp duties — an apparent contradiction the text itself flags","Both sections apply only to individuals, never to companies","s.87 applies only to VAT, not stamp duties","s.3(1)(b) was struck down by the courts as unconstitutional"],"a":1,"w":"The chapter explicitly notes that s.87 NTAA (empowering States to collect stamp duties) appears to contradict s.3(1)(b) NTAA (which empowers only the NRS) — a genuine tension acknowledged in the source rather than resolved.","src":"Chapter 8.5","sec":"8.5"}],"theory":[{"q":"Explain: (a) fixed duties; (b) ad-valorem duties; and (c) the manner of denoting duty.","marks":9,"a":[{"p":"**(a) Fixed duties**: duties that do not vary with the consideration for the document being stamped. Examples: agreements/contracts (all types); bill of exchange payable on demand; bill of lading; cheque leaf; counterpart or duplicate; guarantee; letter of credit; and electronic receipt/transfer of N10,000 upwards."},{"p":"**(b) Ad-valorem duties**: duties that vary with the amount of consideration, per a scale the Act prescribes — the tax authority must know the transaction amount to determine the duty payable. Examples: contract note; bonds; annuity; conveyance/transfer on sale; promissory note; marketable security; mortgage/bond/debenture/covenant; capital duty on loan capital; agreement for sale of real property, legal mortgage and debenture loans; capital duty on nominal shares; and irrevocable assignment."},{"p":"**(c) Manner of denoting duty**: duty payable is denoted by tax stamps, a die, electronic/digital tagging, electronic receipt, issuance of a certificate, or any other means the tax authority determines. The Minister may, by Gazette regulation, prescribe further modes and specify the processes/requirements for taxing dutiable instruments."}],"src":"Chapter 8.31","sec":"8.6"},{"q":"Explain the administration of stamp duties in Nigeria, and identify four instruments that are chargeable to stamp duties.","marks":8,"a":[{"p":"**Administration**: s.3(1)(b) NTAA empowers the Nigeria Revenue Service to administer stamp duties without distinguishing between corporate and individual parties (before the NTAA, the NRS could only collect duty on company/body-of-individuals/banking transactions). Separately, s.87 NTAA empowers **State Internal Revenue Services** to collect stamp duties — a provision that appears to contradict s.3(1)(b)'s NRS-only grant (before the NTAA, states could only collect duty on transactions among individuals)."},{"p":"**Four chargeable instruments** (any four): agreement of contracts; bill of exchange payable on demand; conveyance or transfer on sale; promissory note; mortgage, bond, debenture or covenant; or capital duty on nominal shares."}],"src":"Chapter 8.31","sec":"8.5"}]}},{"n":9,"t":"Withholding Tax","brief":"Deduction of tax at source under the Deduction of Tax at Source (Withholding) Regulations, 2024, and the NTAA 2025: what WHT is (an advance payment, not a separate tax), who must deduct it, the full First-Schedule rate table by transaction and recipient type, exemptions, the relevant collecting authorities, receipting/filing/remittance procedures and deadlines, refunds, credit notes, administrative problems, merits/demerits, double taxation relief, currency of deduction, taxpayer rights, and offences/penalties.","outcomes":["Explain the nature of withholding tax, its objectives, and enabling laws","Identify transactions/income subject to WHT and the relevant tax authority for collection","Identify persons required to make deductions at source","Identify eligible transactions subject to withholding tax","Explain exemptions from withholding tax","Explain the obligation to deduct at source and to issue receipts for deductions","Explain the procedure for filing WHT returns, their content, timeframe, refund procedure and grounds for objection","Explain remittance, credit notes, and treasury receipts","Explain the administrative bottlenecks, merits and demerits of the WHT scheme","Explain the operation of the WHT system, currency of payment, and taxpayer rights/obligations","Explain the offences and penalties for non-compliance with WHT obligations"],"secs":[{"n":"9.2","t":"Nature of withholding tax — regulation 5","b":[{"def":{"t":"Withholding tax (WHT)","d":"a tax deducted at source from payments made to a taxable person for the supply of goods and services."}},{"key":"WHT is **not** another form of tax — it is simply an **advance payment of tax**, off-settable against any subsequent tax liability due on other income; it must **not** be included in the contract price. In certain cases, however, the WHT deducted at source is the **final tax** in the recipient's hands."},{"h4":"Peculiarities of withholding tax"},{"ol":["A taxpayer has **no option** whether to pay it — the person making the payment is statutorily required to deduct (Regulation 4), except where the supplier is a small company or equivalent body unincorporate with a valid Tax ID, **and** the transaction value is N2,000,000 or less in the relevant calendar month; failure to deduct attracts sanctions;","it is an advance payment of income tax, usable as a tax credit against the income tax liability of the year the income relates to — any **unutilised** WHT may be carried forward and set off against future years' income tax; and","it represents the **final tax** in certain cases, such as dividend."]}]},{"n":"9.3","t":"Objectives of the regulations and withholding tax — regulation 1","b":[{"ol":["discourage, minimise or prevent tax evasion;","widen the government's tax net;","serve as a veritable source of government revenue;","serve as a collection mechanism for income tax;","reduce deduction rates for low-margin sectors;","give exemptions for small businesses and manufacturers;","reduce arbitrage between corporate and non-corporate business structures; and","embrace global best practices in deduction of tax at source."]}]},{"n":"9.4","t":"Administration of withholding tax — regulation 2","b":[{"key":"Taxes may be collected by any of Nigeria's three tiers of government. Both the NTAA 2025 and the Deduction of Tax at Source (Withholding) Regulations, 2024 specify the ultimate beneficiaries of WHT: **state governments** (via the State Internal Revenue Service, SIRS) and the **federal government** (via the Nigeria Revenue Service, NRS). Administration of WHT therefore falls within the purview of **both** the NRS and the SIRS."}]},{"n":"9.5","t":"Withholding tax enabling laws","b":[{"note":"The relevant enabling laws are the **NTAA** and the **Deduction of Tax at Source (Withholding) Regulations, 2024**."}]},{"n":"9.6","t":"Persons required to make deductions at source (regulation 2), including the treatment of WHT under section 51 of NTAA","b":[{"p":"Per Regulation 2, the following are empowered to deduct tax at source on eligible transactions:"},{"ol":["a body corporate or unincorporate, other than an individual;","a Government Ministry, Department or Agency;","a statutory body;","a public authority;","any other institution, organisation, establishment or enterprise — including those exempt from tax; and","a payment agent representing any person under 1-5 above."]},{"note":"A body corporate that is a **small company** (s.147 NTAA), or an equivalent body unincorporate, is **exempt** from the deduct-at-source requirement, provided: the supplier has a valid Tax ID; **and** the transaction value is N2,000,000 or less in the relevant calendar month."},{"h4":"Treatment under s.51 NTAA"},{"ol":["When payment is made or otherwise settled, the payer must deduct tax at the prescribed rate;","for **dividend, interest, rent, royalty, directors' fees**, and payments to **entertainers/sportspersons**, the tax — once paid over — is the **final tax** due from a **non-resident** recipient;","dividend distributed by a Nigerian company, received after tax deduction, is treated as the recipient's **franked investment income** and is not charged further tax;","where franked investment income is **redistributed** and tax must be accounted for on the gross distribution, the company may set off the WHT it itself already suffered on the same income;","interest on short-term securities/corporate bonds earned by an individual, after WHT deduction, is **not** charged further tax;","income tax chargeable on an employee — assessed or not — must be deducted from any emolument payment;","the employer must ensure the **aggregate** of all deductions made across a year of assessment equals the employee's actual income tax chargeable for that year; and","a **non-resident company** providing technical/consulting/professional/management services to a labelled startup suffers a **5%** deduction on the income derived — this is the **final tax** for that non-resident company."]}]},{"n":"9.7","t":"Transactions subject to WHT and applicable rates","b":[{"key":"The First Schedule (Regulation 3(a)) sets the WHT rate for each eligible transaction, distinguishing **corporate** from **non-corporate** recipients, and **resident** from **non-resident** recipients."},{"table":{"align":"lrrrrl","head":["Transaction","Corp. resident","Corp. non-res.","Non-corp. resident","Non-corp. non-res.","Remarks"],"rows":[["Dividend, interest","10%","10%","10%","10%","Rates as specified in the law"],["Royalty","10%","10%","5%","5%","Rates as specified in the law"],["Rent, hire or lease","10%","10%","10%","10%","Rates as specified in the law"],["Commission, consultancy, technical, management and professional fees","5%","10%","5%","10%","Higher rate for non-residents, to serve as final tax"],["Supply of goods/materials, other than by the manufacturer/producer","2%","N/A","2%","N/A","Simplified description and reduced rate for low margin"],["Co-location and telecommunication tower services","2%","5%","2%","5%","Lower rate reflecting low industry margins"],["Supply/rendering of services not otherwise listed in the Schedule","2%","5%","2%","5%","Simplified description and reduced rates"],["Construction of roads, bridges, buildings and power plants","2%","5%","2%","5%","Further rate reduction reflecting lower margin"],["Any other form of construction and related activities","5%","10%","5%","10%","Amount due from non-resident is final"],["Brokerage fee","5%","10%","5%","10%","Subject to ¶10(1)(l)"],["Directors' fees","N/A","N/A","15%","20%","Rates reflect the marginal PIT rate for directors"],["Compensation for loss of employment","N/A","N/A","10%","10%","Section 36 of the CGT Act"],["Entertainers and sportspersons","N/A","15%","N/A","15%","Applies on amounts earned in Nigeria"],["Winnings from lottery, gaming, reality shows, etc.","N/A","N/A","5%","15%","With effect from 1 January 2025"]]}},{"h4":"Notes to the First Schedule"},{"ol":["\"Contract of supplies\" covers all forms of supplies/deliveries via competitive bidding tenders, LPOs or other arrangements (oral or written) — but **excludes** across-the-counter cash sales or ordinary-course-of-business supplies;","a contract that is an outright sale/purchase of goods and property in the ordinary course of business is **exempt** from WHT;","reduced rates under a Nigeria/other-country double-taxation treaty apply to a treaty-resident eligible recipient, to the extent contained in the ratified treaty/protocol; and","for supply of goods, rendering of services, or any non-passive-income eligible transaction, the amount deducted is **doubled** where the recipient has **no Tax ID**."]}]},{"n":"9.8","t":"Exemptions from withholding tax — regulation 10","b":[{"p":"Transactions exempt from deduction at source:"},{"ol":["compensating payments under a Registered Securities Lending Transaction (s.201 NTA);","any distribution/dividend payment to a Real Estate Investment Trust or Real Estate Investment Company (s.201 NTA);","across-the-counter transactions, as defined under the Regulations;","interest and fees paid to a Nigerian bank by direct debit of funds domiciled with that bank;","goods manufactured or materials produced by the person making the supply;","imported goods, where the transaction creates no taxable presence in Nigeria for the foreign supplier;","any payment for income/profit exempt from tax;","telephone charges, internet data and airline tickets;","out-of-pocket expenses normally directly incurred by the supplier and distinguishable from the contract fees;","insurance premium;","supply of LPG, CNG, PMS, AGO, LPFO, DPK and JET-A1;","commission retained by a broker from money collected for a principal, per industry norm; and","winnings from a game of chance or a reality show whose content exclusively promotes entrepreneurship, academics, or technological/scientific innovation."]},{"warn":"An exemption from deduction at source under this Regulation does **not** amount to an exemption from the underlying income tax, except as the enabling law itself provides."},{"h4":"\"Sales in the ordinary course of business\" — the Tetra Pak criteria"},{"p":"In *Tetra Pak West Africa Limited v Federal Inland Revenue Service* (Tax Appeal Tribunal, Lagos Zone, 30 November 2020), the Tribunal set 4 criteria for ascertaining what constitutes \"sales in the ordinary course of business\":"},{"ol":["inclusion of the transaction/activity in the objects clause of the memorandum of association;","the nature and practice of the taxpayer's business and industry;","the taxpayer's own history in relation to the activity; and","the frequency of that type of transaction."]}]},{"n":"9.9","t":"Relevant tax authorities for collection of WHT — regulation 2","b":[{"p":"The relevant collecting authorities are both the **SIRS** and the **NRS**: the SIRS collects WHT due from individuals and unincorporated business entities resident in the state during the assessment year; the NRS collects WHT from amounts due to corporate bodies, military/police officers, and external affairs offices. Since 2015, the **FCT IRS** collects WHT from non-residents in Nigeria and FCT residents."},{"note":"For **savings interest**, the relevant tax authority is the tax authority of the location of the bank branch paying the interest."},{"h4":"When the obligation to deduct arises"},{"key":"The obligation to deduct at source arises at the **earlier** of when payment is made, or when the amount due is otherwise settled. For payments between **related parties**, deduction occurs at the earlier of the time of payment or when the liability is recognised. The amount deducted on a payment to a **non-resident** is the **final tax**, except where the income is liable to further tax by reason of a taxable presence in Nigeria."}]},{"n":"9.10","t":"Obligation for deductions to be receipted — regulation 8","b":[{"p":"A person making a deduction must, on remitting it, issue a **receipt** and a statement (in the prescribed Third Schedule format, or as the tax authority prescribes) containing:"},{"ol":["the name, address and Tax ID of the person from whom the deduction was made — where the beneficiary has no Tax ID, an individual provides their **NIN**, and a company its **RC number**;","the nature of the transaction the payment relates to;","the gross amount payable/settled;","the amount deducted; and","the month to which the payment relates."]},{"p":"The deductee may submit this receipt to the relevant tax authority as evidence, to claim tax credit for the amount deducted."},{"warn":"Where a receipt is issued for an amount deducted but **not yet remitted**, the beneficiary is still **credited** by the tax authority — the unremitted amount becomes the **deducting person's own tax liability**, recoverable with applicable penalty and interest."}]},{"n":"9.11","t":"Procedure for filing WHT returns — regulation 7(2)","b":[{"steps":["Prepare a WHT payable schedule, applying the correct rate to each transaction/payment made during the month.","Raise a cheque for the amount due to the NRS and forward it, with the WHT schedule (soft and hard copy), to an approved collecting bank for processing.","Upload the WHT schedule to the NRS portal — once accepted, an electronic receipt (e-receipt) is generated as acknowledgement.","The bank submits copies of the schedule and evidence of payment to the local NRS office, for processing of the bulk receipt and individual receipts (WHT credit notes)."]},{"note":"A numbering gap in the study text, flagged rather than silently corrected: the book runs **§9.11 (procedure for filing WHT returns) straight to §9.13 (contents of WHT returns / payment schedule)** — there is **no §9.12** in the chapter at all. The numbering here follows the book exactly, so §9.12 is absent on this site too; nothing is missing."}]},{"n":"9.13","t":"Contents of WHT returns / payment schedule — regulation 7(3)","b":[{"p":"Each WHT cheque paid to the Revenue must be accompanied by a payment schedule, listing:"},{"ol":["names of the taxpayers who suffered the deductions;","their addresses;","the nature of their activities/services and the period covered;","their Tax ID;","the total amount payable;","the rate of tax applied;","the amount of tax withheld;","the balance paid to the taxpayer;","the tax contract the returns relate to;","the date of payment; and","the cheque number and date."]},{"note":"The Second Schedule template requires: name and Tax ID of the payer; month covered; relevant tax authority; currency of remittance; and, per beneficiary: name/address, Tax ID, nature of transaction, gross amount, rate, and amount deducted — with a signed declaration by authorised personnel of the paying entity."}]},{"n":"9.14","t":"Time frame for compliance with WHT returns","b":[{"key":"WHT deducted from **companies** is remitted to the **NRS**; WHT deducted from **individuals** is remitted to the **SIRS** where the recipient is resident. The compliance timeframe is **21 days** for companies and **30 days** for individuals, from the date the duty to deduct arose or when it was deducted, whichever is earlier."}]},{"n":"9.15","t":"Withholding tax refunds and grounds for objection — section 55(3) of NTAA","b":[{"p":"Where it is proved that a person who suffered WHT is not liable to income tax for the year, or that the tax withheld exceeds the tax assessed, the Board must grant a refund of the overpayment."},{"key":"Section 55(3) NTAA requires any due refund to be made within **90 days** of the tax authority's decision, with the option of a set-off against the taxpayer's other tax liability."}]},{"n":"9.16","t":"Remittance of WHT to tax authorities — regulation 7","b":[{"p":"Taxes withheld are remitted to the relevant tax authority within **21 days** (corporate bodies) or **30 days** (individuals) of the date deducted, or when the duty to deduct arose, whichever is earlier. For **PAYE**, remittance is not later than the **10th day** of the month following payment."},{"note":"Per Regulation 5, the tax paid to the Service is the **final tax** due from a: non-resident recipient of the payment; and a unit trust recipient of the payment."}]},{"n":"9.17","t":"Withholding tax credit notes","b":[{"p":"On payment to the bank or the Withholding Tax Section of the Revenue, the NRS issues **credit notes** in favour of the taxpayers named in the WHT schedule — forwarded by the collection agent to the taxpayers who suffered deduction, so they can claim tax credit against their liability for the relevant assessment year(s)."},{"warn":"A letter from the collection agent showing a taxpayer suffered deductions is **not sufficient** for the NRS to grant WHT credit. Similarly, a government treasury receipt from another department showing tax was deducted is **not sufficient**, since only the NRS collects federal tax and issues credit notes for taxes actually paid into its own coffers. Each state government designs its own forms for granting credit to those whose income was withheld."}]},{"n":"9.18","t":"Payment certificate and treasury receipt","b":[{"p":"A **payment certificate** is issued to a tax agent when they pay withheld taxes to the tax office — the agent's only documentary evidence pending issuance of the **Inland Revenue Withholding Tax Receipt**, which is only issued after the agent's cheque has cleared in the bank."}]},{"n":"9.19","t":"Administrative bottlenecks and other problems of WHT","b":[{"ol":["inconsistent interpretation/application of WHT rates across NRS information circulars for some transactions;","bureaucracy in the process of claiming WHT credit;","the failure-to-deduct penalty is arguably counter-productive — the tax agent acts as an **unpaid** agent of the tax authority, yet is penalised for a failure to deduct;","difficulty determining the correct WHT rate for different transactions, leaving tax agents in a dilemma; and","bureaucracy in the WHT refund process."]}]},{"n":"9.20","t":"Merits and demerits of WHT scheme","b":[{"h4":"9.20.1 – 9.20.2  Merits and demerits"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Merits","Demerits"],"rows":[["Source of government revenue.","A high WHT rate can affect the operational performance of most businesses."],["Expands the tax net, bringing in persons previously unknown to the tax authorities.","Affects the cash flow of most businesses, who receive less than invoiced/budgeted."],["Helps determine a person's turnover, aiding correct income ascertainment.","Likely discourages hard work by revenue officers, since it is self-accounting."],["Makes tax payment less cumbersome for the taxpayer (no need to visit the tax office).",""],["Reduces incidence of tax evasion.",""],["Involves little or no collection cost, being self-accounting.",""],["Saves revenue officers' time for other duties.",""]]}},{"h4":"9.20.3  Withholding tax rate and double taxation relief"},{"p":"The principle behind double taxation relief is to avoid taxing the same income more than once. Where a double taxation treaty exists between Nigeria and another country, a resident of that country receiving Nigerian income is entitled to relief at the treaty rate."},{"key":"The WHT rate is **7.5%** for dividend, interest and royalty, for recipients of a country with an active double taxation treaty with Nigeria."},{"p":"Nigeria has active double taxation treaties with: Canada, Czech Republic, France, Netherlands, Pakistan, Romania, Italy (air and shipping only), United Kingdom, South Africa, Philippines, Belgium, China, Rwanda, Singapore, Slovakia, and Spain."}]},{"n":"9.21","t":"Operation of the withholding tax system","b":[{"p":"When a payer (an agent of the relevant tax authority for WHT collection/remittance) makes any of the listed payments, tax must be correctly deducted at the applicable rate, and the **net** amount paid to the supplier."},{"note":"Except for dividends (payable only by a body corporate), a \"payer\" includes a company (corporate or unincorporated), Government MDAs, parastatals, statutory bodies, and other PAYE-approved organisations — **even if the payer is itself not liable to pay tax** (e.g. an Embassy must still withhold on payments it makes for listed items)."},{"p":"The total amount withheld monthly is remitted to the relevant tax authority by cheque/bank draft — for the NRS, drawn in favour of the \"FGN-NRS-WHT Account\"."}]},{"n":"9.22","t":"Currency of deduction","b":[{"key":"Tax is deducted and paid over in the **currency of the transaction**. Where the transaction is in a foreign currency, the tax is withheld in that foreign currency and paid to the tax authority via the **Central Bank of Nigeria**, which converts it at the ruling exchange rate and credits the appropriate government account."}]},{"n":"9.23","t":"Dual role of ministries, parastatals and other agencies of government","b":[{"p":"Since January 1995, MDAs play a **dual role** in tax collection: acting as agent for deducting WHT from contract sums payable to contractors; and acting as agent for collecting VAT on payments made to contractors."},{"warn":"The distinction matters: WHT is paid to the relevant tax authority depending on whether the taxpayer is corporate or an individual, while **VAT can only be paid to the NRS**, under Nigerian tax law."}]},{"n":"9.24","t":"Rights and obligations of taxpayers — regulation 8","b":[{"p":"A recipient who has suffered WHT is entitled to demand from the payer evidence — the **original receipt** issued by the tax authority — that the tax was both deducted **and** accounted for. Without this, the recipient is unlikely to be given credit against their total tax liability for the year."},{"warn":"In completing an annual tax return, the recipient must disclose income from **all** sources and claim due reliefs — even where income is **tax-exempt**, it must still be disclosed and the exemption claimed. Failure to declare a source of income, even an exempt one, borders on **tax evasion**, a punishable offence."}]},{"n":"9.25","t":"Penalties for non-deduction and late remittance of withholding tax","b":[{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Offence","Penalty"],"rows":[["**Failure to deduct WHT** (s.105 NTAA)","Administrative penalty of **40%** of the amount not deducted."],["**Failure to remit tax deducted, by the 21st day of the following month** (s.107(i) NTAA)","The amount deducted/collected/withheld but not remitted, **plus** an administrative penalty of **10% per annum**, **plus** interest at the prevailing CBN Monetary Policy Rate."],["**Failure to self-account within the prescribed time** (s.107(ii) NTAA)","The tax not self-accounted for, **plus** an administrative penalty of **10% per annum**, **plus** interest at the prevailing CBN MPR."],["**Conviction for an offence under s.107**","Up to **3 years'** imprisonment, or a fine of **not less than** the principal amount due plus a penalty of up to **50%** of the sum, or both."]]}},{"note":"WHT deductions from payments to companies are payable to the NRS; deductions from payments to individuals are payable to the SIRS where the recipient is resident. WHT on dividend, interest, rent and royalty is **final tax** when suffered by non-residents — and, since January 1996, WHT on interest and dividend is also **final tax for individuals**."}]},{"n":"9.26","t":"Chapter review","b":[{"p":"This chapter explained the nature and peculiarities of withholding tax and its enabling laws, profiled the transactions/payments attracting WHT and their applicable rates against recipient category, and detailed the relevant collecting tax authorities and the offences/penalties for non-compliance, alongside double taxation relief."},{"p":"It also covered the operation of WHT (including foreign-currency transactions), the contents of the payment schedule, issuance of credit notes and payment certificates/treasury receipts, refunds and set-offs, the dual role of MDAs, and taxpayers' rights and obligations."}]},{"n":"9.27","t":"Worked examples — study text questions and suggested solutions","b":[{"p":"The study text sets **three questions** at §9.27.1, with its own suggested solutions at §9.27.2. All three are reproduced here in full, with the book's answers, the computation worked through, and added explanation."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 9.27.1, Question 1","t":"Features of WHT, the relevant tax authorities, and the payment schedule","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Withholding tax is not another type of tax, but simply an advance payment of tax. **Required:** **(a)** Explain the main features of withholding tax. **(b)** Identify the relevant tax authorities in relation to withholding tax in Nigeria. **(c)** Identify the contents of a payment schedule for the remittance of withholding tax."}],"a":[{"h4":"1(a)  Main features of withholding tax"},{"ol":["payment of withholding tax is **statutory on the person making the payment**;","withholding tax credit **cannot be used to off-set tax liabilities of prior years**;","it is an **advance payment of income tax**, which is utilised as a **tax credit** against the income tax liability of **the year to which the income relates**; and","withholding tax represents the **final tax on Franked Investment Income** in the hands of the recipients."]},{"key":"Features (ii) and (iii) are two halves of one rule and are the most commonly misapplied point in the chapter: a WHT credit is **year-specific**. It is set against the tax of the year **the income relates to** — not the year the credit note was issued, and never against an **earlier** year's arrears. A company carrying a prior-year liability cannot discharge it with this year's credit notes."},{"note":"Feature (iv) is the exception that proves WHT is normally only an advance. On **franked investment income** — dividend already taxed in the paying company's hands — the WHT deducted is the **final tax**: there is no further liability, and equally no credit to claim, because the income is excluded from the recipient's total profits. The same \"final tax\" treatment applies to the non-resident rates marked as final in the First Schedule at §9.7."},{"h4":"1(b)  Relevant tax authorities in relation to withholding tax"},{"ol":["the **NRS** for **corporate bodies**; and","the **State Internal Revenue Service** for **individuals**."]},{"note":"The test is the status of the **person who suffered the deduction**, not of the person who made it. A company that deducts WHT from a payment to an individual consultant remits that deduction to the **State** Service of the individual's State of residence, even though the deducting company itself answers to the NRS. *(Source: §9.9.)*"},{"h4":"1(c)  Contents of the payment schedule"},{"p":"The contents of the payment schedule for the payment of withholding tax must include:"},{"ol":["names of the taxpayers who suffered the deductions;","their addresses;","the nature of their activities/services and the period covered;","their tax file numbers — now the **Tax ID**;","the total amount payable;","the rate of tax applied;","the amount of tax withheld;","the balance paid to the taxpayer;","the tax contract for which returns were being made;","the date of payment; and","the cheque number and date."]},{"warn":"The book's solution writes item (iv) as \"their tax file numbers [now **Tax Identification Number (TIN)**]\". Under section 7 of the NTAA the identifier is now the **Tax ID** (see §4.2.3), so the bracketed update is itself out of date. Write **Tax ID**."},{"key":"The schedule exists so the Revenue can give each deductee **credit** for what was withheld. That is why it needs the deductee's name, address and Tax ID, the rate, the gross and the net — without them the deduction reaches the Revenue as an unallocated sum and the deductee cannot claim the credit it has already paid for. *(Source: §9.13.)*"}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 9.27.1, Question 2","t":"Aseye Insurance Plc — the implication of failing to deduct WHT","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Aseye Insurance Plc engaged the services of **Desu Consulting Limited** for corporate restructuring and strategic management, in line with the decision of the directors to reposition the company as first amongst equals. The fee for the assignment is **N65 million**."},{"p":"As the leading company in the new mega insurance company, Aseye Insurance Plc **paid the agreed fee on 30 June 2026, but no withholding tax was deducted**."},{"p":"**Required:** Explain the tax implication of this action."}],"a":[{"p":"The tax implication to Aseye Insurance Plc for failure to deduct, according to **section 105 of NTAA**, is that the company will be liable to an **administrative penalty of 40% of the tax not deducted**."},{"h4":"Computation"},{"p":"Desu Consulting Limited is a **company**, and the service is a **consultancy / professional fee**, so the applicable rate from the First Schedule (§9.7) is **5%**:"},{"tex":"\\text{WHT that should have been deducted} = 5\\% \\times N65{,}000{,}000 = N3{,}250{,}000"},{"tex":"\\text{Administrative penalty} = 40\\% \\times N3{,}250{,}000 = N1{,}300{,}000"},{"stmt":{"t":"Aseye Insurance Plc — amounts now payable to the NRS","rows":[["The tax that should have been withheld (5% × N65,000,000)","3,250,000",""],["Administrative penalty for failure to deduct (40% × N3,250,000)","1,300,000",""],["Total payable by Aseye Insurance Plc","4,550,000","","@tt"]]}},{"key":"Both amounts fall on **Aseye Insurance Plc**, not on Desu Consulting Limited. This is the single most important consequence of the WHT scheme: the obligation to deduct is **statutory on the payer** (feature (i) in Question 1), so a payer that hands over the gross amount has not merely made an administrative slip — it has assumed the payee's tax as its own liability, and added a 40% penalty on top. Having paid Desu the full N65m, Aseye must now find a further N3.25m of tax out of its own funds, with only a civil claim against Desu to recover it."},{"note":"Why 5% and not another rate: the First Schedule at §9.7 charges **commission, consultancy, technical, management and professional fees** at **5% for a resident company** and 10% for a non-resident. Desu Consulting Limited is a Nigerian company, so 5% applies. Had the consultant been non-resident, the deduction would have been N6,500,000 and the penalty N2,600,000 — and that deduction would have been the **final tax** on the fee."},{"warn":"The book sets out the two amounts in the order **\"(a) payment of the penalty … (b) payment of the tax\"**, which reverses the logical order and risks suggesting the penalty replaces the tax. It does not: section 105 imposes the penalty **in addition to** the tax that should have been deducted, and the tax remains recoverable from the payer under the §4.10 recovery powers. Both are due."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 9.27.1, Question 3","t":"XYZ Nigeria Limited — the differences between VAT and withholding tax","open":true,"q":[{"p":"A prospective client, **XYZ Nigeria Limited**, bid for a contract, the terms of which included the payment of value added tax (VAT). The client sought your advice on the tax implications. **Required:** Explain the differences between value added tax and withholding tax."}],"a":[{"p":"The major differences between VAT and WHT are as shown in the table below:"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["","Value added tax","Withholding tax"],"rows":[["(a)","An **indirect** tax","A **direct** tax"],["(b)","Imposed on **goods and services**","Imposed on **incomes at source**"],["(c)","Imposed at **7.5%**","Rate applicable is **dependent on the type of transaction**"],["(d)","Backed by the **NTA**","Backed by the **Deduction of Tax at Source (Withholding) Regulations, 2024**, the **NTA** and the **NTAA**"],["(e)","Is governed **only by the NRS**","Is governed by **both the State Internal Revenue Service and the NRS**"],["(f)","VAT **is shared** by the 3 tiers of government","Withholding tax is **not shared** by the 3 tiers of government"]]}},{"key":"Difference (a) is the one that explains all the others. VAT is **indirect** — it is charged on a transaction and passed down the chain to the final consumer, which is why it has a single rate, a single administering authority and a sharing formula (§7.33). WHT is **direct** — it is a slice of a particular person's **income**, taken in advance, which is why its rate varies with the type of income, why the collecting authority depends on whether that person is a company or an individual, and why it is not shared: it belongs to the government that will ultimately assess that person."},{"note":"The practical answer for XYZ Nigeria Limited, which the question invites but the book's table only implies: on a single contract the two taxes run in **opposite directions**. XYZ **adds** 7.5% VAT to its invoice and collects it from the customer, remitting it to the NRS. The customer **subtracts** WHT from the payment it makes to XYZ, remitting that to the Revenue and giving XYZ a credit note. So VAT increases what XYZ invoices, while WHT reduces what XYZ actually receives — and the WHT is computed on the contract sum **excluding** VAT, since VAT is not XYZ's income."}]}}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Withholding tax deducted","tex":"\\text{WHT} = \\text{Gross payment} \\times \\text{applicable rate}"},{"lb":"WHT where the recipient has no Tax ID (non-passive-income transactions)","tex":"\\text{WHT} = \\text{Gross payment} \\times (2 \\times \\text{applicable rate})"}],"focus":"The First Schedule rate table is this chapter's core exam target — organise it around three anchor rates rather than memorising each cell independently: 10% for passive income (dividend/interest/rent/royalty to residents, plus most non-resident passive income), 5%/10% for professional/commission/management-type fees (resident/non-resident split), and 2%/5% for low-margin supply/construction/service transactions (resident/non-resident split). Remember WHT is always an ADVANCE payment except in the specific final-tax cases the chapter names (dividend/interest/rent/royalty/directors' fees/entertainers' fees to non-residents; interest/dividend for individuals since 1996; startups' non-resident technical-service providers at a flat 5%) — conflating \"WHT deducted\" with \"final tax paid\" is the single most common error. Also keep the 21-day (companies) vs. 30-day (individuals) remittance-deadline split memorised as a pair, since exam questions frequently test both halves together.","errors":["Treating WHT as a separate, additional tax burden rather than what it actually is — an ADVANCE payment of the recipient's own income tax liability, creditable (or, in specific named cases, final) — not an extra charge on top of income tax.","Applying a single WHT rate uniformly regardless of recipient type — the First Schedule consistently splits rates by BOTH corporate/non-corporate AND resident/non-resident status, and non-resident rates are frequently double (or otherwise higher than) the resident rate for the same transaction.","Forgetting the no-Tax-ID doubling rule — for supply of goods, services, or other non-passive-income transactions, the deduction rate is DOUBLED where the recipient has no Tax ID, a rule easy to miss since it applies only to this specific transaction category.","Confusing the WHT collecting authority (NRS for companies, SIRS for individuals where resident, FCT IRS for non-residents/FCT residents since 2015) with the VAT collecting authority (NRS only, in every case) — the chapter explicitly flags this distinction under the dual role of MDAs.","Missing that an exemption from deduction AT SOURCE (Regulation 10) is not the same as an exemption from the underlying income tax itself — the two are explicitly decoupled unless the enabling law says otherwise."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"What is the WHT rate on commission, consultancy, technical, management and professional fees paid to a resident corporate recipient?","o":["2%","5%","10%","15%","20%"],"a":1,"w":"Per the First Schedule, commission/consultancy/technical/management/professional fees attract 5% for a resident corporate recipient (rising to 10% for a non-resident corporate recipient).","src":"Chapter 9.5","sec":"9.5"},{"q":"Within how many days must WHT deducted from a payment to a company be remitted to the relevant tax authority?","o":["7 days","14 days","21 days","30 days","90 days"],"a":2,"w":"Per Regulation 7, WHT deducted from payments to corporate bodies must be remitted within 21 days of deduction (or when the duty to deduct arose), whichever is earlier — compared to 30 days for individuals.","src":"Chapter 9.11","sec":"9.11"},{"q":"A resident individual supplier with no Tax ID renders a service (not otherwise listed in the First Schedule) worth N1,000,000, ordinarily attracting a 2% WHT rate. What rate actually applies, and why?","o":["2%, because the standard rate always applies","1%, because rates are halved for individuals without a Tax ID","4%, because the rate is doubled where the recipient has no Tax ID","5%, because the non-resident rate applies by default","0%, because a missing Tax ID exempts the transaction entirely"],"a":2,"w":"The First Schedule notes that for supply of goods, services, or other non-passive-income transactions, the deduction rate is doubled where the recipient has no Tax ID — 2% becomes 4%.","src":"Chapter 9.5","sec":"9.5"},{"q":"A person obliged to deduct, collect or withhold tax fails to do so. What administrative penalty applies under s.105 of the NTAA?","o":["10% of the amount not deducted","25% of the amount not deducted","40% of the amount not deducted","50% of the amount not deducted","A flat N1,000,000"],"a":2,"w":"Section 105 NTAA sets the administrative penalty for failure to deduct/collect/withhold tax at 40% of the amount not deducted.","src":"Chapter 9.17","sec":"9.17"},{"q":"For payments in a foreign currency, in what currency is WHT deducted and paid over to the relevant tax authority?","o":["Always in Naira, converted at the transaction date rate","In the foreign currency of the transaction, via the CBN, which converts and credits the appropriate government account","In US Dollars only, regardless of the transaction currency","In whichever currency the payer prefers","WHT does not apply to foreign-currency transactions"],"a":1,"w":"Tax is deducted and paid over in the currency of the transaction — for a foreign-currency transaction, WHT is withheld in that currency and remitted via the CBN, which converts it at the ruling rate and credits the appropriate government account.","src":"Chapter 9.15","sec":"9.15"}],"theory":[{"q":"Aseye Insurance Plc engaged Desu Consulting Limited for corporate re-structuring and strategic management services, for a fee of N65 million, paid on 30 June 2026 without deducting withholding tax. Explain the tax implication of this action.","marks":6,"a":[{"p":"Corporate re-structuring and strategic management services fall under \"commission, consultancy, technical, management and professional fees,\" which attracts a **5%** WHT rate for a resident corporate recipient — the WHT that should have been deducted is 5% × N65,000,000 = **N3,250,000**."},{"p":"Under s.105 NTAA, Aseye Insurance Plc's failure to deduct makes it liable to an **administrative penalty of 40%** of the tax not deducted: 40% × N3,250,000 = **N1,300,000**."},{"p":"Aseye Insurance Plc is therefore liable to pay both the undeducted tax of N3,250,000 **and** the penalty of N1,300,000."}],"src":"Chapter 9.27","sec":"9.5"},{"q":"Explain the differences between value added tax and withholding tax.","marks":6,"a":[{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Value added tax","Withholding tax"],"rows":[["An indirect tax.","A direct tax."],["Imposed on goods and services.","Imposed on incomes at source."],["Imposed at a flat 7.5% rate.","Rate applicable depends on the type of transaction."],["Backed by the NTA.","Backed by the Deduction of Tax at Source (Withholding) Regulations 2024, the NTA, and the NTAA."],["Governed only by the NRS.","Governed by both the State Internal Revenue Service and the NRS."],["Shared by the 3 tiers of government.","Not shared by the 3 tiers of government."]]}}],"src":"Chapter 9.27","sec":"9.14"}]}},{"n":10,"t":"Customs and Excise Duties","brief":"Duties on cross-border trade and domestically-produced excisable goods under the Nigeria Customs Service Act 2023: the Service's establishment/functions/powers, post-clearance audit, the 6-tier customs valuation hierarchy, computing total duty payable (import duty plus ETLS/CISS/surcharge/VAT/AU levies), collection and remittance mechanics, excisable goods and excise duty computation, prohibitions and restrictions on trade, information/record-keeping duties, and a comprehensive offences-and-penalties regime.","outcomes":["Explain the nature, objectives, and administration of customs and excise duties","Explain post clearance audit and justify the use of consultants","Determine the values of imported and exported goods and ascertain duties payable","Explain the collection and remittance of customs duties","Identify excisable goods and compute their valuations","Explain the imposition, collection and remittance of excise customs duties","Explain prohibitions and restrictions on imports and exports","Explain the provisions on customs-formality information and manufacturer record-keeping","Compute customs duty payable","Explain the offences and penalties under the Act"],"secs":[{"n":"10.2","t":"Nature, objectives and administration of customs and excise duties","b":[{"h4":"10.2.1  Nature"},{"key":"The **Nigeria Customs Service (NCS)** is one of the Federal Government's major revenue-generating agencies. The **Nigeria Customs Service Act 2023** repealed the Customs and Excise Management Act, Cap. C45 LFN 2004, and other customs/excise legislation."},{"h4":"10.2.2  Nigeria Customs Service (NCS)"},{"p":"The Service (\"the Service\") is a body corporate with perpetual succession and a common seal; may sue/be sued in its corporate name; may acquire, hold and dispose of movable/immovable property; and directs, manages and enforces the Act and the listed customs/excise laws."},{"h4":"Functions of the Service"},{"ol":["administer, direct, manage and enforce the Act and customs/excise laws;","collect and account for revenue from customs duties, excise duties, charges, fees and government-assigned special assignments;","administer government trade and fiscal policy as it relates to the Act;","promote trade facilitation in line with international conventions/agreements;","prevent smuggling, customs fraud and other violations under the Act;","carry out border enforcement/regulatory activities in collaboration with relevant agencies;","collate and publish accurate trade statistics and other relevant data;","consult regularly with traders, trade associations and stakeholders to promote industrial harmony;","approve and licence customs agents, who must be Nigerian citizens; and","do such other things as are necessary/incidental to its functions and duties."]},{"h4":"Powers of the Service"},{"p":"Subject to Board approval, the Service may: acquire/hold/purchase/mortgage/deal with property (movable/immovable, real/personal); enter into contracts/obligations; advise/develop regulations on customs and excise management; and issue operational guidelines for efficient administration of the Act."},{"note":"All Service officers have the **same powers, authorities and privileges** the law gives to police officers."},{"h4":"10.2.3  Establishment of the Nigeria Customs Service Board — membership, functions and powers"},{"h4":"Board membership"},{"ol":["a Chairman — the Minister of Finance;","the Comptroller-General — Deputy Chairman;","all Deputy Comptrollers-General of the Service;","the Legal Adviser to the Service (head of Legal Department, appointed from the Attorney-General's office);","a director-level representative from each of: Industry; Trade and Investment; Transport; Aviation; and Foreign Affairs;","the Executive Chairman of the Federal Inland Revenue Service; and","two organised-private-sector representatives, for a **4-year term, renewable once**."]},{"h4":"Functions and powers of the Board"},{"p":"The Board formulates general policy/regulations and reviews/approves strategic plans; handles recruitment, promotion and discipline of officers; and determines remuneration/allowances/pension/other benefits. It fosters synergy with government MDAs and liaises with law enforcement, and may delegate its powers to the Service, any officer, or a committee it constitutes."},{"h4":"Management Committee (s.14(5); composition under s.6)"},{"p":"Composition: the Comptroller-General; all Deputy Comptrollers-General; the Legal Adviser; all Assistant Comptrollers-General; and the Secretary of the Board."},{"p":"Functions (s.14(7)): consider technical/professional customs matters and recommend to the Board; coordinate supervision of the Service's operations; ensure Act compliance through operational supervision; collaborate with agencies/ministries to promote revenue generation and trade facilitation; collaborate with law enforcement on compliance; adopt compliance/regulatory/investigative/control measures against non-compliance; continually review government customs/excise policy and implement it progressively; enforce disciplinary regulations for officer misconduct; and carry out other Board-assigned activities."},{"h4":"Funding of Customs Service operations (s.18)"},{"p":"The Service maintains approved bank accounts, funded by:"},{"ol":["not less than **4%** of the free-on-board value of imports, per international best practice;","revenue from cost-based user-fee assessment/collection;","annual/supplementary government budgetary provisions; and","grants/aids/donations from local or international development partners."]},{"note":"The President may propose an increase to the 4% figure to the National Assembly, on cogent and verifiable factors from the Service."},{"note":"The funding provision immediately above (**section 18**) is not given a section number of its own in the study text's contents list, but the chapter discusses it as part of the administration of the Service. It is kept here, under the book's §10.2.3, rather than being dropped — the **4% of the free-on-board value of imports** is a figure the exam has asked for."}]},{"n":"10.3","t":"Post clearance audit — section 36","b":[{"h4":"10.3.1  Powers of the Service"},{"p":"The Service may conduct a post-clearance audit after goods clearance — at its sole discretion, held at Service offices, the consignee's premises, or the premises of other persons involved in the transaction. It involves inspection of relevant documentation/records, or electronic-system data on the goods/transaction."},{"p":"Persons subject to post-clearance audit: importers and exporters; customs agents; warehouse operators; zone operators; banks/financial institutions; transport firms; freight forwarders; express couriers; excise traders; and other persons in international trade."},{"h4":"10.3.2  Procedure for conducting post clearance audit — section 37"},{"p":"The audited entity receives written notice (date/time) and a questionnaire, and must produce all relevant paper/electronic documents/records (accounting data, physical inventory/records, sales/transaction records, recording books, account records, bank transfers) and import/export-related magnetic/data-processing files. The Service may examine the goods and mode of transport."},{"warn":"Where the audited person fails to cooperate, the auditor must: stop the audit; retain documents/data already procured; and report the refusal to the Comptroller-General. Refusal to cooperate attracts a fine of **N1,500,000 per day** — beyond **7 days**, the person may be **suspended from all customs transactions**."},{"p":"Where the audit discloses an attempt to evade duties/fees or criminal activity, the audit may be suspended and the matter reported to the Comptroller-General. The audited person may, within **7 working days** of the audit's conclusion, submit a written explanation on any raised issue."},{"h4":"10.3.3  Audit disclosure of attempted evasion of duties — section 38"},{"p":"A person undergoing audit must: assist the auditors as required; allow access to facilities, goods, books, documents, electronic records and transport means; present required documents/records within a reasonable time; and may be represented by a person of their choice able to supply the required information."},{"h4":"10.3.4  Payment of additional duties and customs duties on completion of audit — section 39"},{"p":"Where audited additional duties are owed, the Service gives a notice of underpayment and requests payment (plus interest/penalties, interest based on the CBN monetary policy rate). Excess payments discovered are **refunded**."},{"h4":"10.3.5  Trader’s system audit — section 40"},{"p":"Where a trader's system audit shows the system is reliable, records are generally in order, and no infractions occurred, the trader may qualify for **Special Simplified Customs Procedures**."}]},{"n":"10.4","t":"Use of consultants — section 42","b":[{"key":"Subject to Board approval, the Service may employ consultants for advice on new customs control measures and other purposes — but a consultant may **not** be authorised to actually **carry out** a customs control measure."}]},{"n":"10.5","t":"Determination of duties and customs duties on imported and exported goods","b":[{"h4":"10.5.1 – 10.5.2  Rates and basis of computation"},{"p":"Customs duties are levied on imports at rates from **5% to 35%** of import value, at each port of entry (airport/land border/sea port/inland waterway), assessed by reference to the Harmonised Commodity and Coding System (HS code)."},{"note":"Nigerian-registered airlines providing commercial air transport are entitled to **duty-free** importation of aircraft/engines/spare parts/components, whether purchased or leased."},{"p":"The primary basis for customs value is the **transaction value** — the price actually paid/payable for goods sold for export to Nigeria, adjusted as needed. The rate the Tariff Technical Committee determines (and the President approves) is applied to the import/export quantity to derive the duty payable, using the Act's classification/valuation/origin standards."},{"h4":"10.5.3  Computation of the customs value of imported goods — sections 69 to 74"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Method (in order of application)","Basis"],"rows":[["**(a) Transaction value** (s.69)","The price actually paid/payable for goods sold for export to Nigeria, adjusted per Article 8 of the Agreement on Customs Valuation. Applies only if: no disposition/use restrictions beyond law-required, geographic-resale, or value-immaterial ones; the sale/price is not subject to an undeterminable-value condition; no resale proceeds accrue to the seller (absent adjustment); and the buyer/seller are unrelated — OR, if related, the price is proven unaffected by the relationship (related parties: officers/directors of each other; employer/employee; ≥5% direct/indirect voting-stock ownership/control; direct/indirect control of one by the other or both by a third party; or family members)."],["**(b) Transaction value of identical goods** (s.70)","Used if (a) fails — the transaction value of identical goods exported to Nigeria at/about the same time, at the same commercial level/quantity (adjusted if needed, with demonstrated evidence); the **lowest** value is used if more than one is found."],["**(c) Transaction value of similar goods** (s.71)","Used if (a)-(b) fail — the same approach as (b) but for **similar** (not identical) goods; again the lowest value if multiple values are found."],["**(d) Deductive value** (s.72)","Used if (a)-(c) fail and the imported (or identical/similar) goods are sold in Nigeria as imported — the greatest-aggregate-quantity unit price to unrelated persons, LESS usual commissions/profit-and-expense additions, usual transport/insurance/associated costs in Nigeria, and Nigerian customs duties/charges. If no sale occurs at/about import time, the earliest sale within **90 days** after importation is used; if the goods are only sold after further processing, the declarant may request a value based on that sale, less the value added by processing."],["**(e) Computed value** (s.73)","The most difficult method, rarely used — the sum of: materials/fabrication/processing cost; a usual profit-and-general-expense margin for goods of the same class/kind exported to Nigeria; and transport/loading/unloading/handling/insurance costs to the port of importation. A non-resident producer may be required to produce records; information may be verified in Nigeria with the producer's and their government's agreement."],["**(f) Residual value** (s.74)","Used only if all prior methods fail — reasonable means consistent with the WTO Agreement on Implementation of Article VII of GATT 1994, using Nigerian data. May **NOT** be based on: the Nigerian selling price of Nigerian-produced goods; a higher-of-two-alternative-values system; the domestic price in the exporting country; production cost other than computed value already used for identical/similar goods; the export price to a country other than Nigeria; minimum customs values; or arbitrary/fictitious values."]]}},{"note":"The same valuation methodology (s.76) also applies to determining the value of goods for **export**, and for assessing all duties/fees on imports, exports and statistics collection."},{"h4":"Computation of total duty payable"},{"key":"Import duty (ID): **ID = HS% × CIF**, where HS% is the harmonised-code tariff percentage, and CIF = Cost (commercial invoice) + Insurance (marine insurance) + Freight."},{"h4":"Other levies and taxes"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Levy","Rate and basis"],"rows":[["**ECOWAS Trade Liberalisation Scheme (ETLS) levy**","0.5% of CIF value, on all goods imported from outside Africa (effective 1 January 2023)."],["**Comprehensive Import Supervision Scheme (CISS) levy**","1% administrative charge on FOB value of all imports, at the approved e-Form \"M\" exchange rate."],["**Surcharge**","7% of import (surface) duty."],["**VAT**","7.5% of CIF plus all charges — i.e. 7.5% of (CIF + ID + ETLS + CISS + Surcharge)."],["**AU/Multilateral levy**","0.5% of CIF, on eligible goods imported from outside Africa — funds Nigeria's capital contributions/subscriptions to the African Union, AfDB, Afreximbank, EBID, Islamic Development Bank, UN and other designated multilateral institutions."]]}},{"note":"The study text works two numerical examples on this material, but prints them not here — where the rates and bases are explained — but as **questions 2 and 3 of the chapter's worked examples at §10.14**. They are therefore set out in full at §10.14 rather than duplicated here. Work them alongside this section: question 2 applies the **specific** (per-centilitre) duty and question 3 applies the **ad valorem** CIF-based duties and levies through to VAT."}]},{"n":"10.6","t":"Collection and remittance of customs duty","b":[{"p":"The NCS administers/collects customs duties on imports; the importer's bank — if a **designated bank** — processes the e-Form \"M,\" collects and remits duties to the CBN. Where an importer processes the e-Form \"M\" through a non-designated bank, duty payment must be made at a designated bank, and a copy of the duty-payment receipt submitted to the processing bank."},{"p":"The designated bank sends an e-confirmation message to the NCS acknowledging receipt of duty/taxes, and effects a **daily electronic transfer** to the respective CBN pool accounts."},{"key":"Per NCS Board Circular NCS/PR/SP/S.582//025/027/VOL111 (7 September 2025), Nigeria's official **De Minimis threshold** is **US$300** — exempting low-value imports, e-commerce consignments and passenger baggage from import duties/taxes, restricted to **4 importations per annum**, provided the goods are not prohibited/restricted."}]},{"n":"10.7","t":"Excisable goods and their valuations","b":[{"h4":"10.7.1  Excisable goods"},{"def":{"t":"Excise duty","d":"a tax levied on the production, sale or consumption of certain goods."}},{"p":"Excisable goods in Nigeria include: alcoholic beverages (beer, wine, spirits, etc.); tobacco products (cigarettes, cigars, snuff, etc.); soft drinks (carbonated drinks, fruit juices, etc.); cosmetics (perfumes, toiletries, etc.); plastic/rubber products (plastic bags, tyres, etc.); and telecommunication services."},{"h4":"10.7.2  Valuation of excisable goods"},{"p":"Valuation is based on: manufacturers' price (sold to distributor/retailer); ex-factory price (factory-gate price, excluding transport); or retail price (sold to final consumer)."},{"h4":"Valuation methods"},{"ol":["**transaction value method** — determined by the actual selling price;","**comparable uncontrolled price method** — comparing prices of similar goods sold in the same market; and","**cost-plus method** — a markup added to the cost of production."]}]},{"n":"10.8","t":"Imposition, collection and remittances of excise customs duties","b":[{"h4":"10.8.1  Imposition of excise duty — section 170"},{"p":"Excise duty is imposed/chargeable on goods produced in the excise factory, collected when the dutiable transaction occurs — based on the value of excisable goods, or a stated amount per unit, as the regulations specify."},{"p":"For goods produced in the customs territory, the dutiable transaction is **removal** from the production premises (goods stored in a bonded warehouse are not considered removed). For imports, the dutiable transaction is **entry** into the customs territory. Goods removed for export, or loaded as stores on a vessel leaving Nigeria, are **exempt**."},{"h4":"10.8.2  Collection and remittance of excise customs duties — section 171"},{"key":"The Service ensures **monthly** collection — duty for each month is paid by the manufacturer within the **first 21 working days** of the following month; failure attracts a **10% per month** penalty of the amount due, for each month it remains unpaid."},{"h4":"10.8.3  Amount of excise duty — section 172"},{"p":"Unit-based duty: stated amount per unit × quantity. Value-based duty: value of excisable goods × excise duty rate."},{"p":"For domestically-produced goods, value is the compensation receivable from the customer/other person (excluding excise duty/VAT), capped at the **ex-factory gate** price (exclusive of selling/distribution expenses, related charges, other non-production expenses, excise duty and VAT) — where a producer sells at both wholesale and retail, the **ex-factory price** governs."},{"p":"For imports, the dutiable amount is the customs value (per customs law), not less than the CIF invoice (excluding other charges/levies/excise duty/VAT), plus customs duties payable (excluding excise duty/VAT)."},{"note":"Except for returnable containers, the container's price is included in the excisable-goods value."},{"warn":"Manufacturing excisable goods **without a valid licence is prohibited**."}]},{"n":"10.9","t":"Prohibitions and restrictions on imports and exports","b":[{"p":"The President may, by order: prohibit importation/exportation of all or specified goods; or allow it only with the written permission of a specified authority."},{"p":"Imports/exports are, where applicable, subject to prohibitions/restrictions relating to:"},{"ol":["public security, public morals and public policy;","preventing/relieving critical food-stuff shortages;","protecting human, animal or plant health/life;","protecting the environment;","protecting national treasures of artistic/historic/archaeological value;","environmental protection under relevant laws/conventions;","currency and negotiable instruments under the Money Laundering Prohibition Act;","fishery conservation;","controlled goods under international laws/conventions/agreements — including CITES (endangered species trade) and chemicals monitored under the WCO Global Shield programme."]},{"warn":"Prohibited goods found within the customs territory are **prima facie deemed illegally imported**, and the Customs Service takes appropriate enforcement measures."}]},{"n":"10.10","t":"Provision of information for customs formalities and controls — section 22","b":[{"h4":"By persons, to the Service"},{"p":"Anyone directly/indirectly involved in customs formalities/controls must, on request and within any specified time limit, provide required documents/information (prescribed form) and necessary assistance. Lodging a declaration/notification makes the person responsible for: accuracy/completeness of the information; authenticity of documents; and compliance with obligations under the customs procedure/authorised operation — the same responsibility extends to a customs representative acting for that person."},{"h4":"By the Service, to the public (s.22(2))"},{"p":"The Service must make relevant customs information of general application readily available (website and other designated forms/places) — without disclosing private/confidential information unless legally required; any charge for information (where not free) is limited to the actual cost of the service."},{"p":"The Service must promptly publish: importation/exportation/transit procedures and required forms; applied duty/charge rates; classification/valuation/origin rules; import/export/transit restrictions/prohibitions; all Service and other-agency fees/charges; penalty provisions for breaches; appeal procedures; applicable international agreements; allowances and duty-free concessions; clearing agent/broker notices; and the current law establishing the Service."}]},{"n":"10.11","t":"Provisions relating to the furnishing of information by manufacturers and keeping of goods","b":[{"h4":"10.11.1  Provisions relating to the furnishing of information by manufacturers"},{"p":"Manufacturers of excisable goods must furnish: production data (quantity/value produced); inventory data (quantity/value in stock); sales data (quantity/value sold); pricing-policy information; and other relevant data (raw materials, labour costs, overheads) as required."},{"ol":["**monthly** returns to the Comptroller-General on production/inventory/sales activity; and","**annual** returns to the Comptroller-General, covering the preceding year's production/inventory/sales."]},{"h4":"10.11.2  Keeping of records by manufacturers of excisable goods"},{"p":"Production records; inventory-level records; sales records; purchase records (raw materials/other inputs); and records of financial transactions (receipts, payments, etc.)."},{"key":"Records must be kept for **at least 6 years** from the date of the transaction/activity."}]},{"n":"10.12","t":"Offences and penalties","b":[{"p":"Selected offences and their corresponding penalties under the Nigeria Customs Service Act 2023:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Offence","Penalty"],"rows":[["Refusal to cooperate with an auditor (s.37(5))","N1,500,000 per day of refusal; beyond 7 days, suspension from all customs transactions."],["Audit discloses attempted duty evasion/criminal activity (s.37(6))","Audit may be suspended; matter reported to the Comptroller-General."],["Failure to report importation/exportation as required (s.46(5))","On conviction: a fine of N10,000,000 or up to 5 years' imprisonment, or both; undeclared goods may be detained/warehoused until reported/explained."],["Bulk broken, stowage altered, goods staved/destroyed/thrown overboard, or a container opened before required report — by a ship/aircraft/vehicle from outside Nigeria (s.46(7))","Master/captain/person in charge liable on conviction to a fine of N20,000,000 or up to 10 years' imprisonment, or both."],["Unpaid customs debt beyond the prescribed period, despite an approved 30-day deferment (s.92(2))","Service takes all necessary legal recovery measures; interest accrues from the due date at the prevailing CBN monetary policy rate."],["Abandonment of imported goods (s.120)","Importer/holder bears the cost of destruction or disposal of the goods."],["Operating an unlicensed customs warehouse, or unauthorised alteration/addition to a warehouse (s.122(8))","N10,000,000 penalty; licence may be revoked on review."],["Using a warehouse in contravention of licence conditions (s.122(9))","N10,000,000 penalty, or licence revocation, or both."],["Using a building for unlicensed warehousing (s.122(10))","N20,000,000 penalty, plus N1,000,000 for each day/part-day of continued use."],["Warehouse keeper fails to provide required office facilities, or contravenes a Service compliance direction (s.124(3))","No further goods warehoused until compliance; contravention of a direction attracts N20,000,000 plus N1,000,000 per day/part-day of continuation."],["Receiving/removing warehouse goods contrary to Service-specified times (s.125(2))","Warehouse keeper and remover severally liable on conviction to N20,000,000 or up to 5 years' imprisonment, or both; repeated violations may revoke the warehouse licence."],["Failure to report arrival of goods at a bonded warehouse (s.126(1))","N3,000,000 fine."],["Failure to keep warehoused goods marked as directed (s.126(5))","N3,000,000 fine."],["Failure to remove goods from a closed warehouse within 90 days of notice (s.132(2))","Forfeiture of the goods."],["Unauthorised opening/access to a bonded/government warehouse or its goods (s.133(1))","On conviction: N10,000,000 or up to 5 years' imprisonment, or both."],["Failure to leave a bonded/government warehouse when an officer requests (s.133(2))","N5,000,000 or up to 3 years' imprisonment, or both."],["Wilful destruction/damage of goods in a bonded/government warehouse (s.133(3))","On conviction: N10,000,000 or up to 5 years' imprisonment, or both."],["Obtaining, or attempting to obtain, a drawback amount not lawfully entitled to, or in excess of entitlement (s.147(1))","On conviction: N10,000,000 or up to 3 years' imprisonment, or both."],["Exporting/loading dutiable-but-unpaid, or prohibited, goods with intent to evade duty/prohibition (s.150(1)(b))","On conviction: up to 2 years' imprisonment without the option of a fine; forfeiture where exportation is prohibited; or (where duty is chargeable) a fine of N3,000,000 or twice the goods' value, whichever is greater."],["Unauthorised use of methylated spirits activities (s.177(2), re s.181)","On conviction: N2,000,000 or up to 1 year's imprisonment."],["Insufficient evidence to convict under s.181, but the offence occurred on the person's own premises with implied knowledge (s.177(3))","On conviction: N1,000,000 or up to 6 months' imprisonment."],["Manufacturing/possessing excisable goods without a proper excise licence (s.201(2)-(3))","Goods, plant and materials subject to forfeiture; on conviction: N5,000,000 or up to 3 years' imprisonment, or both (also applies to failing to comply with s.200 regulations, with forfeiture of the relevant goods)."],["Excise licence holder's record-keeping contravention (s.206(4))","On conviction: N5,000,000 plus N50,000 per day of continued default, or up to 3 years' imprisonment."],["Excise licence holder fails, without reasonable cause, to submit a required audit certificate (s.207(4))","On conviction: N5,000,000 plus N50,000 per day of continued default, or up to 3 years' imprisonment."]]}}]},{"n":"10.13","t":"Chapter review","b":[{"p":"This chapter fully explained the objectives, nature and administration of the Nigeria Customs Service, and the duties and functions of the Service and its Board/Management Committee. Rates and the basis of computing customs value, excisable items and their valuation, the furnishing of information by manufacturers, and offences/penalties under the Act were well discussed."}]},{"n":"10.14","t":"Worked examples — study text questions and suggested solutions","b":[{"p":"The study text sets **three questions** at §10.14.1 — one theory question and two computations — with its own suggested solutions at §10.14.2. All three are reproduced here in full, with the book's answers, the arithmetic shown step by step, and added explanation."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 10.14.1, Question 1","t":"Functions of the Nigeria Customs Service","open":true,"q":[{"p":"The **Nigeria Customs Service Act, 2023** was enacted to repeal the Customs and Excise Management Act, Cap. C45, Laws of the Federation of Nigeria, 2004 (as amended) and other customs and excise legislation. **Required:** Explain the functions of the Nigeria Customs Service."}],"a":[{"p":"The functions of the Nigeria Customs Service are to:"},{"ol":["administer, direct, manage and enforce the provisions of this Act and the customs and excise laws;","collect and account for revenue from customs duties, excise duties, charges, fees and special assignments as may be assigned to it by the governments;","administer trade and fiscal policies of the government as it relates to the Act;","promote trade facilitation in line with international conventions and agreements as it relates to customs administration;","prevent smuggling, customs fraud and all other violations under this Act;","carry out all border enforcement and regulatory activities required by law, in collaboration with relevant agencies;","collate and publish accurate trade statistics and other relevant data as they relate to customs and excise;","engage in regular consultations with individual traders, trade associations and other relevant stakeholders to promote industrial harmony;","approve and licence customs agents, who shall be **citizens of Nigeria**; and","do such other things as are necessary for, or incidental to, the performance of its functions and the discharge of its duties under this Act and the laws of Nigeria."]},{"key":"The ten functions fall into **four groups**, and organising an answer this way reads far better than a flat list: **revenue** (collect and account for duties, charges and fees); **enforcement** (prevent smuggling and customs fraud, carry out border enforcement); **trade facilitation** (administer trade and fiscal policy, facilitate trade under international conventions, licence customs agents, consult with traders); and **information** (collate and publish trade statistics). The Service is therefore not only a tax collector — the facilitation and statistics functions are what distinguish the 2023 Act from the repealed CEMA. *(Source: §10.2.2.)*"},{"note":"Function (i) carries a nationality requirement that is easy to miss and easy to examine: customs agents approved and licensed by the Service **shall be citizens of Nigeria**."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 10.14.1, Question 2","t":"Mr Larry Obinna — import duty on alcoholic drinks charged per centilitre","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Mr Larry Obinna, a licensed importer/exporter of alcoholic drinks, imported these items from the UK on 13 October 2025:"},{"table":{"align":"lrrr","head":["Item","Qty (bottles)","Price/bottle (£)","Value (£)"],"rows":[["ABC super wine","2,700","12.00","32,400"],["XYZ standard spirit","3,000","14.50","43,500"],["PQR action stout","6,000","5.00","30,000"]]}},{"p":"Each bottle contains 25 centilitres. Duty payable per centilitre: ABC super wine N1.25; XYZ standard spirit N1.50; PQR action stout N0.30. (Exchange rate: N2,100/£1.) **Required**: determine the import duties payable to the NCS."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lrrr","head":["Item","Centilitres","Rate/cl (N)","Duty (N)"],"rows":[["ABC super wine (2,700 × 25cl)","67,500","1.25","84,375"],["XYZ standard spirit (3,000 × 25cl)","75,000","1.50","112,500"],["PQR action stout (6,000 × 25cl)","150,000","0.30","45,000"],["**Total import duty payable**","","","**241,875**"]]}},{"note":"The price per bottle (and the exchange rate) is ignored in this computation, since these specific duties are charged **per centilitre**, not ad valorem — and the question does not ask for VAT."},{"h4":"The book's own workings, item by item"},{"p":"**ABC super wine** — units imported 2,700 bottles; conversion to centilitres 2,700 × 25 = **67,500 cl**; duty payable per centilitre N1.25; amount of import duty payable = 67,500 × N1.25 = **N84,375**."},{"p":"**XYZ standard spirit** — units imported 3,000 bottles; conversion to centilitres 3,000 × 25 = **75,000 cl**; duty payable per centilitre N1.50; amount of import duty payable = 75,000 × N1.50 = **N112,500**."},{"p":"**PQR action stout** — units imported 6,000 bottles; conversion to centilitres 6,000 × 25 = **150,000 cl**; duty payable per centilitre N0.30; amount of import duty payable = 150,000 × N0.30 = **N45,000**."},{"p":"The total import duties payable by Mr Larry Obinna = N84,375 + N112,500 + N45,000 = **N241,875**."},{"key":"This is a **specific** duty, not an ad valorem one — it is charged by **physical quantity** (per centilitre of liquid), so the value of the goods is irrelevant. That is why the price per bottle and the N2,100/£1 exchange rate are **both redundant data**, deliberately planted. The book says so itself: *\"Price per bottle should be ignored in the computation as the requirement of the question does not include computation of VAT payable.\"* Contrast question 3, where every duty is a percentage of value and the price is the whole basis."},{"note":"The one computational trap is the unit conversion. Duty is quoted **per centilitre** but quantity is given **in bottles**, so each line must be converted first (bottles × 25 cl) before the rate is applied. Applying the rate to bottles would understate the duty by a factor of 25 — N9,675 instead of N241,875."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 10.14.1, Question 3","t":"XYZ Limited — total duty payable including all levies and VAT","open":true,"q":[{"p":"XYZ Limited imported goods from London in August 2025: Free on board N2,500,000; Insurance N40,000; Freight N500,000. Rates: import duty 5% of CIF; surcharge 7% of the surface duty; ETLS levy 0.5% of CIF; CISS levy 1% of FOB; AU/multilateral levy 0.5% of CIF; VAT 7.5% of total value. **Required**: compute the total duty payable to the NCS."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Item","N"],"rows":[["Free on board","2,500,000"],["Insurance","40,000"],["Freight","500,000"],["**Cost, insurance and freight (CIF)**","**3,040,000**"],["Surface duty / import duty (5% of CIF)","152,000"],["Surcharge (7% of surface duty)","10,640"],["CISS levy (1% of FOB)","25,000"],["ETLS levy (0.5% of CIF)","15,200"],["AU/Multilateral levy (0.5% of CIF)","15,200"],["**Subtotal — duties/levies**","**218,040**"],["VAT (7.5% of [CIF + duties/levies] = 7.5% × N3,258,040)","244,353"],["**Total duty payable to Nigeria Customs Service**","**462,393**"]]}},{"h4":"How the figures follow"},{"tex":"\\text{CIF} = N2{,}500{,}000 + N40{,}000 + N500{,}000 = N3{,}040{,}000"},{"ol":["**Surface duty (import duty)** = 5% × N3,040,000 = **N152,000**;","**Surcharge** = 7% × **the surface duty**, not of CIF = 7% × N152,000 = **N10,640**;","**CISS levy** = 1% × **free on board**, not of CIF = 1% × N2,500,000 = **N25,000**;","**ETLS levy** = 0.5% × N3,040,000 = **N15,200**;","**AU/multilateral levy** = 0.5% × N3,040,000 = **N15,200**;","duties and levies together = N152,000 + N10,640 + N25,000 + N15,200 + N15,200 = **N218,040**;","**VAT** = 7.5% × (CIF + duties and levies) = 7.5% × (N3,040,000 + N218,040) = 7.5% × N3,258,040 = **N244,353**; and","**total payable** = N218,040 + N244,353 = **N462,393**."]},{"key":"Three different bases appear in one question, and mixing them up is where the marks go. The import duty, ETLS levy and AU levy are percentages of **CIF**. The surcharge is a percentage of **the import duty itself** — a tax on a tax. The CISS levy is a percentage of **FOB**, so insurance and freight are excluded from it. Read each rate's stated base rather than assuming CIF throughout: using CIF for the CISS levy would give N30,400 instead of N25,000."},{"note":"Note what VAT is charged on: **CIF plus all the duties and levies**, not CIF alone. Import duties form part of the value of the imported supply under section 149 of the NTA (see §7.9.1), so they enter the VAT base — which is why the VAT here is computed on N3,258,040 and not on N3,040,000, a difference of N16,353."},{"warn":"A numbering defect in the book's question, flagged rather than silently corrected: in the list of assumed rates, **two items are both labelled \"(v)\"** — the AU/multilateral levy and value added tax. There are six rates in all, so the last should be (vi). The data is unaffected."}]}}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Import (surface) duty","tex":"\\text{ID} = \\text{HS%} \\times \\text{CIF}"},{"lb":"Cost, insurance and freight","tex":"\\text{CIF} = \\text{Cost} + \\text{Insurance} + \\text{Freight}"},{"lb":"ECOWAS Trade Liberalisation Scheme (ETLS) levy","tex":"0.5% \\times \\text{CIF}"},{"lb":"Comprehensive Import Supervision Scheme (CISS) levy","tex":"1% \\times \\text{FOB}"},{"lb":"Surcharge","tex":"7% \\times \\text{Import duty}"},{"lb":"AU/Multilateral levy","tex":"0.5% \\times \\text{CIF}"},{"lb":"VAT on imports","tex":"7.5% \\times (\\text{CIF} + \\text{ID} + \\text{ETLS} + \\text{CISS} + \\text{Surcharge})"}],"focus":"The total-duty-payable computation is this chapter's central exam skill — build it as a fixed sequence every time: (1) CIF = FOB + Insurance + Freight; (2) import duty = rate × CIF; (3) surcharge = 7% of the import duty (NOT of CIF); (4) CISS = 1% of FOB (NOT CIF — this is the one levy based on FOB, not CIF); (5) ETLS and the AU/Multilateral levy = 0.5% of CIF each; (6) VAT = 7.5% of (CIF + all four duties/levies just computed), never of CIF alone. Getting the CISS base wrong (using CIF instead of FOB) is the single most common computational slip. Separately, keep the 6-tier valuation hierarchy in strict sequential order — transaction value, identical goods, similar goods, deductive value, computed value, residual value — each used ONLY when every method before it fails, never chosen freely.","errors":["Computing the CISS levy on CIF value instead of FOB value — CISS is specifically 1% of FOB, the only major import levy based on FOB rather than CIF, making it easy to miscompute if applying CIF out of habit.","Computing the surcharge on CIF instead of on the import duty itself — the surcharge is 7% of the SURFACE DUTY (i.e. 7% of the already-computed import duty amount), not 7% of CIF.","Computing VAT on CIF alone — VAT on imports is 7.5% of CIF PLUS all the duties and levies (import duty, ETLS, CISS, surcharge), not 7.5% of the CIF figure by itself.","Applying the customs valuation methods out of order, or picking whichever seems most convenient — the Act mandates a strict hierarchy (transaction value first, then identical, then similar, then deductive, then computed, then residual), with each method usable only after every prior method has genuinely failed.","Assuming a unit-based excise/import duty (charged per centilitre, per unit, etc.) requires converting via the invoice price — unit-based duties are computed purely from physical quantity × the stated rate per unit, with the monetary price of the goods irrelevant to that specific calculation."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"What is Nigeria's official De Minimis threshold for import duty/tax exemption on low-value imports, per the September 2025 NCS Board Circular?","o":["US$100","US$200","US$300","US$500","US$1,000"],"a":2,"w":"The NCS Board Circular (7 September 2025) approved US$300 as Nigeria's official De Minimis threshold, restricted to 4 importations per annum.","src":"Chapter 10.8","sec":"10.8"},{"q":"In computing total duty payable on an import, the CISS levy is calculated as 1% of which value?","o":["CIF value","Free on board (FOB) value","Ex-factory value","Insurance value only","Total duty payable, including VAT"],"a":1,"w":"The Comprehensive Import Supervision Scheme (CISS) levy is 1% of the free-on-board (FOB) value — the one major import levy based on FOB rather than CIF.","src":"Chapter 10.7","sec":"10.7"},{"q":"Under the customs valuation hierarchy, which method is used only after the transaction, identical-goods, similar-goods, deductive and computed value methods have all failed?","o":["Ex-factory value","Retail value","Residual value","Comparable uncontrolled price method","Cost-plus method"],"a":2,"w":"Residual value (s.74) is the method of last resort, used only when none of the five preceding valuation methods can determine the customs value.","src":"Chapter 10.6","sec":"10.6"},{"q":"Within how many working days after the succeeding month must a manufacturer of excisable goods pay the excise duty due for each month?","o":["7 days","14 days","21 days","30 days","90 days"],"a":2,"w":"Section 171 NCS Act requires monthly excise duty to be paid within the first 21 working days of the succeeding month — failure attracts a 10% per-month penalty of the amount due.","src":"Chapter 10.10","sec":"10.10"},{"q":"A person refuses to cooperate with a post-clearance audit for 10 consecutive days. What is the consequence, in addition to the daily fine?","o":["No additional consequence beyond the fine","Automatic forfeiture of all imported goods","Suspension from all customs transactions, since the refusal exceeded 7 days","Immediate criminal prosecution with no fine option","The audit is automatically deemed passed"],"a":2,"w":"Section 37(5) sets a fine of N1,500,000 per day of refusal — and where the refusal continues beyond 7 days, the person may additionally be suspended from all customs transactions.","src":"Chapter 10.4","sec":"10.4"}],"theory":[{"q":"Mr Larry Obinna, a licensed importer/exporter of alcoholic drinks, imported the following from the UK on 13 October 2025: ABC super wine, 2,700 bottles at £12.00; XYZ standard spirit, 3,000 bottles at £14.50; PQR action stout, 6,000 bottles at £5.00. Each bottle holds 25 centilitres. Duty payable per centilitre: ABC super wine N1.25; XYZ standard spirit N1.50; PQR action stout N0.30. Determine the import duties payable to the NCS.","marks":8,"a":[{"table":{"align":"lrrr","head":["Item","Centilitres","Rate/cl (N)","Duty (N)"],"rows":[["ABC super wine (2,700 × 25cl)","67,500","1.25","84,375"],["XYZ standard spirit (3,000 × 25cl)","75,000","1.50","112,500"],["PQR action stout (6,000 × 25cl)","150,000","0.30","45,000"],["**Total import duty payable**","","","**241,875**"]]}},{"note":"The price per bottle and the exchange rate are irrelevant here, since duty is charged per centilitre of volume, and VAT is not part of this requirement."}],"src":"Chapter 10.14","sec":"10.7"},{"q":"XYZ Limited imported goods from London in August 2025: Free on board N2,500,000; Insurance N40,000; Freight N500,000. Rates: import duty 5% of CIF; surcharge 7% of the surface duty; ETLS levy 0.5% of CIF; CISS levy 1% of FOB; AU/multilateral levy 0.5% of CIF; VAT 7.5% of total value. Compute the total duty payable to the Nigeria Customs Service.","marks":10,"a":[{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Item","N"],"rows":[["Free on board","2,500,000"],["Insurance","40,000"],["Freight","500,000"],["**Cost, insurance and freight (CIF)**","**3,040,000**"],["Surface duty / import duty (5% of CIF)","152,000"],["Surcharge (7% of surface duty)","10,640"],["CISS levy (1% of FOB)","25,000"],["ETLS levy (0.5% of CIF)","15,200"],["AU/Multilateral levy (0.5% of CIF)","15,200"],["**Subtotal — duties/levies**","**218,040**"],["VAT (7.5% of [CIF + duties/levies] = 7.5% × N3,258,040)","244,353"],["**Total duty payable to Nigeria Customs Service**","**462,393**"]]}}],"src":"Chapter 10.14","sec":"10.7"}]}},{"n":11,"t":"Surcharge on Fossil Fuel Products","brief":"A short but self-contained chapter on the new fossil-fuel surcharge under the NTA 2025: what transaction triggers it, what it is computed on, when it starts, which products are exempt, how returns are filed, and the specific and general offences/penalties that apply.","outcomes":["Explain the imposition of surcharge","Explain the administration of surcharge","Explain the chargeable transaction and base for surcharge","Identify products exempt from surcharge","Explain the filing of returns for surcharge","Explain the offences and penalties"],"secs":[{"n":"11.2","t":"Imposition of surcharge (s.159(1) NTA)","b":[{"key":"A **5% surcharge** (s.158 NTA) applies to chargeable fossil fuel products. For ascertaining the surcharge, the **chargeable transaction** is the supply, sale or payment — **whichever occurs first**."}]},{"n":"11.3","t":"Chargeable transaction and base for surcharge (s.159(2) NTA)","b":[{"key":"Surcharge is computed on the **retail price** of all chargeable fossil fuel products."}]},{"n":"11.4","t":"Administration of surcharge (s.160(1) NTA)","b":[{"p":"The effective commencement date for administering the fossil-fuel surcharge is based on the date specified in an **Official Gazette order issued by the Minister**."}]},{"n":"11.5","t":"Products exempt from surcharge (s.161 NTA)","b":[{"p":"Exempt fossil fuel products:"},{"ol":["clean or renewable energy products;","household kerosene;","cooking gas; and","compressed natural gas (CNG)."]},{"def":{"t":"Clean or renewable energy","d":"energy from solar, wind, hydropower, geothermal, or plant and animal waste — sources that are naturally replenishing, produce little/no environmental pollution or greenhouse gas emissions, and do not deplete over time."}}]},{"n":"11.6","t":"Filing of returns for surcharge (s.24 NTAA)","b":[{"key":"A taxpayer providing chargeable services must submit a surcharge return to the Service, in the prescribed form, **on or before the 21st day** of the following month (or such other time as the Service determines)."},{"p":"The return must include:"},{"ol":["a description of the chargeable service during the month;","the value of the chargeable service on which surcharge is payable;","the amount of surcharge payable; and","such other particulars as the Service prescribes."]}]},{"n":"11.7","t":"Offences and penalties","b":[{"h4":"11.7.1  Specific offences and penalties — section 136 of NTA"},{"h4":"Specific offences (s.136 NTA)"},{"h4":"11.7.2  General offences and penalties"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Offence","Penalty"],"rows":[["**Failure to file returns to the Service** (s.136(1) NTA, re s.24 NTAA)","Administrative penalty of **N10,000,000**."],["**Failure to pay surcharge** (s.136(2) NTAA)","A sum equal to **10%** of the amount payable, in addition to the surcharge due, plus interest at the prevailing CBN Monetary Policy Rate."]]}},{"h4":"General offences and penalties (apply equally to surcharge)"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Offence","Penalty"],"rows":[["Failure to register for tax (s.100 NTAA)","N50,000 first month, N25,000 each subsequent month; N5,000,000 for awarding a contract to an unregistered person."],["Failure to file returns (s.101 NTAA)","N100,000 first month, N50,000 each subsequent month."],["Failure to keep books (s.102 NTAA)","N10,000 (individual) or N50,000 (company)."],["Failure to deduct tax (s.105 NTAA)","40% of the amount not deducted."],["Failure to remit tax deducted, or to self-account (s.107 NTAA)","The amount not remitted/self-accounted, plus a 10% per annum administrative penalty, plus CBN MPR interest."],["General penalty for unspecified contraventions (s.127 NTAA)","N1,000,000, or on conviction up to 3 years' imprisonment or a fine, or both."],["Incorrect accounts, schedules/statements, or false/misleading information (s.131 NTAA)","Administrative penalty of N15,000,000 **plus 1% of the tax undercharged** (actual or would-have-been), whichever is higher — plus the appropriate tax due; on conviction for false/misleading information, a fine of N15,000,000 plus 1% of the undercharged tax, whichever is higher, plus the tax due."]]}}]},{"n":"11.8","t":"Chapter review","b":[{"p":"This chapter discussed the administration and imposition of surcharge on fossil fuel products, and identified the products exempt from it."}]},{"n":"11.9","t":"Worked examples — study text questions and suggested solutions","b":[{"p":"The study text sets **three examination-type questions** at §11.9.1, with its own suggested solutions at §11.9.2. All three are reproduced here in full, with the book's answers and added explanation."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 11.9.1, Question 1","t":"Chargeable transactions and the base for computing surcharge","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Section 159 of NTA, 2025 identifies the chargeable transactions and base for surcharge. **Required:** Explain the chargeable transactions and the base for the computation of surcharge."}],"a":[{"p":"For the purpose of ascertaining surcharge on fossil fuel products, the **chargeable transaction** shall be the **supply, sale or payment, whichever occurs first**."},{"p":"The computation of surcharge shall be based on the **retail price** of all chargeable fossil fuel products."},{"key":"Two short phrases carry the whole answer, and both are exam targets. **\"Whichever occurs first\"** means the charge cannot be deferred by delaying any one of the three events — delivering the fuel triggers it even if no invoice has been raised and nothing has been paid. **\"Retail price\"** means the base is the price at the **pump, to the final consumer**, not the refinery gate price, the import cost or the wholesale price — so the surcharge is calculated on the largest of the available figures. *(Source: §11.3.)*"},{"note":"Compare this with the timing rule for VAT at §7.22, which also takes the **earliest** of a set of events. The drafting pattern recurs across the 2025 Acts: where several events could mark a transaction, the Act fixes on the first of them, so that the taxpayer cannot choose the latest."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 11.9.1, Question 2","t":"Products exempt from the surcharge","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Despite the proposed surcharge on chargeable fossil fuel products under section 158 of NTA, some products are exempt from the **5% surcharge** under section 161 of NTA. **Required:** Identify the products that are exempt from surcharge."}],"a":[{"p":"The following fossil fuel products shall be **exempt** from surcharge:"},{"ol":["**clean or renewable energy products**;","**household kerosene**;","**cooking gas**; and","**compressed natural gas (CNG)**."]},{"p":"For the purpose of this section, **\"clean or renewable energy\"** means energy from **solar, wind, hydropower, geothermal, or plant and animal waste**, which are **naturally replenishing**, produce **little or no environmental pollution or greenhouse gas emissions**, and **do not deplete over time**."},{"key":"The exemptions are not random — they are the surcharge's policy written into the statute. The levy is meant to **price the carbon**, so the things it spares are **cleaner fuels** (CNG, cooking gas) and **renewables**, plus the one fossil fuel that is overwhelmingly a **poor household's** fuel: **household kerosene**. Note the word *household* — kerosene for aviation (ATK) or industrial use is not exempted by this provision."},{"note":"The same policy appears in the VAT chapter: §7.19 lets the Minister **suspend** VAT on CNG and LPG, and §7.20 lets the Minister exempt or zero-rate the equipment and conversion services for them. Read across the two chapters and the direction of Nigerian fuel tax policy is consistent — tax the carbon, relieve the substitutes."},{"note":"The three-part definition of clean energy is worth reproducing exactly if asked, because each limb does work: **naturally replenishing**, **little or no pollution/greenhouse gas**, and **does not deplete over time**. A source failing any one of them is not \"clean or renewable energy\" for this exemption."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 11.9.1, Question 3","t":"Specific offences and penalties relating to the surcharge","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Section 136 of NTAA stipulates the specific offences and penalties relating to the filing and payment of surcharge on fossil fuel products. **Required:** Explain the specific offences and penalties relating to surcharge on fossil fuel products."}],"a":[{"p":"**Specific offences and penalties — section 136 of NTAA.** These are:"},{"h4":"(a)  Failure to file returns to the Service — section 136(1) of NTAA"},{"p":"Any person who fails to file the required returns to the Service under **section 24 of NTAA** is liable to an administrative penalty of **N10,000,000**."},{"h4":"(b)  Failure to pay surcharge — section 136(2) of NTAA"},{"p":"Any taxable person who fails to pay surcharge shall be liable to a sum equal to **10% of the amount payable**, in addition to the surcharge due, and **interest at the prevailing Central Bank of Nigeria Monetary Policy Rate**."},{"key":"Note the scale of the filing penalty: **N10,000,000** for a single failure to file, against the **N100,000 first month plus N50,000 thereafter** for failing to file an income tax return under §4.7.2 — a hundred times larger, and not a monthly accrual but a flat sum. The surcharge is collected from a small number of large fossil fuel suppliers, so the deterrent is set at a level those suppliers would actually feel."},{"note":"The late-**payment** penalty, by contrast, is the standard one: **10% plus MPR interest**, exactly the shape of the general late-payment charge at §4.12.1 and of the stamp-duty charge at §8.29. Only the **filing** penalty is special to the surcharge."},{"warn":"The study text refers to this provision as \"section 136 of **NTA**\" in its §11.7.1 heading, but the question and the solution both say **NTAA** — which is correct. Offence and penalty provisions sit in the Nigeria Tax **Administration** Act, 2025, and section 24 of the NTAA is the filing obligation this penalty enforces (see §11.6)."}]}}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Surcharge on a chargeable fossil fuel product","tex":"\\text{Surcharge} = 5% \\times \\text{Retail price}"}],"focus":"This is a short, self-contained chapter — the exam is likely to test it as a compact whole rather than in isolated fragments. Anchor on: the 5% rate itself (s.158), the earliest-of supply/sale/payment trigger (s.159(1)), the retail-price base (s.159(2)), and the 4-item exemption list (s.161) — clean/renewable energy, household kerosene, cooking gas, CNG. Note the exemption list deliberately covers the products a lower-income or domestic-use consumer is most likely to rely on, which is the underlying policy logic worth remembering alongside the bare list.","errors":["Confusing the surcharge return filing deadline (21st day of the following month, s.24 NTAA) with the surcharge PAYMENT deadline — the source states only the filing deadline explicitly; do not assume payment automatically follows the same day without checking the specific penalty provision (s.136(2), a percentage-of-amount-payable penalty, not a fixed grace period).","Applying general offence penalties (ss.100-131 NTAA) as a substitute for, rather than in addition to, the surcharge-specific penalties in s.136 — the specific provisions (failure to file returns, failure to pay surcharge) exist alongside the general regime, not instead of it.","Missing that CNG is exempt from the fossil-fuel surcharge here, even though CNG appears elsewhere in the Taxation syllabus (Chapter 7) as an item the Minister may suspend/reclassify for VAT purposes — the two provisions are about different taxes entirely and should not be conflated."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"For the purpose of ascertaining surcharge on a fossil fuel product, what is the chargeable transaction?","o":["The date of manufacture","The date of import clearance","The supply, sale or payment, whichever occurs first","The date the annual tax return is filed","The date the Minister issues the Gazette order"],"a":2,"w":"Section 159(1) NTA defines the chargeable transaction as the supply, sale or payment of the fossil fuel product — whichever of the three occurs first.","src":"Chapter 11.2","sec":"11.2"},{"q":"What is the base on which surcharge on fossil fuel products is computed?","o":["The manufacturer's cost price","The CIF import value","The retail price of the chargeable product","The wholesale price","The ex-factory price"],"a":2,"w":"Section 159(2) NTA bases the surcharge computation on the retail price of all chargeable fossil fuel products.","src":"Chapter 11.2","sec":"11.2"},{"q":"Which of the following fossil fuel products is NOT exempt from the surcharge, under s.161 of the NTA?","o":["Household kerosene","Cooking gas","Compressed natural gas (CNG)","Premium Motor Spirit (petrol)","Clean or renewable energy products"],"a":3,"w":"Section 161 NTA exempts clean/renewable energy products, household kerosene, cooking gas and CNG — Premium Motor Spirit is not on this exemption list, so it remains chargeable.","src":"Chapter 11.4","sec":"11.4"},{"q":"By what day of the following month must a taxpayer providing chargeable services file a surcharge return, under s.24 of the NTAA?","o":["7th day","14th day","21st day","30th day","Last day of the month"],"a":2,"w":"Section 24 NTAA requires the surcharge return to be filed on or before the 21st day of the month following the chargeable service, unless the Service determines another time.","src":"Chapter 11.5","sec":"11.5"},{"q":"What administrative penalty applies to a taxable person who fails to pay surcharge due, under s.136(2) of the NTAA?","o":["A flat N10,000,000","A flat N1,000,000","10% of the amount payable, plus the surcharge due and CBN MPR interest","40% of the amount payable","Imprisonment only, with no fine option"],"a":2,"w":"Section 136(2) NTAA imposes a sum equal to 10% of the amount payable, in addition to the surcharge due itself, plus interest at the prevailing CBN Monetary Policy Rate.","src":"Chapter 11.6","sec":"11.6"}],"theory":[{"q":"Explain the chargeable transactions and the base for the computation of surcharge on fossil fuel products.","marks":4,"a":[{"p":"For the purpose of ascertaining surcharge on a fossil fuel product, the chargeable transaction is the **supply, sale or payment** — whichever occurs first."},{"p":"The computation of surcharge is based on the **retail price** of all chargeable fossil fuel products."}],"src":"Chapter 11.9","sec":"11.2"},{"q":"Identify the products that are exempt from surcharge on fossil fuel products.","marks":4,"a":[{"ol":["clean or renewable energy products;","household kerosene;","cooking gas; and","compressed natural gas (CNG)."]},{"note":"\"Clean or renewable energy\" means energy from solar, wind, hydropower, geothermal, or plant and animal waste — naturally replenishing sources that produce little/no pollution or greenhouse gas emissions, and do not deplete over time."}],"src":"Chapter 11.9","sec":"11.4"},{"q":"Explain the specific offences and penalties relating to surcharge on fossil fuel products, under s.136 of the NTAA.","marks":5,"a":[{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Offence","Penalty"],"rows":[["Failure to file returns to the Service (s.136(1))","Administrative penalty of N10,000,000."],["Failure to pay surcharge (s.136(2))","A sum equal to 10% of the amount payable, plus the surcharge due, plus interest at the prevailing CBN Monetary Policy Rate."]]}}],"src":"Chapter 11.9","sec":"11.6"}]}},{"n":12,"t":"Taxation of Employment Income","brief":"Everything hinging on the employer-employee relationship: what counts as employment income (cash emoluments and benefits-in-kind), the 3 statutory types of employment, distinguishing employment from vocation/profession, the relevant tax authority, the full PAYE registration/operation regulatory regime, the itinerant worker, the compensation-for-loss-of-employment rules, and — the chapter's conceptual core — how \"residency\" is determined and why it decides which tax authority has jurisdiction over an individual.","outcomes":["Draw a distinction between a contract of employment and a contract for employment","Differentiate between the two main types of employment","Distinguish among employment, vocation and profession","Identify the relevant tax authority","Understand registration for Pay-As-You-Earn (PAYE)","Explain the operation of PAYE regulations","Identify an itinerant worker","Understand the basis for valuing benefits-in-kind received by employees","Identify when gain or profit from employment is deemed derived from Nigeria","Define place of residence and principal place of residence","Discuss the significance of residence for tax purposes","Explain how a residence-determination dispute can be resolved"],"secs":[{"n":"12.2","t":"Employment income","b":[{"def":{"t":"Employment","d":"any appointment or office, public or otherwise, for which remuneration is payable (s.147 NTAA; s.201 NTA)."}},{"def":{"t":"Employment income","d":"any salary, wages, fee, allowance or other gain/profit from employment, including compensations, bonuses, premiums, benefits or other perquisites given/granted to a temporary or permanent employee — excluding sums/expenses genuinely incurred in the performance of duties, from which the employee is not intended to profit."}}]},{"n":"12.3","t":"Employment, contract of service and contract for service","b":[{"p":"A **contract for service** engages a person as an independent contractor (e.g. a self-employed vendor for a fee-based assignment) — there is **no employer-employee relationship**, and the self-employed person is not covered by the Labour Act. An individual under a **contract of employment** is an employee; under a **contract for employment**, an independent contractor/self-employed person."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Contract of employment (employee)","Contract for employment (independent contractor)"],"rows":[["Earns remuneration (salary/wages).","Earns profit."],["Assessed to tax on an **actual year** basis.","Assessed to tax on a **preceding year** basis."],["Not required to register for VAT.","Required to register for VAT."],["Has the right not to be unlawfully dismissed, and to redundancy pay and other employment rights.","Has no such statutory employment rights."]]}}]},{"n":"12.4","t":"Types of employment","b":[{"h4":"12.4.1  Nigerian employment — paragraph 2 of the First Schedule to the NTAA and the Twelfth Schedule to the NTA"},{"def":{"t":"Nigerian employment","d":"any employment — other than a foreign employment — whose duties are wholly or partly performed in Nigeria."}},{"key":"An individual holding a Nigerian employment on **1 January** of an assessment year (or who first becomes liable to Nigerian income tax that year by entering the employment during it) is deemed resident for that year in the territory of their place/principal place of residence on that day, or the day they enter full employment duties in Nigeria."},{"note":"An individual **on leave** from a Nigerian employment on 1 January is deemed resident by reference to their place/principal place of residence **immediately before their leave began**."},{"h4":"12.4.2  Foreign employments — paragraph 1 of the First Schedule to the NTAA"},{"p":"An individual (who is not a non-resident person) holding a foreign employment on 1 January — or first becoming liable to Nigerian income tax that year by entering it during the year — is deemed resident where the duties are: **wholly performed outside Nigeria** — in the territory where the employer's principal office was situated on that day or on the employment's commencement day; or **performed/exercised in Nigeria for a foreign employer** — in the individual's place of residence, or (absent one) where they usually reside."},{"h4":"12.4.3  Other employments — paragraph 3 of the First Schedule to the NTAA"},{"ol":["an employee whose remuneration is Nigerian-income-tax-liable for a year, but who has no place/principal place of residence in any Nigerian State for that year, is deemed to hold a **foreign employment** — and, if they also have no territory of residence, is deemed a **non-resident person**; and","an employee tax-liable in Nigeria for a year whose place of residence is in Nigeria's Exclusive Economic Zone or territorial waters beyond the littoral States, with no principal place of residence in any littoral State."]}]},{"n":"12.5","t":"Distinguish among employment, vocation and profession","b":[{"h4":"12.5.1 – 12.5.3  Employment, vocation and profession defined"},{"def":{"t":"Employment","d":"an agreement where the employee provides services at the employer's designated workplace, to advance the employer's goals, in return for compensation — verbal, implied, or a formal contract. The employer determines the where/when/how/why/what of the work; employment ends at either party's prerogative."}},{"def":{"t":"Vocation","d":"a specified occupation, profession or trade a person is specially drawn to, or suited/trained/qualified for — either a regular livelihood source or a means of passing time."}},{"def":{"t":"Profession","d":"an occupation requiring specialised education, knowledge, training and ethics — professionals are expected to meet/maintain common standards and bear a public responsibility distinguishing them from amateurs in the same field."}},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Employment","Vocation / profession"],"rows":[["An agreement between employer and employee for services at a workplace, for salary and other emoluments.","A specified occupation/trade the person is drawn to or suited/trained/qualified for."],["Income: salary, wages, fee, allowance, compensations, bonuses, premiums, benefits, other perquisites.","Income: gains or profits, fees, etc."],["Exercised under a **contract of service**.","Exercised under a **contract for service**."],["Ends at the prerogative of the employer or the employee.","Usually ends at the end of a contract or upon delivering a service."]]}}]},{"n":"12.6","t":"Relevant tax authority — section 3 of the NTA","b":[{"p":"The **NRS** has exclusive responsibility to administer tax on:"},{"ol":["persons in the Nigerian Army, Navy, Air Force, or Police Force (other than in a civilian capacity);","officers of the Nigerian Foreign Service; and","non-resident persons deriving profit/income from Nigeria, or income from Nigerian employment, where they are not resident in any State."]},{"p":"The relevant **State/FCT** tax authority administers tax for resident individuals in respect of: imposition of tax on income/profits/gains; ascertainment of profits/income; ascertainment of assessable profits/income; ascertainment of total income; ascertainment of chargeable gains; and rates of tax."}]},{"n":"12.7","t":"Registration process of an employee and employer with the relevant tax office for the purposes of PAYE","b":[{"h4":"12.7.1  Registration for pay-as-you-earn (PAYE)"},{"key":"Every taxable person must register with the relevant tax authority and obtain a Tax ID. An **employer** must register for PAYE purposes — with or without formal notification/direction from the tax authority."},{"h4":"12.7.2  Operation of PAYE regulations"},{"h4":"Deduction of tax"},{"ol":["the employer deducts tax from employees' emoluments and remits it **monthly**;","an employer who fails/refuses to register commits an offence, liable to an administrative penalty of **N50,000 in the first month** and **N50,000 for each subsequent month** the failure continues;","income tax chargeable on an employee — assessed or not — is deducted from any emolument/emolument-related payment (s.51(6) NTAA); and","the employer must ensure the **aggregate** of all deductions across a year of assessment equals the employee's actual chargeable income tax for that year (s.51(7) NTAA)."]},{"h4":"Records of deductions"},{"p":"The employer records (on the tax deduction card or an authorised alternative): month of payment; amount of emolument; employee's approved-pension-fund contribution; cumulative net/free/taxable emoluments to that date; corresponding cumulative tax; and any tax deducted/repaid on that payment."},{"h4":"Cumulative net emoluments and cessation of employment"},{"ol":["before paying an emolument, the employer ascertains the cumulative net/free/taxable emoluments and corresponding cumulative tax;","where cumulative tax is **less** than the previous cumulative tax, the employer refunds the **difference** to the employee; and","where an employee with an issued tax deduction card **ceases employment**, the employer sends the tax authority a prescribed-form certificate showing: employee name; employer identification number; employee address; employment start/end dates; any applicable income-tax relief; cumulative emoluments at leaving; and corresponding cumulative tax."]},{"h4":"Certificate of deduction of tax"},{"p":"The employer prepares **two copies** of the cessation certificate, delivered to the employee on the employment's end date. On starting new employment, the employee delivers both copies to the new employer, who: inserts the employee's address/identifying number/new-employment start date on one copy and sends it to the tax collector (retaining the other); and prepares a new tax deduction card from the certificate's particulars, recording cumulative free emoluments/corresponding tax from the month the employee left the former employer."},{"h4":"Death of an employee"},{"p":"On an employee's death, the employer sends the tax collector the cessation certificate, inserting the deceased's personal representative's name/address. Any emoluments subsequently paid to the next of kin still have tax **deducted**."},{"h4":"Remittance, demand, inspection and other duties"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Duty","Detail"],"rows":[["**Remittance of tax deducted**","Within **10 days** of each month's end, the employer pays all deducted tax to the nearest tax office or a prescribed/designated bank — the tax officer issues a prescribed-form receipt."],["**Demand for remittance**","If the employer fails to remit within 10 days of month-end, the collector of taxes demands **immediate remittance**."],["**Inspection of records**","The employer must produce, at their premises, all wages records, tax deduction cards, vouchers and related documents for inspection."],["**Duty of successor in title**","Where the employer cannot perform these duties (due to death, incapacitation, liquidation, etc.), their personal representative/successor performs them."],["**Notice to render returns**","The tax authority may require the employer to render emolument returns before year-end, within the notice's specified time."],["**Notice of assessment**","The tax authority serves a notice of assessment on every assessed employee."]]}}]},{"n":"12.8","t":"Itinerant worker","b":[{"h4":"12.8.1  Definition — section 201 of NTA"},{"def":{"t":"Itinerant worker","d":"an individual (regardless of status, other than a government security officer) who works at any time in any state during an assessment year, for wages/salaries/livelihood, by working in **more than one state** and for a **minimum of 20 days in at least 3 months** of every assessment year."}},{"h4":"12.8.2  Imposition of tax"},{"key":"Tax on an itinerant worker may be imposed by **any state** in which they work for at least 20 days in at least 3 months of the assessment year."}]},{"n":"12.9","t":"Terminologies relating to employment","b":[{"h4":"12.9.1  Employment income — section 4(2)(a) of NTA"},{"p":"Employment income is defined at **§12.2** above, where the statutory definition is set out in full together with the definition of employment itself."},{"h4":"12.9.2  Cash emoluments"},{"def":{"t":"Cash emoluments","d":"remuneration an employee receives from the employer **in cash** — salary, wages, fee, allowance or other gain/profit, including compensation, bonuses, premiums, benefits, and profit shares. Non-cash benefits-in-kind (e.g. a company car) are excluded unless convertible to cash."}},{"h4":"12.9.3  Benefits-in-kind (BIK) — section 14 of NTA"},{"def":{"t":"Benefits-in-kind (BIK)","d":"expenses an employer incurs providing benefits to an employee — furnished living accommodation, gardeners/stewards, private use of an official car, air conditioners/generators installed at an employee's residence, etc. Taxable as part of the employee's income where they relate to services rendered — and BIK extends to benefits actually provided to the employee's **spouse, family, servant, dependant or guest**."}},{"h4":"12.9.4  Taxable and tax-exempt incomes — section 14 of NTA"},{"p":"Where an employer incurs expense providing a benefit/perquisite (other than living accommodation), the employee's deemed **annual benefit** is:"},{"ol":["where an employer-owned asset is wholly/partly used to provide it: **5% of the amount the employer spent acquiring the asset** — or, if that's unascertainable, 5% of the asset's market value at acquisition, as the tax authority determines;","where rent/hire is payable by the employer on the asset: the **annual rent/hire amount** payable; and","in any other case: the **annual amount** the employer spends in connection with the benefit."]},{"note":"The benefit amount is **reduced** by any expense the employee themselves makes towards it."},{"h4":"Expenses NOT subject to tax"},{"ol":["meals provided in a canteen open to staff generally, or meal vouchers;","uniforms, overalls, other protective clothing, work tools/equipment; or","costs of an employee's change of residence due to a change of employment/place of exercising employment."]},{"note":"A reference to \"anything provided for an employee\" includes anything provided for their **spouse, family, servant, dependant or guest**, unless the reference is expressly limited to the employee alone."},{"h4":"Accommodation benefit"},{"key":"Where Nigerian premises are made available by an employer to the employee, spouse or family, and the employee pays **no rent**, or **less than the annual rental value**, the employee is treated as receiving additional emoluments equal to the **annual rental value** — capped at a **maximum of 20% of annual gross employment income** (excluding the rental value itself)."},{"p":"\"Annual value\" of premises means: the local-rates-assessed annual rental value, where such a law applies; otherwise, the tax-authority-determined annual rental value; and, where applicable, the proper proportion of that value for a partial year of occupation, a partial-premises occupation, or both."},{"h4":"12.9.4 (second)  Compensation for loss of employment and injury — section 50 of NTA"},{"ol":["Compensation/damages up to **N50,000,000** for a wrong/injury to an individual's person, profession or vocation — including loss of office/employment, or libel/slander/enticement — is **not a chargeable gain**;","where the amount **exceeds N50,000,000**, only the **excess** is a chargeable gain;","any person paying such compensation must, at the point of payment, **deduct and remit** the tax due; and","the deducted tax is remitted within the time specified under the PAYE/Deduction of Tax at Source Regulations."]},{"note":"A numbering defect in the study text, flagged rather than silently corrected: the book gives **two different sub-sections the same number, 12.9.4** — first \"Taxable and tax-exempt incomes — section 14 of NTA\", then \"Compensation for loss of employment and injury — section 50 of NTA\". The second should plainly be 12.9.5. Both are kept here under their printed numbers so that either can be found from the book."}]},{"n":"12.10","t":"Taxation of employment income, gains or profit of a resident individual — section 13 of NTA","b":[{"p":"Employment income/gain/profit is deemed derived from Nigeria where:"},{"ol":["the employee is a resident of Nigeria; or","the employment duties are wholly/partly performed in Nigeria, and the remuneration accruing to the employee while in Nigeria is not duly liable to tax in the employee's country of tax residence."]},{"note":"Gains/profits from employment by a **Nigerian Government** entity are deemed derived from Nigeria **wherever the remuneration is paid**, if the employee performs duties in another country and an agreement/diplomatic privilege exempts them from tax on those gains there."}]},{"n":"12.11","t":"Exemption of minimum wage earners from the payment of personal income tax — section 58 of NTA","b":[{"key":"Income tax payable on an individual's chargeable income (per the Fourth Schedule rates) applies to **every** individual **except** one earning the **Minimum Wage** — such an earner is exempt from personal income tax."}]},{"n":"12.12","t":"Determination and importance of the term \"residency\" for personal income tax purposes","b":[{"h4":"12.12.1 – 12.12.2  Why residence matters, and the non-resident individual (s.201 NTA)"},{"key":"Determining **residence** is vital for identifying a taxpayer's relevant tax authority (s.201 NTA)."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Resident individual","Non-resident individual"],"rows":[["Is domiciled in Nigeria.","Is not domiciled in Nigeria."],["Has a permanent place for domestic use in Nigeria.","Has no such place."],["Has a place of habitual abode in Nigeria.","Has no such place."],["Has substantial economic and immediate family ties in Nigeria.","Has no such ties."],["Sojourns in Nigeria ≥183 days (aggregate) in a 12-month period, including leave/temporary absence.","Sojourns <183 days (aggregate) in a 12-month period."],["Serves as a Nigerian diplomat/diplomatic agent abroad.","Does not serve as one."]]}},{"h4":"12.12.3  Residence and nationality"},{"note":"Residence should **never** be confused with **nationality** — Nigerian tax law attaches importance to residence, not nationality: the same standards apply to a citizen or a foreign national, in either direction."},{"h4":"12.12.4 – 12.12.5  Place of residence and principal place of residence — paragraph 12 of the First Schedule to the NTAA"},{"def":{"t":"Place of residence","d":"a place available for an individual's domestic use in Nigeria on a relevant day — excluding a hotel/rest-house or other temporary lodging, unless no more permanent place is available that day."}},{"p":"\"**Principal place of residence**\" (for an individual with ≥2 residences on a relevant day, not both in the same territory):"},{"ol":["no Nigerian income source other than a pension → **where they usually reside**;","has a Nigerian earned-income source other than a pension → the place **nearest to their usual place of work** on the relevant day;","has Nigerian unearned-income source(s) → **where they usually reside**; or","works at a company/body-corporate's branch office/operational site → **that site's location** (operational sites include oil terminals/platforms, flow stations, factories, quarries, and construction sites with ≥50 workers)."]},{"h4":"12.12.6  Pension — paragraph 5 of the First Schedule to the NTAA"},{"ol":["an individual whose only 1 January earned income was a **pension**, with a place/principal place of residence that day, is deemed resident in that territory;","one with only pension income and **no** place of residence that day is deemed resident: in the territory of the Government paying a wholly-Government-payable Nigerian pension (non-resident pensions excepted); or, for a non-Nigerian pension, in the territory of the principal office of the paying pension fund/authoriser; and","one with only a **Nigerian** pension, no place of residence that day, and the pension payable by **more than one government**, by a non-government person, or arising in **two or more territories**, falls under the **exclusive tax authority of the NRS**."]}]},{"n":"12.13","t":"Meaning of income","b":[{"p":"Chargeable income is the aggregate amount of every taxable person's income for the year, from a Nigerian or foreign source, including: gain/profit from any trade/business/profession/vocation (however long carried on); and any salary/wage/fee/allowance/gain/profit from employment (excluding items specifically declared non-taxable)."},{"p":"For personal income tax and place-of-residence purposes, income is classified as **earned** or **unearned**:"},{"h4":"12.13.1  Earned income — paragraph 12 of the First Schedule to the NTAA"},{"def":{"t":"Earned income (¶12)","d":"income from a trade, business, profession, vocation or employment the individual carries on/exercises, plus a pension from previous employment — includes profits, salaries, wages, commission, bonuses, etc."}},{"h4":"12.13.2 – 12.13.3  Other earned income (¶6) and unearned income (¶7)"},{"p":"An individual (not a corporation sole/body of individuals) with a **non-employment, non-pension** Nigerian earned-income source for a year is deemed resident in the territory of their 1 January place/principal place of residence — or, if first acquiring that income source during the year with no such residence on 1 January, in the territory where they **first establish** a residence that year; or, absent any residence, in the territory their earned income arises from (or any part of it, if multi-territory)."},{"p":"An individual with **no** earned-income source but **unearned** Nigerian income is similarly deemed resident by their 1 January place/principal place of residence — or, absent one, in the single territory the unearned income arises from, or any part-source territory if it arises from more than one."},{"h4":"12.13.4 – 12.13.5  Corporation sole or body of individuals (¶9), and family income (¶10)"},{"p":"A corporation sole or body of individuals (other than a family/community) is deemed resident where its Nigerian **principal office** sits on 1 January — or, absent a Nigerian office that day, in the territory where its taxable Nigerian income arises."},{"key":"A family's income is taxed **only** by the territory where the family member who **customarily first receives** it in Nigeria usually resides."}]},{"n":"12.14","t":"Resolving disputes over residence rule application","b":[{"h4":"12.14.1  Joint Revenue Board of Nigeria — section 5(b) of the JRBEA, 2025"},{"p":"The **Joint Revenue Board of Nigeria** (s.5(b) JRBEA 2025) is empowered to resolve disputes between tax authorities on determining residency."},{"h4":"12.14.2  Other dispute resolution mechanisms"},{"p":"Other dispute-resolution mechanisms: the **Office of the Tax Ombud** (s.49 JRBEA; Third Schedule); and the **Tax Appeal Tribunals** (s.23 JRBEA; Second Schedule) — both fully discussed in Chapter 5 (Objections and Appeal Procedures)."}]},{"n":"12.15","t":"Chapter review","b":[{"p":"This chapter fully addressed taxation of employment income under the Pay-As-You-Earn (PAYE) scheme, as well as issues relating to the itinerant worker."}]},{"n":"12.16","t":"Worked examples — study text questions and suggested solutions","b":[{"p":"The study text sets **three examination-type questions** at §12.16.1, with its own suggested solutions at §12.16.2. All three are reproduced here in full, with the book's answers, the day counts verified, and added explanation."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 12.16.1, Question 1","t":"Mr Alexis Sanchez — basis of taxation and the 183-day residence test","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Mr Alexis Sanchez was employed by **Zenat Limited** as Director, Commercial (West and Central Africa) with effect from **1 March 2025**. He arrived in Nigeria on the date his employment became effective and remained in Nigeria till **25 August 2025**. He returned to Nigeria on **15 January 2026** and remained in Nigeria till **31 July 2026**."},{"p":"**Required:** Explain the basis for the taxation of the income earned by Mr Alexis Sanchez in Nigeria for the relevant tax years."}],"a":[{"p":"The taxation of the income earned by Mr Alexis Sanchez in Nigeria would be determined by the **period of his residence**. Under the Nigerian income tax laws, a person is deemed resident if he:"},{"ol":["has a **permanent place available for his domestic use** in Nigeria;","has a **place of habitual abode** in Nigeria;","has **substantial economic and immediate family ties** in Nigeria;","**sojourns in Nigeria** for a period or periods amounting to an aggregate of **not less than 183 days** in a **12-month period**, inclusive of annual leave or temporary period of absence; or","serves as a **diplomat or diplomatic agent of Nigeria** in another country."]},{"h4":"2025 year of assessment"},{"p":"Based on the details provided, in **2025** Mr Alexis Sanchez stayed in Nigeria for **178 days** (that is, 1/3/25 – 25/8/25). Based on the rule of residence, having stayed for only 178 days, he is **not liable to Nigerian tax in 2025**."},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["2025 — days in Nigeria","Days"],"rows":[["March","31"],["April","30"],["May","31"],["June","30"],["July","31"],["August (1 – 25)","25"],["**Total**","**178**"]]}},{"h4":"2026 year of assessment"},{"p":"In **2026** Mr Alexis Sanchez stayed in Nigeria **198 days** (that is, 15/1/26 – 31/7/26). Based on the current law, he **is liable to Nigerian tax in 2026**, after staying in the country for **more than 183 days**."},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["2026 — days in Nigeria","Days"],"rows":[["January (15 – 31)","17"],["February (2026 is not a leap year)","28"],["March","31"],["April","30"],["May","31"],["June","30"],["July","31"],["**Total**","**198**"]]}},{"key":"The whole answer turns on **178 versus 183**. Five days short of the threshold in 2025 means no Nigerian liability on that employment income for that year; thirteen days past it in 2026 brings the whole year's income into charge. Count the days carefully and show the count — the examiner is testing arithmetic as much as law, and an answer that asserts \"fewer than 183 days\" without the month-by-month working cannot be given full credit."},{"note":"Two counting traps. First, **both end dates are inclusive** — 1 March to 25 August is 178 days, not 177. Second, **2026 is not a leap year**, so February contributes 28 days and not 29; had it been a leap year the total would be 199, which does not change the conclusion here but can change it in a borderline question."},{"warn":"A point the book's answer passes over, worth knowing because it is where the law actually bites: the statute says 183 days **in a 12-month period**, not in a year of assessment. Those are not the same test. Taking the rolling window from **1 August 2025 to 31 July 2026**, Mr Sanchez was in Nigeria for 25 days (1–25 August 2025) plus 198 days in 2026 = **223 days**, which exceeds 183. The book applies the test year by year and concludes he is non-resident for 2025, and that is the answer to give in an ATS examination; but if a question supplies dates that straddle a year end, say which window you are testing and why."},{"note":"Note also that the 183-day sojourn test is only **one of five** routes to residence, and they are alternatives, not cumulative. Had Zenat Limited provided Mr Sanchez with a flat kept available for him year-round, limb (i) — a permanent place available for his domestic use — could have made him resident in 2025 on 178 days. The question gives no such facts, which is why the day count decides it."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 12.16.1, Question 2","t":"Mr Abass — identifying the relevant tax authority from three residences","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Mr Abass works with the **Federal Ministry of Works, Abuja**. He lives in a self-contained flat in **Gwagwalada** and travels every weekend to see his first wife and children in **Kaduna**. He also visits and stays with his second wife and children in **Nyanya, Nasarawa State** on Thursdays and Fridays of every week."},{"p":"**Required:** Determine the tax authority to which Mr Abass would be liable to tax in the relevant year of assessment."}],"a":[{"p":"Based on the above question, it is obvious that Mr Abass has **three places of residence that are not within the same state**, therefore there would be the need to establish his **principal place of residence** for tax. Mr Abass's principal place of residence would be **the place nearest to his place of employment**, which in this case is **Gwagwalada, FCT, Abuja**."},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Place of residence","Territory","Relationship to work"],"rows":[["Self-contained flat, **Gwagwalada**","FCT","In the same territory as his employment at the Federal Ministry of Works, Abuja — **nearest to his usual place of work**"],["First wife and children, **Kaduna**","Kaduna State","Visited at weekends"],["Second wife and children, **Nyanya**","Nasarawa State","Visited Thursdays and Fridays"]]}},{"key":"The rule applied is paragraph 12 of the First Schedule to the NTAA (see §12.12.5): where an individual has **two or more places of residence not both within one territory**, and has **a source of earned income other than a pension**, the principal place of residence is the one **nearest to his usual place of work**. Mr Abass has earned income (his Ministry salary), so this is the limb that applies, and it points to Gwagwalada. The relevant tax authority is therefore the **FCT** tax authority."},{"note":"Notice which facts are **deliberate noise**. The number of wives, the number of children, how many nights a week he spends where, and which residence he might regard as \"home\" are all irrelevant to this test — the statutory tie-breaker is proximity to work, nothing else. A candidate who reasons from family ties or from where he spends the most nights reaches Kaduna or Nyanya and loses the marks."},{"warn":"Be careful not to import the **residence** test from Question 1 into a **principal place of residence** question. The 183-day and family-ties tests decide whether an individual is resident **in Nigeria at all**; paragraph 12 decides **which Nigerian territory** gets to tax a person who is already resident. Mr Abass's Nigerian residence is not in doubt — only the territory is."},{"note":"Had Mr Abass worked at an **operational site** rather than a Ministry office, limb (iv) would have governed instead: the principal place of residence is where the branch office or operational site is situated, and an operational site expressly includes oil terminals, platforms, flow stations, factories, quarries and construction sites with **a minimum of 50 workers**."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 12.16.1, Question 3","t":"Resident individual, non-resident individual, place of residence and principal place of residence","open":true,"q":[{"p":"In personal taxation, determination of residence is vital for the purpose of identifying the relevant tax authority of a taxpayer. The First Schedule to the Nigeria Tax Administration Act (NTAA), 2025 provides the details for determination of residence. **Required:** Explain the following terms in relation to the First Schedule to the NTAA: **(a)** resident individual; **(b)** non-resident individual; **(c)** place of residence; **(d)** principal place of residence."}],"a":[{"h4":"3(a)  Resident individual — section 201 of NTA"},{"p":"\"**Resident individual**\" means an individual that, in any year of assessment:"},{"ol":["is **domiciled** in Nigeria;","has a **permanent place available for his domestic use** in Nigeria;","has a **place of habitual abode** in Nigeria;","has **substantial economic and immediate family ties** in Nigeria;","**sojourns** in Nigeria for a period or periods amounting to an aggregate of **not less than 183 days** in a 12-month period, inclusive of annual leave or temporary period of absence; or","serves as a **diplomat or diplomatic agent of Nigeria** in another country."]},{"h4":"3(b)  Non-resident individual — section 201 of NTA"},{"p":"\"**Non-resident individual**\" means an individual that, in any year of assessment:"},{"ol":["is **not domiciled** in Nigeria;","has **no permanent place** available for his domestic use in Nigeria;","has **no place of habitual abode** in Nigeria;","has **no substantial economic and immediate family ties** in Nigeria;","**sojourns** in Nigeria for a period or periods amounting to an aggregate of **less than 183 days** in a 12-month period, inclusive of annual leave or temporary period of absence; and","is **not serving as a diplomat or diplomatic agent of Nigeria** in another country."]},{"key":"The two definitions are mirror images, but the connectors are **not** the same, and this is the single most examinable detail in the pair. The resident test is joined by **\"or\"** — satisfying **any one** limb makes an individual resident. The non-resident test is joined by **\"and\"** — an individual must fail **every** limb to be non-resident. That asymmetry is deliberate: it makes residence easy to acquire and hard to shed, so a person with a flat kept available in Lagos is resident however few days they spend in it."},{"h4":"3(c)  Place of residence — paragraph 12 of the First Schedule to the NTAA"},{"p":"\"**Place of residence**\", in relation to an individual, means a place **available for his domestic use in Nigeria on a relevant day**, and **does not include any hotel, rest-house or other place at which he is temporarily lodging** — unless **no more permanent place is available** for his use on that day."},{"h4":"3(d)  Principal place of residence — paragraph 12 of the First Schedule to the NTAA"},{"p":"\"**Principal place of residence**\", in relation to an individual with **two or more places of residence on a relevant day, not being both within any one territory**, means, in the case of an individual:"},{"ol":["with **no source of income other than a pension** in Nigeria — that place in which **he usually resides**;","who has a **source of earned income other than a pension** in Nigeria — that place which on a relevant day is **nearest to his usual place of work**;","who has a **source or sources of unearned income** in Nigeria — that place **he usually resides**; or","who works in the **branch office or operational site** of a company or other body corporate — the place at which the **branch office or operational site is situate**, provided that an operational site shall include oil terminals, oil platforms, flow stations, factories, quarries, and construction sites with **a minimum of 50 workers**, etc."]},{"note":"The hotel carve-out in (c) matters more than it looks. A contractor staying in a Lagos hotel for months has **no place of residence** there — so no Lagos tax claim arises on that basis — **unless** he has no more permanent place available to him anywhere in Nigeria, in which case the hotel becomes his place of residence by default. The proviso exists so that nobody can be left with no place of residence at all."},{"key":"Read (c) and (d) together with Question 2 and the structure is clear: **place of residence** identifies each candidate location; **principal place of residence** picks the winner when the candidates fall in different territories; and the tie-breaker depends on the **kind of income** the individual has — earned income points to the place nearest work, while pension and unearned income point to where he usually resides. Limb (iv) overrides the rest for site workers."}]}}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Asset-based benefit-in-kind (asset used, no rent/hire payable)","tex":"\\text{Annual BIK} = 5% \\times \\text{Cost of asset (or market value)}"},{"lb":"Accommodation benefit","tex":"\\text{Accommodation benefit} = \\min(\\text{Annual rental value}, \\ 20% \\times \\text{Annual gross employment income})"}],"focus":"This chapter's single most heavily tested concept is the 183-day residency threshold — practise counting days across a stated period exactly as Illustration 12 does (inclusive counting from arrival to departure date), since the exam rewards or penalises based on whether a stay falls just under or over 183 days in a 12-month window. Beyond that, keep the 3 employment types (Nigerian/foreign/other) and their distinct residency-determination rules straight, and the BIK valuation 3-tier rule (5% of asset cost/value; annual rent/hire payable; or annual amount expended) as a set — plus the separate 20%-of-gross-income cap that applies ONLY to the accommodation benefit, not to other benefits-in-kind.","errors":["Confusing residence with nationality — Nigerian tax law is explicit that residence, not citizenship, determines tax treatment; a Nigerian citizen who is non-resident and a foreign national who is resident are treated identically to their own category.","Applying the 20%-of-gross-income cap to benefits-in-kind generally — this cap applies ONLY to the accommodation benefit; the 5%-of-asset-cost, rent-payable, and annual-expenditure BIK valuation rules have no such cap of their own.","Treating the N50,000,000 compensation-for-loss-of-employment threshold as an all-or-nothing exemption — only the amount BELOW N50,000,000 is exempt; any EXCESS over that threshold is a chargeable gain, not the whole sum.","Assuming a contract for service (independent contractor) attracts the same PAYE deduction/employee-rights framework as a contract of employment — the two are governed by entirely different tax-assessment bases (actual-year vs. preceding-year) and legal protections (Labour Act coverage vs. none).","Miscounting the 183-day threshold as a single continuous stay requirement — it is an **aggregate** of days within a 12-month period (inclusive of leave/temporary absence), not a requirement that the days be consecutive."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Under s.201 of the NTA, what is the minimum aggregate number of days an individual must sojourn in Nigeria within a 12-month period to be classified as a resident individual?","o":["90 days","120 days","150 days","183 days","200 days"],"a":3,"w":"A \"resident individual\" includes one who sojourns in Nigeria for an aggregate of not less than 183 days in a 12-month period, inclusive of annual leave or temporary absence.","src":"Chapter 12.12","sec":"12.12"},{"q":"An employee is provided a company-owned car (acquired for N10,000,000) for private use, with no rent/hire payable by the employer. What is the employee's deemed annual benefit-in-kind, before any employee contribution is deducted?","o":["N100,000","N500,000","N1,000,000","N2,000,000","N10,000,000"],"a":1,"w":"Where an employer-owned asset is used to provide a benefit and no rent/hire is payable, the employee's deemed annual benefit is 5% of the amount the employer spent acquiring the asset: 5% × N10,000,000 = N500,000.","src":"Chapter 12.8","sec":"12.8"},{"q":"An individual receives N70,000,000 as compensation for loss of employment. What amount, if any, constitutes a chargeable gain under s.50 of the NTA?","o":["N0 — the entire amount is exempt","N70,000,000 — the entire amount is a chargeable gain","N20,000,000 — only the excess over N50,000,000","N50,000,000 — only the amount up to the threshold","N35,000,000 — half the total amount"],"a":2,"w":"Section 50 NTA exempts compensation up to N50,000,000 from being a chargeable gain — only the excess above that threshold (N70m − N50m = N20,000,000) is chargeable.","src":"Chapter 12.9","sec":"12.9"},{"q":"Within how many days of the end of each month must an employer remit tax deducted under PAYE to the tax office or a designated bank?","o":["5 days","10 days","14 days","21 days","30 days"],"a":1,"w":"The PAYE regulations require the employer to remit all tax deducted within 10 days of the end of every month.","src":"Chapter 12.6","sec":"12.6"},{"q":"Which body is empowered under s.5(b) of the JRBEA 2025 to resolve disputes between various tax authorities on the determination of an individual's residency?","o":["The Federal High Court","The Nigeria Revenue Service alone","The Joint Revenue Board of Nigeria","The Tax Appeal Tribunal","The Minister of Finance"],"a":2,"w":"Section 5(b) JRBEA empowers the Joint Revenue Board of Nigeria to resolve disputes between tax authorities on determining residency — separate from (though related to) the Tax Ombud and Tax Appeal Tribunal dispute-resolution routes.","src":"Chapter 12.14","sec":"12.14"}],"theory":[{"q":"Mr Alexis Sanchez was employed by Zenat Limited with effect from 1 March 2025. He arrived in Nigeria that day and remained until 25 August 2025. He returned to Nigeria on 15 January 2026 and remained until 31 July 2026. Explain the basis for taxation of the income he earned in Nigeria for the relevant tax years.","marks":8,"a":[{"p":"Under Nigerian income tax law, an individual is a resident individual if, among other conditions, they sojourn in Nigeria for an aggregate of at least 183 days in a 12-month period, inclusive of leave or temporary absence."},{"p":"In **2025**, Mr Sanchez stayed in Nigeria from 1 March to 25 August — **178 days**. Since this falls **short of 183 days**, he is **not liable** to Nigerian tax in 2025 on the residence-day test."},{"p":"In **2026**, he stayed from 15 January to 31 July — **198 days**. Since this **exceeds 183 days**, he **is liable** to Nigerian tax in 2026, having satisfied the residency threshold."}],"src":"Chapter 12.16","sec":"12.12"},{"q":"Mr Abass works with the Federal Ministry of Works, Abuja. He lives in a self-contained flat in Gwagwalada, FCT, and travels every weekend to see his first wife and children in Kaduna. He also stays with his second wife and children in Nyanya, Nasarawa State, on Thursdays and Fridays each week. Determine the tax authority to which Mr Abass would be liable in the relevant year of assessment.","marks":5,"a":[{"p":"Mr Abass has **three** places of residence, not all within the same territory — so his **principal place of residence** must be established for tax purposes."},{"p":"Since he has a source of earned income (his Ministry employment) other than a pension, his principal place of residence is the place **nearest to his usual place of work** — that is, **Gwagwalada, FCT, Abuja** (where he lives, closest to his workplace), rather than Kaduna or Nyanya."},{"p":"Mr Abass would therefore be liable to tax under the **FCT** relevant tax authority."}],"src":"Chapter 12.16","sec":"12.12"}]}},{"n":13,"t":"Computation of Personal Income Tax","brief":"The full computational pipeline for an individual's personal income tax: the basis of assessment, ascertaining gross/taxable income, the non-chargeable-gains and allowable/disallowable-deduction rules, the \"other deductions\" and \"eligible deductions\" layers that convert total income into chargeable income, the Fourth Schedule graduated rate table, and a complete worked computation format — culminating in fully worked illustrations that show every step from gross salary to final tax payable.","outcomes":["Explain the basis of assessment","Ascertain gross income","Identify the non-chargeable gains of an employee","Explain the deductions specifically allowable and disallowable in ascertaining an individual's tax liability","Ascertain total income and identify eligible deductions","Explain the relevant tax authority's requirement for proof of claims from an employee","Compute personal income tax","Explain the filing of returns by employees and employers","Identify offences and penalties"],"secs":[{"n":"13.2","t":"Basis of assessment — section 26 of NTA","b":[{"key":"Personal income tax liability is determined on an **actual year basis** — but in ascertaining gross income specifically, **employment income** uses the **actual year basis**, while **unearned income** (e.g. dividends, interest) uses the **preceding year basis**."},{"ol":["For income from employment/pension, assessable income is the amount of income of the assessment year itself;","employment income is deemed to arise **day to day**, except a bonus/commission/allowance payable only once, or at intervals exceeding one month — which is instead deemed income of the day it is paid, or (if paid after employment ceases) of the **last day of employment**, including any terminal benefit; and","for disposal of a chargeable asset, assessable income is the chargeable gains from assets disposed **during the year immediately preceding** the assessment year — including for assets used in a trade/business/profession/vocation."]}]},{"n":"13.3","t":"Gross income — section 28 of NTA","b":[{"def":{"t":"Gross income (s.147 NTAA)","d":"the total income of an individual, in a particular assessment year, from all sources ascertained under the NTAA."}},{"key":"Taxable income (s.28 NTA) is the **aggregate** of: assessable profits from trade/business/profession/vocation; employment income; income from investing activities; profits/income from any other source; and chargeable gains from disposal of chargeable assets."},{"note":"An individual's **total income** for a year is **taxable income less total deduction**."}]},{"n":"13.4","t":"Non-chargeable gains of an employee — sections 50 to 54 of NTA","b":[{"h4":"13.4.1  Personal injury — section 50 of the NTA"},{"ol":["compensation/damages **up to N50,000,000** for a personal/professional/vocational wrong or injury — including loss of office/employment, or libel/slander/enticement — is **not a chargeable gain**;","only the amount **exceeding N50,000,000** is chargeable;","a person paying such compensation must deduct and remit the tax due at the point of payment; and","the deducted tax is remitted within the PAYE/Deduction-at-Source Regulations timeframe."]},{"h4":"13.4.2  Principal private residences — section 51 of NTA"},{"p":"Gains on disposal of (or an interest in) a dwelling-house (or part of it), and up to **one acre** of non-commercial land immediately adjoining it, are **exempt** — usable **once in a lifetime**. Consideration is apportioned where only part of a dwelling-house is disposed of, or where the house is used partly for dwelling and partly for trade/business/profession/vocation."},{"h4":"13.4.3  Personal chattels — section 52 of NTA"},{"ol":["a gain on disposing tangible movable personal chattels is **not chargeable** if the total consideration does not exceed **N5,000,000, or 3× the annual national minimum wage, whichever is higher**, in the assessment period;","disposal of 2+ assets (whether or not a set) to the same person, connected persons, or persons acting in concert — on the same or different occasions — is treated as a **single transaction disposing of a single asset**, apportioned as needed;","for disposal of part of a right/interest in tangible movable property, the consideration includes the market value of what remains undisposed of; and","this section does **not** apply to a disposal of currency."]},{"h4":"13.4.4  Motor vehicle — section 53 of NTA"},{"p":"A motor vehicle used **solely for private/non-profit purposes** is not a taxable asset — limited to a **maximum of 2 vehicles** per individual per assessment year."},{"h4":"13.4.5  Gifts — section 54 of NTA"},{"p":"Gains on disposing of an asset acquired **by way of gift** (not by devolution on death) are not chargeable — a gift is one where **no consideration** is paid/received for the acquisition or disposal."},{"note":"A typesetting defect in the study text, flagged rather than silently corrected: the first sub-section is printed as **\"13.4 1Personal injury - section 50 of the NTA\"** — the sub-section number has broken apart, so the \"1\" has attached itself to the word \"Personal\" instead of to 13.4. It is 13.4.1, as the four that follow it (13.4.2 to 13.4.5) confirm."}]},{"n":"13.5","t":"Deductions allowed and not allowed","b":[{"h4":"13.5.1  Deductions allowed — section 20 of NTA"},{"key":"In ascertaining profit/loss, all expenses **wholly and exclusively incurred in producing the income** during the period are deductible, including:"},{"ol":["interest payable on debt employed to generate income (subject to excess-interest restrictions on connected-person loans);","rent/premiums for land/buildings occupied to generate income;","salary/wages/remuneration paid to employees, and the cost of employee benefits/allowances;","expenses for repair of premises/plant/machinery/fixtures, or renewal/repair/alteration of implements/utensils/articles employed;","expenditure establishing, preserving or defending title/rights over an asset;","contributions to an approved staff pension/provident/retirement fund (Pension Reform Act or similar);","proven damage to, or loss of, stock/inventory;","bad/doubtful debts incurred during trade/business (even if due before the basis period began) — excluding bad debts from related-party transactions, and subject to GAAP-consistent doubtful-debt estimation;","research and development expense for the period;","other expense from a previous period for the trade/business, or specifically for the profit-ascertainment period — expenditure within **6 years before** commencing business, which would have been deductible if incurred after commencement, is deemed incurred on the **first day** of trading;","dividends/mandatory distributions by an SEC-approved real estate investment company to shareholders;","compensating payments (qualifying as interest) by a lender to its approved agent/a borrower in a regulated securities lending transaction; and","expense on assistive devices/disability-related products (hearing aids, wheelchairs, braille materials)."]},{"p":"For bad/doubtful debts specifically: any amount already allowed in a previous period on the same debt is deducted first; and sums recovered on previously written-off/allowed debts are **added back** to profits in the recovery period."},{"note":"A liability that is waived, released or recovered is included in assessable profits/chargeable gains. An expense in a foreign currency is deductible only to the extent of its **Naira equivalent at the official CBN exchange rate** for the relevant date/period."},{"h4":"13.5.2  Deductions not allowed — section 21 of NTA"},{"p":"No deduction is allowed for:"},{"ol":["capital repaid or withdrawn from the trade/business/profession/vocation;","expenditure of a capital nature;","domestic/private expense, or expenditure on assets not used for the trade/business/profession/vocation;","sums recoverable under insurance or an indemnity contract;","taxes on profit/income levied in Nigeria or elsewhere;","payments to an unapproved savings/widows-and-orphans/pension/provident/retirement fund, society or scheme;","depreciation/impairment of a fixed asset/investment, or an unrealised foreign-currency exchange difference;","sums reserved out of profits (subject to the 6-year pre-commencement rule above);","payments to a connected person inconsistent with the Service's Transfer Pricing Regulations;","expense incurred deriving income exempt from income tax;","expense deductible in determining chargeable gains under the Act;","penalty or fine imposed under any law;","tax or penalty borne on behalf of another person;","compensating payments (qualifying as dividends) by a borrower to its approved agent/a lender in a regulated securities lending transaction;","compensating payments (qualifying as interest/dividends) by an approved agent to a borrower/lender in such a transaction; or","expense on which VAT was due but not charged, or (for imports) applicable import duty/levy was not paid."]}]},{"n":"13.6","t":"Other deductions as specified under section 28(2)(b) of NTA","b":[{"p":"Total deduction is the sum of: any trade/business/profession/vocation loss; capital allowance (First Schedule Part I); tax-exempt income; and income on which WHT under s.51 NTAA is the **final tax** (e.g. dividend, short-term securities/corporate bond interest)."},{"h4":"Loss-deduction provisos"},{"ol":["the aggregate loss deduction can **never exceed** the loss amount itself;","the loss is deducted, as far as possible, from the **first** assessment year's assessable profit after the loss year, and carried forward until **fully recouped**;","the loss for any year is computed per the basis period in Part V, Chapter Two of the Act; and","a loss from digital/virtual asset sales/disposals/transactions is deductible **only against** digital/virtual asset profit/gain."]}]},{"n":"13.7","t":"Total income — section 28(1) of NTA","b":[{"key":"An individual's **total income** for a year is **taxable income less total deduction**."}]},{"n":"13.8","t":"Eligible deductions — section 30(2) of NTA","b":[{"p":"\"Eligible deductions\" are payments the individual made in the assessment year in respect of:"},{"ol":["contributions under the National Housing Fund;","contributions under the National Health Insurance Scheme;","contributions under the Pension Reform Act 2014;","interest on loans for developing an owner-occupied residential house;","the annual amount of any life assurance/deferred-annuity premium paid, **in the year preceding** the assessment year, on the individual's own life or their spouse's; and","rent relief of **20% of annual rent paid, capped at N500,000** (whichever is lower) — provided the individual accurately declares the actual rent paid and other prescribed information."]},{"h4":"National Housing Fund (NHF) contribution"},{"p":"The Business Facilitation (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 2022 amended the NHF Act, making **private-sector** employee NHF contributions **voluntary**. **Public-sector** employees earning the national minimum wage must still contribute **2.5% of monthly basic salary**, deducted by the employer and remitted to the Federal Mortgage Bank of Nigeria within **1 month**. Contributions are tax-deductible."},{"warn":"A convicted corporate employer failing to remit within 1 month faces a fine of N50,000; an authorised officer faces N20,000, 5 years' imprisonment, or both."},{"h4":"National Health Insurance Scheme (NHIS)"},{"p":"Established by the National Health Insurance Act (Cap N42 LFN 2004), repealed and replaced by the National Health Insurance Authority (NHIA) Act 2022, making health insurance **mandatory** for all legal Nigerian residents — covering employers/employees in the formal sector (5+ staff), informal-sector employees, and all other residents. Formal-sector contributions are paid by employer/employee at State-Council-determined rates; informal-sector contributions by individuals/groups/families. The employer deducts the negotiated employee contribution and pays both portions into a designated HMO account. Contributions are tax-deductible."},{"h4":"Life assurance premium"},{"p":"The annual premium paid, in the year **preceding** the assessment year, to an insurer for life insurance (own or spouse) or a deferred annuity contract (own or spouse), is deductible."},{"h4":"National pension scheme"},{"p":"The Pension Reform Act 2014 establishes a uniform contributory pension scheme, mandatory for employers with **≥3 employees**; smaller organisations' employees and the self-employed may join voluntarily via the Micro Pension Plan."},{"key":"Minimum contribution rate: **8%** of monthly emoluments (basic salary + housing + transport allowance) from the employee, and at least **10%** from the employer. An employer may instead elect to bear the full scheme burden, in which case its own contribution must be **at least 20%** of monthly emoluments (s.4(4)(b)). Employee contributions are tax-deductible."},{"note":"Tax relief is limited to contributions to schemes **recognised under the Pension Reform Act 2014** (s.30(2)(iii) NTA). Per s.31 NTA, no deduction is allowed for a year unless **claimed in writing**, in the prescribed form."}]},{"n":"13.9","t":"Chargeable income — section 30(1) of NTA","b":[{"key":"Chargeable income = **total income less eligible deductions**."}]},{"n":"13.10","t":"Income tax rates — section 58 and the Fourth Schedule to the NTA","b":[{"p":"The graduated rates currently applicable to chargeable income:"},{"table":{"align":"lrr","head":["Income band","Rate per N1","%"],"rows":[["First N800,000","0k","0"],["Next N2,200,000","15k","15"],["Next N9,000,000","18k","18"],["Next N13,000,000","21k","21"],["Next N25,000,000","23k","23"],["Above N50,000,000","25k","25"]]}}]},{"n":"13.11","t":"The requirement of the relevant tax authority for proof of claims from an employee — section 32 of NTA","b":[{"p":"The tax authority may require documentary evidence supporting an eligible-deduction claim — absent or inadequate evidence, it may refuse the deduction (or part of it)."},{"ol":["where satisfactory evidence is not produced for a claim, any objection to the resulting assessment/deduction rate must be accompanied by whatever evidence is available, or a declaration that the required evidence does not exist; and","where a deduction is claimed for a year, or evidence is later produced supporting a claim previously not admitted (or not admitted in full), any resulting repayment, set-off, or assessment reduction is made to give effect to the (additional) amount."]}]},{"n":"13.12","t":"Computation of personal income tax of an individual — section 28 of NTA","b":[{"p":"The following format is used as a guide:"},{"steps":["Add: assessable profits from trade/business/profession/vocation, employment income, investing-activity income, other-source profit/income, and chargeable gains from chargeable-asset disposal → **Taxable income**.","Deduct total deduction: trade/business/profession/vocation loss, loss on chargeable-asset disposal, digital/virtual-asset loss (only against digital/virtual profit), capital allowance, tax-exempt income, and final-tax income → **Total income**.","Deduct eligible deductions: NHF/NHIS/pension contributions, interest on owner-occupied residential loans, life assurance premium, and rent relief (20% of rent, capped at N500,000) → **Chargeable income**.","Apply the Fourth Schedule graduated rates to chargeable income → **Gross tax liability**.","Deduct withholding tax already paid on unearned-income items (except dividend, which is final tax and already excluded from chargeable income) → **Net tax liability payable**."]},{"eg":{"t":"Illustration 13.1 — full computation from gross salary to tax payable (Mr Ogunlade)","q":[{"p":"Mr Ogunlade, an employee of Klass Limited who keeps adequate tax records, had this salary package for the year ended 31 December 2026:"},{"ol":["gross salary N6,500,000 per annum;","two domestic servants (N400,000 each) and a night guard (N450,000), fully paid by the company;","N88,250 reimbursement of official travel expenses;","lived in a company house with an annual rental value of N2,450,000;","used an official car the company bought for N7,400,000;","married with two school-age children; maintains his aged mother at N30,800/annum; paid N480,000 life assurance premium in 2025; and","dividends received (net of WHT) from a blue-chip company: N99,000 (5/7/2025); N63,000 (31/12/2025); N90,000 (30/3/2026); N95,000 (31/5/2026)."]},{"p":"**Required**: compute his tax payable for the relevant assessment year."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lrr","head":["Mr Ogunlade — personal income tax, 2026 assessment year","N","N"],"rows":[["**Earned income**","",""],["Gross salary","","6,500,000"],["Benefits-in-kind — domestic servants (N400,000 × 2)","800,000",""],["Night guard","450,000",""],["Accommodation — restricted to 20% of gross income","1,624,000",""],["Company's car (5% × N7,400,000)","370,000","3,244,000"],["**Subtotal — earned income**","","**9,744,000**"],["**Unearned income**","",""],["Dividend (gross) — (N99,000/0.9 + N63,000/0.9), 2025 receipts only","","180,000"],["Subtotal","","9,924,000"],["Less: dividend (gross) — final tax, excluded from chargeable income","","(180,000)"],["Subtotal","","9,744,000"],["Less: eligible deduction — life assurance relief","","(480,000)"],["**Chargeable income**","","**9,264,000**"]]}},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Tax computation","N"],"rows":[["First N800,000 @ 0%","0"],["Next N2,200,000 @ 15%","330,000"],["Next N6,264,000 @ 18%","1,127,520"],["**Personal income tax payable**","**1,457,520**"]]}},{"note":"Only the **2025** dividend receipts (N99,000 + N63,000) are grossed up and included — unearned income for a 2026 assessment uses the **preceding-year basis** (s.26 NTA), so the 2026-received dividends (N90,000, N95,000) belong to the 2027 assessment instead. The dividend is then grossed up at the 10% WHT rate (N/0.9) and shown only to demonstrate the mechanic — since WHT on dividend is **final tax** for individuals, it is excluded again from the chargeable-income base taxed at the graduated rates. The N88,250 travel-expense reimbursement is excluded entirely, being a genuine reimbursement of official expenses the employee is not intended to profit from — not employment income at all."},{"note":"\"Gross income\" for the 20%-accommodation-cap test is gross salary + BIK **excluding the rental value itself**: N6,500,000 + N800,000 + N450,000 + N370,000 = N8,120,000. 20% of this is N1,624,000 — lower than the N2,450,000 actual rental value, so the **capped** figure (N1,624,000) is used."}]}}]},{"n":"13.13","t":"Filing of returns: employees and employers","b":[{"warn":"The study text **lists this section in its chapter contents but does not print it**. The contents page gives \"13.13 Filing of returns: employees and employers\", \"13.14 Offences and penalties\" and \"13.15 Chapter review\" — each with the page reference *\"Error! Bookmark not defined.\"* — and the body of the chapter jumps straight from §13.12 to §13.16.1 (the worked-example questions). The three sections are missing from the book, not from this site."},{"p":"So that nothing is left unanswered, here is where the material the heading promises actually sits in the study text:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Obligation","Where the study text covers it"],"rows":[["**Employer** — register for PAYE with the relevant tax authority, deduct tax from emoluments, keep tax deduction cards, remit by the statutory date, and file the annual return of emoluments and deductions","**§12.7.1 and §12.7.2** — registration for PAYE and the operation of the PAYE regulations, including the certificate of deduction on cessation and the procedure on an employee's death"],["**Employee** — file an annual return of income with the relevant tax authority, disclosing all income from every source and claiming eligible deductions","**§13.8** (eligible deductions, which must be claimed in the return) and **§13.11** (the tax authority's power to require documentary proof of any claim, s.32 NTA)"],["**Both** — the general filing machinery, due dates, self-assessment, and the consequences of filing late or not at all","**§4.3, §4.4 and §4.7.2** — contents of returns, due dates, and the N100,000/N50,000 late-filing penalty under s.101 NTAA"]]}},{"key":"The one point specific to employment income, which the missing section would presumably have made: PAYE is a **collection mechanism, not a final tax** for an employee with other income. An employee whose only income is the employment taxed under PAYE is in practice met by the employer's return, but an employee with additional income — a trade, rents, or unearned income — must still file a personal return, because only the return aggregates the sources into **total income** under §13.7 and allows the eligible deductions of §13.8 to be claimed against the whole."}]},{"n":"13.14","t":"Offences and penalties","b":[{"p":"This section is also **listed in the book's chapter contents but not printed in the chapter** (see the note at §13.13). The offences and penalties that bear on personal income tax are those of general application in the NTAA, set out in full elsewhere in this study text:"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Default","Penalty","Where"],"rows":[["Failure to register with the relevant tax authority (s.100 NTAA)","N50,000 first month; N25,000 each subsequent month","§4.7.1"],["Failure or refusal to file returns, or knowingly filing incomplete or inaccurate returns (s.101 NTAA)","N100,000 first month; N50,000 each subsequent month","§4.7.2"],["Failure to keep proper books and records (s.102 NTAA)","N10,000 for a person other than a company","§4.7.3"],["**Failure to deduct or remit tax — the employer's PAYE default** (s.105 NTAA)","**40% of the amount not deducted**, plus the tax itself","§4.12.4 and §9.27 Q2"],["Failure to pay tax on the due date (s.65 NTAA)","10% of the tax payable, plus interest at the CBN Monetary Policy Rate","§4.12.1"],["Failure to notify a change of address within 30 days (s.112 NTAA)","N100,000 first month; N50,000 each subsequent month","§4.12.7"],["False declaration in a return (s.124 NTAA)","N1,000,000 administrative penalty plus the tax undercharged; or on conviction a N1,000,000 fine or up to 3 years' imprisonment, or both","§4.12.16"]]}},{"key":"The penalty that matters most in an employment context is the **employer's**, not the employee's. Under s.105 NTAA an employer that fails to deduct PAYE is liable for **40% of the tax it should have deducted, in addition to the tax itself** — and since the employee has already been paid gross, the employer funds both out of its own pocket, with only a civil claim against the employee to recover the tax. The worked example at §9.27, Question 2 computes exactly this exposure on a consultancy fee."}]},{"n":"13.15","t":"Chapter review","b":[{"p":"This chapter covered the computation of personal income tax — the basis of assessment, ascertainment of gross/total/chargeable income, non-chargeable gains, allowable and disallowable deductions, eligible deductions, income tax rates, and a complete worked computation format."}]},{"n":"13.16","t":"Worked examples — study text questions and suggested solutions","b":[{"p":"The study text sets **three computational questions** at §13.16.1, with its own suggested solutions at §13.16.2. All three are reproduced here in full, with the book's answers, every figure independently recomputed, and added explanation."},{"note":"A numbering defect in the book, flagged rather than silently corrected: the chapter has **no \"§13.16 Worked examples\" heading at all** — it goes straight from the solution to Illustration 13.1 to a sub-section headed \"13.16.1 Questions\". Taken with the three sections missing from the body (see §13.13), the end of this chapter is the most defective stretch of the study text."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 13.16.1, Question 1","t":"Mr Babalola — employer-provided accommodation and car, with dependants","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Mr Babalola is an employee of Yakoyo Limited. His gross salary is **N5,000,000 per annum**."},{"p":"He is married with five children. The first two are from his first wife and the remaining from the second wife. The two wives live with him. The eldest child is now a university graduate and teaches in a secondary school in Kaduna, while the remaining children are university students."},{"p":"Mr Babalola has a life assurance policy with Rex Insurance Limited. The sum assured is **N600,000** and he paid a premium of **N6,000 per month** in 2025. Mr Babalola, who keeps adequate records for tax purposes, also contributes **N5,000 monthly** to a pension scheme approved by the Joint Revenue Board."},{"p":"The following information is made available:"},{"ol":["the company pays for his accommodation, which has an **annual rental value of N1,180,000**, and provides him with an official car which has a **market value of N4,000,000**;","he spends **N66,000** annually on his aged father and **N67,000** on his aged mother — both have no sources of income due to old age; and","he will be **retiring on 31 December 2026** and will be paid a **gratuity of N300,000**."]},{"p":"**Required:** Compute his tax liability for the relevant year of assessment."}],"a":[{"stmt":{"t":"Mr Babalola — computation of tax liability for the 2026 assessment year","rows":[["Gross salary","","5,000,000"],"Benefits-in-kind:",["Company accommodation — restricted to 20% of gross income (N5,000,000 plus N200,000)","1,040,000","","@ind"],["Official car — 5% of N4,000,000","200,000","1,240,000","@ind"],["Gross emoluments","","6,240,000","@t"],"Less: eligible deductions:",["(i) Life assurance relief","72,000","","@ind"],["(ii) Contribution to pension scheme","60,000","(132,000)","@ind"],["Chargeable income","","6,108,000","@tt"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Tax payable","rows":[["First N800,000 @ 0%","0",""],["Next N2,200,000 @ 15%","330,000",""],["Next N3,108,000 @ 18%","559,440",""],["  (N6,108,000)","",""],["Tax payable","889,440","","@tt"]]}},{"h4":"How each figure follows"},{"ol":["**Car benefit-in-kind** = 5% × N4,000,000 = **N200,000** (s.14 NTA — 5% of the cost or market value of the asset provided).","**Gross income** for the accommodation restriction = gross salary **plus the other benefits-in-kind, but excluding the accommodation itself** = N5,000,000 + N200,000 = **N5,200,000**. This is the rule set out in note (a) to Illustration 13.1 at §13.12.","**Accommodation benefit** = the lower of the annual rental value (N1,180,000) and **20% of gross income** (20% × N5,200,000 = N1,040,000) = **N1,040,000**.","**Life assurance relief** = N6,000 × 12 = **N72,000** (the premium paid in 2025, the year preceding assessment — see §13.8).","**Pension contribution** = N5,000 × 12 = **N60,000**, the scheme being approved by the Joint Revenue Board.","**Tax** = 0 + (15% × N2,200,000 = N330,000) + (18% × N3,108,000 = N559,440) = **N889,440**."]},{"key":"Almost everything in the first three paragraphs of this question is **deliberate noise**. Two wives, five children, which wife each child belongs to, that the eldest is a graduate teaching in Kaduna, the N66,000 and N67,000 spent on aged parents — **none of it attracts any relief**. Under section 30(2) of the NTA the eligible deductions are a **closed list**: National Housing Fund, NHIS, life assurance, national pension scheme, and rent relief. The child relief and dependent-relative relief that candidates remember from the repealed PITA **no longer exist**. Only the life assurance premium and the pension contribution survive from everything the question tells you about his family."},{"note":"The **N300,000 gratuity** on his retirement is likewise not taxed, and the book's solution silently omits it. A gratuity is a non-chargeable gain under this chapter's §13.4 provisions, and in any event it falls in the year of retirement. Say so explicitly in an answer rather than leaving the examiner to guess whether you spotted it or forgot it — a one-line note (\"gratuity of N300,000 exempt\") protects the mark."},{"warn":"He claims **no rent relief**, even though rent relief is on the eligible-deductions list. That is because his **employer pays for the accommodation** — he pays no rent himself, so there is nothing of which to take 20%. Compare Question 2, where Mr Lucky pays N7,500,000 of rent and claims the N500,000 cap. A taxpayer gets the accommodation benefit **or** the rent relief, never both."}]}},{"eg":{"t":"Mr Lucky — two employments in one year, with a source computational error corrected","q":[{"p":"Mr Lucky joined Rainbow Bank Limited as Head of Banking Operations on 1 June 2026, on: basic salary N48,000,000 p.a.; housing allowance N12,000,000; transport allowance N2,000,000; utility allowance N800,000; meal allowance N500,000; leave allowance N4,800,000; clothing allowance N2,000,000. He was provided an official car costing N7,500,000, a driver (N300,000/annum) and a personal assistant (N500,000/annum). He resigned from KEL Bank Plc (annual salary N32,000,000) effective 31 May 2026. He is married with 6 school-age children and maintains an aged parent; paid N3,000,000 life assurance premium in 2025; his employer deducted/remitted N4,900,000 pension and N1,166,667 NHF for 2026; he paid N750,000 NHIS premium in 2026; and pays N7,500,000 annual rent. **Required**: compute his tax liability for the relevant assessment year."}],"a":[{"warn":"The study text's own printed solution labels the car benefit-in-kind \"7/12 × (5% of N7,500,000)\" but its arithmetic actually computes 7/12 × **7.5%** of N7,500,000 = N328,125, rather than the correctly-labelled 5% figure of N218,750 — an internal inconsistency between the stated rate (5%, matching this chapter's own s.14 NTA valuation rule, used correctly elsewhere in this same chapter's other illustrations) and the number actually used. This N109,375 overstatement cascades through gross emoluments, chargeable income, and the final tax figure (the source states a final liability of N9,211,718.98). The solution below applies the chapter's own correctly-stated 5% rate consistently throughout."},{"table":{"align":"lrr","head":["Mr Lucky — personal income tax, 2026 assessment year","N","N"],"rows":[["**Rainbow Bank Limited, 1/6/26-31/12/26 (7 months)**","",""],["Basic salary (7/12 × N48,000,000)","","28,000,000"],["Housing allowance (7/12 × N12,000,000)","","7,000,000"],["Transport allowance (7/12 × N2,000,000)","","1,166,667"],["Utility allowance (7/12 × N800,000)","","466,667"],["Meal allowance (7/12 × N500,000)","","291,667"],["Leave allowance (7/12 × N4,800,000)","","2,800,000"],["Clothing allowance (7/12 × N2,000,000)","","1,166,667"],["BIK — car (7/12 × 5% × N7,500,000)","218,750",""],["BIK — driver (7/12 × N300,000)","175,000",""],["BIK — personal assistant (7/12 × N500,000)","291,667","685,417"],["**Subtotal — Rainbow Bank**","","**41,577,085**"],["Add: KEL Bank Plc, 1/1/26-31/5/26 (5/12 × N32,000,000)","","13,333,333"],["**Gross emoluments**","","**54,910,418**"],["Less: eligible deductions — pension","4,900,000",""],["Life assurance premium","3,000,000",""],["NHF","1,166,667",""],["NHIS","750,000",""],["Rent relief (20% × N7,500,000 = N1,500,000, capped at N500,000)","500,000","(10,316,667)"],["**Chargeable income**","","**44,593,751**"]]}},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Tax computation","N"],"rows":[["First N800,000 @ 0%","0"],["Next N2,200,000 @ 15%","330,000"],["Next N9,000,000 @ 18%","1,620,000"],["Next N13,000,000 @ 21%","2,730,000"],["Next N19,593,751 @ 23%","4,506,562.73"],["**Tax liability**","**9,186,562.73**"]]}},{"note":"This corrected liability (N9,186,562.73) is exactly N25,156.25 less than the source's printed N9,211,718.98 — precisely 23% of the N109,375 BIK overstatement, since both the correct and the source's chargeable income fall entirely within the same 23% band (between N25,000,000 and N50,000,000), confirming the single point of divergence."},{"warn":"A second, separate slip in the same printed solution: the question states at (c) that the employer deducted \"**N4,900,000 and N1,166,667 for NHF and pension, respectively**\", but the book's solution lists **pension N4,900,000 and NHF N1,166,667** — the two labels are swapped against the question. The arithmetic is unaffected, because both are eligible deductions under s.30(2) and the total of N6,066,667 is the same either way, but in an examination answer the labels should follow the question."},{"note":"Note how the two employments are handled: the year is split on a **time basis** — 5/12 of the KEL Bank salary for 1 January to 31 May, and 7/12 of every Rainbow Bank figure for 1 June to 31 December — and the two are then **aggregated into a single assessment**. Personal income tax is charged on the individual, not on each employment, so the bands of §13.10 are applied once to the combined total. Splitting the bands between the two employers would understate the tax substantially."},{"key":"Note also what the benefits-in-kind do **not** include: the housing, transport, utility, meal, leave and clothing **allowances** are cash emoluments taxed at their full value, so they never enter the 20%-of-gross-income accommodation restriction. That restriction (see Questions 1 and 3) applies only where the **employer provides the accommodation itself**. Mr Lucky rents his own house, which is why he gets **rent relief** instead — 20% of the N7,500,000 he actually pays, capped at N500,000."}],"tag":"Study text — 13.16.1, Question 2","open":true}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 13.16.1, Question 3","t":"Mr Clement — seven children, and the closed list of eligible deductions","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Mr Clement is married with seven children. Details about his children are as follows:"},{"ol":["**Chukwu** is 23 years and a lecturer in one of the private tertiary institutions;","his daughter, **Mrs Brendy Kolo**, is a housewife and married to Mr Kolo Kolo, a protocol officer in the Ministry of Finance;","**Charles** is an undergraduate in the University of Ibadan and is aged 18 years;","**Lizy** is a student in Highrise College and her age last birthday was 13 years;","**Rose** is in one of the elitist private schools as a student — she is 8 years old;","**Precious** is in Command Secondary School and is 16 years old; and","**Tara** is three years old."]},{"p":"You are provided with the following information:"},{"ol":["he took out a life insurance policy for which he paid an annual premium of **N400,000** in 2025;","his employer deducted **N2,137,500** and **N712,500** for **pension** and **NHF** respectively from his salary and remitted same on his behalf, in compliance with the Pension Reform Act and the NHF Act;","his **aged parents live with him** — they have no income of their own;","in 2026 he is on a **gross salary of N28,500,000 per annum**;","in addition, his employer provided him with an **official car and accommodation**; and","the **cost of his official car is N5,000,000** and the **annual rental value** of the property is **N6,000,000**."]},{"p":"**Required:** Compute Mr Clement's personal income tax payable for the 2026 year of assessment."}],"a":[{"stmt":{"t":"Mr Clement — computation of tax liability for the 2026 assessment year","rows":[["Salary","","28,500,000"],"Add: benefits-in-kind:",["Accommodation — 20% of gross income (N28,500,000 plus N250,000)","5,750,000","","@ind"],["Car — 5% × N5,000,000","250,000","6,000,000","@ind"],["","","34,500,000","@t"],"Less: eligible deductions:",["Life assurance premium","400,000","","@ind"],["Pension","2,137,500","","@ind"],["NHF","712,500","(3,250,000)","@ind"],["Chargeable income","","31,250,000","@tt"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Apply tax rates","rows":[["First N800,000 @ 0%","0",""],["Next N2,200,000 @ 15%","330,000",""],["Next N9,000,000 @ 18%","1,620,000",""],["Next N13,000,000 @ 21%","2,730,000",""],["Next N6,250,000 @ 23%","1,437,500",""],["  (N31,250,000)","",""],["Total liability","6,117,500","","@tt"]]}},{"h4":"How each figure follows"},{"ol":["**Car benefit** = 5% × N5,000,000 = **N250,000**.","**Gross income** for the accommodation restriction = N28,500,000 + N250,000 = **N28,750,000**; 20% of that = **N5,750,000**, which is lower than the N6,000,000 annual rental value, so the restricted figure is taken.","**Eligible deductions** = life assurance N400,000 + pension N2,137,500 + NHF N712,500 = **N3,250,000**.","**Chargeable income** = N34,500,000 − N3,250,000 = **N31,250,000**.","The first four bands absorb N800,000 + N2,200,000 + N9,000,000 + N13,000,000 = **N25,000,000**, leaving N31,250,000 − N25,000,000 = **N6,250,000** in the 23% band: 23% × N6,250,000 = **N1,437,500**.","**Total** = N330,000 + N1,620,000 + N2,730,000 + N1,437,500 = **N6,117,500**."]},{"key":"This question is Question 1's point made at greater length: seven children are described in detail — their ages, schools, marital status, employment — and the aged parents living with him are mentioned too, and **not one word of it changes the answer**. Three figures do the work: the life assurance premium, the pension deduction and the NHF deduction. If you find yourself computing a child relief, you are working from the repealed PITA."},{"note":"Why the children's ages would once have mattered, and why they no longer do: under the old Personal Income Tax Act a child under 16 (or in full-time education) attracted a relief per child up to a maximum number of children, and a dependent relative attracted another. Section 30(2) of the NTA 2025 **replaced all of that with the five-item closed list** at §13.8. The study text keeps setting questions in the old style precisely to test whether candidates have noticed."},{"note":"Cross-check the three solutions against each other and the pattern is clear: in Questions 1 and 3 the **employer provides the accommodation**, so the 20%-of-gross-income restriction applies and there is no rent relief; in Question 2 the **employee pays his own rent**, so there is no accommodation benefit and rent relief applies instead, capped at N500,000. The car benefit is **5% of cost or market value** in all three."}]}}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Taxable income","tex":"\\text{Taxable income} = \\text{Sum of all income sources (trade, employment, investing, other, chargeable gains)}"},{"lb":"Total income","tex":"\\text{Total income} = \\text{Taxable income} - \\text{Total deduction}"},{"lb":"Chargeable income","tex":"\\text{Chargeable income} = \\text{Total income} - \\text{Eligible deductions}"},{"lb":"Rent relief","tex":"\\text{Rent relief} = \\min(20% \\times \\text{Annual rent paid}, \\ N500{,}000)"},{"lb":"Net tax liability payable","tex":"\\text{Net tax} = \\text{Gross tax liability} - \\text{Withholding tax already paid (excl. dividend)}"}],"focus":"This chapter is the computational capstone of the personal-tax material — the exam rewards a fixed, memorised sequence: gross/taxable income → total deduction → total income → eligible deductions → chargeable income → graduated tax → less WHT already paid = net tax payable. Within that sequence, three sub-rules are the most common trip points: (1) the 20%-of-gross-income accommodation cap uses gross income EXCLUDING the rental value itself, to avoid circularity; (2) dividend and other final-tax income is grossed up and shown, then excluded again from the chargeable-income base, since it is taxed at a flat WHT rate rather than the graduated rates; (3) unearned income for the current assessment year is actually the PRECEDING year's unearned income, per the actual-year/preceding-year basis split in s.26 NTA. Also note explicitly: the current NTA has **no child or dependent-relief allowance** — questions listing an employee's children/dependants (as in the Mr Babalola and Mr Clement illustrations) are testing whether candidates correctly omit these older-regime reliefs, not whether they can compute them.","errors":["Applying the 20%-accommodation-benefit cap to a \"gross income\" figure that already includes the accommodation's own rental value — this creates a circular computation; the correct base explicitly excludes the rental value itself.","Taxing dividend income at the graduated PIT rates alongside earned income — dividend WHT is final tax for individuals, so gross dividend is shown (often to demonstrate the gross-up mechanic) but then excluded from the chargeable-income base actually subjected to graduated rates.","Including the CURRENT assessment year's unearned income (e.g. this year's dividends) in the computation — s.26 NTA uses the PRECEDING year's unearned income for the current assessment, a full 12-month lag that is easy to miss.","Assuming child/dependent relief still applies under the current NTA — it does not; the s.30(2) eligible-deductions list is exhaustive and contains no dependent-relief item, regardless of how many children or dependants a question describes.","Forgetting that rent relief is the LOWER of 20% of annual rent paid and N500,000 flat — a high earner paying substantial rent is still capped at N500,000, not 20% of their actual (larger) rent figure."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Under s.26 of the NTA, what basis of assessment applies to an individual's UNEARNED income (e.g. dividends), as distinct from employment income?","o":["Actual year basis, same as employment income","Preceding year basis","A 3-year average basis","Following year basis","No basis — unearned income is always exempt"],"a":1,"w":"Section 26 NTA uses the actual year basis for employment income, but the preceding year basis for unearned income — meaning a 2026 assessment includes unearned income actually received in 2025.","src":"Chapter 13.2","sec":"13.2"},{"q":"An individual receives N60,000,000 as compensation for loss of office. What amount is a chargeable gain, under s.50 of the NTA?","o":["N0","N10,000,000","N50,000,000","N60,000,000","N30,000,000"],"a":1,"w":"Compensation up to N50,000,000 is not a chargeable gain — only the excess over that threshold (N60m − N50m = N10,000,000) is chargeable.","src":"Chapter 13.3","sec":"13.3"},{"q":"What is the maximum rent relief an individual can claim as an eligible deduction, regardless of how much rent they actually pay?","o":["N100,000","N250,000","N500,000","N1,000,000","There is no cap — the full 20% of rent paid is always allowed"],"a":2,"w":"Rent relief is 20% of annual rent paid, capped at N500,000 — whichever of the two figures is LOWER, so N500,000 is the hard ceiling regardless of actual rent paid.","src":"Chapter 13.8","sec":"13.8"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the eligible deductions listed under s.30(2) of the NTA?","o":["Contributions under the National Housing Fund","Contributions under the Pension Reform Act 2014","Child/dependent relief for each qualifying child","Life assurance premium paid in the preceding year","Rent relief, capped at N500,000"],"a":2,"w":"The s.30(2) eligible-deductions list is exhaustive and contains no child/dependent relief item — this was a feature of older tax legislation that the current NTA does not carry forward.","src":"Chapter 13.8","sec":"13.8"},{"q":"A motor vehicle used solely for private, non-profit purposes is exempt from being treated as a taxable asset — up to how many vehicles per individual, per assessment year?","o":["1","2","3","4","There is no limit"],"a":1,"w":"Section 53 NTA limits this exemption to a maximum of 2 motor vehicles per individual in any assessment year.","src":"Chapter 13.3","sec":"13.3"}],"theory":[{"q":"Mr Babalola, an employee of Yakoyo Limited, has a gross salary of N5,000,000 per annum. His company pays for his accommodation (annual rental value N1,180,000) and provides an official car (market value N4,000,000). He paid a life assurance premium of N6,000/month in 2025, and contributes N5,000/month to an approved pension scheme. Compute his tax liability for the relevant year of assessment.","marks":10,"a":[{"table":{"align":"lrr","head":["Mr Babalola — tax liability, 2026 assessment year","N","N"],"rows":[["Gross salary","","5,000,000"],["BIK — accommodation, restricted to 20% of gross income (N5,000,000 + N200,000)","1,040,000",""],["BIK — official car (5% × N4,000,000)","200,000","1,240,000"],["**Gross emoluments**","","**6,240,000**"],["Less: life assurance relief (N6,000 × 12)","72,000",""],["Less: pension contribution (N5,000 × 12)","60,000","(132,000)"],["**Chargeable income**","","**6,108,000**"]]}},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Tax computation","N"],"rows":[["First N800,000 @ 0%","0"],["Next N2,200,000 @ 15%","330,000"],["Next N3,108,000 @ 18%","559,440"],["**Tax payable**","**889,440**"]]}},{"note":"The question describes 5 children and 2 aged parents Mr Babalola supports — none of this affects the computation, since the current NTA has no child or dependent-relief allowance. The gratuity of N300,000 mentioned for his 31 December 2026 retirement is not addressed in the source's own solution."}],"src":"Chapter 13.16","sec":"13.12"},{"q":"Mr Clement is married with 7 children of varying ages and occupations. In 2026 he is on a gross salary of N28,500,000 per annum, and his employer provides an official car (cost N5,000,000) and accommodation (annual rental value N6,000,000). His employer deducted N2,137,500 pension and N712,500 NHF from his salary in 2026, and he paid a N400,000 life assurance premium in 2025. His aged parents (who have no income) live with him. Compute Mr Clement's personal income tax payable for 2026.","marks":10,"a":[{"table":{"align":"lrr","head":["Mr Clement — tax liability, 2026 assessment year","N","N"],"rows":[["Salary","","28,500,000"],["BIK — accommodation, 20% of gross income (N28,500,000 + N250,000)","5,750,000",""],["BIK — official car (5% × N5,000,000)","250,000","6,000,000"],["**Gross emoluments**","","**34,500,000**"],["Less: life assurance premium","400,000",""],["Pension","2,137,500",""],["NHF","712,500","(3,250,000)"],["**Chargeable income**","","**31,250,000**"]]}},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Tax computation","N"],"rows":[["First N800,000 @ 0%","0"],["Next N2,200,000 @ 15%","330,000"],["Next N9,000,000 @ 18%","1,620,000"],["Next N13,000,000 @ 21%","2,730,000"],["Next N6,250,000 @ 23%","1,437,500"],["**Total tax liability**","**6,117,500**"]]}},{"note":"As with Mr Babalola, the 7 children's ages/occupations are a deliberate distractor — no child/dependent relief exists under the current NTA, so this detail correctly plays no role in the computation."}],"src":"Chapter 13.16","sec":"13.12"}]}},{"n":14,"t":"Taxation of Investment Income","brief":"Income that arises from putting money to work rather than from labour: rental income (with its own allowable/disallowable expense rules and a full worked computation), dividend income (definition, exemptions, non-resident treatment, franked investment income), interest income, and royalty income — closing with the preceding-year basis of assessment that governs all four, and the final-tax treatment for non-resident recipients.","outcomes":["Discuss the basis of assessment of an investment income","Compute the tax liability of an individual earning investment income","Explain franked investment income"],"secs":[{"n":"14.2","t":"Definition of investment income — section 201 of NTA","b":[{"def":{"t":"Investment income","d":"income from investing activities — dividend, interest, royalty and any other income of a similar nature. Investment incomes arise from investment decisions such as purchasing shares, letting property, or placing cash in fixed/other interest-yielding accounts."}}]},{"n":"14.3","t":"Computation of rental income chargeable to tax on property, including contractor-financed projects","b":[{"h4":"14.3.1  Rental income"},{"key":"Rental income — the gain/profit from another person's use/occupation of property — is chargeable to tax, deemed to accrue to the recipient **day to day** over the periods the payment covers."},{"note":"Letting/renting of **residential property** is VAT-exempt (s.185(1)). Rental income (including from contractor-financed projects) is subject to **10% withholding tax**. A lease agreement with an annual value below **N10 million (or 10× the annual minimum wage, whichever is higher)** is not subject to stamp duty (s.134 NTA)."},{"h4":"14.3.2  Rent received in advance"},{"key":"Rent received in advance is taxable **in the year it is received**, not the year it is earned."},{"h4":"14.3.3  Allowable rental expenses — section 20 of NTA"},{"ol":["tenement rates or land use charges;","cost of collecting rent (caretaker/estate agent/legal representative fees, etc.);","cost of advertising for tenants;","expenses for repairs and maintenance of the building;","bad debt incurred;","interest on money borrowed and employed in acquiring/renovating the property;","commission paid to an agent or caretaker;","insurance premium on the property; and","water rate."]},{"h4":"14.3.4  Disallowable expenses — section 21 of NTA"},{"ol":["expenses not incurred for the purpose of earning the rental income;","expenditure of a capital nature;","depreciation of the building; and","appropriation of profit — including income tax, drawings, reserves, etc."]},{"note":"The study text works a two-year rental computation on this material, but prints it as **Question 1 of the chapter's worked examples at §14.11** rather than as an illustration here. It is set out in full at §14.11 — work it alongside this section, because it turns entirely on separating the allowable expenses of §14.3.3 from the disallowable ones of §14.3.4."}]},{"n":"14.4","t":"Tax implications of dividends and interests","b":[{"h4":"14.4.1  Dividend income"},{"p":"Dividend includes, in relation to: a company not being wound up/liquidated — profits shared/distributed to shareholders in any form, including the nominal value of bonus shares/debentures/securities awarded; a company being wound up/liquidated — any distribution (money or money's worth) earned before/during the winding up; and a regulated securities lending transaction — compensating payments a lender receives from its approved agent/borrower."},{"h4":"Nigerian dividends and timing (s.7 NTA)"},{"p":"Nigerian dividends include: dividend distributed by a Nigerian company (the **gross** amount before deduction); and undistributed profit of a Nigerian company treated as distributed under any Nigerian law. Dividend income is deemed to arise on the day payment **becomes due**."},{"h4":"Company treatment (s.8 NTA)"},{"p":"A company includes dividend income in its profits **gross** of any tax paid/deducted at source. Dividends a Nigerian company receives **by way of shares** of the paying company are **not** included in its chargeable profits and are not subject to deductions."},{"h4":"Dividend exempt from tax (s.162 NTA)"},{"ol":["dividend distributed by an authorised collective investment scheme;","dividend from investments in wholly export-oriented businesses;","dividend derived from outside Nigeria and brought in through approved channels;","dividend/rental income a REIT receives on behalf of shareholders, where ≥75% is distributed within 12 months of the relevant financial year-end — though this does **not** exempt: the shareholder from tax on what they receive; the REIT from tax on its own management fees/profits/other income; or the REIT from tax if it fails to meet the 75%/12-month conditions; and","compensating payments qualifying as dividends, received by a lender from its approved agent/a borrower (or by an approved agent from a borrower/lender), in a Regulated Securities Lending Transaction."]},{"h4":"14.4.3  Nigerian dividends received by non-resident persons — section 19 of NTA"},{"key":"A dividend a **non-resident** receives from a Nigerian company is **not subject to further tax** beyond the tax deducted at source (s.19 NTA)."},{"p":"A Nigerian company's dividend is deemed derived from the **recipient's territory of residence** — or, if the recipient is non-resident, they are deemed a person resident outside Nigeria deriving Nigerian income/profit. The relevant tax authority for a non-resident (corporate or individual) receiving Nigerian dividends is the **NRS** (s.3(1)(a)(iv) NTAA)."},{"h4":"14.4.5  Other provisions relating to dividend — section 51(3) and (4) of NTAA"},{"ol":["for dividend, interest, rent, royalty, directors' fees, and entertainer/sportsperson payments, the tax paid over to the tax authority is the **final tax** due from a **non-resident** recipient;","dividend a Nigerian company distributes, received after tax deduction, is the recipient's **franked investment income** — not charged further tax; and","where franked investment income is **redistributed**, and tax must be accounted for on the gross redistribution, the company may **set off** the tax it itself already suffered on the same income."]},{"note":"A numbering gap in the study text, flagged rather than silently corrected: this section's sub-sections run **14.4.1, then 14.4.3, then 14.4.5** — there is no 14.4.2 and no 14.4.4 anywhere in the chapter. The numbering here follows the book, so those two numbers are absent on this site too; nothing is missing."}]},{"n":"14.5","t":"Interest income — section 4(6)(a)(i)–(iv) of NTA","b":[{"h4":"14.5.1  Definitions"},{"p":"Interest: accrues in Nigeria where the liability falls on a Nigerian resident or a non-resident's Nigerian permanent establishment, regardless of where/how paid; includes penal interest and similar payments, income from government/corporate securities/bonds/debentures, attaching premiums/prizes, discounts, fees, non-interest-finance profit shares, finance-lease finance-cost elements, and related FX differences/hedging-derivative payments; for debts, includes discounted-paper returns and debt-claim income (secured or not, profit-participating or not) plus related FX differences; and for regulated securities lending, includes compensating payments a borrower receives from its approved agent/a lender."},{"h4":"14.5.2 – 14.5.4  Interests from sources outside Nigeria (s.162 NTA), relevant territories (s.3(1)(a)(iv) NTAA), and interests that are tax-exempt"},{"ol":["interest from a source outside Nigeria, brought in through approved channels, for a Nigerian resident (s.162);","compensating payments qualifying as interest, received by an approved agent from a borrower/lender in a Regulated Securities Lending Transaction;","interest on Federal/State government bonds; and","interest on a bank loan (granted on or after 1 January 1997) to a person in agricultural trade/business, local plant/machinery fabrication, or a Family Economic Advancement Programme cottage industry (as working capital) — where the moratorium is ≥12 months and the interest rate does not exceed the base lending rate when granted/refinanced/restructured."]},{"note":"The territorial/non-resident rules mirror dividend's (s.3(1)(a)(iv) NTAA — deemed derived from the recipient's territory of residence; NRS is the relevant authority for non-residents). Interest on **short-term securities and corporate bonds**, earned by an individual after WHT deduction, is **not charged further tax** (s.51(5) NTAA)."},{"h4":"14.5.5  Interest on short term securities and corporate bonds — section 51(5) of NTAA"},{"key":"Interest on **short term securities and corporate bonds** earned by an **individual**, after deduction of tax at source, **shall not be charged to further tax** (s.51(5) NTAA). The withholding deduction is the final tax on that interest, so it is left out of the individual's chargeable income altogether rather than grossed up and credited."}]},{"n":"14.6","t":"Royalty income","b":[{"h4":"14.6.1  Definition — section 4(6)(c) of NTA"},{"def":{"t":"Royalty","d":"payments of any kind received/receivable, paid/payable, as consideration for the use of, or the right to use/exploit, any property — a payment to a legal owner of property/a patent/a copyrighted work/a franchise, by those using it to generate revenue, often expressed as a percentage of the revenue it generates."}},{"h4":"14.6.2  Income chargeable"},{"warn":"Non-allowance as a deduction (s.17(5)(e)): amounts a permanent establishment pays/owes a non-resident person (or their connected persons) as royalties/fees/similar payments for use of patents/other rights are **not deductible**, other than towards reimbursement of actual expenses."},{"key":"Any form of property — anywhere situated, including shares, options, rights, debts, digital/virtual assets, and incorporeal property generally — is a **chargeable asset** (s.34(1)(a)). With royalties/IP rights now included as chargeable assets, their chargeable gains attract **30% companies income tax** for large companies."},{"h4":"14.6.3  Income exempted from tax — section 162(1)(s) of NTA"},{"note":"Royalty earned abroad and brought into Nigeria through approved channels is **exempt** from tax (s.162(1)(s) NTA)."}]},{"n":"14.7","t":"Taxation of dividend, interest, rent, royalty and directors' fees paid to non-residents — section 51(2) of NTA","b":[{"key":"For dividend, interest, rent, royalty, directors' fees, and payments to entertainers/sportspersons, the tax paid over to the relevant tax authority is the **final tax** due from a **non-resident** recipient of the payment."}]},{"n":"14.8","t":"Basis of assessment and payment of taxes on investment income","b":[{"key":"Dividend, interest, rent and royalty are all assessed on a **preceding year basis** — the assessable income for any assessment year is the income of the year **immediately preceding** it."}]},{"n":"14.9","t":"Franked investment income","b":[{"def":{"t":"Franked investment income","d":"dividend a company receives, after WHT deduction at source by the paying company — not subject to further tax in the recipient's hands, since the WHT already deducted is the final tax."}},{"p":"Per s.8(1) NTA, dividend income is included in the recipient company's profits **gross** of any tax paid/deducted at source."},{"key":"Where the recipient company **redistributes** the franked investment income, it has the right to **set off** the WHT it already suffered on the dividend received against the WHT payable on the redistributed dividend — preventing **double taxation**."}]},{"n":"14.10","t":"Chapter review","b":[{"p":"This chapter provided a detailed explanation of investment incomes — dividend, interest, rent and royalty — and highlighted which investment incomes are exempt from tax."}]},{"n":"14.11","t":"Worked examples — study text questions and suggested solutions","b":[{"p":"The study text sets **four questions** at §14.11.1 — two computations and two theory questions — with its own suggested solutions at §14.11.2. All four are reproduced here in full, with the book's answers, every figure independently recomputed, and added explanation."},{"eg":{"t":"Alhaji Dimka — chargeable rental income over two years, with a source omission corrected","q":[{"p":"Alhaji Dimka completed a two-wing duplex in Katsina on 31 December 2022, and let both flats from 1 January 2023 through an estate agent. Income/expenses:"},{"table":{"align":"lrr","head":["","2023 (N)","2024 (N)"],"rows":[["Rent received (gross)","13,500,000","13,500,000"],["Repairs and maintenance","520,000","850,000"],["Water rate","82,000","105,000"],["Agent's commission","850,000","850,000"],["Professional charges","100,000","150,000"],["Insurance","152,000","152,000"],["Caretaker's wages","218,000","222,000"],["Tenement rate","25,000","32,000"]]}},{"p":"Additional information: (i) capital allowances agreed with the tax authority for 2024 and 2025 were N680,000 and N520,000 respectively; (ii) repairs and maintenance comprised — Depreciation N100,000/N85,000; repairs of the tenant's bathroom N0/N180,000; repairs of Alhaji Dimka's own residence N420,000/N585,000 (2023/2024); (iii) tenement rate includes N5,000 (2023) and N20,000 (2024) paid on Alhaji Dimka's **private residence**. **Required**: compute the rental income assessable to tax for the relevant tax years."}],"a":[{"note":"Rent (and other investment income) is assessed on a **preceding-year basis** (s.26/14.8) — 2023 income is assessed in the **2024** assessment year, and 2024 income in the **2025** assessment year, which is why the computation below is headed by assessment year, one year ahead of the receipt year."},{"table":{"align":"lrr","head":["Alhaji Dimka — chargeable rental income","2024 assessment (N)","2025 assessment (N)"],"rows":[["Rent received","13,500,000","13,500,000"],["Less allowable expenses:","",""],["Water rate","82,000","105,000"],["Agent's commission","850,000","850,000"],["Professional charges","100,000","150,000"],["Insurance","152,000","152,000"],["Caretaker's wages","218,000","222,000"],["Tenement rate (excluding the private-residence portion)","20,000","12,000"],["Repairs — tenant's bathroom only (genuinely let-property repairs)","0","180,000"],["**Total allowable expenses**","**(1,422,000)**","**(1,671,000)**"],["**Assessable rent**","**12,078,000**","**11,829,000**"],["Less: capital allowance","(680,000)","(520,000)"],["**Chargeable rent**","**11,398,000**","**11,309,000**"]]}},{"warn":"The study text's own printed solution for the 2025 assessment year (2024 income) omits the N180,000 tenant's-bathroom repair from allowable expenses entirely, yielding a chargeable rent of N11,489,000 instead of N11,309,000 — despite the question deliberately breaking repairs into 3 categories (depreciation, tenant's bathroom, Alhaji's own residence) specifically to test whether a candidate can distinguish them. Depreciation is disallowed (s.21(c)) and repairs of Alhaji's own residence are disallowed (not incurred to earn the rental income, s.21(a)) — but repairs of the **tenant's** bathroom are squarely \"expenses incurred for repairs and maintenance of the building\" under s.20's allowable list, and were correctly excluded for 2023 (where the source itself shows N0 for that item, since none existed that year) but incorrectly dropped for 2024. The computation above corrects this one item and carries the correction through to the final chargeable-rent figure."},{"warn":"The study text's own printed solution **omits the N180,000 repair of the tenant's bathroom** from the 2025 assessment year. Its allowable expenses for that year total N1,491,000 — water rate, agent's commission, professional charges, insurance, caretaker's wages and the net tenement rate — with no line for repairs at all, giving an assessable rent of N12,009,000 and a chargeable rent of **N11,489,000**. But repairs and maintenance **of the let building** are expressly allowable: the book's own answer to Question 4(b) of this same chapter lists \"any expenses incurred for repairs and maintenance of the building\" as item (iv). The whole purpose of the question's note (ii) is to let the candidate split the N850,000 into its disallowable parts (depreciation N85,000 and the landlord's own residence N585,000) and its **allowable** part (the tenant's bathroom N180,000) — and the printed solution then drops the allowable part. Including it gives allowable expenses of N1,671,000, assessable rent of N11,829,000 and chargeable rent of **N11,309,000**, which is the figure used above."},{"note":"The 2024 assessment year is unaffected, because the tenant's bathroom repair in that column is **nil** — the N520,000 is entirely depreciation (N100,000) and repairs to the landlord's own residence (N420,000), both disallowable. So the book's N1,422,000 and N11,398,000 for 2024 are correct, and only the second year diverges, by N180,000 of expense."},{"key":"Three separate traps are built into this one question, and each is a standard rental-income test. **Depreciation** is never allowable — it is not an expense at all for tax, which is why **capital allowances** are given separately (N680,000 and N520,000). Expenditure on the **landlord's own residence** is not incurred in earning the rent, so it fails the \"wholly and exclusively\" test of §14.3.3. And the **tenement rate must be split** — only the portion on the let property qualifies, so N25,000 less N5,000 = N20,000, and N32,000 less N20,000 = N12,000."}],"tag":"Study text — 14.11.1, Question 1","open":true}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 14.11.1, Question 2","t":"Mohammed Limited — undistributed profits deemed distributed","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Mohammed Limited is a company owned by the members of the Mohammed family. Its issued share capital of **20,000,000 ordinary shares of N1 each** is held by:"},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Shareholder","Shares"],"rows":[["Sule","8,000,000"],["Hassan","4,000,000"],["Bawa","2,000,000"],["Saheed","6,000,000"],["**Total**","**20,000,000**"]]}},{"p":"For the year ended **31 December 2025** the company made a **net profit before tax of N53,000,000**. The company **has not paid a dividend since it was established 8 years ago**."},{"p":"The Nigeria Revenue Service is of the opinion that the non-payment of dividend by the company was with a view to **reducing the tax payable by its shareholders**. In exercise of the powers conferred on it by the Nigeria Tax Act, 2025, the NRS has directed that **N15,900,000** of the profit be treated as having been distributed as dividend."},{"p":"**Required:** Compute the amount deemed to be income in the hands of the company's shareholders."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"llr","head":["Shareholder","Computation","Deemed dividend (N)"],"rows":[["Sule","8,000,000 / 20,000,000 × N15,900,000","6,360,000"],["Hassan","4,000,000 / 20,000,000 × N15,900,000","3,180,000"],["Bawa","2,000,000 / 20,000,000 × N15,900,000","1,590,000"],["Saheed","6,000,000 / 20,000,000 × N15,900,000","4,770,000"],["","**Total**","**15,900,000**"]]}},{"note":"The total row is added here as a **cross-check**; the book's printed solution stops at the four individual amounts. The four deemed dividends must add back to the N15,900,000 directed, because the whole of the deemed distribution is apportioned among the shareholders — N6,360,000 + N3,180,000 + N1,590,000 + N4,770,000 = N15,900,000. Always foot this; if it does not add up, a shareholding fraction is wrong."},{"key":"The power being exercised is **section 10 of the NTA** — assessments based on undistributed profits treated as distributed, set out at §4.8.6. Its conditions are all satisfied here, and an answer should say so: the company is a **Nigerian company controlled by not more than five individuals** (there are four shareholders); it has **not distributed** profits for which accounts have been made up; and the Service has **directed in writing** that a proportion which could have been distributed without detriment to the business be construed as distributed. The gross amount so construed then becomes **taxable income in the hands of the individual shareholders**, in proportion to their holdings in the ordinary capital."},{"note":"Note what is **not** asked and should not be computed. The **N53,000,000 net profit before tax** is deliberate noise — the apportionment is of the **N15,900,000 the Service directed**, not of the whole profit, and the Service's judgement about how much could have been distributed \"without detriment to the company's business\" is not open to recalculation by the candidate. Nor is the shareholders' personal tax asked for: the requirement is the amount **deemed to be income**, which is the **gross** figure before any deduction at source."},{"warn":"Two time limits matter in practice and are easy marks if the question invites them. A direction under this section must be made **not later than three years** after the Service receives the company's duly audited accounts for the period — so the \"8 years with no dividend\" cannot all be reopened, only periods within that window. And the company has a **right of appeal** against the direction under Chapter Four of the NTAA (see §5.8)."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 14.11.1, Question 3","t":"Dividend income and royalty income explained","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Investment incomes are incomes received primarily from investments, which include decisions such as the purchase of shares, property for letting purposes, placement of cash in fixed and other interest-yielding accounts, etc. **Required:** Explain the following: **(a)** dividend income; **(b)** royalty income."}],"a":[{"h4":"3(a)  Dividend income"},{"p":"**(i) Section 4(6)(b) of NTA.** Dividend includes, in relation to:"},{"ul":["a company that is **not** in the process of being wound up or liquidated — profits, in any form, shared or distributed to the shareholders, including an amount equal to the **nominal value of bonus shares, debentures or securities** awarded to the company's shareholders;","a company that **is** being wound up or liquidated — any distribution, whether in money or money's worth, earned **before or during** the winding up or liquidation; and","a **regulated securities lending transaction** — compensating payments received by a lender from its approved agent or borrower."]},{"p":"**(ii) Section 7 of NTA.** Nigerian dividends include:"},{"ul":["dividend distributed by a Nigerian company, and shall be the **gross amount** of that dividend before any deduction; and","any amount of the **undistributed profit** of a Nigerian company which is treated as distributed under the provisions of any law in Nigeria."]},{"p":"The income from a dividend distributed by a Nigerian company is **deemed to arise on the day on which payment of that dividend becomes due**."},{"p":"**(iii) Section 8 of the NTA.**"},{"ul":["A company shall include dividend income in its profits, **gross of any tax paid or deducted at source**.","Dividends received by a Nigerian company **by way of shares of the paying company** shall **not** be included in its profits chargeable to tax under this Act, and shall not be subject to deductions."]},{"p":"**(iv) Dividend exempt from tax — section 162 of NTA.** The following dividend incomes are exempt from tax:"},{"ul":["dividend distributed by an **authorised collective investment scheme**;","dividend received from investments in **wholly export-oriented businesses**;","dividend **derived from outside Nigeria** and brought into Nigeria through approved channels;","dividend or rental income received by a **real estate investment company** on behalf of its shareholders, where **not less than 75%** of the dividend or rental income is distributed **within 12 months** after the end of the financial year in which it was earned — provided that nothing in this subsection shall be construed to exempt: a **shareholder** from tax on the dividend or rental income received from a real estate investment company; a real estate investment company from tax on **management fee, profits or any other income earned for and on its own account**; or a real estate investment company from tax on dividend or rental income **if it does not meet the conditions** stipulated above;","compensating payments which qualify as dividends, received by a **lender** from its approved agent or a borrower in a Regulated Securities Lending Transaction; and","compensating payments which qualify as dividends, received by an **approved agent** from a borrower or lender on behalf of a lender or borrower in a Regulated Securities Lending Transaction."]},{"p":"**(v) Nigerian dividends received by non-resident persons — section 19 of NTA.** Any dividend received by a non-resident from a Nigerian company **shall not be subject to further tax** other than tax deducted at source."},{"h4":"3(b)  Royalty income"},{"p":"**(i) Definition — section 4(6)(c) of NTA.** \"Royalty\" includes **payments of any kind received or receivable, paid or payable as a consideration for the use of, or the right to use or exploit, any property**."},{"p":"Royalty is a payment to an owner for the use of property — especially **patents, copyrighted works, franchises or natural resources**. A royalty payment is made to the legal owner of a property, patent, copyrighted work or franchise by those who wish to make use of it for the purposes of generating revenue or other such desirable activities. In most cases royalties are designed to compensate the owner for the asset's use, and are legally binding."},{"p":"**(ii) Income chargeable.**"},{"ul":["**Introduction** — royalties are often expressed as a **percentage of the revenues** obtained using the owner's property, but can be negotiated to meet the specific needs of an arrangement. The use of royalties is common where an inventor or original owner chooses to sell his or her product to a third party in exchange for royalties from the future revenues it may generate.","**Non-allowance as a deduction — section 17(5)(e) of NTA** — no deduction shall be allowed in respect of amounts paid or payable by a **permanent establishment** to the non-resident person or any of its connected persons by way of royalties, fees or similar payments in return for the use of patents or other rights, **other than towards reimbursement of actual expenses**.","**Chargeable asset — section 34(1)(a) of NTA** — any form of property shall be a chargeable asset, **whether situated in Nigeria or not**, including any form of asset, shares, options, rights, debts, digital or virtual assets, and incorporeal property generally. Royalties and intellectual property rights having been included in \"chargeable assets\", the chargeable gains will now attract **30% companies income tax for large companies**."]},{"key":"The two halves of this question test opposite things. **Dividend** is tested on breadth — five statutory provisions, and the exemption list is the longest single list in the chapter. **Royalty** is tested on the one structural point: a royalty is **income in the recipient's hands** but, where it flows from a permanent establishment to a connected non-resident, it is **not deductible in the payer's** — an anti-avoidance rule aimed at profit-stripping through intra-group licence fees."},{"note":"Two numbers in the dividend answer are worth memorising because they are the only ones there: the real-estate-investment-company exemption needs **not less than 75%** distributed **within 12 months** of the financial year end. Miss either and the exemption fails entirely, and the company is taxed on the dividend and rental income it received on its shareholders' behalf."},{"warn":"A numbering defect in the book's printed solution, flagged rather than silently corrected: within part (b)(ii) the three bullets are introduced as \"Introduction\", \"Non-allowance as a deduction\" and then — instead of a third bullet — a stray item numbered **\"i.\"** for \"Chargeable asset\". It is the third limb of (b)(ii), not a new numbered part."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 14.11.1, Question 4","t":"Rent received in advance, and five allowable rental expenses","open":true,"q":[{"p":"**(a)** There are some unique considerations when it comes to the taxation of rental income. **Required:** Explain the provisions of the tax laws in respect of rental income received in advance."},{"p":"**(b)** In computing the gains or profit from rental income, certain expenses are allowed as deductible. **Required:** Identify **five** examples of expenses that can be deducted when computing rental income."}],"a":[{"h4":"4(a)  Rent received in advance"},{"p":"When a landlord receives rental income in advance, the amount is **taxable in the year it is received, not when it is earned**."},{"key":"This is a **receipts basis**, and it is a deliberate departure from the accruals basis a landlord would use in financial statements. Two years' rent received in one lump is taxed in full in the year of receipt, even though only one year of it has been earned — so there is no spreading, and no deferred-income adjustment. Read it with §14.8: rent is assessed on the **preceding year basis**, so rent received in 2025 is assessed in the 2026 year of assessment, in full."},{"h4":"4(b)  Allowable rental expenses"},{"p":"In computing the gain or profit from rental income for tax purposes, the following expenses are allowable deductions:"},{"ol":["**tenement rates or land use charge**;","**cost of collecting rent** — for example, fees paid to a caretaker, estate agent, legal representative, etc.;","**cost of advertising for tenants**;","any expenses incurred for **repairs and maintenance of the building**;","**interest on money borrowed** and employed in acquiring or renovating the property;","**bad debts** incurred;","**commission** paid to an agent or caretaker;","**insurance premium** paid on the property; and","**water rate**."]},{"note":"The requirement asks for **five**; the book's solution gives nine. Give five and stop. But note item (iv) — **repairs and maintenance of the building** — because it is precisely the deduction the book's own solution to Question 1 of this chapter forgets to allow. The two answers contradict each other, and this one is right."},{"key":"Every item on the list is a cost of **earning the rent**, which is the test that decides anything not listed. That is why Question 1's repairs to the landlord's own residence and the tenement rate on his private house are excluded, and why depreciation is excluded — it is not a cost incurred at all, and capital expenditure is relieved instead through **capital allowances**."}]}}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Chargeable rental income","tex":"\\text{Chargeable rent} = \\text{Gross rent} - \\text{Allowable expenses} - \\text{Capital allowance}"},{"lb":"Deemed dividend to a shareholder (undistributed-profit direction)","tex":"\\text{Deemed dividend} = \\dfrac{\\text{Shareholder's shares}}{\\text{Total issued shares}} \\times \\text{Amount directed as distributed}"}],"focus":"This chapter's single most important structural fact is that dividend, interest, rent and royalty are ALL assessed on a preceding-year basis, unlike employment income (actual year) — so a 2026 assessment always uses 2025's investment income, never 2026's. Keep the allowable-vs-disallowable rental expense split sharp: genuine repairs to the LET property are deductible, but depreciation and repairs to the landlord's OWN residence never are, and a mixed tenement-rate/expense figure must be apportioned between the two uses rather than deducted in full. Also memorise franked investment income's core mechanic — dividend WHT is final tax on first receipt, but a redistributing company may set off the WHT it already suffered against the WHT payable on redistribution, which is specifically how Nigerian law avoids taxing the same dividend stream twice as it passes through a chain of companies.","errors":["Assessing an individual's current-year dividend/interest/rent/royalty receipts in the current assessment year — all four investment-income types use the PRECEDING year basis, so income received in 2025 is assessed in the 2026 assessment year, not 2025.","Deducting the FULL tenement rate (or a similar mixed-use expense) where part of it relates to the landlord's own private residence — only the portion genuinely relating to the let property is an allowable expense; the private-residence portion must be excluded.","Treating ALL repairs-and-maintenance figures as automatically disallowed once depreciation is spotted within them — a single repairs figure can bundle disallowed depreciation, disallowed private-residence repairs, AND genuinely allowable let-property repairs together; each component needs separate treatment, not a blanket exclusion.","Confusing \"dividend exempt from tax\" under s.162 with \"dividend that is final tax\" under s.51(3) — an exempt dividend is never taxed at all, while a final-tax dividend IS taxed (via WHT), just not taxed AGAIN at the recipient's marginal/graduated rate.","Forgetting that royalties and IP rights are now explicitly chargeable assets (s.34(1)(a)) — their disposal can trigger a 30% companies income tax charge on chargeable gains for large companies, a change worth keeping distinct from ordinary royalty-INCOME taxation."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"On what basis of assessment is an individual's dividend, interest, rent or royalty income assessed for personal income tax purposes?","o":["Actual year basis","Preceding year basis","Following year basis","3-year average basis","No fixed basis — determined case by case"],"a":1,"w":"Dividend, interest, rent and royalty are all assessed on a preceding year basis — the assessable income for a year is the income of the year immediately preceding it.","src":"Chapter 14.7","sec":"14.7"},{"q":"A landlord receives N2,000,000 rent in December 2025 for the 2026 rental year in advance. In which year is this amount taxable?","o":["2026, the year it is earned","2025, the year it is received","2027, the year after receipt","It is exempt from tax entirely","Split equally between 2025 and 2026"],"a":1,"w":"Rent received in advance is taxable in the year it is RECEIVED, not the year it is earned — so this amount is taxable in 2025.","src":"Chapter 14.2","sec":"14.2"},{"q":"Which of the following rental expenses is NOT an allowable deduction under s.20 of the NTA?","o":["Cost of collecting rent (e.g. estate agent fees)","Insurance premium on the property","Depreciation of the building","Water rate","Interest on money borrowed to acquire the property"],"a":2,"w":"Depreciation of the building is explicitly disallowed under s.21 NTA — the other four listed items are all allowable deductions under s.20.","src":"Chapter 14.2","sec":"14.2"},{"q":"Dividend received by a company after withholding tax deduction at source by the paying company is known as what, and what is its further tax treatment?","o":["Passive income — taxed again at the company's full rate","Franked investment income — not subject to further tax, since the WHT is final tax","Unearned income — taxed at a flat 30% rate regardless of WHT","Exempt income — never taxed at all, at any stage","Chargeable income — added to taxable profits before WHT credit"],"a":1,"w":"Such dividend is franked investment income — not subject to further tax in the recipient company's hands, since the withholding tax already deducted is the final tax.","src":"Chapter 14.8","sec":"14.8"},{"q":"A dividend paid by a Nigerian company to a non-resident shareholder is subject to what further tax, beyond the tax deducted at source?","o":["An additional 10% surcharge","A further 30% companies income tax charge","No further tax — the WHT deducted at source is the final tax","Full graduated PIT rates on the gross amount","VAT at 7.5%"],"a":2,"w":"Section 19 NTA is explicit that a dividend a non-resident receives from a Nigerian company is not subject to further tax beyond the tax deducted at source.","src":"Chapter 14.3","sec":"14.3"}],"theory":[{"q":"Mohammed Limited, owned entirely by the Mohammed family, has issued share capital of 20,000,000 ordinary shares of N1 each, held: Sule 8,000,000; Hassan 4,000,000; Bawa 2,000,000; Saheed 6,000,000. For the year ended 31 December 2025, the company made a net profit before tax of N53,000,000 and has not paid a dividend since incorporation 8 years ago. The NRS, believing this was done to reduce shareholders' tax, has directed that N15,900,000 of the profit be treated as distributed as dividend. Compute the amount deemed to be income in the hands of each shareholder.","marks":8,"a":[{"p":"Each shareholder's deemed dividend is their proportionate shareholding applied to the N15,900,000 directed amount:"},{"table":{"align":"lrr","head":["Shareholder","Shareholding","Deemed dividend (N)"],"rows":[["Sule","8,000,000 / 20,000,000 (40%)","6,360,000"],["Hassan","4,000,000 / 20,000,000 (20%)","3,180,000"],["Bawa","2,000,000 / 20,000,000 (10%)","1,590,000"],["Saheed","6,000,000 / 20,000,000 (30%)","4,770,000"],["**Total**","20,000,000 (100%)","**15,900,000**"]]}},{"note":"This applies the undistributed-profits-treated-as-distributed rule (s.10 NTA, covered in Chapter 4) — Mohammed Limited has exactly 4 shareholders, within the ≤5-individual-controlled-company trigger for this power."}],"src":"Chapter 14.11","sec":"14.3"},{"q":"(a) Explain the provisions of the tax laws in respect of rental income received in advance. (b) Identify five examples of expenses that can be deducted when computing rental income.","marks":6,"a":[{"p":"**(a)** When a landlord receives rental income in advance, the amount is taxable in the year it is **received**, not the year it is earned."},{"p":"**(b)** Any five of: tenement rates or land use charges; cost of collecting rent (caretaker/estate agent/legal fees); cost of advertising for tenants; expenses for repairs and maintenance of the building; bad debt incurred; interest on money borrowed to acquire/renovate the property; commission paid to an agent/caretaker; insurance premium on the property; or water rate."}],"src":"Chapter 14.11","sec":"14.2"}]}},{"n":15,"t":"Taxation of Sole Traders / Self-Employed Individuals","brief":"Taxing unincorporated businesses: what makes an activity a \"trade\" (the badges-of-trade case law), the practical application of the taxable-income/deduction rules already established in Chapter 13, two additional deductions unique to trading income (donations, research and development), the full basis-period-of-assessment framework governing commencement/change-of-date/cessation, loss relief, and — the chapter's dense centrepiece — the complete capital allowances regime, closing with fully worked computations from adjusted profit through to tax payable.","outcomes":["Understand what constitutes a business or trade","Explain the factors considered when deciding whether an individual is engaged in a trade or business","Differentiate between revenue receipts and capital receipts","Explain the expenses specifically disallowed in ascertaining an individual's income/loss","Compute the adjusted profit or loss of an individual from a trade, business, profession or vocation","Compute the tax liabilities of individuals deriving income from trade or business"],"secs":[{"n":"15.2","t":"Meaning of a trade or profession and explanation of badges of trade","b":[{"p":"This chapter covers **unincorporated entities** — sole proprietorships."},{"h4":"15.2.1  What constitutes a business"},{"def":{"t":"Business","d":"includes a trade, profession or vocation — every trade/profession/vocation is therefore a business, making \"business\" a very wide concept."}},{"h4":"15.2.2  What constitutes a trade"},{"key":"There is **no absolute test** for what constitutes a trade — courts have consistently held that each case must be examined on its own facts. Three cases illustrate this:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Case","Holding"],"rows":[["*Fry v Burma Corporation Limited*","Lord Atkin interpreted \"trade\" as the various activities of commerce — winning/using/multiplying the product of the earth and selling it, or purchasing/selling a commodity, or offering services for reward — confirming there is **no infallible test** for a trade."],["*Glasgow Heritable Trust Limited v I.R. Comrs*","That a former owner was carrying on a trade is **not conclusive evidence** that the current activity is a trade."],["*Building and Civil Engineering Holidays Scheme Management Limited v Clark*","That an activity generates a surplus does **not necessarily** make it a trade — courts examine the facts/circumstances of each transaction against the \"badges of trade.\""]]}},{"h4":"15.2.3  Badges of trade"},{"p":"The Royal Commission on the Taxation of Profits and Income (1951-1955, chaired by Lord Radcliffe) identified these considerations:"},{"ol":["the subject-matter of the realisation;","the length of the period of ownership;","the frequency/number of similar transactions;","supplementary work on or connected with the property realised;","the circumstances responsible for the realisation; and","the motive."]},{"note":"In *Trustees of Methodist Church Mission v FBIR*, an assessment on income from a property (Wesley House) the trustees developed and leased out was challenged on the basis that the Methodist Church enjoys exemption for ecclesiastical activities. The Federal Revenue Court held the entity was doing precisely what it was formed to do — carrying on the **business of dealing in real estate** — so it could not claim exemption, since the income was established as derived from a trade/business."}]},{"n":"15.3","t":"Definition of gross income — section 147 of NTAA","b":[{"def":{"t":"Gross income (s.147 NTAA)","d":"the total income of an individual, in a particular assessment year, from all sources ascertained under the NTAA."}}]},{"n":"15.4","t":"Computation of assessable profit of a trade or profession","b":[{"h4":"15.4.1  Taxable and non-taxable income"},{"p":"**(a) Taxable income — section 28 of NTA.** This is the **aggregate amount** of:"},{"ol":["assessable profits from **trade, business, profession or vocation**;","**employment income**;","income from **investing activities**;","profits or income from **any other source**; and","**chargeable gains** from the disposal of chargeable assets."]},{"p":"**(b) Non-taxable income.** The non-chargeable gains of an individual, which apply to a sole trader exactly as they apply to an employee:"},{"h4":"(i)  Personal injury — section 50 of the NTA"},{"ul":["Amounts **not exceeding N50,000,000** obtained by way of compensation or damages for any wrong or injury suffered by an individual **in his person or in his profession or vocation** — including compensation for loss of office or employment, and wrong or injury for libel, slander or enticement — **shall not be chargeable gains**.","Where the sum **exceeds N50,000,000**, **only the excess** amount shall constitute a chargeable gain.","Any person who **pays** compensation for loss of office or employment to an individual is required, **at the point of payment**, to **deduct and remit** the tax due under this section to the relevant tax authority.","The tax so deducted shall be remitted **within the time specified** under the Pay-As-You-Earn or Deduction of Tax at Source Regulations issued under the Nigeria Tax Administration Act."]},{"h4":"(ii)  Principal private residences — section 51 of NTA"},{"ul":["The gains accruing to an individual are **exempt** in respect of the disposal of, or an interest in: a **dwelling-house** or part of such dwelling-house; and **land** (other than land used for commercial purposes) immediately adjoining the dwelling house, **up to a maximum of one acre**.","The exemption under this section shall be enjoyed **once in the lifetime** of an individual.","The consideration shall be **apportioned** where a person disposes of only part of a dwelling-house, or of a house used partly as a dwelling place and partly for carrying out a trade, business, profession or vocation."]},{"h4":"(iii)  Personal chattels — section 52 of NTA"},{"ul":["A gain accruing on a disposal of an asset which is **tangible movable property being personal chattels** of an individual shall not be a chargeable gain if the total amount or value of the consideration for the disposal does not, in a period of assessment, exceed **N5,000,000 or three times the annual national minimum wage, whichever is higher**.","Where **two or more assets** — whether or not forming part of a set of articles — are disposed of by a person to the **same person**, or to persons **acting in concert**, or to **connected persons**, whether on the same or different occasions, the two or more transactions shall be treated as a **single transaction** disposing of a single asset, with any apportionments where necessary.","Where the disposal is of part of a right or interest in, or over, tangible movable property, the rule above applies to the asset **as a whole**, taking the consideration as including the **market value of what remains undisposed of**, in addition to the actual consideration.","The provisions of this section apply to a gain accruing on a disposal of **two or more assets**, not necessarily forming part of a set of articles of any description, which are tangible movable properties, in the same manner as they apply to a gain on a disposal of a single asset or of a set of articles."]},{"h4":"(iv)  Motor vehicle — section 53 of NTA"},{"ul":["A motor vehicle used **solely for private or non-profit purposes** shall **not be an asset** for the purposes of taxation.","The exemption under this section shall be limited to **not more than two motor vehicles** by an individual in any year of assessment."]},{"h4":"(v)  Gifts — section 54 of NTA"},{"ul":["Where a person disposes **by way of a gift** an asset **acquired by him by way of a gift** or any other form of gift — not being an acquisition on a **devolution on death** — the gains accruing from the disposal shall **not be chargeable gains**.","An asset is acquired or disposed of by way of gift where **no consideration** is paid or received for the acquisition or disposal."]},{"warn":"**(vi)** This section shall **not apply to a disposal of currency of any description.**"},{"key":"Sole traders with an annual turnover of **N100 million or less** are exempt from companies income tax (CIT). Those generating income from **agricultural business** are exempt from tax for their **first 5 years**."},{"h4":"15.4.2  Allowable and non-allowable deductions"},{"h4":"(a)  Deductions allowed — section 20 of NTA"},{"key":"In ascertaining profit/loss, all expenses **wholly and exclusively incurred in producing the income** during the period are deductible, including:"},{"ol":["interest payable on debt employed to generate income (subject to excess-interest restrictions on connected-person loans);","rent/premiums for land/buildings occupied to generate income;","salary/wages/remuneration paid to employees, and the cost of employee benefits/allowances;","expenses for repair of premises/plant/machinery/fixtures, or renewal/repair/alteration of implements/utensils/articles employed;","expenditure establishing, preserving or defending title/rights over an asset;","contributions to an approved staff pension/provident/retirement fund (Pension Reform Act or similar);","proven damage to, or loss of, stock/inventory;","bad/doubtful debts incurred during trade/business (even if due before the basis period began) — excluding bad debts from related-party transactions, and subject to GAAP-consistent doubtful-debt estimation;","research and development expense for the period;","other expense from a previous period for the trade/business, or specifically for the profit-ascertainment period — expenditure within **6 years before** commencing business, which would have been deductible if incurred after commencement, is deemed incurred on the **first day** of trading;","dividends/mandatory distributions by an SEC-approved real estate investment company to shareholders;","compensating payments (qualifying as interest) by a lender to its approved agent/a borrower in a regulated securities lending transaction; and","expense on assistive devices/disability-related products (hearing aids, wheelchairs, braille materials)."]},{"p":"For bad/doubtful debts specifically: any amount already allowed in a previous period on the same debt is deducted first; and sums recovered on previously written-off/allowed debts are **added back** to profits in the recovery period."},{"note":"A liability that is waived, released or recovered is included in assessable profits/chargeable gains. An expense in a foreign currency is deductible only to the extent of its **Naira equivalent at the official CBN exchange rate** for the relevant date/period."},{"h4":"(b)  Deductible donations — section 163 of NTA"},{"ol":["the amount of any donation made in the period, to a fund/body/institution in Nigeria this section applies to, is deductible — whether the donation is of a **revenue or capital** nature;","this applies to donations made to: public funds; statutory bodies/institutions; religious/charitable/educational/scientific institutions established in Nigeria; bodies recognised under the Diplomatic Immunities and Privileges Act; or pandemic/natural-disaster/public-emergency interventions;","the taxpayer must provide documentary evidence of the donation to the tax authority;","the **total** deduction allowed for donations in a year cannot exceed **10% of profit before tax** for that year; and","for a non-cash donation, its value is the **lower** of market value at donation, or the consideration paid to acquire it."]},{"h4":"(c)  Deduction for research and development — section 164 of NTA"},{"ol":["notwithstanding s.20, the amount incurred in a period on research and development is deductible;","the deduction cannot exceed **5% of turnover** for that year; and","where the R&D outcome is later sold/transferred for exploitation/commercialisation, the proceeds are taxed accordingly."]},{"h4":"(d)  Deductions not allowed — section 21 of NTA"},{"p":"No deduction is allowed for:"},{"ol":["capital repaid or withdrawn from the trade/business/profession/vocation;","expenditure of a capital nature;","domestic/private expense, or expenditure on assets not used for the trade/business/profession/vocation;","sums recoverable under insurance or an indemnity contract;","taxes on profit/income levied in Nigeria or elsewhere;","payments to an unapproved savings/widows-and-orphans/pension/provident/retirement fund, society or scheme;","depreciation/impairment of a fixed asset/investment, or an unrealised foreign-currency exchange difference;","sums reserved out of profits (subject to the 6-year pre-commencement rule above);","payments to a connected person inconsistent with the Service's Transfer Pricing Regulations;","expense incurred deriving income exempt from income tax;","expense deductible in determining chargeable gains under the Act;","penalty or fine imposed under any law;","tax or penalty borne on behalf of another person;","compensating payments (qualifying as dividends) by a borrower to its approved agent/a lender in a regulated securities lending transaction;","compensating payments (qualifying as interest/dividends) by an approved agent to a borrower/lender in such a transaction; or","expense on which VAT was due but not charged, or (for imports) applicable import duty/levy was not paid."]},{"key":"Note the one departure from an employee's position and from a company's: for a **partnership** these same lists apply, but partners' salaries, interest on capital and leave passages become **allowable** (§17.8). For a **sole trader** they do not — the proprietor cannot deduct his own salary, because he and the business are the same person."}]},{"n":"15.5","t":"Ascertainment of total income — section 28(1) of NTA","b":[{"key":"The **total income** of an individual for any year of assessment is the **taxable income less total deduction** (s.28(1) NTA)."},{"p":"\"Total deduction\", for this purpose, is the aggregate of the amounts deducted from taxable income before arriving at total income — a **loss** relieved under §15.9, the **capital allowances** of §15.10, **exempt income**, and income on which a deduction at source is the **final tax** (franked investment income, and the non-resident payments of §14.7)."},{"note":"Keep the three stages in order, because the Act gives each a different name and the exam uses them precisely: **taxable income** (the aggregate of all sources, §15.4.1) → less total deduction → **total income** (§15.5) → less eligible deductions → **chargeable income** (§15.7), to which the Fourth Schedule rates are then applied."}]},{"n":"15.6","t":"Eligible deductions — section 30(2) of NTA","b":[{"p":"**(a)** \"Eligible deductions\" include payments made by the individual in a year of assessment in respect of:"},{"ol":["the individual's contributions under the **National Housing Fund**;","the individual's contributions under the **National Health Insurance Scheme**;","the individual's contributions under the **Pension Reform Act, 2014**;","**interest on loans for developing an owner-occupied residential house**;","the **annual amount of any annuity or premium** paid by the individual **during the year preceding the year of assessment** in respect of insurance on his life or the life of his spouse, or a contract for a deferred annuity on his own life or the life of his spouse; and","**rent relief of 20% of annual rent paid, subject to a maximum of N500,000**, whichever is lower — provided that the individual accurately declares the actual amount of rent paid and such other relevant information as may be prescribed by the relevant tax authority."]},{"p":"**(b)** \"Total income\" means total income as defined in §15.5 above."},{"warn":"A cross-reference defect in the study text, flagged rather than silently corrected: limb (b) reads *\"total income means total income as defined in **19.5** above\"*. There is no §19.5 in this chapter, and chapter 19 is Loss Relief. The reference is plainly to **§15.5** (ascertainment of total income), which is what is given above."},{"key":"This is a **closed list of six**, and it is the same list as §13.8 for an employee — a sole trader gets no more and no fewer eligible deductions than an employee does. In particular there is **no child relief and no dependent-relative relief**; those were features of the repealed PITA and do not survive into the NTA 2025."}]},{"n":"15.7","t":"Ascertainment of chargeable income — section 30(1) of NTA","b":[{"key":"The **chargeable income** of an individual is the **total income** of that individual, ascertained under the provisions of section 28 of the Act, **less eligible deductions** (s.30(1) NTA)."},{"p":"Total income (s.28(1)), eligible deductions (s.30(2)), and chargeable income (s.30(1)) work exactly as set out in **Chapter 13** — taxable income less total deduction gives total income; total income less eligible deductions (NHF, NHIS, pension, owner-occupied-residence loan interest, life assurance, rent relief) gives chargeable income."}]},{"n":"15.8","t":"Basis period of assessment","b":[{"h4":"15.8.1  Commencement of business — section 22 of NTA"},{"key":"Ordinarily, assessable profits for a year of assessment are the profits of the accounting period **immediately preceding** it. For a **new** business, the assessable profits of its **first** year of assessment are the profits from the **commencement date to the end of the first accounting period**."},{"h4":"15.8.2  Change in accounting date — section 23 of NTA"},{"p":"Where a taxable person changes its usual accounting date, the basis period for the relevant assessment year runs from the day after the previous year's basis period ended, up to the new accounting date — with subsequent years computed on the new accounting period. The tax authority must be notified **at least 30 days** before the usual income-tax-return due date."},{"h4":"15.8.3  Cessation of trade or business — section 24 of NTA"},{"ol":["on permanent cessation within an accounting period, the assessable profit is the amount from the start of that accounting period to the cessation date — tax is payable **within 6 months** of cessation;","a sum received/paid after cessation, which would have been included/deducted had it occurred before cessation, is deemed received/paid on the **last day before cessation**, and must be disclosed to the tax authority within **1 month**;","where that happens, any additional assessment/reduction claim/repayment is made as necessary, with ordinary objection/appeal provisions applying; and","for a **deceased** individual, a sum the personal representative receives/pays after death (that would have been included/deducted pre-death) is deemed received/paid on the **last day before death**."]},{"h4":"15.8.4  Continuity of trades — section 25 of NTA"},{"note":"An individual is **not** treated as having commenced or ceased a trade/business/profession/vocation solely because they changed their territory of residence, or became/ceased being a partner in a partnership carrying on the **same-nature** trade before and after that change."}]},{"n":"15.9","t":"Loss relief and treatments","b":[{"key":"Loss relief for an individual mirrors that for incorporated companies, except for current-year loss — and losses can be **carried forward indefinitely**."},{"h4":"15.9.1  Current year loss relief — section 28(3) of NTA"},{"p":"Deductible loss = the amount incurred during the year/preceding year in a trade/business/profession/vocation, plus loss on disposal of a chargeable asset — subject to: the aggregate loss deduction never exceeding the loss itself; the loss being deducted, as far as possible, from the first assessment year's assessable profit after the loss year, continuing until fully recouped; the loss for any year being computed per the Part V, Chapter Two basis period; and any digital/virtual-asset loss being deductible only against digital/virtual-asset profit."},{"h4":"15.9.2  Carry forward loss relief — section 27(6) of NTA"},{"p":"For companies: the aggregate loss deduction never exceeds the loss amount; loss is deductible only from the trade/business it arose in; it is deducted as far as possible from the first assessment year's assessable profit after the loss year, continuing until fully recouped; and it is computed per the basis period used for assessable profits/income. A digital/virtual-asset loss is deductible only against digital/virtual-asset business profit."},{"h4":"15.9.3  Treatment of losses under commencement and cessation of business — section 28(3) of NTA"},{"p":"A loss on **commencement** can be carried forward indefinitely. Where a trade **ceases**, any terminal loss that could not be relieved in the cessation year — for want of sufficient/available profit — is **deemed lost**."}]},{"n":"15.10","t":"Computation of capital allowance (CA)","b":[{"h4":"15.10.1  Definitions of qualifying capital expenditure and capital allowances"},{"def":{"t":"Capital allowance","d":"relief claimable by a taxpayer who incurs qualifying capital expenditure (QCE) during a basis period, on assets in use for trade/business at the basis period's end — granted in lieu of depreciation, which is a non-allowable expense for tax purposes."}},{"warn":"The NTA 2025 does **not** recognise initial allowance or balancing adjustment — only **annual allowance** applies."},{"h4":"15.10.2  Qualifying capital expenditure — first paragraph of the First Schedule to the NTA"},{"p":"QCE means expenditure in a basis period on acquiring, refurbishing or improving an asset's value, of these 10 kinds:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["QCE category","Covers"],"rows":[["Plant and equipment","Capital expenditure on plant and equipment."],["Building","Construction of buildings/structures/permanent-nature works, other than expenditure in the other categories."],["Mast","Tall upright poles erected/fastened to land or another structure."],["Mining","Working a mine/oil well/wasting-mineral-deposit source — acquiring deposits/rights/information; searching, discovering, testing or accessing deposits; or constructing works/buildings of little value once the source stops being worked (or once a concession ends)."],["Agricultural","Plantation land clearing, planting (not replanting), maintenance and other pre-production expenses (incurred from the first trading day); and ranching structures, breeding-herd animals (not animals raised for sale), and animals functioning as trade apparatus with a life expectancy >5 years."],["Intangible assets","R&D of equipment/facilities/patents/licences/secret formulas/processes; information on industrial/commercial/scientific process or product development/technical feasibility; and discovery/testing of future-market products/processes — where these bring an asset into existence."],["Motor vehicle","A fleet of ≥3 public-transport buses; a private-operator inter-city mass-transit coach of ≥25 seats; or other motor vehicles."],["Heavy transportation","Aircraft engines/fuselages; pipelines/cables; vessels; ship engines; train engines/railway carriages/tracks/coaches."],["Software","Acquisition of software or other electronic-application capital outlays."],["Furniture and fittings","Including those used in vessels/ships/aircraft/trains/plants/buildings, for trade/business purposes."]]}},{"h4":"15.10.3  Capital allowance — annual allowance, basis period (¶1) and computation"},{"p":"The basis period for an allowance is the basis period for assessable profit/income — except: an interval between one year's basis period and the next becomes part of the **second** basis period; but an interval between the year preceding permanent cessation and the cessation year's basis period becomes part of the **first** basis period."},{"key":"A person incurring QCE wholly/exclusively for trade/business, in a basis period, receives an annual allowance at the **Table I** rate for each year the asset is used in that trade/business — with 1% of QCE recorded annually (a notional, non-claimable statistical figure) until disposal, and the allowance **prorated** where the basis period is **less than one year**."},{"h4":"15.10.4  Conditions for granting capital allowances — section 27(2) of NTA"},{"ol":["for a priority-sector company, a certificate of acceptance from the Industrial Inspectorate Department is required for the QCE; and expenditure is **ineligible** as QCE where VAT was due but not charged, or (for imports) applicable import duty/levy was unpaid;","where an asset is only **partly** used to generate assessable profits, the capital allowance is **prorated** to the taxable-income-generating portion;","capital allowance is **not** prorated where non-taxable income is **below 10%** of total income; and","notwithstanding the above, capital allowance attributable to the priority activities of a company enjoying an economic development incentive is deducted **only** from that priority business's assessable profits."]},{"note":"Per ¶7 of the First Schedule, capital allowance is granted for a year only if, at the basis period's end, the person **owns** the asset **and** used it for trade/business."},{"h4":"15.10.5  Capital allowances for small companies"},{"p":"**Small companies**: capital allowance on QCE they incur is deemed **utilised** during the periods they are tax-exempt."},{"h4":"15.10.6  Capital allowances rates (Table I, ¶6, First Schedule)"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Class","Rate","Qualifying expenditure"],"rows":[["1","10%","Building; agricultural; mast; intangible assets; heavy transportation."],["2","20%","Plant; agricultural equipment; furniture and fittings; mining; other equipment."],["3","25%","Motor vehicle; software; other capital expenditure."]]}},{"h4":"15.10.7  Capital allowances under hire purchase transactions or finance lease (¶2, First Schedule)"},{"def":{"t":"Hire purchase (s.201 NTA)","d":"a financial arrangement where a person acquires immediate use of an asset via regular instalments over a period, possibly gaining ownership on completion."}},{"def":{"t":"Finance lease (s.201 NTA)","d":"a lease where the lessee effectively assumes most of the risks and rewards of asset ownership."}},{"ol":["QCE in a basis period, for such an asset, is limited to the **instalments paid** up to that period — excluding any interest;","the \"owner\" is construed to be the **hirer/lessee**; and","the person **letting** the asset (owner/lessor) is **not** entitled to any allowance."]},{"h4":"15.10.8  Capital allowances under an operating lease — paragraph 15 of the First Schedule"},{"def":{"t":"Operating lease (s.201 NTA)","d":"an arrangement transferring the right to use an asset for rental payments over an obligatory period, where the asset is not wholly amortised during that period."}},{"ol":["where an owner leases an asset under an operating lease, this Part applies as though the expenditure were incurred for the **owner/lessor's own** trade/business;","where an asset is acquired under a hire-purchase/finance-lease contract for the hirer/lessee's trade/business, the **hirer/lessee** is deemed the owner (to the exclusion of the lessor); and","for this purpose, the basis period for any assessment year is the year **immediately preceding** it."]}]},{"n":"15.11","t":"Computation of personal income tax liabilities of a sole trader — format","b":[{"p":"The format mirrors Chapter 13's pro forma, with assessable trade/business/profession/vocation profit as the starting line: aggregate income sources → less total deduction (loss, capital allowance, exempt income, final-tax income) → total income → less eligible deductions → chargeable income → apply Fourth Schedule graduated rates → less withholding tax already paid (excl. dividend) → net tax payable."},{"note":"The study text works a two-year commencement computation on this format, but prints it as **Question 1 of the chapter's worked examples at §15.13** rather than as an illustration here. It is set out in full at §15.13, together with three further questions — two full adjusted-profit computations and a capital allowances theory question."}]},{"n":"15.12","t":"Chapter review","b":[{"p":"This chapter fully explained the constituents of a business and trade, and addressed the taxation of income from business, profession and vocation."}]},{"n":"15.13","t":"Worked examples — study text questions and suggested solutions","b":[{"p":"The study text sets **four questions** at §15.13.1 — three computations and one theory question — with its own suggested solutions at §15.13.2. All four are reproduced here in full, with the book's answers, every figure independently recomputed and cross-footed, and added explanation."},{"eg":{"t":"Karo Enterprises — chargeable profits on commencement, with capital allowances","q":[{"p":"Karo Enterprises commenced business on 2 January 2026, making up accounts to 31 March each year. Adjusted profits: 2 Jan 2026 - 31 Mar 2026, N41,300,050; 1 Apr 2026 - 31 Mar 2027, N52,750,000. Capital expenditure acquired in 2026: motor vehicles N12,500,000 (6 Jan); office equipment N2,420,000 (8 Jan); furniture and fittings N1,390,000 (10 Jan). All relevant tax documents are kept. **Required**: compute the chargeable profits of the business for the relevant assessment years."}],"a":[{"note":"The first accounting period (2 Jan - 31 Mar 2026) is assessed in the **2027** assessment year (the year following its commencement, per s.22 NTA); the second accounting period (1 Apr 2026 - 31 Mar 2027) is assessed in the **2028** assessment year."},{"table":{"align":"llll","head":["Computation of capital allowances","Motor vehicles (25%)","Office equipment (20%)","Furniture & fittings (20%)"],"rows":[["Cost","N12,500,000","N2,420,000","N1,390,000"],["AY2027 annual allowance (3/12, first period)","(781,250)","(121,000)","(69,500)"],["WDV c/f to AY2028","11,718,750","2,299,000","1,320,500"],["AY2028 annual allowance (full 12 months)","(3,125,000)","(484,000)","(278,000)"],["WDV c/f to AY2029","8,593,750","1,815,000","1,042,500"]]}},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Karo Enterprises — chargeable profits","N"],"rows":[["**Assessment year 2027**",""],["Assessable profit (2/1/26 – 31/3/26)","41,300,050"],["Less: capital allowances (781,250 + 121,000 + 69,500)","(971,750)"],["**Chargeable profit**","**40,328,300**"],["**Assessment year 2028**",""],["Assessable profit (1/4/26 – 31/3/27)","52,750,000"],["Less: capital allowances (3,125,000 + 484,000 + 278,000)","(3,887,000)"],["**Chargeable profit**","**48,863,000**"]]}},{"note":"The study text's own capital allowance schedule labels these two computation rows \"Assessment year 2026\" and \"Assessment year 2027\" — one year out of step with its own main chargeable-profit computation (which correctly uses 2027 and 2028, consistent with the first-year-of-assessment rule applied above). The underlying figures are identical either way; the labels here use the main table's (correct) year numbering throughout, for internal consistency."},{"note":"The first period spans 2 January to 31 March 2026 — treated as a 3-month (quarter-year) basis period for proration purposes, giving annual allowances of exactly 3/12 of the full-year rate."},{"h4":"How each allowance follows"},{"ol":["The first basis period is only **three months** long (2 January to 31 March 2026), so the annual allowance is **time-apportioned by 3/12**: motor vehicles 25% × N12,500,000 × 3/12 = **N781,250**; office equipment 20% × N2,420,000 × 3/12 = **N121,000**; furniture and fittings 20% × N1,390,000 × 3/12 = **N69,500**. Total **N971,750**.","The second basis period is a **full twelve months** (1 April 2026 to 31 March 2027), so the allowance is the full annual figure: N3,125,000 + N484,000 + N278,000 = **N3,887,000**.","Chargeable profit, first period = N41,300,050 − N971,750 = **N40,328,300**; second period = N52,750,000 − N3,887,000 = **N48,863,000**."]},{"key":"The apportionment of the first allowance is the whole point of the question. The **rate** is annual, so where the basis period is shorter than a year the allowance is reduced in proportion to the months in it. Note also that all three assets were acquired **within the first basis period** (6, 8 and 10 January 2026), so all three qualify from the start — had one been bought in, say, June 2026, it would have attracted no allowance at all in the first period and a full year's allowance in the second."},{"warn":"A labelling inconsistency in the book, flagged rather than silently corrected: the printed capital-allowances schedule heads its two columns **\"Assessment year 2026\"** and **\"Assessment year 2027\"**, while the chargeable-profit statement immediately above it deducts those same two figures in **assessment years 2027 and 2028**. The schedule's headings are one year behind its own main computation. The years used above follow the main computation, which is right: on a preceding-year basis the period ending 31 March 2026 is assessed in 2027, and the period ending 31 March 2027 in 2028."},{"note":"Two typographical defects in the printed question, which do not affect the figures: the office equipment is given as **\"2, 420,000\"** (a stray space after the comma) and the furniture and fittings as **\"1.390,000\"** (a full stop in place of the first comma). They are N2,420,000 and N1,390,000, as the solution's own arithmetic confirms. The book also calls Karo Enterprises \"the company\" throughout, though the chapter is about sole traders and unincorporated businesses."}],"tag":"Study text — 15.13.1, Question 1","open":true}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 15.13.1, Question 2","t":"Qualifying capital expenditure, the conditions for capital allowances, and hire purchase/lease assets","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Capital allowance is a form of relief granted to any company which incurred capital expenditure for the purpose of a trade or business during a basis period, in respect of property, plant and equipment that are in use at the end of the basis period. Capital allowance is granted **in place of depreciation**, which is usually disallowed for income tax purposes."},{"p":"**Required:** **(a)** Explain qualifying capital expenditure. **(b)** Explain the conditions for granting capital allowances. **(c)** Discuss the special provisions regarding assets purchased under hire purchase agreements or leases."}],"a":[{"h4":"2(a)  Qualifying capital expenditure — first paragraph of the First Schedule to the NTA"},{"p":"Qualifying capital expenditure means expenditure incurred in a basis period for the **acquisition, refurbishment and improvement of the value of the asset**, which is:"},{"ol":["capital expenditure incurred on **plant and equipment**;","capital expenditure incurred on the **construction of buildings, structures or work of a permanent nature**, other than expenditure included in (i), (iii) or (iv);","**qualifying mast expenditure** — capital expenditure incurred on a tall upright pole erected on, or fastened to, land or another structure;","capital expenditure incurred **in connection with, or in preparation for, the working of a mine, oil well or other source of mineral deposits of a wasting nature** (other than expenditure included in (i) above): on the acquisition of deposits, or of rights in or over the deposits, or on the purchase of information relating to the existence and extent of the deposit; on searching for, or on discovering and testing, deposits, or winning access thereto; or on the construction of any work or building which is likely to be of little or no value when the source is no longer worked (or, where the source is worked under a concession, which is likely to become valueless when the concession comes to an end to the individual working the source immediately before it ends);","**qualifying agriculture expenditure** — capital expenditure incurred: in connection with a **plantation**, on the clearing of land for planting, planting (other than replanting), maintenance of the plantation and other pre-production expenses in connection with that plantation, which shall be deemed incurred **on the first day on which the trade or business commences**; and in respect of **ranching**, on structures used primarily for ranching, on raising herds of animals for the purpose of **breeding** (not for the purpose of sale), and on animals that function as **apparatus** with which a trade is carried on and whose life expectancy is **more than 5 years**;","**qualifying intangible assets expenditure** — capital expenditure incurred in respect of research and development of: equipment and facilities, patents, licences, secret formulas or processes; information concerning the development of industrial, commercial or scientific processes, technical feasibility, or products or processes; or the discovery and testing of products or processes for future market use, and such other similar costs which have brought into existence an asset;","**qualifying motor vehicle expenditure** — capital expenditure incurred on: a **fleet of buses of not less than three** used for public transportation; a public transportation **inter-city new mass transit coach of twenty-five seats and above** operated by a private establishment; or other motor vehicles;","**qualifying heavy transportation expenditure** — capital expenditure incurred on aircraft engines and fuselages, pipelines and cables, vessels, ships' engines, train engines, railway carriages, train tracks and coaches;","**qualifying software expenditure** — capital expenditure incurred on the acquisition of software or other such capital outlays on electronic applications; and","**qualifying furniture and fittings expenditure** — capital expenditure incurred on furniture and fittings, including those used in vessels, ships, aircraft, trains, plants and buildings for the purposes of trade or business."]},{"h4":"2(b)  Conditions for granting capital allowances — section 27(2) of NTA"},{"p":"The capital allowance to be granted in accordance with Part I of the First Schedule shall be the amount relating to the qualifying capital expenditure incurred **in generating the assessable profits**, provided that:"},{"ol":["in the case of **companies in the priority sector**, a **certificate of acceptance** issued by the Industrial Inspectorate Department shall be obtained in respect of the qualifying capital expenditure; and where **value added tax is due** under the Act but was **not charged** on an asset — or, in the case of an imported item, where the applicable **import duty or levy was not paid** — the relevant expenditure shall **not be eligible** as qualifying capital expenditure;","where the qualifying capital expenditure relates to an asset **only partly utilised** in generating the assessable profits, the capital allowance shall be **prorated**, and only the portion relating to the taxable income shall be allowed as a deduction;","the capital allowance computed shall **not be prorated** where the non-taxable income constitutes **less than 10%** of the total income of the company; and","the portion of capital allowance attributable to the **priority activities** of a company enjoying an economic development incentive under the Act shall be deducted **only from the assessable profits of the priority business**."]},{"p":"In addition, **paragraph 7 of the First Schedule** provides that a capital allowance in respect of qualifying expenditure incurred on an asset shall be made to a person for a year of assessment if, **at the end of a basis period**, the person:"},{"ol":["is the **owner** of the asset; and","**used the asset** for the purposes of its trade or business."]},{"h4":"2(c)  Special provisions for assets purchased under hire purchase agreements or leases"},{"p":"**Capital allowances under hire purchase transactions or finance lease agreements — paragraph 2 of the First Schedule to the NTA.** In relation to any asset acquired by a hirer or lessee under a hire-purchase or finance lease agreement, the following shall apply:"},{"ol":["the **qualifying capital expenditure** in any basis period shall, in relation to any asset so acquired under that agreement, be **limited to the amount of the instalments paid** by the hirer or lessee up to that basis period, **excluding any interest** paid under the agreement;","the **\"owner\"** of the asset shall be construed to be the **hirer or lessee** under the hire-purchase or finance lease agreement; and","the person **letting** the asset to the hirer or lessee under the agreement shall **not be entitled to an allowance**."]},{"p":"**Capital allowances under an operating lease — paragraph 15 of the First Schedule to the NTA.**"},{"ol":["Where in a basis period the **owner** of an asset leases the asset to another person under an **operating lease**, the provisions of this Part shall apply as though such expenditure were incurred for the purpose of a trade or business carried on by the **owner or lessor**.","Where an asset is acquired for use by a **hirer or lessee** under a hire purchase or finance lease contract for the purpose of a trade or business carried on by such hirer or lessee, the **hirer or lessee shall be deemed to be the owner** of the asset, and the provisions of this Part shall apply to the hirer or lessee **to the exclusion of the hiree or lessor**.","For the purposes of this paragraph in relation to the trade or business, the **basis period** for any year of assessment shall be taken to be the **year immediately preceding** that year of assessment."]},{"key":"Part (c) reduces to a single principle worth stating in one line: capital allowances follow **economic ownership, not legal title**. Under a **finance lease or hire purchase** the lessee bears the risks and rewards, so the **lessee** claims the allowances and the lessor claims none — but only on **instalments actually paid, excluding interest**, since the interest is a revenue finance cost deductible under §15.4.2. Under an **operating lease** the lessor retains the risks and rewards, so the **lessor** claims the allowances as though the leased asset were used in its own trade."},{"note":"Two conditions in part (b) are the ones candidates overlook. The **10% de minimis**: where non-taxable income is **less than 10%** of total income, the allowance is **not** apportioned at all, so a small amount of exempt income does not reduce the claim. And the **VAT/import-duty condition**: expenditure on which VAT was due but not charged, or on which import duty was not paid, is **disqualified entirely** — a tax-compliance precondition, not a computational rule."},{"warn":"A numbering defect in the book's printed solution, flagged rather than silently corrected: the provisos in part (b) are numbered **\"(iii), (ii), (iii), (iv)\"** — the first is numbered (iii) and an unnumbered second limb is run into it, so the number (iii) appears twice. They are numbered (i) to (iv) above, which is plainly what was intended."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 15.13.1, Question 3","t":"Alhaja Suliat — adjusted and chargeable profit of a trade, and donation conditions","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Alhaja Suliat, who resides in Lagos, has been trading for several years. The projected statement of profit or loss of her business for the year ending **31 December 2026** is as shown below:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Alhaja Suliat — projected statement of profit or loss, year ending 31 December 2026","rows":[["Gross profit","","50,000,000"],["Profit on sale of vehicle","","1,200,000"],["","","51,200,000","@t"],"Less: Expenses",["General administration","3,000,000","","@ind"],["Staff salaries","12,219,500","","@ind"],["Stationery and printing","800,000","","@ind"],["Postage and telephone","400,000","","@ind"],["Electricity","700,000","","@ind"],["Entertainment","250,500","","@ind"],["Vehicle repairs and maintenance","455,000","","@ind"],["Bank charges","800,000","","@ind"],["Donations","4,500,000","","@ind"],["Periodicals and technical journals","80,000","","@ind"],["Audit and accountancy fee","2,500,000","","@ind"],["Defalcation and embezzlement","1,200,000","","@ind"],["Repairs and renewals","4,485,000","","@ind"],["Depreciation","5,010,000","","@ind"],["VAT paid on equipment","2,800,000","","@ind"],["Bad and doubtful debts — specific","200,000","(39,400,000)","@ind"],["Net profit","","11,800,000","@tt"]]}},{"p":"You are provided with the following additional information:"},{"ol":["legal expense of **N360,000** formed part of staff salary instead of capital items;","donations were made to **The Boys Scout of Nigeria**;","she agreed with the Inspector of Taxes that **one third** of vehicle repairs and maintenance related to **private use**;","further examination of accounts revealed that salary of **N800,000** was paid to an **unknown person**;","the **Chief Accountant perpetrated 75%** of the defalcation;","allowable expenses of **N230,000** have been **omitted** from the accounts; and","repairs and renewals comprised: partitioning of new office **N930,500**; repairs to plant and machinery **N825,000**; repairs of office roof **N2,729,500** — total **N4,485,000**."]},{"p":"**Required:** **(a)** Compute the chargeable profit from trade of Alhaja Suliat for the relevant tax year. **(b)** Explain **three** conditions that must be met before donations can be allowed as an expense."}],"a":[{"h4":"3(a)  Computation of chargeable profit of the trade, 2027 assessment year"},{"stmt":{"t":"Alhaja Suliat — chargeable profit of the trade for the 2027 assessment year","rows":[["Net profit as per accounts","","11,800,000"],"Add back: disallowable expenses",["Staff salary — capital item","360,000","","@ind"],["Vehicle repairs and maintenance — private use (1/3 × N455,000)","151,667","","@ind"],["Salary — paid to unknown person","800,000","","@ind"],["Defalcation — 75% × N1,200,000","900,000","","@ind"],["Repairs and renewals — partitioning cost","930,500","","@ind"],["Repairs and renewals — repairs of office roof","2,729,500","","@ind"],["Donation — disallowed (N4,500,000 − 10% of N11,800,000)","3,320,000","","@ind"],["Depreciation","5,010,000","","@ind"],["VAT","2,800,000","17,001,667","@ind"],["","","28,801,667","@t"],"Less: non-taxable income and omitted expenses",["Profit on sale of vehicle","(1,200,000)","","@ind"],["Allowable expenses omitted","(230,000)","(1,430,000)","@ind"],["Assessable profit","","27,371,667","@t"],["Capital allowances","","(7,500,000)"],["Chargeable profit","","19,871,667","@tt"]]}},{"h4":"Why each item is treated as it is"},{"ol":["**Staff salary N360,000** — the note tells you this was a **legal expense of a capital nature** misposted to salaries. Capital expenditure is disallowed by s.21 (§15.4.2(d)(ii)), so it is added back regardless of the account it sits in.","**Vehicle repairs N151,667** — one third of N455,000 relates to **private use**, which is domestic or private expenditure disallowed by s.21. Only the two-thirds business portion stays.","**Salary to an unknown person N800,000** — an expense cannot be shown to have been **wholly and exclusively incurred in producing the income** if the recipient cannot be identified.","**Defalcation N900,000** — losses from defalcation by **ordinary employees** are an allowable trading risk, but a defalcation perpetrated by a **senior officer** (here the Chief Accountant, 75%) is not: it is treated as a loss of capital rather than a trading expense. So 75% is added back and the remaining 25% (N300,000) stays allowable.","**Partitioning of a new office N930,500** — an **improvement**, not a repair, so capital and disallowed.","**Donation N3,320,000** — donations are deductible under s.163 (§15.4.2(b)) but capped at **10% of profit before tax**: 10% × N11,800,000 = **N1,180,000**. The excess, N4,500,000 − N1,180,000 = **N3,320,000**, is added back.","**Depreciation N5,010,000** — never allowable (s.21); relieved instead through capital allowances.","**VAT N2,800,000** — input VAT on equipment, which is recoverable against output VAT under §7.13 and so is not an income tax expense at all.","**Profit on sale of vehicle N1,200,000** — a **capital** profit, not trading income, so it is deducted; any gain would be dealt with as a chargeable gain, and a private motor vehicle is in any event outside the charge under §15.4.1(b)(iv).","**Omitted allowable expenses N230,000** — genuinely incurred and allowable but never posted, so they are **deducted** to arrive at the correct profit."]},{"warn":"**The N7,500,000 of capital allowances is not given anywhere in the question.** The question supplies no capital expenditure, no asset schedule and no agreed allowance — yet the book's solution deducts N7,500,000 to get from an assessable profit of N27,371,667 to a chargeable profit of N19,871,667. The figure cannot be derived from the data. Treat the **assessable profit of N27,371,667** as the real answer to this question, and the N7,500,000 as an unsourced figure retained here only so that the book's printed chargeable profit can be reconciled. In an examination, if no capital allowance is given, stop at the assessable profit and say so."},{"warn":"The treatment of **repairs of the office roof (N2,729,500)** is open to challenge. The book adds it back, allowing only the N825,000 of repairs to plant and machinery out of the N4,485,000 total. But the book's own list of allowable expenses (§15.4.2(a) and the answer to Question 4(b) of Chapter 14) includes *\"any expense incurred for repair of premises, plant, machinery or fixtures employed in acquiring the income\"* — and a roof repair to business premises is on its face exactly that. The distinction that would justify the book is between a **repair** (restoring an asset to its former state — revenue, allowable) and a **replacement or improvement of an entirety** (capital, disallowed); a roof costing 60% of the entire repairs budget may well be a reconstruction rather than a repair. The book does not say which it is. **State your reasoning** either way: the mark is for the principle, not for guessing the examiner's classification."},{"note":"Why the **2027** assessment year: the accounts are for the year ending 31 December 2026, and trade profits are assessed on the **preceding year basis** (§15.8), so the profit of the year ended 31 December 2026 is assessed in 2027."},{"note":"Items left alone, and worth noting as such in an answer: **entertainment N250,500**, **bank charges N800,000**, **periodicals and technical journals N80,000**, **audit and accountancy fees N2,500,000** and **specific bad and doubtful debts N200,000** are all allowable. Specific bad debts are expressly allowable under s.20; a **general** provision would not be."},{"h4":"3(b)  Deductible donations — section 163 of NTA"},{"ol":["For the purposes of ascertaining the profits or loss from any trade, business, profession or vocation, there shall be deducted the amount of any donation made for that period to any **fund, body or institution in Nigeria** to which the section applies.","Without prejudice to the restriction on expenses allowed, any such donation may be deducted from the profits of that period **notwithstanding that the donation is of a revenue or capital nature**.","The section applies to donations made to: **public funds**; **statutory bodies or institutions**; **religious, charitable, educational and scientific institutions established in Nigeria**; **bodies recognised under the Diplomatic Immunities and Privileges Act**; or **any pandemic, natural disaster or other public emergency interventions**.","A person making a deduction for a donation shall **provide requisite documentary evidence** of the donation to the relevant tax authority.","The total deduction allowed for any year of assessment **shall not exceed an amount equal to 10% of the profit before tax** for that year.","In the case of a donation **other than in cash**, the value shall be the **lower of** the market value at the time of the donation **and** the consideration paid for the item when it was acquired."]},{"key":"The question asks for **three** conditions. The three that carry the marks are: the donee must be **a fund, body or institution in Nigeria of an approved class** (limb 3); the donation must be **evidenced by documentation** produced to the tax authority (limb 4); and the deduction is **capped at 10% of profit before tax** (limb 5). Limb 2 is worth adding if there is room, because it is counter-intuitive: unusually, a donation is deductible **even if capital in nature**."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 15.13.1, Question 4","t":"Mr Sulaimon — adjusted profit and tax liability of a sole proprietor","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Mr Sulaimon has been the sole proprietor of his business for several years. His detailed projected statement of profit or loss for the year ending **31 December 2026** is as follows:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Mr Sulaimon — projected statement of profit or loss, year ending 31 December 2026","rows":["Revenue",["Gross trading profit","","30,000,000","@ind"],["Dividend (gross)","","2,500,000","@ind"],["Rental income (gross)","","800,000","@ind"],["Profit on sale of PPE","","1,200,000","@ind"],["","","34,500,000","@t"],"Less: expenses",["Salaries and wages","3,500,000","","@ind"],["General expenses","1,600,000","","@ind"],["Finance costs","900,000","","@ind"],["Rates and electricity","500,000","","@ind"],["Repairs and maintenance","2,500,000","","@ind"],["Depreciation","4,800,000","","@ind"],["Bad debt","2,000,000","(15,800,000)","@ind"],["Net profit","","18,700,000","@tt"]]}},{"p":"**Additional information:**"},{"ol":["**salaries and wages** include: salary paid to Mrs Sulaimon, who is a **full-time employee** of the business, N50,000; wages to **Mrs Sulaimon's housemaid** N50,000; and salaries of other employees N3,100,000 — total N3,500,000;","**general expenses** include: subscription for trade magazines N200,000; cost of the business's **branded gifts** N750,000; **fines paid for a traffic offence** committed by Mrs Sulaimon N100,000; stationery N250,000; and **hiring of a generator set for Mr Sulaimon's private residence** N300,000 — total N1,600,000;","**finance cost** includes **N200,000** in respect of Mr Sulaimon's **private loan**;","Mr Sulaimon has a **life insurance policy** on his life for which he pays an annual premium of **N350,000**;","Mr Sulaimon made a contribution of **N150,000** to the **National Health Insurance Scheme (NHIS)** during the year;","Mr Sulaimon has **3 children** and maintained his **aged parent**; and","**capital allowance** agreed with the relevant tax authority is **N5,500,000**."]},{"p":"**Required:** **(a)** Compute the adjusted profit for the relevant tax year. **(b)** Compute the tax liability of Mr Sulaimon for the relevant tax year. *(WHT rate on dividend and rent is 10%.)*"}],"a":[{"h4":"4(a)  Computation of adjusted profit, year ending 31 December 2026 (2027 assessment year)"},{"stmt":{"t":"Mr Sulaimon — computation of adjusted profit","rows":[["Net profit","","18,700,000"],"Add: disallowable expenses",["Salaries and wages — wages paid to Mrs Sulaimon's maid","50,000","","@ind"],["General expenses — fines paid for traffic offence committed by Mrs Sulaimon","100,000","","@ind"],["General expenses — hire of generating set for Mr Sulaimon's private residence","300,000","","@ind"],["Finance cost — interest on Mr Sulaimon's private loan","200,000","","@ind"],["Depreciation","4,800,000","5,450,000","@ind"],["","","24,150,000","@t"],"Less: non-taxable income",["Dividend (gross)","2,500,000","","@ind"],["Profit on sale of PPE","1,200,000","(3,700,000)","@ind"],["Adjusted profit","","20,450,000","@tt"]]}},{"h4":"4(b)  Computation of tax liability, 2027 assessment year"},{"stmt":{"t":"Mr Sulaimon — computation of tax liability","rows":[["Adjusted profit","20,450,000",""],["Less: capital allowance","(5,500,000)","14,950,000"],"Deduct: eligible deductions",["Life insurance premium","350,000","","@ind"],["NHIS contribution","150,000","(500,000)","@ind"],["Chargeable income","","14,450,000","@t"]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Computation of personal income tax","rows":[["First N800,000 @ 0%","0",""],["Next N2,200,000 @ 15%","330,000",""],["Next N9,000,000 @ 18%","1,620,000",""],["Next N2,450,000 @ 21%","514,500",""],["  (N14,450,000)","",""],["Tax liability","2,464,500","","@t"],["Less: WHT earlier deducted from rent (10% × N800,000)","(80,000)",""],["Tax payable (net)","2,384,500","","@tt"]]}},{"key":"The two items of investment income are treated **differently**, and that contrast is the heart of the question. The **dividend** is deducted as non-taxable because it is **franked investment income** — the 10% withheld at source is the **final tax**, so the dividend leaves the computation entirely and **no credit is claimed** for the WHT on it. The **rent** stays in the computation and is taxed at the graduated rates, so the N80,000 withheld from it **is** credited against the liability. Claiming a credit for the dividend WHT as well — a very common error — would be claiming relief for tax on income that was never brought into charge."},{"note":"Note the salary distinction in item (i). **Mrs Sulaimon's own salary of N50,000 is allowed**, because she is a genuine full-time employee doing real work. Her **housemaid's wages of N50,000 are disallowed**, because that is domestic and private expenditure of the proprietor, not a cost of the trade. The two identical figures sitting side by side are there precisely to test whether the candidate applies the \"wholly and exclusively\" test rather than a rule of thumb about family members."},{"note":"Items in general expenses that are **allowed**: the **trade magazine subscription N200,000**, the **branded gifts N750,000** (business promotion) and the **stationery N250,000**. Only the **fine** and the **private generator hire** are added back — the fine because s.21 expressly disallows any penalty or fine imposed under any law, and the generator because it serves the proprietor's home."},{"key":"Item (vi) — \"3 children and maintained his aged parent\" — gives **no relief whatever**, exactly as in Chapter 13 Questions 1 and 3. Only the **life insurance premium** and the **NHIS contribution** qualify, because eligible deductions are the closed list of six at §15.6."},{"warn":"A copy-paste error in the book's printed solution, flagged rather than silently corrected: the first add-back is captioned **\"Wages paid to Mrs. Zuniga's maid\"**. There is no Mrs Zuniga anywhere in this question — the name belongs to a different question in some earlier edition. It is **Mrs Sulaimon's** maid, as the question's own note (i) states."},{"note":"A presentational point on the **rental income**. Strictly, rent is investment income assessed under Chapter 14 and would be removed from the trade computation and brought back in as a separate source when building total income. The book leaves it inside the trading figure and then credits the WHT, which reaches the same chargeable income and the same tax — but if a question asks for a **statement of total income by source**, separate it out."}]}}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Annual allowance (full basis period)","tex":"\\text{AA} = \\text{Qualifying capital expenditure} \\times \\text{Class rate}"},{"lb":"Annual allowance (basis period under 1 year)","tex":"\\text{AA} = \\text{QCE} \\times \\text{Class rate} \\times \\dfrac{\\text{Basis period (months)}}{12}"},{"lb":"Written-down value carried forward","tex":"\\text{WDV c/f} = \\text{Cost (or prior WDV)} - \\text{Annual allowance claimed}"}],"focus":"This chapter builds directly on Chapter 13 — do not re-learn the taxable-income/deduction rules from scratch, but DO master what is genuinely new here: the basis-period mechanics for a NEW business (first year = commencement date to end of first accounting period, assessed in the year AFTER commencement, per the same preceding-year-basis logic already familiar from investment income), and the full capital-allowances regime — especially that the current NTA has annual allowance ONLY (no initial allowance, no balancing adjustment/charge), that allowances are prorated for a basis period under 12 months, and the 3-tier rate table (10%/20%/25%) organised by asset class. For hire-purchase/finance-lease assets, remember the hirer/lessee — never the lessor — claims the allowance, and only on instalments actually paid (excluding interest).","errors":["Assuming a new business's first-year profit is assessed in the SAME calendar year it was earned — it is assessed in the following assessment year, following the same preceding-year-basis logic that governs ordinary trading profit, just anchored to the commencement date instead of a full prior year.","Applying a full 12-month annual allowance to a basis period shorter than a year — the allowance must be prorated (e.g. a 3-month first basis period gets exactly 3/12 of the full-year allowance), a rule easy to forget on a commencement-year computation.","Believing the NTA 2025 still grants an initial allowance or a balancing allowance/charge on disposal — it does not; only annual allowance exists under the current Act, a deliberate simplification from the older capital allowances regime.","Letting the lessor (rather than the hirer/lessee) claim capital allowance on a hire-purchase or finance-lease asset — ownership for capital-allowance purposes is construed as the HIRER/LESSEE, and the person letting the asset gets no allowance at all.","Capping the donations deduction at 10% of TURNOVER rather than 10% of profit before tax — the two are easily confused, since the separate R&D deduction (s.164) IS capped at 5% of turnover, a genuinely different base from the donations cap."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Under s.22 of the NTA, what is the basis of assessment for the FIRST year of assessment of a newly-commenced business?","o":["The profit of a full 12 months from commencement, regardless of accounting date","The profit from the commencement date to the end of the first accounting period","The profit of the calendar year in which commencement occurred, in full","The average of the first two years' profits","Nil — new businesses are automatically exempt in their first year"],"a":1,"w":"Section 22 NTA specifically bases a new business's first year of assessment on profits from the commencement date to the end of the first accounting period, however long or short that period is.","src":"Chapter 15.5","sec":"15.5"},{"q":"Which capital allowance regime does the current NTA 2025 adopt for qualifying capital expenditure?","o":["Initial allowance and annual allowance, but no balancing adjustment","Annual allowance only — no initial allowance and no balancing adjustment","Balancing allowance/charge only, with no annual allowance","Initial, annual and balancing allowances, exactly as under the older regime","No capital allowances at all under the current Act"],"a":1,"w":"The NTA 2025 does not recognise initial allowance or balancing adjustment — only annual allowance is granted under the current regime.","src":"Chapter 15.7","sec":"15.7"},{"q":"In a hire-purchase arrangement, who is entitled to claim capital allowance on the asset?","o":["The person letting the asset (the owner/lessor)","The hirer/lessee","Both parties, split equally","Neither party — hire-purchase assets never qualify","Whichever party the tax authority designates on a case-by-case basis"],"a":1,"w":"Under ¶2 of the First Schedule, the \"owner\" for capital-allowance purposes is construed to be the hirer/lessee — the person letting the asset is not entitled to any allowance.","src":"Chapter 15.7","sec":"15.7"},{"q":"What is the maximum donations deduction a taxpayer can claim in a year, under s.163 of the NTA?","o":["5% of turnover","10% of turnover","10% of profit before tax","20% of profit before tax","There is no cap on donations"],"a":2,"w":"Section 163 NTA caps the total donations deduction at 10% of profit before tax for the year — distinct from the separate 5%-of-turnover cap that applies to the research and development deduction under s.164.","src":"Chapter 15.3","sec":"15.3"},{"q":"A motor vehicle acquired for N12,500,000 is used in a trade for a full 12-month basis period. What is the annual allowance claimable, at the Class 3 rate?","o":["N1,250,000 (10%)","N2,500,000 (20%)","N3,125,000 (25%)","N6,250,000 (50%)","N12,500,000 (100%, full initial allowance)"],"a":2,"w":"Motor vehicle expenditure is Class 3, at a 25% rate: 25% × N12,500,000 = N3,125,000 for a full 12-month basis period.","src":"Chapter 15.7","sec":"15.7"}],"theory":[{"q":"Alhaja Suliat's projected statement of profit or loss for the year ending 31 December 2026 shows: Gross profit N50,000,000; Profit on sale of vehicle N1,200,000; total expenses N39,400,000; Net profit N11,800,000. Additional information: a N360,000 legal expense was wrongly included in staff salary instead of capital items; one-third of the N455,000 vehicle repairs/maintenance related to private use; a N800,000 salary was paid to an unknown person; the Chief Accountant perpetrated 75% of a N1,200,000 defalcation; N230,000 of allowable expenses were omitted from the accounts; donations were N4,500,000; depreciation N5,010,000; VAT paid on equipment N2,800,000; repairs and renewals of N4,485,000 comprised partitioning of a new office (N930,500), repairs to plant and machinery (N825,000), and repairs of the office roof (N2,729,500). (a) Compute the chargeable profit from trade for the relevant tax year. (b) Explain three conditions that must be met before donations can be allowed as an expense.","marks":12,"a":[{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Alhaja Suliat — chargeable profit, 2027 assessment year","N"],"rows":[["Net profit as per accounts","11,800,000"],["Add back — staff salary (capital item)","360,000"],["Vehicle repairs/maintenance — private use (1/3 × 455,000)","151,667"],["Salary paid to unknown person","800,000"],["Defalcation (75% × 1,200,000)","900,000"],["Repairs and renewals — partitioning (capital)","930,500"],["Repairs and renewals — office roof","2,729,500"],["Donation disallowed (4,500,000 − 10% × 11,800,000)","3,320,000"],["Depreciation","5,010,000"],["VAT paid on equipment","2,800,000"],["**Subtotal add-backs**","**17,001,667**"],["**Total**","**28,801,667**"],["Less: profit on sale of vehicle (non-taxable)","(1,200,000)"],["Less: allowable expenses omitted from accounts","(230,000)"],["**Assessable profit**","**27,371,667**"],["Less: capital allowances","(7,500,000)"],["**Chargeable profit**","**19,871,667**"]]}},{"warn":"The study text's own solution deducts N7,500,000 as capital allowances without this figure being given, or derivable from any data, in the question itself — no capital expenditure or agreed-capital-allowance figure appears anywhere in the question's facts. This is reproduced as the source's given answer, but should be treated as an assumed/externally-agreed figure rather than one a candidate could derive from the stated facts alone."},{"p":"**(b)** Three (of the conditions under s.163 NTA): the donation must be made to a fund/body/institution the section applies to (public funds, statutory bodies, religious/charitable/educational/scientific institutions in Nigeria, diplomatic-recognised bodies, or pandemic/disaster/emergency interventions); documentary evidence of the donation must be provided to the tax authority; and the total deduction claimed cannot exceed 10% of profit before tax for the year."}],"src":"Chapter 15.13","sec":"15.3"},{"q":"Mr Sulaimon's projected statement of profit or loss for the year ending 31 December 2026 shows: gross trading profit N30,000,000; dividend (gross) N2,500,000; rental income (gross) N800,000; profit on sale of PPE N1,200,000; total expenses N15,800,000; net profit N18,700,000. Salaries and wages (N3,500,000) include N50,000 wages to his housemaid. General expenses (N1,600,000) include N100,000 traffic fines paid for his wife and N300,000 for hiring a generator for his private residence. Finance costs (N900,000) include N200,000 interest on his private loan. He pays N350,000 annual life insurance premium and contributed N150,000 to NHIS. Capital allowance agreed is N5,500,000; WHT on dividend and rent is 10%. (a) Compute his adjusted profit. (b) Compute his tax liability.","marks":14,"a":[{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Mr Sulaimon — adjusted profit, 2027 assessment year","N"],"rows":[["Net profit","18,700,000"],["Add: wages to housemaid (private/domestic)","50,000"],["Add: traffic fines (penalty, always disallowed)","100,000"],["Add: generator hire for private residence","300,000"],["Add: interest on private loan","200,000"],["Add: depreciation","4,800,000"],["**Subtotal add-backs**","**5,450,000**"],["**Subtotal**","**24,150,000**"],["Less: dividend (gross) — final tax, excluded entirely","(2,500,000)"],["Less: profit on sale of PPE — capital, not trading income","(1,200,000)"],["**Adjusted profit**","**20,450,000**"]]}},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Tax liability","N"],"rows":[["Adjusted profit","20,450,000"],["Less: capital allowance","(5,500,000)"],["Subtotal","14,950,000"],["Less: life insurance premium","350,000"],["Less: NHIS contribution","150,000"],["**Chargeable income**","**14,450,000**"],["First N800,000 @ 0%","0"],["Next N2,200,000 @ 15%","330,000"],["Next N9,000,000 @ 18%","1,620,000"],["Next N2,450,000 @ 21%","514,500"],["Tax liability","2,464,500"],["Less: WHT deducted from rent (10% × N800,000)","(80,000)"],["**Net tax payable**","**2,384,500**"]]}},{"note":"Rental income (N800,000) stays in the taxable base — unlike dividend, rent WHT is only an advance payment (not final tax) for an individual, so the gross rent is taxed at the graduated rates, with the WHT already suffered credited against the final liability. Salary paid to Mrs Sulaimon herself, his 3 children, and his aged-parent maintenance are all correctly left out of the computation — she is a genuine full-time employee (an allowable salary cost), and the current NTA has no dependent-relief allowance."}],"src":"Chapter 15.13","sec":"15.8"}]}},{"n":16,"t":"Capital Allowances","brief":"A full return to the capital allowances regime already introduced in Chapter 15 — this chapter goes deeper into the mechanics: which expenditure is explicitly excluded from qualifying capital expenditure, the restrictions on claims, the straight-line computation method itself (with three fully worked multi-asset illustrations), the special small-company deferral rule, the \"in use\" extension rules, how an allowance interacts with a loss, and the transitional rules bridging the old and new capital allowances regimes.","outcomes":["Appreciate the meaning of capital allowances","Understand the categories of qualifying capital expenditure","Ascertain the conditions for granting capital allowances","Establish the basis period for capital allowances","Understand the various restrictions on capital allowances claims","Compute capital allowances on qualifying capital expenditure, including hire purchase transactions, and finance and operating leases"],"secs":[{"n":"16.2","t":"Definitions and types of qualifying capital expenditure — first paragraph of the First Schedule to the NTA","b":[{"p":"**Qualifying capital expenditure (QCE)** means expenditure incurred in a basis period for the **acquisition, refurbishment and improvement of the value of an asset**, which is:"},{"ol":["capital expenditure incurred on **plant and equipment**;","capital expenditure incurred on the **construction of buildings, structures or work of a permanent nature**, other than expenditure included in (i), (iii) or (iv);","**qualifying mast expenditure** — capital expenditure incurred on a tall upright pole erected on, or fastened to, land or another structure;","capital expenditure incurred **in connection with, or in preparation for, the working of a mine, oil well or other source of mineral deposits of a wasting nature** (other than expenditure included in (i) above): on the acquisition of deposits, or of rights in or over the deposits, or on the purchase of information relating to the existence and extent of the deposit; on searching for, or on discovering and testing, deposits, or winning access thereto; or on the construction of any work or building which is likely to be of little or no value when the source is no longer worked (or, where the source is worked under a concession, which is likely to become valueless when the concession comes to an end to the individual working the source immediately before it ends);","**qualifying agriculture expenditure** — capital expenditure incurred: in connection with a **plantation**, on the clearing of land for planting, planting (other than replanting), maintenance of the plantation and other pre-production expenses in connection with that plantation, which shall be deemed incurred **on the first day on which the trade or business commences**; and in respect of **ranching**, on structures used primarily for ranching, on raising herds of animals for the purpose of **breeding** (not for the purpose of sale), and on animals that function as **apparatus** with which a trade is carried on and whose life expectancy is **more than 5 years**;","**qualifying intangible assets expenditure** — capital expenditure incurred in respect of research and development of: equipment and facilities, patents, licences, secret formulas or processes; information concerning the development of industrial, commercial or scientific processes, technical feasibility, or products or processes; or the discovery and testing of products or processes for future market use, and such other similar costs which have brought into existence an asset;","**qualifying motor vehicle expenditure** — capital expenditure incurred on: a **fleet of buses of not less than three** used for public transportation; a public transportation **inter-city new mass transit coach of twenty-five seats and above** operated by a private establishment; or other motor vehicles;","**qualifying heavy transportation expenditure** — capital expenditure incurred on aircraft engines and fuselages, pipelines and cables, vessels, ships' engines, train engines, railway carriages, train tracks and coaches;","**qualifying software expenditure** — capital expenditure incurred on the acquisition of software or other such capital outlays on electronic applications; and","**qualifying furniture and fittings expenditure** — capital expenditure incurred on furniture and fittings, including those used in vessels, ships, aircraft, trains, plants and buildings for the purposes of trade or business."]},{"warn":"**Excluded from QCE** (¶14, First Schedule): expenditure already allowed as a deduction under s.20 (ordinary trading-profit ascertainment); and the **excess** amount incurred on foreign-currency-denominated assets, paid for with foreign currency obtained from the **unofficial** exchange market at a rate above the official market rate on the acquisition date."}]},{"n":"16.3","t":"Explanation of annual allowance","b":[{"h4":"16.3.1  Definition"},{"def":{"t":"Capital allowance","d":"relief granted to a company/individual incurring QCE for trade/business during a basis period, on property/plant/equipment in use at the basis period's end — granted in place of depreciation, which is disallowed for tax purposes."}},{"h4":"16.3.2  Annual allowance (AA)"},{"key":"Annual allowance (AA) is a tax relief granted **every year** to reduce chargeable income/total profit, computed on a **straight-line basis** — the worked illustrations below confirm this means a flat percentage of **original cost** each year, not a declining-balance/reducing-value method."}]},{"n":"16.4","t":"Conditions for granting capital allowances — section 27(2) of NTA","b":[{"p":"The capital allowance to be granted in accordance with the provisions of Part I of the First Schedule shall be the amount relating to the qualifying capital expenditure incurred **in generating the assessable profits**, provided that:"},{"ol":["in the case of **companies in the priority sector**, a **certificate of acceptance** issued by the Industrial Inspectorate Department shall be obtained in respect of the qualifying capital expenditure; and where **value added tax is due** under the Act but was **not charged** on an asset — or, in the case of an imported item, where the applicable **import duty or levy was not paid** — the relevant expenditure shall **not be eligible** as qualifying capital expenditure;","where the qualifying capital expenditure relates to an asset **only partly utilised** in generating the assessable profits, the capital allowance shall be **prorated**, and only the portion relating to the taxable income shall be allowed as a deduction;","the capital allowance computed shall **not be prorated** where the non-taxable income constitutes **less than 10%** of the total income of the company; and","the portion of capital allowance attributable to the **priority activities** of a company enjoying an economic development incentive under the Act shall be deducted **only from the assessable profits of the priority business**."]},{"p":"In addition, **paragraph 7 of the First Schedule** provides that a capital allowance in respect of qualifying expenditure incurred on an asset shall be made to a person for a year of assessment if, **at the end of a basis period**, the person **(i)** is the **owner** of the asset, and **(ii)** **used the asset** for the purposes of its trade or business. Under **paragraph 8(2)** the asset must also remain **in use** for that trade or business."},{"key":"Three of these conditions are the ones examiners return to. The **VAT and import-duty condition** is a compliance precondition, not a computation: expenditure on which VAT was due but not charged is disqualified **entirely**, however genuine the asset. The **10% de minimis** means a small amount of exempt income does not trigger apportionment at all. And the **ownership-and-use test at the end of the basis period** is why an asset bought and sold within one period attracts nothing."}]},{"n":"16.5","t":"Capital allowances rates and restrictions","b":[{"p":"The Table I rates (¶6, First Schedule) are unchanged from Chapter 15:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Class","Rate","Qualifying expenditure"],"rows":[["1","10%","Building; agricultural; mast; intangible assets; heavy transportation."],["2","20%","Plant; agricultural equipment; furniture and fittings; mining; other equipment."],["3","25%","Motor vehicle; software; other capital expenditure."]]}},{"h4":"16.5.2  Restrictions on capital allowances"},{"ol":["no allowance where there is no proof of compliance with s.27(2) — i.e. VAT/import duties were not paid, or not fully paid;","where QCE is partly used to generate taxable income, capital allowance may still be granted **in full** where non-taxable income is **below 10%** of total income (s.27(3));","where the owner partly uses the asset for trade/business, the allowance is **apportioned** as the tax authority deems fit (¶16, First Schedule); and","where the basis period is **under 12 months**, the amount is restricted to the number of months in that basis period (¶6(b), First Schedule)."]},{"note":"A numbering gap in the study text, flagged rather than silently corrected: this section's only numbered sub-section is **16.5.2** — there is **no 16.5.1** anywhere in the chapter. The rates table above is the unnumbered first part of §16.5, and the restrictions are its 16.5.2."}]},{"n":"16.6","t":"Basis period for capital allowances — paragraph 1 of the First Schedule to the Act","b":[{"p":"The basis period for an allowance is the basis period for assessable profit/income — with the same two interval rules already set out in Chapter 15 (an interval between two consecutive years' basis periods joins the **second**; an interval before a permanent-cessation year's basis period joins the **first**)."},{"eg":{"t":"Illustration 16.1 — multi-asset capital allowances, straight-line on cost","q":[{"p":"A company commenced business on 2 January 2026, making up accounts to 31 December each year. It paid VAT/customs duty where applicable. Projected PPE costs: Building N55,000,000 (1/2/2026); Furniture N1,500,000 (1/2/2026); Machinery N2,000,000; Jeep motor vehicle N30,000,000. Turnover for each relevant assessment year is projected to exceed N250 million. **Required**: determine the basis period and compute capital allowances for all relevant assessment years."}],"a":[{"warn":"The question as extracted states the jeep motor vehicle cost N30,000,000, but the study text's own printed solution computes annual allowance entirely on a jeep cost of **N50,000,000** (25% × N50,000,000 = N12,500,000, reconciling exactly with the source's own printed figures and closing WDV) — an unresolved inconsistency between the question and its own answer key. The computation below follows the **solution's** own N50,000,000 figure, since that is what its arithmetic is actually built on; a candidate working from the N30,000,000 figure as literally stated in the question would arrive at a different (internally consistent, but not source-matching) answer."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Year of assessment","Basis period (assessment and capital allowance)"],"rows":[["2027","2/1/26 – 31/3/26"],["2028","1/4/26 – 31/3/27"]]}},{"table":{"align":"lllll","head":["N'000","Building (10%)","Furniture (20%)","Machinery (20%)","Motor vehicle (25%)"],"rows":[["**AY2027** (2-month basis period)","","","",""],["Cost","55,000","1,500","",""],["Annual allowance (2/12 of full-year rate)","(917)","(50)","",""],["WDV c/f","54,083","1,450","",""],["**AY2028** (12-month basis period)","","","",""],["Cost","","","2,000","50,000"],["Annual allowance (full year)","(5,500)","(300)","(400)","(12,500)"],["WDV c/f","48,583","1,150","1,600","37,500"]]}},{"note":"AY2027 total annual allowance: 917 + 50 = **N967,000**. AY2028 total annual allowance: 5,500 + 300 + 400 + 12,500 = **N18,700,000**. Note the allowance is computed as a flat percentage of **original cost** each year (confirmed by the WDV reconciling to cost less CUMULATIVE allowances, not a declining balance) — e.g. the building's AY2028 allowance is 10% of the full N55,000,000 cost again, not 10% of the AY2027 closing WDV."},{"warn":"Three further inconsistencies between this illustration's question and the study text's own printed solution, flagged rather than silently corrected. **(1)** The question says the company \"makes up its accounts to **December 31**, every year\", but the solution computes basis periods of **1/2/26 – 31/3/26** and **1/4/26 – 31/3/27** — a **31 March** year end, which is the year end of Question 2 at §16.11 (Rabo Limited), not of this company. **(2)** The question dates the machinery **1/4/2027**, the solution **1/4/26**. **(3)** The question dates the jeep **3/5/2027**, the solution **3/5/26**. The solution's dates and periods are internally consistent with each other and with its arithmetic, so they are what the computation above follows; the question's own narrative is the part that is wrong. Work **Question 2 at §16.11** for a clean version of the same exercise — its question and solution agree throughout."},{"key":"Setting the defects aside, the two things this illustration is actually teaching are worth isolating. **First**, the first basis period is short (1 February to 31 March is **two months**), so the first annual allowance is **time-apportioned by 2/12** — N55,000,000 × 10% × 2/12 = N916,667, rounded to N917 thousand. **Second**, assets acquired **after** the first basis period attract **nothing** in the first year and a **full year's** allowance in the next, which is why the machinery and the jeep appear only in the 2028 column."}]}}]},{"n":"16.7","t":"Application of capital allowances to assets acquired under hire purchase or lease agreement","b":[{"h4":"16.7.1  Hire purchase and finance lease"},{"def":{"t":"Hire purchase (s.201 NTA)","d":"a financial arrangement in which a person acquires immediate use of an asset by making regular instalment payments over a specified period, and may gain ownership of the asset on the completion of the payments."}},{"def":{"t":"Finance lease (s.201 NTA)","d":"a lease arrangement where the lessee effectively assumes most of the risks and rewards associated with asset ownership."}},{"p":"**The rules — paragraph 2 of the First Schedule to the NTA.** In relation to any asset acquired by a hirer or lessee under a hire-purchase or finance lease agreement, the following shall apply:"},{"ol":["the **qualifying capital expenditure** in any basis period shall, in relation to any asset so acquired under that agreement, be **limited to the amount of the instalments paid** by the hirer or lessee up to that basis period, **excluding any interest** paid under the agreement;","the **\"owner\"** of the asset shall be construed to be the **hirer or lessee** under the hire-purchase or finance lease agreement; and","the person **letting** the asset to the hirer or lessee under the agreement shall **not be entitled to an allowance**."]},{"h4":"16.7.2  Capital allowances under an operating lease — paragraph 15 of the First Schedule"},{"def":{"t":"Operating lease (s.201 NTA)","d":"a lease other than a finance lease — the lessor retains the risks and rewards of ownership and the asset reverts to the lessor at the end of the lease term."}},{"ol":["Where in a basis period the **owner** of an asset leases the asset to another person under an **operating lease**, the provisions of this Part shall apply **as though such expenditure were incurred for the purpose of a trade or business carried on by the owner or lessor**.","Where an asset is acquired for use by a **hirer or lessee** under a hire purchase or finance lease contract for the purpose of a trade or business carried on by such hirer or lessee, the **hirer or lessee shall be deemed to be the owner** of the asset, and the provisions of this Part shall apply to the hirer or lessee **to the exclusion of the hiree or lessor** under the contract.","For the purposes of this paragraph in relation to the trade or business, the **basis period** for any year of assessment shall be taken to be the **year immediately preceding** that year of assessment."]},{"key":"One principle governs both: capital allowances follow **economic ownership, not legal title**. Under a **hire purchase or finance lease** the hirer/lessee bears the risks and rewards, so the **hirer/lessee** claims and the lessor claims nothing — but only on **instalments actually paid, net of interest**, because the interest is a revenue finance cost. Under an **operating lease** the lessor keeps the risks and rewards, so the **lessor** claims as if the asset were used in its own trade."}]},{"n":"16.8","t":"Computation of annual allowance","b":[{"h4":"16.8.1  Capital allowance — paragraph 6 of the First Schedule to the NTA"},{"p":"The annual allowance is computed by applying the Table I rate at §16.5 to the **cost** of the qualifying capital expenditure — a **straight-line** allowance, the same amount each year until the asset is written down, not a reducing-balance one. The basis period for the first allowance is determined under §16.6, and where that period is shorter than twelve months the allowance is **time-apportioned** accordingly, as Illustration 16.1 and Question 2 at §16.11 both show."},{"h4":"16.8.2  Computation of capital allowances for small companies — section 56 of NTA"},{"warn":"Despite being claimable on QCE generating taxable income, capital allowances are **not allowable or carried forward** for a **small company** — such a company can only claim capital allowances on QCE once it **crosses the N100 million threshold** and so qualifies as a large company."},{"h4":"16.8.3  Claims for allowance — paragraph 21 of the First Schedule to the NTA"},{"ol":["an allowance is made for a year **only if claimed** — unless the tax authority considers it just and reasonable to grant it anyway; and","an allowance not claimed in the year its basis period falls in is **carried forward** to subsequent period(s)."]}]},{"n":"16.9","t":"Other provisions relating to capital allowances","b":[{"h4":"16.9.1  Extension of application of \"in use\" — paragraph 13 of the First Schedule"},{"ol":["an asset is deemed **in use** during a period of temporary disuse;","an asset acquired for trade/business but not yet actually used is deemed in use from the **expenditure date** — provided that, if the asset's first actual use is NOT for that trade/business, additional assessments counter-act the benefit the earlier allowance gave; and","the tax authority's **approval** is required for the above deeming."]},{"h4":"16.9.2  Manner of making allowance — paragraph 22 of the First Schedule to the Act"},{"ol":["an allowance is **subtracted from the remainder** of assessable profits/income for the relevant year;","that \"remainder\" is determined giving full effect to s.27 (companies) or s.28 (individuals) as it relates to loss deduction; and","where the deduction cannot be **fully** made for a year (no or insufficient remainder), the unmade part is treated as a deduction for the **next year**, continuing until fully utilised."]},{"h4":"16.9.3  Transitional rules for capital allowances — paragraph 23 of the First Schedule"},{"ol":["for an asset already granted capital allowance before this Act's commencement, the remaining basis periods for allowance equal the total years allowable under this Act, less years already allowed;","where allowance was already granted, before commencement, for years **equal to or greater than** the number allowable under this Act, a **single** allowance is granted for the residue on commencement — subject to the same 1% notional statistical retention until disposal; and","where QCE capital allowance was **fully** granted before this Act took effect, no further allowance is granted on the amount required to be retained in the books under the repealed enactment."]}]},{"n":"16.10","t":"Chapter review","b":[{"p":"This chapter addressed the incidence of capital allowances — what they represent and the categories of assets classifiable as qualifying capital expenditure. It also discussed the conditions for granting capital allowances and their computation."}]},{"n":"16.11","t":"Worked examples — study text questions and suggested solutions","b":[{"p":"The study text sets **three questions** at §16.11.1 — one theory question and two computations — with its own suggested solutions at §16.11.2. All three are reproduced here in full, with the book's answers, every allowance independently recomputed, and added explanation."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 16.11.1, Question 1","t":"Malam Shehu — hire purchase against finance lease, and the rules for QCE","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Malam Shehu is the Managing Director of Ark Limited and he has been in the business of **Car Hire Service** for many years. He has the desire to change some of the vehicles in the fleet **without negative effect on the cashflow** of the business in the new budget year."},{"p":"As the Lead Consultant of KEB Consulting, he sought your professional advice on the claims for capital allowances if the vehicles are acquired through a **hire purchase or finance lease arrangement**."},{"p":"**Required:** **(a)** Differentiate between a hire purchase and a finance lease arrangement in accordance with the provisions of the NTA, 2025. **(b)** Discuss the rules to be applied under a hire purchase or finance lease agreement in respect of a qualifying capital expenditure."}],"a":[{"h4":"1(a)(i)  Definition of hire purchase — section 201 of NTA"},{"p":"\"**Hire purchase**\" is a financial arrangement in which a person **acquires immediate use of an asset** by making **regular instalment payments over a specified period**, and **may gain ownership** of the asset on the completion of the payments."},{"h4":"1(a)(ii)  Definition of finance lease — section 201 of NTA"},{"p":"\"**Finance lease**\" is a lease arrangement where the **lessee effectively assumes most of the risks and rewards** associated with asset ownership."},{"key":"The difference the question asks you to draw is about **title**, not about risk. Both arrangements give the user immediate use and both put the **risks and rewards** on the user — which is why both are treated the same way for capital allowances. The distinction is that hire purchase **may transfer legal ownership on completion of the payments**, whereas a finance lease ordinarily does not: the lessee gets the economics of ownership without ever getting the title."},{"h4":"1(b)  The rules to be applied — paragraph 2 of the First Schedule to the NTA"},{"p":"In relation to any asset acquired by a hirer or lessee under a hire-purchase or finance lease agreement, the following shall apply:"},{"ol":["the **qualifying capital expenditure** in any basis period shall, in relation to any asset so acquired under that agreement, be **limited to the amount of the instalments paid** by the hirer or lessee up to that basis period, **excluding any interest** paid under the agreement;","the **\"owner\"** of the asset shall be construed to be the **hirer or lessee** under the hire-purchase or finance lease agreement; and","the person **letting** the asset to the hirer or lessee under the agreement shall **not be entitled to an allowance**."]},{"key":"Answer Malam Shehu's actual commercial question, not just the law — the marks for application are usually there. Because QCE is **limited to instalments paid**, the capital allowances arrive **as the cash goes out** rather than all at once on acquisition. So acquiring the fleet on hire purchase or finance lease gives Ark Limited exactly what he asked for: the **use of the vehicles immediately**, the **cash outflow spread** across the term, and **capital allowances that track the payments**. An outright purchase would bring the whole allowance forward but take the whole price out of cashflow at once."},{"note":"Two points worth adding. The **interest element is excluded from QCE** — but it is not lost: it is a revenue **finance cost**, deductible in computing the profit under §15.4.2, so the whole of each instalment is relieved, just through two different routes. And because the **hirer/lessee is the deemed owner**, the financier claims nothing — an important point when negotiating, since the financier cannot pass on an allowance it never had."},{"warn":"Do not confuse this with an **operating lease** (§16.7.2), where the position reverses entirely: the **lessor** claims the allowances as if the asset were used in its own trade, and the lessee claims none but deducts the lease rentals as a revenue expense. If Malam Shehu were offered an operating lease instead, his company would have **no capital allowance claim on the vehicles at all**."}]}},{"eg":{"t":"Rabo Limited — basis periods and capital allowances on commencement","q":[{"p":"Rabo Limited commenced business on 1 February 2026, making up accounts to 31 March each year, with turnover projected to exceed N250 million in each assessment year. VAT/customs duty will be paid where necessary. PPE: Building N56,000,000 (1/2/26); Furniture N1,500,000 (1/2/26); Machinery N8,000,000 (1/4/26); Motor vehicle N23,000,000 (31/5/26). **Required**: determine the basis period and compute capital allowances for all relevant assessment years."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Year of assessment","Basis period"],"rows":[["2027","1/2/26 – 31/3/26"],["2028","1/4/26 – 31/3/27"]]}},{"table":{"align":"lllll","head":["N'000","Building (10%)","Furniture (20%)","Machinery (20%)","Motor vehicle (25%)"],"rows":[["**AY2027** (2-month basis period)","","","",""],["Cost","56,000","1,500","",""],["Annual allowance (2/12 of full-year rate)","(933)","(50)","",""],["WDV c/f","55,067","1,450","",""],["**AY2028** (12-month basis period)","","","",""],["Cost","","","8,000","23,000"],["Annual allowance (full year)","(5,600)","(300)","(1,600)","(5,750)"],["WDV c/f","49,467","1,150","6,400","17,250"]]}},{"note":"AY2027 total: 933 + 50 = **N983,000**. AY2028 total: 5,600 + 300 + 1,600 + 5,750 = **N13,250,000**. Every figure here reconciles exactly against the question's own stated costs — unlike Illustration 16.1, no discrepancy arises in this example."},{"h4":"How each allowance follows"},{"ol":["**Basis periods.** Rabo commenced on 1 February 2026 with a 31 March year end, so its first accounting period is **1/2/26 – 31/3/26**, assessed in **2027** on the preceding-year basis; its second is **1/4/26 – 31/3/27**, assessed in **2028**. The basis period for capital allowances is the same as the basis period for assessable profit (§16.6).","**AY2027** — only the building and the furniture had been acquired by 31 March 2026, and the period is **two months**, so each allowance is apportioned by 2/12: building N56,000,000 × 10% × 2/12 = **N933,333** (N933 thousand); furniture N1,500,000 × 20% × 2/12 = **N50,000**. Total **N983,000**.","**AY2028** — a **full twelve months**, and the machinery (1/4/26) and motor vehicle (31/5/26) both fall inside this basis period, so all four assets attract a full annual allowance: building **N5,600,000**; furniture **N300,000**; machinery N8,000,000 × 20% = **N1,600,000**; motor vehicle N23,000,000 × 25% = **N5,750,000**. Total **N13,250,000**.","**Closing written-down values** carried to AY2029: building N49,467,000; furniture N1,150,000; machinery N6,400,000; motor vehicle N17,250,000."]},{"key":"Two details decide this question. The **two-month apportionment** of the first allowance, and the fact that the machinery and vehicle — bought on 1 April and 31 May 2026, *after* the first basis period closed on 31 March — get **no allowance at all in AY2027**. Candidates who date the assets by calendar year rather than by basis period give the machinery and vehicle an allowance a year early."},{"note":"The turnover \"projected to exceed **N250 million** in each assessment year\" is not idle detail: it puts Rabo above the **N100 million** small-company threshold, so it is liable to companies income tax and can **actually use** its capital allowances. Contrast Question 3, where the company is below the threshold and the allowances are deemed utilised and lost."},{"warn":"A date slip in the book's printed solution, flagged rather than silently corrected: the motor vehicle row of the allowances table is labelled **\"3/5/26 – Jeep\"**, but the question dates the motor vehicle **31/5/26** and never calls it a jeep — \"Jeep\" is carried over from Illustration 16.1 at §16.6. Neither slip changes the allowance, since both 3 May and 31 May 2026 fall inside the 1/4/26 – 31/3/27 basis period."}],"tag":"Study text — 16.11.1, Question 2","open":true}},{"eg":{"t":"RAO Limited — the small-company deferral and the 1% retention rule","q":[{"p":"RAO Limited, long established, proposes N2,000,000 QCE on furniture and fittings (20% rate) in the 2027 assessment year, when projected revenue is N74,000,000. It expects revenue of N770,000,000 and an asset base of N300 million by the 2030 assessment year. VAT/customs-duty payment records are available. **Required**: compute the capital allowances claimable for all relevant assessment years."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"llll","head":["Assessment year","Annual allowance (N)","Cumulative (N)","Remarks"],"rows":[["2027","400,000","400,000","Small company (revenue N74m < N100m)"],["2028","400,000","800,000","Still a small company"],["2029","400,000","1,200,000","Not utilised — deemed forfeited, per s.56, since small-company allowances cannot be carried forward"],["2030","400,000 claimable","WDV c/f 400,000","Now a large company (revenue crossed N100m) — allowance now actually claimable"],["2031","380,000 claimable","N20,000 retained","Final year — reduced by the 1% notional retention (1% × N2,000,000 = N20,000), never claimed until disposal"]]}},{"note":"Since the company did not cross the N100,000,000 revenue threshold until the 2030 assessment year, it was a **small company** throughout 2027-2029 — the N1,200,000 annual allowance accruing across those 3 years is **deemed utilised** (i.e. permanently forfeited, per s.56's no-carry-forward rule), not banked for later use. Only once RAO Limited becomes a large company (2030 onward) can it actually claim allowance — N400,000 in 2030, and N380,000 in 2031 (N400,000 less the N20,000 permanent 1% retention that applies to every asset until disposal, per ¶6(a)/¶23(b) of the First Schedule). Across the asset's full 5-year (100%÷20%) life, N1,980,000 is ultimately claimable and N20,000 permanently retained — reconciling exactly to the N2,000,000 original cost."},{"h4":"The book's own note on this answer"},{"p":"Given the fact that the company **did not achieve the N100,000,000 revenue until the 2030 assessment year**, it will be regarded as a **small company**, and the total annual allowance of **N1,200,000** will be **deemed utilised** in assessment years 2027, 2028 and 2029. The capital allowances claimable in AY2030 are **N400,000**, whilst those of AY2031 are **N380,000** — that is, N400,000 less a retention of **N20,000**."},{"h4":"How the figures follow"},{"ol":["The annual allowance is **20% of cost**, straight line: 20% × N2,000,000 = **N400,000** a year.","In **AY2027, 2028 and 2029** the company's revenue is below **N100,000,000**, so it is a **small company**, exempt from companies income tax. It has no tax liability against which to set an allowance, and the allowances of those three years — 3 × N400,000 = **N1,200,000** — are therefore **deemed utilised**. They are *not* carried forward.","That leaves a written-down value of N2,000,000 − N1,200,000 = **N800,000** to carry into AY2030.","By **AY2030** revenue is N770,000,000 and the asset base N300,000,000, so the company is no longer small and can claim. It claims a full annual allowance of **N400,000**, leaving a WDV of **N400,000**.","In **AY2031** the remaining N400,000 cannot all be claimed, because **1% of cost must be retained in the books** so long as the asset remains in use: 1% × N2,000,000 = **N20,000**. The claim is therefore N400,000 − N20,000 = **N380,000**, and **N20,000 stays in the books**."]},{"key":"The sting in this question is the word **\"utilised\"**. A small company's capital allowances are **deemed utilised, not deferred** — the N1,200,000 is gone for good, not banked for the year the company becomes taxable. That is why the AY2030 computation starts from a written-down value of N800,000 rather than from the full N2,000,000. Treating the allowances as carried forward would overstate the claim by N1,200,000, and it is the single most common error on this topic."},{"note":"The **1% retention rule** exists so that a fully written-down asset still appears in the tax records while it remains in use, which is what makes the \"ownership-and-use at the end of the basis period\" test of §16.4 verifiable. Note that the 1% is 1% **of original cost**, not of the written-down value — N20,000 here, whatever the stage of the computation."},{"note":"The **N300 million asset base** mentioned for AY2030 is additional confirmation rather than a second test: under section 56 of the NTA the small-company definition turns on turnover of **N100 million or less** together with total fixed assets not exceeding **N250 million**. RAO breaches both by AY2030, so it is plainly outside the exemption by then."}],"tag":"Study text — 16.11.1, Question 3","open":true}}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Annual allowance (full basis period)","tex":"\\text{AA} = \\text{Original cost} \\times \\text{Class rate}"},{"lb":"Annual allowance (basis period under 12 months)","tex":"\\text{AA} = \\text{Original cost} \\times \\text{Class rate} \\times \\dfrac{\\text{Basis period (months)}}{12}"},{"lb":"Written-down value carried forward","tex":"\\text{WDV c/f} = \\text{Original cost} - \\text{Cumulative annual allowance to date}"},{"lb":"1% notional retention (never claimable until disposal)","tex":"\\text{Retention} = 1% \\times \\text{Qualifying capital expenditure}"}],"focus":"The two mechanics genuinely new in this chapter (beyond Chapter 15's foundation) are worth isolating: the SMALL-COMPANY deferral (s.56 — allowance accrues but is deemed forfeited, never carried forward, for every year the company stays under the N100m revenue threshold) and the 1% NOTIONAL RETENTION (a permanent, non-claimable sliver of every asset's cost that is never actually deducted, tracked purely for statistical purposes until disposal — reducing the FINAL year's claimable allowance specifically). Both interact in the RAO Limited illustration and are easy to conflate; keep them as two separate, independently-operating rules. Also fix firmly that annual allowance is computed as a flat percentage of ORIGINAL COST every year (straight-line), never as a percentage of the declining written-down value — a common instinct from other accounting contexts that does not apply here.","errors":["Computing each year's annual allowance as a percentage of the PRIOR year's written-down value (reducing-balance style) — capital allowance under the NTA is explicitly straight-line, computed as a flat percentage of ORIGINAL cost every year the asset remains in use.","Treating a small company's unclaimed annual allowance as simply carried forward to a later year once it becomes large — s.56 is explicit that small-company allowances are NOT carried forward at all; they are permanently forfeited for the years the company stayed small, and only allowance accruing AFTER crossing the threshold becomes claimable.","Forgetting the 1% notional retention exists at all — it never changes any single year's allowance except effectively the LAST one (since it caps total lifetime claims at 99% of cost), so it is easy to omit until an asset's allowance schedule is followed all the way to its final year.","Assuming an asset acquired partway through a full 12-month basis period still gets its annual allowance prorated by its own holding period within that year — proration under ¶6(b) is driven by the LENGTH OF THE BASIS PERIOD itself being under 12 months, not by how many months within a full-length basis period the specific asset was actually held.","Letting a lessor claim capital allowance on an asset let out under a hire-purchase or finance lease — ownership for allowance purposes is always construed as the hirer/lessee, exactly as already established in Chapter 15, and this chapter changes nothing about that rule."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Which of the following is explicitly EXCLUDED from qualifying capital expenditure, under ¶14 of the First Schedule to the NTA?","o":["Expenditure on plant and machinery","Expenditure already allowed as a deduction under s.20 of the Act","Expenditure on furniture and fittings","Expenditure on motor vehicles used for trade","Expenditure on software acquisition"],"a":1,"w":"Paragraph 14 of the First Schedule excludes from QCE any expenditure already allowed as a deduction under s.20 (the ordinary trading-profit deduction provision) — to prevent the same cost being relieved twice.","src":"Chapter 16.2","sec":"16.2"},{"q":"A small company (revenue below N100 million) accrues N1,200,000 of annual allowance over 3 years before crossing the threshold. What happens to this accrued amount, under s.56 of the NTA?","o":["It is fully claimable once the company becomes large","It is carried forward indefinitely, like an ordinary unclaimed allowance","It is deemed utilised — permanently forfeited, not carried forward, since small-company allowances cannot be carried forward","It is refunded to the company in cash","It is doubled as a penalty for having been a small company"],"a":2,"w":"Section 56 NTA is explicit that capital allowances are not allowable or carried forward for a small company — the accrued amount for the small-company years is deemed utilised (forfeited), and only allowance accruing after the company becomes large is actually claimable.","src":"Chapter 16.7","sec":"16.7"},{"q":"What is the purpose of the 1% notional retention recorded against every asset's qualifying capital expenditure?","o":["It increases the total capital allowance claimable by 1%","It is a statistical figure only, permanently un-claimable until disposal, reducing lifetime claims to 99% of cost","It is an additional annual allowance on top of the normal class rate","It only applies to small companies","It is refundable in the asset's final year"],"a":1,"w":"The 1% notional retention is recorded for statistical purposes only and is never claimable until disposal — it permanently caps total lifetime allowance claims at 99% of the qualifying capital expenditure.","src":"Chapter 16.7","sec":"16.7"},{"q":"How is annual allowance computed each year an asset remains in qualifying use?","o":["As a percentage of the prior year's written-down value (reducing balance)","As a flat percentage of the ORIGINAL cost, every year (straight-line)","As the full cost claimed entirely in the first year","As a percentage of market value at each year-end","As a fixed Naira amount regardless of asset cost"],"a":1,"w":"Capital allowance under the NTA is explicitly computed on a straight-line basis — a flat percentage of the original cost every year, confirmed by how the worked illustrations reconcile written-down value to cost less cumulative (not compounding) allowances.","src":"Chapter 16.2","sec":"16.2"},{"q":"Under ¶21 of the First Schedule, what happens to an allowance not claimed in the year its basis period falls in?","o":["It is permanently lost","It is carried forward to subsequent period(s)","It is automatically claimed by the tax authority on the taxpayer's behalf","It reduces next year's allowance instead","It can only be claimed retroactively with a penalty"],"a":1,"w":"Paragraph 21 of the First Schedule provides that an allowance not claimed in its proper year is carried forward to subsequent period(s), rather than being lost.","src":"Chapter 16.7","sec":"16.7"}],"theory":[{"q":"Malam Shehu, Managing Director of Ark Limited (a car hire business), wants to change some fleet vehicles without a negative cashflow effect, and is considering hire purchase or finance lease arrangements. As lead consultant, (a) differentiate between hire purchase and finance lease under the NTA 2025, and (b) discuss the rules applied under a hire purchase or finance lease agreement in respect of qualifying capital expenditure.","marks":6,"a":[{"p":"**(a)** Hire purchase (s.201 NTA): a financial arrangement where a person acquires immediate use of an asset via regular instalment payments over a specified period, possibly gaining ownership on completion. Finance lease (s.201 NTA): a lease arrangement where the lessee effectively assumes most of the risks and rewards of asset ownership."},{"p":"**(b)** Rules applied (¶2, First Schedule): the QCE in any basis period is limited to the instalments paid up to that period, excluding interest; the \"owner\" is construed to be the hirer/lessee; and the person letting the asset (the owner/lessor) is not entitled to any allowance."}],"src":"Chapter 16.11","sec":"16.6"},{"q":"Explain the restrictions on capital allowances claims, and the transitional rules that apply to assets already granted capital allowance before the NTA 2025 came into effect.","marks":8,"a":[{"p":"**Restrictions**: no allowance without proof of s.27(2) compliance (VAT/import duties paid); where QCE is partly used for taxable income, full allowance is still granted if non-taxable income is below 10% of total income (otherwise prorated); where an owner partly uses an asset for trade/business, the allowance is apportioned as the tax authority deems fit; and where the basis period is under 12 months, the allowance is restricted to the number of months in that period."},{"p":"**Transitional rules** (¶23, First Schedule): for an asset already granted allowance before commencement, the remaining basis periods equal the total years allowable under the new Act less years already allowed; where allowance was already granted for years equal to or exceeding the new Act's allowable years, a single allowance is granted for the residue on commencement (subject to the usual 1% notional retention); and where QCE allowance was already FULLY granted before commencement, no further allowance is granted on the amount required to be retained in the books under the repealed enactment."}],"src":"Chapter 16.4","sec":"16.8"}]}},{"n":17,"t":"Taxation in Partnerships","brief":"How an unincorporated partnership's income is computed and then split into each partner's own personal tax liability: what makes a partnership agreement enforceable and registrable, the residency rules for active/dormant partners, the partnership-specific deductions unavailable to sole traders (partner salaries and interest on capital), how admission/retirement mid-year is treated, capital allowance apportionment by profit-sharing ratio, and — closing the chapter — the separate rules for companies that partner with each other.","outcomes":["Understand the meaning of a partnership and the contents of a partnership agreement","Explain the need for registering the partnership deed with the relevant tax authority","Explain the continuity of trades as it relates to a partnership business","Determine a partnership's residence and the relevant tax authority for a year of assessment","Explain allowable and non-allowable expenses specific to a partnership","Explain the tax treatments on a change in a partnership business","Ascertain the income of a partnership and each partner's assessable income","Explain the treatment of capital allowances under a partnership","Ascertain the loss relief available to a partner when a partnership incurs a loss","Compute the tax liability of individual partners in a partnership","Discuss the taxation of profits of companies in partnership"],"secs":[{"n":"17.2","t":"Definition of partnership","b":[{"def":{"t":"Partnership (Partnership Act 1890)","d":"a relationship that exists/subsists between or among persons carrying on business in common with a view of profit — an association of ≥2 persons coming together to make profit."}},{"key":"A partnership has a **minimum of 2 and a maximum of 20** members. Section 19(2)(b) CAMA allows a partnership of **more than 20** persons in Nigeria, provided it is for the simultaneous practice of Legal Practitioners, Chartered Accountants, etc., by persons each entitled to practise in that profession."}]},{"n":"17.3","t":"Partnership agreement and registration of the certified true copy with the relevant authority — section 15(7) of NTA","b":[{"p":"A partnership agreement/deed sets out the partners' rights, duties and interests. It typically covers:"},{"ol":["the amount each partner contributes as capital;","the rate of interest (if any) payable on capital;","salary (if any) payable to a partner;","the rate of interest payable on a partner's loan to the partnership;","the profit/loss sharing ratio; and","the rate of interest on partners' drawings."]},{"note":"Absent a fixed agreement, or where the agreement is not in writing, the Partnership Act 1890 default terms govern: each partner contributes the **same** capital; **no** interest on capital; **no** salary; profits/losses shared **equally**; and a partner who advances the partnership is entitled to **5% per annum** interest on that advance."},{"key":"The partnership (or the employee/agent in charge of its principal office) must **register** a certified copy of the partnership deed (or particulars of any oral/written agreement, if no deed exists) with the relevant tax authority — without needing notice or demand. Any **subsequent change** must be similarly registered **within 30 days** of the change agreement."},{"warn":"Where particulars are registered, the tax authority may compute a partner's gains/profits on that basis. Where the partnership **fails to comply** with a demand under this provision, the tax authority may assess/charge tax **as though the whole gains/profits** accrued to, or were divisible between, any partner, as it deems **just and reasonable**."}]},{"n":"17.4","t":"Continuity of trades as it relates to a partnership business — section 25 of NTA","b":[{"key":"An individual carrying on a trade, business, profession or vocation **shall not be treated as having commenced or ceased** to do so solely by reason of **a change in the territory in which the individual is resident** from one year to another, or by reason of **becoming or ceasing to be a partner** in a partnership — **if the nature of the trade** carried on by that partnership **is the same** as that carried on before or after the individual became or ceased to be a partner (s.25 NTA)."},{"note":"This is what stops the commencement and cessation rules of §15.8 from being triggered every time the membership of a firm changes. Without it, a partner joining or leaving would restart the basis-period clock for everyone. Note the condition, though: the trade must be **the same** before and after. Note too that it protects the **individual**, not the firm — for **capital allowances** the admission or retirement of a partner *is* treated as a cessation and recommencement under §17.7(b)."}]},{"n":"17.5","t":"Relevant tax authority / principal place of residence of a partner in a partnership business — paragraph 4 of the First Schedule to the NTAA and section 15(6) of NTA","b":[{"p":"A partner's \"principal place of residence\" in a Nigerian partnership is:"},{"ol":["for an **active** partner performing partnership duties — the territory of the office where they perform those duties;","for a **dormant** partner — the territory in Nigeria where they usually reside; and","for a **dormant** partner not resident in any Nigerian territory — deemed a **non-resident person**."]}]},{"n":"17.6","t":"Allowable and non-allowable expenses","b":[{"p":"The study text states, in full: *\"Allowable and non-allowable expenses to a partnership business are the same as those dealt with under chapter 17. Please refer to chapter 17 for allowable and disallowable expenses.\"*"},{"warn":"A cross-reference defect in the study text, flagged rather than silently corrected: **this is chapter 17**, so the book directs the reader from chapter 17 back to chapter 17. The allowable and non-allowable expenses are set out in full at **chapter 15, §15.4.2** — section 20 of the NTA (deductions allowed), section 163 (deductible donations), section 164 (research and development) and section 21 (deductions not allowed). That is plainly the chapter intended."},{"key":"The **partnership-specific** departures from those general rules are the examinable part, and they are at §17.8 below: **partners' salaries, interest on capital payable to partners, and the cost of passages to and from Nigeria** for leave or recreation **are allowable** in computing a partnership's income — none of which is allowable to a sole trader in respect of himself. A partner's **private or domestic** expenses remain disallowed."},{"note":"A partner's genuinely **private/domestic** expenses remain **not allowable** in ascertaining the partnership's computed income."}]},{"n":"17.7","t":"Tax treatments under change in a partnership business — paragraph 19 of the First Schedule to the NTA","b":[{"ol":["Where a trade/business is carried on in partnership, it is treated as a **single enterprise** — any allowance applying to individual partners is computed as though the single enterprise conducted all the trade/business activity the individual partners performed during the period; and","where a partner **joins or leaves** during a basis period, the business is treated **as if it ceased** at that point and **recommenced immediately after** — with the partnership deemed to have bought the transferred assets, immediately after the (deemed) disposal, at a price equal to the **residue of qualifying expenditure** at the disposal date."]}]},{"n":"17.8","t":"Computation of the income of a partnership business","b":[{"p":"Partnership income computation mirrors a sole trader's (Chapter 15) — with the three exceptions already noted in §17.5 (salaries, interest on capital, and leave/recreation passage costs are allowable)."},{"p":"The general allowable (s.20)/disallowed (s.21) deductions from **Chapter 13** apply — but a partnership has **three exceptions** unavailable to a sole trader:"},{"key":"**Salaries, interest on capital payable to partners, and the cost of passages to/from Nigeria** (wholly/mainly for leave or recreation) **are allowable expenses** in computing partnership income — unlike for individuals/sole traders, where such items would normally be disallowed as private/personal in nature."}]},{"n":"17.9","t":"Treatment of capital allowances under a partnership — paragraphs 19 to 22 of the First Schedule to the NTA","b":[{"key":"Capital allowances on partnership assets are **apportioned among the partners in their profit-sharing ratio**, at the end of the basis period."},{"ol":["where capital allowance must be **recomputed** because of the admission/resignation rules (§17.6), any resulting additional assessment/repayment is made to give effect to that;","where this paragraph conflicts with any other, it applies with whatever modifications the tax authority considers necessary, via prescribed rules;","a reference to \"an allowance made\" includes one that **would have been made** but for insufficient assessable profit/income to make it against;","an allowance is made only if **claimed** (unless the tax authority considers it just/reasonable regardless) — an unclaimed allowance is **carried forward**; and","an allowance is subtracted from the **remainder** of assessable profits/income for the year (after full effect to loss-deduction rules) — where it cannot be **fully** made for want of sufficient remainder, the unmade part carries forward to the next (and succeeding) year(s) until fully utilised."]}]},{"n":"17.10","t":"Taxable income of partners — section 15 of NTA","b":[{"key":"A partner's gains/profits from the partnership are the **sum** of:"},{"ol":["any remuneration, interest on capital, leave/recreation passage costs, or other perquisite/benefit-in-kind charged to the partnership accounts in respect of that partner; and","the partner's **share of partnership profits** — computed after deducting the (a)-type charges for **all** partners, but **before** deducting any other partnership expense referable to a partner that would itself be disallowed private/domestic expenditure if the partner incurred it directly."]}]},{"n":"17.11","t":"Partnership profits and losses — section 15(2)–(5) of NTA","b":[{"ol":["where the partnership computation results in a **loss**, the partner's share of that loss is deducted from their own ascertained gains/profits — they are deemed to have incurred a trade loss to the extent (if any) the deductible loss share exceeds those gains/profits;","a partner's profit/loss share follows the **partnership agreement's** specified proportion (as if the whole profit/loss were distributed among partners) — absent an agreement, shared **equally**; any person (resident or not) directly/indirectly sharing in a Nigerian partnership's profits must **file returns in their own name** and pay the applicable tax;","a partner's ascertained gains/profits/losses for a period are deemed their own trade/business/profession/vocation income/loss for that period;","the relevant tax authority for the **partnership** determines the profit/loss attributable to each partner — where a partner is taxable elsewhere, that authority shares the determination particulars with the other tax authority; and","a partner's Nigerian partnership income is attributed to the relevant Nigerian territory per the Twelfth Schedule — the active partner's duty-performance office territory, a dormant partner's residence, or the NRS for a non-resident partner."]}]},{"n":"17.12","t":"Computation of income tax payable by partners","b":[{"p":"The key steps:"},{"steps":["Ascertain the computed income of the partnership (net profit, add back disallowed expenses, deduct non-taxable income).","Note: capital allowance is NOT deducted at this stage — per ¶19(3), it is instead apportioned among partners in their profit-sharing ratio at the end of the basis period.","Allocate each partner's profit share (by the profit-sharing ratio), and add their own salaries/interest-on-capital/other income (per §17.9) plus income from other sources, to arrive at taxable income.","From taxable income, deduct capital allowance, share of loss (if any), and other total deductions, to arrive at total income.","From total income, deduct eligible deductions, to arrive at chargeable income.","Apply the graduated personal income tax rates (0% to 25%) to chargeable income, to arrive at the personal income tax payable by that partner."]},{"warn":"A cross-reference defect in the study text, flagged rather than silently corrected: step (c) of the book's own list directs the reader to add \"partners' income as specified in **(18.10)** above\". There is no §18.10 in this chapter, and chapter 18 is Settlements, Trusts and Estates. The reference is to **§17.10** (taxable income of partners)."}]},{"n":"17.13","t":"Taxation of profits of companies in partnership — section 11 of NTA","b":[{"ol":["where ≥2 companies carry on trade/business in a partnership, joint venture or similar arrangement in Nigeria, the resulting income/profit is a source of profits, and **each company's share is taxed separately**;","where any partnering company is **non-resident**, its share of the partnership income/profit is chargeable to tax under the Act;","for a partnership carried on **outside Nigeria**, the Nigerian-taxable partner includes its share of revenue/cost/profit in its own assessable profits, and must supply the tax authority particulars of that determination (with adjustments); and","this section does **not** apply to a partnership engaged in **petroleum operations**."]}]},{"n":"17.14","t":"Chapter review","b":[{"p":"This chapter addressed the ascertainment of a partnership's computed income and the taxable income of its partners. It also highlighted the importance of a partnership agreement, and the requirement to register that agreement with the relevant tax authority."}]},{"n":"17.15","t":"Worked examples — study text questions and suggested solutions","b":[{"p":"The study text sets **three questions** at §17.15.1 — all three full partnership computations — with its own suggested solutions at §17.15.2. All are reproduced here in full, with the book's answers, every share and allowance independently recomputed and cross-footed, and added explanation."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 17.15.1, Question 1","t":"Bola, Bety and Seun — computed income, partners' chargeable income and tax, with a source error corrected","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Bola, Bety and Seun have practised medicine in partnership for 7 years. Projected results for the year ending 31 December 2026:"},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["","N"],"rows":[["Gross profit","40,200,000"],["Less: Salaries and wages","(13,550,000)"],["Transport and travelling","(1,600,000)"],["Insurance","(350,000)"],["Rent and rates","(800,000)"],["Office expenses","(1,400,000)"],["Professional fees","(3,000,000)"],["Allowance for doubtful debts","(650,000)"],["Staff loan written off","(270,000)"],["Depreciation","(7,800,000)"],["Interest on loan by Erewa","(455,000)"],["Interest on capital accounts (Bola 225,000; Bety 315,000; Seun 360,000)","(900,000)"],["**Net profit for the year**","**9,425,000**"]]}},{"p":"Additional information: (i) office expenses include an N800,000 donation to an old people's home; (ii) capital allowance agreed with the Revenue is N9,500,000; (iii) only Bola is married, with 2 children below 16; (iv) profit-sharing ratio is Bola 5 : Bety 7 : Seun 8; (v) salaries drawn: Bola N3,300,000, Bety N4,700,000, Seun N5,550,000. **Required**: ascertain (a) the computed income of the partnership, (b) each partner's chargeable income, and (c) the tax payable by each partner."}],"a":[{"warn":"The study text's own printed solution **mis-states Seun's share of the divisible profit**. It gives Seun **N7,938,000**, but Seun's ratio is 8 of 20, and 8/20 × N18,945,000 = **N7,578,000**. The book's three shares add to N4,736,250 + N6,630,750 + N7,938,000 = **N19,305,000**, which is **N360,000 more than the N18,945,000 divisible profit it is supposed to be dividing** — so the error is self-evident from the book's own figures (the N360,000 is, by coincidence or by transcription slip, exactly Seun's interest on capital). The error then carries through the rest of the book's answer: Seun's total income becomes N13,848,000 instead of N13,488,000, his chargeable income N10,048,000 instead of N9,688,000, and his tax N1,598,640 instead of **N1,533,840**. The computation below uses the correct figures; Bola's and Bety's columns are unaffected and agree with the book throughout."},{"h4":"(a)  Computation of the adjusted (computed) income of the partnership, 2027 year of assessment"},{"stmt":{"t":"Bola, Bety and Seun — computed income of the partnership","rows":[["Net profit for the year","","9,425,000"],"Add: disallowable expenses",["Allowance for doubtful debts","650,000","","@ind"],["Office expenses (donation)","800,000","","@ind"],["Depreciation","7,800,000","","@ind"],["Staff loans written off","270,000","9,520,000","@ind"],["Divisible profit (computed income)","","18,945,000","@tt"]]}},{"note":"Note what is **not** added back. The **salaries and wages of N13,550,000** and the **interest on capital of N900,000** stay deducted, because in a **partnership** both are allowable expenses (§17.8) — unlike a sole trader, who cannot deduct his own salary. The N13,550,000 is exactly the sum of the three partners' drawn salaries (N3,300,000 + N4,700,000 + N5,550,000), which confirms there were no other staff. Each partner then picks up his own salary and interest as **income** in part (b), so nothing escapes tax — it is simply taxed on the partner rather than on the firm."},{"note":"Why each add-back: the **allowance for doubtful debts** is a *general* provision (only **specific** bad and doubtful debts are allowable under s.20); the **donation** of N800,000 to an old people's home exceeds the 10%-of-profit cap under s.163 in full here, since the firm's own working is to disallow it entirely; **depreciation** is never allowable; and a **staff loan written off** is a capital loss, not a trading expense."},{"h4":"(b)  Computation of each partner's chargeable income"},{"table":{"align":"lrrr","head":["","Bola (N)","Bety (N)","Seun (N)"],"rows":[["**Earned income**","","",""],["Salaries","3,300,000","4,700,000","5,550,000"],["Interest on capital","225,000","315,000","360,000"],["Share of profit (5 : 7 : 8)","4,736,250","6,630,750","7,578,000"],["","**8,261,250**","**11,645,750**","**13,488,000**"],["**Unearned income**","","",""],["Interest on loan","455,000","—","—"],["**Total income**","**8,716,250**","**11,645,750**","**13,488,000**"],["Deduct: capital allowance (5 : 7 : 8 of N9,500,000)","(2,375,000)","(3,325,000)","(3,800,000)"],["**Chargeable income**","**6,341,250**","**8,320,750**","**9,688,000**"]]}},{"h4":"How the shares and allowances are split"},{"ol":["The profit-sharing ratio is **5 : 7 : 8**, a total of **20** parts, so one part is N18,945,000 ÷ 20 = **N947,250**. Bola takes 5 parts = **N4,736,250**; Bety 7 parts = **N6,630,750**; Seun 8 parts = **N7,578,000**. These three add back exactly to **N18,945,000** — the check the book's own answer fails.","The **capital allowance of N9,500,000** is apportioned in the **same profit-sharing ratio** at the end of the basis period (§17.9(a)): one part is N475,000, so Bola **N2,375,000**, Bety **N3,325,000**, Seun **N3,800,000**, totalling N9,500,000.","Capital allowance is deducted from each partner's **total income**, not from the partnership's profit before division. This is the change the book itself notes at §17.12(b): under paragraph 19(3) of the First Schedule the allowance is **apportioned among the partners**, whereas it was formerly deducted before arriving at the computed income."]},{"h4":"(c)  Computation of tax payable by each partner"},{"table":{"align":"lrrr","head":["","Bola (N)","Bety (N)","Seun (N)"],"rows":[["First N800,000 @ 0%","0","0","0"],["Next N2,200,000 @ 15%","330,000","330,000","330,000"],["Balance @ 18%","601,425","957,735","1,203,840"],["","*(on N3,341,250)*","*(on N5,320,750)*","*(on N6,688,000)*"],["**Tax payable**","**931,425**","**1,287,735**","**1,533,840**"]]}},{"key":"Each partner's chargeable income falls in the **18% band** — above the N3,000,000 cumulative point where that band starts, but below the N12,000,000 point where the 21% band begins — so no partner here reaches the higher rates. Note also that **none** of the three claims an eligible deduction: the question gives no NHF, NHIS, pension, life assurance or rent figures."},{"note":"Additional information (iii) — \"**only Bola is married with two children below 16 years of age**\" — is deliberate noise and earns nothing. The NTA's eligible deductions are the closed list of six at §15.6, and **child relief and dependent-relative relief no longer exist**. The detail is there to catch candidates answering from the repealed PITA."},{"warn":"A further oddity in the question, flagged rather than resolved: the profit and loss account deducts **\"interest on loan by Erewa — N455,000\"**, and the book's solution then shows N455,000 as **Bola's** unearned income. **Erewa is not one of the three partners.** Either the name is a misprint for Bola, or the lender is an outsider — in which case the interest is still an allowable partnership expense but is **not any partner's income** and Bola's total income would be N8,261,250, with tax of N849,525. The figures above follow the book's treatment. If a question presents this ambiguity, state the assumption you are working on."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 17.15.1, Question 2","t":"Dejo, Akanbi, David and Dandy — a retirement and an admission mid-period","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Dejo, Akanbi and David have been in partnership for many years, providing specialised engineering services to the oil sector. Accounts are made up to **31 December** each year. The following are the adjusted profits:"},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Period","N"],"rows":[["Year ended 31 December 2024","1,440,000"],["Year ended 31 December 2025","1,650,000"],["Year ending 31 December 2026 (projected)","2,400,000"]]}},{"p":"Additional information is provided as follows:"},{"ol":["partners are to share profits in the ratio **1 : 2 : 1**;","salaries are drawn in this order: Dejo **N360,000**; Akanbi **N240,000**; David **N180,000**;","interest on capital is **6%**;","the capital account of each partner is: Dejo **N150,000**; Akanbi **N300,000**; David **N210,000**; and","on **31 May 2026 Dejo retired** and **Dandy was admitted on 1 June** of the same year, on a salary of **N180,000 per annum** — he brought in capital of **N210,000** and was to have the sharing ratio which Dejo used to enjoy."]},{"p":"**Required:** **(a)** Compute each partner's income from the partnership business for the **2027 year of assessment**. **(b)** Itemise the rules of commencement in respect of a partnership business."}],"a":[{"h4":"2(a)  Computation of each partner's income, 2027 assessment year"},{"p":"**Preceding year basis — basis period 1/1/2026 to 31/12/2026** (income assessable):"},{"table":{"align":"lrrrr","head":["","Dejo (N)","Akanbi (N)","David (N)","Dandy (N)"],"rows":[["Salaries","150,000","240,000","180,000","105,000"],["Interest on capital","3,750","18,000","12,600","7,350"],["Share of profit","250,000","1,200,000","600,000","350,000"],["**Partners' income**","**403,750**","**1,458,000**","**792,600**","**462,350**"]]}},{"h4":"Computation of partners' salaries"},{"ol":["Dejo — 5/12 × N360,000 = **N150,000** (1/1/2026 – 31/5/2026);","Dandy — 7/12 × N180,000 = **N105,000** (1/6/2026 – 31/12/2026);","Akanbi — full salary = **N240,000**; and","David — full salary = **N180,000**."]},{"h4":"Computation of interest on capital"},{"ol":["Dejo — 6% × 5/12 × N150,000 = **N3,750** (1/1/2026 – 31/5/2026);","Dandy — 6% × 7/12 × N210,000 = **N7,350** (1/6/2026 – 31/12/2026);","Akanbi — 6% × N300,000 = **N18,000**; and","David — 6% × N210,000 = **N12,600**."]},{"h4":"Computation of share of adjusted profit"},{"stmt":{"t":"Adjusted profit for the year ending 31 December 2026","rows":[["Adjusted profit","","2,400,000"],["Dejo (1/4 × 5/12 × N2,400,000)","250,000","","@ind"],["Dandy (1/4 × 7/12 × N2,400,000)","350,000","","@ind"],["Akanbi (1/2 × N2,400,000)","1,200,000","","@ind"],["David (1/4 × N2,400,000)","600,000","2,400,000","@ind"]]}},{"key":"The whole question is **time apportionment on a change of membership**. Dejo was a partner for **5 of the 12 months** and Dandy for the other **7**, so each of their three entitlements — salary, interest on capital and share of profit — is scaled by 5/12 and 7/12 respectively. Akanbi and David were partners throughout, so they take their full entitlements. The four shares of profit add back to exactly **N2,400,000**, which is the check that confirms the apportionment."},{"note":"Dandy takes \"the sharing ratio which Dejo used to enjoy\" — **1 of the 4 parts** in the 1 : 2 : 1 ratio, or one quarter. So the quarter share is itself split 5/12 : 7/12 between Dejo and Dandy, while Akanbi's half and David's quarter are undisturbed."},{"note":"The **2024 and 2025 adjusted profits are noise**. The 2027 year of assessment is assessed on the **preceding year basis**, so its basis period is the year ended 31 December **2026**, and only the N2,400,000 is relevant. The earlier figures are there to tempt a candidate into averaging, or into applying commencement rules that §17.4 expressly switches off."},{"key":"Note what does **not** happen: the partnership is **not** treated as having ceased and recommenced when Dejo left and Dandy joined. Section 25 of the NTA (§17.4) provides that an individual is not treated as commencing or ceasing a trade merely by becoming or ceasing to be a partner, where the nature of the trade is unchanged. The cessation-and-recommencement fiction in paragraph 19(b) of the First Schedule (§17.7) applies only for the purpose of **recomputing capital allowances**, which this question does not ask for."},{"h4":"2(b)  Commencement of a new trade — section 22(2) of NTA"},{"p":"The assessable income of an individual from a trade, business, profession or vocation carried on by him in Nigeria, for the year of assessment in which he commenced, and for the two following years of assessment (referred to as \"the first year\", \"the second year\" and \"the third year\"), shall be ascertained in accordance with the following provisions:"},{"ol":["for the **first year**, the assessable income shall be the income **from the date on which the individual commenced** such trade, business, profession or vocation in Nigeria **to the end of its first accounting period**;","for the **second year**, the assessable income shall be the income **from the first day after the first accounting period to the end of its second accounting period**; and","for the **third year, and each subsequent year** thereafter, the assessable income shall be the profits **from the day after the accounting period just ended**."]},{"note":"These are the **actual-basis** commencement rules: the first three years are assessed on the income of the accounting periods themselves rather than on a preceding-year basis, so that no period of profit escapes assessment and none is assessed twice. Question 2 of Chapter 16 (§16.11) and Question 1 of Chapter 15 (§15.13) both apply the same rules to capital allowances and chargeable profits."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 17.15.1, Question 3","t":"Worland & Associate — computed income and the partners' tax liabilities","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Professor Wachukwu and Dr Orlando are partners in a legal firm based in Calabar. The firm of **Worland & Associate (Legal Practitioners)** renders legal and secretarial services. The projected statement of profit or loss of the firm for the year ending **31 December 2026** disclosed the following details:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Worland & Associate — projected statement of profit or loss, year ending 31 December 2026","rows":[["Legal fees","","33,000,000"],["Fees for secretarial services","9,500,000","","@ind"],["Sundry incomes","4,500,000","","@ind"],["Interest on fixed deposit","2,800,000","16,800,000","@ind"],["","","49,800,000","@t"],"Expenses",["Salaries","13,200,000","","@ind"],["Office rent","2,200,000","","@ind"],["Sundry expenses","1,200,000","","@ind"],["Depreciation","4,800,000","","@ind"],["Finance cost","900,000","","@ind"],["Administrative expenses","200,000","","@ind"],["Repairs and maintenance","700,000","(23,200,000)","@ind"],["Net profit","","26,600,000","@tt"]]}},{"p":"**Additional information:**"},{"ol":["salary includes salaries paid to the partners: Professor Wachukwu **N5,550,000**; Dr Orlando **N4,400,000**;","sundry expenses include **N300,000** paid to **Dr Orlando's personal driver** as salary for the year;","finance cost is made up of: interest on loan to the firm N300,000; bank charges N150,000; interest on partners' **private** loans — Professor Wachukwu N150,000 and Dr Orlando N100,000; and interest paid **to Professor Wachukwu on loan to the firm** N200,000 — total N900,000;","repairs and maintenance is made up of: maintenance of the **residence** of each partner — Professor Wachukwu N200,000 and Dr Orlando N200,000; and **office repairs** N300,000 — total N700,000;","both partners are married with 3 children, all of school age; Professor Wachukwu maintains his aged parents and incurred **N30,000** annually on them;","Dr Orlando paid a **life insurance premium of N2,800,000** annually on his life;","sundry income includes **profit on sale of property, plant and equipment of N980,000**;","capital allowance agreed with the relevant tax authority amounted to **N6,400,000**; and","profits and losses are to be shared **equally**."]},{"p":"**Required:** Compute the assessable income and tax liabilities of the partners for the relevant tax year."}],"a":[{"h4":"Ascertainment of computed income and the partners' tax liabilities, 2027 assessment year"},{"table":{"align":"lrrr","head":["","Total (N)","Prof. Wachukwu (N)","Dr Orlando (N)"],"rows":[["Net profit","26,600,000","",""],["**Add: disallowed expenses**","","",""],["Sundry expenses — salary paid to Dr Orlando's personal driver","300,000","",""],["Depreciation","4,800,000","",""],["Interest on partners' private loan — Professor Wachukwu","150,000","",""],["Interest on partners' private loan — Dr Orlando","100,000","",""],["Repairs and maintenance — Professor Wachukwu's residence","200,000","",""],["Repairs and maintenance — Dr Orlando's residence","200,000","",""],["","**32,350,000**","",""],["Less: sundry income (profit on sale of PPE)","(980,000)","",""],["**Computed income**","**31,370,000**","",""],["**Share of divisible profit** — Professor Wachukwu, 50% × N31,370,000","(15,685,000)","15,685,000",""],["Dr Orlando, 50% × N31,370,000","(15,685,000)","","15,685,000"],["","**0**","",""],["Interest on partner's loan to the firm","","200,000","0"],["Salaries","","5,550,000","4,400,000"],["","","**21,435,000**","**20,085,000**"],["Capital allowance (50 : 50 of N6,400,000)","","(3,200,000)","(3,200,000)"],["","","**18,235,000**","**16,885,000**"],["**Deduct: eligible deductions** — life insurance premium","","(0)","(2,800,000)"],["**Chargeable income**","","**18,235,000**","**14,085,000**"]]}},{"table":{"align":"lrr","head":["Computation of tax liabilities","Prof. Wachukwu (N)","Dr Orlando (N)"],"rows":[["First N800,000 @ 0%","0","0"],["Next N2,200,000 @ 15%","330,000","330,000"],["Next N9,000,000 @ 18%","1,620,000","1,620,000"],["Next N6,235,000 @ 21% (Professor Wachukwu)","1,309,350",""],["Next N2,085,000 @ 21% (Dr Orlando)","","437,850"],["**Total tax liabilities**","**3,259,350**","**2,387,850**"]]}},{"h4":"Why each item is treated as it is"},{"ol":["**Partners' own salaries (N9,950,000 of the N13,200,000) are NOT added back** — they are allowable to the partnership under §17.8, and are instead picked up as each partner's own income lower down. The same applies to the **N200,000 interest paid to Professor Wachukwu on his loan to the firm**: allowable to the firm, taxable on him.","**Dr Orlando's personal driver (N300,000)** is his private domestic expense, not a cost of the firm, so it is added back. Note that it is added back to the **firm's** profit and therefore borne 50 : 50 by both partners — the firm paid it, so the adjustment is the firm's.","**Interest on partners' private loans (N150,000 and N100,000)** is added back for the same reason — the loans are personal, not the firm's. Contrast the **N300,000 interest on loan to the firm** and the **N150,000 bank charges**, which are genuine business finance costs and stay allowable.","**Maintenance of each partner's residence (N200,000 each)** is private expenditure, added back; the **N300,000 of office repairs** is allowable and is not.","**Depreciation (N4,800,000)** is never allowable; relief comes instead through the **N6,400,000 capital allowance**, apportioned equally because profits are shared equally (§17.9(a)).","**Profit on sale of PPE (N980,000)** is a capital profit, not trading income, so it is deducted from the computed income.","**Dr Orlando's life insurance premium of N2,800,000** is an eligible deduction under s.30(2) (§15.6), claimed against **his** total income alone — Professor Wachukwu has none, hence the nil in his column."]},{"key":"The structure worth internalising: adjustments to the **firm's** profit are shared between the partners in the profit-sharing ratio, whatever which partner caused them — Dr Orlando's driver and his private loan interest both increase Professor Wachukwu's share too. Adjustments that are **personal to a partner** — his own salary, interest on his loan to the firm, and his eligible deductions — attach to that partner alone. Getting this split the wrong way round is the commonest error in partnership questions."},{"note":"Additional information (v) — three school-age children each, and N30,000 a year on aged parents — gives **no relief at all**, for the reason set out in Question 1. Only the life insurance premium qualifies."},{"note":"The **N2,800,000 interest on fixed deposit** is left inside the firm's computed income by the book and so is taxed through the partners' shares. Strictly it is investment income rather than trade income and would be shown as a separate source when presenting a statement of total income — the arithmetic is unaffected here, but say so if a question asks for income **by source**."},{"warn":"A spelling slip in the book's solution to Question 2 of this section, worth noting since the same names recur: Akanbi is written **\"Akanni\"** in the line computing his share of profit. It is the same partner."}]}}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Partner's interest on capital (part-year)","tex":"\\text{Interest} = \\text{Rate} \\times \\text{Capital} \\times \\dfrac{\\text{Months as partner}}{12}"},{"lb":"Partner's salary (part-year)","tex":"\\text{Salary} = \\text{Annual salary} \\times \\dfrac{\\text{Months as partner}}{12}"},{"lb":"Partner's share of divisible profit","tex":"\\text{Share} = \\text{Divisible profit} \\times \\dfrac{\\text{Partner's ratio}}{\\text{Total ratio}}"},{"lb":"Partner's capital allowance share","tex":"\\text{CA share} = \\text{Total capital allowance} \\times \\dfrac{\\text{Partner's profit-sharing ratio}}{\\text{Total ratio}}"}],"focus":"This chapter's defining skill is handling a MID-YEAR change in partnership membership (a partner joining or retiring) — treat it as the partnership ceasing and immediately recommencing at that point (¶19(a)), which in practice means: continuing partners get their full-year salary/interest/profit-share as normal, while the joining/retiring partner gets everything prorated strictly to the months they were actually a partner, and the profit itself is split by the SAME time-apportionment logic before applying each partner's ratio. Also fix firmly the three items a partnership can deduct that a sole trader cannot — partner salaries, interest on partners' capital, and leave/recreation passage costs — since this exception is the single most-tested distinction between this chapter and Chapter 15. Capital allowance is apportioned by PROFIT-SHARING ratio, not by capital-contribution ratio — the two can differ, and only the profit-sharing ratio governs the capital allowance split.","errors":["Applying full-year salary/interest/profit-share figures to a partner who joined or retired partway through the year — every component (salary, interest on capital, AND profit share) must be time-apportioned to the months that partner actually held their position, while continuing partners keep their full-year figures unchanged.","Treating partner salaries and interest on capital as disallowed private expenses, by analogy with the sole-trader rule — a partnership is the specific, narrow exception where these ARE allowable deductions in computing partnership income.","Apportioning capital allowance by capital-contribution ratio instead of profit-sharing ratio — ¶19(3) of the First Schedule is explicit that capital allowance is apportioned strictly by profit-sharing ratio, which need not match how much capital each partner contributed.","Deducting capital allowance at the partnership level before splitting divisible profit among partners — capital allowance is deliberately NOT part of the partnership-level computed-income calculation; it is apportioned separately, directly to each partner's own tax computation, after profit-sharing.","Forgetting that ANY person sharing (directly or indirectly) in a Nigerian partnership's profits — resident or non-resident — must file returns in their OWN name and pay the applicable tax themselves; the partnership itself is not the taxable entity for income tax purposes, its individual partners are."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Which THREE expense categories are specifically allowable deductions in computing partnership income, despite normally being disallowed for a sole trader?","o":["Depreciation, bad debts, and donations","Partner salaries, interest on partners' capital, and leave/recreation passage costs","Capital allowance, VAT, and stamp duty","Rent, insurance, and utilities","Fines, penalties, and taxes on profit"],"a":1,"w":"Unlike a sole trader, a partnership may deduct salaries and interest on capital payable to partners, and the cost of leave/recreation passages to and from Nigeria, as allowable expenses in computing its income.","src":"Chapter 17.5","sec":"17.5"},{"q":"Under ¶19(3) of the First Schedule to the NTA, on what basis is capital allowance apportioned among partners?","o":["Equally among all partners regardless of any ratio","By each partner's capital-contribution ratio","By each partner's profit-sharing ratio","By seniority (longest-serving partner gets the largest share)","By each partner's personal income tax bracket"],"a":2,"w":"Capital allowances related to partnership assets are apportioned among the partners in their PROFIT-SHARING ratio at the end of the basis period — not by capital contribution.","src":"Chapter 17.8","sec":"17.8"},{"q":"What is the maximum number of partners an ordinary Nigerian partnership may have, before the special CAMA exception for professional practices applies?","o":["10","15","20","25","There is no limit"],"a":2,"w":"A partnership has a minimum of 2 and a maximum of 20 members — s.19(2)(b) CAMA allows more than 20 only for simultaneous professional practice (e.g. Legal Practitioners or Chartered Accountants).","src":"Chapter 17.2","sec":"17.2"},{"q":"Within how many days must a subsequent change to a registered partnership agreement be similarly registered with the tax authority, under s.15(7) of the NTA?","o":["7 days","14 days","21 days","30 days","60 days"],"a":3,"w":"Notice of any subsequent change to a registered partnership agreement must be registered with the tax authority within 30 days of the change agreement.","src":"Chapter 17.2","sec":"17.2"},{"q":"How is a mid-year change in partnership membership (a partner joining or leaving) treated for tax purposes, under ¶19(b) of the First Schedule to the NTA?","o":["The entire year is simply ignored for tax purposes","The business is treated as having ceased at that point and recommenced immediately after","Only the joining/leaving partner is taxed differently; the business itself is unaffected","The partnership must dissolve and re-register as an entirely new legal entity","Tax liability is suspended until the next full accounting year"],"a":1,"w":"Paragraph 19(b) treats the business as if it ceased at the point of the membership change and recommenced immediately after, with the deemed-disposal/re-acquisition rules applying to the transferred assets.","src":"Chapter 17.6","sec":"17.6"}],"theory":[{"q":"Dejo, Akanbi and David have been in partnership for many years, sharing profits 1:2:1, with salaries Dejo N360,000, Akanbi N240,000, David N180,000 per annum, and 6% interest on capital (Dejo N150,000, Akanbi N300,000, David N210,000). The adjusted profit for the year ended 31 December 2026 is N2,400,000. On 31 May 2026, Dejo retired, and Dandy was admitted on 1 June 2026 on a salary of N180,000 per annum, bringing in capital of N210,000, taking over Dejo's former profit-sharing ratio. (a) Compute each partner's income from the partnership for the 2027 year of assessment. (b) Itemise the rules of commencement for a partnership business.","marks":14,"a":[{"table":{"align":"lrrrr","head":["(a) Partners' income, 2027 assessment year","Dejo","Akanbi","David","Dandy"],"rows":[["Salaries (Dejo/Dandy prorated 5/12 and 7/12; Akanbi/David full year)","150,000","240,000","180,000","105,000"],["Interest on capital (Dejo/Dandy prorated 5/12 and 7/12; Akanbi/David full year)","3,750","18,000","12,600","7,350"],["Share of adjusted profit (Dejo/Dandy: 1/4 ratio prorated 5/12 and 7/12; Akanbi 1/2 full year; David 1/4 full year)","250,000","1,200,000","600,000","350,000"],["**Partners' income**","**403,750**","**1,458,000**","**792,600**","**462,350**"]]}},{"note":"Interest on capital: Dejo = 6% × 5/12 × N150,000 = N3,750; Dandy = 6% × 7/12 × N210,000 = N7,350; Akanbi = 6% × N300,000 = N18,000 (full year, unchanged); David = 6% × N210,000 = N12,600 (full year, unchanged). Salaries: Dejo = 5/12 × N360,000 = N150,000; Dandy = 7/12 × N180,000 = N105,000; Akanbi/David draw their full annual salaries unchanged. Profit share: Dejo = 1/4 × 5/12 × N2,400,000 = N250,000; Dandy = 1/4 × 7/12 × N2,400,000 = N350,000; Akanbi = 1/2 × N2,400,000 = N1,200,000; David = 1/4 × N2,400,000 = N600,000 — summing to the full N2,400,000 adjusted profit."},{"p":"**(b)** Rules of commencement (s.22(2) NTA) — for the year of commencement (the \"first year\") and the two following years (\"second year\", \"third year\"): in the first year, assessable income is the income from the commencement date to the end of the first accounting period; in the second year, assessable income is the income from the day after the first accounting period ended, to the end of the second accounting period; and in the third year (and every year after), assessable income is the profit from the day after the immediately-preceding accounting period ended — reverting to the ordinary preceding-year basis."}],"src":"Chapter 17.15","sec":"17.6"},{"q":"Professor Wachukwu and Dr. Orlando are equal partners in a legal firm. The projected statement of profit or loss for the year ending 31 December 2026 shows legal fees N33,000,000, other income N16,800,000 (including N980,000 profit on sale of PPE), total expenses N23,200,000, and net profit N26,600,000. Expenses include: N300,000 salary to Dr. Orlando's personal driver; N4,800,000 depreciation; N150,000/N100,000 interest on Wachukwu/Orlando's private loans (distinct from N200,000 interest paid TO Wachukwu on his loan TO the firm); N200,000/N200,000 maintenance of each partner's personal residence (distinct from N300,000 office repairs); and partner salaries of N5,550,000 (Wachukwu) and N4,400,000 (Orlando) within the N13,200,000 salaries line. Dr. Orlando pays N2,800,000 annual life insurance premium. Capital allowance agreed is N6,400,000. Compute the assessable income and tax liabilities of the partners.","marks":16,"a":[{"table":{"align":"lrrr","head":["Worland & Associate — 2027 assessment year","Total","Prof. Wachukwu","Dr. Orlando"],"rows":[["Net profit","26,600,000","",""],["Add: driver's salary (private)","300,000","",""],["Depreciation","4,800,000","",""],["Interest on partners' private loans (150,000 + 100,000)","250,000","",""],["Maintenance of partners' residences (200,000 + 200,000)","400,000","",""],["Subtotal","32,350,000","",""],["Less: profit on sale of PPE (non-taxable)","(980,000)","",""],["**Computed income**","**31,370,000**","",""],["Share of divisible profit (50:50)","","15,685,000","15,685,000"],["Interest on partner's loan to the firm","","200,000","0"],["Salaries","","5,550,000","4,400,000"],["**Taxable income**","","**21,435,000**","**20,085,000**"],["Less: capital allowance (50:50 of 6,400,000)","","(3,200,000)","(3,200,000)"],["Subtotal","","18,235,000","16,885,000"],["Less: life insurance premium","","(0)","(2,800,000)"],["**Chargeable income**","","**18,235,000**","**14,085,000**"]]}},{"table":{"align":"lrr","head":["Tax computation","Prof. Wachukwu","Dr. Orlando"],"rows":[["First N800,000 @ 0%","0","0"],["Next N2,200,000 @ 15%","330,000","330,000"],["Next N9,000,000 @ 18%","1,620,000","1,620,000"],["Next N6,235,000 / N2,085,000 @ 21%","1,309,350","437,850"],["**Total tax liability**","**3,259,350**","**2,387,850**"]]}},{"note":"Interest on a partner's loan TO the firm (a genuine business financing cost) stays deductible at partnership level, and is separately recognised as that partner's own investment income — unlike interest on a partner's PRIVATE loan (personal, unrelated to the firm), which is disallowed entirely. Similarly, office repairs stay deductible, while maintenance of a partner's own residence does not. Both partners' 3 school-age children, and Professor Wachukwu's aged-parent maintenance, are correctly left out of the computation — no dependent relief exists under the current NTA."}],"src":"Chapter 17.15","sec":"17.11"}]}},{"n":18,"t":"Settlement, Trusts and Estates","brief":"How income held for the benefit of others — under a settlement, a trust, or a deceased person's estate — gets taxed: who is treated as chargeable (settlor, annuitant, trustee, executor, beneficiary), how computed income is apportioned between fixed/discretionary beneficiaries and the trustee, the special anti-avoidance rules for settlements on a settlor's own unmarried children, and the territorial rules for non-resident trustees — closing with two fully worked, multi-beneficiary computations.","outcomes":["Understand the meaning and nature of settlement, trusts and estates","Appreciate the relevant tax laws guiding settlement, trusts and estates","Understand the computation of tax liabilities relating to settlement, trusts and estates"],"secs":[{"n":"18.2","t":"Definitions of settlement, trusts and estates","b":[{"h4":"18.2.1  Settlement — paragraph 9 of the Fifth Schedule to the NTA"},{"def":{"t":"Settlement","d":"a means by which enjoyment of an estate (or part of it) is transferred to another person — via a disposition, trust, covenant, agreement, arrangement, or asset transfer by reference to a trust — for the benefit of specified persons."}},{"h4":"18.2.2  Trust"},{"def":{"t":"Trust","d":"an equitable obligation binding a person (the trustee) to deal with property in their custody (the trust property) for the benefit of persons (beneficiaries), of whom the trustee may be one."}},{"h4":"18.2.3  Estate"},{"def":{"t":"Estate","d":"the total of a person's properties — commonly used in executorships, or to mean the property of a deceased person."}}]},{"n":"18.3","t":"Explanations of other definitions — paragraph 9 of the Fifth Schedule to the NTA","b":[{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Term","Meaning"],"rows":[["**Child**","Includes a stepchild, an adopted child, and an illegitimate child."],["**Settlor**","A person by whom a settlement was made/entered into, directly or indirectly — including one who provided/undertook to provide funds for it, or made a reciprocal arrangement with another person to make/enter the settlement."],["**Personal representative** (s.201 NTA)","The executor or administrator charged with administering a deceased person's estate, in whom the estate vests for distribution."],["**Executor** (s.147 NTAA)","Any person administering a deceased person's estate."]]}}]},{"n":"18.4","t":"Persons chargeable","b":[{"h4":"18.4.1  The settlor — paragraph 1 of the Fifth Schedule to the NTA"},{"key":"A settlement/trust's income is deemed the **settlor's** income where the settlor: retains/acquires an immediately exercisable general power of appointment over the capital assets or income; makes use (directly/indirectly, by borrowing or otherwise) of any part of the income; or the settlement/trust is **revocable** in circumstances where the settlor (or spouse) resumes control over any part of the income/assets."},{"note":"A settlement/trust is **not** revocable merely because income/assets may revert to the settlor (or spouse) if a beneficiary predeceases them, or on an uncertain event the settlement is limited by."},{"h4":"18.4.2  Annuitant — paragraph 11 of the Fifth Schedule to the NTA"},{"p":"An individual receiving a **fixed annual amount** annuity, paid from a settlement/trust/estate's income, is assessed on the **full amount** of the annuity."},{"h4":"18.4.3  Trustee, executor, etc. — paragraph 12 of the Fifth Schedule to the NTA"},{"key":"Income assessable in the hands of any settlor/trustee/executor/beneficiary/annuitant for a year of assessment is the amount ascertained for the year **preceding** that year — the same preceding-year basis already familiar from investment income."}]},{"n":"18.5","t":"Relevant tax authority — paragraph 11 of the First Schedule to the NTAA","b":[{"key":"A trustee's (or an executor's) income from any settlement/trust/estate is taxed **only by the territory in which the settlor** (or the person creating the trust) is **resident**, to the extent the Fifth Schedule provides."}]},{"n":"18.6","t":"Basis period — section 26(4) of NTA","b":[{"p":"The basis period for assessing a trustee/executor/beneficiary/annuitant's income is the **preceding year's** income. A trustee (or an estate's executor) in Nigeria must prepare accounts of the settlement/trust/estate's income from all sources, for successive periods ending **31 December** each year, and to the date the assets are **finally distributed**."},{"warn":"Unlike an ordinary trade/business, the **commencement and cessation** provisions do **not apply** under a settlement, trust or estate arrangement."}]},{"n":"18.7","t":"Allowable and non-allowable expenses — paragraph 3 of the Fifth Schedule to the NTA","b":[{"p":"Allowable expenses peculiar to an estate:"},{"ol":["expenses of the trustee/executor(/administrator) connected with the settlement/trust/estate, authorised by the trust deed or Will; and","any fixed annuity paid from income under the deed or Will."]},{"note":"Where the income includes a gain/profit from trade/business/profession/vocation, or rent/premium, the usual Chapter Two additions/deductions apply — as if that income were an individual's own assessable income for the year."}]},{"n":"18.8","t":"Computation of income from settlement, trusts and estates — paragraphs 3 and 12 of the Fifth Schedule to the NTA","b":[{"key":"Computed income = **total income less allowable expenses**. Where the income includes a trade/business/profession/vocation gain, or rent/premium, the relevant additions/deductions are ordinarily factored in to arrive at the computed income."}]},{"n":"18.9","t":"Computation of the income assessable in the hands of the Trustee — paragraph 10 of the Fifth Schedule to the NTA","b":[{"h4":"18.9.1  Capital allowances"},{"p":"Where a deceased individual's trade/business/profession/vocation asset (eligible for capital allowance in their year of death) forms part of the estate, the estate is deemed to have incurred QCE on the asset's acquisition equal to the **residue of expenditure** on the day following the death."},{"h4":"18.9.2  Losses — section 28(3) of the NTA"},{"p":"The loss deductible in arriving at a trust's computed income is **the amount of the loss incurred during the preceding year** in a trade, business, profession or vocation, plus **the amount of loss incurred on the disposal of a chargeable asset** — subject to the provisos of section 28(3), set out in full below."},{"p":"In order to arrive at the **total income of an individual**, the loss to be deducted is:"},{"ol":["the amount of a loss incurred by the individual **during the year or preceding year of assessment** in a trade, business, profession or vocation; and","the amount of loss incurred **on the disposal of a chargeable asset**,"]},{"p":"provided that:"},{"ol":["**in no circumstances shall the aggregate loss deductions from income exceed the amount of that loss**;","the loss shall be deducted **as far as possible from the assessable profit** of a trade, business, profession or vocation **of the first year of assessment after that in which the loss was incurred**, and in subsequent years **until the loss is fully recouped**;","the loss incurred during any year of assessment shall be computed **in accordance with the basis period** used in ascertaining the assessable profits and income; and","any loss incurred in any period from sales, disposal or any other transaction in **digital or virtual assets** shall only be deductible **against the profit or gain from digital or virtual assets**."]},{"note":"These are the same provisos that govern an individual (§19.4.1), because a trustee or executor is assessed as an individual. The practical consequence for a trust is the third proviso: the loss is computed **on the basis period**, which for an estate is the twelve-month accounting period of §18.12."},{"h4":"18.9.3  Discretionary payments"},{"def":{"t":"Discretionary payment","d":"a fixed amount payable to a direct beneficiary as authorised by the trust deed — a trustee/executor has no power to make one unless the appointing instrument authorises it."}},{"warn":"Where authorised, the instrument generally provides for apportioning net income **after** such payments. Discretionary payments are **not expenses** but **appropriations** of the estate's income — they are therefore **not deducted** in computing the computed income (only later, when apportioning it)."},{"h4":"18.9.4  Special provisions as to settlement on unmarried children"},{"ol":["income paid to/for the benefit of a settlor's **infant, unmarried** child, during the settlor's life, is treated as the **settlor's own income** (not the child's) — unless the aggregate paid to that child in the year does not exceed the **National Minimum Wage**; this rule does not apply where the settlor is out of Nigeria for the whole year, or absent for ≥183 days in any 12-month period spanning that year;","income that may become payable/applicable to a child in future (on a condition, contingency, or exercised discretion) is deemed paid to/for that child now — and, if not allocated to a particular child, deemed paid in **equal shares** among the children it may become payable to;","where the settlor pays income tax chargeable under this rule, they may **recover** the tax from the trustee/payee (with a tax-authority certificate as conclusive evidence) — any excess recovered must be repaid, and any dispute over the amount/apportionment is decided by the tax authority, **final and not appealable**; and","where a settlement has **more than one settlor**, these rules apply to each settlor as if they were the only settlor — income from that settlor may be treated as paid to/for their own child accordingly."]}]},{"n":"18.10","t":"Computation of tax liability payable by beneficiaries and trustees","b":[{"key":"The assessable income of a trustee, an executor, or a trust/estate beneficiary is determined under the Fifth Schedule, on a **preceding-year basis**. Income of a settlement/trust **other than** for a deceased individual's estate is limited to income from a Nigerian source, plus any income brought into/received in Nigeria."},{"h4":"Apportionment (¶4, Fifth Schedule)"},{"p":"Where the deed/Will fixes **specific proportions** for beneficiaries (or, on intestacy, the law fixes them), each beneficiary's income for the year is their **proportionate share** of the computed income."},{"p":"Where a trustee/executor has **discretion** to pay a beneficiary any amount from income: the amount actually paid in a year is that beneficiary's assessable income for the year; and the **remainder** of computed income (after all such discretionary payments) is apportioned among beneficiaries with a **specified proportional interest**, by that proportion."},{"warn":"If the aggregate of discretionary payments **exceeds** the computed income, each payment treated as a beneficiary's income is **reduced proportionally** so the aggregate does not exceed the computed income."},{"key":"Any **remainder** of computed income, after all beneficiary apportionments, is apportioned to the **trustee/executor**, assessed in their own name."}]},{"n":"18.11","t":"Taxation of non-resident trustees — paragraph 2 of the Fifth Schedule to the NTA","b":[{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Settlor/estate status","Income taxed"],"rows":[["**Resident**","Income derived from a source in Nigeria AND outside Nigeria."],["**Non-resident**","Income derived from a Nigerian source, AND income brought into or received in Nigeria."]]}},{"p":"Per s.17(2)/(3)/(9) NTA (governing non-resident taxation generally, applicable here): a non-resident's chargeable-asset disposal gains are taxable in Nigeria where they relate to a Nigerian trade/business/profession/vocation, a Nigerian-located asset, or an asset deemed Nigerian-located. A non-resident's trade/business profits are taxable in Nigeria where: they have a **permanent establishment (PE)** or **significant economic presence (SEP)** in Nigeria (to the extent profit is attributable to it); payment is made by a Nigerian resident/PE for services furnished from outside Nigeria to a Nigerian resident/PE — except payments to an employee, for teaching, or borne by a foreign PE of a Nigerian resident; or payment is made for insurance premiums/risks insured from Nigeria."},{"h4":"Deemed permanent establishment (s.17(9))"},{"p":"A non-resident has a Nigerian PE where they: have a place in Nigeria through/at which business is wholly/partly carried on; operate through an authorised person in Nigeria; maintain Nigerian goods/merchandise stock from which deliveries are made; execute a Nigerian project (surveys, design, construction, installation, commissioning/decommissioning, supervision), regardless of how the project was split across entities/jurisdictions; or furnish a Nigerian service through employees/agents/subcontractors."},{"p":"A non-resident has **significant economic presence** where it transmits signals/data/content to Nigeria for activities including e-commerce, app stores, high-frequency trading, electronic data storage, online adverts, participative network platforms, online payments, user-data supply, search engines, digital content, online gaming, cloud computing, or online teaching — with profit attributable to that activity."},{"note":"A non-resident is **not** deemed to have a PE/SEP solely for employing Nigeria-resident persons, to the extent their duties are not primarily for Nigerian customers. Per s.19 NTA, a dividend a non-resident receives from a Nigerian company faces **no further tax** beyond the tax deducted at source."}]},{"n":"18.12","t":"Accounting period of an estate — paragraph 3 of the First Schedule to the NTA","b":[{"key":"Estate accounts must be prepared for each **12-month period ending 31 December**. Responsibility for preparing the accounts rests with the **trustee/executor** (¶15, Fifth Schedule)."}]},{"n":"18.13","t":"Procedure for objections and appeals — paragraph 16 of the Fifth Schedule to the NTA","b":[{"p":"The objection/appeal procedure for this Schedule is as already fully set out in **Chapter 5** — the same Tax Ombud / Tax Appeal Tribunal / Federal High Court / Court of Appeal ladder."}]},{"n":"18.14","t":"Responsibility of the trustee to pay tax to the relevant tax authority — paragraph 14 of the Fifth Schedule to the NTA","b":[{"key":"A trustee of a settlement/trust, and every executor, is **answerable** for everything to be done regarding tax under the Act — income apportioned to a trustee/executor is assessed by the relevant tax authority in relation to that settlement, trust or estate."}]},{"n":"18.15","t":"Offences and penalties — sections 100 to 127 of the NTAA","b":[{"p":"The study text heads this section \"**sections 100 to 127 of the NTAA**\" — the general offence and penalty provisions, which apply to a trustee, executor or administrator exactly as they apply to any other taxable person. They are set out in full at §4.7 and §4.12; the ones most likely to arise on a settlement, trust or estate are:"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Default","Penalty","Where"],"rows":[["Failure to register with the relevant tax authority (s.100 NTAA)","N50,000 first month; N25,000 each subsequent month","§4.7.1"],["Failure or refusal to file returns, or knowingly filing incomplete or inaccurate returns (s.101 NTAA)","N100,000 first month; N50,000 each subsequent month","§4.7.2"],["Failure to keep proper books and records (s.102 NTAA)","N10,000 for a person other than a company","§4.7.3"],["Failure to pay tax on the due date (s.65 NTAA)","10% of the tax payable, plus interest at the CBN Monetary Policy Rate","§4.12.1"],["Failure to deduct or remit tax at source — relevant where the trustee pays an annuity or makes a discretionary payment (s.105 NTAA)","40% of the amount not deducted, plus the tax itself","§4.12.4"],["Failure to attend to a demand, request or notice (s.108 NTAA)","N100,000 first day; N10,000 each subsequent day","§4.12.6"],["False declaration in a return (s.124 NTAA)","N1,000,000 administrative penalty plus the tax undercharged; or on conviction a N1,000,000 fine or up to 3 years' imprisonment, or both","§4.12.16"],["Any contravention for which no specific penalty is provided (s.127 NTAA)","N1,000,000 administrative penalty; or on conviction, imprisonment up to 3 years or a fine, or both","§4.12.19"]]}},{"key":"The penalty with the sharpest edge for a trustee is **s.105** — failure to deduct. Read with §18.14, the trustee is **personally responsible** for paying over the tax on the settlement's income, so a trustee who distributes to beneficiaries gross, without deducting, funds both the tax and the 40% penalty from the trust estate or from his own pocket."}]},{"n":"18.16","t":"Chapter review","b":[{"p":"This chapter defined settlement, trust, estate and related income (including annuities payable to beneficiaries), and explained the distinction between income taxable in the hands of beneficiaries and that of trustees."}]},{"n":"18.17","t":"Worked examples — study text questions and suggested solutions","b":[{"p":"The study text sets **four questions** at §18.17.1 — three full computations and one definitions question — with its own suggested solutions following. All four are reproduced here in full, with the book's answers, every figure independently recomputed and cross-footed, and added explanation."},{"note":"A numbering defect in the book, flagged rather than silently corrected: the questions are at **§18.17.1** but the solutions are headed **§18.18.2** — there is no §18.18 in the chapter, and the solutions plainly belong at 18.17.2."},{"eg":{"t":"Mr Akwa's trust — income chargeable on each beneficiary and on the trustee","q":[{"p":"Mr Akwa created a trust for his four children — Ade, Baruwa, Chidi and Dafur. Projected trustee income for the year ending 31 December 2026: profit from trading activities N980,000; interest on fixed deposits N270,000; rent from property (gross) N620,000; other income N163,000."},{"p":"Additional information: (i) Dafur receives a fixed annuity of N35,000/annum; (ii) trustee remuneration is fixed at N20,000/annum plus 2.5% of computed income; (iii) the trustee's allowable expenses were N25,000; (iv) discretionary payments: Ade N40,000, Baruwa N30,000, Chidi N20,000, Dafur N15,000; (v) capital allowance agreed for the trustee's business was N22,000; (vi) half of the net distributable income is shared equally among the beneficiaries. **Required**: (a) compute the income chargeable to tax in the hands of each beneficiary; (b) compute the undistributed income assessable to tax in the hands of the trustee."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["(b) Computed and distributable income","N"],"rows":[["Profit from trading activities","980,000"],["Less: capital allowance agreed","(22,000)"],["Subtotal","958,000"],["Interest received on fixed deposits","270,000"],["Rent from property (gross)","620,000"],["Other income","163,000"],["**Total**","**2,011,000**"],["Less: Dafur's fixed annuity","(35,000)"],["Less: trustee's fixed remuneration","(20,000)"],["Less: trustee's variable remuneration (2.5/102.5 of 1,931,000)","(47,098)"],["Less: trustee's allowable expenses","(25,000)"],["**Computed income**","**1,883,902**"],["Less: discretionary payments (40,000 + 30,000 + 20,000 + 15,000)","(105,000)"],["**Distributable income**","**1,778,902**"],["Half shared equally among the 4 beneficiaries","889,451"],["**Balance assessable in the hands of the trustee**","**889,451**"]]}},{"note":"The trustee's variable remuneration (2.5% of COMPUTED income, which is itself net of that same remuneration) is solved via the 2.5/102.5 grossing-up shortcut: 2.5/102.5 × (2,011,000 − 35,000 − 20,000 − 25,000) = 2.5/102.5 × 1,931,000 = N47,098."},{"table":{"align":"lrrrr","head":["(a) Beneficiary income","Ade","Baruwa","Chidi","Dafur"],"rows":[["Share of distributable income (889,451 ÷ 4, equal)","222,363","222,363","222,363","222,363"],["Fixed annuity","—","—","—","35,000"],["Discretionary payment","40,000","30,000","20,000","15,000"],["**Chargeable income**","**262,363**","**252,363**","**242,363**","**272,363**"]]}},{"note":"Distributable income (N1,778,902) is split exactly in half: one half (N889,451) is shared equally among the 4 beneficiaries as their base share; the OTHER half (also N889,451) remains taxable in the trustee's own hands, on top of the discretionary payments and Dafur's annuity, which are added to each specific beneficiary's own personal chargeable income individually."},{"h4":"The gross-up that decides this question"},{"p":"The trustee's variable remuneration is **2.5% of computed income**, but the remuneration is itself one of the expenses deducted in arriving at computed income — so it cannot be found by applying 2.5% to a figure that does not yet exist. The book solves it by **grossing up**: take the total income less every *other* allowable expense, then apply **2.5/102.5**:"},{"tex":"\\text{Base} = N2{,}011{,}000 - N35{,}000 - N20{,}000 - N25{,}000 = N1{,}931{,}000"},{"tex":"\\text{Variable remuneration} = \\dfrac{2.5}{102.5} \\times N1{,}931{,}000 = N47{,}098"},{"note":"Proof that the gross-up is right: computed income came out at N1,883,902, and **2.5% of N1,883,902 = N47,098**. The remuneration is therefore exactly 2.5% of the computed income it helped to produce, which is what the trust deed says. Applying 2.5% directly to N1,931,000 would give N48,275 and would not reconcile."},{"key":"Follow the order of the waterfall, because every later figure depends on it: **total income** (after capital allowances on the trading profit only) → less **the annuity, the trustee's fixed and variable remuneration, and the trustee's allowable expenses** → **computed income** → less **discretionary payments** → **distributable income** → half of which is shared equally among the four beneficiaries, and **half of which remains assessable on the trustee**. Here the two halves are the same figure, N889,451."},{"note":"Each beneficiary is taxed on **his own share of distributable income plus whatever was paid specifically to him** — the fixed annuity (Dafur only) and the discretionary payment. That is why Dafur's N272,363 is the largest even though all four share the distributable income equally. The four columns add to N1,029,452, which is the N889,451 shared out plus the N35,000 annuity plus the N105,000 of discretionary payments (the book prints N1,029,451 — a one-naira rounding difference from sharing N889,451 among four)."}],"tag":"Study text — 18.17.1, Question 1","open":true}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 18.17.1, Question 2","t":"Settlement, settlor, personal representative and child defined","open":true,"q":[{"p":"In relation to the provisions of the **Fifth Schedule to the NTA, 2025**, explain the following terms: **(a)** settlement; **(b)** settlor; **(c)** personal representative; **(d)** child."}],"a":[{"h4":"2(a)  Settlement"},{"p":"It is a means by which the **enjoyment of an estate, or part of it, is transferred to another person** — either through a **disposition, trust, covenant, agreement, arrangement or transfer of assets** by reference to a trust, for the benefit of persons specified."},{"h4":"2(b)  Settlor"},{"p":"In relation to a settlement, **includes a person by whom the settlement was made or entered into, directly or indirectly** — and in particular (but without prejudice to the generality of the foregoing) includes a person who **has provided or undertaken to provide funds**, directly or indirectly, for the purpose of the settlement, or has made with any other person a **reciprocal arrangement** for that other person to make or enter into the settlement."},{"h4":"2(c)  Personal representative"},{"p":"This is the person — **whether executor or administrator** — who is charged with the **administration of the estate of a deceased person**, and **in whom the estate is vested for the purpose of distribution**."},{"h4":"2(d)  Child"},{"p":"It **includes a step child, an adopted child and an illegitimate child**."},{"warn":"A striking typographical defect in the book's printed solution, flagged rather than silently corrected: part (d) of the solution is headed **\"Sunday\"** instead of **\"Child\"**. The definition that follows — \"it includes a step child, an adopted child and an illegitimate child\" — is unmistakably the definition of *child*, which is what the question asks for."},{"key":"Two of these definitions are deliberately **wide**, and that width is the examinable point. \"**Settlor**\" reaches a person who acted **indirectly**, who merely **undertook to provide funds**, or who entered a **reciprocal arrangement** for someone else to make the settlement — an anti-avoidance drafting, so that a person cannot escape the §18.4.1 charge by settling through an intermediary. \"**Child**\" reaches **step, adopted and illegitimate** children, which matters for the unmarried-children provisions at §18.9.4, where income paid for an infant unmarried child is deemed the settlor's own."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 18.17.1, Question 3","t":"Mallam Junaid Dikko settlement — trustee's net income and each beneficiary's assessable income","open":true,"q":[{"p":"**Baba Ali Musa** is the trustee of a settlement made by the late **Mallam Junaid Dikko** in favour of his four children, grandchildren and others. He submitted the following information to the **Zamfara State Internal Revenue Service** for assessment purposes for the fiscal year ending **31 December 2026**:"},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["","N"],"rows":[["Interest received","2,000,000"],["Dividend (gross)","8,000,000"],["Rental income","6,000,000"],["Business profit","18,000,000"],["Miscellaneous income","3,200,000"],["Trustee's remuneration — fixed (2% of gross income)","450,000"],["Fixed annuity to grandchildren — Aminat","50,000"],["Fixed annuity to grandchildren — Danladi","30,000"],["Administrative expenses","2,600,000"]]}},{"p":"**Additional information:** **(i)** the trustee made discretionary payments in line with the trust deed to the beneficiaries as follows — Wakilu N150,000; Aishat N200,000; Illyasu N250,000; Badamasi N180,000; **(ii)** each beneficiary is entitled to **1/6th share of 2/3rd** of the distributable income; **(iii)** capital allowance agreed with the relevant tax authority — **N4,200,000**."},{"p":"**Required:** **(a)** Compute the net income assessable in the hands of the trustee. **(b)** Compute the assessable income in the hands of each beneficiary."}],"a":[{"h4":"3(a)  Computation of assessable income in the hands of the trustee, 2027 tax year"},{"stmt":{"t":"Mallam Junaid Dikko settlement — income assessable on the trustee","rows":["Income:",["Interest received","","2,000,000","@ind"],["Dividend (gross)","","8,000,000","@ind"],["Rental income","","6,000,000","@ind"],["Business profit","18,000,000","","@ind"],["Less: capital allowance","(4,200,000)","13,800,000","@ind"],["Miscellaneous income","","3,200,000","@ind"],["Gross income","","33,000,000","@t"],"Less: allowable expenses",["Trustee's remuneration — fixed","450,000","","@ind"],["Trustee's remuneration — variable, 2% × N33,000,000","660,000","","@ind"],["Fixed annuity to grandchildren — Aminat","50,000","","@ind"],["Fixed annuity to grandchildren — Danladi","30,000","","@ind"],["Administrative expenses","2,600,000","(3,790,000)","@ind"],["Computed income","","29,210,000","@t"],"Less: discretionary payments",["Wakilu","150,000","","@ind"],["Aishat","200,000","","@ind"],["Illyasu","250,000","","@ind"],["Badamasi","180,000","(780,000)","@ind"],["","","28,430,000","@t"],["Less: franked investment income — dividend (gross)","","(8,000,000)"],["Amount available for distribution","","20,430,000","@t"],"Less: distribution — 1/6 each of 2/3 × N20,430,000 = N13,620,000",["Wakilu (N13,620,000 × 1/6)","2,270,000","","@ind"],["Aishat (N13,620,000 × 1/6)","2,270,000","","@ind"],["Illyasu (N13,620,000 × 1/6)","2,270,000","","@ind"],["Badamasi (N13,620,000 × 1/6)","2,270,000","","@ind"],["Aminat (N13,620,000 × 1/6)","2,270,000","","@ind"],["Danladi (N13,620,000 × 1/6)","2,270,000","(13,620,000)","@ind"],["Balance assessable in the hands of the trustee","","6,810,000","@tt"]]}},{"h4":"3(b)  Computation of assessable income in the hands of each beneficiary"},{"table":{"align":"lrrrrrr","head":["","Wakilu (N)","Aishat (N)","Illyasu (N)","Badamasi (N)","Aminat (N)","Danladi (N)"],"rows":[["Fixed annuity","0","0","0","0","50,000","30,000"],["Discretionary payments","150,000","200,000","250,000","180,000","0","0"],["Distributed income","2,270,000","2,270,000","2,270,000","2,270,000","2,270,000","2,270,000"],["**Beneficiary's assessable income**","**2,420,000**","**2,470,000**","**2,520,000**","**2,450,000**","**2,320,000**","**2,300,000**"]]}},{"key":"The step that distinguishes this question from Question 1 is the **removal of franked investment income before distribution**. The gross dividend of N8,000,000 is included in gross income (and so bears its share of the trustee's expenses), but is then **deducted before the distributable figure is struck**, because the withholding tax on it was the **final tax**. The book's own note puts it plainly: *\"In order to avoid the dividend received being taxed twice, it will be treated as part of the income in the hands of the trustee and beneficiaries but it will not be subjected to further tax.\"*"},{"note":"Note the arithmetic of the distribution: **2/3 of N20,430,000 = N13,620,000** is distributed, and each of the **six** beneficiaries takes **1/6 of that = N2,270,000**. The remaining **1/3, or N6,810,000**, stays assessable on the trustee. Six beneficiaries appear even though the question speaks of \"four children, grandchildren and others\" — the four discretionary recipients plus the two annuitants, Aminat and Danladi."},{"note":"Observe that the two **annuitants also take a share of the distributed income**, and the four discretionary recipients take **no annuity**. A beneficiary's assessable income is simply the sum of whatever reaches him by each route — annuity, discretionary payment, and share of distribution."},{"warn":"An ambiguity in the question, flagged rather than resolved: the data line reads **\"Trustee's remuneration: Fixed (2% of gross income) — 450,000\"**, which on its face describes the N450,000 *as* 2% of gross income. The book's solution instead treats the N450,000 as a fixed fee **and adds a separate variable fee of 2% × N33,000,000 = N660,000**, giving N1,110,000 of remuneration in total. On the alternative reading only one of the two would be deducted. The figures above follow the book. Note also that no **gross-up** is needed here (unlike Question 1), because the variable fee is a percentage of **gross income** — a figure struck before any expense — rather than of computed income."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 18.17.1, Question 4","t":"Dr Solomon Olubiyo Trust — net computed income, with five source errors corrected","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Dr Solomon Olubiyo created a trust for his children — **Kemmy, Busayo and Samuel** — before he died in 2025. For the year ending **31 December 2026** the following projected information was extracted from the records of the trustee:"},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["","N"],"rows":[["Projected adjusted trading profit for the year ending 31 December 2026","2,400,000"],["Rental income (gross) 2026","1,100,000"],["Other miscellaneous income","1,100,000"]]}},{"p":"The following information is also relevant:"},{"ol":["fixed annuity to a beneficiary — Kemmy **N120,000**;","fixed remuneration for the trustee — **N80,000**;","variable trustee remuneration — **5% of total computed income**;","other expenses — **N400,000**;","the trust provides discretionary payments to the children towards their maintenance and education as follows: Kemmy **N240,000**; Busayo **N230,000**; Samuel **N250,000**;","capital allowance was agreed at **N240,000**;","**N300,000** of other expenses are **not tax deductible**; and","it is stipulated in the trust that the beneficiaries are to share **half of the distributable income equally**."]},{"p":"**Required:** Calculate **(a)** the net computed income; **(b)** the amount each beneficiary will include in his or her income tax returns in the relevant year of assessment."}],"a":[{"warn":"The study text's printed solution to this question contains **five separate errors**, set out in the final note below. The computation here works the question **as stated**, so its figures differ from the book's throughout. Read the final note before relying on either."},{"h4":"4(a)  Computation of net computed income, 2027 tax year"},{"stmt":{"t":"Dr Solomon Olubiyo Trust — net computed income","rows":["Income",["Adjusted trading profit","2,400,000","","@ind"],["Less: capital allowances","(240,000)","2,160,000","@ind"],["Rental income (gross)","","1,100,000","@ind"],["Other miscellaneous income","","1,100,000","@ind"],["Total income","","4,360,000","@t"],"Less: expenses",["Trustee remuneration — fixed","80,000","","@ind"],["Trustee remuneration — variable (W1)","193,333","","@ind"],["Other expenses (N400,000 − N300,000 not deductible)","100,000","","@ind"],["Fixed annuity (Kemmy)","120,000","(493,333)","@ind"],["Computed income","","3,866,667","@tt"]]}},{"h4":"W1 — variable trustee remuneration"},{"p":"The variable fee is **5% of computed income**, and the fee is itself deducted in arriving at computed income, so it must be **grossed up** at **5/105** on the total income net of every other expense — the same device as Question 1:"},{"tex":"\\text{Base} = N4{,}360{,}000 - N100{,}000 - N80{,}000 - N120{,}000 = N4{,}060{,}000"},{"tex":"\\text{Variable remuneration} = \\dfrac{5}{105} \\times N4{,}060{,}000 = N193{,}333"},{"note":"Check: **5% of the computed income of N3,866,667 = N193,333**, so the fee is exactly 5% of the figure it helped produce."},{"h4":"4(b)  Amount each beneficiary will include in his or her income tax returns"},{"stmt":{"t":"Distribution of the computed income","rows":[["Computed income","","3,866,667"],["Less: discretionary payments (N240,000 + N230,000 + N250,000)","","(720,000)"],["Remainder of computed income","","3,146,667","@t"],["Share of distributable income — one half","","1,573,333"],["Retained and assessable on the trustee — one half","","1,573,334","@t"]]}},{"table":{"align":"lrrr","head":["","Kemmy (N)","Busayo (N)","Samuel (N)"],"rows":[["Share of distributable income (N1,573,333 ÷ 3)","524,444","524,444","524,444"],["Discretionary payment","240,000","230,000","250,000"],["Fixed annuity","120,000","—","—"],["**Amount to include in the income tax return**","**884,444**","**754,444**","**774,444**"]]}},{"note":"Cross-check: the three beneficiaries' amounts total N2,413,332, which is the N1,573,333 shared out, plus the N720,000 of discretionary payments, plus Kemmy's N120,000 annuity — N2,413,333, the one-naira difference being the rounding of N1,573,333 into three equal shares."},{"key":"The structural points are the same three as in Questions 1 and 3, and they are what the marks are for. **Capital allowances come off the trading profit only** — not off rent or miscellaneous income. The **variable trustee fee needs a gross-up** whenever it is expressed as a percentage of *computed* income. And the **discretionary payments come out before the half-share is struck**, so a beneficiary receives his discretionary payment *and* a share of what is left, never one instead of the other."},{"warn":"The five errors in the study text's printed solution, itemised so that a reader working from the book can reconcile it: **(1)** it uses **other miscellaneous income of N1,000,070** where the question states **N1,100,000**, giving a total income of N4,260,070 instead of N4,360,000; **(2)** it computes allowable other expenses as **\"(440 − 300)\" = N140,000**, using N440,000 where the question states **N400,000** (so N100,000); **(3)** it shows the discretionary payments as **N240,000 to each of the three children**, where the question gives **N240,000, N230,000 and N250,000** — the N720,000 total is right but the allocation between the children is not, which changes each child's own figure; **(4)** two of the three equal shares of distributable income are printed as **N502,223.33** against the first at **N502,233.34**, so the three do not add to the N1,506,700 being divided; and **(5)** the trustee's half is printed as **N1,506,750** where the matching half is N1,506,700. Working from the question as stated gives a computed income of **N3,866,667** against the book's N3,733,400."},{"note":"Despite the arithmetic, the book's **method** is sound and is the method reproduced above — including its own working note for the variable remuneration, which correctly grosses up at 5/105 on total income less other expenses, fixed remuneration and the fixed annuity. It is the inputs, not the approach, that go wrong."}]}}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Computed income of a settlement/trust/estate","tex":"\\text{Computed income} = \\text{Total income} - \\text{Allowable expenses}"},{"lb":"Variable trustee remuneration (a percentage of computed income itself)","tex":"\\text{Variable remuneration} = \\dfrac{\\text{Rate}}{100 + \\text{Rate}} \\times \\text{Income before variable remuneration}"},{"lb":"Beneficiary's proportionate share","tex":"\\text{Share} = \\text{Distributable income} \\times \\text{Beneficiary's specified proportion}"}],"focus":"The recurring computational trick in this chapter is the grossing-up shortcut for a trustee's VARIABLE remuneration, since it is stated as a percentage of computed income that the remuneration itself reduces — always solve it as Rate/(100+Rate) × the income figure BEFORE deducting that remuneration, exactly as shown in the illustration. Equally important structurally: discretionary payments are appropriations, not expenses — they reduce distributable income only AFTER computed income is already determined, never inside the computed-income calculation itself. And keep the settlor-attribution anti-avoidance rule sharp: income paid to a settlor's own infant, unmarried child remains taxed as the SETTLOR's income (not the child's), specifically to prevent a parent from sheltering their own income by routing it through a child's lower tax position — unless the amount is below the National Minimum Wage, or the settlor is substantially absent from Nigeria.","errors":["Deducting discretionary payments as an EXPENSE when computing a trust's computed income — they are appropriations of already-computed income, deducted only at the later apportionment stage, never inside the computed-income calculation itself.","Computing variable trustee remuneration as a straightforward percentage of the pre-remuneration income figure — since the rate is defined as a percentage of income AFTER the remuneration is deducted, a direct percentage overstates the remuneration; the Rate/(100+Rate) grossing-up shortcut must be used instead.","Assuming income paid to any minor child of a settlor is automatically taxed as the child's own income — where the settlor is still alive and the child is an infant and unmarried, the income is instead attributed to the SETTLOR, unless it falls below the National Minimum Wage exemption or the settlor was substantially absent from Nigeria that year.","Applying ordinary commencement/cessation basis-period rules to a settlement, trust or estate — these provisions explicitly do NOT apply in this context; the basis period is simply the preceding year's income throughout, with no special first/second/third-year treatment.","Treating a dividend received by the trust as taxable again when apportioned to beneficiaries or the trustee — as with individuals, dividend WHT is final tax; the gross dividend passes through the distribution mechanics (so it is not double-counted elsewhere) but is excluded before final apportionment, and is never subjected to further tax in anyone's hands."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Under the Fifth Schedule to the NTA, how are discretionary payments treated in computing a trust's computed income?","o":["As an ordinary allowable expense, deducted before arriving at computed income","As appropriations of income, NOT deducted in computing computed income — only apportioned afterward","As a disallowed expense that must be added back","As chargeable gains in the trustee's own hands","As exempt income, excluded from the computation entirely"],"a":1,"w":"Discretionary payments are appropriations of the estate's income, not expenses — they are not deducted in computing the computed income, only later when apportioning distributable income among beneficiaries.","src":"Chapter 18.7","sec":"18.7"},{"q":"Income paid to or for the benefit of a settlor's infant, unmarried child during the settlor's lifetime is, by default, treated as whose income for tax purposes?","o":["The child's own income","The trustee's income","The settlor's own income","Nobody's — it is automatically tax-exempt","The National Minimum Wage board's income"],"a":2,"w":"Under ¶5 of the Fifth Schedule, such income is treated as the settlor's own income (not the child's), unless the amount falls below the National Minimum Wage, or the settlor was substantially absent from Nigeria that year.","src":"Chapter 18.7","sec":"18.7"},{"q":"What is the basis period for assessing the income of a trustee, executor, beneficiary or annuitant, under s.26(4) of the NTA?","o":["The actual year of assessment","The preceding year's income","The following year's projected income","A 3-year rolling average","There is no fixed basis period"],"a":1,"w":"The basis period is that person's income for the year PRECEDING the year of assessment — the same preceding-year-basis logic already established for investment income.","src":"Chapter 18.4","sec":"18.4"},{"q":"Which of the following is explicitly stated to NOT apply under a settlement, trust or estate arrangement?","o":["The preceding-year basis of assessment","Capital allowance claims","The commencement and cessation provisions","The objection and appeal procedure","The requirement to register with the relevant tax authority"],"a":2,"w":"Section 26(4) NTA explicitly states that the ordinary commencement and cessation basis-period provisions do not apply under a settlement, trust or estate arrangement.","src":"Chapter 18.4","sec":"18.4"},{"q":"Where a trustee/executor has discretion to pay a beneficiary any amount from income, and the aggregate of all such discretionary payments in a year EXCEEDS the computed income, what happens?","o":["The excess is simply written off with no adjustment","Each payment treated as beneficiary income is reduced proportionally so the aggregate does not exceed computed income","The trustee becomes personally liable for the shortfall","The beneficiaries must repay the excess to the settlor","The tax authority automatically doubles next year's computed income to compensate"],"a":1,"w":"Per ¶4 of the Fifth Schedule, where the aggregate of discretionary payments exceeds the computed income, each payment treated as a beneficiary's income is reduced proportionally so the total does not exceed the computed income.","src":"Chapter 18.8","sec":"18.8"}],"theory":[{"q":"Baba Ali Musa is trustee of a settlement made by the late Mallam Junaid Dikko in favour of 4 children (Wakilu, Aishat, Illyasu, Badamasi) and 2 grandchildren (Aminat, Danladi). For the year ending 31 December 2026: interest received N2,000,000; dividend (gross) N8,000,000; rental income N6,000,000; business profit N18,000,000 (capital allowance agreed N4,200,000); miscellaneous income N3,200,000. Trustee remuneration: fixed N450,000, plus 2% of gross income. Fixed annuities to grandchildren: Aminat N50,000, Danladi N30,000. Admin expenses N2,600,000. Discretionary payments per the trust deed: Wakilu N150,000, Aishat N200,000, Illyasu N250,000, Badamasi N180,000. Each beneficiary (all 6) is entitled to 1/6 of 2/3 of the distributable income. Compute (a) the net income assessable in the hands of the trustee, and (b) the assessable income of each beneficiary.","marks":16,"a":[{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["(a) Assessable income in the hands of the trustee","N"],"rows":[["Interest received","2,000,000"],["Dividend (gross)","8,000,000"],["Rental income","6,000,000"],["Business profit (18,000,000 less CA 4,200,000)","13,800,000"],["Miscellaneous income","3,200,000"],["**Gross income**","**33,000,000**"],["Less: trustee remuneration — fixed","450,000"],["Trustee remuneration — variable (2% × 33,000,000)","660,000"],["Fixed annuities (Aminat 50,000 + Danladi 30,000)","80,000"],["Admin expenses","2,600,000"],["**Total allowable expenses**","**(3,790,000)**"],["**Computed income**","**29,210,000**"],["Less: discretionary payments (150,000+200,000+250,000+180,000)","(780,000)"],["Subtotal","28,430,000"],["Less: dividend (gross) — franked investment income, excluded","(8,000,000)"],["**Amount available for distribution**","**20,430,000**"],["Less: distribution — 2/3 × 20,430,000 = 13,620,000, split 1/6 each among 6 beneficiaries (2,270,000 each)","(13,620,000)"],["**Balance assessable in the hands of the trustee**","**6,810,000**"]]}},{"table":{"align":"lrrrrrr","head":["(b) Beneficiary income","Wakilu","Aishat","Illyasu","Badamasi","Aminat","Danladi"],"rows":[["Fixed annuity","—","—","—","—","50,000","30,000"],["Discretionary payment","150,000","200,000","250,000","180,000","—","—"],["Distributed income (1/6 of 13,620,000)","2,270,000","2,270,000","2,270,000","2,270,000","2,270,000","2,270,000"],["**Assessable income**","**2,420,000**","**2,470,000**","**2,520,000**","**2,450,000**","**2,320,000**","**2,300,000**"]]}},{"note":"To avoid taxing the dividend twice, it is treated as part of the income in both the trustee's and beneficiaries' distribution mechanics (its gross amount flows through the \"amount available for distribution\" base) but is excluded before apportionment and never itself subjected to further tax — the WHT already deducted at source remains the dividend's final tax."}],"src":"Chapter 18.17","sec":"18.8"},{"q":"Dr. Solomon Olubiyo created a trust for his children (Kemmy, Busayo, Samuel) before his death in 2025. For the year ending 31 December 2026: projected adjusted trading profit N2,400,000 (capital allowance agreed N240,000); rental income (gross) N1,100,000; other miscellaneous income N1,100,000. Fixed annuity to Kemmy N120,000; fixed trustee remuneration N80,000; variable trustee remuneration 5% of total computed income; other expenses N400,000 (of which N300,000 is not tax deductible); discretionary payments Kemmy N240,000, Busayo N230,000, Samuel N250,000; beneficiaries share half the distributable income equally. Calculate (a) the net computed income, and (b) the amount each beneficiary includes in their own tax return.","marks":14,"a":[{"warn":"The study text's own printed solution for this illustration does not fully match the question as extracted: it computes \"other income\" as N1,000,070 rather than the question's stated N1,100,000; its \"other expenses\" line implies an original figure of N440,000 (shown as \"440-300 = 140,000\") rather than the question's stated N400,000; and its discretionary-payments row applies an equal N240,000 to each of the 3 children, rather than the question's own individually-stated N240,000/N230,000/N250,000 split (even though both versions happen to total N720,000). These discrepancies could not be resolved from the extracted text alone. The computation below reproduces the source's own solution figures throughout, for internal consistency with its own final answer — NOT the question's literal stated figures where they differ."},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["(a) Net computed income","N"],"rows":[["Adjusted trading profit","2,400,000"],["Less: capital allowance","(240,000)"],["Subtotal","2,160,000"],["Rental income (gross)","1,100,000"],["Other income (per the solution's own figure)","1,000,070"],["**Total income**","**4,260,070**"],["Less: trustee remuneration — fixed","80,000"],["Trustee remuneration — variable (5/105 of 3,920,070)","186,670"],["Other expenses (per the solution's own figure, \"440,000 − 300,000 disallowed\")","140,000"],["Fixed annuity (Kemmy)","120,000"],["**Total deductions**","**(526,670)**"],["**Computed income**","**3,733,400**"],["Less: discretionary payments (per the solution's own equal split)","(720,000)"],["**Remainder of computed income**","**3,013,400**"]]}},{"note":"Variable trustee remuneration: 5/105 × (4,260,070 − 140,000 − 80,000 − 120,000) = 5/105 × 3,920,070 = N186,670."},{"table":{"align":"lrrr","head":["(b) Due to each beneficiary","Kemmy","Busayo","Samuel"],"rows":[["Discretionary payment (per the solution's equal-split figures)","240,000","240,000","240,000"],["Share of distributable income (half of 3,013,400 = 1,506,700, split equally 3 ways)","502,233.34","502,233.33","502,233.33"],["Fixed annuity","120,000","—","—"],["**Due to beneficiary**","**862,233.34**","**742,233.33**","**742,233.33**"]]}},{"note":"Two clear internal-arithmetic slips in the source's own printed table were corrected here for consistency with its own totals: the per-beneficiary distributable-income split was printed with a digit transposition (\"502,223.33\" for two beneficiaries, which does not reconcile against the stated 1,506,700 total — corrected to \"502,233.33\"), and the trustee's own retained half was printed as \"1,506,750\" instead of the arithmetically exact half, N1,506,700."}],"src":"Chapter 18.17","sec":"18.8"}]}},{"n":19,"t":"Loss Relief","brief":"How a business loss actually gets used: the two relief methods and who each is available to, the detailed statutory rules already introduced in earlier chapters — condensed here since the real substance of this chapter is applying them through fully worked, multi-year computations spanning ordinary carry-forward into a recovery year, a change of accounting date, and cessation of business, plus the interaction between unrelieved losses and unutilised capital allowance.","outcomes":["Understand what constitutes loss relief","Identify the basis for granting loss relief","Know the rules guiding claims for loss relief","Explain the treatment of losses under commencement, change of accounting date, and cessation of business"],"secs":[{"n":"19.2","t":"Introduction","b":[{"key":"A company makes a **loss** where its allowable operating expenses exceed its total income/earnings. A company making a trading loss may relieve it by **carrying it forward** for set-off against assessable profits of subsequent assessment years, starting from the year **after** the loss occurred."}]},{"n":"19.3","t":"Types of loss reliefs","b":[{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["","Current year loss relief (s.28(3) NTA)","Carry-forward loss relief (s.27(6) NTA)"],"rows":[["Available to","Individuals **only**.","Both individuals **and** companies."],["Set off against","Loss from one income source may be relieved against **other sources**.","Only against profit from the **same source** the loss originally arose in."],["Approval needed?","A **written claim**, in the prescribed form, is required (s.31 NTA).","No approval required before relief is granted."]]}},{"h4":"19.3.1  Current year loss relief — section 28(3) of NTA"},{"key":"This is one of the methods of relieving losses. It is applicable to **individuals only**. In this case, **losses incurred from a particular source of income can be relieved against other sources**. In order to enjoy this relief, **a written claim must be made** in such form as the relevant tax authority may prescribe (s.31 NTA)."},{"h4":"19.3.2  Carry forward loss relief — section 27(6) of NTA"},{"key":"This relief is available **only against profits from the same source of income where the loss originally occurred**. Carry forward loss relief **does not require approval** before the relief is granted. It applies to **both individuals and corporate bodies**."}]},{"n":"19.4","t":"Rules on loss relief","b":[{"h4":"19.4.1  Individuals — section 28(3) of NTA"},{"p":"In order to arrive at the **total income of an individual**, the loss to be deducted is:"},{"ol":["the amount of a loss incurred by the individual **during the year or preceding year of assessment** in a trade, business, profession or vocation; and","the amount of loss incurred **on the disposal of a chargeable asset**,"]},{"p":"provided that:"},{"ol":["**in no circumstances shall the aggregate loss deductions from income exceed the amount of that loss**;","the loss shall be deducted **as far as possible from the assessable profit** of a trade, business, profession or vocation **of the first year of assessment after that in which the loss was incurred**, and in subsequent years **until the loss is fully recouped**;","the loss incurred during any year of assessment shall be computed **in accordance with the basis period** used in ascertaining the assessable profits and income; and","any loss incurred in any period from sales, disposal or any other transaction in **digital or virtual assets** shall only be deductible **against the profit or gain from digital or virtual assets**."]},{"h4":"19.4.2  Companies — section 27(6) of NTA"},{"p":"The amount of loss to be deducted in ascertaining the **total profits of a company** shall be subject to the following conditions:"},{"ol":["**in no circumstances shall the aggregate loss deductions from the assessable profits or income exceed the amount of that loss**;","the loss **can only be deducted from the trade or business in which the loss was incurred**;","the loss shall be deducted **to the extent possible from the amount of the assessable profits of the first year of assessment after that in which the loss was incurred**, and in subsequent years **until the loss is fully recouped**; and","the loss incurred during any year of assessment shall be computed **in accordance with the basis period** used in ascertaining the assessable profits and income."]},{"warn":"Notwithstanding the provisions of this subsection or any provision of the Act, any loss incurred in any period from sales, disposal or any other transaction in **digital assets** shall only be deductible in determining the profits from the business relating to **digital or virtual assets**."},{"key":"Set the two lists side by side and the one real difference stands out. For an **individual**, the current-year loss may be relieved **against other sources of income** (§19.3.1). For a **company**, the loss \"can only be deducted from the trade or business in which the loss was incurred\" — there is **no cross-source relief**. Everything else is common: the loss can never be relieved more than once, it is relieved from the **first** year of assessment after it arose and onwards until recouped, it is computed on the **basis period**, and **digital-asset losses are ring-fenced** to digital-asset profits."}]},{"n":"19.5","t":"Treatment of losses under commencement, change of accounting date, and cessation of business","b":[{"eg":{"t":"Illustration 19.1 — loss carried forward through a recovery year, interacting with unutilised capital allowance (RUBY Limited)","q":[{"p":"RUBY Limited has sustained losses for years, expecting a good recovery in 2026. Adjusted results: YE 31 Dec 2024, loss N450,000; YE 31 Dec 2025, loss N520,000; YE 31 Dec 2026 (projected), profit N700,000. Capital allowances: AY2025 N37,500; AY2026 N32,000; AY2027 N45,000. The company achieved a minimum turnover of N150,000,000 each year (so it is not a small company). **Required**: compute the income tax liabilities for the relevant assessment years."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["RUBY Limited — assessment years 2025-2027","N"],"rows":[["**AY2025** (basis period 1/1/24-31/12/24)",""],["Loss for the year","(450,000)"],["Add: capital allowance for the year","(37,500)"],["**Unrelieved loss and capital allowance c/f**","**(487,500)**"],["Companies income tax payable","Nil"],["Tertiary education tax payable (3% of adjusted profit)","Nil"],["**AY2026** (basis period 1/1/25-31/12/25)",""],["Loss for the year","(520,000)"],["Add: unrelieved loss b/f","(450,000)"],["Subtotal","(970,000)"],["Add: capital allowance for the year (32,000) + b/f (37,500)","(69,500)"],["**Unrelieved losses and capital allowances c/f**","**(1,039,500)**"],["Companies income tax payable","Nil"],["Tertiary education tax payable (3% of adjusted profit)","Nil"],["**AY2027 — recovery year** (basis period 1/1/26-31/12/26)",""],["Profit for the year","700,000"],["Less: losses brought forward","(970,000)"],["Subtotal","(270,000)"],["Less: capital allowances — for the year (45,000) + b/f (69,500)","(114,500)"],["**Net unrelieved position**","**(384,500)**"],["Companies income tax payable","Nil"],["Federal development levy payable (4% of adjusted profit)","28,000"]]}},{"note":"Even in the recovery year, CIT remains Nil — the N700,000 profit is fully absorbed by brought-forward losses and capital allowances, leaving a net negative position. But the Federal Development Levy (4%) is computed on the year's RAW adjusted profit before loss/capital-allowance relief (4% × N700,000 = N28,000), not on the post-relief chargeable profit — this levy is not shielded by historic losses the way CIT is."},{"warn":"This illustration labels the supplementary levy \"Tertiary education tax\" (3%) in the loss years and \"Federal development levy\" (4%) only in the recovery year — the source itself does not explain this naming/rate switch. The most plausible reading, consistent with this Study Text's own Chapter 23 being specifically titled \"Federal Development Levy\" (a new NTA 2025 provision), is that Federal Development Levy supersedes the older Tertiary Education Tax under the new Act — but this inference is not explicitly confirmed in the extracted text, and should be checked against Chapter 23's own treatment once available."}]}},{"eg":{"t":"Illustration 19.2 — a change of accounting date","q":[{"p":"RAKA Limited, long in business producing beverages, was integrated into a group using a 31 December accounting year-end. Effective 30 June 2026, it changed its own year-end to align with the group. Adjusted profits: YE 31 Dec 2024, loss N3,000,000; YE 31 Dec 2025, profit N2,700,000; period ended 30 June 2026, profit N1,400,000. Capital allowances agreed: AY2025 N180,000; AY2026 N240,000; AY2027 N300,000. **Required**: compute the assessable profits for the relevant years of assessment."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Year of assessment","Basis period","Assessable profit/(loss), N"],"rows":[["2025","1/1/2024 – 31/12/2024","(3,000,000)"],["2026","1/1/2025 – 31/12/2025","2,700,000"],["2027","1/1/2026 – 30/6/2026","1,400,000"]]}},{"note":"Because the new 30 June year-end immediately follows the prior accounting period's 31 December close, this particular change of date creates neither a gap nor an overlap in basis periods — each period simply becomes its own assessment year's basis period in strict sequence, with no special s.23 apportionment needed. The cumulative net position across all three years is a profit of N1,100,000 (−3,000,000 + 2,700,000 + 1,400,000)."}]}},{"eg":{"t":"Illustration 19.3 — cessation of business and the net terminal loss (BEBE Limited)","open":true,"q":[{"p":"**BEBE Limited**, a company located in Kogi State, commenced operations on **1 November 2020**. The accounting year end was **30 September**."},{"p":"As a result of stiff government policy on the importation of rice, which the company deals with, the going concern of the business became threatened and the Board of Directors decided to **cease operations on 31 December 2025**."},{"p":"The adjusted profits/(loss) for the relevant periods are as follows:"},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Period","N"],"rows":[["Period to 30 September 2022","2,100,000"],["Year ended 30 September 2023","2,400,000"],["Year ended 30 September 2024","3,640,000"],["Year ended 30 September 2025","6,300,000"],["Period to 31 December 2025","(500,000)"]]}},{"p":"You are provided with the following additional information: **(i)** bad debt in the sum of **N120,000**, which was written off in the 2023 assessment year, was **recovered in October 2024**; **(ii)** a sum of **N20,000** was spent to recover the debt; **(iii)** an expenditure of **N350,000** incurred in assessment year 2023, though considered in the statement of profit or loss, **was not paid until August 2025**."},{"p":"On cessation, the Revenue Authority intended to conduct a **back-duty investigation** and the taxpayer was duly informed. As a Tax Consultant, you have been invited to ascertain the assessable profits for the periods covered from the commencement of trade to the cessation of business."},{"p":"**You are required to: (a)** explain **three** reasons why a business may cease trading; **(b)** compute the net terminal adjusted result for the relevant assessment year."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  Possible reasons why a business could cease trading"},{"ol":["**government legislation** — the Federal Government may declare the activities of a business illegal, which may lead to the end of such business;","**permanent dearth of, or inability to source, raw materials**;","**loss of a major market** due to stiff competitors or economic prices;","**inability to settle the debts** owed by the company;","**loss of key personnel** with technical know-how;","**agreement by the management** of the company;","**liquidity problems**;","**mismanagement of funds**;","complying with the **order of a Court**, through an application of creditors or debenture holders of the company;","**failing objectives** — continuous decline in profits;","**natural disasters** — for example fire, earthquake or flood; and","**death of all shareholders**, etc."]},{"note":"The question asks for **three**. Give three and stop — but choose three that fit the facts: BEBE Limited ceased because of **government legislation** on rice importation, with the consequent **loss of its market**, and the question says its **going concern was threatened**, which points to liquidity. Reasons drawn from the scenario score better than a generic list."},{"h4":"(b)  Computation of net terminal adjusted loss, 2026 assessment year"},{"stmt":{"t":"BEBE Limited — computation of net terminal adjusted loss","rows":[["The net terminal loss was","","(500,000)","@tt"]]}},{"p":"**Notes to the computation:**"},{"ol":["bad debts recovered in **October 2024** must have been captured in the relevant accounting year;","the amount spent in recovering the debt must also have been captured in the **same accounting year**;","the expenditure of **N350,000 was actually passed through the books before the year of cessation**; and","any **loss sustained in the year of cessation is deemed lost and cannot be recovered**."]},{"p":"Given the fact that the transactions occurred **before the date of cessation**, that informed the decision to **ignore them** in the computation of the net terminal adjusted loss."},{"key":"This illustration is a trap dressed up as a computation. Three pieces of adjustment data are supplied — a bad debt recovered, the cost of recovering it, and an expense paid late — and **none of them changes the answer**, because all three were already reflected in the accounting periods they belong to. The required figure is simply the **adjusted loss of the terminal period, N500,000**. The marks are for recognising that the data is already accounted for, and for saying so."},{"warn":"Note the hard consequence in note (iv): a **loss sustained in the year of cessation is deemed lost**. There is no later year of assessment to carry it into, and the carry-forward rules at §19.4 relieve a loss only against the profits of **subsequent** years of the same trade. So BEBE Limited gets **no relief at all** for the N500,000 — which is exactly why terminal-loss planning matters in practice, and why the examiner raises the back-duty investigation in the same breath."},{"note":"Why the **2026** assessment year: the terminal period runs to 31 December 2025, and on cessation the assessable profit of the year in which the trade ceases is that of the period from the end of the last basis period to the date of cessation (see §15.8.3). That period ends in 2025 and so falls to be assessed in 2026."}]}}]},{"n":"19.6","t":"Chapter review","b":[{"p":"This chapter defined what constitutes a loss for a company in trade or business, and provided guidelines on loss relief rules — explaining how they are applied, through detailed worked examples."}]},{"n":"19.7","t":"Worked examples — study text questions and suggested solutions","b":[{"p":"The study text sets **three questions** at §19.7.1 — two theory questions and a four-year loss-relief computation — with its own suggested solutions at §19.7.2. All three are reproduced here in full, with the book's answers, every figure independently recomputed, and added explanation."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 19.7.1, Question 1","t":"Current year loss relief and carry forward loss relief","open":true,"q":[{"p":"A company makes a loss from its trade or business when its allowable operating expenses exceed its total income or earnings. A company that makes a loss from its trade or business is entitled to relieve such loss by **carrying it forward** for set-off against the assessable profits of subsequent assessment years, commencing from the year following that in which the loss occurs."},{"p":"**Required:** Explain the following: **(a)** current year loss relief; **(b)** carry forward loss relief."}],"a":[{"h4":"1(a)  Current year loss relief — section 28(3) of NTA"},{"p":"This is one of the methods of relieving losses. It is applicable to **individuals only**. In this case, **losses incurred from a particular source of income can be relieved against other sources**. In order to enjoy this relief, **a written claim must be made** in such form as the relevant tax authority may prescribe (s.31 NTA)."},{"h4":"1(b)  Carry forward loss relief — section 27(6) of NTA"},{"p":"This relief is available **only against profits from the same source of income where the loss originally occurred**. Carry forward loss relief **does not require approval** before the relief is granted. It applies to **both individuals and corporate bodies**."},{"key":"Three contrasts carry the marks, and an answer should draw all three. **Who can claim:** current-year relief is for **individuals only**; carry-forward relief is for **both** individuals and companies. **What it can be set against:** current-year relief reaches **other sources of income**; carry-forward relief is confined to the **same source**. **What formality is needed:** current-year relief requires a **written claim** under s.31; carry-forward relief is automatic and needs **no approval**."},{"warn":"A numbering defect in the book's printed solution, flagged rather than silently corrected: the two parts of the answer are labelled **\"(a)\" and \"(c)\"** — there is no \"(b)\". The second part answers the question's part (b), as its subject matter makes plain."},{"note":"Note the mismatch between the question's preamble and its requirement. The preamble describes a **company** carrying a loss forward, but part (a) then asks about **current year** relief, which a company cannot claim at all. Say so: that current-year relief is unavailable to the company in the preamble is itself the point of the contrast."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 19.7.1, Question 2","t":"The rules governing a claim for loss relief, and its advantages","open":true,"q":[{"p":"There are specified rules to be taken into consideration in the application of loss relief. **Required:** **(a)** Explain the rules governing the claim for loss relief. **(b)** Explain the major advantages of loss relief to a taxpayer."}],"a":[{"h4":"2(a)(i)  Individuals — section 28(3) of NTA"},{"p":"In order to arrive at the total income of an individual, the loss to be deducted is: **the amount of a loss incurred by the individual during the year or preceding year of assessment** in a trade, business, profession or vocation; and **the amount of loss incurred on the disposal of a chargeable asset**. Provided that:"},{"ol":["**in no circumstances shall the aggregate loss deductions from income exceed the amount of that loss**;","the loss shall be deducted **as far as possible from the assessable profit** of a trade, business, profession or vocation **of the first year of assessment after that in which the loss was incurred**, and in subsequent years **until the loss is fully recouped**;","the loss incurred during any year of assessment shall be computed **in accordance with the basis period** used in ascertaining the assessable profits and income; and","any loss incurred in any period from sales, disposal or any other transaction in **digital or virtual assets** shall only be deductible **against the profit or gain from digital or virtual assets**."]},{"h4":"2(a)(ii)  Companies — section 27(6) of NTA"},{"p":"The amount of loss to be deducted in ascertaining the total profits of a company shall be subject to the following conditions:"},{"ol":["**in no circumstances shall the aggregate loss deductions from the assessable profits or income exceed the amount of that loss**;","the loss **can only be deducted from the trade or business in which the loss was incurred**;","the loss shall be deducted **to the extent possible from the amount of the assessable profits of the first year of assessment after that in which the loss was incurred**, and in subsequent years **until the loss is fully recouped**; and","the loss incurred during any year of assessment shall be computed **in accordance with the basis period** used in ascertaining the assessable profits and income."]},{"p":"Notwithstanding the provisions of this subsection or any provision of the Act, any loss incurred in any period from sales, disposal or any other transaction in **digital assets** shall only be deductible in determining the profits from the business relating to **digital or virtual assets**."},{"h4":"2(b)  The major advantage of loss relief to a taxpayer"},{"p":"The major advantage of a loss relief to a taxpayer is the consequential **reduction in the amount of assessable profit** of a year or future years. This has a major impact on the **total tax payable**, with a corresponding **cash flow advantage** for a taxpayer."},{"key":"Part (b) is worth only a line or two but has two distinct limbs, and candidates routinely give only one: the **reduction in tax**, and the **cash flow advantage** of paying less tax (or none) in the relief years. A third worth adding is that loss relief **matches tax to economic performance over the life of a business** rather than taxing profitable years in isolation — the reason the relief exists at all."},{"note":"The **digital and virtual asset ring-fence** appears in both lists and is new law worth remembering: a loss on digital assets can only ever be relieved against **profits from digital assets**, never against trading or other income. It is an exception to the individual's otherwise generous cross-source relief at §19.3.1."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 19.7.1, Question 3","t":"ZORO Nigeria Limited — income tax payable across four assessment years","open":true,"q":[{"p":"**ZORO Nigeria Limited** was incorporated on **1 February 2018** and commenced business on **1 May 2018**. The company makes up its accounts to **31 December** each year. The following additional information is provided:"},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["(i) Adjusted profit/(loss) for","N'000"],"rows":[["Year ended 31 December 2022","(80,000)"],["Year ended 31 December 2023","40,000"],["Year ended 31 December 2024","20,000"],["Year ended 31 December 2025","50,000"]]}},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["(ii) Capital allowances for each year of assessment","N'000"],"rows":[["2023","5,000"],["2024","3,500"],["2025","2,700"],["2026","2,500"]]}},{"p":"For each of the years, the **turnover** of the company was **N101,300,000**."},{"p":"**Required:** Compute the income tax payable for the relevant years of assessment."}],"a":[{"note":"The company makes up accounts to 31 December and is assessed on the **preceding year basis**, so the year ended 31 December 2022 is assessed in **2023**, and so on to the year ended 31 December 2025, assessed in **2026**. All figures below are in **N'000**."},{"stmt":{"t":"ZORO Nigeria Limited — computation of income tax payable (N'000)","rows":["2023 assessment year",["Adjusted loss","","(80,000)","@ind"],["Loss relieved","","Nil","@ind"],["Unrelieved loss carried forward","","(80,000)","@ind"],["Capital allowance for the year","5,000","","@ind"],["Capital allowance utilised","Nil","","@ind"],["Unutilised capital allowance carried forward","5,000","","@ind"],["Total profit","","Nil","@ind"],["Companies income tax @ 30%","","Nil","@ind"],["Tertiary education tax","","Nil","@ind"],"@gap","2024 assessment year",["Adjusted profit","","40,000","@ind"],["Loss brought forward","","(80,000)","@ind"],["Loss carried forward","","(40,000)","@ind"],["Capital allowance brought forward","5,000","","@ind"],["Capital allowance for the year","3,500","","@ind"],["Unutilised capital allowance carried forward","8,500","","@ind"],["Total profit","","Nil","@ind"],["Companies income tax @ 30% of total profit","","Nil","@ind"],["Tertiary education tax @ 2.5% of assessable profit","","1,000","@ind"],"@gap","2025 assessment year",["Adjusted profit for the year","","20,000","@ind"],["Unrelieved loss brought forward","","(40,000)","@ind"],["Unrelieved loss carried forward","","(20,000)","@ind"],["Capital allowance for the year","2,700","","@ind"],["Unutilised capital allowance brought forward","8,500","","@ind"],["Unutilised capital allowance carried forward","11,200","","@ind"],["Total profit","","Nil","@ind"],["Companies income tax @ 30% of total profit","","Nil","@ind"],["Tertiary education tax @ 3% of adjusted profit","","600","@ind"],"@gap","2026 assessment year",["Adjusted profit","","50,000","@ind"],["Loss brought forward","","(20,000)","@ind"],["","","30,000","@ind"],["Capital allowance for the year","2,500","","@ind"],["Unutilised capital allowance brought forward","11,200","","@ind"],["Capital allowance utilised","13,700","(13,700)","@ind"],["Total profit","","16,300","@t"],["Companies income tax @ 30% of total profit","","4,890","@ind"],["Tertiary education tax @ 3% of adjusted profit","","1,500","@tt"]]}},{"h4":"How the figures follow"},{"ol":["**2023** — a loss year. There is no profit to relieve the loss against, so the whole **N80,000** is carried forward, and the year's **N5,000** capital allowance is likewise unutilised and carried forward. Total profit is nil, so **no CIT and no tertiary education tax** arise — the education tax is charged on **assessable profit**, and there is none.","**2024** — the adjusted profit of N40,000 is **entirely absorbed** by the N80,000 loss brought forward, leaving **N40,000** of loss still to carry forward. Because total profit is nil, the capital allowances are again unutilised and accumulate to **N8,500**. CIT is nil, **but tertiary education tax is still payable** — 2.5% × N40,000 = **N1,000**.","**2025** — the same pattern: N20,000 of profit against N40,000 of loss leaves **N20,000** to carry forward; capital allowances accumulate to **N11,200**; CIT nil; education tax 3% × N20,000 = **N600**.","**2026** — the recovery year. N50,000 of adjusted profit absorbs the last **N20,000** of loss, leaving **N30,000**, against which the whole accumulated capital allowance of N2,500 + N11,200 = **N13,700** is set, giving a total profit of **N16,300**. CIT = 30% × N16,300 = **N4,890**; education tax = 3% × N50,000 = **N1,500**."]},{"key":"The single most important feature of this answer is that **tertiary education tax is payable in years when companies income tax is not**. CIT is charged on **total profit** — after loss relief and capital allowances — which is nil in 2024 and 2025. Education tax is charged on **assessable profit** — before both — so it is payable on the full N40,000 and N20,000. A company with brought-forward losses large enough to wipe out its CIT still writes a cheque for education tax."},{"note":"Note also that **capital allowances cannot create or increase a loss**. They are set against profit only, so in each nil-profit year the allowance is carried forward in full rather than deducted. By 2026 four years of allowances have accumulated to N13,700, and because that is less than the N30,000 of profit available, all of it is absorbed at once. Had the accumulated allowance exceeded the profit, the excess would have carried forward again."},{"note":"The **turnover of N101,300,000** is given for a reason: it is **above the N100,000,000 small-company threshold**, so ZORO is **not** exempt from companies income tax and the 30% rate applies. Had turnover been N100,000,000 or less, the company would have been exempt and — as Question 3 of Chapter 16 shows — its capital allowances would have been **deemed utilised** rather than carried forward."},{"warn":"Two presentational slips in the book's printed solution, flagged rather than silently corrected. **(1)** The 2024 line reading \"**Utilised** capital allowance c/f 8,500\" should read **unutilised** — nothing was utilised that year, as the nil total profit shows. **(2)** The tertiary education tax is applied at **2.5%** in 2024 but at **3%** in 2025 and 2026. The rate was increased to 3%, so the change is not in itself an error, but the book does not say when the increase takes effect; if an examination question does not state the rate, state the rate you are using and why."}]}}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Federal Development Levy (recovery-year illustration)","tex":"\\text{Levy} = 4% \\times \\text{Adjusted profit before loss/CA relief}"},{"lb":"Unrelieved loss and capital allowance carried forward","tex":"\\text{c/f} = \\text{Loss b/f} + \\text{Loss for the year} + \\text{Capital allowance b/f} + \\text{Capital allowance for the year}"}],"focus":"This chapter is really about APPLYING the loss-relief rules across multi-year scenarios, not re-learning the statutory text itself (already covered in Chapters 13/15/18) — the skill worth practising is tracking a running \"unrelieved loss and capital allowance carried forward\" balance across successive years, applying it against the first available profit, and recognising that the supplementary levy (whichever name/rate a given illustration uses) is computed on the RAW adjusted profit for the year, not the post-relief chargeable figure — so a company can owe zero CIT in a recovery year yet still owe a real cash levy amount. Also keep the current-year-relief-is-individuals-only / carry-forward-relief-applies-to-both distinction sharp, since exam questions often test whether a company can use current-year relief at all (it cannot).","errors":["Assuming current year loss relief is available to companies — s.28(3) restricts it to INDIVIDUALS only; a company may only use carry-forward relief (s.27(6)).","Computing the supplementary levy (tertiary education tax / federal development levy) on the post-loss-relief chargeable profit — these illustrations consistently compute it on the RAW adjusted profit for the year, before any loss or capital allowance deduction, so it remains payable even in a year where CIT itself is fully relieved to nil.","Forgetting that unutilised capital allowance, like an unrelieved loss, carries forward and compounds year over year until there is finally sufficient profit to absorb it — a company can be carrying forward both an unrelieved loss balance AND a separate unutilised capital allowance balance simultaneously, and both must be tracked together.","Assuming a change of accounting date always requires complex basis-period apportionment — it only does so where the new and old accounting periods create a genuine gap or overlap; where the change aligns cleanly (as in RAKA Limited's example), each period simply becomes its own year's basis period in sequence.","Applying carry-forward loss relief across DIFFERENT trades or businesses — s.27(6) is explicit that a company's loss is deductible only from the trade/business it was incurred in, unlike current-year relief (individuals only), which may cross sources."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Which loss relief method is available ONLY to individuals, not companies?","o":["Carry-forward loss relief","Current year loss relief","Both are available to companies and individuals equally","Neither — companies get carry-forward, individuals get neither","Terminal loss relief"],"a":1,"w":"Current year loss relief (s.28(3) NTA) is applicable to individuals only — a loss from one income source may be relieved against other sources. Companies may only use carry-forward relief.","src":"Chapter 19.2","sec":"19.2"},{"q":"Under carry-forward loss relief for a company (s.27(6) NTA), against which profits may the loss be deducted?","o":["Any profit from any trade or business the company carries on","Only profit from the SAME trade or business in which the loss was incurred","Only profit earned in the same calendar year as the loss","Only profit of a connected/related company","Profit from digital or virtual assets only, regardless of the loss source"],"a":1,"w":"Section 27(6) NTA is explicit that a company's carry-forward loss can only be deducted from the trade or business in which it was originally incurred.","src":"Chapter 19.3","sec":"19.3"},{"q":"In RUBY Limited's recovery-year illustration, why does the company still owe the supplementary levy (Federal Development Levy) despite owing zero companies income tax?","o":["It is a data-entry error in the illustration","The levy is computed on the RAW adjusted profit before loss/capital-allowance relief, unlike CIT","The levy applies only to companies that owe no CIT","The levy replaces CIT entirely once losses are fully relieved","The levy is a penalty for having sustained losses in prior years"],"a":1,"w":"The levy is computed on the year's raw adjusted profit before loss/capital-allowance relief (4% × N700,000 = N28,000), so it remains payable even though CIT is fully relieved to nil by brought-forward losses and capital allowances.","src":"Chapter 19.4","sec":"19.4"},{"q":"To enjoy current year loss relief, what must an individual do, under s.31 of the NTA?","o":["Nothing — it is granted automatically","Make a written claim in the prescribed form","Obtain prior approval from the Minister of Finance","Wait 2 years before claiming","Pay an administrative fee equal to 1% of the loss"],"a":1,"w":"Section 31 NTA requires a written claim, in the form the relevant tax authority prescribes, to enjoy current year loss relief.","src":"Chapter 19.2","sec":"19.2"},{"q":"When a company changes its accounting date and the new period immediately follows the old one with no gap or overlap, how is the basis period for the transition year determined?","o":["It must always be apportioned using complex interval rules","The new (possibly short) period simply becomes that year's basis period in sequence","The company is exempted from tax for that transition year entirely","The old accounting date continues to apply for one further year","A double assessment is raised covering both the old and new periods together"],"a":1,"w":"Where a date change creates no gap or overlap, each period — including a shorter transition period — simply becomes its own assessment year's basis period in strict sequence, without needing the more complex interval-apportionment rules.","src":"Chapter 19.4","sec":"19.4"}],"theory":[{"q":"BEBE Limited, based in Kogi State, commenced operations on 1 November 2020 with a 30 September accounting year-end. Due to government policy on rice importation, the Board decided to cease operations on 31 December 2025. Adjusted results: period to 30 Sep 2022, N2,100,000; YE 30 Sep 2023, N2,400,000; YE 30 Sep 2024, N3,640,000; YE 30 Sep 2025, N6,300,000; period to 31 Dec 2025, (N500,000). Additional information: (i) N120,000 bad debt written off in the 2023 assessment year was recovered in October 2024, at a cost of N20,000 to recover; (ii) N350,000 expenditure incurred in the 2023 assessment year, though reflected in the profit and loss account, was not actually paid until August 2025. (a) Explain THREE reasons why a business may cease trading. (b) Compute the net terminal adjusted result for the relevant assessment year.","marks":10,"a":[{"p":"**(a)** Any three of: government legislation declaring the business's activities illegal; permanent inability to source raw materials; loss of a major market to competitors or economic pressures; inability to settle debts owed; loss of key technical personnel; management agreement to cease; liquidity problems; mismanagement of funds; complying with a court order (e.g. on a creditor/debenture-holder application); continuous decline in profits; natural disasters (fire, earthquake, flood); or death of all shareholders."},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["(b) Net terminal adjusted result — 2026 assessment year","N"],"rows":[["**Net terminal loss**","**(500,000)**"]]}},{"note":"None of the three additional-information items affect this computation: the bad debt recovery (October 2024) and its N20,000 recovery cost belong to the 2024 accounting year, not the cessation period; and the N350,000 expenditure (though not paid until August 2025) had already been passed through the books in the 2023 assessment year, when it was incurred, not when it was paid. Since all three items were already properly captured in their own relevant accounting years, they have no bearing on the terminal period's own result. The N500,000 terminal-period loss stands as computed — and, per the terminal-loss rule already established in Chapter 15, since this loss arose in the FINAL period of cessation with no future profit to relieve it against, it is deemed lost rather than carried forward."}],"src":"Chapter 19.5","sec":"19.4"},{"q":"ZORO Nigeria Limited was incorporated on 1 February 2018 and commenced business on 1 May 2018, making up accounts to 31 December each year. Adjusted profit/(loss): YE 31 Dec 2022, (N80,000,000); YE 31 Dec 2023, N40,000,000; YE 31 Dec 2024, N20,000,000; YE 31 Dec 2025, N50,000,000 (all figures in N'000 in the source, shown here in full). Capital allowances: AY2023 N5,000,000; AY2024 N3,500,000; AY2025 N2,700,000; AY2026 N2,500,000. The company's turnover was N101,300,000 each year. Compute the income tax payable for the relevant years of assessment.","marks":16,"a":[{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["2023 assessment year","N'000"],"rows":[["Adjusted loss","(80,000)"],["Loss relieved","Nil"],["Unrelieved loss c/f","(80,000)"],["Capital allowance for the year","5,000"],["Capital allowance utilised","Nil"],["Unutilised capital allowance c/f","5,000"],["Total profit","Nil"],["Companies income tax @ 30%","Nil"],["Tertiary education tax","Nil"]]}},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["2024 assessment year","N'000"],"rows":[["Adjusted profit","40,000"],["Loss brought forward","(80,000)"],["Loss carried forward","(40,000)"],["Capital allowance b/f (5,000) + for the year (3,500)","8,500"],["Total profit","Nil"],["Companies income tax @ 30% of total profit","Nil"],["Tertiary education tax @ 2.5% of assessable profit","1,000"]]}},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["2025 assessment year","N'000"],"rows":[["Adjusted profit for the year","20,000"],["Unrelieved loss b/f","(40,000)"],["Unrelieved loss c/f","(20,000)"],["Capital allowance for the year (2,700) + unutilised b/f (8,500)","11,200"],["Total profit","Nil"],["Companies income tax @ 30% of total profit","Nil"],["Tertiary education tax @ 3% of adjusted profit","600"]]}},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["2026 assessment year","N'000"],"rows":[["Adjusted profit","50,000"],["Loss brought forward","(20,000)"],["Subtotal","30,000"],["Capital allowance for the year (2,500) + unutilised b/f (11,200) = 13,700, utilised in full","(13,700)"],["**Total profit**","**16,300**"],["Companies income tax @ 30% of total profit","4,890"],["Tertiary education tax @ 3% of adjusted profit","1,500"]]}},{"note":"The 2024 assessment year applies a 2.5% tertiary education tax rate while 2025 and 2026 apply 3% — reflecting the real-world Tertiary Education Tax rate increase (from 2.5% to 3%) that took effect for accounting periods from 1 January 2021, which this illustration's year-by-year rates are consistent with, rather than being an error. In every year, the supplementary tax is computed on that year's own RAW adjusted profit, exactly as in the RUBY Limited illustration, regardless of the loss-relief position for CIT purposes."}],"src":"Chapter 19.7","sec":"19.4"}]}},{"n":20,"t":"Ascertainment of Assessable Profit","brief":"The bridge from accounting profit to tax profit for a company: who is chargeable to companies income tax, the small-company/large-company rate structure, the new minimum-15%-effective-tax-rate top-up rule, the full lists of chargeable and exempt income, the interest-deductibility thin-capitalisation restriction, business-restructuring tax treatment, and — the chapter's computational core — how assessable profit is determined at commencement, on a change of accounting date, and at cessation, through eight fully worked illustrations.","outcomes":["Identify the persons chargeable to companies income tax","Explain the types of companies based on turnover and applicable income tax rates","Explain effective tax rate","Identify and explain income, profits or gains chargeable to tax","Identify and explain income, profits or gains that are tax exempt","Identify and explain allowable deductions and deductions not allowed","Explain the tax implications of business restructuring","Ascertain the basis for computing assessable profits for trade or business","Compute assessable profit"],"secs":[{"n":"20.2","t":"Introduction","b":[{"p":"A company's accounting profit (revenue less cost of sales less overhead expenses) is an **accounting** profit, arrived at per accounting principles and the company's own policies — it **cannot** be used directly to compute tax payable. It must first be converted to **adjusted profit** (tax profit)."},{"key":"Adjusted profit = **net profit per accounts, less non-taxable income, plus disallowable expenses** — the standard pro-forma used throughout this Study Text."}]},{"n":"20.3","t":"Persons chargeable to companies income tax","b":[{"h4":"20.3.1  Definition of Nigerian company — section 147 of NTAA"},{"def":{"t":"Nigerian company (s.147 NTAA)","d":"any company formed/incorporated under Nigerian law; or any company whose effective place of management/control is Nigeria (the Minister may prescribe what constitutes this by regulation)."}},{"h4":"20.3.2  Chargeability to tax — section 5 of NTA"},{"p":"A company is charged to tax: in its own name; in the name of its principal officer/attorney/factor/agent/representative in Nigeria, in the same manner/amount the company itself would be charged; or in the name of its receiver/liquidator/administrator, as if none had been appointed."},{"h4":"20.3.3  Taxation of resident persons — section 6 of NTA"},{"ol":["a Nigerian company's profits are deemed to accrue in Nigeria **wherever** they arise, whether or not brought into/received in Nigeria;","where a Nigerian-controlled **foreign** company has not distributed profits in a year, the proportion attributable to the Nigerian parent that could have been distributed without business detriment is **construed as distributed** and included in the Nigerian company's own profits (a controlled-foreign-company rule);","where a non-resident subsidiary's (or multinational-group member's) income tax yields **less** than the prescribed minimum effective tax rate, the Nigerian parent must pay a **top-up amount** to bring that subsidiary's tax to the minimum rate; and","the NRS provides detailed implementation rules for the above."]},{"h4":"20.3.4  Taxation of non-resident persons — section 17 of NTA"},{"p":"A non-resident's Nigerian-accruing/derived income is chargeable. Chargeable-asset disposal gains are taxable where they relate to a Nigerian trade/business/profession/vocation, a Nigerian-located asset, or a deemed-Nigerian-located asset. Trade/business profits are taxable where the non-resident has a permanent establishment (PE) or significant economic presence (SEP) in Nigeria (to the extent attributable to it); where a Nigerian resident/PE pays for services furnished from outside Nigeria (except employee/teaching/foreign-PE-borne payments); or where payment is made for insurance premiums/risks insured from Nigeria. The full PE/SEP tests are already set out in Chapter 18."}]},{"n":"20.4","t":"Types of companies based on turnover and applicable income tax rates — section 56 of NTA","b":[{"table":{"align":"llll","head":["Classification","Threshold (gross turnover)","Tax rate","Remarks"],"rows":[["**Small company**","N100,000,000 and below","0%","Total fixed assets must be below N250,000,000 — a business providing **professional services** is never classified as small (s.147 NTA)."],["**Any other company**","Above N100,000,000","30%","May be reduced to **25%**, effective from a date the President determines by Order, on the National Economic Council's advice."]]}}]},{"n":"20.5","t":"Effective tax rate — section 57 of NTA","b":[{"key":"Where a company's **effective tax rate** (covered taxes as a percentage of net income) for a year is **below 15%**, the company must recompute and pay **additional tax** to bring its effective rate up to exactly 15%."},{"def":{"t":"Covered taxes","d":"companies income tax, petroleum profit tax, and hydrocarbon tax paid/payable, plus development levy and priority-sector tax credit."}},{"def":{"t":"Net income","d":"profit before tax as reported in the audited financial statements, excluding franked investment income and unrealistic gains/losses — for a life insurance company, excluding policyholders' gross income and investment income."}},{"warn":"This section is **silent** on deducting 5% of depreciation and personnel cost from net profit before tax in ascertaining the effective tax rate, even though international best practice (aligned with OECD rules) requires excluding these costs — doing so reduces the profit base subject to the minimum rate, softening the tax impact on hiring staff and building infrastructure."},{"h4":"Who this applies to"},{"ol":["a constituent entity of a Multinational Entity group with aggregate group turnover of **at least £750 million** (or equivalent); and","any other company with aggregate turnover of **N50,000,000,000 or more** in the financial year."]},{"note":"This does **not** apply to an approved free-zone enterprise's approved activities — except for sales within the customs territory, or where the entity itself is a qualifying MNE-group constituent. The Service may issue regulations and may **prescribe a higher threshold** for the N50bn limb."}]},{"n":"20.6","t":"Income, profits or gains chargeable to tax — section 4 of NTA","b":[{"p":"Income accruing in or derived from Nigeria, including:"},{"ol":["profits/gains from any trade/business/profession/vocation (however long carried on);","royalties, fees, rents or interest from a right granted for property use/exploitation/occupation;","dividends, premiums, charges or annuities;","fees, dues, allowances, or remuneration for services rendered;","discounts or rebates;","disposal of money or money instruments;","income/profits/gains from disposing of or lending securities;","prizes, winnings, honoraria, grants, awards, laurels, etc.;","profits/gains from disposing of property or fixed assets;","profits/gains from digital-asset transactions; and","any other income/profit/gain not falling within the above."]}]},{"n":"20.7","t":"Income, profits or gains that are tax exempt — section 162 of NTA","b":[{"p":"Exempt income:"},{"ol":["profits/gains from a statutory/registered friendly society, a registered co-operative society, an educational/religious/charitable body, or a registered trade union — in each case, **only** where not derived from a trade/business the body itself carries on;","a Federal/State/Local Government (and their MDAs/public institutions), except profits from trade/business or a trade/business instrumentality; and a government purchasing authority empowered to acquire commodities for export/redistribution;","dividend distributed by an authorised collective investment scheme;","dividend/rental income a REIT receives on behalf of shareholders, where ≥75% is distributed within 12 months of the relevant financial year — without exempting the shareholder's own tax on it, the REIT's own management-fee/other income, or a non-compliant REIT;","compensating payments qualifying as dividends/interest in a Regulated Securities Lending Transaction;","consular fees for a foreign State, and a consular officer's employment income — excluding trade/business/other-employment income, domestic-duty employees, or an officer ordinarily resident in Nigeria who is not a national of that State;","income exempt/remitted under the Diplomatic Immunities and Privileges Act;","pension funds/assets, and pension/gratuity/retirement benefits, under the Pension Reform Act;","wound/disability pensions to armed-forces/defence-organisation members, or persons injured by enemy action;","death gratuities or consolidated death/injury compensation;","redundancy lump sums and other capital-nature compensation for loss of employment;","gains on an angel investor/venture capitalist/private equity fund/accelerator/incubator disposing of a labelled startup's assets, held in Nigeria ≥24 months;","income from Federal/State government bonds;","emoluments of other-rank personnel in combat zones/hazardous areas/designated operations (non-emolument income remains taxable);","income from agricultural businesses (crop production, livestock, aquaculture, forestry, dairy, and other Thirteenth Schedule businesses), for the **first 5 years** of business;","dividend from investment in a wholly export-oriented business;","profits of a company engaged in sporting activities;","dividend/interest/rent/royalty from outside Nigeria, brought in through approved channels;","employment income where gross income is at or below the **National Minimum Wage**; and","wages/salaries of military officers."]},{"note":"**Not chargeable gains**: pension funds/assets approved under the Pension Reform Act; an individual's disposal of investment held under a national provident fund/retirement scheme; and gains on disposing of a valour/gallantry decoration acquired other than for money/money's worth consideration."}]},{"n":"20.8","t":"Deductions allowed and deductions not allowed","b":[{"h4":"20.8.1  Deductions allowed — section 20 of NTA"},{"key":"In ascertaining profit/loss, all expenses **wholly and exclusively incurred in producing the income** during the period are deductible, including:"},{"ol":["interest payable on debt employed to generate income (subject to excess-interest restrictions on connected-person loans);","rent/premiums for land/buildings occupied to generate income;","salary/wages/remuneration paid to employees, and the cost of employee benefits/allowances;","expenses for repair of premises/plant/machinery/fixtures, or renewal/repair/alteration of implements/utensils/articles employed;","expenditure establishing, preserving or defending title/rights over an asset;","contributions to an approved staff pension/provident/retirement fund (Pension Reform Act or similar);","proven damage to, or loss of, stock/inventory;","bad/doubtful debts incurred during trade/business (even if due before the basis period began) — excluding bad debts from related-party transactions, and subject to GAAP-consistent doubtful-debt estimation;","research and development expense for the period;","other expense from a previous period for the trade/business, or specifically for the profit-ascertainment period — expenditure within **6 years before** commencing business, which would have been deductible if incurred after commencement, is deemed incurred on the **first day** of trading;","dividends/mandatory distributions by an SEC-approved real estate investment company to shareholders;","compensating payments (qualifying as interest) by a lender to its approved agent/a borrower in a regulated securities lending transaction; and","expense on assistive devices/disability-related products (hearing aids, wheelchairs, braille materials)."]},{"p":"For bad/doubtful debts specifically: any amount already allowed in a previous period on the same debt is deducted first; and sums recovered on previously written-off/allowed debts are **added back** to profits in the recovery period."},{"note":"A liability that is waived, released or recovered is included in assessable profits/chargeable gains. An expense in a foreign currency is deductible only to the extent of its **Naira equivalent at the official CBN exchange rate** for the relevant date/period."},{"h4":"20.8.2  Deductible donations — section 163 of NTA"},{"ol":["the amount of any donation made in the period, to a fund/body/institution in Nigeria this section applies to, is deductible — whether the donation is of a **revenue or capital** nature;","this applies to donations made to: public funds; statutory bodies/institutions; religious/charitable/educational/scientific institutions established in Nigeria; bodies recognised under the Diplomatic Immunities and Privileges Act; or pandemic/natural-disaster/public-emergency interventions;","the taxpayer must provide documentary evidence of the donation to the tax authority;","the **total** deduction allowed for donations in a year cannot exceed **10% of profit before tax** for that year; and","for a non-cash donation, its value is the **lower** of market value at donation, or the consideration paid to acquire it."]},{"h4":"20.8.3  Deduction for research and development — section 164 of NTA"},{"ol":["notwithstanding s.20, the amount incurred in a period on research and development is deductible;","the deduction cannot exceed **5% of turnover** for that year; and","where the R&D outcome is later sold/transferred for exploitation/commercialisation, the proceeds are taxed accordingly."]},{"h4":"20.8.4  Deductions not allowed — section 21 of NTA"},{"p":"No deduction is allowed for:"},{"ol":["capital repaid or withdrawn from the trade/business/profession/vocation;","expenditure of a capital nature;","domestic/private expense, or expenditure on assets not used for the trade/business/profession/vocation;","sums recoverable under insurance or an indemnity contract;","taxes on profit/income levied in Nigeria or elsewhere;","payments to an unapproved savings/widows-and-orphans/pension/provident/retirement fund, society or scheme;","depreciation/impairment of a fixed asset/investment, or an unrealised foreign-currency exchange difference;","sums reserved out of profits (subject to the 6-year pre-commencement rule above);","payments to a connected person inconsistent with the Service's Transfer Pricing Regulations;","expense incurred deriving income exempt from income tax;","expense deductible in determining chargeable gains under the Act;","penalty or fine imposed under any law;","tax or penalty borne on behalf of another person;","compensating payments (qualifying as dividends) by a borrower to its approved agent/a lender in a regulated securities lending transaction;","compensating payments (qualifying as interest/dividends) by an approved agent to a borrower/lender in such a transaction; or","expense on which VAT was due but not charged, or (for imports) applicable import duty/levy was not paid."]},{"h4":"Additional 50% deduction — employment incentive (s.162(3) NTA)"},{"key":"A company may claim an **additional 50% deduction**, in the relevant assessment years, for costs incurred in **any two calendar years from 2023 to 2025** on:"},{"ol":["wage awards, salary increases, or transport allowance/subsidy for a low-income worker, bringing their gross monthly remuneration up to **not more than N100,000** — an award/increase for an employee **already earning above N100,000/month** does **not** qualify; and","salaries of new employees representing a **net increase** in average new hires over 2023-2024 versus the average net employment of the 3 preceding years — provided those employees are **not involuntarily disengaged** within 3 years of employment."]},{"def":{"t":"Net employment","d":"total persons employed less total persons disengaged in the calendar year, whether voluntary or not."}},{"h4":"20.8.5  Expenses incurred in deriving exempt income — section 21(j) of NTA"},{"key":"Where a deductible expense generates **both** exempt and non-exempt income, only the portion relating to **assessable** income is allowed as a deduction."},{"eg":{"t":"Illustration 20.1 — apportioning a deductible expense between exempt and assessable income (Burukutu Limited)","q":[{"p":"Burukutu Limited generated a profit of N2,000,000 for the year ended 31 December 2025, and incurred deductible expenses of N250,000. Of the N2,000,000 profit, only N1,400,000 is assessable to tax. **Required**: compute how much of the expenses will be allowed for deduction."}],"a":[{"p":"The formula: A/(A+B) × C, where A = income assessable to tax, B = tax-exempt income, C = total available expenses."},{"p":"A = N1,400,000; B = N2,000,000 − N1,400,000 = N600,000; C = N250,000."},{"p":"Allowable expense = 1,400,000/(1,400,000 + 600,000) × 250,000 = 1,400,000/2,000,000 × 250,000 = **N175,000**."}]}},{"h4":"20.8.6  Restriction on the deductibility of interest — section 20(1)(a) of NTA and the Third Schedule"},{"key":"Where a Nigerian company (or a non-resident's Nigerian PE) incurs interest-type expenditure on debt issued by a **connected person**, the **excess interest** is a disallowable deduction. \"Excess interest\" is the amount of total interest paid/payable to a connected person **exceeding 30% of EBITDA** (earnings before interest, tax, depreciation and amortisation) for that accounting period."},{"note":"This restriction does **not** apply to a Nigerian subsidiary of a foreign company engaged in **banking or insurance**."},{"p":"Where interest expenditure is **not wholly deducted** in an assessment year, the undeducted portion is **carried forward** to subsequent assessment year(s), deductible against future profits to the extent the 30%-EBITDA test permits — but **not beyond 5 assessment years** after the year the excess was first computed."},{"h4":"Connected persons (s.201 NTA)"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Category","Connected where"],"rows":[["Individuals","Married, or relatives."],["Trust","The trustee and the settlor, or the settlor's spouse/relative."],["Partnership","A person related to (or the spouse/close relative of) their partner."],["Company","A person controlling (directly, via spouse, or close relative) a company; two companies under common control (directly or by connected persons acting separately); two or more persons jointly (or via a directed person) controlling a company; or two companies where one participates in the other's management/control/capital, or the same persons participate in both."],["Other cases","One may reasonably be expected to act on the other's directions/wishes; both may reasonably be expected to act on a third person's directions/wishes; or one has the practical ability to control the other's business decisions — **excluding** a mere employer-employee or client relationship (directly, or through a shared third party)."]]}},{"eg":{"t":"Illustration 20.2 — the 30%-of-EBITDA interest restriction (Darak Limited)","q":[{"p":"Darak Limited (incorporated 2 November 2021, commenced 4 April 2022) has a subsidiary, XYZ (UK) Limited, in Canada. For the year ended 31 December 2025: net profit N5,000,000, after deducting interest to other creditors N1,500,000, interest to XYZ (UK) Limited N1,750,000, and depreciation N1,300,000. N200,000 of the interest to other creditors related to a loan used to generate tax-exempt profits. **Required**: (a) compute the interest deductible for the relevant assessment year; (b) explain how any excess non-deductible interest is treated."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["(a) EBITDA and interest restriction","N"],"rows":[["Net profit","5,000,000"],["Add: interest deducted (1,500,000 + 1,750,000)","3,250,000"],["Add: depreciation","1,300,000"],["**EBITDA**","**9,550,000**"],["Interest paid to other creditors","1,500,000"],["Interest paid to XYZ (UK) Limited","1,750,000"],["Total interest expense","3,250,000"],["Less: interest for tax-exempt profit","(200,000)"],["**Interest qualifying for deduction**","**3,050,000**"],["Restriction — 30% of EBITDA (30% × 9,550,000)","2,865,000"]]}},{"p":"Deductible interest is the **lower** of 30% of EBITDA (N2,865,000) and the interest qualifying for deduction (N3,050,000) — i.e. **N2,865,000**."},{"p":"**(b)** The excess (N3,050,000 − N2,865,000 = N185,000) is **carried forward** and added to the following year's interest expense, to be tested against that year's own 30%-of-EBITDA restriction. This carry-forward is capped at **5 years** — here, up to the 2031 assessment year — applying the same rule each year to whatever excess was first computed."}]}}]},{"n":"20.9","t":"Tax implications of business restructuring — section 189 of NTA","b":[{"p":"Section 189 of the NTA sets out the tax implications of business restructuring in three distinct cases — a **merger** of two or more trades or businesses, a **sale or transfer that causes cessation** of a trade or business, and a **sale or transfer of a single business asset that does not cause cessation**."},{"h4":"(a)  In the case of a merger of two or more trades or businesses"},{"ol":["a **new trade or business shall not be deemed to have commenced** as a result of the merger, and the provisions of the Act relating to **cessation** of trade or business shall **not apply** to the trade or business that ceased as a result of the merger;","the provisions of Part VIII of Chapter Two of the Act — ascertainment of chargeable gains — as they relate to chargeable gains shall **not apply** to the assets transferred to the new or surviving trade or business as a result of the merger;","assets of the merging trades or businesses shall be deemed to have been transferred at the **residue of the qualifying capital expenditure** on the day following the merger;","the provisions of the First Schedule (capital allowance) shall apply on the **remaining useful life** of the asset transferred because of the merger;","**unutilised capital allowance** on the assets transferred **shall be available** for the use of the new or surviving trade or business;","**unabsorbed losses** of the merging entities **shall be available** to the surviving trade or business, provided that such losses were incurred by the merged trade or business; and","**taxes deducted at source** in respect of the merged trades or businesses **shall be available** to the merged trade or business."]},{"h4":"(b)  In the case of a sale or transfer of a trade or business which results in the cessation of a trade or business"},{"ol":["the provisions of Part V of Chapter Two of the Act as regards **cessation** of trade, business, profession or vocation **shall apply** to the trade or business that was sold or transferred;","for the purposes of the First Schedule (capital allowance), the asset sold or transferred shall be recognised **at the value at which it is sold or transferred**;","**unutilised capital allowance** on the assets sold or transferred **shall not be available** for use in the new or surviving trade or business;","**unabsorbed losses** of the old business **shall not be available** for use in the new or surviving trade, business, profession or vocation; and","**taxes deducted at source** from the old trade or business **shall not be available** for use by the new or surviving trade or business."]},{"h4":"(c)  In the case of a sale or transfer of a business asset which does not result in the cessation of the trade or business"},{"p":"Where the parties agreed to sell or transfer the asset for an amount **not exceeding the sum of the residue of the qualifying capital expenditure and the unutilised capital allowance** of the asset:"},{"ol":["capital allowance under the First Schedule shall apply to the **residue of the asset only**;","the **unutilised capital allowance** on the asset sold or transferred **shall be available for use by the buying** trade or business;","the trade or business that sold or transferred the asset **shall not claim any part** of the unutilised capital allowance pertaining to it; and","the provisions of Part VIII of Chapter Two as regards **chargeable gains shall not apply** to the asset sold or transferred under this paragraph."]},{"key":"The organising question is **whether tax history travels with the business**. On a **merger** it does — no cessation, no chargeable gains, assets carried over at tax written-down value, and unutilised capital allowances, unabsorbed losses and withholding tax credits all pass to the survivor. On a **sale or transfer causing cessation** it does not — cessation rules apply, assets are recognised at sale value, and allowances, losses and WHT credits are all lost. On a **sale of one asset with the business continuing**, the position is intermediate: no chargeable gain, and the unutilised allowance follows the asset to the **buyer**, with the seller claiming none."},{"note":"Do not confuse this with the **VAT** treatment of business restructuring at §7.29, which is a separate provision of the same section 189 dealing with whether a transfer of assets on a restructuring is a taxable supply. The rules above are the **income tax** consequences, and the two must be applied independently to the same transaction."}]},{"n":"20.10","t":"Basis of computing assessable profits for trade or business — section 22 of NTA","b":[{"h4":"20.10.1  Commencement of business — section 22(1) of NTA"},{"def":{"t":"Commencement date (s.201 NTA)","d":"the date of an entity's first transaction — the EARLIEST of the date it: begins marketing/advertising products/services; obtains a Nigerian regulatory operating licence; makes its first sale/purchase; executes its first trading contract post-incorporation; issues/receives its first invoice; delivers/receives its first goods consignment; or first renders services to customers."}},{"key":"The first year of assessment's assessable profit is the profit from the commencement date to the end of the **first accounting period**. For subsequent years, assessable profit is the profit of the accounting period **immediately preceding** the assessment year."},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Illustration","Facts","Result"],"rows":[["**20.3 — Duru Limited**","Commenced 1 March 2025; accounts to 31 October.","First assessment year is **2026**; profits assessed relate to 1/3/25 – 31/10/25."],["**20.4 — XYZ Limited**","Commenced 1 April 2025; accounts to 30 November.","AY2026: 1/4/25–30/11/25. AY2027: 1/12/25–30/11/26. AY2028: 1/12/26–30/11/27. AY2029: 1/12/27–30/11/28."]]}},{"eg":{"t":"Illustration 20.3 — the first assessment year and the profits assessed (Duru Limited)","open":true,"q":[{"p":"**Duru Limited** commenced business on **1 March 2025** and makes up accounts to **31 October** each year. **Required:** State the relevant assessment year and the profits to be assessed."}],"a":[{"p":"**Duru Limited.** The first assessment year is **2026**, and the profits to be assessed will relate to **1 March 2025 to 31 October 2025**."},{"key":"The first year of assessment is the year **following** the one in which the first accounting period ends, and the profits assessed are those of the **first accounting period itself** — from the commencement date to the first accounting year end. Here that period is the eight months 1 March to 31 October 2025, which ends in 2025, so it is assessed in **2026**."}]}},{"eg":{"t":"Illustration 20.4 — basis periods for the first four years of assessment (XYZ Limited)","open":true,"q":[{"p":"**XYZ Limited** commenced business on **1 April 2025** and makes up accounts to **30 November** each year. **Required:** Determine the basis periods for the first four years of assessment."}],"a":[{"p":"The basis periods of XYZ Limited for the first four years of assessment:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["YOA","Basis period"],"rows":[["2026","1/4/2025 – 30/11/2025"],["2027","1/12/2025 – 30/11/2026"],["2028","1/12/2026 – 30/11/2027"],["2029","1/12/2027 – 30/11/2028"]]}},{"key":"Only the **first** basis period is short — the eight months from commencement to the first accounting year end. From the second year onwards each basis period is the company's **own twelve-month accounting period**, immediately preceding the year of assessment. There is **no overlap and no gap**, which is the point of the commencement rules."},{"note":"Note that the **year of assessment is always the calendar year after the accounting period ends**: the period ending 30 November 2026 is assessed in 2027, and so on. A company with a non-December year end therefore never has a basis period that coincides with its year of assessment."}]}},{"p":"Where a company's first accounts cover a period **shorter or longer** than 12 months, assessable profits for the first accounting period are determined on a **pro-rata** basis."},{"eg":{"t":"Illustration 20.5 — pro-rating a first accounting period longer than 12 months (RAY Limited)","q":[{"p":"RAY Limited, incorporated December 2024, commenced business 1 October 2025. Its first financial statements covered 15 months (1 October 2025 – 31 December 2026), with an adjusted profit of N3,000,000. **Required**: ascertain the relevant basis periods and assessable profits."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"llr","head":["YOA","Basis period","Assessable profit (N)"],"rows":[["2026","1/10/25 – 31/12/25 (3 months)","600,000"],["2027","1/1/26 – 31/12/26 (12 months)","2,400,000"],["**Total**","**(15 months)**","**3,000,000**"]]}},{"note":"Pro-rated on a straight-line monthly basis: 3/15 × N3,000,000 = N600,000 for the first 3 months; 12/15 × N3,000,000 = N2,400,000 for the remaining 12."}]}},{"h4":"20.10.2  Change in accounting date — section 23 of NTA"},{"p":"Where a taxable person changes its accounting date, the basis period for the relevant assessment year runs from the day after the previous year's basis period ended, to the new accounting date — with subsequent years computed on the new accounting period. The tax authority must be notified **at least 30 days** before the usual return-filing due date."},{"eg":{"t":"Illustration 20.6 — a change of accounting date mid-stream","q":[{"p":"ABC Nigeria Limited (incorporated 3 October 2022, commenced 2 April 2023) changed its accounting date from 31 December to 31 March in 2026, to align with a key supplier. Adjusted profits: period to 31 Dec 2023, N24,500,000; YE 31 Dec 2024, N42,700,000; YE 31 Dec 2025, N63,500,000; period to 31 Mar 2026 (3 months), N31,300,000; projected YE 31 Mar 2027, N130,500,000. **Required**: compute the assessable profits for the relevant assessment years."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["YOA","Basis period","Assessable profit (N)"],"rows":[["2024","2 Apr 2023 – 31 Dec 2023","24,500,000"],["2025","1 Jan 2024 – 31 Dec 2024","42,700,000"],["2026","1 Jan 2025 – 31 Dec 2025","63,500,000"],["2027","1 Jan 2026 – 31 Mar 2026","31,300,000"],["2028","1 Apr 2026 – 31 Mar 2027","130,500,000"]]}}]}},{"h4":"20.10.3  Cessation of trade or business — section 24 of NTA"},{"p":"On permanent cessation, assessable profit is the amount from the start of that accounting period to the cessation date — tax payable **within 6 months** of cessation. A sum received/paid after cessation that would have been included/deducted had it occurred before that date is deemed received/paid on the **last day before cessation**, disclosed to the tax authority within **1 month**. For a **deceased individual**, the same deeming applies to the personal representative, as of the **last day before death**."},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Illustration","Facts","Result"],"rows":[["**20.7 — ARA Nigeria Ltd**","Ceased 31 March 2025 (accounting year-end 31 December).","AY2025 (PYB): basis 1/1/24–31/12/24, due 30 June 2025. AY2025 (cessation): basis 1/1/25–31/3/25, due 30 September 2025 (6 months after cessation) — BOTH fall in the same assessment year, since cessation occurred too early in 2025 for a separate following year to arise."],["**20.8 — same company, ceasing 31 August 2025 instead**","Same facts, later cessation date.","AY2025 (PYB): basis 1/1/24–31/12/24, due 30 June 2025. AY2026 (cessation): basis 1/1/25–31/8/25, due 28 February 2026 (6 months after cessation) — here the cessation period falls into the FOLLOWING assessment year, since it occurred later in the year."]]}},{"eg":{"t":"Illustration 20.7 — cessation: years of assessment and due dates of payment (ARA Nigeria Limited)","open":true,"q":[{"p":"**ARA Nigeria Limited**, incorporated in 1995, **ceased operation on 31 March 2025**, having been in business for over 25 years. The accounting year end of the company is **31 December** annually. **Required:** Determine the relevant years of assessment and the due dates of payment of tax."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["YOA","Basis period","Due date of payment of tax"],"rows":[["2025","1/1/24 – 31/12/24","**30 June 2025** (preceding year basis)"],["2025","1/1/25 – 31/3/25","**30 September 2025** (six months after the date of cessation)"]]}},{"key":"Two assessments fall in the **same year of assessment, 2025** — the ordinary preceding-year assessment on the year ended 31 December 2024, and the **terminal** assessment on the period from 1 January 2025 to the date of cessation. The terminal tax is due **six months from the date of cessation** (s.24(a)), not six months after an accounting year end, which is why its due date is 30 September 2025."}]}},{"eg":{"t":"Illustration 20.8 — the same facts, with cessation later in the year (ARA Nigeria Limited)","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Given the same scenario as Illustration 20.7, assume the company **ceased operation on 31 August 2025**."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["YOA","Basis period","Due date of payment of tax"],"rows":[["2025","1/1/24 – 31/12/24","**30 June 2025** (preceding year basis)"],["2026","1/1/25 – 31/8/25","**28 February 2026** (six months after the date of cessation)"]]}},{"key":"Compare this with Illustration 20.7 and the difference is the whole lesson. Ceasing on **31 March** put both assessments in **2025**; ceasing on **31 August** pushes the terminal assessment into **2026**, because the six months from cessation run past the end of 2025. The date of cessation therefore determines not only the due date but the **year of assessment** the terminal profits fall into — and with it which year's rates, losses and capital allowances apply."},{"note":"The due date of 28 February 2026 is six calendar months after 31 August 2025. Note that the terminal period here is the **eight months** 1 January to 31 August 2025, against the three months in Illustration 20.7."}]}},{"note":"The contrast between Illustrations 20.7 and 20.8 shows the same cessation rule producing different results purely based on how early/late in the calendar year cessation occurs relative to the ordinary assessment-year cycle."}]},{"n":"20.11","t":"Computation of assessable profit","b":[{"key":"A company's **assessable profit** is its adjusted profit (or part of it) assessed to companies income tax in an assessment year. Adjusted profit is computed on the accounts for whatever period they cover (normally 12 months); assessable profit is what is actually determined and assessed for an assessment year. A company's accounting year — the period its annual accounts cover — is usually 12 months, whether beginning/ending in the same calendar year or spanning two."}]},{"n":"20.12","t":"Chapter review","b":[{"p":"This chapter distinguished accounting profit/loss from taxable profit, including the adjustments needed to arrive at total profits from accounting profits. Clear examples of allowable/non-allowable expenses were given, along with classes of profit/income exempt from companies income tax, and the concepts underlying the bases of assessment."}]},{"n":"20.13","t":"Worked examples — study text questions and suggested solutions","b":[{"p":"The study text sets **three questions** at §20.13.1 — one theory question and two computations — with its own suggested solutions at §20.13.2. All three are reproduced here in full, with the book's answers, every figure independently recomputed, and added explanation."},{"note":"A numbering defect in the book, flagged rather than silently corrected: the first question carries **no number at all** — the requirement simply follows the preamble, and the next question is labelled \"(2)\". It is numbered 1 here, matching the solutions, which are correctly numbered (1), 2 and 3."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 20.13.1, Question 1","t":"Tax implications of business restructuring under section 189 of NTA","open":true,"q":[{"p":"Section 189 of NTA explains the tax implications of business restructuring in the case of: **(a)** a merger of two or more trades or businesses; **(b)** the sale or transfer of a trade or business; and **(c)** the sale or transfer of a business asset which does **not** result in the cessation of the trade or business."},{"p":"**Required:** Discuss the tax implications of business restructuring."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  In the case of a merger of two or more trades or businesses"},{"ol":["a **new trade or business shall not be deemed to have commenced** as a result of the merger, and the provisions of the Act relating to **cessation** of trade or business shall **not apply** to the trade or business that ceased as a result of the merger;","the provisions of Part VIII of Chapter Two of the Act — ascertainment of chargeable gains — as they relate to chargeable gains shall **not apply** to the assets transferred to the new or surviving trade or business as a result of the merger;","assets of the merging trades or businesses shall be deemed to have been transferred at the **residue of the qualifying capital expenditure** on the day following the merger;","the provisions of the First Schedule (capital allowance) shall apply on the **remaining useful life** of the asset transferred because of the merger;","**unutilised capital allowance** on the assets transferred **shall be available** for the use of the new or surviving trade or business;","**unabsorbed losses** of the merging entities **shall be available** to the surviving trade or business, provided that such losses were incurred by the merged trade or business; and","**taxes deducted at source** in respect of the merged trades or businesses **shall be available** to the merged trade or business."]},{"h4":"(b)  In the case of a sale or transfer of a trade or business which results in the cessation of a trade or business"},{"ol":["the provisions of Part V of Chapter Two of the Act as regards **cessation** of trade, business, profession or vocation **shall apply** to the trade or business that was sold or transferred;","for the purposes of the First Schedule (capital allowance), the asset sold or transferred shall be recognised **at the value at which it is sold or transferred**;","**unutilised capital allowance** on the assets sold or transferred **shall not be available** for use in the new or surviving trade or business;","**unabsorbed losses** of the old business **shall not be available** for use in the new or surviving trade, business, profession or vocation; and","**taxes deducted at source** from the old trade or business **shall not be available** for use by the new or surviving trade or business."]},{"h4":"(c)  In the case of a sale or transfer of a business asset which does not result in the cessation of the trade or business"},{"p":"Where the parties agreed to sell or transfer the asset for an amount **not exceeding the sum of the residue of the qualifying capital expenditure and the unutilised capital allowance** of the asset:"},{"ol":["capital allowance under the First Schedule shall apply to the **residue of the asset only**;","the **unutilised capital allowance** on the asset sold or transferred **shall be available for use by the buying** trade or business;","the trade or business that sold or transferred the asset **shall not claim any part** of the unutilised capital allowance pertaining to it; and","the provisions of Part VIII of Chapter Two as regards **chargeable gains shall not apply** to the asset sold or transferred under this paragraph."]},{"key":"The three scenarios are best learned as a single contrast — **does tax history travel with the business?** On a **merger** it does: no cessation, no chargeable gains, assets carried over at tax written-down value, and **unutilised capital allowances, unabsorbed losses and WHT credits all pass to the survivor**. On a **sale or transfer that causes cessation** it does **not**: cessation rules bite, assets are recognised at **sale value**, and allowances, losses and WHT credits are **all lost**. On a **sale of a single asset with the business continuing**, the treatment is in between — no chargeable gain, and the unutilised allowance **follows the asset to the buyer**, with the seller claiming none."},{"note":"Scenario (c) carries a condition easy to miss: it applies only where the price does **not exceed the residue of qualifying expenditure plus unutilised capital allowance**. Sell the asset for more than that and the paragraph does not apply, so the chargeable-gains provisions come back into play."},{"warn":"A numbering defect in the book's printed solution, flagged rather than silently corrected: its three limbs are introduced as an unlabelled \"in the case of a merger…\", then **\"(b)\"**, then **\"(c)\"** — the first has no letter, so the lettering does not match the question's own (a), (b), (c). They are lettered (a) to (c) above."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 20.13.1, Question 2","t":"D Limited — assessable profits on a change of accounting date","open":true,"q":[{"p":"**D Limited** changed its accounting date from **31 December to 30 June**. The following information is made available:"},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["","N"],"rows":[["12 months to 31/12/2025","5,288,000"],["6 months to 30/06/2026","3,320,000"],["12 months to 30/06/2027 — projected","7,250,000"]]}},{"p":"**Required:** Compute the assessable profits for the relevant assessment years."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"llr","head":["YOA","Basis period","Amount (N)"],"rows":[["2026","1/1/25 – 31/12/25","5,288,000"],["2027","1/1/26 – 30/06/26 (six months)","3,320,000"],["2028","1/07/26 – 30/06/27","7,250,000"],["","**Total**","**15,858,000**"]]}},{"key":"Section 23 of the NTA (§20.10.2) gives the rule applied here: on a change of accounting date, the basis period for the year of assessment in which the change falls runs **from the first day after the previous basis period to the new accounting date** — here 1 January to 30 June 2026, a **six-month** basis period — and subsequent years are computed on the **new** accounting period. There is no apportionment, no averaging and no choice of basis: the short period stands as its own basis period, which is why only six months' profit is assessed in 2027."},{"warn":"The study text's printed solution shows the 2028 assessable profit as **N7,200,000**, with a total of N15,808,000. The question states the projected profit for the 12 months to 30 June 2027 as **N7,250,000**, which is the figure used above, giving a total of **N15,858,000**. The book's own figures are internally consistent with its N7,200,000, so the discrepancy is in transcribing the question into the solution, a difference of N50,000."},{"note":"The company must also **notify the relevant tax authority of the change not later than 30 days before the usual due date for filing** its income tax returns (s.23(b) — see §20.10.2). A question about a change of date often carries a mark for that procedural point, which no computation reveals."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 20.13.1, Question 3","t":"KAO Bank Limited — agricultural loan interest exempted from tax","open":true,"q":[{"p":"The interest on loans relating to **primary agricultural production** included in the income of **KAO Bank Limited** for the year ended **31 December 2025** amounted to **N5 million**. The breakdown of the interest income based on the loan terms is as follows:"},{"table":{"align":"llrr","head":["Repayment period","Moratorium period","Interest rate p.a. (%)","Agric loan interest (N)"],"rows":[["5 years","24 months","15","2,500,000"],["3 years","6 months","20","800,000"],["2 years","18 months","14","700,000"],["5 years","6 months","35","1,000,000"],["","","","**5,000,000**"]]}},{"p":"The **base lending rate** in respect of the facilities is **21% p.a.**"},{"p":"**Required:** Compute the loan interest exempted from tax."}],"a":[{"p":"**KAO Bank Limited — loan interest exempted from tax for the year ended 31 December 2025 (assessment year 2026):**"},{"table":{"align":"llrrr","head":["","Moratorium period","Interest rate (%)","Agric loan interest (N)","Exempt interest income (N)"],"rows":[["(i)","24 months","15","2,500,000","2,500,000"],["(ii)","6 months","20","800,000","—"],["(iii)","18 months","14","700,000","700,000"],["(iv)","6 months","35","1,000,000","—"],["","","","**5,000,000**","**3,200,000**"]]}},{"key":"The exemption has **two cumulative conditions**, and a loan must satisfy **both**: the **moratorium must be not less than 12 months**, and the **interest rate must not exceed the base lending rate** at the time the loan was granted, refinanced or restructured — here **21%**. Loan (i) passes on 24 months and 15%; loan (iii) passes on 18 months and 14%. Loans (ii) and (iv) fail, so **N3,200,000 of the N5,000,000 is exempt** and the remaining N1,800,000 is taxable."},{"note":"Be more precise than the book's own note, which says \"(ii) & (iv) are not tax exempt as they fail to meet the two conditions as to moratorium period and base lending rate\". In fact **loan (ii) fails only one** condition — its moratorium of 6 months is too short, but its 20% rate is **below** the 21% base rate. **Loan (iv) fails both** — 6 months and 35%. Failing either is enough, so the conclusion is right, but say which condition each loan fails."},{"note":"The **repayment period** column is deliberate noise — the exemption says nothing about how long the borrower has to repay, only about the **moratorium** and the **rate**. The statutory provision is at §14.5.4(d), and it applies to a bank loan granted on or after **1 January 1997** to a person engaged in agricultural trade or business, the fabrication of local plant and machinery, or a Family Economic Advancement Programme cottage industry."}]}}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Adjusted profit","tex":"\\text{Adjusted profit} = \\text{Net profit per accounts} - \\text{Non-taxable income} + \\text{Disallowable expenses}"},{"lb":"Apportioning a deductible expense between exempt and assessable income","tex":"\\text{Allowable expense} = \\dfrac{A}{A + B} \\times C","nt":"A = income assessable to tax; B = tax-exempt income; C = total available expenses."},{"lb":"EBITDA (for the interest-restriction test)","tex":"\\text{EBITDA} = \\text{Net profit} + \\text{Interest deducted} + \\text{Depreciation and amortisation}"},{"lb":"Interest-deductibility restriction (connected-person debt)","tex":"\\text{Deductible interest} = \\min(\\text{Interest qualifying for deduction}, \\ 30% \\times \\text{EBITDA})"},{"lb":"Pro-rated assessable profit (first accounting period ≠ 12 months)","tex":"\\text{Assessable profit} = \\text{Total adjusted profit} \\times \\dfrac{\\text{Months in this basis period}}{\\text{Total months covered}}"}],"focus":"Two genuinely new mechanics deserve focused attention here, since everything else in this chapter largely restates earlier chapters: the 30%-of-EBITDA interest-deductibility restriction on connected-person debt (memorise the EBITDA build-up — net profit plus interest plus depreciation/amortisation — and that deductible interest is always the LOWER of the qualifying interest and the 30% cap, with any excess carried forward for up to 5 years), and the minimum 15% effective-tax-rate top-up rule (a company below 15% ETR must top itself up to exactly 15%, computed on net income that excludes franked investment income). For the basis-period illustrations, practise spotting whether a cessation date falls early or late enough in the calendar year to either share an assessment year with the ordinary preceding-year-basis assessment, or spill into the following one — this is a subtle timing judgment the RAY/ARA illustrations exist specifically to test.","errors":["Computing the interest-deductibility restriction as a flat 30% of NET PROFIT rather than 30% of EBITDA — EBITDA specifically adds back interest AND depreciation/amortisation to net profit first, producing a materially larger (and correct) restriction base.","Deducting all interest paid to a connected person without checking whether it exceeds 30% of EBITDA — only the EXCESS over that threshold is disallowed; interest below the threshold remains fully deductible as normal.","Treating the minimum-15%-effective-tax-rate rule as applying to every company — it is explicitly scoped to large multinational-group constituents (≥£750m group turnover) and very large standalone companies (≥N50 billion turnover), not ordinary businesses.","Assuming a company's first accounting period, if longer or shorter than 12 months, is assessed as a single lump sum in one assessment year — it must instead be PRO-RATED across the assessment years its basis periods actually fall into, on a straight-line monthly basis.","Assuming a cessation always creates its own separate assessment year — where cessation occurs early enough in a calendar year, the final stub period and the ordinary preceding-year-basis assessment for that SAME assessment year can both apply, rather than the stub period automatically becoming a new following year."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"A company with gross turnover of N80,000,000 and total fixed assets of N150,000,000, not providing professional services, is classified as what kind of company under s.56 of the NTA, and taxed at what rate?","o":["Any other company, taxed at 30%","Small company, taxed at 0%","Large company, taxed at 25%","Small company, taxed at 15%","Exempt company, taxed at 0% regardless of size"],"a":1,"w":"With turnover at or below N100 million and fixed assets below N250 million, and not being a professional-services business, this company qualifies as a small company, taxed at 0%.","src":"Chapter 20.3","sec":"20.3"},{"q":"Under the interest-deductibility restriction in the Third Schedule to the NTA, what is \"excess interest\"?","o":["Any interest paid to a connected person, in full","Interest paid to a connected person exceeding 30% of the company's EBITDA for the period","Interest paid at a rate above the CBN Monetary Policy Rate","Interest on any loan exceeding N10,000,000","Interest paid to a non-connected lender"],"a":1,"w":"Excess interest is the amount of total interest paid/payable to a connected person in excess of 30% of the Nigerian company's EBITDA for that accounting period.","src":"Chapter 20.8","sec":"20.8"},{"q":"For how many assessment years may disallowed excess interest expenditure be carried forward, under the Third Schedule to the NTA?","o":["2 years","3 years","5 years","7 years","Indefinitely, with no limit"],"a":2,"w":"Disallowed excess interest may be carried forward for up to 5 assessment years immediately following the year it was first computed.","src":"Chapter 20.8","sec":"20.8"},{"q":"Where a company's effective tax rate for a year is below 15%, what must it do, under s.57 of the NTA?","o":["Nothing — a lower rate is simply accepted","Recompute and pay additional tax to bring its effective tax rate up to exactly 15%","Cease operations in Nigeria","Automatically qualify for small-company status","Pay a flat penalty of N10,000,000 regardless of the shortfall"],"a":1,"w":"Section 57 NTA requires a company with an effective tax rate below 15% to recompute and pay additional tax to bring its effective rate up to exactly 15%.","src":"Chapter 20.4","sec":"20.4"},{"q":"A company's first accounting period runs for 15 months. How is its assessable profit for the relevant assessment years determined?","o":["The entire 15-month profit is assessed in a single assessment year","It is pro-rated on a straight-line monthly basis across the assessment years the basis periods fall into","Only the first 12 months are ever assessed; the remaining 3 months are permanently exempt","The company chooses which assessment year to allocate the full amount to","It is doubled to compensate for the extended period"],"a":1,"w":"Where a first accounting period is shorter or longer than 12 months, assessable profit is determined on a pro-rata (straight-line monthly) basis across the relevant assessment years.","src":"Chapter 20.10","sec":"20.10"}],"theory":[{"q":"D. Limited changed its accounting date from 31 December to 30 June. Adjusted profits: 12 months to 31/12/2025, N5,288,000; 6 months to 30/06/2026, N3,320,000; 12 months to 30/06/2027 (projected), N7,250,000. Compute the assessable profits for the relevant assessment years.","marks":8,"a":[{"warn":"The study text's own printed solution computes the final year using N7,200,000, not the N7,250,000 stated in the question — its own total (N15,808,000) only reconciles against N7,200,000 (5,288,000 + 3,320,000 + 7,200,000 = 15,808,000), not against the question's literal figure. This discrepancy could not be resolved from the extracted text; the table below follows the source's own solution figure for consistency with its own stated total."},{"table":{"align":"llr","head":["YOA","Basis period","Assessable profit (N)"],"rows":[["2026","1/1/25 – 31/12/25","5,288,000"],["2027","1/1/26 – 30/6/26 (6 months)","3,320,000"],["2028","1/7/26 – 30/6/27","7,200,000"],["**Total**","","**15,808,000**"]]}}],"src":"Chapter 20.13","sec":"20.10"},{"q":"The interest on loan relating to primary agricultural production, included in KAO Bank Limited's income for the year ended 31 December 2025, amounted to N5,000,000, broken down as: (i) 5-year repayment, 24-month moratorium, 15% p.a., N2,500,000; (ii) 3-year repayment, 6-month moratorium, 20% p.a., N800,000; (iii) 2-year repayment, 18-month moratorium, 14% p.a., N700,000; (iv) 5-year repayment, 6-month moratorium, 35% p.a., N1,000,000. The base lending rate for these facilities is 21% p.a. Compute the loan interest exempted from tax.","marks":8,"a":[{"table":{"align":"llrl","head":["Loan","Moratorium / rate","Interest (N)","Exempt?"],"rows":[["(i)","24 months / 15%","2,500,000","Yes — moratorium ≥12 months AND rate ≤21% base rate"],["(ii)","6 months / 20%","800,000","No — moratorium below 12 months"],["(iii)","18 months / 14%","700,000","Yes — moratorium ≥12 months AND rate ≤21% base rate"],["(iv)","6 months / 35%","1,000,000","No — fails both the moratorium AND the rate condition"],["**Total exempt**","","**3,200,000**",""]]}},{"note":"The exemption (s.4(6)(a) NTA, first covered in Chapter 14) requires a bank loan (granted on/after 1 January 1997) to agricultural trade/business, with BOTH a moratorium of at least 12 months AND an interest rate not exceeding the base lending rate — loans (ii) and (iv) each fail at least one condition, so only loans (i) and (iii), totalling N3,200,000, qualify."}],"src":"Chapter 20.13","sec":"20.6"}]}},{"n":21,"t":"Computation of Companies Income Tax","brief":"Pulling together the whole companies-income-tax framework into one working template, then applying it through four distinct assessment bases: ordinary total-profit computation, the dividend-substitution rule (and its own exclusions), deemed-profit/best-of-judgement assessment, and the effective-tax-rate top-up regime — closing with the small-company exemption mechanics and a consolidated tour of the Act's anti-tax-abuse provisions.","outcomes":["Identify the rates of tax","Understand the various bases of computing companies income tax","Compute companies income tax","Explain and compute effective tax rate","Identify income, dividend or payment that cannot be treated as substitution for total profit","Explain justifications for tax exemptions of the profits of a small company","Explain the anti-tax abuse provisions put in place by the NTA and NTAA"],"secs":[{"n":"21.2","t":"Computation of CIT based on total profit","b":[{"p":"A consolidated template for computing companies income tax payable:"},{"steps":["Start with net profit/(loss) before tax; add back non-deductible expenses (depreciation, unrealised FX loss, provision for lease liability, donations, excess interest on related-party loans, penalties/fines); deduct non-taxable income (unrealised FX gain, payment for lease liability) → assessable profit/(loss).","Add chargeable gains; deduct available reliefs (R&D relief, employee-compensation relief, new-employment relief, if any) → revised assessable profit.","Deduct loss relief — unabsorbed loss b/f plus the year's loss, absorbed to the extent possible — noting the unabsorbed balance carried forward.","Deduct capital allowance — unutilised b/f plus the year's allowance, utilised to the extent possible — noting the unutilised balance carried forward → total taxable profit.","Compute companies income tax (30% of total taxable profit) and Federal Development Levy (4% of assessable profit) → total covered taxes payable.","Separately test the effective tax rate: net profit before tax, less 5% each of depreciation and personnel cost, gives the qualifying profit for minimum-ETR purposes; divide total covered taxes by this figure to get the ETR, and compare against the 15% minimum.","Where ETR is below 15%, compute the top-up rate (15% minus ETR) and top-up tax (qualifying profit × top-up rate); add to covered tax payable, less any economic development incentive tax credit, to reach the final tax liability payable."]}]},{"n":"21.3","t":"Computation of CIT based on substitution of dividend for total profit — section 9(1) of NTA","b":[{"key":"Where a Nigerian company declares dividend out of profits on which no tax is payable — because there are no total profits, or total profits are less than the dividend declared — the company is charged tax at the s.56 rate (30%, currently) **as if the dividend were its total profits** for the relevant assessment year, regardless of whether the dividend recipient is a Nigerian company. This rule was first introduced in Chapter 4."},{"eg":{"t":"Illustration 21.1 — comparing the normal and dividend bases (BOS Nigeria Limited)","q":[{"p":"BOS Nigeria Limited's projected results for the year ending 31 December 2026: assessable profit N18,000,000; net profit N32,000,000; capital allowances agreed with the NRS N10,000,000; dividend declared and paid N24,000,000. **Required**: compute the total taxable profit and CIT payable, applying the relevant NTA 2025 provisions."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Normal basis","N"],"rows":[["Adjusted profit as per accounts","18,000,000"],["Less: capital allowances","(10,000,000)"],["**Total taxable profit**","**8,000,000**"],["Companies income tax (30% × 8,000,000)","2,400,000"],["Federal development levy (4% × 18,000,000)","720,000"]]}},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Dividend basis","N"],"rows":[["Dividend paid","24,000,000"],["Companies income tax (30% × dividend paid)","7,200,000"],["Development levy (4% of assessable profit)","720,000"]]}},{"note":"Since the dividend paid (N24,000,000) exceeds the computed total taxable profit (N8,000,000), the dividend basis governs — CIT payable is **N7,200,000**, not N2,400,000. The Federal Development Levy stays anchored to assessable profit (4% × N18,000,000 = N720,000) regardless of which CIT basis applies."}]}}]},{"n":"21.4","t":"Computation of CIT based on deemed profit assessment or income assessment — section 38 of NTAA","b":[{"p":"Where a company or trade produces **either no assessable profit, or an assessable profit which is less than what would be expected** from that business, **or the true amount of the assessable profit cannot be readily ascertained**, the relevant tax authority may, in the case of:"},{"ol":["a **resident** — assess and charge that taxable person on such **fair and reasonable percentage of the gross income** from the trade or business as the relevant tax authority may determine; or","a **non-resident** — the profits shall be **any amount resulting from applying the profit margin of the person to the turnover generated from Nigeria**."]},{"p":"For the purpose of the non-resident limb, \"**profit margin**\" shall be the proportion of **Earnings Before Interest and Tax (EBIT) to income or revenue** in the published audited financial statement of the business; and in the case of persons that are **not required to publish financial statements**, the profit margin as may be ascertained by the relevant tax authority **from the financial statements of comparative companies** (s.38 NTAA — see also §4.8.4)."},{"eg":{"t":"Illustration 21.2 — a deemed-profit revision after a desk examination (DEDE (Nigeria) Limited)","q":[{"p":"DEDE (Nigeria) Limited, general merchandising, filed for the 2027 assessment year: revenue N100,000,000; assessable profit N5,850,000; less unrelieved loss b/f N1,200,000 = N4,650,000; less capital allowance N2,500,000; total profit N2,150,000; CIT at 30% = N645,000. Fixed assets at 31 December 2026 totalled N265 million. A desk examination revealed revenue understated by N5,000,000 and expenses overstated by N1,500,000; the tax official found the company's own ~10% gross-profit percentage unreasonable against the industry average, and decided to apply a fair/reasonable **25%** to revenue instead. **Required**: re-compute the CIT liability."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["DEDE (Nigeria) Limited — revised CIT, 2027 assessment year","N"],"rows":[["Revised assessable profit (25% × (100,000,000 + 5,000,000))","26,250,000"],["Less: unrelieved losses brought forward","(1,200,000)"],["Subtotal","25,050,000"],["Less: capital allowances","(2,500,000)"],["**Total profit**","**22,550,000**"],["**Companies income tax payable (30%)**","**6,765,000**"]]}},{"note":"Once assessment shifts to the 25%-of-revenue deemed-profit basis, the \"expenses overstated by N1,500,000\" fact becomes irrelevant — the deemed-profit method is applied directly to (corrected) revenue and does not reference the company's own expense figures at all. Only the revenue correction (understated by N5,000,000) feeds into the computation."}]}}]},{"n":"21.5","t":"Computation of CIT based on effective tax rate (ETR) — section 57 of NTA","b":[{"key":"Where, in any year of assessment, the **effective tax rate** of a company — that is, **covered taxes as a percentage of net income** — is **less than 15%**, such company shall **recompute and pay an additional tax** that makes its effective tax rate equal to **15%** (s.57 NTA)."},{"p":"The provisions of this section apply to:"},{"ol":["a company that is a **constituent entity of a Multinational Entity (MNE) group** with aggregate group turnover of **at least €750 million** or its equivalent; and","any other company with an **aggregate turnover of N50,000,000,000 and above** in the financial year."]},{"key":"Recommended computation steps: (1) ascertain net income (profit before tax, adjusted for franked investment income and unrealised gains/losses); (2) further adjust for **5% of depreciation and personnel cost** — the substance-based income exclusion (SBIE) adjustment; (3) ascertain covered taxes (CIT, hydrocarbon tax, petroleum profit tax, development levy, priority-sector tax credit); (4) divide covered taxes by the qualifying profit from steps (1)-(2)."},{"eg":{"t":"Illustration 21.3 — computing the top-up tax two ways (ABC Limited)","q":[{"p":"ABC Limited's projected results for the year ending 31 December 2026: turnover N120,000,000,000; profit before tax N750,000,000; salaries N900,000,000; depreciation N87,000,000; assessable profit N320,000,000; capital allowances N65,000,000; total taxable profit N255,000,000; CIT paid N76,500,000; Federal development levy paid N12,800,000. **Required**: compute the top-up tax payable."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Solution A — applying the 5% SBIE adjustment (global best practice)","N"],"rows":[["Qualifying net profit (750,000,000 less 5% of [900,000,000 + 87,000,000])","700,650,000"],["Covered taxes (76,500,000 + 12,800,000)","89,300,000"],["Effective tax rate (89,300,000 / 700,650,000 × 100%)","12.745%"],["Top-up tax (15% × 700,650,000, less 89,300,000)","15,797,500"]]}},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Solution B — applying s.57 NTA literally, no SBIE adjustment","N"],"rows":[["Qualifying net profit (unadjusted)","750,000,000"],["Covered taxes (76,500,000 + 12,800,000)","89,300,000"],["Effective tax rate (89,300,000 / 750,000,000 × 100%)","11.91%"],["Top-up tax (15% × 750,000,000, less 89,300,000)","23,200,000"]]}},{"warn":"The two solutions differ by over N7.4 million in top-up tax owed, purely because s.57 NTA is silent on the 5%-depreciation/personnel-cost SBIE deduction that OECD/international best practice requires (already flagged in Chapter 20). This illustration exists specifically to demonstrate that ambiguity in practice — a candidate should be prepared to show either computation, and to explain the difference, rather than assume only one is \"correct.\""}]}}]},{"n":"21.6","t":"Income, dividend or payment that cannot be treated as substitution for total profit — section 9(2) of NTA","b":[{"p":"The s.9(1) substitution rule does **not** apply to:"},{"ol":["dividends declared from a company's **retained earnings**, to the extent the profits/gains within those retained earnings have already been taxed;","dividends declared from profits/gains **exempt** from income tax;","**franked investment income**; or","distributions a REIT makes to shareholders from rental/dividend income received on their behalf — whether paid from the current year's profits or prior reporting periods."]},{"eg":{"t":"Illustration 21.4 — applying the retained-earnings and no-current-profit exclusions (Apex Limited and SDP Limited)","q":[{"p":"For the year ending 31 December 2026: Apex Limited — total profit N3,600,000, retained earnings b/f N4,800,000, dividend declared (payable 2027) N4,200,000. SDP Limited — total loss (N1,200,000), retained earnings b/f N2,000,000, dividend declared (payable 2027) N750,000."}],"a":[{"p":"**Apex Limited**: of the N4,200,000 dividend, only N600,000 is regarded as paid out of retained earnings (already-taxed profit) — so only **N3,600,000** (the portion matching the year's own total profit) falls within the s.9(2) exclusion analysis."},{"p":"**SDP Limited**: since the company made a **loss** for the year, the entire N750,000 dividend cannot have been paid from current-year profit — it must come from retained earnings, so it is **not** subject to s.9(1) substitution at all."}]}}]},{"n":"21.7","t":"Tax exemption of the profits of a small company","b":[{"h4":"21.7.1  Definition of a small company — section 201 of NTA"},{"def":{"t":"Small company (s.201 NTA)","d":"a business with gross turnover of N100 million or less per annum, and total fixed assets not exceeding N250 million."}},{"h4":"21.7.2  Criteria that must be met by small companies in order to be granted tax exemption"},{"p":"A small company's profits are exempt from tax (s.56 NTA), provided it:"},{"ol":["registers with the relevant tax authority and obtains a Tax ID (s.4 NTAA); and","maintains accurate financial records and a fixed-assets register as evidence of its small-company status (s.102 NTAA)."]},{"h4":"21.7.3  Exemption from payment of federal development levy"},{"key":"Because a small company whose profit is **exempt from tax under section 56 of the NTA** will not have its assessable profit subjected to tax, it will **not pay the federal development levy** in respect of those exempt profits. The levy is charged on **assessable profit** (see §23.4), so where there is no assessable profit brought into charge there is no levy."},{"h4":"21.7.4  Computation of capital allowances"},{"key":"Since a small company's exempt profit is not subject to tax, it correspondingly pays **no Federal Development Levy** on that exempt profit. And — as already established in Chapter 16 — capital allowances are **not allowable or carried forward** for a small company; it may only claim them once its gross turnover crosses N100 million **and** its fixed assets exceed N250 million (becoming a large company)."},{"eg":{"t":"Illustration 21.5 — small-company capital allowance deferral (Deez Limited)","q":[{"p":"Deez Limited incurred N2,000,000 QCE on furniture and fittings in the 2024 assessment year, when revenue was N24,000,000. It expects revenue of N127,000,000 and fixed assets of N280 million by the 2027 assessment year. The (source-stated) depreciation rate is 20%. **Required**: compute the capital allowances claimable for all relevant assessment years."}],"a":[{"warn":"This illustration's own printed solution applies a **25% initial allowance** alongside the 20% annual allowance — directly contradicting the explicit statement already established in Chapters 15 and 16 that \"the NTA 2025 does not recognise initial allowance and balancing adjustment, hence only annual allowance is captured.\" This is reproduced below exactly as the source presents it, since it is the given answer, but the contradiction with the Act's own stated position elsewhere in this Study Text is a genuine, unresolved internal inconsistency worth flagging rather than silently picking a side."},{"table":{"align":"llll","head":["","Furniture & fittings (N)","Cumulative (N)","Remarks"],"rows":[["**2024**: cost","2,000,000","",""],["Initial allowance (25% × 2,000,000)","500,000","",""],["Annual allowance (20% × [2,000,000 − 500,000])","300,000","800,000","Small company — deemed utilised, not carried forward"],["**2025**: annual allowance","300,000","1,100,000","Still a small company"],["**2026**: annual allowance","300,000","1,400,000","Still a small company — not utilised"],["WDV c/f to 2027 (2,000,000 − 1,400,000)","","600,000",""],["**2027**: annual allowance — claimable","300,000","","Now a large company (revenue crossed N100m)"],["WDV c/f to 2028","","300,000",""],["**2028**: annual allowance claimable (300,000 less 1% retention)","280,000","","Final year"],["Amount permanently retained in the books","20,000","","1% of the N2,000,000 cost"]]}},{"note":"Since the company did not cross the N100,000,000 revenue threshold until the 2027 assessment year, the initial allowance (N500,000) and the first three years' annual allowances (N300,000 each, N900,000 total) are all deemed utilised during 2024-2026 — forfeited under the small-company rule, not banked. The written-down value carried to 2027 is N2,000,000 − N500,000 (IA) − N900,000 (3 years' AA) = N600,000. The final retention figure is **1%** of the asset's cost (N20,000) — the source's own printed note describes this as \"10% of the cost of the asset,\" which does not match its own N20,000 figure (10% of N2,000,000 would be N200,000); this is corrected here to 1%, consistent with the notional-retention rule already established in Chapter 15/16 and with the actual arithmetic used."},{"warn":"Two further slips in the book's own note to this illustration, flagged rather than silently corrected. **(1)** It says the allowances \"will be deemed utilised in assessment years **2023, 2024 and 2025**\" — but the expenditure was incurred in the **2024** assessment year, so the three small-company years are **2024, 2025 and 2026**, as the solution's own table correctly shows. **(2)** It describes the N20,000 retained in the books as \"**10% of the cost of the asset**\" — N20,000 is **1%** of N2,000,000, and 1% is the correct retention rule (see §16.11, Question 3). Ten per cent would be N200,000."}]}}]},{"n":"21.8","t":"Anti-tax abuse provisions","b":[{"p":"A consolidated summary of anti-abuse provisions across the NTA and NTAA:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Provision","Mechanism"],"rows":[["**Effective tax rate** (s.57 NTA)","A company below 15% ETR must top up to 15% — scoped to ≥£750m MNE-group constituents and ≥N50bn standalone companies (Chapter 20)."],["**Disclosure of tax planning** (s.30 NTAA)","Taxpayers must, without notice/request, provide the tax authority information on a disclosable transaction/agreement (Chapter 2)."],["**Non-registration / anti-contract-splitting** (s.100(2) NTAA)","A statutory body/company awarding a contract to an unregistered person faces a N5,000,000 administrative penalty (Chapter 4)."],["**Power of substitution** (s.60 NTAA)","The tax authority may appoint any person as agent of a taxpayer who has refused/failed to pay due tax, and may require the agent to pay the taxpayer's adjudged debt (Chapter 4)."],["**VASP returns** (s.25 NTAA)","Virtual asset service providers must submit monthly reports to the tax authority, with or without notice (Chapter 4/9)."],["**Banker/financial-institution information** (s.29 NTAA)","Financial institutions prepare annual returns on new/existing customers where cumulative monthly transactions reach N50 million (individual) or N250 million (body corporate) (Chapter 4)."],["**Deductible interest restriction** (s.20(1)(a); Third Schedule)","Excess interest to a connected person, above 30% of EBITDA, is a disallowable deduction (Chapter 20)."],["**VAT fiscalisation** (s.23 NTAA)","A person making a taxable supply must use the Electronic Fiscal System (EFS) where the Service deploys one (Chapter 7)."]]}}]},{"n":"21.9","t":"Chapter review","b":[{"p":"This chapter addressed applicable tax rates and the various bases for computing companies income tax under the NTA 2025."}]},{"n":"21.10","t":"Worked examples — study text questions and suggested solutions","b":[{"p":"The study text sets **three questions** at §21.10.1 — two computations with theory parts, and a report-writing question — with its own suggested solutions at §21.10.2. All three are reproduced here in full, with the book's answers, every figure independently recomputed, and added explanation."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 21.10.1, Question 1","t":"Hispac Limited — income tax liability, and the anti-tax abuse provisions","open":true,"q":[{"p":"**Hispac Limited** is owned by Nigerians and has been in business since 2022. The projected financial results of the company for the year ending **31 December 2026** are as follows:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Hispac Limited — projected statement of financial position","rows":["Assets employed",["Non-current assets","","180,000,000","@ind"],["Current assets","550,000,000","","@ind"],["Less: current liabilities","(360,000,000)","","@ind"],["Net current assets","","190,000,000","@ind"],["Net assets","","370,000,000","@t"],"Financed by:",["Share capital","","140,000,000","@ind"],["Statutory reserves","","60,000,000","@ind"],["General reserves","","100,000,000","@ind"],["Long term loans","","70,000,000","@ind"],["","","370,000,000","@t"]]}},{"p":"You are provided with the following additional information: **(i)** the projected revenue of the company for the year ending 31 December 2026 is **N240,000,000**; **(ii)** gross profit **N35,000,000**; **(iii)** assessable profit **N2,100,000**; **(iv)** unrelieved capital allowances brought forward from the 2026 year of assessment was **N300,000**; **(v)** capital allowances for the 2027 year of assessment amount to **N800,000**."},{"p":"**Required:** **(a)** Compute the company's income tax liability for the 2027 year of assessment. **(b)** In 2025, four tax laws were enacted in Nigeria to unify fragmented tax laws, enhance compliance by taxpayers and increase government revenue; additionally, the laws are to ensure simplification of tax administration and widen the tax base to capture transactions relating to digital services. Explain some of the **anti-tax abuse provisions** put in place by the government."}],"a":[{"h4":"1(a)  Computation of companies income tax liability for the 2027 assessment year"},{"stmt":{"t":"Hispac Limited — income tax liability","rows":[["Assessable profit","","2,100,000"],"Deduct:",["Unrelieved capital allowances brought forward","300,000","","@ind"],["Current year capital allowance","800,000","(1,100,000)","@ind"],["Total profit","","1,000,000","@t"],["Companies income tax (N1,000,000 at 30%)","","300,000"],["Federal development levy (4% of N2,100,000)","","84,000"],["Total tax liability","","384,000","@tt"]]}},{"warn":"The study text's printed solution adds the two capital allowances — **N300,000 + N800,000** — and shows the total as **N1,110,000** instead of **N1,100,000**. That one-digit slip carries through: it gives a total profit of N990,000 instead of **N1,000,000**, companies income tax of N297,000 instead of **N300,000**, and a total liability of N381,000 instead of **N384,000**. The federal development levy of N84,000 is unaffected, being charged on assessable profit. The figures above are the corrected ones."},{"key":"Note the **two different bases** in this one computation, which is the real teaching point. **Companies income tax** is charged at 30% on **total profit** — after capital allowances. The **federal development levy** is charged at 4% on **assessable profit** — before them. So capital allowances reduce the CIT but not the levy, and a company can pay the levy in a year when its CIT is nil (compare §19.7, Question 3, where tertiary education tax behaves the same way)."},{"note":"Everything in the statement of financial position is **noise**. Net assets, share capital, reserves and long-term loans play no part in the computation — the answer needs only the **assessable profit** and the **two capital allowance figures**. The revenue of **N240,000,000** matters only in that it exceeds the N100,000,000 threshold, so Hispac is not a small company and the 30% rate applies; the gross profit of N35,000,000 is not used at all."},{"h4":"1(b)  Anti-tax abuse provisions"},{"p":"Some of the provisions in the NTA and the NTAA include:"},{"ol":["**Effective tax rate — section 57 of NTA.** Where, in any year of assessment, the effective tax rate (covered taxes as a percentage of net income) of a company is **less than 15%**, such company shall **recompute and pay an additional tax** that makes its effective tax rate equal to 15%. The section applies to a company that is a constituent entity of a **Multinational Entity group with aggregate group turnover of at least €750 million** or its equivalent, and to any other company with an **aggregate turnover of N50,000,000,000 and above** in the financial year.","**Disclosure of tax planning — section 30 of NTAA.** Taxpayers shall, **without notice or request**, provide the relevant tax authority with information relating to a disclosable transaction or agreement.","**Non-registration and anti-contract splitting — section 100(2) of NTAA.** A statutory body or company that awards a contract to an **unregistered person** shall be liable to an administrative penalty of **N5,000,000**.","**Power of substitution — section 60 of NTAA.** The relevant tax authority is empowered to **appoint any person as agent** of a taxpayer where tax has become due and the taxable person has refused or failed to pay, and may require the agent to pay the taxpayer's adjudged debt.","**Returns for virtual asset service providers — section 25 of NTAA.** Virtual asset service providers shall, with or without notice, submit **monthly reports** to the relevant tax authority.","**Information to be delivered by bankers and others — section 29 of NTAA.** Financial institutions shall prepare, with or without demand by the Service, **annual returns** specifying the names, customer location and transactions of new and existing customers where cumulative monthly transactions reach **N50 million** (an individual) or **N250 million** (a body corporate).","**Deductible interest — section 20(1)(a) of NTA and the Third Schedule.** Any **excess interest** — total interest paid or payable to a **connected person** in excess of **30% of EBITDA** of the Nigerian company in that accounting period — is a **disallowable deduction** in ascertaining assessable profit.","**Value added tax fiscalisation system — section 23 of NTAA.** Any person making a taxable supply shall use the **electronic fiscal system (EFS)** for recording and reporting all supplies, where the Service deploys an EFS."]},{"key":"The eight provisions attack avoidance along **three different lines**, and grouping them that way makes a better answer than a flat list. **Minimum taxation** — the 15% effective tax rate floor and the 30%-of-EBITDA interest cap stop profit being stripped out of Nigeria. **Information** — mandatory disclosure of tax planning, bank and VASP reporting, and the e-invoicing fiscalisation system make transactions visible before a return is filed. **Collection** — the power of substitution and the contract-splitting penalty reach the tax once it is due."},{"note":"The book's own text writes the MNE-group threshold as \"**£750 million**\". The OECD Pillar Two threshold the provision implements is **€750 million** (euro), and the figure is conventionally quoted in euro. Use **€750 million**."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 21.10.1, Question 2","t":"Apic Nigeria Limited — dividend substitution, loss relief, R&D, and the tax payable","open":true,"q":[{"p":"The Managing Director of **Apic Nigeria Limited** attended a workshop during which he came across the following tax matters under the NTA and NTAA: **(i)** computation of CIT based on substitution of dividend for total profit; **(ii)** treatment of losses in the ascertainment of total profits; and **(iii)** deduction of the cost of research and development in the ascertainment of the profit or loss of a company. On his return to the office he requested the Finance Director to explain these matters to him."},{"p":"Information already agreed with the Nigeria Revenue Service in respect of the **2027 assessment year** is as follows:"},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["","N'000"],"rows":[["Assessable profit","26,000"],["Losses brought forward","9,000"],["Capital allowances","4,200"]]}},{"p":"The projected **gross turnover** for the year ending 31 December 2026 is **N120,000,000**."},{"p":"**Required (as the tax consultant):** **(a)** Explain the provisions of the NTA, 2025 dealing with the three areas of interest identified by the Managing Director. **(b)** Using the information provided above, determine the tax payable by the company in the 2027 assessment year."}],"a":[{"note":"The book answers part (a) in the form of a **letter to the client**, on the consultant's letterhead and signed by the Managing Partner. Where a question identifies you as the tax consultant and names the person asking, present the answer as a letter or memorandum — the presentation itself carries marks."},{"h4":"2(a)(i)  Computation of CIT based on substitution of dividend for total profit — section 9(1) of NTA"},{"p":"Where a Nigerian company declares a dividend out of profits on which **no tax is payable** due to **there being no total profits**, or **total profits which are less than the amount of the dividend declared** — whether or not the recipient of the dividend is a Nigerian company — the company paying the dividend shall be **charged to tax at the rate prescribed in section 56** (that is, **30%** for now) **as if the dividend were the total profits** of the company for the year of assessment to which the accounts out of which the dividend is declared relate."},{"h4":"2(a)(ii)  Treatment of losses in the ascertainment of total profits — section 27(6) of NTA"},{"p":"The amount of loss deductible in the ascertainment of the total profits of companies shall be subject to the following conditions:"},{"ol":["**in no circumstances shall the aggregate loss deductions** from the assessable profits or income **exceed the amount of that loss**;","loss **can only be deducted from the trade or business in which the loss was incurred**;","the loss shall be deducted to the extent possible from the assessable profits of the **first year of assessment after that in which the loss was incurred**, and in subsequent years **until the loss is fully recouped**; and","the loss incurred during any year of assessment shall be computed **in accordance with the basis period provided in sections 22 to 25** of the Act."]},{"p":"Notwithstanding the above, any loss incurred in any period from sales, disposal or any other transaction in **digital assets** shall only be deductible in determining the profits from the business relating to **digital or virtual assets**."},{"h4":"2(a)(iii)  Deduction of the cost of research and development — section 164 of NTA"},{"ol":["for the purpose of ascertaining the profit or loss of any company for any period from any source chargeable with tax, there shall be deducted the **amount incurred in that period for research and development**;","the deduction allowed for any year of assessment **shall not exceed 5% of the turnover** for that year; and","where the company **subsequently sells or transfers the outcome** of the research and development to another person for exploitation or commercialisation, the **proceeds of such sale or transfer shall be taxed accordingly**."]},{"h4":"2(b)  Tax payable in the 2027 assessment year"},{"stmt":{"t":"Apic Nigeria Limited — tax payable, 2027 assessment year (N'000)","rows":[["Assessable profit","","26,000"],["Loss relief","","(9,000)"],["","","17,000","@t"],["Capital allowance","","(4,200)"],["Total profit","","12,800","@t"],["Companies income tax payable (total profit × 30%)","","3,840"],["Federal development levy (4% of assessable profit)","","1,040"],["Total liability","","4,880","@tt"]]}},{"key":"Note the **order of the deductions**, which the Act fixes and which candidates often reverse: **loss relief first, then capital allowances**. Assessable profit N26,000 less the N9,000 loss gives N17,000; the N4,200 of capital allowances then reduces that to a total profit of N12,800. Reversing the order would give the same answer here, but not where a restriction on capital allowance utilisation bites."},{"note":"Again the **federal development levy is computed on the assessable profit of N26,000**, not on the total profit of N12,800: 4% × N26,000 = **N1,040**. And the turnover of **N120,000,000** confirms the company is above the N100,000,000 threshold, so it is not exempt as a small company and the 30% rate applies."},{"note":"Part (a)(iii) ties back to the figures: R&D is deductible but **capped at 5% of turnover**, which for Apic would be 5% × N120,000,000 = **N6,000,000**. No R&D figure is given, so nothing is deducted — but if a question supplies one, that cap is the first thing to test it against."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 21.10.1, Question 3","t":"Ada, Torera and Peace — converting a partnership into a limited liability company","open":true,"q":[{"p":"**Ada, Torera and Peace**, based at Okokomaiko in Lagos State, have run the firm of **Ada, Torera, Peace and Company** as builders for several years. The partnership agreement provided for the following: **(i)** salaries paid to partners — Ada **N7,120,000**, Torera **N7,240,000**, Peace **N7,480,000**; **(ii)** profit-sharing ratio — Ada **1/5**, Torera **3/10**, Peace **1/2**."},{"p":"In January 2024 there was a decision to review the partnership agreement. Messrs Ada, Torera and Peace have been unable to find worthy successors to take over as partners. Rather than review the partnership agreement, they agreed to **convert the partnership into a limited liability company**. In view of this, a firm of chartered accountants was contacted to incorporate the name **ATP Consultants Limited**."},{"p":"The authorised share capital of the proposed company was agreed at **N10,000,000**, made up of 10,000,000 ordinary shares of N1.00 each. The shareholding structure was agreed as: Ada **20%**, Torera **30%**, Peace **50%**."},{"p":"The certificate of incorporation was **approved** by the Registrar-General of the Corporate Affairs Commission (CAC), Abuja on **1 August 2024**. The certificate of incorporation was **dated 10 August 2024**. The company **commenced business on 1 October 2024**."},{"p":"The cost of incorporation includes: payment for stamp duty **N80,000**; professional fee for incorporation **N50,000**; CAC registration fees **N100,000**; additional costs of incorporation **N40,000** — total **N270,000**."},{"stmt":{"t":"Financial results for the year ended 31 December 2024","rows":[["Income","","25,500,000"],"Less:",["Cost of incorporation","270,000","","@ind"],["Transport and travelling","135,000","","@ind"],["Medical","120,000","","@ind"],["Hotel and accommodation","125,000","","@ind"],["Audit and accountancy","110,000","","@ind"],["Postages and telephone","150,000","","@ind"],["Depreciation","500,000","","@ind"],"Salaries:",["Ada","7,120,000","","@ind"],["Torera","7,240,000","","@ind"],["Peace","7,480,000","","@ind"],["","21,840,000","(23,250,000)","@ind"],["Net profit","","2,250,000","@tt"]]}},{"p":"*Assume: fees and expenses were earned/incurred evenly throughout the year.*"},{"p":"**Required:** Write a report to Messrs Ada, Torera and Peace highlighting: **(a)** the tax implication of the decision to convert to a limited liability company, limiting yourself to the facts and details provided in the case study; **(b)** your comment on the breakdown of the cost of incorporation of N270,000, and the tax implication of each item."}],"a":[{"note":"The requirement is to **write a report**. The book's answer is laid out as a letter addressed to the Directors of ATP Consultants Limited with a subject line, numbered submissions and a sign-off. Adopt that form: a report or letter that is merely a list of points loses the presentation marks."},{"h4":"3(a)  Tax implications of the conversion"},{"ol":["the **partnership ceased on 30 September 2024**, and so all partnership income shall be assessed to **personal income tax**;","**commencement rules will apply from 1 October 2024**, when ATP Consultants Limited commenced operation;","the **incorporation expenses** shall be applicable only to ATP Consultants Limited, though they are **not an allowable expense** for tax purposes;","ATP Consultants Limited will be required to **register with the Nigeria Revenue Service** for VAT and CIT, etc.; and","ATP Consultants Limited, in accepting the incorporation expense, shall **deduct and remit withholding tax from the professional fees at the rate of 5%**."]},{"h4":"3(b)  Tax implications of the cost of incorporation"},{"ol":["the total cost of **N270,000**, though paid during the partnership period, shall be considered as **pre-operational expenses of ATP Consultants Limited**;","the pre-operational expenses, **when added back, will increase taxable income**, thereby increasing the tax payable by ATP Consultants Limited; and","the **N270,000 charged to ATP Consultants Limited's account is not an allowable expense** for tax purposes, **even if spread over a number of years**."]},{"key":"The conversion is a **cessation and a commencement**, and that single characterisation drives the whole answer. The partnership **ceases** on 30 September 2024, so its final period is assessed on the partners personally under §17.15 — and, as §19.5 shows, a loss in a terminal period would be lost. The company **commences** on 1 October 2024, so the commencement rules of §20.10.1 fix its first basis period as 1 October to 31 December 2024. Note too that the **continuity-of-trades protection in section 25 does not help here**: that provision saves an *individual* from being treated as commencing or ceasing on becoming or ceasing to be a partner, not a partnership from ceasing when it incorporates."},{"note":"Why the **incorporation costs are disallowed**: all four items — stamp duty on the share capital, the professional fee, CAC registration fees and the additional costs — are incurred in **bringing the company into existence**, not in earning its income. They are therefore **capital in nature** and caught by section 21 (§20.8.4). Treating them as \"pre-operational expenses\" written off over several years makes no difference: the accounting treatment does not change the tax character."},{"note":"Observe the three different dates the question supplies and what each is for: **approval on 1 August 2024** and the **certificate dated 10 August 2024** go to when the company legally exists; **commencement of business on 1 October 2024** is what fixes the basis period. It is the commencement of business, not incorporation, that starts the tax clock — which is the point the two August dates are there to test."},{"warn":"Two defects in the printed material, flagged rather than silently corrected. **(1)** The statement of profit or loss prints the total expenses as **\"23.250,000\"** with a full stop in place of the first comma; it is N23,250,000, as N25,500,000 − N2,250,000 confirms. **(2)** The book's answer to part (a)(v) reads \"withholding tax from the professional fees at the rate of **5% if the beneficiary is a company and also 5% if the recipient is an individual partnership or incorporated entity**\" — a garbled sentence that says 5% twice. The rate on professional fees is **5% for a resident** recipient, whether company or individual (see the First Schedule at §9.7); 10% applies to a **non-resident**."},{"note":"A presentational inconsistency in the book worth noting if you are comparing answers: the letter in part (a) is signed off \"**For: XYZ & Co.**\" by R. O. Enigma, Managing Partner, while the report in Question 3 is signed \"**For: ABC Consultants Limited**\" by B. James, Partner — two different firms answering for the same consultant."}]}}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Companies income tax (normal basis)","tex":"\\text{CIT} = 30% \\times \\text{Total taxable profit}"},{"lb":"Companies income tax (dividend-substitution basis)","tex":"\\text{CIT} = 30% \\times \\text{Dividend declared} \\quad \\text{(used only if dividend} > \\text{total taxable profit)}"},{"lb":"Federal Development Levy","tex":"\\text{FDL} = 4% \\times \\text{Assessable profit}"},{"lb":"Deemed-profit assessment (resident)","tex":"\\text{Deemed profit} = \\text{Fair/reasonable rate} \\times \\text{Gross income}"},{"lb":"Effective tax rate","tex":"\\text{ETR} = \\dfrac{\\text{Covered taxes}}{\\text{Qualifying net income}} \\times 100%"},{"lb":"Top-up tax","tex":"\\text{Top-up tax} = (15% \\times \\text{Qualifying net income}) - \\text{Covered taxes paid}"}],"focus":"This chapter's real skill is knowing WHICH of the four assessment bases governs a given scenario, and switching between them correctly: use ordinary total-profit CIT by default; switch to the DIVIDEND basis only when declared dividend exceeds total taxable profit (and remember the s.9(2) exclusions — retained-earnings dividends, exempt-profit dividends, franked investment income, and REIT distributions — can take a dividend out of scope entirely); switch to DEEMED-PROFIT assessment when the tax authority rejects the taxpayer's own figures as unreliable (and remember this ignores the taxpayer's own expense figures entirely, working off gross income/revenue instead); and always run the EFFECTIVE-TAX-RATE test in parallel for a large/MNE-scoped company, since a company can owe ordinary CIT correctly and STILL owe top-up tax if its blended effective rate falls under 15%. The Federal Development Levy (4% of ASSESSABLE profit) stays constant across every CIT basis — it never switches to a dividend or deemed-profit footing the way CIT itself does.","errors":["Applying the dividend-substitution basis whenever a company pays a dividend — it applies ONLY where the dividend exceeds total taxable profit for the year (or there is no total profit at all); a dividend within the year's own profit is taxed normally.","Forgetting the s.9(2) exclusions when a dividend appears to exceed total profit — a dividend paid from already-taxed retained earnings, exempt profits, franked investment income, or a REIT's pass-through rental/dividend income is NOT subject to substitution, even if it numerically exceeds the current year's profit.","Applying the deemed-profit percentage to the taxpayer's adjusted/net profit rather than to gross income/revenue — the deemed-profit method under s.38 NTAA is explicitly a percentage of GROSS income (for residents), replacing the ordinary profit-and-expense computation entirely, not adjusting it.","Assuming every company must run the effective-tax-rate top-up computation — it is scoped to large MNE-group constituents (≥£750m) and very large standalone companies (≥N50bn turnover) only; an ordinary SME never performs this test.","Treating the Federal Development Levy as switching bases along with CIT — FDL stays pinned to 4% of assessable profit throughout, even in illustrations where CIT itself shifts to a dividend or deemed-profit basis."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Under s.9(1) of the NTA, when does the dividend-substitution rule apply to a company's tax computation?","o":["Whenever a company pays any dividend at all","Only where the dividend declared exceeds the company's total taxable profit for the year (or there is no total profit)","Only where the dividend is paid to a non-resident shareholder","Only for companies with turnover above N100 million","Never — this rule was repealed under the NTA 2025"],"a":1,"w":"The dividend-substitution rule applies only where a company declares dividend out of profits on which no tax is payable — because there are no total profits, or total profits are less than the dividend declared.","src":"Chapter 21.2","sec":"21.2"},{"q":"Which of the following dividends is explicitly EXCLUDED from the s.9(1) dividend-substitution rule, under s.9(2) of the NTA?","o":["A dividend paid entirely from the current year's untaxed profit","A dividend paid from retained earnings that have already been taxed","A dividend paid to a non-resident shareholder","A dividend that exceeds total profit by more than 50%","A dividend paid by a company with no capital allowance claims"],"a":1,"w":"Section 9(2) NTA excludes dividends declared from retained earnings, to the extent the profits within those retained earnings have already been taxed.","src":"Chapter 21.5","sec":"21.5"},{"q":"When a tax authority applies a deemed-profit assessment to a resident company under s.38 of the NTAA, what is the assessment based on?","o":["The company's own reported net profit, adjusted for disallowed expenses","A fair and reasonable percentage of the company's gross income","The company's profit margin (EBIT/revenue) from comparable companies only","The company's total assets","The average CIT paid by the company over the preceding 5 years"],"a":1,"w":"For a resident person, deemed-profit assessment applies a fair and reasonable percentage of gross income from the trade/business, as the tax authority determines — the profit-margin (EBIT/revenue) approach is specifically for non-residents.","src":"Chapter 21.3","sec":"21.3"},{"q":"For a small company's profits to be exempt from tax under s.56 of the NTA, what must the company do?","o":["Nothing — exemption is automatic for all companies below the turnover threshold","Register with the relevant tax authority for a Tax ID, and maintain accurate financial records and a fixed-assets register","Pay a one-time exemption fee to the NRS","Obtain a certificate from the Ministry of Finance annually","Convert to a different legal structure"],"a":1,"w":"A small company's profits are exempt only if it registers with the relevant tax authority for a Tax ID (s.4 NTAA) and maintains accurate financial records and a fixed-assets register as evidence of its status (s.102 NTAA).","src":"Chapter 21.6","sec":"21.6"},{"q":"Which anti-tax-abuse provision empowers the tax authority to appoint any person as agent of a taxpayer who has refused or failed to pay due tax?","o":["Disclosure of tax planning (s.30 NTAA)","Power of substitution (s.60 NTAA)","VAT fiscalisation system (s.23 NTAA)","Deductible interest restriction (s.20(1)(a) NTA)","Non-registration and anti-contract-splitting (s.100(2) NTAA)"],"a":1,"w":"The power of substitution (s.60 NTAA) lets the tax authority appoint any person as agent of a defaulting taxpayer, with the right to require the agent to pay the taxpayer's adjudged debt.","src":"Chapter 21.7","sec":"21.7"}],"theory":[{"q":"Hispac Limited, Nigerian-owned, has been in business since 2022. For 2027 assessment year: assessable profit N2,100,000; unrelieved capital allowances brought forward from 2026 N300,000; capital allowances for 2027 N800,000. (a) Compute the company's income tax liability for 2027. (b) Explain some of the anti-tax-abuse provisions the government has put in place.","marks":12,"a":[{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["(a) Hispac Limited — CIT liability, 2027 assessment year","N"],"rows":[["Assessable profit","2,100,000"],["Less: unrelieved capital allowances b/f","300,000"],["Current year capital allowance","800,000"],["Total capital allowance","(1,100,000)"],["**Total profit**","**990,000**"],["Companies income tax (990,000 × 30%)","297,000"],["Federal development levy (4% × 2,100,000)","84,000"],["**Total tax liability**","**381,000**"]]}},{"p":"**(b)** Any of the 8 anti-abuse provisions in Chapter 21.7 — for example: the effective tax rate top-up (s.57 NTA); mandatory disclosure of tax planning arrangements (s.30 NTAA); the N5,000,000 penalty for awarding a contract to an unregistered person (s.100(2) NTAA); the power of substitution over a defaulting taxpayer's agent (s.60 NTAA); and the 30%-of-EBITDA restriction on deductible interest to connected persons (s.20(1)(a); Third Schedule)."}],"src":"Chapter 21.10","sec":"21.2"},{"q":"Apic Nigeria Limited has agreed the following with the NRS for the 2027 assessment year: assessable profit N26,000,000; losses brought forward N9,000,000; capital allowances N4,200,000. As tax consultant, determine the tax payable by the company for 2027.","marks":8,"a":[{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Apic Nigeria Limited — tax payable, 2027 assessment year","N"],"rows":[["Assessable profit","26,000,000"],["Less: loss relief","(9,000,000)"],["Subtotal","17,000,000"],["Less: capital allowance","(4,200,000)"],["**Total profit**","**12,800,000**"],["Companies income tax (total profit × 30%)","3,840,000"],["Federal development levy (4% of assessable profit)","1,040,000"],["**Total liability**","**4,880,000**"]]}},{"note":"This mirrors the general s.27(6) carry-forward loss relief rules (Chapter 19) — loss brought forward is deducted before capital allowance, both subject to the same \"never exceeding the loss/allowance itself\" and \"same trade/business only\" conditions."}],"src":"Chapter 21.10","sec":"21.2"}]}},{"n":22,"t":"Taxation of Specialised Businesses","brief":"By far the largest chapter in the subject — a tour through the peculiar tax rules for real estate/REIT companies, agricultural business, foreign air/sea transport and cable undertakings, banks, life/non-life/reinsurance insurance, unit trust schemes, export processing zones, regulated securities lending, NGOs, gas utilisation, non-interest (Islamic) finance, labelled startups, the Defence and Security Infrastructure Fund, lottery/gaming, digital/virtual assets, and the digital economy — closing with four substantial worked computations spanning insurance, foreign air transport, agriculture, and unit trusts.","outcomes":["Understand the peculiar tax laws relating to specialised businesses","Compute the taxes payable by specialised businesses"],"secs":[{"n":"22.2","t":"Definitions","b":[{"def":{"t":"Agricultural trade or business","d":"primary crop production (raw/semi-processed crops, excluding processing); primary livestock production (live animals, raw meat/poultry, fresh eggs/milk, excluding processing); primary forestry production (timber, firewood, charcoal, uncultivated materials, seeds/saplings, excluding processing); and primary fishing production (all fish including ornamental, excluding processing)."}},{"def":{"t":"Real estate investment","d":"a company duly SEC-approved to operate as a real estate investment scheme (REIS) in Nigeria."}},{"def":{"t":"Gross turnover","d":"the gross inflow of economic benefits during a period, arising from an entity's operating activities where the inflow increases equity — sales, services, interest, rents, royalties or dividends — excluding equity-participant contributions."}},{"p":"Regulated securities lending terms: **regulated securities lending transaction** (any securities-lending transaction under SEC rules); **lender**/**borrower** (an approved lender/borrower in such a transaction); **approved agent** (an SEC-approved intermediary for such a transaction); **compensating payments** (payments made in lieu of interest/dividend under such a transaction)."},{"def":{"t":"Digital assets","d":"digital representations of value that can be digitally exchanged — crypto assets, utility tokens, security tokens, NFTs, similar digital representations/derivatives, and any other asset a relevant regulatory authority defines."}},{"note":"**Traceability to the study text's own sub-sections.** The book divides this section into the numbered sub-sections below. Every one of them is covered in the material above, which groups the discussion by subject rather than repeating each heading; use this table to go from a printed sub-section number straight to the right part of this section."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Study text sub-section","Heading"],"rows":[["22.2.1","\"Agricultural trade or business\" means:"],["22.2.2","\"Real estate investment”"],["22.2.3","\"Gross turnover” means the gross inflow of economic benefits during the period"],["22.2.4","“Regulated securities lending transaction'' means any securities lending"],["22.2.5","“Lender'' in a regulated securities lending transaction means approved lender."],["22.2.6","“Borrower in a regulated securities lending transaction means an approved"],["22.2.7","“Approved Agent'' means any person approved by the Securities and Exchange"],["22.2.8","“Compensating payments'' means any payments made in lieu of interest or"],["22.2.8","\"Digital assets\" means digital representation of value that can be digitally"]]}}]},{"n":"22.3","t":"Tax provision for real estate companies, and real estate investment companies approved by the SEC under the relevant provisions of real estate investment schemes (REIS)","b":[{"h4":"Real estate companies — the applicable taxes"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Tax","Treatment"],"rows":[["**CIT/PIT**","Rent/premium from granting another person use/occupation of property is taxable. Individuals pay the graduated 15%-25% rates; corporate bodies pay 0% (small company, turnover ≤N100m, fixed assets <N250m) or 30% (otherwise)."],["**WHT**","Commission/consultancy/technical/management/professional fees 5%; supply of goods/materials (other than by manufacturer/producer) 2%; construction of roads/bridges/buildings/power plants 2% — remitted within the statutory period."],["**Federal development levy**","4% of assessable profit — a small company (turnover ≤N100m) is exempt."],["**VAT**","Must register for VAT on commencement. Land/building (including any interest in it) and rent on land/building are VAT-**exempt** (s.185(1)). Input tax on a taxable supply is deductible per s.155(4) to the extent used for taxable supplies."],["**Stamp duties**","A lease agreement with annual value below N10 million (or 10× the annual minimum wage, whichever is higher) is exempt (s.134)."],["**Rental expense deductions**","Agency fees, repairs, insurance, etc. relating to rental income are deductible (s.20)."]]}},{"h4":"Real estate investment companies (REICs) under a REIS"},{"def":{"t":"Real estate investment company (s.191, Investments and Securities Act 2025)","d":"a body corporate incorporated to acquire intermediate/long-term interests in real estate/property development, raising funds via the capital markets (income certificates, ordinary shares)."}},{"p":"REIS pool investor funds (individuals, companies, pension funds, associations) to acquire/develop/hold real estate portfolios — generating rental income, financing via mortgages, or both. A REIC's income falls into 4 classes: rental income; dividend from another REIC; gains from asset disposal; and other income (interest, fees, etc.)."},{"key":"Dividend/rental income a REIC receives under a REIS is exempt from both the s.9(1) dividend-substitution rule and further tax in the REIC's own hands (s.162(1)(c)) — treated as received on the shareholders' behalf — **provided**: at least **75%** is redistributed as dividend to shareholders; and this happens within **12 months** of the relevant financial year-end. This does **not** exempt the shareholder's own tax on what they receive, the REIC's own management-fee/other income, or a non-compliant REIC."},{"note":"Dividends/mandatory distributions a REIC makes under this exemption are allowable deductions (s.20(1)(k)) — but since they relate to EXEMPT income, they are not additionally deducted in computing assessable profits. Gains from asset disposal, fees and other non-REIS income remain fully taxable (s.33). Distributions/dividend payments by a REIT/REIC are exempt from WHT (Regulation 10, Deduction at Source Regulations 2024)."},{"note":"**Traceability to the study text's own sub-sections.** The book divides this section into the numbered sub-sections below. Every one of them is covered in the material above, which groups the discussion by subject rather than repeating each heading; use this table to go from a printed sub-section number straight to the right part of this section."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Study text sub-section","Heading"],"rows":[["22.3.1","Real estate companies"],["22.3.2","Real estate investment companies that are approved by SEC under REIS"]]}}]},{"n":"22.4","t":"Tax provisions relating to agricultural business and primary agricultural production","b":[{"p":"**Qualifying agriculture expenditure** — paragraph 1(e) of the First Schedule to the NTA — is capital expenditure incurred:"},{"ol":["in connection with a **plantation**, on the **clearing of land for planting**, **planting** (other than replanting), **maintenance of the plantation** and **other pre-production expenses** in connection with that plantation, which shall be qualifying agriculture expenditure incurred **on the first day on which the trade or business commences**; and","in respect of **ranching**, on: **structures used primarily for ranching**; **raising herds of animals for the purpose of breeding** (and **not** raising animals for the purpose of **sale**); and **animals that function as apparatus** with which a trade is carried on and whose **life expectancy is more than 5 years**."]},{"key":"Two distinctions decide whether agricultural spending attracts capital allowances. For a **plantation**, the first planting is **capital** and all **replanting** is revenue. For **livestock**, animals are capital only where they are a **breeding herd** or working **apparatus with a life over five years** — animals raised **for sale** are trading stock, which is why the cockerels and cattle purchased in §22.21, Question 3 are left as a revenue expense."},{"key":"Income from agricultural businesses (crop production, livestock, aquaculture, forestry, dairy, and other Thirteenth Schedule businesses) is tax-exempt for the **first 5 years** of business (s.162(1)(p); Thirteenth Schedule)."},{"p":"Conditions (2026 Compendium of Investment Incentives, NIPC): the incentive applies only to businesses **exclusively** carrying on the qualifying agricultural activities; exemption runs from the **initial year of commercial operation**, not incorporation date; the business must comply with Federal Ministry of Agriculture and NRS registration/reporting obligations; **non-agricultural** income is not exempt; and renewal beyond 5 years needs separate ministerial approval."},{"h4":"Exempt agricultural sub-sectors and products (illustrative)"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Sub-sector","Exempted products"],"rows":[["Crop production","All crops (perennial and non-perennial)."],["Livestock (ranching/farming)","Cattle, swine/pigs, sheep, goat, poultry (incl. processed eggs); livestock processing (meat/poultry processing)."],["Forestry","Latex and gum Arabic (rubber/acacia plantations)."],["Dairy","Fresh/pasteurised/sterilised/homogenised/UHT liquid milk; dried/concentrated milk; cream; solid milk/cream; cheese, curd and lactose."],["Cocoa processing","Cocoa, cocoa butter/fat/oil; chocolate."],["Animal feeds","Animal feed, edible oils/by-products/concentrates, grain mill products, feed supplements."]]}},{"note":"**Traceability to the study text's own sub-sections.** The book divides this section into the numbered sub-sections below. Every one of them is covered in the material above, which groups the discussion by subject rather than repeating each heading; use this table to go from a printed sub-section number straight to the right part of this section."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Study text sub-section","Heading"],"rows":[["22.4.1","Agricultural trade or business - section 201 of NTA"],["22.4.2","Qualifying capital expenditure for capital allowance purposes - paragraph 1(e) of"],["22.4.3","Tax incentives - section 162(1)(p) of NTA and the Thirteenth Schedule to the Act"]]}}]},{"n":"22.5","t":"Taxation of foreign air and sea transport companies","b":[{"p":"Sections 60-64 NTA cover specialised-business taxation: export processing/free trade zones (s.60); insurance (s.61); lottery/gaming (s.62); collective investment schemes (s.63); and mining operations (s.64)."},{"key":"A foreign air/sea transport company is assessable on Nigerian income from **loading** passengers/mail/livestock/goods into an aircraft/ship **in Nigeria** — plus leasing/container/non-freight/other incidental income (s.18(1)). **Exempt** (s.18(2)): income from passengers/mail/livestock/goods loaded on routes **outside** Nigeria but terminating in Nigeria, or **in transit** through Nigeria (trans-shipment/connecting flight)."},{"h4":"Monthly returns (s.21 NTAA)"},{"p":"A non-resident sea/air transport operator must file monthly returns (with evidence of tax payment) by the **21st day** of the following month, containing a detailed gross-revenue statement of Nigerian operations (total sums receivable, supported by invoices) — without prejudice to also filing annual returns and returns/tax on non-freight/incidental income."},{"note":"Where no separate Nigerian-operations financial statement is filed, the company submits a detailed gross-revenue statement certified by a director **and** external auditor, supported by the contract agreement (s.18(7)). Regulatory agencies may require, as a condition of carrying on business: evidence of prior-year tax filing; and evidence of tax declaration/payment for the intended carriage/shipment (s.18(9))."},{"h4":"Method 1 — global adjusted profit/depreciation ratio (s.18(3)-(4))"},{"p":"Used where the NRS is satisfied the foreign company's home tax authority assesses shipping/aircraft operators on a basis not materially different from the NTA, and certifies this."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Ratio","Formula"],"rows":[["Global adjusted profit ratio","Adjusted profit ÷ Gross revenue × 100%"],["Global depreciation ratio","Depreciation allowance ÷ Gross revenue × 100%"]]}},{"p":"Applied via the pro-forma: Assessable profit = Nigeria-receivable gross revenue × global profit ratio; less Capital allowance = Nigeria-receivable gross revenue × global depreciation ratio; giving total profit, taxed at the appropriate rate."},{"eg":{"t":"Illustration 22.1 — Method 1 applied to a foreign airline (Globe Airways Limited)","q":[{"p":"Globe Airways Limited (UK-based) — statement of profit or loss, year ended 31 March 2026: income from passengers' freight into Nigeria N1,300,000,000; goods loaded into aircraft in Nigeria N1,220,000,000; passengers loaded/flown out of Nigeria N680,000,000; goods loaded on other routes N500,000,000 (total N3,700,000,000); less salaries/other expenses N2,000,000,000, depreciation N320,000,000, other disallowable expenses N240,000,000; net profit N1,140,000,000. The NRS is satisfied the UK computes/assesses on a similar basis and has certified the ratios; depreciation is agreed as a proxy for capital allowance. Salaries and other expenses include: N180,000,000 deposit for a new aircraft; N54,000,000 for VIP-lounge use by first-class passengers; and N72,000,000 rent for crew transit-flat accommodation. **Required**: compute (a) total profit for Nigerian tax purposes, (b) income tax payable."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Computation of adjusted profit","N'000"],"rows":[["Net profit per accounts","1,140,000"],["Add: depreciation","320,000"],["Other disallowable expenses","240,000"],["Deposit for aircraft — capital","180,000"],["**Adjusted profit**","**1,880,000**"]]}},{"p":"Global adjusted profit ratio = 1,880,000 / 3,700,000 × 100% = **50.81%**. Since the tax authority agreed depreciation charged stands in lieu of a separately-computed depreciation ratio, capital allowance is simply N320,000,000 (the depreciation figure)."},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["(a) Total profit","N'000"],"rows":[["Assessable profit (N1,880,000 × 50.81%)","955,228"],["Less: capital allowance","(320,000)"],["**Total profit liable to tax in Nigeria**","**635,228**"]]}},{"p":"**(b)** Income tax payable = 30% × N635,228,000 = **N190,568,400**."},{"warn":"The pro-forma this chapter itself states (§22.4) bases assessable profit on **Nigeria-receivable gross revenue** × the global profit ratio — here, that would be the goods-loaded-in-Nigeria and passengers-loaded-out-of-Nigeria income (N1,220,000,000 + N680,000,000 = N1,900,000,000), not the GLOBAL adjusted profit figure (N1,880,000,000). The source's own printed solution applies the ratio to the global adjusted profit instead, which is what is reproduced above for consistency with the given answer — but this is an unresolved inconsistency between the chapter's own stated method and its own worked illustration (contrast with the Habib Limited illustration later in this chapter, which correctly restricts its computation to Nigeria-source income only)."}]}},{"eg":{"t":"Illustration 22.2 — Method 1 applied to a foreign cable-undertaking company (Hero Limited)","q":[{"p":"Hero Limited, a Ghanaian company in cable undertakings in Nigeria — worldwide accounts, year ending 30 September 2026: income from cable messages terminating in Nigeria N1,200,000,000; routed through Nigeria N1,150,000,000; originating in Nigeria N1,100,000,000 (total N3,450,000,000); less salaries/wages N1,880,000,000, depreciation N290,000,000, overhead expenses N185,000,000 (of which N123,000,000 is disallowable), purchase of equipment N439,000,000; net profit N656,000,000. Ghana computes/assesses on the same basis, and has certified the ratios. **Required**: compute (a) adjusted profit, (b) the ratios, (c) total profits and tax payable."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["(a) Adjusted profit","N'000"],"rows":[["Net profit per accounts","656,000"],["Add: depreciation","290,000"],["Purchase of capital equipment","439,000"],["Other disallowable expenses","123,000"],["**Adjusted profit**","**1,508,000**"]]}},{"p":"**(b)** Global adjusted profit ratio = 1,508,000/3,450,000 × 100% = **43.71%**; global depreciation ratio = 290,000/3,450,000 × 100% = **8.41%**."},{"p":"A cable company's profits are deemed derived from Nigeria via messages transmitted **from** Nigeria to places outside it — cable messages originating in Nigeria (N1,100,000,000) are the Nigeria-source base, treated equivalently to loading passengers/goods in Nigeria for an air/sea transport company."},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["(c) Income tax payable","N'000"],"rows":[["Assessable profit (N1,100,000 × 43.71%)","480,810"],["Less: capital allowance (N1,100,000 × 8.41%)","(92,510)"],["**Total profit**","**388,300**"],["**Income tax payable (30%)**","**116,490**"]]}},{"note":"Unlike Illustration 22.1, this computation correctly restricts the base to Nigeria-source revenue (N1,100,000,000, cable messages originating in Nigeria) rather than the full worldwide figure — consistent with the chapter's own stated pro-forma."}]}},{"h4":"Method 2 — fair percentage of full sum receivable (s.18(5))"},{"key":"Used only where the NRS is **not satisfied** as to Method 1's conditions — the NRS has discretion to apply a fair percentage to the full sum receivable in Nigeria for carriage of passengers/mail/livestock/goods. A company assessed under Method 2 may **request revision to Method 1**, valid only if made within **6 years** of the relevant assessment year and supported by an appropriate home-country tax-authority certificate."},{"warn":"Regardless of method, **minimum tax** for air/sea transport is never less than **2%** of the gross amount for carriage of passengers/mail/livestock/goods shipped/loaded in Nigeria (excluding VAT and other collected-on-behalf-of-others levies) — s.18(6)."},{"note":"Section 18(8) carves out income from leasing vessels/containers, non-freight operations, or other incidental income — taxed under the ordinary NTA provisions instead. A foreign company transmitting messages by cable/wireless apparatus (telecommunications) is assessed identically to an air/sea transport company, with Nigerian-originating cable messages standing in for Nigeria-loaded passengers/goods."},{"note":"**Traceability to the study text's own sub-sections.** The book divides this section into the numbered sub-sections below. Every one of them is covered in the material above, which groups the discussion by subject rather than repeating each heading; use this table to go from a printed sub-section number straight to the right part of this section."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Study text sub-section","Heading"],"rows":[["22.5.1","Introduction"],["22.5.2","Filing of monthly returns by non-residents shipping and airline companies"],["22.5.3","Assessment of profit liable to tax and evidence of payment of tax"],["22.5.4","Global adjusted profit/depreciation ratio basis - section 18(3) of NTA"],["22.5.5","Minimum tax -section 18(6) of NTA"],["22.5.6","Other provision - section 18(8) of NTA"],["22.5.7","Taxation of foreign company engaged in cable undertakings (telecommunication)"]]}}]},{"n":"22.6","t":"Taxation of banks","b":[{"p":"Banks are liable to CIT, VAT and other applicable taxes like any other company, and must file both **monthly and annual** tax returns."},{"key":"Per the 2026 NIPC Compendium of Investment Incentives, banks receive **100% tax exemption** on interest income from loans for: export-oriented manufacturing; agricultural production; local plant/machinery fabrication; and cottage-industry working capital — provided: the loan is bank-granted; funds are strictly applied to the stated purpose; the loan agreement allows **≥12 months' moratorium**; and the interest rate does not exceed the CBN base lending rate."},{"p":"Also exempt: interest banks receive on Federal/State government bonds; interest on foreign placements, and dividend/rent/royalty from outside Nigeria brought in through approved channels; and dividend treated as franked investment income."},{"warn":"Disallowed for banks: increases in provisions on performing loans, other assets/receivables, finance-lease advances, investment-value diminution, off-balance-sheet engagements, and gratuity; and unrealised exchange loss."},{"note":"**Traceability to the study text's own sub-sections.** The book divides this section into the numbered sub-sections below. Every one of them is covered in the material above, which groups the discussion by subject rather than repeating each heading; use this table to go from a printed sub-section number straight to the right part of this section."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Study text sub-section","Heading"],"rows":[["22.6.1","Introduction"],["22.6.2","Determination of assessable profit and tax liability"]]}}]},{"n":"22.7","t":"Taxation of insurance companies","b":[{"p":"Governed by s.61 NTA and the Nigeria Insurance Industry Reform Act (NIIRA) 2025 — covering both Nigerian and non-Nigerian insurers, and distinguishing **life** from **non-life** business."},{"key":"For life assurance, premium received from the assured does **not** count as income (since it will definitely be paid out on death/policy maturity) — so claims paid are correspondingly **not** deductible either. The reverse applies for non-life business (premium IS income; claims ARE deductible)."},{"note":"Since 1995, a **composite** insurer (life + non-life) has each source taxed **separately** — a loss from life business cannot be relieved against non-life income (s.61(8)). Dividend received is franked investment income, exempt from further tax, as with any company."},{"h4":"General (non-life) insurance business (s.61(2)(a), (3), (9))"},{"p":"Profits are ascertained as if the whole premium/investment income were Nigerian-derived — taking gross premium and other income receivable, less reinsurance, less: a reserve for **unexpired risks** (calculated on a **time-apportionment** basis of risks accepted in the year — replacing the old flat-percentage method); and **outstanding claims and outgoings** (the total estimated amount, with any unutilised portion **added back** to next year's profits, replacing the old 25%-of-premium method) — plus the ordinary Chapter Two deductions."},{"h4":"Life insurance business (s.61(2)(b), (4)-(6), (10))"},{"p":"Profits are ascertained as if the whole investment/other income were received, and all expenses incurred, in Nigeria. Taxable profit = investment income + other income, **less** management expenses (including commission). Any dividend distributed from an actuarial revaluation of unexpired risks (or other revaluation) is deemed part of total profits — the company must provide the Service full revaluation particulars (including the actuary's certificate) **within 3 months** of the revaluation."},{"p":"Deductions from investment/other income: an amount building a general reserve/fund equal to the **net liabilities on policies in force** at the actuarial-valuation date; the **higher of** 1% of gross premium earned or 10% of profits, to a special reserve fund (accumulated until it reaches the statutory minimum paid-up capital); and all normal allowable business outgoings."},{"h4":"Reinsurance business (s.61(11))"},{"p":"Deductible to a general reserve fund: **up to 50%** of gross profits, where the fund is below the initial statutory minimum paid-up capital; or **up to 25%**, where the fund is at/above that minimum."},{"h4":"Other insurance-specific rules"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Rule","Detail"],"rows":[["**Separate books/returns** (s.61(7))","A life-and-non-life composite insurer keeps separate funds/books/annual returns for each class, as though unrelated."],["**Separate assessment** (s.61(8))","Each class is assessed separately (\"life insurance assessment\" / \"non-life (other) insurance assessment\"). Within a class, multiple products form one business (losses cross-usable within class), but losses carry forward **only within the same class**, indefinitely (effective 1 February 2020)."],["**Minimum capital** (s.15 NIIRA)","Non-life: higher of N15bn or risk-based capital. Life: higher of N10bn or risk-based capital. Reinsurance: higher of N35bn or risk-based capital — the Commission weighs insurance/market/credit/operational risk. A new company's minimum capital consists of government bonds/treasury bills, or cash/cash equivalents."],["**Additional filing** (s.61(12))","An insurer using an agent/loss adjuster/broker must annex a schedule of their name/address, engagement/termination dates, and payments made to them, to its annual returns."],["**Dividend declaration bar** (s.35 NIIRA)","An insurer cannot declare/pay a dividend until: preliminary/organisational/commission/brokerage/loss expenses not represented by tangible assets are fully written off; adequate provision is made for actual/contingent losses on risk assets/liabilities; and it meets NIIRA capital/solvency requirements."],["**Loss carry-forward**","Insurance-company losses (within the same class) carry forward **indefinitely** (s.27(6))."]]}},{"p":"Key definitions (s.61(13)): **investment income** (for life business) = income from investing shareholders' funds; **gross premium** = total premiums written/received/receivable, excluding unearned premium and premium returned to the insured; **gross income** = total income (investment income, fees, commission, other-asset income), excluding franked investment income, premium received and reinsurer-paid claims; **other income** (non-life) = all income other than gross premium, excluding franked investment income."},{"eg":{"t":"Illustration 22.3 — reserve for unexpired risk and assessable profit (Kebu Insurance Plc)","q":[{"p":"Kebu Insurance Plc (non-life business) — extracts for 2026/2027 assessment years: reserve for unexpired risk b/f N43,000,000/N50,000,000; c/f N50,000,000/N65,000,000; estimated claims/outgoings N3,000,000/N4,200,000; actual claims/outgoings N2,800,000/N2,500,000; net profit before claims N11,000,000/N12,000,000 (2026/2027 respectively). **Required**: compute (a) the unexpired risk attributable to risk accepted each year, (b) assessable profits."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lrr","head":["(a) Unexpired risk attributable to the year","2026 (N)","2027 (N)"],"rows":[["Reserve c/f","50,000,000","65,000,000"],["Less: reserve b/f","(43,000,000)","(50,000,000)"],["**Attributable to risk accepted in the year**","**7,000,000**","**15,000,000**"]]}},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["(b) 2026 assessable profit","N"],"rows":[["Net profit before claims","11,000,000"],["Less: actual claims/outgoings","(2,800,000)"],["Less: estimated claims/outgoings","(3,000,000)"],["**Assessable profit**","**5,200,000**"]]}},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["(b) 2027 assessable profit","N"],"rows":[["Net profit before claims","12,000,000"],["Add: 2026 estimated claims/outgoings (unutilised, added back)","3,000,000"],["Subtotal","15,000,000"],["Less: actual claims/outgoings (2027)","(2,500,000)"],["Less: estimated claims/outgoings (2027)","(4,200,000)"],["**Assessable profit**","**8,300,000**"]]}},{"note":"The 2026 estimated claims/outgoings figure is added BACK to 2027's profit before the 2027 actual/estimated figures are deducted — directly applying s.61(9)(b)'s \"unutilised estimate added to the following year's profit\" rule."}]}},{"note":"**Traceability to the study text's own sub-sections.** The book divides this section into the numbered sub-sections below. Every one of them is covered in the material above, which groups the discussion by subject rather than repeating each heading; use this table to go from a printed sub-section number straight to the right part of this section."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Study text sub-section","Heading"],"rows":[["22.7.1","Introduction"],["22.7.2","Taxation of insurance companies"],["22.7.3","Additional information to be filed by insurance business"],["22.7.4",". Declaration of dividend -section 35 of NIIRA, 2025"],["22.7.4","Determination of assessable profit and tax liability"]]}}]},{"n":"22.8","t":"Taxation of authorised unit trust schemes","b":[{"def":{"t":"Authorised collective investment / unit trust scheme","d":"an SEC-authorised arrangement (under the Investments and Securities Act) providing for public participation, as trust beneficiaries, in profits/income from acquiring/holding/managing/disposing of securities or other property."}},{"p":"For tax purposes, the trustees of such a scheme are treated as if they were a company whose business is mainly investment, unit-holders' rights are treated as shares, and income available for payment to unit-holders is treated as dividend on those shares (s.63(1))."},{"key":"Assessable profit = income accruing to the trustees from all scheme investments, **less** management expenses (including managers' remuneration) (s.63(2))."},{"p":"Where the trustees receive a payment already suffering tax by deduction (not franked investment income), that tax is an **advance payment**, set off against the scheme's own assessment (s.63(3)). Profit available for payment to unit-holders (or for investment) is deemed **dividend** paid/payable to them in proportion to their rights, and taxed in **their** hands (s.63(4))."},{"note":"**Traceability to the study text's own sub-sections.** The book divides this section into the numbered sub-sections below. Every one of them is covered in the material above, which groups the discussion by subject rather than repeating each heading; use this table to go from a printed sub-section number straight to the right part of this section."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Study text sub-section","Heading"],"rows":[["22.8.1","Definitions / introduction - section 63(5) of NTA"],["22.8.2","Taxation of unit trust income - section 63(1)-(4) of NTA"]]}}]},{"n":"22.9","t":"Criteria that must be met for exemption of profits of a small company from companies income tax (CIT)","b":[{"p":"Building on the conditions already set out in Chapters 20/21, a small company's profits are exempt (s.56 NTA) where it: registers for a Tax ID (s.4 NTAA); maintains accurate financial records and a fixed-assets register (ss.31, 102 NTAA); and **files its simplified annual income tax return** (s.15 NTAA), complying with s.11(1) NTAA."}]},{"n":"22.10","t":"Taxation of export processing and free trade entities — section 60 of NTA and the Second Schedule to the Act","b":[{"def":{"t":"Export processing zone entity","d":"an approved/licensed enterprise under the Nigeria Export Processing Zones Act, Cap. N107 LFN 2004."}},{"def":{"t":"Export free zone entity","d":"an approved/licensed enterprise under the Oil and Gas Free Zones Authority Act, Cap. O5 LFN 2004."}},{"warn":"The NTA 2025 **deleted** the old blanket exemption (from all Federal/State taxes, levies, rates, and legislative/foreign-exchange provisions) that these zone Acts previously granted — replacing it with the conditional regime below."},{"p":"Approved zone enterprises still enjoy: repatriation of foreign capital investment (with capital appreciation) at any time; remittance of profits/dividends earned by foreign investors; no import/export licences required; up to **25%** of production sellable in the customs territory (against a valid permit, duties paid); rent-free land at construction stage (rent thereafter as the authority determines); up to 100% foreign business ownership; and freedom to employ foreign managers/qualified personnel."},{"h4":"Conditions for full exemption (¶3, Second Schedule)"},{"key":"Subject to the customs-territory-sales restriction and the effective-tax-rate rule, an export processing zone entity's profits are **fully exempt** where: total sales arise from exporting goods/services (or inputs exclusively for export); **not more than 25%** of sales go to the Nigerian customs territory; or the goods/services are sold to persons in upstream/midstream/downstream petroleum/gas operations."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Rule","Detail"],"rows":[["**Over 25% customs-territory sales** (¶4)","Where >25% of sales in a year go to the customs territory, tax accrues in Nigeria on the entity's **total** customs-territory sales profit."],["**Full taxation from 2028** (¶5)","From **1 January 2028**, an entity's customs-territory sales profits are fully taxable regardless of the sales percentage."],["**Compliance** (¶6)","Must comply with NTAA registration, return-filing, and tax-deduction-at-source obligations."],["**Connected resident company** (¶7)","Where a zone entity contracts manufacturing/approved activity to a connected resident company outside the zone, income from the zone entity's sale of the goods is treated as the connected company's own income — unless the Service is satisfied the transaction was at arm's length."],["**Non-manufacturing services** (¶8)","Where a resident (non-zone) company provides non-manufacturing services to a connected zone entity, Transfer Pricing Regulations apply."],["**Customs-territory services** (¶9)","Services a customs-territory person renders to (or services a free-zone entity consumes from) the customs territory are also covered."],["**Evidence of export proceeds** (¶10)","Licensed entities must evidence export proceeds (cash inflow, or imported raw materials/equipment) to claim incentives."]]}},{"note":"**Traceability to the study text's own sub-sections.** The book divides this section into the numbered sub-sections below. Every one of them is covered in the material above, which groups the discussion by subject rather than repeating each heading; use this table to go from a printed sub-section number straight to the right part of this section."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Study text sub-section","Heading"],"rows":[["22.10.1","Definitions / Introduction - paragraph 2 of the Second Schedule to the Act"],["22.10.2","Special tax incentives for enterprises in an export processing zone section 18(1)"],["22.10.3","Special tax incentives for enterprises in an oil and gas export processing zone"],["22.10.4","Other provisions"]]}}]},{"n":"22.11","t":"Tax implications of the operation of the regulated securities lending transactions (\"SEC Lending\") in Nigeria","b":[{"p":"Income under SEC Lending covers: dividends; interest; securities-lending fees or other rights/rewards from the loaned securities; rights; bonus; and redemption benefits."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Provision","Treatment"],"rows":[["**s.162(1)(d)-(e) NTA**","Compensating payments qualifying as dividend, received by a lender from its approved agent/a borrower, are tax-**exempt** — as are compensating payments qualifying as dividend/interest an approved agent receives on a lender's/borrower's behalf."],["**s.20(1)(l) NTA**","Compensating payments qualifying as interest, paid by a lender to an agent/borrower, are an **allowable deduction**."],["**s.21(n) NTA**","Dividend a borrower generates and pays to an agent/lender is **not** an allowable deduction to the borrower."],["**Regulation 10, Deduction at Source Regs 2024**","Compensating payments are exempt from WHT."],["**Fees, rights, bonuses**","Taxable income to whichever party (borrower/lender) receives them."],["**s.21(o) NTA**","A compensating payment an approved agent makes (qualifying as interest/dividend) to a borrower/lender is **not** an allowable deduction."],["**Stamp duties** (s.184)","Exempt: documents transferring stocks/shares; shares/stocks/securities returned to a lender/agent by a borrower; and shares/stocks/securities a lender transfers to its agent/a borrower, under SEC Lending."]]}},{"note":"**Traceability to the study text's own sub-sections.** The book divides this section into the numbered sub-sections below. Every one of them is covered in the material above, which groups the discussion by subject rather than repeating each heading; use this table to go from a printed sub-section number straight to the right part of this section."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Study text sub-section","Heading"],"rows":[["22.11.1","Definitions and nature of transaction"],["22.11.2","Relevant regulatory provisions"],["22.11.3","Income"],["22.11.4","Taxation of a SEC lending transactions based on the provisions of NTA, 2025"],["22.11.5","Relevance of Stamp Duties Act to SEC Lending - section 184 of NTA"]]}}]},{"n":"22.12","t":"Tax provisions of non-governmental organisations (NGOs) in Nigeria, and other obligations and exemptions under the relevant tax legislation","b":[{"def":{"t":"NGO","d":"a not-for-profit association incorporated as a company limited by guarantee (CAMA Part A) or registered under CAMA Part C or other Nigerian law, or registered abroad and approved in Nigeria — providing a public good, not distributable profit to members. Includes ecclesiastical, charitable, benevolent, literary, scientific, social, cultural and sporting bodies of a public character, and (similarly treated) registered co-operative societies."}},{"def":{"t":"Public character (s.201 NTA)","d":"an organisation that is registered under relevant Nigerian law, and does not distribute/share its profit to members/promoters — income must be wholly utilised for its own public-interest objects. Distributing any asset (cash or in kind) to a promoter/member counts as profit distribution."}},{"h4":"Registration and filing"},{"p":"NGOs register for a Tax ID, filing: CAC registration certificate (or foreign incorporation instrument/incorporation-exemption evidence); certified memorandum/articles/constitution/rules; trustee/board-member list and profiles; and other required documents — at one of 6 designated Medium Tax Offices (Lagos Island; Abuja for North-Central/FCT; Kano for North-East/North-West; Enugu for South-East; Port Harcourt for South-South; Ibadan for South-West)."},{"p":"An NGO files annual self-assessment returns (s.11 NTAA) containing: audited accounts, tax computations, and a true/correct computed-surplus statement per source; required particulars on profits/allowances/reliefs/deductions; a signed declaration by a trustee/director/secretary/authorised person; within the stipulated filing period."},{"h4":"CIT liability"},{"key":"An NGO's profits from educational/religious/charitable activities of a public character are tax-exempt (s.162(1)(a)(iii)) — including subscription fees, donations, grants, zakat, offerings, tithes, and launching proceeds. **Taxable**: profit from trade/business (goods/merchandise sales, consultancy/professional fees) and investment income (interest, rent, royalty, dividend). An NGO must still deduct WHT on contracts and qualifying payments, remitted in the transaction currency."},{"p":"An NGO making donations receives tax relief under **section 163 of the NTA**, on the same conditions that apply to any other donor:"},{"ol":["the donee must be a **fund, body or institution in Nigeria** of an approved class — a **public fund**; a **statutory body or institution**; a **religious, charitable, educational or scientific institution established in Nigeria**; a body **recognised under the Diplomatic Immunities and Privileges Act**; or **any pandemic, natural disaster or other public emergency intervention**;","the donation is deductible **whether it is of a revenue or a capital nature**;","the donor must **provide requisite documentary evidence** of the donation to the relevant tax authority;","the total deduction for any year of assessment **shall not exceed 10% of the profit before tax** for that year; and","for a donation **other than in cash**, the value is the **lower of** the market value at the time of the donation **and** the consideration paid for the item when it was acquired."]},{"h4":"Personal tax, capital gains, VAT and other obligations"},{"p":"Despite the NGO's own exemption, **individual** promoters' emoluments, trustee/guarantor fees/benefits, and employee salaries remain **fully taxable** — the NGO must operate PAYE. Gains from disposing of chargeable assets are exempt **only** if: the asset was not acquired for a trade/business the NGO carries on; **and** the gains are used purely for the NGO's own activities (ss.34(1), 162(1)(iii))."},{"p":"VAT on goods NGOs purchase for humanitarian donor-funded projects is exempt (the donor pays VAT first, then claims a refund) — but services procured/consumed, and goods not directly for such projects, remain VAT-liable at the prevailing rate. NGOs self-account for VAT on supplies from non-VAT-liable persons/non-resident suppliers, and must charge/remit VAT on their own taxable supplies (returns due by the 21st of the following month). NGOs must also maintain accurate employee records and proper books of account (s.31 NTA) — non-compliance attracts standard penalties."},{"note":"**Traceability to the study text's own sub-sections.** The book divides this section into the numbered sub-sections below. Every one of them is covered in the material above, which groups the discussion by subject rather than repeating each heading; use this table to go from a printed sub-section number straight to the right part of this section."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Study text sub-section","Heading"],"rows":[["22.12.1","Introduction"],["22.12.2","Definition of an NGO"],["22.12.3","Definition of public character"],["22.12.4","Registration with NRS for tax purposes"],["22.12.5","Designated medium tax offices (MTOs) of NRS"],["22.12.6","Filing of tax returns"],["22.12.7","Obligation to pay companies income tax (CIT)"],["22.12.8","Tax relief to any company making donations to any fund, body or institution in"],["22.12.9","Liability to personal income tax (PIT)"],["22.12.10","Liability for capital gains"],["22.12.11","Liability to value added tax (VAT)"],["22.12.12","Other statutory responsibilities of NGOs - section 31 of NTA"]]}}]},{"n":"22.13","t":"Incentives that are granted to companies engaged in gas utilisation (downstream operations)","b":[{"def":{"t":"Gas utilisation","d":"marketing/distribution of natural gas for commercial purposes, including power plants, LNG, gas-to-liquid plants, fertiliser plants, and gas transmission/distribution pipelines."}},{"def":{"t":"Sunset (Tenth Schedule)","d":"the period from the Act's commencement after which a sector/industry/activity ceases to be eligible for the economic development incentive — for gas utilisation (downstream), this is **20 years**."}},{"key":"A gas-utilisation (downstream) priority-sector company (Tenth Schedule) receives: an economic development tax credit of **5% per annum for 5 years**, on each eligible QCE acquired within 5 years of the production date; the credit may offset any priority-period year's tax payable (except additional tax under s.57 ETR); and unutilised credit/eligible QCE may be carried forward for a further **5 years** after the priority period, with any remainder then **lapsing**."},{"note":"The company must file returns during the priority period and evidence compliance with the qualifying criteria (s.179 NTA). An economic development incentive certificate may be extended for **one further 5-year period** (no more), conditional on the priority company reinvesting **100% of its profits** during the incentive period into expanding the same product/service (s.170(3))."},{"note":"**Traceability to the study text's own sub-sections.** The book divides this section into the numbered sub-sections below. Every one of them is covered in the material above, which groups the discussion by subject rather than repeating each heading; use this table to go from a printed sub-section number straight to the right part of this section."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Study text sub-section","Heading"],"rows":[["22.13.1","Definitions"],["22.13.2","Introduction"]]}}]},{"n":"22.14","t":"Taxation of institutions offering non-interest financial products and services in Nigeria, in line with the Non-Interest Finance (Taxation) Regulations, 2022","b":[{"p":"The Non-Interest Finance (Taxation) Regulations 2022 (effective 1 April 2022) give authorised non-interest financial institutions (NFIs) a tax framework ensuring parity with conventional finance, legal/regulatory certainty, sector growth, alignment with Sharia principles (no interest/*riba*, asset-backed/ethical investment), compliance/revenue clarity, and investment/financial-inclusion facilitation."},{"p":"The Regulations cover 12 instrument types across 7 parts: sale-based (Murabaha; Istisna/Parallel Istisna; Salam/Parallel Salam); equity-based (Musharakah; Diminishing Musharakah; Mudarabah); lease-based (Ijarah wa Iqtina — finance lease; Ijarah — operating lease); fee/agency-based (Takaful); Sukuk (Islamic bonds); Islamic fund management; and Islamic REITs."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Instrument","Tax treatment"],"rows":[["**Murabaha** (cost-plus mark-up)","Initial asset purchase: not taxable (treated as loan). Markup (profit): treated as interest, subject to WHT — no VAT/stamp duty on the markup itself, though the contract document may attract stamp duty."],["**Istisna/Parallel Istisna** (manufacturing/construction financing)","Financing to the customer: loan-treated, no WHT/VAT. Supply of goods/services BY third parties TO the institution: taxable (VAT + WHT)."],["**Salam/Parallel Salam** (advance-payment contracts)","Advance payment: loan-treated, tax-exempt. Subsequent sale to third parties: taxable (VAT + WHT); profit margin realised subject to income tax."],["**Musharakah** (joint venture)","Capital contributed: loan-treated, not taxable. Profit share received: interest-equivalent, WHT applies. Ownership-interest transfers: stamp duty applies."],["**Diminishing Musharakah**","Gradual ownership transfer: loan-repayment-treated, not taxable. Rental (Ijara) component: WHT applies. Final transfer: stamp duty."],["**Mudarabah** (trust-based investment)","Capital provider's return: WHT applies (interest-equivalent). Manager's (Mudarib's) profit share: taxable business income. Capital losses: per general tax rules."],["**Ijarah wa Iqtina** (finance lease)","Lease rentals: VAT + WHT apply. Customer may treat payments as QCE. Final asset transfer: stamp duty."],["**Ijarah** (operating lease)","Lessor claims capital allowance on the asset; lease rentals taxable (VAT + WHT); lessee deducts rentals as a business expense."],["**Takaful** (Islamic insurance)","Operator's management fees/investment income: taxable (VAT + WHT). Surplus returned to participants: WHT applies. Participant-operator agreements: stamp duty."],["**Sukuk** (Islamic bonds)","Taxed similarly to conventional bonds — returns to holders subject to WHT; certificate transfers subject to stamp duty (unless exempt under capital-market rules)."],["**Islamic fund management**","Treated the same as conventional fund management."],["**Islamic REIT (I-REIT)**","Treated the same as a conventional real estate investment for tax purposes."]]}},{"note":"General provisions: double-taxation relief and transfer-pricing rules apply where relevant; instruments must be **genuinely** Sharia-structured to benefit; non-compliance/misrepresentation disqualifies the tax benefits. Home finance typically uses Diminishing Musharakah or Ijarah wa Iqtina; project financing typically uses Istisna, Musharakah, or Sukuk; commodity and vehicle-asset finance typically use Murabaha, Ijarah or Salam; direct (equity) investment uses Musharakah/Mudarabah; and an \"investment bond\" in this context refers to Sukuk."},{"eg":{"t":"Illustration 22.4 — tax treatment across four non-interest finance products (Al-Juda Finance Limited)","q":[{"p":"Al-Juda Finance Limited, a licensed non-interest financial institution, undertook in 2025: (i) a Murabaha transaction — purchased machinery worth N100,000,000 and sold it to a client at a 20% mark-up, payable in 5 equal annual instalments, with 7.5% VAT charged on the initial purchase; (ii) an Ijarah lease — acquired vehicles worth N35,000,000, leased for N15,000,000/year over 4 years, vehicles remaining Al-Juda's property; (iii) a Sukuk issuance — N200,000,000, backed by real estate assets, investors earning 15% annual returns from lease rentals on the properties; (iv) a Takaful scheme — members' monthly contributions pooled, with a N15,000,000 surplus distributed to contributors by participation ratio. **Required**: (a) explain the Nigerian tax implications of each transaction; (b) explain the challenges Al-Juda may face applying Nigerian tax law to these products."}],"a":[{"p":"**(a)(i) Murabaha (machinery)**: the initial purchase is subject to VAT and WHT and treated as a loan to the customer. The markup — 20% × N100,000,000 = **N20,000,000** — is treated as interest, subject to WHT. Loan repayments (price excluding markup) are exempt from VAT, stamp duties and CGT. The markup earned is subject to CIT; WHT deducted on it is remitted per WHT regulations. Transaction agreements generally attract stamp duty."},{"p":"**(ii) Ijarah (vehicle lease)**: for the lessor, the interest element of lease rentals is subject to CIT, WHT and VAT. For the lessee, lease payments are allowable expenses if the asset generates income. Only the **lessor** claims capital allowance."},{"p":"**(iii) Sukuk issuance**: returns to investors — 15% × N200,000,000 = **N30,000,000** — are treated as interest-equivalent, subject to WHT (typically a **10%** final tax to the recipient). Transfer of Sukuk certificates attracts stamp duty unless capital-market-rule exempt. Income from corporate Sukuk issuances is subject to CIT."},{"p":"**(iv) Takaful**: contributions are treated as premiums, not taxable to Al-Juda. The N15,000,000 surplus distributed to participants is subject to WHT. Commissions/management fees Al-Juda earns are subject to VAT at 7.5%. Participant-operator agreements attract stamp duty."},{"p":"**(b)** Challenges: an inadequate dedicated legal framework for Islamic finance (reliance on circulars); risk of double/multiple taxation on Murabaha and Sukuk (VAT, stamp duty, CGT overlapping); capacity gaps among tax officials on Sharia-compliant structures; lack of secondary markets; competition from conventional banks; low public awareness; and a shortage of appropriately trained personnel."}]}},{"note":"**Traceability to the study text's own sub-sections.** The book divides this section into the numbered sub-sections below. Every one of them is covered in the material above, which groups the discussion by subject rather than repeating each heading; use this table to go from a printed sub-section number straight to the right part of this section."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Study text sub-section","Heading"],"rows":[["22.14.1","Introduction"],["22.14.2","Objectives of the Regulations"],["22.14.3","Scope of the Regulations"],["22.14.4","Tax provisions"],["22.14.5","Home finance"],["22.14.6","Project financing"],["22.14.7","Commodity finance and vehicle asset finance"],["22.14.8","Direct investment"],["22.14.9","An investment bond"]]}}]},{"n":"22.15","t":"Tax and other incentives available to a labelled startup under the Nigeria Startup Act, 2022","b":[{"p":"Objectives: a legal/institutional framework for Nigerian startups; an enabling establishment/operation environment; development of technology-related talent; and positioning Nigeria as Africa's leading digital-technology centre."},{"h4":"Eligibility for labelling"},{"ol":["registered as a CAMA limited liability company, in existence **≤10 years**;","objects are innovation/development/production/improvement/commercialisation of a digital technology product/process;","holds/owns a digital-technology product/process, or authors registered software;","at least **1/3 local shareholding** held by Nigerian founder(s)/co-founder(s); and","a sole proprietorship/partnership must separately satisfy criteria 2-4 above."]},{"note":"The Act does not apply to a holding company/subsidiary of an existing, non-startup-registered company. A sole proprietorship/partnership gets a **6-month pre-label status** to comply with the full criteria — failing which, that status lapses."},{"h4":"Tax and fiscal benefits for a labelled startup"},{"ol":["expeditious NIPC Pioneer Status Incentive (PSI) approval, if in a PSI-captured industry;","simplified access to existing fiscal incentives (government discretion);","**income tax exemption for 3 years**, extendable by **2 more years** (still within the labelled-startup period) — running from the **startup-label issuance date**;","full deduction of Nigeria-incurred R&D expenses — CITA restrictions do not apply;","non-resident companies providing technical/consulting/professional/management services to a labelled startup suffer a **5% WHT**, which is their **final tax** (s.51(8) NTAA);","exemption from Industrial Training Fund contributions, while providing in-house training during the labelled period;","export incentives/financial assistance (Export Development Fund, Export Expansion Grant, Export Adjustment Scheme Fund), for eligible export activity;","access to CBN/Bank of Industry/other SME-support grants and loans; and","a **Credit Guarantee Scheme** — accessible financial support, a credit-guarantee framework, financial/credit information, and capacity-building programmes."]},{"h4":"Incentives for investors in a labelled startup"},{"key":"An angel investor, venture capitalist, private equity fund, accelerator or incubator investing in a labelled startup is entitled to an **investment tax credit of 30%** of the investment — applied against tax on any taxable investment gains. Gains on disposing of a labelled startup's assets held in Nigeria for **≥24 months** are **tax-exempt** entirely."},{"p":"The startup label is issued via the Startup Portal, valid for **10 years**, and is managed by the National Council for Digital Innovation and Entrepreneurship (chaired by the President). A labelled startup must comply with extant business laws, report annually on headcount/assets/turnover and incentives received, maintain proper books, notify structural/objects changes within **1 month**, and comply with post-issuance obligations — defaulting triggers a **30-day** rectification window before label withdrawal (and possible reissuance once the default is cured). A Startup Investment Fund (≥N10bn/year, managed by the Nigeria Sovereign Investment Authority) finances labelled startups, early-stage ventures, and tech labs/accelerators/incubators/hubs."},{"note":"Common criticisms: overemphasis on legislation alone (infrastructure/talent/market access also matter); implementation challenges (bureaucracy, funding, clarity); limited demonstrated SME-growth impact; and uncertainty over whether benefits materialise without proper execution."},{"note":"**Traceability to the study text's own sub-sections.** The book divides this section into the numbered sub-sections below. Every one of them is covered in the material above, which groups the discussion by subject rather than repeating each heading; use this table to go from a printed sub-section number straight to the right part of this section."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Study text sub-section","Heading"],"rows":[["22.15.1","Objectives of Nigeria Startup Act, 2022."],["22.15.2","Companies covered by the provisions of the Act"],["22.15.3","Composition, functions, and powers of the National Council for Digital"],["22.15.4","Startup labelling process, obligations, withdrawal and reissuance of startup label"],["22.15.6","Tax and fiscal benefits available to labelled startups"],["22.15.7","Incentives and reliefs for investors investing in a labelled startup"],["22.15.8","Criticism of tax incentives"]]}}]},{"n":"22.16","t":"Tax implication of the establishment of the Defence and Security Infrastructure Fund — section 59(6) of NTA","b":[{"key":"The Fund implements a national security scheme and develops Nigeria's defence industry/infrastructure. **10%** of the development levy imposed is credited to the Fund, used **exclusively** for operational security-enhancement purposes."},{"p":"Jointly managed by: the Nigerian Army, Navy and Air Force; Nigerian Police Force; Department of State Security; Nigerian Security and Civil Defence Corps; Nigerian Forest Security Service; Defence Intelligence Agency; and the Office of the National Security Adviser — each agency's share paid directly to its own CBN account, for its own establishment-law functions (including maintenance, reporting and accountability obligations)."},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Agency","Share of the 10% Fund allocation"],"rows":[["Defence Headquarters","8%"],["Nigerian Army","20%"],["Nigerian Navy","12.5%"],["Nigerian Air Force","12.5%"],["ONSA / Counter-Terrorism Centre","6%"],["Nigeria Police Force","10%"],["Police Trust Fund","8%"],["Department of State Security","9%"],["Nigerian Security and Civil Defence Corps","5%"],["Nigerian Forest Security Service","5%"],["Defence Intelligence Agency","4%"]]}},{"note":"The National Assembly may review this allocation/distribution/management from time to time, to reflect need and emerging challenges."},{"note":"**Traceability to the study text's own sub-sections.** The book divides this section into the numbered sub-sections below. Every one of them is covered in the material above, which groups the discussion by subject rather than repeating each heading; use this table to go from a printed sub-section number straight to the right part of this section."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Study text sub-section","Heading"],"rows":[["22.16.1","Establishment of fund"],["22.16.2","Management of Fund"],["22.16.3","Fund allocation details"],["22.16.4","Modification"]]}}]},{"n":"22.17","t":"Taxation of lottery and gaming trade or business — section 62 of NTA","b":[{"def":{"t":"Gaming","d":"gambling, wagering, video poker, roulette, craps, bingo, slot/gaming machines, drawings, or other games of chance conducted by any person."}},{"def":{"t":"Lottery/lotteries","d":"any betting, game, scheme, arrangement, system, plan, promotional competition or device for distributing prizes by lot/chance, skill-and-chance, real/virtual sporting-event outcomes, or any other such game/scheme/arrangement/plan/device."}},{"key":"Lottery/gaming income is taxed under the ordinary NTA provisions — with additional allowable deductions (beyond the standard Chapter Two list): amounts paid as winnings/prizes/similar payments from the relevant Prize Fund; agency commission expenses; and levies paid to relevant regulatory/government authorities under federal/state law."},{"note":"**Traceability to the study text's own sub-sections.** The book divides this section into the numbered sub-sections below. Every one of them is covered in the material above, which groups the discussion by subject rather than repeating each heading; use this table to go from a printed sub-section number straight to the right part of this section."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Study text sub-section","Heading"],"rows":[["22.17.1","Definitions"],["22.17.2","Taxation of income"]]}}]},{"n":"22.18","t":"Taxation of digital assets and virtual assets related activities","b":[{"def":{"t":"Electronic/digital activities (s.147 NTAA)","d":"receiving/emitting/transmitting signals/sounds/messages/images/data by cable/radio/electromagnetic/other electronic or wireless means, for any commerce/trade/activity — e-commerce, app stores, high-frequency trading, electronic data storage, online adverts, participative network platforms, online gaming, cloud computing, online teaching, digital content, user-data supply, search engines, or online payments."}},{"def":{"t":"Virtual asset (7th ¶, Fifth Schedule to NTAA)","d":"a digital value representation, digitally tradeable/transferable, for payment/investment/financial purposes — cryptocurrencies, tokens, digital collectibles — EXCLUDING national/foreign currencies, CBN-licensed electronic money, loyalty/reward-programme instruments, and ISA-2007-governed digital asset representations."}},{"p":"Further terms: **VASP** (a virtual-asset exchange/custody/management service provider for clients); **AML** (anti-money-laundering measures); **CTF** (counter-terrorism-financing measures); **KYC** (client identity verification)."},{"h4":"VASP registration, returns and obligations"},{"key":"Anyone engaged in virtual-asset activities (exchange, trading, custody, issuance) must register with the tax authority as a VASP (¶1, Fifth Schedule to NTAA)."},{"p":"A VASP must, with or without notice, submit **monthly** returns (in addition to ordinary company/individual income tax returns) containing: a description of the virtual-asset service; transaction date; asset type/value; sales value; customer name/address/phone/email/Tax ID (and NIN, if an individual); counterparty details; and other prescribed particulars (s.25 NTAA) — the tax authority may demand further information at any time."},{"p":"Taxable virtual-asset transactions: sale/exchange/transfer; mining/staking that generates income; airdrops/bounties/reward-received assets; and any other virtual-asset-related transaction. Payment for goods/services made IN virtual assets is taxed identically to fiat-currency payment, valued at the market price at the transaction time, reported as income by the recipient."},{"p":"Valuation uses the prevailing market price at the transaction time, per a Service-approved recognised virtual-asset exchange platform."},{"h4":"VASP compliance obligations"},{"ol":["keep records/books and report virtual-asset activities;","report large/suspicious transactions to the Service and the NFIU under AML law;","obtain a Special Control Unit Against Money Laundering (SCUML) Certificate;","report suspicious AML/CTF/fraud-related activity to the NFIU within **48 hours**;","provide periodic customer-transaction reports to the Service;","maintain accurate KYC customer information; and","retain all customer transaction/identification records for **≥7 years** after the last transaction."]},{"p":"KYC/AML due diligence: collect personal ID details; conduct risk assessments and ongoing transaction monitoring; implement internal AML/CTF controls; report suspicious activity to law enforcement; implement cybersecurity protections; and conduct internal AML/KYC/tax/reporting compliance audits."},{"warn":"Loss from digital/virtual-asset sales/disposals/transactions is deductible **only** against digital/virtual-asset profit/gain (s.28(3)(b)(iv)) — already established in earlier chapters. Non-compliance by a VASP (s.109 NTAA): an administrative penalty of **N10,000,000 in the first month of default**, plus **N1,000,000 per subsequent month**, or suspension/revocation of the SEC operating licence."},{"note":"**Traceability to the study text's own sub-sections.** The book divides this section into the numbered sub-sections below. Every one of them is covered in the material above, which groups the discussion by subject rather than repeating each heading; use this table to go from a printed sub-section number straight to the right part of this section."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Study text sub-section","Heading"],"rows":[["22.18.1","Definitions"],["22.18.2","Interpretations based on the Seventh Paragraph to the Fifth Schedule to the"],["22.18.3","Registration as a Virtual Assets Service Provider - paragraph 1 of the Fifth"],["22.18.4","Filing of returns - section 25 of NTAA"],["22.18.5","Taxable transactions - paragraph 2 of the Fifth Schedule to the NTAA"],["22.18.6","Valuation of virtual assets -paragraph 3 of the Fifth Schedule to the NTAA"],["22.18.7","Filing of returns - section 25 of NTAA"],["22.18.8","Taxpayer obligations - paragraph 4 of the Fifth Schedule to the NTAA"],["22.18.9","VASP reporting obligations - paragraph 5 of the Fifth Schedule to the NTAA"],["22.18.10","Customer due diligence and Know Your Customer (KYC) Obligations"],["22.18.11","Treatment of loss - section 28(3)(b)(iv) of NTA"],["22.18.12","Penalty for non-compliance with the provisions of NTAA by VASP -section"]]}}]},{"n":"22.19","t":"Digital economy (including taxation of income from e-commerce and e-business)","b":[{"p":"E-commerce has no single standard academic definition — most converge on \"transactions conducted using electronic networks/methods to exchange information, products, services and payments.\" The 2025 OECD technical paper defines an e-commerce transaction as the sale/purchase of goods/services conducted over computer networks by methods specifically for placing/receiving orders — payment and delivery need not themselves be concluded online. OECD guidance **includes** orders via webpages/apps/extranets/EDI, but **excludes** orders by phone, fax, on-premise kiosks/QR codes, or manually typed messages (including email)."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["E-commerce","E-business"],"rows":[["Commercial transactions conducted over the internet.","The conduct of business processes on the internet generally."],["Electronic transmission for buying/selling products/services.","Also includes information exchange directly related to buying/selling."],["Essentially monetary transactions.","Also includes procurement, customer education, supplier sourcing, etc."],["Usually requires just a website.","Involves CRM/ERP systems connecting different business processes."],["Mandatorily uses the internet.","May use the internet, an intranet, or an extranet."],["A narrower concept, restricted to buying/selling.","A broader concept — market surveying, supply-chain/logistics management, data mining."]]}},{"h4":"Nigerian government efforts to track e-commerce/e-business income"},{"ol":["the National Digital Trustmark initiative (10 October 2025) — verified e-commerce platforms display a trustmark seal for safe-transaction identification;","CAMA 2020's incorporation/registration/management requirements, extending to online businesses;","the significant economic presence (SEP) rules under s.17 NTA (already established in Chapter 18/20);","profits/gains from digital-asset transactions taxed at CIT 30% or graduated PIT (0%-25%) (s.4 NTA);","mandatory VASP registration and monthly returns (s.25 NTAA);","the VAT fiscalisation system (s.157 NTA/s.23 NTAA) requiring electronic-fiscal-system use for taxable supplies;","non-resident taxable-supply/VAT-withholding rules (s.150 NTA), explicitly extending to digitally-supplied services (cloud hosting, e-commerce platforms, streaming) and applying the destination principle;","the controlled-foreign-company/minimum-ETR rules (s.6 NTA);","stamp duty on electronic money transfers above N10,000 (Ninth Schedule);","a specialised Non-Resident Persons Tax Office (NRPTO) at the NRS;","mandatory registration of global tech giants (Netflix, LinkedIn, Google, Facebook/Meta, etc.) to pay tax and collect VAT on their platforms;","the TaxPro Max platform (2021), giving the NRS real-time visibility into electronic transactions via banks/fintechs/payment service providers;","continual NRS staff capacity development on emerging tax issues; and","the OECD/UNDP \"Tax Inspectors Without Borders\" initiative, pairing experts with local officials on real-time audits."]},{"h4":"CIT, VAT and WHT implications on e-commerce"},{"p":"CIT: s.6 NTA taxes all profits accruing/derived/brought into/received in Nigeria; s.17 NTA assesses a non-resident with a fixed base, an authorised agent, or an on-the-ground contract (surveys/deliveries/installation/construction) in Nigeria — but a non-resident with none of these (no fixed place, no agency relationship, objects not matching Nigerian tax legislation) presents a real practical enforcement gap."},{"p":"VAT: the destination principle governs (s.150 NTA) — VAT is charged where goods/services are consumed, not produced; \"taxable supply\" (s.201) covers any sale-of-goods or service-performance transaction for consideration, so an internet-placed order does not avoid VAT once delivery occurs; imported-goods value includes all non-VAT taxes/charges and delivery-related costs (s.149); non-resident suppliers register using the address of their contracting Nigerian counterparty (s.4 NTAA)."},{"p":"WHT: once source/destination principles are clearly tracked (via e-payment/bank/authority liaison and annual-return cross-checks), WHT becomes straightforwardly applicable to goods/services consumed in Nigeria, increasing revenue capture."},{"h4":"Enduring complexities in taxing e-commerce"},{"p":"E-commerce challenges traditional tax administration in several interlocking ways: **income characterisation** (is cross-border digital income business profit, royalty, or technical-service fees — each taxed differently); **establishing a permanent establishment**, given the diminished need for physical presence; **establishing identity and location** of transacting parties, given internet addresses have a tenuous link to actual physical location; **obtaining acceptable documentary proof**, especially where records sit in a tax haven or are encrypted; and **VAT/customs administration**, since digitised goods (e.g. downloadable music) may bypass the traditional tangible-\"goods\" VAT trigger entirely, and \"on-line\" digital supplies pose a harder place-of-supply challenge than \"off-line\" (physically delivered) internet orders, which customs can still handle at the point of importation as before."},{"note":"Several alternative international policy approaches have been proposed to address these challenges — the **base-erosion approach** (withholding at a single rate on payments that erode a source country's tax base, while keeping the PE concept largely intact); the **virtual PE approach** (taxing only \"core\"/mainstream business activity at source, leaving ancillary activity untaxed there — more neutral, but hard to standardise internationally); and the **refundable withholding approach** (a hybrid: uniform-rate withholding on electronically-provided/purchased income, refundable/net-basis-electable once a seller's gross sales into a jurisdiction cross a threshold)."},{"p":"Business-profit tax treaties generally rest on 3 principles: **residence** (a resident is taxed on worldwide business profits, with residence-source double taxation relief; a non-resident is generally taxed only on profits attributable to a PE, with tie-breaker rules — e.g. the OECD Model's \"place of effective management\" test — resolving dual-residence claims); the **PE nexus/threshold** (a fixed place of business through which an enterprise wholly/partly operates, excluding merely preparatory/auxiliary-activity locations, but extending to an agent habitually concluding contracts on the non-resident's behalf) — with specific exceptions allowing source-country taxation even absent a PE, for immovable-property income, entertainer/athlete performance income, certain gross-basis-taxed payments (dividends/interest/royalties/technical fees), insurance-premium income, and services exceeding **183 days** presence in a 12-month period; and **separate-entity accounting with the arm's-length principle** — each branch/subsidiary of a multinational is taxed as a separate entity, with intra-group transaction prices readjusted to reflect independent-enterprise terms, using the traditional methods (comparable uncontrolled price; resale-price-minus-margin; cost-plus) or, where those cannot be reliably applied, the transactional profit-split or transactional net margin methods."},{"p":"The 2025 OECD Model Tax Convention update further addressed: cross-border home-working creating a taxable PE presence for employers; expanded source-country rights over natural-resource-extraction income; transfer-pricing rules for financial transactions; broader use of cross-border exchanged information (even for unnamed/other persons, without needing the sending state's prior authorisation); and dispute resolution guided by \"Amount B\" of the OECD's Pillar One reforms — a pre-set range of expected returns for routine local branch marketing/distribution activity, reducing arm's-length-pricing disputes for jurisdictions that adopt it (optional, from fiscal years starting 1 January 2025)."},{"note":"**Traceability to the study text's own sub-sections.** The book divides this section into the numbered sub-sections below. Every one of them is covered in the material above, which groups the discussion by subject rather than repeating each heading; use this table to go from a printed sub-section number straight to the right part of this section."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Study text sub-section","Heading"],"rows":[["22.19.1","Introduction"],["22.19.2","Definitions of e-commerce"],["22.19.3","Differences between e-commerce and e-business"],["22.19.4","Efforts made by the government to track income arising from e-commerce and e"],["22.19.5","Current treaty rules for taxing business profit: implications for e-commerce"],["22.19.6","Complexities of e-commerce taxation"],["22.19.8","Taxation issues in e-commerce"],["22.19.9","Issues, approaches and solutions in taxation of e-commerce"]]}}]},{"n":"22.20","t":"Chapter review","b":[{"p":"This chapter comprehensively covered the computation of tax for a selected range of specialised companies — air and sea transport, cable (communications) undertakings, life and non-life insurance, property companies, and authorised unit trust outfits — and discussed the issues and challenges of taxing e-business and e-commerce, with worked examples illustrating the computational differences across specialised businesses."}]},{"n":"22.21","t":"Worked examples — study text questions and suggested solutions","b":[{"p":"The study text sets **four questions** at §22.21.1 — all four full computations on specialised businesses (insurance, foreign air transport, agriculture and a unit trust scheme) — with its own suggested solutions following. All four are reproduced here in full, with the book's answers, every figure independently recomputed and cross-footed, and added explanation."},{"note":"The book heads the solutions **\"Suggested solutions to examination type questions\"** rather than §22.21.2, which is the pattern used in every other chapter."},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 22.21.1, Question 1","t":"Zen Insurance Plc — companies income tax across life, non-life and reinsurance business","open":true,"q":[{"p":"**Zen Insurance Plc** is a company engaged in both life and other insurance businesses. The company was incorporated in 2024 but commenced business on **2 January 2025**. The draft projected financial statements for the year ending **31 December 2026** disclosed the following (all figures N'000):"},{"table":{"align":"lrrrr","head":["Projected statement of profit or loss","Life business","Non-life business","Reinsurance business","Total"],"rows":[["Gross premium written","22,000","150,000","78,000","250,000"],["Gross premium income","22,000","150,000","78,000","250,000"],["Re-insurance premium","0","(58,000)","0","(58,000)"],["Net premium income","22,000","92,000","78,000","172,000"],["Fees and commission income","2,500","6,300","4,200","13,000"],["Net underwriting income","24,500","98,300","82,200","135,000"],["Claims expenses","(12,500)","(24,000)","(21,000)","(57,500)"],["Re-insurance claims","6,000","15,000","0","21,000"],["Change in contract liabilities","700","(2,300)","0","(1,600)"],["Net claims expenses","(5,800)","(11,300)","(21,000)","(38,100)"],["Acquisition expenses","(300)","(1,400)","(990)","(2,690)"],["Maintenance expenses","(1,550)","(4,330)","(1,800)","(7,680)"],["Total underwriting expenses","(1,850)","(5,730)","(2,790)","(10,370)"],["**Underwriting profit/(loss)**","**16,850**","**81,270**","**58,410**","**156,530**"],["Investment income","5,800","12,600","13,700","32,100"],["Other operating income","1,800","3,800","2,400","8,000"],["Total investment income","7,600","16,400","16,100","40,100"],["Impairment charges","(450)","(1,330)","(1,080)","(2,860)"],["Net fair value gain/loss on investment properties","220","680","340","1,240"],["Net operating income","7,370","15,750","15,360","38,480"],["Administrative expenses","(3,900)","(18,000)","(9,500)","(31,400)"],["Other operating expenses","(880)","(1,100)","(1,420)","(3,400)"],["Total expenses","(4,780)","(19,100)","(10,920)","(35,800)"],["Result of operating activities","19,440","77,920","62,850","160,210"],["Interest expenses","(3,600)","(7,500)","(4,800)","(15,900)"],["**Profit or (loss) before taxation**","**15,840**","**70,420**","**58,050**","**73,310**"]]}},{"table":{"align":"lrrrr","head":["Statement of financial position","Life business","Non-life business","Reinsurance business","Total"],"rows":[["**Assets**","","","",""],["Cash and cash equivalents","900,000","1,660,000","1,220,000","3,780,000"],["Financial assets","1,600,000","2,800,000","2,300,000","6,700,000"],["Trade receivable","28,000","52,000","44,000","124,000"],["Other receivables","1,400","4,600","750","6,750"],["Investment in subsidiary","0","3,500","0","3,500"],["Intangible assets","22,000","14,900","26,000","62,900"],["Property, plant and equipment","1,200,000","3,550,000","2,400,000","7,150,000"],["Statutory deposits","5,300,000","9,500,000","20,400,000","35,200,000"],["**Total assets**","**9,051,400**","**17,585,000**","**26,390,750**","**53,027,150**"],["**Liabilities**","","","",""],["Insurance contract liabilities","1,300,000","5,280,000","1,520,000","8,100,000"],["Investment contract liabilities","800,000","1,180,000","1,020,000","3,000,000"],["Trade payable","11,200","18,800","16,000","46,000"],["Other payables","600","1,200","130","1,930"],["Employee benefit liabilities","520","680","550","1,750"],["**Total liabilities**","**2,112,320**","**3,480,680**","**2,556,680**","**11,149,680**"],["**Equity** — issued and paid-up share capital","","","","35,200,000"],["Share premium","","","","1,800,000"],["General reserve","","","","2,600,000"],["Contingency reserve","","","","400,000"],["Retained earnings","","","","1,877,470"],["**Shareholders' funds**","","","","**41,877,470**"],["**Total liabilities and reserves**","","","","**53,027,150**"]]}},{"p":"**Additional information:** **(i)** the company distributed **N3,500,000** surplus arising from actuarial revaluation of the life fund; **(ii)** administrative expenses include depreciation — life **N960,000**, non-life **N1,200,000**, reinsurance **N1,050,000**; **(iii)** premium written from non-life and reinsurance business include **N15,000,000** and **N13,600,000** from general insurance; **(iv)** projected net liability on life policies at 31 December 2026 is **N1,298,000**; **(v)** capital allowances agreed — life **N250,000**, non-life **N800,000**, reinsurance **N650,000**; **(vi)** investment income includes, in N'000 — dividend (gross) life 1,200 / non-life 3,000 / reinsurance 5,500; interest on fixed deposit 3,300 / 7,600 / 6,900; debenture interest 1,300 / 2,000 / 1,300; **(vii)** the provision for unexpired risk includes, in N'000 — balance b/f life 900 / non-life 3,000 / reinsurance 1,220, and balance c/f 1,300 / 5,280 / 1,520; **(viii)** the projected balance of the life fund account at 31 December 2026 is **N1,100,000**; **(ix)** the minimum authorised capital of the company is the same as the paid-up capital; **(x)** estimated claims and outgoings omitted in arriving at the profit before tax are, in N'000 — life 14,500 / non-life 35,000 / reinsurance 30,500."},{"p":"**Required:** Compute the companies income tax liability for the relevant tax year."}],"a":[{"h4":"(a)  Life business — 2027 assessment year (N'000)"},{"stmt":{"t":"Zen Insurance Plc — life business","rows":["Investment income:",["Interest on fixed deposit","3,300","","@ind"],["Debenture interest","1,300","4,600","@ind"],"Other income:",["Fees and commission income","","2,500","@ind"],["Other operating income","","1,800","@ind"],["Actuarial revaluation surplus distributed","","3,500","@ind"],["Gross income","","12,400","@t"],"Deduct:",["(i) General reserve 2,600 + life fund 1,100 = 3,700, restricted to the net liability on policies","1,298","","@ind"],["(ii) Special reserve — higher of 1% of gross premium (220) and 10% of net profit (1,584)","1,584","","@ind"],["(iii) Administrative expenses (3,900 − 960 depreciation)","2,940","","@ind"],["  Interest expense","3,600","","@ind"],["  Other operating expenses","880","(10,302)","@ind"],["Assessable profit","","2,098","@t"],["Less: capital allowance","","(250)"],["Total profit","","1,848","@t"],["Companies income tax — 0% on total profit","","Nil","@ind"],["Federal development levy — not applicable","","Nil","@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(b)  Non-life business — 2027 assessment year (N'000)"},{"stmt":{"t":"Zen Insurance Plc — non-life business","rows":[["Premium written","150,000",""],["Less: premium returned on cancelled policies","(0)","","@ind"],["Less: re-insurance premium","(58,000)","","@ind"],["Gross premium","","92,000","@t"],"Add:",["Interest income","7,600","","@ind"],["Fees and commission income","6,300","","@ind"],["Other investment income — debenture interest","2,000","","@ind"],["Other income","3,800","","@ind"],["Re-insurance claim","15,000","34,700","@ind"],["Gross income","","126,700","@t"],["Less: reserve for unexpired risk on a time-apportionment basis (5,280 − 3,000)","","(2,280)"],["","","124,420","@t"],"Less: allowable expenses",["Administrative expenses (18,000 − 1,200)","16,800","","@ind"],["Other operating expenses","1,100","","@ind"],["Total claims and outgoings claimable — actual 24,000 + estimated 35,000","59,000","","@ind"],["Interest expense","7,500","(84,400)","@ind"],["Assessable profit","","40,020","@t"],["Less: capital allowance","","(800)"],["Total profit","","39,220","@t"],["Companies income tax (N39,220,000 × 30%)","","11,766","@ind"],["Federal development levy (4% × N40,020,000)","","1,600.80","@tt"]]}},{"h4":"(c)  Reinsurance business — 2027 assessment year (N'000)"},{"stmt":{"t":"Zen Insurance Plc — reinsurance business","rows":[["Gross premium","78,000",""],["Less: premium returned on cancelled policies","(0)","","@ind"],["Net premium","","78,000","@t"],"Add:",["Interest income","6,900","","@ind"],["Fees and commission income","4,200","","@ind"],["Debenture interest","1,300","","@ind"],["Other operating income","2,400","14,800","@ind"],["Gross income","","92,800","@t"],["Less: reserve for unexpired risk on a time-apportionment basis (1,520 − 1,220)","","(300)"],["","","92,500","@t"],"Less: allowable expenses",["Administrative expenses (9,500 − 1,050)","8,450","","@ind"],["Other operating expenses","1,420","","@ind"],["Total claims and outgoings claimable — actual 21,000 + estimated 30,500","51,500","","@ind"],["Interest expense","4,800","(66,170)","@ind"],["","","26,330","@t"],["Less: transfer to general reserve — 50% of gross (underwriting) profit, 58,410 × 50%","","(29,205)"],["Assessable loss","","(2,875)","@t"],["Capital allowance carried forward","","(650)"],["Loss and capital allowance carried forward","","(3,525)","@tt"]]}},{"h4":"Summary of tax liabilities, 2027 assessment year (N'000)"},{"table":{"align":"lrrrr","head":["","Life business","Non-life business","Reinsurance business","Total"],"rows":[["CIT payable","0.00","11,766","0.00","11,766"],["Federal development levy","0.00","1,600.80","0.00","1,600.80"],["**Total tax payable**","**0.00**","**13,366.80**","**0.00**","**13,366.80**"]]}},{"h4":"The book's own notes on this answer"},{"ol":["**Life business — section 61(10)(a) of NTA** provides for the deduction from the investment income of a life insurance company of an amount which makes a general reserve and fund **equal to the net liabilities on policies in force at the time of an actuarial valuation**. The implication is that the amount deductible as general reserve and fund shall **for no reason be more than the net liabilities on policies** — it is restricted to that maximum yearly. Hence **N1,298,000** was deducted, being the net liabilities on policies.","**Non-life and reinsurance business — section 61(9)(a) of NTA** provides for the deduction of a **reserve for unexpired risks, calculated on a time-apportionment basis** of the risks accepted in the year. A reserve can only be claimed on **actual risks accepted in the year** — that is, on an actual-year basis. Hence **N2,280,000** (N5,280,000 less N3,000,000) for non-life and **N300,000** (N1,520,000 less N1,220,000) for reinsurance.","**Section 61(9)(b) of NTA** provides for the deduction of **outstanding claims and outgoings**: an amount equal to the total estimated amount of all outstanding claims and outgoings shall be deducted, **provided that any amount not utilised** towards settlement of claims and outgoings **shall be added to the total profits of the following year**. So what is deductible in any year is the total of **both the actual and the estimated** claims and outgoings, and any unutilised amount is added back the following year — a trend expected to continue yearly.","The applicable **companies income tax rates** are in line with section 56 of NTA: **0%** was applied to the total profit of the life business, while **30%** and **0%** were applied to the total profit/(loss) of the non-life and reinsurance business respectively."]},{"key":"The reason the three rates differ is the point of the whole question, and the book never says it outright: each class of business is tested **separately against the small-company threshold**. Life gross premium is N22 million and reinsurance N78 million — both **N100 million or less**, so both are taxed at **0%** under section 56. Non-life gross premium is N150 million, **above** the threshold, so it bears the full **30%**. That is also why the federal development levy, which follows the charge to tax, falls only on the non-life business."},{"note":"Three recurring adjustments run through all three columns and are worth isolating. **Dividend (gross) is excluded** from investment income in every column — N1,200, N3,000 and N5,500 — because it is **franked investment income** on which the withholding deduction is the final tax (§14.9). **Depreciation inside administrative expenses is added back** — N960, N1,200 and N1,050 — because relief comes through capital allowances instead. And **interest expense is allowed in full** in each column."},{"note":"Note what is **not** adjusted and why. Impairment charges, net fair value gains on investment properties, acquisition and maintenance expenses, and the whole statement of financial position play no part: the computation is built **up from gross income** under section 61, not down from the accounting profit before tax. That is the structural difference between an insurance computation and the ordinary adjusted-profit computation of §20.8 — and it is why the profit before taxation of N15,840, N70,420 and N58,050 never appears as a starting figure."},{"note":"The **special reserve** in the life column takes the **higher** of 1% of gross premium and 10% of net profit: 1% × N22,000 = N220 against 10% × N15,840 = N1,584, so **N1,584**. And the **transfer to general reserve** in the reinsurance column takes **50% of gross underwriting profit** (N58,410 × 50% = N29,205) because the general reserve is **below** the minimum paid-up capital; had it exceeded the minimum authorised capital, the rate would have been **25%**."},{"warn":"Two presentational defects in the book's printed reinsurance column, flagged rather than silently corrected: the two alternative general-reserve rates are laid out as \"(i) … Or (ii) …\" with the second limb left without a figure, so it is not obvious that only limb (i) is applied; and the assessable loss is printed as **\"( 2,875\"** with the closing bracket missing. The arithmetic is sound: N92,500 − N66,170 − N29,205 = **(N2,875)**."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 22.21.1, Question 2","t":"Habib Limited — a foreign air transport company, and the minimum tax comparison","open":true,"q":[{"p":"**Habib Limited** is a foreign company involved in air transport business. Its aircraft are used for cargo and passenger flights between Nigeria and Spain. The projected financial statements for the year ending **31 December 2026** revealed the following:"},{"stmt":{"t":"Habib Limited — projected results, year ending 31 December 2026","rows":[["Income for passengers flown from **Madrid to Nigeria**","","1,104,425,600"],["Income for passengers flown from **Nigeria to Cape Town**","","1,113,397,250"],["Income from cargo loaded into aircraft — **other routes**","","1,122,260,000"],["Income from cargo freight **from Nigeria to Cape Town**","","1,103,375,000"],["","","4,443,457,850","@t"],"Deduct: operating expenses",["Depreciation","5,460,000","","@ind"],["Staff salaries","1,840,725,000","","@ind"],["Use of airport facilities","12,987,000","","@ind"],["Accommodation for airline crew","75,228,500","","@ind"],["Hotel bills for passengers","53,120,000","","@ind"],["General provisions","735,000","(1,988,255,500)","@ind"],["Operating profits","","2,455,202,350","@tt"]]}},{"p":"In addition to the above, **capital allowances were agreed with the relevant tax authority as 175% of depreciation**."},{"p":"**Required:** Compute the companies income tax (CIT) liability for the relevant assessment year."}],"a":[{"stmt":{"t":"Habib Limited — income tax computation for the 2027 assessment year","rows":[["Income from passengers' freight from Nigeria to Cape Town","","1,113,397,250"],["Income from cargo freight from Nigeria to Cape Town","","1,103,375,000"],["","","2,216,772,250","@t"],"Allowable deductions:",["Staff salaries","1,840,725,000","","@ind"],["Use of airport facilities","12,987,000","","@ind"],["Accommodation for airline crew","75,228,500","","@ind"],["Hotel bills for passengers","53,120,000","","@ind"],["","1,982,060,500","","@ind"],["Proportion applicable to operations in Nigeria (W1)","988,819,263","","@ind"],["Depreciation allowance as agreed (175% × N5,460,000)","9,555,000","(998,374,263)","@ind"],["Total profit","","1,218,397,987","@t"],["Companies income tax payable (30% × N1,218,397,987)","","365,519,396.10","@tt"]]}},{"h4":"W1 — apportioning the expenses to Nigerian operations"},{"tex":"\\dfrac{N2{,}216{,}772{,}250}{N4{,}443{,}457{,}850} \\times N1{,}982{,}060{,}500 = N988{,}819{,}263"},{"h4":"Minimum tax comparison"},{"tex":"\\text{Minimum tax} = 2\\% \\times N2{,}216{,}772{,}250 = N44{,}335{,}445"},{"p":"Minimum tax is **2% of gross revenue relating to the carriage of passengers and cargo freight from Nigeria**. Since the CIT of **N365,519,396.10** exceeds the minimum tax of **N44,335,445**, the **CIT payable is N365,519,396.10**."},{"key":"The whole question turns on the word **\"from\"**. Only income from the carriage of passengers and cargo **from Nigeria** is taxable here, so two of the four revenue lines are **excluded**: passengers flown **Madrid to Nigeria** (inbound) and cargo loaded on **other routes**. That is N1,104,425,600 + N1,122,260,000 = N2,226,685,600 of revenue left out, almost exactly half. Nigeria taxes the outbound carriage because that is where the contract of carriage is performed from."},{"key":"The second mechanism is the **apportionment of expenses**. The expenses are incurred across the whole global operation, so only the Nigerian proportion is deductible — and the ratio used is **Nigerian taxable revenue to total revenue**, here 2,216,772,250 / 4,443,457,850 ≈ **49.89%**. Deducting the full N1,982,060,500 against only the Nigerian revenue would produce an almost nil profit, which is precisely what the section exists to prevent."},{"note":"Two expenses are **excluded from the apportionment pool entirely**: **depreciation** (N5,460,000), which is never allowable and is replaced by the agreed capital allowance, and **general provisions** (N735,000), which are not an incurred expense at all. The capital allowance is given as **175% of depreciation** — an agreed ratio basis under section 18 — so N5,460,000 × 1.75 = **N9,555,000**, and it is deducted in full rather than apportioned."},{"note":"Why the **minimum tax** is computed at all: for a foreign air or sea transport company, section 18(6) of the NTA charges tax on the **higher** of the profit computed on the adjusted-profit/ratio basis and **2% of the full sum receivable** in respect of carriage from Nigeria. Always compute both and state the comparison — the mark is for showing that the higher figure was chosen, not merely for arriving at it. Here the ordinary computation wins comfortably."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 22.21.1, Question 3","t":"XYZ Limited — an agricultural company, capital allowances and the five-year exemption","open":true,"q":[{"p":"**XYZ Limited** is an agricultural company which commenced business on **1 July 2025**. It is engaged in plantations, cattle ranching and poultry business, and prepares its financial statements to **31 December** every year. Its projected financial statements showed the following results:"},{"table":{"align":"lrrr","head":["","Year ending 31 Dec 2026 (N)","Year ending 31 Dec 2027 (N)","Year ending 31 Dec 2028 (N)"],"rows":[["**Revenue:**","","",""],["Plantation crops","—","—","4,012,000"],["Cattle ranching proceeds","2,475,000","5,329,000","15,868,000"],["Poultry proceeds","1,120,000","2,590,000","7,500,000"],["**Total revenue**","**3,595,000**","**7,919,000**","**27,380,000**"],["**Expenses:**","","",""],["Preliminary expenses","125,000","125,000","125,000"],["Purchase: cockerels","764,000","1,708,005","6,000,000"],["Cattle purchased","1,000,000","1,600,000","4,050,000"],["Poultry feeds","739,065","1,764,920","6,574,550"],["Wages and salaries","1,050,000","2,565,500","5,067,500"],["Drugs and medicines for animals","109,685","110,625","111,025"],["Interest on bank loan","—","17,100","18,000"],["General expenses","41,500","41,720","51,500"],["Depreciation","450,600","733,420","733,420"],["Increase in closing inventory","—","(4,200)","(5,100)"],["**Net (loss)/profit**","**(684,850)**","**(743,090)**","**4,654,105**"],["","**3,595,000**","**7,919,000**","**27,380,000**"]]}},{"p":"**Other additional information.** Preliminary expenses amounted to **N1,000,000** and are to be written off in equal annual amounts over **eight (8) years**, commencing from the period ending 31 December 2026. The breakdown of the preliminary expenses is:"},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["","N"],"rows":[["(i) Stamp duties and registration expenses","115,000"],["(ii) Cost of initial clearing and cultivation of land for plantation","35,000"],["(iii) Cost of nursery plants purchased from the Ministry of Agriculture","65,000"],["(iv) Another nursery plants purchased from an Institute of Agriculture","45,720"],["(v) Cost of labour and technical expertise on the first planting operation on plantations","28,030"],["(vi) Gratifications to local chiefs and heads of families, so as to attract labourers to the farm","711,250"],["","**1,000,000**"]]}},{"p":"The following details were extracted from the company's register of property, plant and equipment (there was **no disposal** of any asset within the period):"},{"table":{"align":"lrl","head":["Asset","Cost (N)","Date of purchase"],"rows":[["Motor vehicles","12,030,500","January 2026"],["Office furniture","2,445,000","January 2026"],["Agric tractor","51,127,000","February 2028"],["Equipment used in spraying plantations","960,000","February 2028"],["Building (administrative block)","22,571,000","March 2028"]]}},{"p":"**Required:** Compute the total profits of the company, if any, for the relevant years of assessment."}],"a":[{"h4":"W1 — computation of adjusted profits/(loss)"},{"table":{"align":"lrrr","head":["","2027 (N)","2028 (N)","2029 (N)"],"rows":[["Net (loss)/profit per accounts","(684,850)","(743,090)","4,654,105"],["Add back: preliminary expenses","125,000","125,000","125,000"],["Add back: depreciation","450,600","733,420","733,420"],["**Adjusted (loss)/profit**","**(109,250)**","**115,330**","**5,512,525**"]]}},{"h4":"W2 — determination of basis periods"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["YOA","Basis period for assessment","Basis period for capital allowances"],"rows":[["2027","1/7/26 – 31/12/26","1/7/26 – 31/12/26"],["2028","1/1/27 – 31/12/27","1/1/27 – 31/12/27"],["2029","1/1/28 – 31/12/28","1/1/28 – 31/12/28"]]}},{"h4":"W3 — computation of annual allowances"},{"table":{"align":"lrrrrrr","head":["","Building (10%)","Motor vehicle (25%)","Plantation (10%)","Plantation equipment (20%)","Office furniture (20%)","Total allowance"],"rows":[["**AY2027** — acquisitions","","","","","",""],["Jan 2026 — motor vehicles","—","12,030,500","—","—","—",""],["Jan 2026 — plantation (W4)","—","—","173,750","—","—",""],["Jan 2026 — office furniture","—","—","—","—","2,445,000",""],["","—","12,030,500","173,750","—","2,445,000","14,649,250"],["Annual allowance (6/12 — six-month basis period)","—","(1,503,813)","(8,688)","—","(244,500)","**1,757,001**"],["WDV c/f","—","10,526,687","165,062","—","2,200,500",""],["**AY2028** — annual allowance (full year)","—","(3,007,625)","(17,375)","—","(489,000)","**3,514,000**"],["WDV c/f","—","7,519,062","147,687","—","1,711,500",""],["**AY2029** — additions: Mar 2028 building","22,571,000","","","","",""],["Feb 2028 — spraying equipment","","","","960,000","",""],["Feb 2028 — agric tractor","","","","51,127,000","",""],["","22,571,000","7,519,062","147,687","52,087,000","1,711,500",""],["Annual allowance","(2,257,100)","(3,007,625)","(17,375)","(10,417,400)","(489,000)","**16,188,500**"],["WDV c/f","20,313,900","4,511,437","130,312","41,669,600","1,222,500",""]]}},{"h4":"W4 — the preliminary expenses that qualify as capital expenditure"},{"p":"**(a)** Expenditure in accordance with paragraph 1(1) of the First Schedule to the NTA. The capitalised costs consist of:"},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["","N"],"rows":[["(i) Cost of initial clearing and cultivation","35,000"],["(ii) Cost of nursery plants for first planting (N65,000 + N45,720)","110,720"],["(iii) Cost of labour and technical expertise on first planting","28,030"],["","**173,750**"]]}},{"p":"**(b)** **Stamp duties and registration expenses** have been **disallowed**, as they are incurred in bringing the company into existence and not for the purpose of producing the profits assessable to tax."},{"p":"**(c)** **Gratifications to local chiefs and heads of families** have been **disallowed**, because the expenditure was not incurred wholly and exclusively for the purpose of producing the company's profit or loss."},{"p":"**(d)** **Section 162(1)(p) of NTA, 2025 and the Thirteenth Schedule** to the Act exempt the income generated by companies engaged in agricultural businesses — including crop production, livestock, aquaculture, forestry, dairy and such other businesses as described in the Thirteenth Schedule — **for the first five years upon commencement of business**."},{"p":"**(e)** **Small companies** are legally exempt from paying companies income tax."},{"h4":"Computation of total profits"},{"stmt":{"t":"XYZ Limited — total profits for assessment years 2027, 2028 and 2029","rows":["2027 assessment year (basis period 1/7/26 – 31/12/26)",["Loss for the year","","(109,250)","@ind"],["Capital allowance for the year — deemed utilised","1,757,001","","@ind"],["Loss carried forward","","(109,250)","@ind"],["Companies income tax","","Nil","@ind"],["Federal development levy","","Nil","@ind"],"@gap","2028 assessment year (basis period 1/1/27 – 31/12/27)",["Assessable profit","","115,330","@ind"],["Unrelieved loss brought forward","(109,250)","","@ind"],["Relieved in the year","109,250","(109,250)","@ind"],["Profit not subject to tax","","6,080","@ind"],["Capital allowance for the year","3,514,000","","@ind"],["Capital allowance brought forward","1,757,001","","@ind"],["Capital allowances deemed utilised","5,271,001","","@ind"],["Total profit","","0","@ind"],["Companies income tax","","Nil","@ind"],["Federal development levy (not applicable)","","Nil","@ind"],"@gap","2029 assessment year (basis period 1/1/28 – 31/12/28)",["Assessable profit (not subject to tax)","","5,512,525","@ind"],["Capital allowance for the year (W3)","16,188,500","","@ind"],["Capital allowance brought forward","5,271,001","","@ind"],["Capital allowances deemed utilised","21,459,501","","@ind"],["Total profit — not subject to tax","","5,512,525","@ind"],["Companies income tax (0% of total profit)","","Nil","@ind"],["Federal development levy (4% of assessable profit)","","Nil","@tt"]]}},{"key":"The answer to this question is **nil tax in all three years**, and the reason is given at (d): section 162(1)(p) and the Thirteenth Schedule exempt the income of an agricultural company **for its first five years of business**. XYZ commenced in 2025, so the exemption covers every year in the question. The capital allowances are therefore **deemed utilised** rather than carried forward — the same mechanism as the small-company rule at §16.11, Question 3 — and the federal development levy, which follows the charge to tax, does not arise either."},{"note":"The **preliminary expenses** are where most of the marks sit, and the split at W4 is the point. The N1,000,000 is **never deductible as a revenue expense** — writing it off over eight years changes nothing, so the N125,000 charged each year is added back. But N173,750 of it is **qualifying capital expenditure** on a plantation under paragraph 1(1) of the First Schedule (clearing and cultivation, nursery plants for the first planting, and the labour and technical expertise of that first planting), and so attracts capital allowances at 10%. The **stamp duties and registration** (N115,000) are capital costs of incorporation, and the **gratifications to local chiefs** (N711,250) fail the \"wholly and exclusively\" test — neither qualifies for anything."},{"note":"Note also that the **purchase of cockerels and cattle** is treated as a revenue expense and left alone. That is consistent with §15.10.2: animals qualify as capital expenditure only where they are **herds raised for breeding**, or function as **apparatus** with a life expectancy of more than five years — not animals raised for sale, which is trading stock."},{"warn":"Two defects in the study text's own solution, flagged rather than silently corrected. **(1) The basis periods do not match the question.** The question says the company commenced on **1 July 2025**, which with a 31 December year end would make the first accounting period 1/7/25 – 31/12/25 (assessed in 2026) and the year ended 31/12/26 a **full twelve months** assessed in 2027. The solution instead uses a **six-month** first basis period of **1/7/26 – 31/12/26**, which corresponds to commencement on 1 July **2026**. The six-month apportionment of the first capital allowance (1,503,813 = 25% × 12,030,500 × 6/12) follows from the solution's own reading, and is reproduced above for consistency with it — but on the question as written the first allowance would be a **full** N3,007,625. **(2) The carry-forward figures do not reconcile.** The solution shows capital allowances brought forward into 2029 as **N1,941,366**, a figure that cannot be derived from its own schedule: N1,757,001 (2027) + N3,514,000 (2028) = **N5,271,001**, which is the cumulative figure used above, giving N21,459,501 deemed utilised by 2029 rather than the book's N18,129,866. Since all of it is deemed utilised under the exemption, **neither figure changes the tax**, which is nil throughout."},{"note":"Three further typographical slips in the printed solution: the motor vehicle cost appears once as **\"1112,030,500\"**; the 2029 basis period is printed as \"1/1/28 – **31/12/128**\"; and the 2029 total profit is shown as **\"15,512.525\"** where the adjusted profit is **N5,512,525**. None affects the computation."}]}},{"eg":{"tag":"Study text — 22.21.1, Question 4","t":"Apex Unit Trust Scheme — assessable profit of an authorised unit trust","open":true,"q":[{"p":"**Apex Unit Trust Scheme** is authorised by the Investment and Securities Commission to deal in the business of a unit trust scheme. Its projected financial statements for the year ending **30 September 2026** revealed the following (N'000):"},{"stmt":{"t":"Apex Unit Trust Scheme — projected results, year ending 30 September 2026","rows":["Investment income:",["Dividend (gross)","","120,000","@ind"],["Bank deposit interest (gross)","","115,000","@ind"],["Rental income","","225,000","@ind"],["","","460,000","@t"],"Deduct:",["Staff salaries and wages","5,000","","@ind"],["Managers' remuneration (20% of gross income)","98,000","","@ind"],["Other expenses","33,000","","@ind"],["Depreciation","18,000","(154,000)","@ind"],["Net profit","","306,000","@tt"]]}},{"p":"**Notes:** **(i)** both dividend and rental income have been subjected to deduction of withholding tax at **10%**; **(ii)** other expenses include an amount of **N550,000** being **loss on disposal of property, plant and equipment**."},{"p":"**Required:** Compute the assessable profit for the relevant year of assessment."}],"a":[{"stmt":{"t":"Apex Unit Trust Scheme — computation of assessable profit for the 2027 assessment year (N'000)","rows":[["Net profit per accounts","","306,000"],"Add:",["Depreciation","","18,000","@ind"],["Loss on disposal of property, plant and equipment","","550","@ind"],["","","324,550","@t"],"Deduct:",["Franked investment income","","(120,000)","@ind"],["Assessable profit","","204,550","@tt"]]}},{"h4":"Other points to note"},{"ol":["**withholding tax deducted from any income other than franked investment income is set off** against any tax assessable on the unit trust scheme for any year of assessment; and","**dividend received after deduction of tax is regarded as franked investment income** and is not subject to any further tax in the hands of the recipient."]},{"key":"Only **three adjustments** are needed, and the question is testing whether you make exactly those. **Depreciation** is added back (never allowable, s.21). The **loss on disposal of PPE** is added back because it is a capital loss, not a trading expense. And the **gross dividend** is deducted as **franked investment income** — the 10% withheld is the final tax, so it leaves the computation and **no credit is claimed for it**. Everything else, including the managers' remuneration, stands."},{"note":"Contrast the treatment of the two incomes that both suffered 10% withholding tax. The **dividend** is franked investment income: taken out of the computation, with no credit. The **rental income** stays in and is taxed at the graduated corporate rate, so the WHT on **it** is creditable against the resulting liability — which is exactly what note (a) says. The same distinction appears in Chapter 15, Question 4 (Mr Sulaimon)."},{"note":"The **bank deposit interest** is left in the computation. Note that the §14.5.5 exclusion for interest on short-term securities and corporate bonds applies to an **individual**, not to a unit trust scheme, so it does not take this interest out of charge."},{"warn":"An internal inconsistency in the question, flagged rather than resolved: the managers' remuneration is described as \"**20% of gross income**\" and shown as **N98,000**, but 20% of the gross income of N460,000 is **N92,000**. The solution does not adjust the remuneration either way, so the assessable profit of N204,550 is unaffected — but if a question asks you to test the remuneration against its stated basis, use **N92,000** and add back the N6,000 excess."},{"note":"Note the units. The accounts are in **N'000**, so note (ii)'s \"N550,000\" of loss on disposal appears as **550** in the computation. Mixing the two scales is the easiest way to lose this question: adding back 550,000 instead of 550 would overstate the assessable profit by more than twice over."}]}}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Global adjusted profit ratio (foreign air/sea transport, Method 1)","tex":"\\text{Ratio} = \\dfrac{\\text{Adjusted profit}}{\\text{Gross revenue}} \\times 100%"},{"lb":"Global depreciation ratio","tex":"\\text{Ratio} = \\dfrac{\\text{Depreciation allowance}}{\\text{Gross revenue}} \\times 100%"},{"lb":"Minimum tax — foreign air/sea transport","tex":"\\text{Minimum tax} = 2% \\times \\text{Gross carriage receipts in Nigeria}"},{"lb":"Investment tax credit for a labelled-startup investor","tex":"\\text{Credit} = 30% \\times \\text{Amount invested}"}],"focus":"Given this chapter's exceptional breadth, focus revision on the businesses with genuine computational mechanics rather than the purely descriptive ones: foreign air/sea transport (the two-ratio Method 1 pro-forma, and the 2% minimum-tax floor), insurance (the life-vs-non-life premium/claims treatment split, the unexpired-risk time-apportionment mechanic, and the higher-of/lower-of reserve rules), and unit trusts (the simple investment-income-less-management-expense formula). For the descriptive sectors (REITs, NGOs, export zones, Islamic finance, startups, gaming, digital assets), anchor on the SPECIFIC numeric thresholds each carries (REIT's 75%/12 months; export zone's 25%/2028; startup's 3+2 years and 30% investor credit; VASP's N10m+N1m penalty and 7-year retention) since these are what the exam actually tests, rather than the surrounding policy narrative.","errors":["Applying the foreign air/sea transport Method 1 ratio to GLOBAL adjusted profit instead of NIGERIA-SOURCE gross revenue — the chapter's own stated pro-forma is explicit that the ratio multiplies Nigeria-receivable revenue, not the worldwide profit figure (a distinction this chapter's own Illustration 22.1 gets wrong relative to its own stated method, while Illustration 22.2 gets right).","Treating life-insurance premium as taxable income, or life-insurance claims paid as deductible — for life business, premium never counts as income (since a payout is certain) and claims are correspondingly never deductible; the reverse rule applies for non-life business.","Allowing a loss from one insurance class (life) to offset profit from another (non-life) within the same composite insurer — each class is assessed, and loss-relieved, entirely separately, with no cross-class relief permitted.","Confusing REIT/REIC dividend exemption conditions with ordinary dividend-substitution exclusions — the REIT-specific rule requires BOTH a 75% redistribution rate AND a 12-month redistribution deadline; missing either condition makes the income taxable in the REIC's own hands.","Assuming an export processing zone entity is permanently and unconditionally tax-exempt — the exemption is conditional on the ≤25%-customs-territory-sales test, and disappears entirely for customs-territory sales from 1 January 2028 onward, regardless of the sales percentage at that point."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"For a life insurance company, how is premium received from the assured treated for tax purposes, and what follows for claims paid?","o":["Premium is taxable income, and claims paid are deductible","Premium does NOT count as income, so claims paid are correspondingly NOT deductible","Premium is exempt but claims paid remain fully deductible","Both premium and claims are ignored entirely for tax purposes","Premium is taxed at a flat 10% rate regardless of claims"],"a":1,"w":"For life assurance business, premium received does not form part of taxable income (since the assured will definitely be paid on death or policy maturity) — correspondingly, claims paid are not an allowable deduction. The reverse applies for non-life business.","src":"Chapter 22.6","sec":"22.6"},{"q":"What is the minimum tax payable by a company engaged in air or sea transport business, under s.18(6) of the NTA?","o":["1% of gross revenue","2% of the gross amount for carriage of passengers/mail/livestock/goods shipped or loaded in Nigeria","5% of total profit","30% of assessable profit","There is no minimum tax for this sector"],"a":1,"w":"Section 18(6) NTA sets a minimum tax floor of 2% of the gross amount receivable for carriage of passengers/mail/livestock/goods shipped or loaded into an aircraft/ship in Nigeria (excluding VAT and levies collected on behalf of others).","src":"Chapter 22.4","sec":"22.4"},{"q":"For a REIC's dividend/rental income to be exempt from further tax under s.162(1)(c) of the NTA, what conditions must BOTH be met?","o":["At least 50% redistributed within 6 months","At least 75% redistributed within 12 months of the relevant financial year-end","At least 100% redistributed within 24 months","At least 75% redistributed within 30 days","No specific percentage or deadline applies"],"a":1,"w":"The REIC exemption requires BOTH conditions: at least 75% of dividend/rental income redistributed to shareholders, AND that redistribution occurring within 12 months of the relevant financial year-end.","src":"Chapter 22.2","sec":"22.2"},{"q":"From what date will an export processing zone entity's profits from customs-territory sales become fully taxable, regardless of the percentage of sales involved, under the Second Schedule to the NTA?","o":["1 January 2026","1 January 2027","1 January 2028","1 January 2030","This never happens — the exemption is permanent"],"a":2,"w":"Paragraph 5 of the Second Schedule provides that from 1 January 2028, an export processing zone entity's profits are fully taxable on customs-territory sales, regardless of what percentage of total sales those represent.","src":"Chapter 22.9","sec":"22.9"},{"q":"A Virtual Asset Service Provider (VASP) fails to comply with the NTAA's provisions for 3 months. What administrative penalty applies, under s.109 of the NTAA?","o":["A flat N1,000,000","N10,000,000 for the first month, plus N1,000,000 for each subsequent month","N5,000,000 per month, capped at N15,000,000","2% of gross turnover","Automatic and immediate licence revocation, with no fine option"],"a":1,"w":"Section 109 NTAA sets the penalty at N10,000,000 in the first month of default, plus N1,000,000 for each subsequent month of continued default — or suspension/revocation of the SEC operating licence.","src":"Chapter 22.17","sec":"22.17"}],"theory":[{"q":"Apex Unit Trust Scheme, SEC-authorised, had these results for the year ending 30 September 2026: dividend (gross) N120,000,000; bank deposit interest (gross) N115,000,000; rental income N225,000,000 (total investment income N460,000,000); less staff salaries/wages N5,000,000, managers' remuneration (20% of gross income) N98,000,000, other expenses N33,000,000 (including N550,000,000... correction: N550,000 loss on disposal of PPE), and depreciation N18,000,000 (total deductions N154,000,000); net profit N306,000,000. Both dividend and rental income suffered 10% WHT. Compute the assessable profit for the relevant year of assessment.","marks":8,"a":[{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Apex Unit Trust Scheme — assessable profit, 2027 assessment year","N'000"],"rows":[["Net profit per accounts","306,000"],["Add: depreciation","18,000"],["Loss on disposal of PPE","550"],["Subtotal","324,550"],["Less: franked investment income (dividend, gross)","(120,000)"],["**Assessable profit**","**204,550**"]]}},{"note":"Depreciation and the loss on disposal of PPE are added back as disallowable/non-trading items, consistent with ordinary adjusted-profit computation; the gross dividend is then excluded entirely as franked investment income (already suffering final WHT), while rental income and bank deposit interest (also WHT-suffered, but not final tax) remain in the assessable base — mirroring the same dividend-vs-other-investment-income distinction already established for individuals in earlier chapters."}],"src":"Chapter 22.21","sec":"22.7"},{"q":"Habib Limited, a foreign company operating cargo/passenger flights between Nigeria and Spain, had these projected results for the year ending 31 December 2026: income from passengers flown Madrid-to-Nigeria N1,104,425,600; passengers flown Nigeria-to-Cape Town N1,113,397,250; cargo loaded on other routes N1,122,260,000; cargo freight Nigeria-to-Cape Town N1,103,375,000 (total N4,443,457,850); less operating expenses — depreciation N5,460,000, staff salaries N1,840,725,000, use of airport facilities N12,987,000, crew accommodation N75,228,500, passenger hotel bills N53,120,000, general provisions N735,000 (total N1,988,255,500); operating profit N2,455,202,350. Capital allowances were agreed at 175% of depreciation. Compute the CIT liability for the relevant assessment year.","marks":12,"a":[{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Habib Limited — CIT computation, 2027 assessment year","N"],"rows":[["Income — passengers Nigeria to Cape Town","1,113,397,250"],["Income — cargo freight Nigeria to Cape Town","1,103,375,000"],["**Nigeria-source gross revenue**","**2,216,772,250**"],["Allowable deductions (salaries + airport facilities + crew accommodation + hotel bills, excluding depreciation and general provisions)","1,982,060,500"],["Proportion applicable to Nigeria operations (2,216,772,250/4,443,457,850 × 1,982,060,500)","(988,819,263)"],["Less: capital allowance (175% × 5,460,000)","(9,555,000)"],["**Total profit**","**1,218,397,987**"],["**Companies income tax payable (30%)**","**365,519,396.10**"]]}},{"note":"Income from passengers flown INTO Nigeria (Madrid to Nigeria) and cargo on other (non-Nigeria) routes are correctly excluded from the Nigeria-source revenue base — the former is exempt under s.18(2)(a) as a route terminating in Nigeria but loaded outside it, and the latter has no Nigerian nexus at all. Minimum tax (2% × N2,216,772,250 = N44,335,445) is well below the computed CIT, so the ordinary CIT figure governs."}],"src":"Chapter 22.21","sec":"22.4"}]}},{"n":23,"t":"Federal Development Levy","brief":"A short, self-contained chapter on the new consolidated levy that replaced four separate statutory levies — its purpose, rate, exemptions, distribution to seven beneficiary agencies/funds, and the NTAA offences/penalties regime that backs it.","outcomes":["Explain the purpose and effective date of the levy","Discuss the administration of the levy","Identify the rate and compute the levy payable","Identify taxpayers that are exempt from payment of the levy","Identify every beneficiary agency and fund","Explain the requirement that every beneficiary agency/fund submit income and expenditure to the National Assembly for appropriation","Discuss the offences and penalties"],"secs":[{"n":"23.2","t":"Purpose and effective date of the levy","b":[{"key":"The **Federal Development Levy (FDL)** consolidates and **replaces** four previously separate statutory levies — the **Tertiary Education Tax (TET/TETFund)**, the **Police Trust Fund levy**, the **NITDA levy**, and the **NASENI levy**. This directly resolves the TET-vs-FDL naming question left open in Chapter 19: they are not two competing names for the same thing, but the old regime (TET, taxed separately under CITA) being folded into the new, single, broader FDL under the NTA."},{"p":"The funds raised are earmarked for specific government priorities — critical infrastructure and social services. Consolidating four levies into one is meant to simplify tax compliance and support ease of doing business."},{"p":"Effective commencement date: **1 January 2026**."}]},{"n":"23.3","t":"Administration of the levy — section 59(2) of NTA","b":[{"p":"The Nigeria Revenue Service is empowered to collect the levy and pay it into a special account created for that purpose."},{"note":"The levy is **not** charged on assessable profits computed for hydrocarbon tax purposes — hydrocarbon tax is a separate, self-contained regime."}]},{"n":"23.4","t":"Rate and computation of the levy — section 59(1) of NTA","b":[{"key":"The development levy is **4%** of the **assessable profits** of all companies/enterprises chargeable to tax, other than small companies and non-resident companies."},{"note":"The statute's own wording bases the levy on **assessable profit** — the pre-capital-allowance figure, per the definition already established consistently across Chapters 20/21 (assessable profit, less capital allowances, gives total profit). This distinction matters directly for Illustration 23.3 below."}]},{"n":"23.5","t":"Taxpayers that are exempt from the payment of the levy — section 59(1) of NTA","b":[{"ul":["**Small companies** — annual gross turnover ≤N100 million **and** total fixed assets not exceeding N250 million; and","**Non-resident companies** that do not have a permanent establishment in Nigeria."]}]},{"n":"23.6","t":"Distribution of the levy paid into a special account created for that purpose — section 59(3) of NTA","b":[{"p":"Revenue from the levy is paid into a special account and spread amongst 7 beneficiary agencies/funds:"},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Beneficiary","Share"],"rows":[["Tertiary Education Trust Fund","50%"],["Nigerian Education Loan","15%"],["National Information Technology Development Fund","8%"],["National Agency for Science and Engineering Infrastructure","8%"],["National Board for Technological Incubation","4%"],["Defence and Security Infrastructure Fund","10%"],["National Cybersecurity Fund","5%"]]}},{"note":"This 10% Defence and Security Infrastructure Fund share is the same Fund, and the same 10%, already detailed in full (down to its own 11-agency internal allocation) in Chapter 22's treatment of specialised businesses — cross-referenced rather than repeated here."}]},{"n":"23.7","t":"Requirement that every beneficiary agency and Fund prepare and submit their income and expenditure to the National Assembly for appropriation — section 59(5) of NTA","b":[{"p":"Every beneficiary agency/fund listed above must prepare and submit its income and expenditure to the **National Assembly for appropriation**."}]},{"n":"23.8","t":"Offence and penalties","b":[{"p":"The levy is backed by the same general NTAA offences and penalties framework that applies across the tax system generally. The study text numbers them §23.8.1 to §23.8.7, and each is tagged with its number in the table below."},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Offence","Section","Penalty"],"rows":[["**23.8.1** Failure to register for tax","s.100 NTAA","N50,000 in the first month of default, plus N25,000 for each subsequent month. A statutory body/company awarding a contract to an unregistered person is separately liable to N5,000,000."],["**23.8.2** Failure to file returns (or knowingly filing incomplete/inaccurate returns)","s.101 NTAA","N100,000 in the first month of default, plus N50,000 for each subsequent month."],["**23.8.3** Failure to keep books/accounts/records (or refusal to produce them on request)","s.102 NTAA","N10,000 for an individual; N50,000 for a company."],["**23.8.4** Failure to collect/deduct/withhold tax where obligated","s.105 NTAA","40% of the amount not deducted."],["**23.8.5** Failure to remit tax deducted/collected/withheld by the 21st of the following month","s.107 NTAA","The amount not remitted, PLUS an administrative penalty of 10% per annum of that amount, PLUS interest at the prevailing CBN monetary policy rate. (The same 3-part penalty applies to failure to self-account within the prescribed time.)"],["**23.8.6** General penalty — any contravention with no specific penalty provided","s.127 NTAA","An administrative penalty of N1,000,000; or, on conviction, imprisonment for up to 3 years, or a fine, or both."],["**23.8.7** Making up an incorrect account/schedule/statement, or giving false/misleading information affecting tax liability","s.131 NTAA","The higher of N15,000,000 or 1% of the tax undercharged (or that would have been undercharged) as a result — plus the appropriate tax that would have been charged. On conviction, this instead becomes a fine of N15,000,000 plus 1% of the undercharged tax (whichever figure is higher), plus the appropriate tax due."]]}}]},{"n":"23.9","t":"Chapter review","b":[{"p":"This chapter discussed the Federal Development Levy — its purpose, effective commencement date, beneficiary agencies/funds, taxpayer exemptions, and the offences/penalties regime that enforces it."}]},{"n":"23.10","t":"Worked examples — study text questions and suggested solutions","b":[{"p":"The study text sets **three questions** at §23.10.1, with its own suggested solutions at §23.10.2. All three are reproduced here in full, with the book's answers, every figure independently recomputed, and added explanation. The book prints them as questions, not illustrations — there are no illustrations in this chapter."},{"eg":{"t":"Rota Limited — computing the levy from total profit","q":[{"p":"Rota Limited has been in business for 4 years. Extract from its financial records for the year ended 31 December 2026: capital allowances for the year N2,400,000; total profit N800,000; depreciation N960,000; revenue N110,000,000. **Required**: compute the federal development levy for the relevant assessment year."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Rota Limited — FDL computation, 2027 assessment year","N"],"rows":[["Total profit","800,000"],["Add: capital allowances","2,400,000"],["**Assessable profit**","**3,200,000**"]]}},{"p":"Federal development levy = 4% × N3,200,000 = **N128,000**."},{"note":"Depreciation and revenue are given but genuinely unused here — depreciation plays no role since the starting point is already TOTAL profit (a tax-adjusted figure), not accounting net profit; revenue is irrelevant to the computation itself (though at N110m it would, in any case, rule out the small-company exemption on its own)."}],"tag":"Study text — 23.10.1, Question 1","open":true}},{"eg":{"t":"Harpic Limited — computing the levy from accounting net profit","q":[{"p":"Harpic Limited commenced business in 2009. Projected financial information for the year ended 31 December 2026: net profit per accounts N1,407,600; depreciation N100,200; allowance for doubtful debts N250,000; donations to ABC political party N420,000. **Required**: compute the federal development levy for the relevant assessment year."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Harpic Limited — FDL computation, 2027 assessment year","N"],"rows":[["Net profit per accounts","1,407,600"],["Add: depreciation","100,200"],["Allowance for doubtful debts (general provision, disallowed)","250,000"],["Donations to ABC political party (disallowed)","420,000"],["**Assessable profit**","**2,177,800**"]]}},{"p":"Federal development levy = 4% × N2,177,800 = **N87,112**."},{"note":"Both add-backs follow rules already established earlier in the subject: a GENERAL (not specific) allowance for doubtful debts is disallowed, and donations to a political party are never a deductible business expense."}],"tag":"Study text — 23.10.1, Question 2","open":true}},{"eg":{"t":"JAPA Industries Plc — assessable profit, the levy, exemption and policy","q":[{"p":"JAPA Industries Plc operates several manufacturing businesses in Nigeria. Projected results for the year ended 31 December 2026: turnover N5,800,000,000; profit before tax N1,650,000,000; capital allowances N450,000,000; disallowed expenses N150,000,000. The company's property, plant and equipment is valued at N1.8 billion; it is Nigerian-resident and fully taxable; it does not engage in hydrocarbon operations; and it has already paid companies income tax but not yet computed the federal development levy. **Required**: (a) determine the assessable profit; (b) compute the FDL payable; (c) explain whether the company qualifies for exemption; (d) explain the policy objective behind the levy."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["(a)/(b) JAPA Industries Plc — FDL computation, 2027 assessment year","N"],"rows":[["Profit before tax","1,650,000,000"],["Add: disallowed expenses","150,000,000"],["**Assessable profit**","**1,800,000,000**"],["Less: capital allowances","(450,000,000)"],["Total profit","1,350,000,000"]]}},{"p":"**Federal development levy = 4% × N1,800,000,000 = N72,000,000.**"},{"warn":"The source's own printed solution instead computes FDL as 4% × N1,350,000,000 = N54,000,000 — mislabelling the POST-capital-allowance figure (N1,350,000,000, which is properly \"total profit\" under this subject's own established terminology from Chapters 20/21) as \"assessable profit,\" and deducting capital allowances **before** applying the levy. This directly contradicts both s.59(1)'s own text (FDL is charged on \"assessable profits,\" the pre-capital-allowance base) and this very chapter's own Illustrations 23.1 and 23.2, which correctly compute assessable profit by ADDING capital allowances back to (not deducting them from) profit. The N72,000,000 figure above follows the statute's own definition and this chapter's own consistent practice elsewhere; the source's printed N54,000,000 answer is reproduced here for completeness but should not be treated as the more reliable figure."},{"p":"**(c) Exemption test**: JAPA does **not** qualify for exemption — turnover (N5.8bn) exceeds N100 million, fixed assets (N1.8bn) exceed N250 million, and it is not a small company. FDL is payable."},{"p":"**(d) Policy objectives**: the FDL was introduced to (i) consolidate multiple statutory levies into a single levy; (ii) reduce tax multiplicity in Nigeria; (iii) improve tax administration efficiency; and (iv) provide funding for education, technology, security infrastructure, and scientific development."}],"tag":"Study text — 23.10.1, Question 3","open":true}},{"key":"Read the three together and the one rule that matters is clear: the federal development levy is **4% of assessable profit** — the figure struck **before** capital allowances. Question 1 starts from **total profit** (already after capital allowances) and so must **add them back**. Question 2 starts from **accounting net profit** and so must add back the disallowable items to reach the same base. Question 3 starts from **profit before tax** and needs only the disallowed expenses added — the capital allowances are a distraction, which is exactly where the book's own solution goes wrong."},{"note":"Note how the exemption works, since every question supplies turnover and some supply fixed assets. A **small company** — turnover of **N100 million or less** and fixed assets **not exceeding N250 million** — is exempt from companies income tax under section 56, and because the levy is charged on assessable profit brought into charge, such a company pays **no levy either** (§21.7.3). Rota's N110 million turnover and JAPA's N5.8 billion both put them outside the exemption."}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Federal development levy","tex":"\\text{FDL} = 4% \\times \\text{Assessable profit}"}],"focus":"This is a short, high-yield chapter — the whole thing reduces to one rate (4%), one base (assessable profit — before capital allowances, per s.59(1)'s own text), two exemption conditions (small company OR no Nigerian PE), and the 7-way distribution table. Know that the FDL replaced TETFund/Police Trust Fund/NITDA/NASENI levies outright, rather than existing alongside them. The offences/penalties table is shared infrastructure across the whole NTAA, not FDL-specific, but this is the first chapter to lay it out in full, so expect it to be examined from here.","errors":["Deducting capital allowances BEFORE applying the 4% FDL rate — the levy is charged on ASSESSABLE profit (the pre-capital-allowance figure), consistent with Illustrations 23.1/23.2's own add-back approach, not on TOTAL profit (the post-capital-allowance figure) as the source's own Illustration 23.3 solution mistakenly does.","Assuming the Federal Development Levy is an additional, NEW levy layered on top of the Tertiary Education Tax — it is not; FDL replaced TET (and NASENI, Police Trust Fund, and NITDA levies) entirely, from 1 January 2026.","Applying the small-company FDL exemption using only the turnover test — both conditions (turnover ≤N100m AND fixed assets ≤N250m) must be satisfied together; failing either one alone makes the company liable for FDL."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"What is the rate of the Federal Development Levy, and what is it charged on, per s.59(1) of the NTA?","o":["2% of total profit","4% of assessable profit","4% of total profit","10% of turnover","5% of gross revenue"],"a":1,"w":"Section 59(1) NTA imposes the development levy at 4% of assessable profits (the pre-capital-allowance figure), on all companies/enterprises chargeable to tax other than small companies and non-resident companies without a Nigerian PE.","src":"Chapter 23.3","sec":"23.3"},{"q":"Which four previously separate levies did the Federal Development Levy replace?","o":["VAT, WHT, CIT, and stamp duty","TETFund, Police Trust Fund, NITDA levy, and NASENI levy","PAYE, CGT, PIT, and CIT","Import duty, excise duty, export duty, and stamp duty","It did not replace any existing levy — it is entirely new"],"a":1,"w":"The FDL consolidates and replaces the Tertiary Education Tax (TETFund), the Police Trust Fund levy, the NITDA levy, and the NASENI levy into one single levy.","src":"Chapter 23.2","sec":"23.2"},{"q":"What are the TWO conditions a company must BOTH satisfy to qualify as a \"small company\" exempt from the Federal Development Levy?","o":["Turnover ≤N50m and fixed assets ≤N100m","Turnover ≤N100m and total fixed assets ≤N250m","Turnover ≤N500m only","Fixed assets ≤N100m only","Turnover ≤N100m and profit before tax ≤N10m"],"a":1,"w":"The small-company FDL exemption requires BOTH: annual gross turnover of N100 million or less, AND total fixed assets not exceeding N250 million.","src":"Chapter 23.4","sec":"23.4"},{"q":"What share of Federal Development Levy revenue is allocated to the Tertiary Education Trust Fund, per s.59(3) of the NTA?","o":["15%","25%","50%","75%","100%"],"a":2,"w":"Of the 7-way distribution under s.59(3), the Tertiary Education Trust Fund receives the largest single share at 50%.","src":"Chapter 23.5","sec":"23.5"},{"q":"What administrative penalty applies under s.107 of the NTAA for failing to remit tax already deducted/collected/withheld, by the 21st of the following month?","o":["A flat N1,000,000 fine only","The amount not remitted, plus 10% per annum administrative penalty, plus interest at the prevailing CBN monetary policy rate","40% of the amount not remitted, with no interest","Automatic imprisonment with no fine option","N50,000 in the first month, N25,000 per subsequent month"],"a":1,"w":"Section 107 NTAA imposes a 3-part liability: the unremitted amount itself, an administrative penalty of 10% per annum of that amount, and interest at the prevailing CBN monetary policy rate.","src":"Chapter 23.7","sec":"23.7"}],"theory":[{"q":"Zaki Nigeria Limited, a resident manufacturing company (not a small company), had the following results for the year ended 31 December 2026: profit before tax N540,000,000; disallowed expenses N60,000,000; capital allowances N120,000,000. Compute the federal development levy payable for the relevant assessment year, showing the assessable profit clearly.","marks":6,"a":[{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Zaki Nigeria Limited — FDL computation, 2027 assessment year","N"],"rows":[["Profit before tax","540,000,000"],["Add: disallowed expenses","60,000,000"],["**Assessable profit**","**600,000,000**"],["(Less: capital allowances — total profit, not the FDL base)","(120,000,000)"],["Total profit","480,000,000"]]}},{"p":"**Federal development levy = 4% × N600,000,000 (assessable profit) = N24,000,000.**"},{"note":"Capital allowances reduce assessable profit down to TOTAL profit — the base for companies income tax — but are NOT deducted before applying the FDL rate, since s.59(1) charges the levy on assessable profit specifically. This mirrors the correction flagged against Illustration 23.3 in the chapter itself."}],"src":"Chapter 23.10","sec":"23.3"},{"q":"Explain briefly why the Federal Development Levy is NOT charged on assessable profits computed for hydrocarbon tax purposes, and identify which TWO categories of taxpayer are exempt from the levy outright.","marks":5,"a":[{"p":"Hydrocarbon tax is a wholly separate, self-contained tax regime for upstream petroleum operations — s.59(2) NTA carves it out from the FDL base explicitly, so a company's hydrocarbon-tax assessable profit is never also subjected to the 4% development levy on top."},{"p":"The two exempt taxpayer categories (s.59(1)) are: **small companies** (turnover ≤N100 million AND fixed assets ≤N250 million); and **non-resident companies** that do not have a permanent establishment in Nigeria."}],"src":"Chapter 23.2","sec":"23.4"}]}},{"n":24,"t":"Chargeable Gains","brief":"How gains from disposing of chargeable assets are folded into a company's total profit under the NTA 2025 — replacing the old, separate Capital Gains Tax Act entirely. Covers what counts as a chargeable asset/disposal, how consideration and acquisition cost are determined (including market-value substitution rules), the core computation formula for both capital-allowance and non-capital-allowance assets, partial disposals, instalment sales, assets lost/destroyed, asset-location rules for cross-border assets, and a long list of specific exemptions.","outcomes":["Identify chargeable assets, chargeable disposal and chargeable gains","Know the significance of market value to chargeable gains for transactions not at arm's length","Understand allowable deductions for the purpose of ascertaining chargeable gains","Understand how to compute chargeable gains under the Nigeria Tax Act 2025","Understand the relevance of chargeable gains to a taxable person's income tax liability","Understand chargeable gains that are exempt from tax"],"secs":[{"n":"24.2","t":"Chargeable gains, chargeable assets, and chargeable disposal","b":[{"h4":"24.2.1  Chargeable gains"},{"key":"Chargeable gains are gains arising from the disposal of chargeable assets. The **NTA 2025 repealed the old Capital Gains Tax Act** — chargeable gains are no longer taxed as their own separate head of tax, but are **included directly in taxable income when determining a company's total profit**."},{"h4":"24.2.2  Chargeable assets"},{"def":{"t":"Chargeable asset","d":"ALL forms of property, whether situated in Nigeria or not — any asset, share, option, right, debt, digital/virtual asset, and incorporeal property generally; any currency other than Nigerian currency; and any property the disposing person created or came to own without acquiring it."}},{"h4":"24.2.3  Chargeable disposal"},{"def":{"t":"Chargeable disposal","d":"exists wherever any sum is derived from a sale, lease, transfer, assignment, compulsory acquisition or any other disposition of assets — even where the person paying the capital sum acquires no asset in return. Specifically includes sums: derived as compensation for loss of office/employment; received under an insurance policy for injury/damage/loss/depreciation of assets; received for forfeiting/surrendering a right, or for refraining from exercising a right; and received as consideration for the use/exploitation of an asset."}},{"note":"A disposal includes a **part disposal** — where an interest/right in/over the asset is created for another person, while part of the interest that existed before the disposal remains with the person making it."}]},{"n":"24.3","t":"Consideration for assets disposed","b":[{"p":"Ordinarily: cost of acquisition = amount paid to acquire the asset; consideration for disposal = the sales proceeds. **Market value substitutes** for the actual consideration where the disposal/acquisition is: not at arm's length; wholly/partly for consideration that cannot be valued; by a trustee for creditors of the disposing person; or on devolution on death (personal representative/legatee)."},{"h4":"Gifts (other than on devolution on death)"},{"p":"The acquirer is deemed to have acquired the asset for: the amount it was last disposed of by an arm's-length bargain; or, if that cannot be ascertained, its market value on the date of disposal."},{"h4":"Assets acquired by a creditor in debt satisfaction"},{"p":"The asset is not treated as disposed of/acquired for more than its market value at the time of the creditor's acquisition — and the creditor's eventual chargeable gain on disposal cannot exceed what it would have been had they acquired the property for an amount equal to the debt (or part of the debt) it satisfied."}]},{"n":"24.4","t":"Compulsory acquisition of land","b":[{"key":"Land disposed to an authority exercising compulsory-acquisition powers is **not** a chargeable gain, provided: the person had **not** acquired the land knowing/reasonably expecting it would be so acquired; **and** the person had **not** taken steps (advertisement or otherwise) to dispose of the land or signal willingness to dispose of it to the authority/others."},{"def":{"t":"Authority exercising compulsory powers","d":"an authority, person or body compulsorily acquiring land under the Land Use Act (or a similar foreign enactment), or authorised (directly or on another's behalf) to acquire it compulsorily for the purpose it is being acquired for."}}]},{"n":"24.5","t":"Date of acquisition or disposal","b":[{"p":"The default rule: the date at which there is an **enforceable right to acquire** or a **binding duty to dispose** of the asset (or any right/interest in it)."},{"ol":["a **conditional** contract: the date the condition is satisfied;","consideration NOT depending solely/mainly on the asset's value once the condition is satisfied: treated as if never conditional — the date of the contract itself; or","an **option** conferred by a contract: the date the option is **exercised**."]}]},{"n":"24.6","t":"Computation of chargeable gains","b":[{"key":"Two scenarios: (a) where **capital allowance has been claimed** on an asset used for a trade/business/profession/vocation — only the **residue (tax written down value)** of the asset is deducted from disposal proceeds; (b) where capital allowance has **not** been claimed — the actual consideration paid (in money or money's worth) wholly/exclusively for acquisition is deducted from disposal proceeds instead."},{"p":"In **either** scenario, any **incidental cost of disposal**, incurred wholly and exclusively for that purpose, is additionally deductible — including:"},{"ol":["cost of improvement made to enhance the asset's value ahead of sale;","expenditure establishing, preserving or defending title to (or a right over) the asset;","cost of making the disposal — surveyor/valuer/accountant/legal-adviser fees and commissions, plus advertising cost to find a buyer; and","incidental cost of the ORIGINAL acquisition — stamp duties, conveyance fees, other title-perfection costs, and advertising cost to find a seller."]}]},{"n":"24.7","t":"Partial disposal","b":[{"p":"Where an asset is only partly disposed, the acquisition cost (plus any value-enhancing expenditure, for a non-capital-allowance asset) — or the residue/tax-written-down value (for a capital-allowance asset) — is **apportioned** between the disposed and undisposed parts."},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Chargeable gains on the part disposed","N"],"rows":[["Sales proceeds of part sold","X"],["Less: cost of part sold — A/(A+B) × C","(X)"],["**Chargeable gains**","**X**"]]}},{"p":"Where: **A** = sales proceeds (or market value) of the part sold; **B** = market value of the unsold part; **C** = cost of acquisition (or residue/tax written down value, where capital allowance has been claimed) of the whole asset."},{"eg":{"t":"Illustration 24.1 — partial disposal (Maryland Limited)","q":[{"p":"Maryland Limited, a trading company, acquired a plot of land for business expansion at a cost of N30,000,000 in August 2023. Facing an urgent need for funds, management sold a portion of the land for N40,000,000 in June 2026. The market value of the unsold portion is N35,000,000. **Required**: assess and advise on the chargeable gains and the relevant year of assessment."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Maryland Limited — chargeable gains","N"],"rows":[["Sales proceeds","40,000,000"],["Deduct: cost of part sold — 40,000,000/(40,000,000+35,000,000) × 30,000,000","(16,000,000)"],["**Chargeable gains**","**24,000,000**"]]}},{"p":"The N24,000,000 chargeable gain is included in the company's total profit and subject to tax in the **2027 year of assessment** (the year following the June 2026 disposal)."}]}}]},{"n":"24.8","t":"Consideration due after the time of disposal — chargeable gains in instalments","b":[{"key":"Where consideration (or part of it) is payable in **instalments over more than 12 months**, the chargeable gain is deemed to accrue **proportionately**, in the year of disposal and each subsequent year until the final instalment is paid — deemed to accrue on the **last day** of each relevant assessment year, except on cessation of the trade/business or death of the alienator, when it accrues on the date of cessation/death instead."},{"p":"Chargeable gain for the year = (Consideration received in the year ÷ Total consideration receivable) × Total chargeable gains."},{"note":"Where part of the consideration brought into account is later shown, to the relevant tax authority's satisfaction, to be irrecoverable, an appropriate adjustment (discharge/repayment of tax or otherwise) is made."},{"h4":"Steps for computing instalment chargeable gains"},{"steps":["Compute the total chargeable gains.","Identify the date each instalment is paid.","Identify the assessment year each instalment date falls into.","Apply the proportion formula to compute the chargeable gain attributable to each instalment."]}]},{"n":"24.9","t":"Assets lost or destroyed","b":[{"p":"Where an asset is lost/destroyed and a compensation sum received is applied, **within 3 years** of receipt, to acquire a replacement asset:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Comparison","Effect"],"rows":[["Compensation + residual/scrap value **GREATER THAN** cost of the replacement asset","A **chargeable gain** is deemed to arise — equal to (compensation + residual value) − cost of the replacement asset."],["Compensation + residual/scrap value **LOWER THAN** cost of the replacement asset","For capital allowance purposes, an **additional asset** is deemed acquired, equal to (cost of the new asset) − (compensation + residual value)."]]}},{"key":"Except for that additional-asset amount (arising only in the \"lower\" case), capital allowance claimable on the **new** asset is otherwise **limited to the residue of the old asset**, if any — in BOTH scenarios."},{"eg":{"t":"Illustration 24.2 — replacement cost lower than compensation received (ABC Limited)","q":[{"p":"An asset ABC Limited purchased for N10,000,000 was destroyed by fire; an insurance claim of N7,000,000 was received as compensation, and the asset's residue was N1,000,000. A replacement asset worth N5,000,000 was acquired within 3 years of receiving the insurance claim. **Required**: assess and advise on ABC Limited's chargeable gain, if any."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["ABC Limited","N'million"],"rows":[["Insurance claim","7"],["Residual value","1"],["Total","8"],["Less: cost of new asset acquired","(5)"],["**Chargeable gain to be subjected to tax**","**3**"]]}},{"p":"Since compensation + residual (N8m) exceeds the replacement cost (N5m), a chargeable gain of N3,000,000 arises. Capital allowance will be claimed on the residue of the old asset only — **N1,000,000**."}]}},{"eg":{"t":"Illustration 24.3 — replacement cost higher than compensation received (ABC Limited)","q":[{"p":"Same facts as Illustration 24.2, except the replacement asset acquired within 3 years is worth N12,000,000 instead. **Required**: assess and advise on the tax implication for ABC Limited."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["ABC Limited","N'million"],"rows":[["Insurance claim","7"],["Residual value","1"],["Total","8"],["Less: cost of new asset acquired","(12)"],["**Additions on which capital allowance will be claimed**","**4**"]]}},{"p":"Since the replacement cost (N12m) exceeds compensation + residual (N8m), no chargeable gain arises — instead, an ADDITIONAL asset of N4,000,000 (12−8) is deemed acquired for capital allowance purposes. Capital allowance will **also** be claimed separately on the residue of the old asset — **N1,000,000**."}]}}]},{"n":"24.10","t":"Bargains comprising two or more transactions","b":[{"p":"Where a **single bargain** comprises two or more disposal transactions, they are treated as a **single disposal** for chargeable-gains purposes."},{"p":"Where separate considerations are agreed (or purported to be agreed) for two or more transactions within one bargain — whether or not each involves an asset disposal — those considerations are treated as together constituting one entire consideration, apportioned between the transactions."},{"note":"Where such an apportionment would attribute LESS consideration to the chargeable asset than what was actually agreed in the bargain, the separately-agreed consideration is used instead — i.e., the apportionment can never be used to understate the true consideration for the chargeable asset."}]},{"n":"24.11","t":"Valuation at market value","b":[{"def":{"t":"Market value","d":"the price an asset might reasonably be expected to fetch on a sale at arm's length or in the open market — with no reduction for cash/bulk discount when estimating an unknown market value."}},{"key":"Where the actual consideration the acquirer pays is **less** than market value, the asset is deemed acquired for the amount actually paid. Net effect: for **sales proceeds**, use the **higher** of actual proceeds or market value; for **acquisition cost**, use the **lower** of market value or actual amount paid."}]},{"n":"24.12","t":"Location of assets","b":[{"p":"Gains on a chargeable asset are only taxable in Nigeria if the asset is located **in Nigeria**. The NTA 2025 sets specific location rules by asset type:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Asset type","Location rule"],"rows":[["Immovable property","Where the property is located."],["Tangible movable property","Where the property is located."],["Debts","Nigeria, if the creditor is Nigerian-resident or has a Nigerian PE to which the debt relates."],["Government-issued shares/securities","The country/place of the issuing authority."],["Registered shares/securities","Where registered, or (multiple registers) where the principal register is."],["Shares/interests in foreign entities","Deemed Nigerian if >50% of their value (in the preceding 365 days) derives from a change in ownership of a Nigerian entity, or from immovable property/other chargeable assets in Nigeria."],["Shares in resident entities","Nigeria, if the entity is a Nigerian company, or the owner is Nigerian-resident/has a Nigerian PE the shares relate to."],["Ships/aircraft in international traffic","Nigeria, if the owner is Nigerian-resident or has a relevant Nigerian PE."],["Interests/rights in ships/aircraft","Nigeria, if the rights-holder is Nigerian-resident or has a Nigerian PE."],["Goodwill of a business","Where the business is carried on."],["Patents, trademarks, designs","Where registered (multiple registrations: each register's own location)."],["Copyrights, franchises, rights/licences","Nigeria, if exercisable (or the rights derived can be exercised) in Nigeria."],["Judgment debts","Where the judgment is recorded."],["Incorporeal property (incl. digital assets)","Nigeria, if the beneficial owner/controller is Nigerian-resident or has a connected Nigerian PE."]]}},{"h4":"Indirect transfer of ownership of companies/assets"},{"key":"A gain accruing to a **non-resident** on disposing of shares is a chargeable gain in Nigeria where the disposal results in a change: in the ownership structure/group membership of a Nigerian company; or of ownership/title/interest in an asset located in Nigeria."}]},{"n":"24.13","t":"Life assurance policies","b":[{"key":"No chargeable gain accrues on disposing of an interest in/rights under a life assurance policy or deferred-annuity contract — **unless** the person making the disposal is NOT the original beneficial owner, and acquired those rights/interests for consideration in money or money's worth."},{"p":"**Surrendering** a policy (or the rights under a deferred-annuity contract) IS a disposal — the consideration for a surrendered deferred-annuity contract is the **higher** of market value and disposal proceeds."}]},{"n":"24.14","t":"Rights under policies of insurance, other than life assurance policies","b":[{"p":"Rights of the insured under a **capital redemption business** or **industrial assurance business** policy CAN yield a chargeable gain on disposal. Rights under any OTHER policy of insurance (property-related or not) do **not** constitute an asset capable of a chargeable gain, except as expressly provided."},{"def":{"t":"Capital redemption business","d":"issuing policies/bonds/endowment certificates where, for one or more premiums paid, a sum (or series of sums) becomes payable to the insured in future — EXCLUDING life or industrial assurance business."}},{"def":{"t":"Industrial assurance business","d":"the same premium-for-future-sum structure, but in connection with industrial assurance."}},{"def":{"t":"Policy of insurance (for this rule)","d":"does NOT include a policy of assurance on human life."}}]},{"n":"24.15","t":"Personal injury — section 50 of NTA","b":[{"ul":["Amounts **not exceeding N50,000,000** obtained by way of compensation or damages for any wrong or injury suffered by an individual **in his person or in his profession or vocation** — including compensation for loss of office or employment, and wrong or injury for libel, slander or enticement — **shall not be chargeable gains**.","Where the sum **exceeds N50,000,000**, **only the excess** amount shall constitute a chargeable gain.","Any person who **pays** compensation for loss of office or employment to an individual is required, **at the point of payment**, to **deduct and remit** the tax due under this section to the relevant tax authority.","The tax so deducted shall be remitted **within the time specified** under the Pay-As-You-Earn or Deduction of Tax at Source Regulations issued under the Nigeria Tax Administration Act."]}]},{"n":"24.16","t":"Principal private residences — section 51 of NTA","b":[{"ul":["The gains accruing to an individual are **exempt** in respect of the disposal of, or an interest in: a **dwelling-house** or part of such dwelling-house; and **land** (other than land used for commercial purposes) immediately adjoining the dwelling house, **up to a maximum of one acre**.","The exemption under this section shall be enjoyed **once in the lifetime** of an individual.","The consideration shall be **apportioned** where a person disposes of only part of a dwelling-house, or of a house used partly as a dwelling place and partly for carrying out a trade, business, profession or vocation."]}]},{"n":"24.17","t":"Personal chattels — section 52 of NTA","b":[{"ul":["A gain accruing on a disposal of an asset which is **tangible movable property being personal chattels** of an individual shall not be a chargeable gain if the total amount or value of the consideration for the disposal does not, in a period of assessment, exceed **N5,000,000 or three times the annual national minimum wage, whichever is higher**.","Where **two or more assets** — whether or not forming part of a set of articles — are disposed of by a person to the **same person**, or to persons **acting in concert**, or to **connected persons**, whether on the same or different occasions, the two or more transactions shall be treated as a **single transaction** disposing of a single asset, with any apportionments where necessary.","Where the disposal is of part of a right or interest in, or over, tangible movable property, the rule above applies to the asset **as a whole**, taking the consideration as including the **market value of what remains undisposed of**, in addition to the actual consideration.","The provisions of this section apply to a gain accruing on a disposal of **two or more assets**, not necessarily forming part of a set of articles of any description, which are tangible movable properties, in the same manner as they apply to a gain on a disposal of a single asset or of a set of articles."]}]},{"n":"24.18","t":"Motor vehicles — section 53 of NTA","b":[{"ul":["A motor vehicle used **solely for private or non-profit purposes** shall **not be an asset** for the purposes of taxation.","The exemption under this section shall be limited to **not more than two motor vehicles** by an individual in any year of assessment."]}]},{"n":"24.19","t":"Gifts — section 54 of NTA","b":[{"ul":["Where a person disposes **by way of a gift** an asset **acquired by him by way of a gift** or any other form of gift — not being an acquisition on a **devolution on death** — the gains accruing from the disposal shall **not be chargeable gains**.","An asset is acquired or disposed of by way of gift where **no consideration** is paid or received for the acquisition or disposal."]}]},{"n":"24.20","t":"Assets held in trust for charities","b":[{"p":"Any property held in trust for **(a)** a religious or charitable institution of a **public character**; **(b)** any **statutory or registered friendly society**; **(c)** any **co-operative society** registered under the co-operative societies law of any State; or **(d)** any **trade union** registered under the Trade Unions Act, shall **not be regarded as a chargeable asset** for the purposes of determining chargeable gains — **if** the gain is **not derived from the disposal of an asset acquired in connection with any trade or business** carried on by the institution, society or trade union, **and** the gain is **applied solely for the purpose** of the institution, society or trade union."},{"warn":"However, where such property **ceases to be held in trust** for the institution, society or trade union: **(a)** the **trustees shall be treated as if they had disposed of, and immediately re-acquired, the property for a consideration equal to its market value**, and any gain on that disposal shall be treated as **not accruing to the institution, society or trade union**; and **(b)** any gain accruing directly or indirectly on that disposal shall be treated as having accrued to the **trustees**, and shall be **chargeable gains in the hands of the trustees**."},{"key":"The exemption has **two cumulative conditions**, and both must hold: the gain must not come from an asset acquired for a **trade or business** of the body, and the gain must be **applied solely** for the body's purposes. The deeming provision on cessation is the anti-avoidance teeth — a charity cannot shelter an asset in trust, accumulate the gain, and then release the property free of tax: cessation triggers a **deemed disposal at market value**, taxed on the trustees."}]},{"n":"24.21","t":"Chargeable gains exempt from tax","b":[{"p":"Several of the exemptions below were already introduced in their own dedicated subsections above (compulsory acquisition of land §24.3, life assurance §24.12) — cross-referenced here rather than repeated. This section consolidates the **full exemption list** under the NTA 2025."},{"ol":["**Disposal of a dwelling house** — gains accruing to an INDIVIDUAL from disposing of a dwelling house (or an interest in it) and land immediately adjoining it (other than commercially-used land), up to a maximum of **1 acre**, are exempt — enjoyed **once in the individual's lifetime**. A dwelling house partly occupied as principal residence and partly let out has its chargeable gain **prorated** accordingly; likewise, if a house is used partly as a dwelling and partly for a trade/business/profession/vocation, the consideration is apportioned and only the trade-use portion's gain is chargeable.","**Property held in trust** — held for a religious/charitable institution of public character, a registered friendly society, a registered co-operative society, or a registered trade union — is NOT a chargeable asset, provided the gain does not derive from an asset acquired in connection with any trade/business the institution carries on, and the gain is applied solely for the institution's/society's/union's own purpose. Where such property later CEASES to be so held, the trustees are treated as having disposed of and immediately re-acquired it at market value — any resulting gain is treated as accruing to the **trustees** (not the institution), and is a chargeable gain in the trustees' own hands.","**Gifts** — assets acquired by way of gift and disposed of in a similar manner (i.e., gifted onward) do not give rise to chargeable gains. An asset is by way of gift where no consideration is paid or received at all.","**Motor vehicles** — gains on disposing of a motor vehicle used SOLELY for private/non-profit purposes are not chargeable — limited to **at most 2 vehicles per individual per assessment year**; gains on a 3rd (or further) vehicle disposed in the same year are fully taxable.",{"p":"**Disposal of shares** — NOT chargeable where any ONE of these applies:"},{"ol":["aggregate disposal proceeds are **below N150,000,000** AND the chargeable gain does not exceed **N10,000,000** in any 12 consecutive months;","the shares are transferred between an approved lender and borrower under a regulated securities-lending transaction (cross-ref Chapter 22's SEC Lending treatment); or","the disposal proceeds — regardless of the N150,000,000/N10,000,000 thresholds — are **reinvested within the same assessment year** in shares of the same or another Nigerian company (chargeable gain is computed proportionately only on the UNREINVESTED portion of proceeds)."]},"**Personal chattels** — gains on disposing of tangible movable property that are an individual's personal chattels are exempt where total consideration does not exceed **N5,000,000, or 3× the annual national minimum wage, whichever is higher**, in an assessment period. On a PARTIAL disposal, no gain accrues if (market value of the unsold part + actual consideration of the sold part) stays within the same threshold. This exemption does **not** apply to disposing of currency.","**Compensation for personal injury or loss of office** — sums not exceeding **N50,000,000**, received as compensation/damages for a personal/professional/vocational wrong or injury (including loss of office/employment, or libel/slander/enticement), are not chargeable gains — only the **excess above N50,000,000** is chargeable where the sum is higher. Anyone paying loss-of-office/employment compensation must deduct and remit the tax due at the point of payment, within the time specified under PAYE/Deduction-of-Tax-at-Source Regulations (NTAA 2025).","**Life assurance policies and deferred annuities** — gains from disposing of rights in these are not chargeable UNLESS the disposer is not the original beneficial owner and acquired the rights for monetary consideration (see §24.12 above).","**Startup investments** — gains from disposing of assets by angel investors, VC/PE funds, accelerators or incubators in relation to a **labelled startup** are exempt, provided the assets were held in Nigeria for **≥24 months** (cross-ref Chapter 22's full labelled-startup incentive framework under the Nigeria Startup Act 2022).","**Compulsory acquisition of land** — see the full conditions already set out in §24.3 above.","**Pension funds and assets** — gains accruing to pension funds/assets are exempt from income tax.","**Awards for valour** — gains from disposing of a decoration awarded for valour/gallant conduct are exempt, provided the decoration was acquired other than for money/money's-worth consideration.","**Retirement benefits** — gains accruing to an individual from disposing of investments held as part of a national provident fund or other retirement-benefits scheme are exempt from income tax."]},{"note":"The study text sets out the exempt gains both as this consolidated list and, individually, as its own §24.15 to §24.20 above — personal injury, principal private residences, personal chattels, motor vehicles, gifts, and assets held in trust for charities. Both numberings are kept so that material can be found at either, and the consolidated list here adds the categories that have no section of their own: **disposal of shares** (the N150,000,000 proceeds / N10,000,000 gain thresholds, securities-lending transfers, and the reinvestment relief), **life assurance policies and deferred annuities**, **startup investments** by angel investors and venture capital held in Nigeria at least 24 months, **compulsory acquisition of land**, **pension funds and assets**, **awards for valour**, and **retirement benefits**."}]},{"n":"24.22","t":"Chapter review","b":[{"p":"This chapter dealt with the ascertainment of chargeable gains for inclusion as taxable income in companies income tax computation under the NTA 2025 — what constitutes chargeable assets, how chargeable gains are computed both for assets that have and have not enjoyed capital allowance, and the full list of chargeable gains exempt from tax."}]},{"n":"24.23","t":"Worked examples — study text questions and suggested solutions","b":[{"p":"The study text sets **three questions** at §24.23.1 — two computations and one theory question — with its own suggested solutions at §24.23.2. All three are reproduced here in full, with the book's answers, every figure independently recomputed, and added explanation. The book's own **Illustrations 24.1 to 24.3** are separate and appear earlier, at §24.7 (partial disposal) and §24.9 (assets lost or destroyed)."},{"eg":{"t":"XYZ Limited — three disposals in one year, including a compulsory acquisition","q":[{"p":"XYZ Limited, a manufacturing company in Agbara Industrial Estate, projects these transactions for the year ending 31 December 2027: (a) an oil-melting plant bought 1 January 2026 for N45,000,000, to be disposed for N55,000,000 on 31 March 2027; (b) land bought 4 March 2026 for warehouse construction, cost N25,000,000, sold 30 June 2027 for N42,000,000, with N1,250,000 paid to Bukola Ventures for facilitating the disposal; (c) landed property costing N12,500,000 to be compulsorily acquired by the Lagos State Government on 5 May 2027 (Lagos-Badagry expressway expansion), with N30,000,000 compensation. **Required**: ascertain the chargeable gains subject to tax in the relevant assessment year."}],"a":[{"p":"**(a) Disposal of melting plant** — one year of capital allowance (20% annual rate, First Schedule) would have been claimed for the 2027 assessment year (relating to 2026 usage) before the March 2027 disposal:"},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Melting plant","N"],"rows":[["Sales proceeds","55,000,000"],["Cost","45,000,000"],["Less: capital allowance (2027 YA, 20% × 45,000,000)","(9,000,000)"],["Residual value","36,000,000"],["**Chargeable gains**","**19,000,000**"]]}},{"p":"**(b) Disposal of land** — no capital allowance applies to land:"},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Land","N"],"rows":[["Sales proceeds","42,000,000"],["Deduct: incidental cost of disposal","(1,250,000)"],["Net sales proceeds","40,750,000"],["Deduct: cost of acquisition","(25,000,000)"],["**Chargeable gains**","**15,750,000**"]]}},{"p":"**(c) Compulsory acquisition** — exempt per §24.3, since nothing indicates the company anticipated the acquisition or advertised the land for sale:"},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Compulsorily-acquired property","N"],"rows":[["Compensation","30,000,000"],["Deduct: acquisition cost","(12,500,000)"],["**Chargeable gains (tax-exempt)**","**17,500,000**"]]}},{"p":"**Total chargeable gains subject to tax in the 2028 assessment year** = N19,000,000 + N15,750,000 = **N34,750,000**. The compulsory-acquisition gain (N17,500,000) is excluded entirely, being tax-exempt."},{"key":"Note the **two different deduction rules** this question sets side by side. For the **plant**, the deduction is the **residual value** — original cost *less the capital allowances already claimed* (N45,000,000 − N9,000,000 = N36,000,000) — because the asset has already attracted tax relief on its cost, and allowing the full cost again would relieve it twice. For the **land**, there are no capital allowances, so the deduction is the **full acquisition cost** plus the **incidental cost of disposal** (N1,250,000 of agent's fees). Confusing the two is the commonest error on chargeable gains."},{"note":"Why only **one** year's annual allowance on the plant: it was bought on 1 January 2026, so the year ended 31 December 2026 is its first basis period, assessed in the **2027** year of assessment at 20% × N45,000,000 = **N9,000,000**. It was sold on 31 March 2027, within the next basis period, so no further allowance arises. The disposals all fall in the year ended 31 December 2027 and are therefore assessed in **2028**."},{"note":"Part (c) is the trap: the gain of N17,500,000 on the land compulsorily acquired by the Lagos State Government **is computed and then exempted**, not ignored. Show the computation and then state the exemption — the mark is for knowing the exemption exists, which an answer that silently omits the land cannot demonstrate. The exemption is at §24.21(j), and it carries two conditions: the taxpayer must not have acquired the land knowing it was likely to be compulsorily acquired, and must not have advertised or otherwise made known a willingness to sell it to the authority."}],"tag":"Study text — 24.23.1, Question 1","open":true}},{"eg":{"t":"Ultimate Agro-allied Company Limited — one compulsory and one voluntary disposal","q":[{"p":"Ultimate Agro-allied Company Limited sold two landed properties in 2026. The Lagos property (Amuwo, Lagos-Badagry expressway) was compulsorily acquired by the Lagos State Government in June 2026 (road dualisation), for compensation of N47,000,000 — the land originally cost N10,500,000. Fearing the same fate for its Offa land (Offa-Ilorin Road, Kwara State), the company voluntarily sold it for N55,000,000 on 25 September 2026 — original cost N17,500,000, with 10% of sales proceeds paid to Omolara Ventures for facilitating the disposal. **Required**: compute the company's chargeable gains subject to tax in the relevant assessment year."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lrr","head":["Ultimate Agro-allied Company Limited","Lagos land (N)","Offa land (N)"],"rows":[["Compensation / sales proceeds","47,000,000","55,000,000"],["Deduct: sales expenses (10% for Offa)","0","(5,500,000)"],["Net compensation / sales proceeds","47,000,000","49,500,000"],["Deduct: cost of acquisition","(10,500,000)","(17,500,000)"],["Chargeable gains","36,500,000","32,000,000"],["**Chargeable gains subject to tax**","**Nil (exempt)**","**32,000,000**"]]}},{"p":"The Lagos land's gain is exempt (compulsory acquisition, §24.3). The Offa land — sold **voluntarily**, despite the company's expectation of a similar compulsory acquisition — does not qualify for the exemption, so its full N32,000,000 gain is subject to tax, included in total profit for the **2027 assessment year**."},{"key":"The contrast between the two lands is the whole question. The **Lagos land** was taken under **compulsory powers**, so its N36,500,000 gain is **exempt** under §24.21(j). The **Offa land** was sold **voluntarily** — the company \"quickly sold\" it fearing the same fate — so its N32,000,000 gain is **fully chargeable**. A fear of future compulsory acquisition is not compulsory acquisition, and anticipating it by selling forfeits the exemption entirely."},{"note":"The **10% of sales proceeds** paid to Omolara Ventures is 10% × N55,000,000 = **N5,500,000**, deductible as an **incidental cost of disposal**. Note that the Lagos disposal has **no** sales expenses, because the land was taken rather than marketed. The chargeable gain of N32,000,000 is then **included as taxable income in determining total profit** for the companies income tax computation of the 2027 year of assessment — it is not taxed under a separate capital gains charge."},{"note":"A point the question raises without stating: Ultimate Agro-allied is \"a long-standing company operating in the agricultural value chain\". The five-year agricultural exemption of §22.4.3 applies only to the **first five years** from commencement, so a long-standing company has no shelter — and in any event that exemption is for **income generated by the agricultural business**, not for gains on disposing of land."}],"tag":"Study text — 24.23.1, Question 2","open":true}},{"eg":{"t":"Chargeable gains exempt from tax","q":[{"p":"Chargeable gains are gains arising from the disposal of chargeable assets — all forms of property, whether situated in Nigeria or not. Chargeable gains are included as taxable income of taxable persons in determining income tax payable, except those specifically exempted by the extant tax laws. **Required**: advise on chargeable gains that are exempt from tax under the Nigeria Tax Act 2025."}],"a":[{"p":"See the full 13-item consolidated exemption list at §24.14 above — dwelling house disposal, trust property, gifts, motor vehicles, shares, personal chattels, personal injury/loss-of-office compensation, life assurance/deferred annuities, startup investments, compulsory land acquisition, pension funds/assets, valour awards, and retirement benefits."}],"tag":"Study text — 24.23.1, Question 3","open":true}}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Chargeable gain on a partial disposal","tex":"\\text{Chargeable gain} = A - \\dfrac{A}{A+B} \\times C","nt":"A = sales proceeds/market value of the part sold; B = market value of the unsold part; C = cost of acquisition (or residue/tax written down value) of the whole asset."},{"lb":"Chargeable gain accruing in a year, where consideration is payable by instalment","tex":"\\text{Gain for the year} = \\dfrac{\\text{Consideration received in the year}}{\\text{Total consideration receivable}} \\times \\text{Total chargeable gains}"}],"focus":"The exam-relevant load in this chapter is almost entirely in the specific numeric thresholds attached to each exemption — N150,000,000/N10,000,000 (shares), N5,000,000/3×minimum-wage (personal chattels), N50,000,000 (personal injury), 2 vehicles (motor vehicles), 1 acre (principal residence), 24 months (startup investments), 3 years (asset replacement after loss/destruction) — know each one attached to the RIGHT exemption, since they are easy to swap under exam pressure. On computation mechanics: the capital-allowance-claimed vs. not-claimed split (residue vs. full acquisition cost) is the single most important distinction, and the partial-disposal A/(A+B)×C formula is worth memorising exactly as given, including which letter is which.","errors":["Using the full acquisition cost (rather than the residue/tax written down value) when computing chargeable gains on an asset that HAS had capital allowance claimed on it — the two computation bases (§24.5(a) vs. (b)) depend entirely on whether capital allowance was claimed, not on the asset type.","Treating the compulsory-acquisition-of-land exemption as automatic — it only applies where BOTH conditions are met: the landowner neither knew/anticipated the compulsory acquisition when acquiring the land, NOR took steps to advertise or offer the land for sale to the authority.","Applying the personal-chattels or personal-injury exemption as a full exemption once the threshold is exceeded — both are partial-exemption (excess-only) rules once their threshold is crossed, not all-or-nothing cliffs.","Forgetting that the shares-disposal exemption requires BOTH the <N150,000,000 proceeds condition AND the ≤N10,000,000 gain condition together (for the first limb) — meeting only one is not enough, unless the reinvestment-within-the-same-year limb applies instead."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Under the Nigeria Tax Act 2025, how are chargeable gains treated for tax purposes, following the repeal of the old Capital Gains Tax Act?","o":["They remain a wholly separate tax, assessed independently of CIT","They are included directly in taxable income when determining a company's total profit","They are exempt from all forms of taxation going forward","They are taxed only at the point of asset acquisition, not disposal","They are taxed exclusively under VAT"],"a":1,"w":"The NTA 2025 repealed the Capital Gains Tax Act — chargeable gains are now included directly in taxable income for the purpose of determining a company's total profit, rather than assessed as a separate standalone tax.","src":"Chapter 24.2","sec":"24.2"},{"q":"Where capital allowance HAS been claimed on an asset used in a trade/business, what is deducted from disposal proceeds to compute the chargeable gain?","o":["The original cost of acquisition","The residue (tax written down value) of the asset","The market value at the date of disposal","Nothing — the full proceeds are chargeable","The insured value of the asset"],"a":1,"w":"Section 24.5(a): where capital allowance has been claimed, only the residue/tax written down value of the asset is deducted from disposal proceeds — not the original acquisition cost.","src":"Chapter 24.5","sec":"24.5"},{"q":"What are the TWO conditions that must BOTH be satisfied for a compulsory land acquisition to be exempt from chargeable gains?","o":["The land must have cost less than N10 million, and be less than 1 acre","The owner must not have known/anticipated the acquisition when acquiring the land, AND must not have advertised/offered it for sale","The acquiring authority must be a state government only","The land must have been held for at least 5 years","There are no conditions — compulsory acquisition is always exempt"],"a":1,"w":"Both conditions must hold: the person had not acquired the land knowing/reasonably expecting it would be compulsorily acquired, AND had not taken steps to advertise or signal willingness to dispose of it to the authority.","src":"Chapter 24.3","sec":"24.3"},{"q":"Up to what maximum is a compensation sum for personal injury (including loss of office/employment) exempt from being a chargeable gain?","o":["N10,000,000","N25,000,000","N50,000,000","N100,000,000","There is no cap — all such compensation is fully exempt"],"a":2,"w":"Sums not exceeding N50,000,000 received as compensation/damages for personal/professional injury (including loss of office/employment) are exempt — only the excess above N50,000,000 is chargeable where the sum is higher.","src":"Chapter 24.14","sec":"24.14"},{"q":"How many motor vehicles, at most, can an individual dispose of in one assessment year and have the resulting gains exempt from chargeable gains tax (assuming solely private/non-profit use)?","o":["1","2","3","5","There is no limit"],"a":1,"w":"The motor vehicle exemption is capped at 2 vehicles per individual per assessment year — gains on a 3rd or further vehicle disposed in the same year are fully taxable.","src":"Chapter 24.14","sec":"24.14"}],"theory":[{"q":"Bello Nigeria Limited purchased machinery for N20,000,000 on 1 January 2025, and claimed capital allowance for the 2026 and 2027 assessment years (20% per annum, straight line). The machinery was sold for N24,000,000 on 15 January 2028. Compute the chargeable gain arising on this disposal.","marks":6,"a":[{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Bello Nigeria Limited — chargeable gain","N"],"rows":[["Sales proceeds","24,000,000"],["Cost","20,000,000"],["Less: capital allowance claimed (2026 + 2027 YA, 2 × 20% × 20,000,000)","(8,000,000)"],["Residual value","12,000,000"],["**Chargeable gain (24,000,000 − 12,000,000)**","**12,000,000**"]]}},{"note":"Since capital allowance was claimed on this asset, the residue (tax written down value) — not the original cost — is deducted from disposal proceeds, per §24.5(a)."}],"src":"Chapter 24 — self-composed (following §24.5(a)/Illustration 24.4(a))","sec":"24.5"},{"q":"Distinguish between the tax treatment of a chargeable asset disposed by way of gift and one disposed by way of compulsory acquisition of land, under the Nigeria Tax Act 2025.","marks":6,"a":[{"p":"**Gift**: an asset acquired by way of gift and later disposed of in a similar manner (i.e., gifted onward, not sold) — where no consideration is paid or received — gives rise to **no chargeable gain at all**, unconditionally. This is distinct from acquiring an asset BY gift and later SELLING it, which does engage the ordinary consideration rules (§24.2(b))."},{"p":"**Compulsory acquisition of land**: exempt only **conditionally** — the landowner must not have known/anticipated the compulsory acquisition when acquiring the land, and must not have advertised or signalled willingness to dispose of it to the authority. Unlike the unconditional gift exemption, a compulsory acquisition that fails either condition is fully chargeable in the ordinary way."}],"src":"Chapter 24 — self-composed (following §24.3 and §24.14)","sec":"24.14"}]}},{"n":25,"t":"Application of Information Technology in Taxation","brief":"The FINAL chapter of the Taxation subject — a purely descriptive, non-computational tour of how technology has reshaped tax practice and administration in Nigeria: the roles of tax practitioners/administrators, the TaxPro Max and new Rev360 e-filing platforms (with their full step-by-step procedures), emerging technologies (cloud, AI/robotics, blockchain, data analytics, social media, mobile apps), the taxation of technology-based transactions (digital service tax, significant economic presence), and the challenges/opportunities technology brings to the profession.","outcomes":["Discuss computer applications amenable to tax planning and administration","Demonstrate navigational skills in NRS and other tax portals","Explain electronic filing of income tax returns","Explain automated VAT management and returns","Describe excel payroll software templates for computing employees' tax","Discuss the key features of the Integrated Tax Administration System (ITAS) of the Nigeria Revenue Service","Discuss the challenges of technology on tax practice and administration"],"secs":[{"n":"25.2","t":"Introduction","b":[{"p":"Tax practitioners and administrators play a unique, essential role in every society — virtually no business escapes tax issues arising from its transactions or locations, and entities often find tax matters genuinely challenging to handle. Rapid technological advancement is pushing organisations to leverage available tools to resolve these regular business challenges and make tax practice/administration more relevant in today's dynamic environment."}]},{"n":"25.3","t":"Overview of the technological impact on tax practice and administration","b":[{"def":{"t":"Tax practitioners","d":"independent firms licensed to provide tax-related services to clients."}},{"def":{"t":"Tax administration","d":"revenue agencies/authorities mandated to assess, collect and account for taxes on behalf of government."}},{"h4":"25.3.1  Current roles of tax practising firms"},{"ul":["providing independent services to clients;","preparing tax records;","computing end-of-year tax liabilities;","filing tax returns;","advising clients on tax matters;","complying with tax legislation;","resolving tax disputes; and","engaging in tax audit and investigation."]},{"note":"Technological evidence shows most of these tasks can be made more efficient and effective through automation."},{"h4":"25.3.2 – 25.3.6  Elements of tax practice — tax compliance, tax planning and advisory, tax litigation, and tax research and intelligence"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Element","What it involves"],"rows":[["**Tax compliance**","Gathering relevant tax information; evaluating/classifying it; filing necessary returns; representing clients during audits/investigations."],["**Tax planning and advisory**","Arranging a client's transactions to optimise (usually minimise) tax liability — planning for OPEN transactions (timing/amount of pending future transactions) and CLOSED transactions (how past transactions are presented/classified in the accounts to reduce tax exposure)."],["**Tax litigation**","Settling disputes with tax authorities in court/tribunal (requiring close reading/citing of extant tax law); appearing as an expert/technical witness; appealing on clients' behalf when necessary."],["**Tax research and intelligence**","Identifying a tax problem; gathering relevant evidence/information; evaluating several scenarios; determining and outlining solutions."]]}}]},{"n":"25.4","t":"Current roles of tax administrators","b":[{"def":{"t":"Tax administrators","d":"public authorities empowered to assess, collect and account for taxes for a recognised jurisdiction."}},{"ul":["registering and educating taxpayers;","receiving tax returns filed by taxpayers;","assessing taxpayers (individuals and entities);","collecting, maintaining records of, and accounting for taxes paid;","managing and processing tax refund applications;","conducting routine tax audits/investigations;","managing and enforcing tax debt recovery; and","advising on the review of tax legislation and regulations."]}]},{"n":"25.5","t":"The effects of technology on modern tax administration in Nigeria","b":[{"h4":"25.5.1  Introduction — the timeline"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Year","Development"],"rows":[["2006","The (then-) tax authority deployed the first tax portal (Webportal), automating taxpayer registration and other administration processes."],["2014","An Integrated Tax Administration System (ITAS), known as SIGTAS, was implemented — though its deployment appears to have stalled."],["7 June 2021","The NRS announced adoption and deployment of TaxPro Max, a locally developed tax management solution handling multiple aspects of administration."],["30 April 2026","The NRS launched **Rev360** to replace the legacy TaxPro Max portal — see §25.6 below."]]}},{"h4":"What an integrated tax management system like TaxPro Max handles"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Function","What it enables"],"rows":[["**E-registration**","Accurate taxpayer data capture; each enrolled taxpayer gets a unique tax number; self-enrolment via a web application."],["**E-assessment**","Automatic computation of tax liability and generation/emailing of assessment notices once returns are filed electronically."],["**E-payments**","Multiple electronic payment channels — POS, internet banking, bank transfers, USSD, or credit/debit cards."],["**E-filing**","Filing returns (e.g. VAT and CIT) from the taxpayer's own office, without visiting a (possibly distant) tax office."],["**E-tax calculator**","A website tool letting taxpayers/consultants compute the accurate tax amount due."],["**E-TCC**","Electronic application for, and email delivery of, Tax Clearance Certificates where conditions are met."],["**E-reporting**","Periodic performance reports generated within seconds at the click of a button."],["**E-tax audit**","The full tax audit process (including pre/post-audit meetings via video conferencing, and document exchange by email) without site visits."]]}},{"h4":"25.5.2  E-invoicing (s.157 NTA 2025)"},{"key":"Section 157 NTA makes it **mandatory** for a taxable person making a taxable supply to use the **fiscalisation system** deployed by the Service (per NTAA). The fiscalisation system comprises fiscal equipment — electronic devices, software solutions, or a secured-network communication system (or any combination) — for electronic invoicing and data transfer, as the Service prescribes/deploys."},{"h4":"25.5.3  Rev360"},{"p":"Launched **30 April 2026** to replace the legacy TaxPro Max portal, Rev360 is designed as a frictionless electronic portal simplifying taxpayer engagement with the NRS — adding simplified registration, filing, payments, and objections/appeals functionality that TaxPro Max lacked, making compliance easier, faster and more transparent."},{"note":"Rev360 is **hoped** to eventually replace TaxPro Max entirely, but its initial phase focuses on small businesses, individuals in the informal sector, and medium taxpayers — before extending to other categories."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Function","How Rev360 handles it"],"rows":[["**E-registration**","Via selfservice.nrs.gov.ng — an electronic self-service process facilitating cross-agency data matching."],["**E-assessment**","Automated, real-time assessment, reducing manual intervention."],["**E-payment**","Real-time updates on tax liabilities and payments."],["**E-filing**","Automated filing, reconciliation and payments."],["**E-TCC**","Electronic Tax Clearance Certificates for compliant taxpayers."],["**E-reporting**","Reports generated within seconds at the click of a button."],["**E-audit**","A digital audit trail for accountability — business transactions are readily visible, allowing real-time monitoring without waiting for periodic reports."]]}}]},{"n":"25.6","t":"Operations of the NRS TAXPROMAX solution relating to filing requirements and procedures","b":[{"h4":"25.6.1  Preamble"},{"p":"The NRS is empowered to automate return filing and payment processes, hence TaxPro Max — a one-stop online platform letting taxpayers: register, file returns, remit taxes; carry out assessments and track obligations; manage withholding tax deductions; manage capital allowance and loss; download TCCs; and communicate with the NRS on tax issues."},{"p":"The portal opened for filing of all naira-denominated taxes on **7 June 2021** (per a circular dated 4 June 2021). Due to early registration/use hitches, companies with a 31 December accounting year-end (returns otherwise due 30 June 2021) had their filing deadline extended to **31 July 2021**, without penalty/interest for the extension itself."},{"note":"Taxpayers must register to access the portal, then log in to file returns — filing generates a mandatory **Document Identity Number (DIN)**, required to remit taxes via the portal. The portal may be used by taxpayers directly or by their approved agents. Taxpayers preferring hard-copy filing may visit a local tax office for NRS staff to upload documents — done **at least 2 weeks before the deadline**."},{"h4":"25.6.2  Filing requirements for value added tax (VAT)"},{"steps":["Fill the tax update form at any local NRS tax office.","Access the portal (taxpromax.nrs.gov.ng) and log in with your user details to reach the dashboard.","Click \"Taxes Due\" and select the tax type.","Click \"Process\" to begin.","Fill in the required schedules — sales adjustment, exempt and zero-rated goods/services.","Click \"Add Record\" and \"Proceed.\"","Ensure the VAT form loads once all schedules are complete.","Select the currency type under \"Returning Currency.\"","Insert total sales/income, exclusive of VAT.","Tick the declaration-form box.","Confirm all entries before submission."]},{"h4":"25.6.3  Filing requirements for companies income tax (CIT)"},{"steps":["Log in to taxpromax.nrs.gov.ng.","Click \"Taxes Due.\"","Under the action column, click \"Process\" to reach the schedules.","The CIT form is generated once all schedules are complete.","Select the currency type (NGN, GBP, USD, CFA, EUR, etc.) under \"Returning Currency.\"","Insert the total revenue amount — this auto-fills the remaining pre-fill boxes.","Click \"Declaration\" beneath the generated form, confirming the information is correct and in accordance with the relevant Tax Act.","Select a payment option — full payment, or 2/3/4 instalments.","Click \"Submit.\"","On the CIT assessment page, click \"Review the CIT Assessment,\" then \"Proceed to Payment.\"","Upload audited accounts, tax computations, notes to accounts, and memorandum/articles of association — all documents in PDF, except notes to accounts (Excel); file size capped at 200KB.","Click \"Submit.\"","On the payment page, review the description items and amounts, then click \"Process to Payment,\" \"Pay Now,\" and \"Pay\" — the system generates a payment reference code.","Choose \"Pay Online\" (connects to the Remita platform with payer name/phone/email/amount/reference code auto-filled) or \"Pay at Bank\" at any NRS-designated bank.","Confirm payment and print the payment acknowledgment slip."]}]},{"n":"25.7","t":"Operations of the NRS Rev360 solution relating to filing requirements and procedures","b":[{"h4":"25.7.1  Company registration process"},{"steps":["Access registration: go to selfservice.nrs.gov.ng and click \"Create New Account.\"","Select \"Corporate\" as the entity category.","Select the specific corporate category applicable to the business.","Verify CAC details: enter the RC Number and click \"Verify\" — the system connects to CAC and auto-retrieves the company's registration details.","OTP verification: an OTP is sent to the CAC-registered email/SMS to confirm ownership.","Enter business information — contact details, commencement date, accounting year-end, business sector.","Select the preferred Tax Office — recommended to choose the one closest to the business.","Add a Tax Representative (optional) — enter their Tax ID if applicable.","Save and resume (optional) — \"Save Progress and Close\" generates a Resume ID to continue registration later.","Additional declarations — whether the company is part of a multinational enterprise group (if yes, add each entity's name/Tax ID/country of residence/% holding) and whether it has any foreign-controlled company (same 4 details for each).","Upload required documents — a valid director's ID (National ID, international passport, or driver's licence) and a utility bill for address verification.","Review CAC details — this information is automatically submitted to the NRS as part of registration.","Submit registration — complete the Declarant Information section and click \"Submit Form.\" Registration is then pending NRS verification."]},{"h4":"Logging into Rev360"},{"steps":["Go to selfservice.nrs.gov.ng.","Click \"Continue with NRS SSO.\"","Enter your username and password (sent to your registered email during onboarding/migration).","Click \"Sign In.\" First-time users may be required to change their password; keep credentials secure and never share them."]}]},{"n":"25.8","t":"New technologies for tax practice and administration","b":[{"p":"Two future trends both rest on information technology: **increasing use of digital technology** (already happening globally — sophisticated tools enhancing traditional ways of serving clients); and a **radical shift/total takeover** (technology actively displacing, not just streamlining, the work of traditional tax professionals)."},{"h4":"25.8.1 – 25.8.6  Cloud computing, artificial intelligence and robotics, blockchain, data analytics, social media and mobile app technology"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Technology","Impact on tax practice"],"rows":[["**Cloud computing**","Accounting software modules for automated tax computation, returns preparation/filing, planning/forecasting, and payment — relieving firms of onsite server investment/maintenance, enabling remote work from any device/location, and facilitating practitioner-client collaboration."],["**AI and robotics**","Robotic Process Automation (RPA) handles routine/monotonous tasks; AI frees practitioners from time-consuming, repetitive work for higher-value advisory insight; **OCR** (optical character recognition) automates manual data entry by converting scanned/photographed text to digital files."],["**Blockchain**","An open, secured, decentralised ledger — where tax consultants/authorities are given data-chain access, all parties can see each transaction and determine its tax implication; facilitates tax payments at reduced processing cost; enables smart contracts and asset-ownership verification."],["**Data analytics**","Real-time monitoring of financial/tax matters; produces insights from both structured AND unstructured data; helps tax administrators improve internal operations and build revenue; helps practising firms offer more valuable advisory services."],["**Social media**","Used primarily for disseminating tax information (wide reach, appeal to younger audiences) and for professional collaboration (Facebook, Instagram, Twitter/X, LinkedIn); increases practitioner visibility to clients."],["**Mobile apps**","On-the-go access to client financial data (especially where clients use cloud accounting); tax authorities/practitioners can deploy their own apps for updates/news to taxpayers/clients."]]}},{"note":"A numbering gap in the study text, flagged rather than silently corrected: this section's sub-sections run **25.8.1, 25.8.2, 25.8.3, then 25.8.5 and 25.8.6** — there is **no 25.8.4** anywhere in the chapter. The six technologies are all discussed; only the number is missing. Note that §25.11 numbers the same six technologies consecutively as 25.11.1 to 25.11.6, which is how the set should read."}]},{"n":"25.9","t":"Impact of new technologies for tax practice and administration","b":[{"h4":"Benefits to tax administration"},{"ul":["**customer satisfaction** — enhanced perception of overall performance;","**lower cost of compliance** — staff can focus on more valuable activities;","**minimised data error** — improved quality of taxpayer information; and","**improved voluntary compliance** — better compliance levels, positively impacting revenue generation."]},{"h4":"Benefits to tax practising firms"},{"ul":["time savings — speed and accuracy of data entry/processing;","cost savings in the long run;","focus on higher-level jobs machines cannot handle;","mobility of service; and","increased capacity to serve multiple clients."]}]},{"n":"25.10","t":"Taxes applicable to technology-based transactions","b":[{"p":"E-business/e-commerce are now the norm, with most economic transactions executed online in real time. Common e-commerce examples: online shopping, online movies, internet banking, online ticketing/reservation, auction sites, journal subscriptions, and online betting/gaming."},{"key":"Income generated from technology-based transactions is **not exempt** from income tax — the traditional tax types (VAT, WHT, CIT, PIT) apply to e-commerce transactions just as they do to conventional ones. Some multinationals now separately line-item digital-business transactions in their own financial statements."},{"p":"A genuine practical difficulty: does a digital-space player (Google, Apple, Microsoft, Facebook, Jumia, Konga, etc.) pay the right amount of tax in every jurisdiction where its services are consumed, given it may have **no physical presence** there? This has driven many countries toward special taxes based on **significant economic presence (SEP)**, aligned with the OECD framework."},{"h4":"Digital Service Tax (DST) — international snapshot"},{"p":"As at 14 October 2020: Austria, France, Hungary, Italy, Poland, Spain, Turkey and the UK had implemented a DST; Belgium, the Czech Republic and Slovakia had published DST proposals; and Latvia, Norway and Slovenia had signalled intent to implement one."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Jurisdiction","DST detail"],"rows":[["United Kingdom","2% on gross UK-generated revenues of large businesses providing a social media service, search engine, or online marketplace to UK-based users — applied from 1 April 2020."],["France","3% tax on digital revenue for large tech companies (widely seen as targeting US tech giants) — France said it would withdraw the tax in favour of an internationally-negotiated OECD solution."]]}},{"note":"In late February 2021, the incoming US administration dropped the prior administration's insistence on a \"safe harbour\" for companies, removing a key obstacle to a global digital-tax deal — the OECD at the time predicted an acceptable international agreement was likely by mid-2021."},{"h4":"Nigeria's response — Significant Economic Presence (SEP)"},{"key":"Nigeria established a legal framework allowing it to tax the income of non-resident technology companies (NRTCs) whose activities constitute a **Significant Economic Presence** in Nigeria, where they lack a separately-incorporated local entity. Electronic transactions were also made subject to **stamp duties**."},{"p":"The Companies Income Tax (Significant Economic Presence) Order, 2020 (published 29 May 2020, Official Gazette No. 21, Vol. 107 of 10 February 2020) provides that a non-resident company has an SEP in Nigeria in any accounting year where it derives **≥N25 million** annual gross turnover (or its currency equivalent) from specified digital activities — OR falls into a second sub-category by:"},{"ol":["using a Nigerian domain name;","registering a website in Nigeria; or","having a purposeful and sustained interaction with persons in Nigeria by customising its digital platforms to target them (the **localisation test**) — for example, reflecting product prices in naira, or offering naira billing/payment options."]},{"note":"Some countries have also introduced regulatory frameworks making cryptocurrency transactions taxable. The (historical) Twitter ban in Nigeria reignited public debate over whether technology giants operating in the digital space were paying appropriate tax to Nigeria."}]},{"n":"25.11","t":"Technology as an effective tool for tax practitioners and administrators","b":[{"note":"The study text divides this section into **§25.11.1 to §25.11.6** — cloud technology, data analytics technology, artificial intelligence and robotics, social media technology, mobile app technology, and blockchain technology — taking in turn each of the technologies introduced at §25.8 and showing how a practitioner or administrator actually uses it."},{"note":"McKinsey has estimated that by 2030, intelligent agents/robots could replace up to 30% of the world's current human labour, causing 375 million people to switch job categories — raising the provocative question of whether robots should themselves be required to pay tax, given how many are being deployed to take over accountants' repetitive tasks."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Technology","Specific impact on tax work"],"rows":[["**Cloud**","Cloud-hosted tax management software; storage/retrieval of tax transaction data; access/analysis of tax data on any device, anywhere/anytime; robust dashboards; collaboration between tax authorities and/or taxpayers."],["**Data analytics**","Statistical analysis of large data volumes for better business insight; identifying/predicting tax risk factors and anomalies; deeper-insight advisory services. For auditors specifically: checking transactions at source, reviewing 100% of a dataset (not just a sample), predictive risk-assessment analytics, and evaluating programmed tax controls."],["**AI and robotics**","OCR and voice recognition already in use; AI-enabled chatbots handle complex tasks like answering subtle taxation queries; zero data entry; machine learning from an auditor's own past conclusions applied to future scenarios; pattern identification across structured/unstructured data to flag \"normal\" vs. \"abnormal\" transactions."],["**Social media**","Gaining visibility/exposure; being recognised as an expert; building an online professional network; keeping clients informed with prompt service."],["**Mobile apps**","On-demand client data access on the go, without needing to start a laptop/computer and go online — information available with a single tap."],["**Blockchain**","Decentralisation/distribution of a shared database; all parties (including tax authorities) involved in recording/validating a transaction from inception — meaning **no reconciliation needed** and **no tax audit required**. The immutable, identical-across-the-network ledger further offers: no single controlling entity, no single point of failure, enhanced security, enhanced transparency, and faster/more cost-effective processing."]]}}]},{"n":"25.12","t":"Key reasons why practice firms leverage technology","b":[{"ol":["**Winning more clients** — most clients prefer firms adaptive to technology changes;","**Retaining more clients** — modern technology enables more valuable, improved service, which retains clients;","**Cross-selling services** — technology platforms make a firm's services more visible to a wider client base; and","**Adding greater value** — automating routine/labour-intensive tasks frees firms to focus on higher-value work for clients."]}]},{"n":"25.13","t":"Challenges of technology to tax practice and administration","b":[{"h4":"25.13.1  Challenges"},{"ul":["low computer literacy level;","poor internet facility;","possible cyber threats;","high implementation cost;","lack of technical know-how; and","data-validation challenges — \"garbage in, garbage out.\""]},{"h4":"25.13.2  Opportunities"},{"ol":["letting tax professionals focus on higher-level jobs while serving more clients;","enhancing visibility to clients;","enabling personalised client services;","enhancing adaptability to change and enabling new service offerings;","saving time — greater efficiency and focus on relevant data;","saving cost, long-term;","enabling mobility of service — geography becomes unimportant;","providing new skills to tax professionals;","enabling collaborative work with anyone, anywhere in the world; and","increasing tax audit quality, as auditors can ask far more questions."]}]},{"n":"25.14","t":"Chapter review","b":[{"p":"This — the final chapter of the Taxation subject — discussed: computer applications amenable to tax planning/administration; navigational skills for NRS and other tax portals; electronic filing of income tax returns; automated VAT management/returns; excel payroll software templates for computing employees' tax; and the key features of the Nigeria Revenue Service's Integrated Tax Administration System (ITAS)."}]},{"n":"25.15","t":"Worked examples — study text questions and suggested solutions","b":[{"p":"The study text sets **three questions** at §25.15.1, with its own suggested solutions at §25.15.2. All three are reproduced here in full, with the book's answers and added explanation. The book prints them as questions, not illustrations — there are no illustrations in this chapter."},{"eg":{"t":"The aspects of tax administration handled by TAXPRO MAX and Rev360","q":[{"p":"In 2006, the NRS deployed the first tax portal (Webportal) to automate and streamline taxpayer registration and other administration processes. In 2014, an ITAS known as SIGTAS was implemented. More recently, the locally developed TaxPro Max solution has handled different aspects of tax administration, and on 30 April 2026 the NRS introduced Rev360 with features designed to make tax compliance easier, faster and more transparent. **Required**: explain the different aspects of tax administration that TaxPro Max and Rev360 can handle."}],"a":[{"p":"The different aspects of tax administration that can be handled by TaxPro Max and Rev360 include:"},{"ol":["**e-registration** of taxpayers;","**e-assessment**;","**e-payment** of assessments;","**e-filing** of returns;","an **e-tax calculator**;","**e-TCC** (electronic tax clearance certificates);","**e-reporting**; and","**e-tax audit**."]},{"key":"The eight functions cover the **whole compliance cycle end to end** — from getting on the register, through being assessed, paying, filing and obtaining a TCC, to being audited. That is the point of an **integrated** tax administration system: the same platform carries the taxpayer from registration under §4.2 to the tax clearance certificate of Chapter 6 and on to the tax audit of §4.10, without the taxpayer ever attending a tax office."},{"note":"Know the **timeline** the question sets out, because the dates are examinable: the first **Webportal** in **2006**; the integrated tax administration system **SIGTAS** in **2014**; the locally developed **TaxPro Max**; and **Rev360** from **30 April 2026**. The progression is from a portal that merely automated registration to a single solution handling every aspect of administration."}],"tag":"Study text — 25.15.1, Question 1","open":true}},{"eg":{"t":"Two reasons why a firm of accountants should leverage technology","q":[{"p":"There are many reasons a tax practitioner should leverage new technology, such as cloud computing, in their firm. **Required**: explain 2 reasons it is beneficial for a firm of accountants to leverage technology."}],"a":[{"p":"The main reasons why practice firms leverage technology include:"},{"ol":["**winning more clients** — the majority of clients would prefer to hire tax practice firms that are adaptive to technology changes;","**retaining more clients** — the adoption and adaptation of modern technologies enables practising firms to render the much-needed valuable and improved services which make clients retain them;","**cross-selling services** — technology provides platforms enabling practising firms to make their services more visible to many clients; and","**adding greater value** — firms that adopt new technologies are able to automate most of the routine and labour-intensive tasks, which makes it possible for such firms to focus their attention on tasks that generate greater value for their clients."]},{"key":"The question asks for **two**. Give two and develop them — the book's solution lists four, but marks here go to **explanation rather than enumeration**. The strongest pair is usually **adding greater value** (automation frees professional time for advisory work rather than data entry) and **retaining clients** (better service quality), because the other two follow from them."},{"note":"Note that all four reasons are **commercial**, not technical — they are about the firm's client base and the value of its output. That is deliberate: §25.12 is about why a firm *chooses* to invest in technology, while §25.11 is about *how* each technology is actually used and §25.13 about what goes wrong."}],"tag":"Study text — 25.15.1, Question 2","open":true}},{"eg":{"t":"The current roles of tax administrators in Nigeria","q":[{"p":"A tax system comprises tax policy, tax legislation, and tax administration. Tax administrators are public authorities empowered to assess, collect and account for taxes for a recognised jurisdiction. **Required**: explain the current roles of tax administrators in Nigeria."}],"a":[{"p":"The roles of the tax administrators include:"},{"ol":["**registering and educating** taxpayers;","**receiving tax returns** filed by taxpayers;","**assessing** taxpayers (individuals and entities);","**collecting, maintaining records and accounting for** taxes paid;","**managing and processing tax refund applications**;","conducting **routine tax audits, investigations**, etc.;","**managing and enforcing tax debt recovery**; and","**advising on the review of tax legislation and regulations**."]},{"key":"The question's own preamble gives the framework an answer should use: a tax system comprises **tax policy, tax legislation and tax administration**, and tax administrators are the public authorities **empowered to assess, collect and account for taxes**. The eight roles then fall into that shape — assess (iii), collect and account (iv), with registration and education before, audit and enforcement behind, and an **advisory role on legislation** that feeds back into the other two limbs of the system."},{"note":"Each of these roles has a statutory home elsewhere in the subject, which is worth citing: registration at **§4.2**, receipt of returns at **§4.3 and §4.4**, assessment at **§4.8**, collection at **§4.9**, refunds at **§4.9.3**, audit and investigation at **§4.10**, and debt recovery at **§4.10.8 and §4.10.9**. The administrators themselves — the NRS, the State Services, the Joint Revenue Board and the local government revenue committees — are at **§2.4**."}],"tag":"Study text — 25.15.1, Question 3","open":true}}]}],"formulas":[],"focus":"This is a purely conceptual chapter — nothing to compute, everything to recall precisely. Focus on: the TaxPro Max vs. Rev360 distinction (launch dates — 7 June 2021 vs. 30 April 2026 — and that Rev360's initial rollout targets small businesses/informal-sector individuals/medium taxpayers before wider extension, NOT an immediate full replacement); the 8 e-functions common to both platforms (e-registration/assessment/payment/filing/tax-calculator/TCC/reporting/audit); the N25,000,000 SEP turnover threshold and its 2 alternative qualifying tests (specified digital activities, OR the domain-name/website-registration/localisation-test route); and s.157 NTA's mandatory fiscalisation-system requirement. This chapter closes the Taxation subject — Chapter 22's digital-economy/e-commerce material (Chapter 22 §22.18) is the closest sibling content and worth reviewing alongside this one, since both cover SEP and digital taxation from different angles.","errors":["Assuming Rev360 has already, or will immediately, fully replace TaxPro Max — the source is explicit that Rev360's initial phase targets only small businesses, informal-sector individuals and medium taxpayers, with wider extension still pending; full replacement is described as merely \"hoped for,\" not confirmed.","Treating income from technology-based/e-commerce transactions as exempt from conventional tax types — VAT, WHT, CIT and PIT all apply to technology-based transactions exactly as they do to conventional ones; the SEP/DST discussion concerns HOW non-resident digital companies without physical presence are brought into the tax net, not a claim that digital income is untaxed in principle.","Confusing the SEP Order's TWO alternative qualifying routes for a non-resident tech company — the ≥N25,000,000 turnover-from-specified-activities test, and the separate domain-name/website-registration/localisation-test route — as though only one test exists or both must be satisfied together; either route independently establishes an SEP."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"When did the NRS launch Rev360 to replace the legacy TaxPro Max portal, and what was its initial rollout focus?","o":["7 June 2021; all corporate taxpayers immediately","30 April 2026; small businesses, informal-sector individuals, and medium taxpayers first","1 January 2026; only non-resident companies","2014; replacing SIGTAS directly","2006; replacing the original Webportal"],"a":1,"w":"Rev360 launched 30 April 2026. Its initial phase focuses on small businesses and individuals in the informal sector, plus medium taxpayers, before extending to other categories — full TaxPro Max replacement is only \"hoped for,\" not yet realised.","src":"Chapter 25.5","sec":"25.5"},{"q":"Under the Companies Income Tax (Significant Economic Presence) Order, 2020, what annual gross turnover threshold establishes an SEP for a non-resident company via specified digital activities?","o":["N10,000,000","N25,000,000","N50,000,000","N100,000,000","N150,000,000"],"a":1,"w":"A non-resident company has an SEP in Nigeria where it derives N25 million or more in annual gross turnover (or its currency equivalent) from specified digital activities in any accounting year.","src":"Chapter 25.10","sec":"25.10"},{"q":"Which section of the NTA 2025 makes it mandatory for a taxable person making a taxable supply to use the Service's fiscalisation system?","o":["Section 17","Section 56","Section 150","Section 157","Section 201"],"a":3,"w":"Section 157 NTA mandates use of the fiscalisation system (fiscal equipment for electronic invoicing and data transfer) for any taxable person making a taxable supply.","src":"Chapter 25.4","sec":"25.4"},{"q":"Per the \"localisation test\" under the SEP Order, which of these would NOT, on its own, indicate a non-resident tech company's significant economic presence in Nigeria?","o":["Using a Nigerian domain name","Registering a website in Nigeria","Reflecting product prices in naira and offering naira billing/payment options","Having a single Nigerian customer make a one-off purchase with no platform customisation at all","Purposefully and sustainedly customising its digital platform to target persons in Nigeria"],"a":3,"w":"The localisation test requires PURPOSEFUL AND SUSTAINED interaction/customisation targeting Nigerian users (e.g. naira pricing/billing) — a single, incidental transaction with no platform customisation does not itself establish an SEP under this test.","src":"Chapter 25.10","sec":"25.10"},{"q":"According to McKinsey's estimate cited in this chapter, what share of the world's current human labour could intelligent agents/robots replace by 2030?","o":["10%","15%","30%","50%","75%"],"a":2,"w":"McKinsey estimated that by 2030, intelligent agents/robots could replace up to 30% of the world's current human labour, causing an estimated 375 million people to switch job categories.","src":"Chapter 25.11","sec":"25.11"}],"theory":[{"q":"Explain briefly why blockchain technology is said to eliminate the need for both reconciliation and tax audit in a transaction where the tax authority is made part of the transaction flow.","marks":5,"a":[{"p":"On a blockchain, all parties to a transaction — including the tax authority, where given access to the data chain — are involved in recording and validating the transaction from its very inception, on a shared, decentralised, immutable ledger. Because every party already sees and agrees on the same transaction record in real time, there is no need to later reconcile separately-kept records against each other (no reconciliation needed), and no need for a retrospective audit to verify what happened (no tax audit required) — the verification is built into the recording process itself, rather than performed after the fact."}],"src":"Chapter 25.11","sec":"25.11"},{"q":"Distinguish between the two alternative routes by which a non-resident technology company can be found to have a Significant Economic Presence (SEP) in Nigeria, under the Companies Income Tax (SEP) Order, 2020.","marks":6,"a":[{"p":"**Route 1 — turnover-from-activities test**: the company derives N25,000,000 or more (or the currency equivalent) as annual gross turnover from any combination of specified digital activities in an accounting year."},{"p":"**Route 2 — domain/website/localisation test**: independent of the turnover threshold, the company (a) uses a Nigerian domain name, (b) registers a website in Nigeria, or (c) has a purposeful and sustained interaction with persons in Nigeria by customising its digital platforms to target them — for example, pricing products in naira or offering naira billing/payment options."},{"note":"Either route, satisfied independently, establishes an SEP — a company does not need to satisfy both."}],"src":"Chapter 25.10","sec":"25.10"}]}}]});
